parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ca3c1e3561f2856045116fc4",
  "rewritten_text": "I must go, unsure of what will happen, but I will return or find you as soon as I can. A single word or look will decide if I ever step foot in your father’s house again. Recovering from such a letter was impossible. Ten minutes of solitude were not enough to calm her; instead, her excitement only grew. Before she could process her emotions, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta arrived. She tried to act normal, but soon she could not follow their conversation and had to claim she felt unwell. They were shocked by her pale appearance and refused to leave her side, which only made her more anxious. Desperate for peace, she decided to go home. Mrs. Musgrove insisted she take a sedan chair, but Anne refused. She could not bear the thought of losing her chance to run into Captain Wentworth on her walk through town. After Mrs. Musgrove confirmed Anne had not suffered a head injury, she finally let her go. Determined to ensure everything was clear, Anne added, Please tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your whole party tonight. I am worried there was a misunderstanding, so please assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we expect them both. Oh, my dear, it is perfectly understood, I promise you."
}

