parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "bcef59b1ab0f5cbdb67d27b1",
  "rewritten_text": "My father had been the center of my existence, and his memory remained the essence of my life. While I could feel thankful to others, I could never again love or hope as I once had; everything was agony, and even my joys were merely tolerated rather than savored. I existed like a lonely valley surrounded by sheer, dark cliffs, where no warmth could reach and no path led to brighter pastures. Consequently, while the presence of friendship offered temporary relief, it could not heal me. It arrived like a soft breeze, then vanished, leaving me barely aware of its absence. My inner vitality had withered, so do not wonder that I greeted the arrival of a friend—that greatest of blessings—without joy, and mourned his departure without deep sorrow. My friend was named Woodville. I shall recount his story briefly so you might understand how frozen my heart must have been to remain unmoved by his moving words and gentle compassion, and how, as he was also deeply troubled, we were suited to comfort one another, had I not been turned to stone by the Gorgon of Misery. Woodville’s sorrows were not as profound as mine; his was a natural grief, meant to refine rather than ruin the spirit, from which he might eventually emerge even more radiant. Woodville, the son of an impoverished minister, possessed a classical education. He was among the rare few favored by fortune from birth, gifted with boundless intellect and charm, and shielded by her from even the slightest flaw or fleeting disappointment. She appeared to have crafted his mind with a purity that no corruption could stain, and his judgment was beyond error. His genius was extraordinary, rising like a brilliant star that drew every gaze in admiration. He was a poet, though that title has been so cheapened it fails to capture his true nature. He resembled the poets of antiquity, blessed by the Muses at birth. Moving among men, he seemed surrounded by a divine aura that set him apart and elevated him above them. His exceptional beauty, the intense light in his eyes, and his eloquent speech, which left listeners in silent, rapturous awe, made him so superior that others seemed created merely to serve his greatness."
}

