parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "de217fd114663057b8e1e61f",
  "rewritten_text": "Jupiter was overjoyed at the sight, but his master looked deeply disappointed. He urged us to keep digging, and I had barely started again when I tripped over a large iron ring buried in the dirt. We worked frantically for ten minutes, feeling more excited than ever. We uncovered a wooden chest, remarkably well-preserved and hard, likely treated with chemicals. It measured three and a half feet long, three feet wide, and two and a half feet deep, reinforced with riveted iron bands. Six iron rings on the sides suggested it was meant to be carried by six people. We could barely budge it, so we realized we could not move it. Fortunately, the lid was held only by two sliding bolts. We pulled them back, shaking with nerves. Suddenly, a priceless treasure sparkled before us. As our lantern light hit the pit, a dazzling pile of gold and jewels blinded us. I cannot describe my shock. Legrand was exhausted and silent. Jupiter looked pale with terror, completely stunned. He knelt in the pit and buried his arms deep into the gold, as if bathing in it. Finally, he sighed and muttered to himself, \"And this is all because of the gold-bug! The pretty gold-bug! The poor little gold-bug I treated so badly! Aren't you ashamed of yourself?\" Eventually, I had to remind them that we needed to move the treasure. It was getting late, and we had to get everything home before sunrise. We were so overwhelmed that we struggled to decide what to do next."
}

