parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c2f5bb761d6d8667a4a656d4",
  "rewritten_text": "That hardly matters. She replied, I might as well ask why you chose to tell me you loved me against your will, your reason, and your own character, if your clear intention was to insult me. Was that not justification enough for my rudeness? But I have other reasons. You know I do. Even if my own feelings had not been against you, even if I were indifferent or favorable, do you think I would ever accept the man who destroyed the happiness of my beloved sister? Darcy paled as she spoke, but he quickly composed himself and listened without interrupting. I have every reason to think poorly of you. Nothing excuses your unjust, ungenerous actions. You cannot deny that you were the primary cause of their separation, exposing one to public criticism for instability and the other to mockery, leaving them both in deep misery. She paused, noticing with indignation that he seemed entirely unrepentant, even offering a skeptical smile. Can you deny it? she repeated. He replied with forced calm, I have no desire to deny that I did everything possible to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with my success. I was kinder to him than to myself. Elizabeth ignored his self-serving remark, though it only deepened her resentment. It is not just that affair, she continued, that fuels my dislike. My opinion of you was formed long ago, based on what Mr. Wickham told me months ago. What can you say to that? How can you defend yourself or claim you were misunderstood? You take a keen interest in that man, Darcy said, his voice tightening and his face flushing. Who could know his misfortunes and not feel for him? His misfortunes! Darcy repeated with contempt. Yes, his misfortunes have been great indeed. And you caused them, Elizabeth exclaimed. You reduced him to his current state of poverty."
}

