parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dbd0d5374d048f46d0365a26",
  "rewritten_text": "Do not misunderstand; I was never truly insane. I remained aware of my condition even when frantic thoughts pushed me toward madness, keeping them hidden in quiet isolation. Those around me noticed nothing of this. They merely observed a spirit-broken girl who spoke softly and shed hidden tears beneath lowered lashes. They saw someone who avoided company, shunned attention, and never smiled—that was all. Eventually, I fled. I departed my guardian’s home and vanished; based on the letters I left, it was assumed I had taken my own life. Consequently, the search for me was half-hearted, and I was soon forgotten. I sailed from London on a small ship heading north. Having succeeded, I found peace in my solitude. As the vessel glided over calm waters, I sat on deck beneath the sky, feeling transformed. I was no longer the frantic, wretched Mathilda, but a young recluse committed to a quiet life, striving to keep my heart free from turmoil and despair. My simple, nun-like attire, the secret of my survival, and the prospect of permanent isolation fostered a sense of calm in my wounded spirit. The breeze refreshed me, and I watched the sunlit waves and birds skimming the water with serene eyes. I slept without dreams and awoke to enjoy my peaceful liberty. Four days later, we reached our destination. I left the coast immediately, heading inland to a location I had already chosen. I sought a lonely house on a vast plain, far from any neighbors, where I could view the entire horizon and wander undisturbed. I was not a misanthrope, yet I knew my emotional stability required solitude. I settled in a desolate expanse, a rocky heath covered in sparse grass, marked only by a few rushes near a small pond."
}

