parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "059dfba1f6500818a8fa2fac",
  "rewritten_text": "Anne soon had plenty of chances to observe the group. She formed her own opinions, though she kept them to herself to avoid conflict at home. While she saw that Louisa was the favorite, she felt certain that Captain Wentworth was not in love with either sister. The girls were infatuated with him, but it was merely a passing phase of admiration rather than true love. Charles Hayter seemed to feel ignored, yet Henrietta appeared torn between the two men. Anne wished she could warn them of the potential trouble they were causing, though she believed no one acted with malice. She was relieved that Captain Wentworth seemed unaware of the pain he caused; he showed no arrogance and likely did not even consider Charles Hayter a rival. His only fault was accepting the attention of two women simultaneously. Eventually, Charles Hayter withdrew. He stopped visiting Uppercross for three days and even declined a dinner invitation. When Mr. Musgrove found him studying intensely, he worried that Charles was overworking himself. Mary hoped Henrietta had rejected him, while her husband expected to see him the next day. Anne simply thought Charles was acting wisely. One morning, while the men were out shooting, the sisters from the Great House visited the cottage. It was a beautiful November day, and the Miss Musgroves stopped by to announce they were going for a long walk, assuming Mary would not want to join. When Mary insisted she loved long walks, Anne could tell by the sisters' expressions that they did not actually want her company. She marveled at the family habit of forcing everyone to do everything together, regardless of whether it was convenient or desired."
}

