parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "31776eb86bc7b4dc3ce1a773",
  "rewritten_text": "I regretted hearing Jim say that; it diminished my respect for him. My conscience pricked me so severely that I finally promised myself I would row to shore at dawn and report him. Immediately, I felt relieved and lighthearted, my worries vanished. I kept a sharp lookout for a light, humming to myself until one appeared. Jim shouted, \"We are safe, Huck! Jump for joy! That is Cairo at last, I am certain!\" I replied, \"I will take the canoe to check, Jim. It might not be the place.\" He eagerly prepared the canoe, placing his coat inside for me to sit on. As I pushed off, he said, \"Soon I will be shouting for joy, and I will say it is all because of Huck. I am a free man, and I never could have been without you. Jim will never forget you; you are the best friend I have ever had, and the only one I have now.\" I was paddling away, desperate to betray him, but his words drained my resolve. I slowed down, no longer certain if I was glad to be leaving. When I was fifty yards away, Jim called out, \"There you go, the true Huck; the only white gentleman who ever kept his word to old Jim.\" I felt sick, yet I told myself I had to go through with it. Just then, a skiff with two armed men approached. They stopped, and I did too. One asked, \"What is that over there?\" \"A piece of a raft,\" I replied. \"Are you on it?\" \"Yes, sir.\" \"Any men on it?\" \"Only one, sir.\" \"Well, five slaves ran off tonight up the river. Is your man white or black?\" I hesitated, unable to speak. I tried to gather the courage to tell the truth, but I lacked the nerve. Realizing I was failing, I finally said, \"He is white.\" \"We will go see for ourselves,\" the man said. \"I wish you would,\" I answered, \"because it is my father on board, and perhaps you could help me tow the raft to the light.\""
}

