parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "96bd546fb300f90b6860aa6b",
  "rewritten_text": "He stayed inside the next day, mostly confined to his room, paralyzed by a frantic fear of death yet feeling detached from life. The sensation of being hunted and trapped consumed him. A fluttering curtain made him tremble, and dead leaves against the window felt like his own failed promises and regrets. Closing his eyes, he saw the sailor’s face in the glass again, and terror gripped him. Perhaps it was just his imagination conjuring vengeance. Reality was chaotic, but the mind was terrifyingly logical, using remorse to haunt sin and give every crime its consequences. In the real world, morality rarely dictated outcomes; the strong succeeded while the weak failed. Surely, if a stranger had been lurking, the staff would have noticed. He convinced himself it was just a hallucination; Sibyl Vane’s brother had likely perished at sea, and his youthful appearance had kept his identity hidden. Yet, if it were only an illusion, the power of his conscience to manifest such horrors was terrifying. What kind of existence would he have if his crimes haunted his every moment, mocking him from the shadows? The thought turned him pale, and the room felt suddenly freezing. He had been mad to kill his friend. The memory returned in vivid, gruesome detail, and his sin rose before him, stained in scarlet."
}

