parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "09c8ccadb112fbab8f3d8631",
  "rewritten_text": "“Starve? Oh, Sir Simon, are you famished? I have a sandwich in my bag if you would like it.” “No, thank you; I do not eat anymore. Still, it is kind of you, and you are far more pleasant than your dreadful, ill-mannered, and dishonest relatives.” “Stop!” Virginia exclaimed, stamping her foot. “You are the rude one. As for dishonesty, you stole my paints to touch up that absurd bloodstain in the library. You took my reds, so I could not paint sunsets, then my greens and yellows, leaving me only indigo and white for depressing moonlight scenes. I never reported you, though I was quite annoyed; besides, who has ever seen emerald-green blood?” “Well,” the Ghost replied meekly, “what was I to do? Real blood is hard to find, and since your brother started this with his detergent, I saw no reason not to use your paints. Color is a matter of taste; the Cantervilles have blue blood, though I know you Americans do not value such things.” “You know nothing. You should emigrate to improve your mind. My father would gladly provide a free passage, and since the customs officers are Democrats, there would be no trouble with the authorities. You would be a sensation in New York; many people there would pay a fortune for a family ghost.” “I do not think I would care for America.” “I suppose because we lack ruins and curiosities,” Virginia remarked sarcastically. “No ruins or curiosities? You have your navy and your manners,” the Ghost retorted. “Good evening. I am going to ask my father for an extra week of holiday for the twins.” “Please do not leave, Miss Virginia,” he pleaded. “I am lonely and miserable, and I cannot find rest.” “That is ridiculous! You simply need to go to bed and extinguish your candle.”"
}

