parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "567d205c2123be10745e911b",
  "rewritten_text": "April 19th. At nine this morning, I was terrified by how close the moon appeared, but my condenser pump finally showed signs of a change in the air. By ten, the air density had clearly increased. By eleven, the equipment required little effort, and at noon, I cautiously opened the rubber chamber. Although I immediately suffered from painful spasms and a headache, I decided to endure them, knowing I was descending into thicker air. However, my descent remained dangerously fast. I had assumed the moon’s atmosphere would be dense enough to support my balloon’s weight, but I was wrong. My rapid fall proved that the air was not thick enough, likely due to the geological issues I mentioned before. I was plummeting toward the surface at a terrifying speed. I quickly threw out my ballast, water barrels, equipment, and everything else in the basket, but it did not slow me down. With only half a mile to go, I removed my coat, hat, and boots, then cut the heavy basket loose. Clinging to the netting, I saw that the landscape was covered in tiny houses before I crashed into the center of a strange city. I landed in a crowd of ugly little people who stood there like idiots, grinning and staring at me and my balloon with their hands on their hips, offering no help at all."
}

