parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eaf99e2faba4082ad26fe811",
  "rewritten_text": "Unfortunate Jack was later killed in a duel by Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara passed away from grief at Tunbridge Wells before the year concluded; thus, in every sense, the event was a triumph. It proved an arduous transformation, if one might apply such theatrical terminology to a profound mystery of the supernatural—or, more scientifically, the higher-natural realm—requiring three full hours of preparation. Finally, he was satisfied with his guise. The leather riding-boots were slightly oversized, and he located only one of the two horse-pistols, yet he felt content, gliding from the wainscoting at a quarter past one to creep down the hall. Upon arriving at the Blue Bed Chamber, where the twins slept, he noticed the door slightly open. Seeking a dramatic entrance, he threw it wide, only for a heavy water jug to tumble onto him, soaking him completely and narrowly missing his shoulder. Simultaneously, he heard muffled laughter erupting from the four-poster bed. The nervous shock drove him to retreat to his room immediately, and he spent the following day suffering from a severe cold. His only solace was that he had left his head behind; had he carried it, the outcome could have been dire. He abandoned all ambition to terrify the boorish American family, settling instead for shuffling through the corridors in soft slippers, a thick red muffler protecting his throat from drafts, and carrying a small arquebuse for defense against the twins. The final indignity occurred on September 19th. He had descended to the main entrance-hall, confident he would remain undisturbed, and was mocking the large Saroni photographs of the United States Minister and his wife that had replaced the Canterville portraits. He wore a simple, tidy shroud stained with graveyard soil, his jaw bound with yellow linen, while he carried a lantern and a sexton’s spade."
}

