parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "bca0b86cf039fa70efdf348c",
  "rewritten_text": "Goodness, she was an expert conversationalist. It would have been rude to mention the late hour or my own exhaustion. I regretted not retiring earlier, but I was trapped now. She prattled on within the silent, ghostly castle until a distant, muffled scream rose from below, sounding so agonizing that it made my skin prickle. The queen paused, her eyes brightening with delight as she tilted her head like a listening bird. The noise pierced the stillness once more. I asked what it was. She replied that it was a stubborn soul enduring the rack for many hours. When I asked what he was enduring, she invited me to witness the spectacle, promising he would be torn apart if he did not reveal his secret. She was a polished monster, calm and serene, while my own legs ached in sympathy with the victim. Escorted by armored guards with torches, we descended through damp, moldy stone corridors that smelled of ancient darkness. The journey was long and unsettling, made worse by the sorceress discussing the prisoner's crime: killing a stag in the royal woods based on an anonymous tip. I suggested that anonymous accusations were unjust and that the accused should face his accuser. She dismissed this as trivial, noting the accuser had arrived masked at night and remained unknown to the forester. I asked if anyone else witnessed the act, and she admitted no one saw the killing, only that the informant spotted the man near the carcass. I pointed out that the masked informant might be the true culprit, but she insisted the torture was necessary to force a confession. She claimed that while his life was forfeit by law, she needed him to confess and receive absolution to protect her own soul."
}

