parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6f808974e875a701edbd6bfc",
  "rewritten_text": "The beetle was remarkably heavy, and I could understand why Jupiter thought it was special. However, I could not fathom why Legrand agreed with him. When I finished looking at the insect, Legrand spoke grandly. He said he had summoned me to help him follow the path set by fate and the beetle. I interrupted him, telling him he was clearly unwell and needed to rest. I offered to stay with him for a few days until he recovered. He told me to check his pulse, and to my surprise, he had no fever. I insisted he was still ill, but he claimed he was simply excited and needed me to help him calm down. He explained that he and Jupiter were heading into the hills on the mainland and needed a trusted companion. He insisted I was the only one he could rely on. I asked if the beetle was involved in this trip, and he confirmed it was. I refused to take part in such a ridiculous plan. He expressed his regret, saying they would have to go alone. Worried he had lost his mind, I asked how long they would be gone. He said they would leave immediately and return by sunrise. I made him promise that once this obsession was settled, he would listen to my medical advice. He agreed, and we left at four o'clock. Jupiter carried the scythe and spades, seemingly more concerned about keeping the tools away from his master than out of any desire to work."
}

