parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9b0f60c3e7144a9b58a82cdc",
  "rewritten_text": "Sandy and I were back on the road early the next morning. It felt wonderful to breathe in the fresh, clean forest air after spending two days trapped in that miserable, foul-smelling castle. Of course, the place had suited Sandy well enough, as she was accustomed to high society. The poor girl had been quiet for a while, and I knew she was eager to talk. Since she had supported me so well in the castle with her elaborate stories, I decided to let her speak as much as she liked without complaint. She began, \"Now we turn to Sir Marhaus, who rode south with a thirty-year-old damsel.\" I asked if she was picking up the trail of the cowboys again, and she confirmed it. I told her to go ahead and start from the beginning, promising to listen closely while I smoked my pipe. She continued, \"Sir Marhaus and the damsel rode into a deep forest and were overtaken by night. They eventually reached a courtyard belonging to the Duke of South Marches, where they asked for shelter. The next morning, the Duke challenged Sir Marhaus to a fight. Sir Marhaus armed himself, attended mass, ate breakfast, and mounted his horse in the courtyard. The Duke and his six sons were waiting, each holding a spear. They charged; the Duke and two of his sons broke their spears against Sir Marhaus, but he did not strike back. The remaining four sons attacked in pairs, breaking their spears as well, while Sir Marhaus still refused to hit them. Finally, Sir Marhaus charged the Duke and struck him with his spear, knocking both the man and his horse to the ground.\""
}

