parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "37b03a2fe67a2b507932ade1",
  "rewritten_text": "“Basil, this is remarkable! I must meet Dorian Gray.” Hallward rose and paced the garden before returning. “Harry,” he stated, “Dorian Gray is merely an artistic inspiration to me. You might perceive nothing in him, yet I see everything. He influences my work even when his likeness is absent; he suggests a new style. I discover him in specific lines and subtle colors. That is all.” “Then why refuse to display his portrait?” Lord Henry inquired. “Because I have inadvertently infused it with a strange artistic devotion I have never mentioned to him. He remains unaware, and he shall stay that way. However, the public might discern it, and I refuse to expose my soul to their superficial scrutiny. My heart will not be examined under their lens. There is too much of myself in that work, Harry—far too much.” “Poets lack your hesitation. They understand that passion sells; a broken heart often leads to multiple editions.” “I despise them for it,” Hallward exclaimed. “An artist should produce beauty without injecting his own life into it. We live in an era where art is treated as autobiography, and we have abandoned the abstract sense of beauty. One day I will reveal its true nature, which is why the world will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.” “I believe you are mistaken, Basil, but I will not debate you. Only the intellectually adrift argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray fond of you?” The painter pondered briefly. “He likes me,” he replied eventually. “I flatter him excessively, finding a perverse pleasure in remarks I later regret. Generally, he is delightful, and we converse on countless topics in the studio. Occasionally, however, he is thoughtlessly cruel, seemingly enjoying my distress. At those moments, Harry, I feel I have surrendered my entire soul to someone who treats it like a mere flower for his lapel, a trinket to flatter his vanity, or a fleeting ornament for a summer day.”"
}

