parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "36ba7e4ad186fe4844d74638",
  "rewritten_text": "Yet how could I, a bewildered country boy, escape that strange contradiction that even the wisest men encounter daily? I told Biddy that while her words might be true, I adored her intensely. I buried my face in my hands, clutching my hair and pulling it in frustration. I was fully aware of my own foolishness, feeling that my face deserved to be struck against the stones for being part of such a fool. Biddy, being incredibly sensible, stopped arguing. She placed her work-roughened but comforting hand over mine, pulling them away from my hair. She patted my shoulder as I wept into my sleeve, much like I had in the brewery yard, feeling a vague sense of being mistreated by the world. Biddy expressed gratitude that I trusted her, promising to keep my secrets. She remarked that if her former self—a poor teacher needing instruction—were still my mentor, she would assign a difficult lesson, though I had surpassed her now. Sighing, she stood up and asked if we should continue our walk or return home. I stood, embraced her, and kissed her, promising to always share everything with her. She replied that I would do so until I became a gentleman. I insisted I never would, meaning I would always tell her everything, as she already knew my secrets. She whispered softly while gazing at the ships, then asked again if we should walk further or head back. I chose to continue our stroll, and as the summer afternoon faded into a beautiful evening, we walked on."
}

