parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5441e98f11df0c5dda679de0",
  "rewritten_text": "It was the best of times and the worst of times, an age of wisdom and foolishness, of belief and skepticism, of light and darkness, of hope and despair. We had everything and nothing, heading straight to heaven or the opposite. In short, that era was so much like our own that some of its loudest voices insisted it be described only in the most extreme terms. A king with a heavy jaw and a plain-faced queen sat on England's throne, while a similarly jawed king and a beautiful queen ruled France. In both nations, the ruling elite were certain that their power was permanent. It was 1775. England experienced spiritual revelations just as we do now. Mrs. Southcott had recently turned twenty-five, and a prophetic soldier had announced that London and Westminster were destined to be swallowed up. The Cock-lane ghost had been silenced only twelve years prior, after tapping out messages much like the unoriginal spirits of our own time. Meanwhile, messages of far greater consequence had reached the English Crown from British subjects in America, proving more significant to humanity than any ghostly communications. France, less spiritually blessed, slid smoothly toward ruin, printing and wasting paper money. Guided by their clergy, the French amused themselves with brutal acts, such as sentencing a youth to have his hands severed, his tongue torn out, and his body burned alive for failing to kneel in the rain before a passing procession of monks. It is likely that in the forests of France and Norway, trees were already marked by Fate to be felled and fashioned into the terrifying guillotine that would soon make history."
}

