parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d2cd8214e1fe069a8d0da01d",
  "rewritten_text": "The next morning, the Otis family discussed the ghost over breakfast. Mr. Otis was annoyed that his gift had been rejected. He stated that while he did not want to hurt the ghost, throwing pillows at him was rude, considering how long he had lived there. The twins laughed at this, but Mr. Otis insisted that if the ghost refused to use the lubricant, they would have to take his chains away because the noise made sleeping impossible. For the rest of the week, they were left alone, though the bloodstain on the library floor kept reappearing. This was odd because Mr. Otis locked the doors and windows every night. The stain also changed colors, shifting from dull red to vermilion, purple, and even bright emerald-green. The family found these changes amusing and often bet on the next color, though little Virginia was always distressed by the stain and nearly cried when it turned green. The ghost appeared again on Sunday night. A loud crash in the hall brought the family downstairs, where they found a suit of armor on the floor and the ghost sitting in a chair, rubbing his knees in pain. The twins immediately shot pellets at him, while Mr. Otis pointed his revolver and ordered him to put his hands up. The ghost shrieked in anger and vanished through them like mist, blowing out Washington’s candle and leaving everyone in the dark."
}

