parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ef1185aa186cfce9f5050233",
  "rewritten_text": "Four days following these strange events, a funeral procession departed Canterville Chase around eleven at night. Eight black horses, adorned with nodding ostrich plumes, pulled the hearse, while the leaden casket rested beneath a purple pall embroidered with the golden Canterville crest. Servants carrying torches flanked the carriages, creating a striking scene. Lord Canterville, having traveled from Wales, served as chief mourner, riding in the lead carriage with young Virginia. The American Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the boys, with Mrs. Umney in the final carriage; it seemed fitting that she, having endured the ghost’s presence for over fifty years, should witness his final departure. The Reverend Augustus Dampier conducted the solemn service at a grave prepared beneath an ancient yew tree. As the coffin descended, the servants doused their torches, and Virginia placed a cross of almond blossoms upon it. Moonlight suddenly illuminated the churchyard, and a nightingale sang from a nearby thicket. Recalling the ghost’s vision of the Garden of Death, Virginia wept silently throughout the return journey. The following morning, Mr. Otis met with Lord Canterville to discuss the jewels the spirit had bestowed upon Virginia. These items, particularly a sixteenth-century Venetian ruby necklace, were so valuable that Mr. Otis hesitated to let his daughter keep them. He insisted that, under the laws of mortmain, the gems were rightfully family heirlooms. He urged Lord Canterville to reclaim them as recovered property, noting that his daughter was merely a child with no desire for such extravagant luxuries."
}

