parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2d0e06111a30ef98f5bd4d3d",
  "rewritten_text": "Is that all? That is not much. Many people have angered the Republic and must face the guillotine. Jarvis Lorry, banker, English. Which one is he? I am he, as I am the last one left. Jarvis Lorry answered these questions while standing by the coach door, speaking to a group of officials. They slowly inspected the carriage and checked the luggage on the roof. Local people crowded around, staring intently, while a mother held out her child to touch the wife of an aristocrat who had been executed. Here are your signed papers, Jarvis Lorry. May I leave now, citizen? You may. Move on, drivers! Have a safe trip! I salute you, citizens. The first danger is behind us! Jarvis Lorry clasped his hands and looked upward. Inside the carriage, there was terror, weeping, and the heavy breathing of the unconscious passenger. Are we going too slowly? Can you make them go faster? Lucie asked, clinging to the old man. It would look like we are running away, my dear. I cannot push them too hard or it will raise suspicion. Look back and see if we are being followed! The road is clear, my dear. We are not being followed yet. We passed scattered houses, farms, and factories, moving through open country and rows of bare trees. The carriage rattled over uneven pavement and struggled through deep mud. Our impatience was so intense that we felt like jumping out to run or hide, anything to avoid stopping. We passed more ruins, farms, and cottages. Had the drivers tricked us and brought us back the same way? Was this the same place? Thankfully, no. It was a village. Look back and check for pursuers! Quiet, we are at the station. The four horses were removed slowly. The coach sat in the street, motionless. New horses appeared one by one, and the new drivers arrived, lazily preparing their whips while the old drivers argued over their payment."
}

