parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "618a5985204ed9822a342326",
  "rewritten_text": "The following day, the ghost felt exhausted. The intense stress of the past month had taken its toll, leaving his nerves frayed and his reactions jumpy. After five days of seclusion, he decided to abandon the bloodstain in the library; if the Otis family did not want it, they clearly did not deserve it. They were materialistic people, incapable of appreciating symbolic phenomena. While he could not control his astral manifestations, he felt a solemn duty to haunt the corridor weekly and moan from the oriel window twice a month. Despite his wicked past, he remained conscientious regarding his supernatural obligations. For the next three Saturdays, he patrolled the hallway between midnight and three, taking care to remain undetected. He removed his boots, stepped lightly on the creaky floorboards, wore a velvet cloak, and used the Rising Sun Lubricator to silence his chains. Though initially humiliated by the need for oil, he eventually recognized its utility. Nevertheless, the family continued to harass him. They stretched strings across the hall to trip him, and once, while dressed as Black Isaac, he slipped on a butter-slide the twins had built, suffering a painful fall. Enraged by this final insult, he decided to reclaim his dignity by confronting the boys the following night as Reckless Rupert, the Headless Earl. He had not used this disguise in seventy years, not since he had terrified Lady Barbara Modish so thoroughly that she broke her engagement to the current Lord Canterville’s grandfather and eloped with Jack Castleton, swearing she would never marry into a family that tolerated such a ghastly phantom."
}

