parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f6c8142f89ef874f2d6dbedf",
  "rewritten_text": "The shock I felt at this sudden change was hard to explain, even though the shift was natural and expected. I knew that once my balloon reached the point where the moon’s gravity became stronger than the Earth’s, this reversal would happen. I had simply woken from a deep sleep to find it had already occurred. The transition must have been smooth, as I felt no physical discomfort or disruption to my equipment during the process. Once I overcame my initial terror, I focused entirely on the moon below. It looked like a map, and despite the distance, its features were incredibly sharp. I was struck by the complete lack of oceans, lakes, or rivers. Instead, I saw vast, flat plains and countless cone-shaped volcanic mountains that looked almost man-made. While the tallest peaks were under four miles high, they resembled the volcanic regions of the Campi Phlegraei. Most were actively erupting, and I watched with growing dread as volcanic rocks shot past my balloon with alarming frequency."
}

