parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "961552928a8f74427086b1c4",
  "rewritten_text": "So you won't, won't you? Oh, you precious fool! It's naughty, isn't it, Oliver? asked Charley Bates. He'll end up hanged, won't he? I don't know what that means, Oliver replied. Something like this, old fellow, said Charley. He grabbed the end of his neckerchief, held it up, dropped his head to his shoulder, and made a sharp sound through his teeth, clearly pantomiming a hanging. That's what it means, Charley said. Look how he stares, Jack! I've never seen such great company; he'll be the death of me. Charley laughed heartily and returned to his pipe, eyes watering. You've been raised poorly, the Dodger said, admiring his polished boots. Fagin will make something of you, or you'll be his first failure. You should start now; you'll be in the trade sooner than you think, and you're just wasting time. Master Bates added his own moral advice, and then he and the Dodger described the many pleasures of their life, hinting that Oliver should win Fagin's favor immediately. And remember this, Nolly, the Dodger said as the Jew unlocked the door above, if you don't take handkerchiefs and watches— What's the point of talking like that? Charley interrupted. He doesn't know what you mean. If you don't take them, the Dodger explained, someone else will. The people who lose them will be worse off, you'll be worse off, and nobody gains anything except the thieves—and you have just as much right to them as they do. Exactly! said the Jew, who had entered unnoticed. It's all very simple, my dear; take the Dodger's word for it. He understands his trade perfectly. The old man rubbed his hands together, chuckling at his pupil's skill. The conversation ended as the Jew returned with Miss Betsy and a man Oliver had never seen, whom the Dodger called Tom Chitling, who had lingered on the stairs to flirt with the lady before entering."
}

