parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "70ca4c81ed22ca5237a70d2b",
  "rewritten_text": "He crept out from the wall with a cruel smile. The moon hid behind a cloud as he passed the large window, where his family crest and that of his murdered wife were displayed in blue and gold. He glided forward like a dark shadow, as if the night itself rejected him. He paused when he heard a sound, but it was only a dog barking at the Red Farm. He continued on, muttering old curses and waving his rusty dagger. Finally, he reached the hallway leading to Washington’s room. He stopped, the wind blowing his grey hair and twisting his burial shroud into strange shapes. When the clock struck the quarter-hour, he knew it was time. He chuckled and turned the corner, but immediately recoiled with a cry of terror, hiding his pale face in his bony hands. Standing before him was a horrific ghost, still as a statue and monstrous as a nightmare. Its head was bald, its face round and white, and its features were frozen in a permanent, hideous grin. Red light streamed from its eyes, its mouth was like a pit of fire, and it wore a shroud similar to his own. On its chest was a sign with strange, ancient writing—a list of sins or a record of crimes—and it held a gleaming steel sword in its right hand. Having never seen a ghost before, he was terrified. After one quick look, he fled back to his room, tripping over his long shroud and dropping his dagger into the Minister’s boots, where the butler found it the next morning. Once inside his room, he threw himself onto his bed and hid under the covers. Eventually, his brave spirit returned, and he decided to speak to the other ghost at daybreak. As dawn touched the hills, he went back to where he had seen the phantom, thinking that two ghosts were better than one and that his new ally might help him deal with the twins."
}

