parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1a18071829c944258d94ccf4",
  "rewritten_text": "I am going, Master Will, I truly am, the man replied promptly. I am almost at the end now. At the end? Legrand shouted. Do you mean the end of that branch? I will be there soon, master. Oh, Lord have mercy! What is this on the tree? Well, Legrand cried, delighted, what is it? It is nothing but a skull. Someone left their head up here, and the crows have picked it clean. A skull? Very well. How is it attached to the branch? I must look, master. This is a curious circumstance. There is a large nail in the skull, pinning it to the tree. Jupiter, listen to me carefully. Do you hear? Yes, master. Pay attention. Find the left eye of the skull. There is no eye left at all. Curse your stupidity! Do you know your left hand from your right? Yes, I know that. It is the hand I use to chop wood. Exactly. Your left eye is on the same side as your left hand. Can you find the left eye now? After a long pause, the man asked, Is the skull's left eye on the same side as its left hand? It does not have any hands. Never mind, I have the left eye now. What should I do with it? Drop the beetle through it as far as the string reaches, but do not let go of the string. That is done, Master Will. It was easy to put the bug through the hole. Look out below! Jupiter remained hidden, but the beetle descended, glistening like polished gold in the final rays of the setting sun. It hung clear of the branches, directly above our feet. Legrand used his scythe to clear a circular space three or four yards wide beneath the insect, then ordered Jupiter to release the string and climb down."
}

