parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ef0096521b4a9743d78a9291",
  "rewritten_text": "He explained that he was wasting his time where he was and that friends in London wanted him back to finalize his Indian business venture. He could not delay his departure any longer, but since his trip to London might soon be followed by a much longer voyage, he begged me to spend as much time with him as possible. He asked me to leave my lonely island and meet him in Perth so we could travel south together. This letter brought me back to life, and I decided to leave my island in two days. Before I left, I had a task to perform that made me shudder: I had to pack my chemical tools. This meant entering the room where I had done my hateful work and touching the equipment that sickened me. At dawn the next morning, I gathered my courage and unlocked the laboratory door. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered on the floor, and I felt as if I had mangled a living human being. I paused to compose myself before entering. With trembling hands, I moved the tools out of the room. Realizing I could not leave these remains to horrify the local peasants, I placed them in a basket with heavy stones, planning to dump them into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach cleaning my equipment. My feelings had changed completely since the night the monster appeared. Before, I had viewed my promise with gloomy despair, believing it had to be fulfilled regardless of the consequences. Now, it felt as if a veil had been lifted from my eyes and I could see clearly for the first time. The thought of resuming my work never crossed my mind. Although the monster's threat weighed on me, I no longer believed my own actions could prevent it. I decided that creating another creature like the first would be an act of pure, selfish cruelty, and I refused to consider it any further."
}

