{
  "request_id": "2fbeefd4a8ca4df1346b345a",
  "rewritten_text": "Goodbye, Uncle George. I will be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thanks for the information. I prefer to learn everything about new friends and nothing about old ones. Where are you eating, Harry? At Aunt Agatha’s. I invited myself and Mr. Gray, her latest protégé. Humph! Tell her to stop bothering me with charity appeals. I am tired of them. She thinks I exist only to write checks for her whims. I will tell her, but it won’t matter. Philanthropists lose all sense of humanity; it is their defining trait. The old man grunted in agreement and rang for his servant. Lord Henry walked toward Berkeley Square. So that was the story of Dorian Gray’s parentage. Though told crudely, it stirred him with its hint of a modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for passion, a few weeks of happiness cut short by a treacherous crime, months of agony, and a child born in pain. The mother died, leaving the boy to the tyranny of a loveless man. It was an interesting background that made the lad seem more perfect. Every exquisite thing has a tragic origin; worlds must suffer for a flower to bloom. How charming he had been at dinner, sitting opposite him with wide, wonder-filled eyes. Talking to him was like playing a violin; he responded to every touch. There was something enthralling about exerting influence. To project one’s soul into another, to hear one’s own ideas echoed back with the music of youth, to infuse one’s temperament into another like a subtle perfume—that was a true joy in an age so vulgar and limited. He was a marvelous type, this lad met by chance in Basil’s studio, or at least, he could be fashioned into one."
}

