parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3dbd2ce462812a9cbf5dc484",
  "rewritten_text": "I uncovered the fire, and a light breeze quickly fanned it into a flame. I made a fan out of branches to keep the embers alive when they began to fade. By night, I was pleased to find that the fire provided both light and warmth. It also helped with my food; I discovered that some scraps left by travelers had been roasted and tasted much better than the berries I gathered. I tried cooking my own food on the embers, finding that while it ruined the berries, it greatly improved the nuts and roots. Food eventually became scarce, and I often spent all day searching for acorns to satisfy my hunger. I decided to leave my home to find a place where my needs would be easier to meet. I deeply regretted losing the fire, as I did not know how to start it again. After thinking about it for hours, I gave up, wrapped myself in my cloak, and headed west through the woods. After three days of wandering, I reached open country. A heavy snowfall had covered the fields in white, and the cold, damp ground chilled my feet. Around seven in the morning, desperate for food and shelter, I spotted a small shepherd's hut on a hill. I examined the building with curiosity and, finding the door open, went inside. An old man sat by a fire, preparing breakfast. When he heard me, he turned, shrieked, and ran across the fields with surprising speed for his frail frame. His appearance and sudden flight surprised me, but I was delighted by the hut. It was dry and protected from the snow and rain, feeling like a perfect sanctuary to me."
}

