parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4cfaec41ec11d8084bf7b081",
  "rewritten_text": "I agree. It is for the best. Will you just hold hands and swear that we— Oh no, that will not suffice for this. That is fine for trivial matters, especially with girls, because they always go back on their word and talk if they get upset, but there ought to be a written record for something this significant. And blood. Tom was thrilled by this idea. It was profound, dark, and ominous; the time and the setting were perfectly suited to it. He picked up a clean pine shingle lying in the moonlight, took a piece of red chalk from his pocket, and carefully scrawled these lines, biting his tongue in concentration as he pressed down on the strokes. Huck Finn and Tom Sawyer swear they will keep quiet about this and they wish they may drop dead in their tracks if they ever tell and rot. Huckleberry was filled with admiration for Tom’s writing skill and the gravity of his language. He immediately took a pin from his lapel to prick his finger, but Tom stopped him. Wait! Do not do that. A pin is brass. It might have verdigris on it. What is verdigris? It is poison. That is what it is. You swallow some of that and you will see. So Tom unwound thread from one of his needles, and each boy pricked his thumb to squeeze out a drop of blood. After much effort, Tom signed his initials using his fingertip as a pen. He then showed Huckleberry how to make an H and an F, and the oath was complete. They buried the shingle near the wall with somber rituals, believing their secret was now sealed forever. A figure crept stealthily through a gap in the other end of the ruined building, but they did not notice. Tom, Huckleberry whispered, does this keep us from ever telling, always? Of course it does. It does not matter what happens, we have to stay silent. We would drop dead, do you not know that? Yes, I suppose that is so. They whispered for a while longer. Suddenly, a dog let out a long, mournful howl just ten feet away. The boys clutched each other in terror. Which one of us does he mean? Huckleberry gasped. I do not know, look through the crack. Quick! No, you do it, Tom! I cannot, I cannot do it, Huck!"
}

