parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b2acf08472680fb38f333c7e",
  "rewritten_text": "At that instant, both our gazes landed on the fragment of parchment, which I initially mistook for paper. It lay partially submerged in the sand, with one edge protruding. Nearby, I noticed the decaying remains of what seemed to be a ship’s longboat. The wreckage appeared ancient, as the structure of the timbers was barely discernible. Jupiter retrieved the parchment, used it to wrap the beetle, and handed it to me. Shortly after, we headed home and encountered Lieutenant G——. I displayed the insect, and he requested to bring it to the fort. When I agreed, he immediately tucked it into his waistcoat, leaving behind the parchment I had held throughout his examination. Perhaps he feared I might retract my offer, eager to secure his prize, given his intense passion for natural history. Unknowingly, I must have slipped the parchment into my own pocket. You recall that when I approached the table to sketch the beetle, I found no paper in its usual place. I checked the drawer and my pockets, hoping for a scrap of paper, only to discover the parchment. I recount these details precisely because the sequence of events struck me as significant. You may find me imaginative, but I had already forged a connection. I linked two pieces of a larger puzzle: a boat on the shore and a parchment—not paper—bearing a skull. You might ask where the connection lies. I contend that the skull is the quintessential pirate emblem, flown on their flags during battle. I noted the material was parchment, not paper. Parchment is remarkably durable, nearly indestructible. Trivial matters are seldom recorded on it, as it is less suited for routine writing than paper. This observation implied a deeper significance to the death’s-head. I also noted the shape of the parchment; despite a damaged corner, it was clearly oblong."
}

