parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1909ba4205944b22c49ba2f6",
  "rewritten_text": "But when I saw you loved by another, or imagined you might love someone else more deeply than you loved me, a monster awoke inside me. I sent your suitor away, and since then, I have known no peace. I could not sleep, and my blood felt constantly agitated. I woke to a new life, like someone dying in hope only to wake in Hell. I will not stain your mind by describing my inner battles, my self-hatred, and my despair. Let the feelings of a guilty father remain hidden; such agony should not be exposed. My mind was a storm of crime, remorse, and hate, yet beneath it all, I still felt the deepest love. Seeing your bitter, sympathetic sorrow is what finally pushed me to resolve to overcome my passion and return to being a proper father. I came here thinking that if I could relive the grief I felt when your mother died—memories I had suppressed for seventeen years—my love for her child would vanish. In a moment of false heroism, I decided to leave you, the light of my life, and never see you again until I could do so without guilt. But it failed; I overestimated my strength and underestimated my love. I would have died if you had not come to me. I wish I had died. Now, Mathilda, I must make my final confession. I was wrong to think I could conquer my love for you; I never can. Seeing this house and these woods where my first love lived has only made it stronger. In my madness, I told myself that your mother’s spirit lived on in you, and that you should be to me what she was. Every attempt to cast this off only makes this unnatural, guilty love cling tighter, destroying both your hopes and my life. No time or distance can remove from my soul what has become a part of it. Since arriving here, I have lived in the hell of a passion that will burn until I am cold and dead."
}

