parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "992e83964d71bb5ffca7657d",
  "rewritten_text": "It is quite remarkable that she always hears Jane better than she hears me, though Jane speaks so clearly. Still, she will find her grandmother no deafer than she was two years ago, which is impressive at her age. It has been two full years since she was last here. We have never gone so long without seeing her, and as I told Mrs. Cole, we will hardly know how to make enough of her now. Are you expecting Miss Fairfax soon? Oh yes, next week. Indeed, that must be a great pleasure. Thank you, you are very kind. Yes, next week. Everyone is surprised and says such nice things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends in Highbury as they are to see her. She will arrive Friday or Saturday, though she cannot say which because Colonel Campbell needs the carriage himself. It is so kind of them to send her the whole way, but they always do. She wrote early to tell us, as we would not have heard from her until next Tuesday or Wednesday otherwise. I imagined as much; I feared there was little chance of hearing about Miss Fairfax today. How kind of you to say so. We would not have heard if not for her upcoming visit. My mother is delighted, as she will stay with us for at least three months. She says so clearly in the letter I am about to read to you. The Campbells are going to Ireland because Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her parents to visit her immediately. They had not planned to go until summer, but she is impatient to see them. Until she married last October, she had never been away from them for more than a week, so being in different countries is very strange for her. She wrote an urgent letter, perhaps in Mr. Dixon’s name as well as her own, to press them to come over. They plan to meet in Dublin and then travel to their country estate, Bally-craig, which I imagine is a beautiful place."
}

