  "request_id": "d318603836ff7fad9aa69dd4",
  "rewritten_text": "He remained silent on the subject until their guest departed for the evening, but once alone, he addressed his daughter: Jane, I offer my congratulations. You shall be a very happy woman. Jane immediately approached him, kissed his cheek, and expressed her gratitude for his kindness. You are a good girl, he answered, and it brings me great joy to imagine you so well established. I have no doubt you will thrive together. Your dispositions are quite similar. You are both so accommodating that you will never reach a decision, so easygoing that your servants will surely deceive you, and so generous that you will constantly overspend. I hope not, she replied. Such financial recklessness would be inexcusable in me. Overspend! My dear Mr. Bennet, his wife exclaimed, what are you saying? He possesses four or five thousand a year, if not more. Turning to her daughter, she continued, Oh, my dear Jane, I am overjoyed! I am certain I will not sleep a wink tonight. I knew this would happen. I always insisted it must eventually come to pass. I was certain your beauty could not be for nothing! I recall thinking, the moment I saw him arrive in Hertfordshire last year, how likely it was that you would unite. He is the most handsome young man ever seen! Wickham and Lydia were entirely forgotten. Jane was, without question, her favorite child, and for that moment, she cared for no one else. Her younger sisters soon began soliciting her for future favors. Mary requested access to the library at Netherfield, while Kitty pleaded for several balls there each winter. From that point on, Bingley became a daily visitor at Longbourn, arriving often before breakfast and staying until after supper, unless some detestable neighbor forced him to accept a dinner invitation. Elizabeth had little time to converse with her sister, as Jane focused entirely on him while he was present. However, she proved useful to them during their brief separations. When Jane was away, he sought Elizabeth out to speak of her, and when Bingley left, Jane consistently turned to her for the same comfort. He has made me so happy, she remarked one evening, by revealing that he was completely unaware of my presence in town last spring!"
}

