parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c798ddc4fb2a305f6c000deb",
  "rewritten_text": "It is strange, yet everyone who vanishes is supposedly spotted in San Francisco. It must be a charming city, offering all the allure of the afterlife. What do you suppose became of Basil? Dorian asked, lifting his Burgundy to the light, surprised at his own calm. I have no clue. If Basil prefers to vanish, that is his affair. If he is dead, I prefer not to dwell on it. Death is the sole thing that frightens me. I loathe it. Why? the younger man asked tiredly. Because, Lord Henry replied, sniffing his vinaigrette box, one can endure anything today except that. Death and vulgarity are the only nineteenth-century realities one cannot rationalize. Let us take coffee in the music-room. You must play Chopin. The man my wife eloped with played it beautifully. Poor Victoria! I was quite fond of her. The house feels empty. Marriage is merely a habit, a poor one, yet one misses even the worst habits. Perhaps one misses them most, as they are integral to one's character. Dorian rose silently, moved to the next room, and let his fingers wander over the piano keys. Once coffee arrived, he paused and asked, Harry, have you considered that Basil might have been murdered? Lord Henry yawned. Basil was well-liked and carried a Waterbury watch. Why murder him? He lacked the wit to make enemies. He possessed artistic genius, certainly, but one can paint like Velasquez and remain utterly tedious. Basil was dull. He only intrigued me once, when he confessed his intense adoration for you and that you were the driving force of his art. I was quite fond of Basil, Dorian said, his voice tinged with sorrow. But do people not claim he was murdered? Some newspapers suggest it, though it seems unlikely. I know Paris has its dark corners, but Basil lacked the curiosity to frequent them. That was his primary flaw. What would you say, Harry, if I confessed to murdering Basil? the younger man asked."
}

