parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8d1351919924cda441ff5834",
  "rewritten_text": "By eight o’clock, I had reached an altitude of seventeen miles. My ascent was clearly accelerating, a trend that would have persisted even without my releasing ballast. Though I suffered intermittent, violent headaches and occasional nosebleeds, my condition was better than anticipated. Breathing, however, became increasingly labored, with each breath causing painful chest spasms. I prepared my condensing equipment for immediate use. The view of the earth was stunning. To the west, north, and south, a vast, calm ocean stretched out, deepening in color and showing a slight curve. Far to the east, the British Isles, the Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a sliver of North Africa were visible. Human structures had vanished; the world’s greatest cities were gone. Gibraltar appeared as a mere speck, while the Mediterranean, scattered with islands like stars in the sky, stretched eastward until it seemed to pour over the horizon’s edge, leaving me straining to hear the roar of a great waterfall. Above, the sky was pitch black, filled with brilliant stars. The pigeons were clearly suffering, so I decided to release them. I placed a gray-mottled bird on the wicker rim. It looked around anxiously, fluttering and cooing, but refused to leave. When I tossed it a few yards away, it did not descend as I expected; instead, it struggled frantically to return, crying out in a shrill, piercing voice. It managed to reach the rim, but its head immediately slumped, and it fell dead into the car."
}

