  "rewritten_text": "He was certainly entertaining, but I felt let down. When I asked about the chainmail, he just laughed and claimed it was too cold for England. Oh, look, Mr. Podgers is here! Mr. Podgers, please read the Duchess of Paisley’s palm. Duchess, take off your glove—the right one, not the left. The Duchess reluctantly unbuttoned her slightly dirty glove, protesting that it did not seem proper. Lady Windermere replied that nothing interesting ever was, then introduced them, warning Mr. Podgers not to suggest the Duchess had a larger mountain of the moon than she did. The Duchess insisted she had no such thing. Mr. Podgers examined her short, square fingers and agreed, noting her mountain of the moon was undeveloped. He praised her life line and predicted she would live to a great age and be very happy. He noted her moderate ambition and strong sense of duty. When the Duchess seemed pleased, he added that she was thrifty, which made Lady Windermere laugh. The Duchess defended her thrift, explaining that when she married, her husband had eleven castles but no livable homes. Lady Windermere quipped that he now had twelve houses and no castles. Mr. Podgers stepped in, praising the Duchess for valuing comfort and modern conveniences. Lady Windermere then asked him to read Lady Flora’s palm. A tall girl with sandy hair and sharp shoulders stepped forward, offering a bony hand. Mr. Podgers immediately identified her as a pianist, though perhaps not a true musician."
}

