  "rewritten_text": "The refined gentlemen of high status wore small hanging charms that chimed with their languid movements. These golden ornaments rang like bells, and combined with the rustle of silk, brocade, and fine linen, they created a sensory distraction that kept the starving masses of Saint Antoine at a distance. Fashion served as the primary tool for maintaining social order. Everyone appeared dressed for an endless masquerade. From the Tuileries Palace and the Court down to the legal chambers and the executioner, the mandate for elaborate attire remained absolute. Even the hangman was required to perform his duties while powdered, wearing a gold-laced coat, pumps, and white silk stockings. At the gallows, this official presided in such delicate finery. In that year of 1780, no one at the reception doubted that a system anchored by a powdered, gold-clad executioner would endure forever. After Monseigneur finished his chocolate, he opened the doors to his inner sanctum and emerged. The resulting display of submission, fawning, and servility was absolute; the guests offered such total humiliation that they left nothing for Heaven, which perhaps explains why they never sought divine intervention. Offering vague promises and smiles to his followers, Monseigneur moved through his rooms before eventually retreating back into his sanctuary, hidden away by his attendants. Once the spectacle ended, the atmosphere calmed, and the guests departed. Only one man remained, walking slowly past the mirrors with his hat tucked under his arm and a snuffbox in his hand. Stopping at the final doorway, he looked toward the sanctuary and whispered, I consign you to the Devil. He flicked the remaining snuff from his fingers as if shaking off dust and quietly walked away."
}

