  "rewritten_text": "Were our grand visions destined to vanish? We claimed a status just below the angels, yet we proved as fleeting as insects. We styled ourselves the pinnacle of creation, only to reveal our essence as mere dust. We lamented that pyramids survived their creators, yet even a humble straw hut possesses more permanence than all of humanity. How can we reconcile this decline with our former ambitions and perceived potential? Suddenly, a distinct inner voice spoke: This was ordained from eternity; the horses of Time have carried this outcome since the dawn of existence. Would you attempt to reverse the immutable laws of Necessity? Eternal, unyielding Necessity, weaver of the unbreakable chain of events, I shall not complain of your decrees. Though my mind struggles to accept that all which exists is right, I will sit among the wreckage and smile, knowing we were born not for pleasure, but for submission and hope. Will the reader grow weary if I detail our arduous trek from Paris to Geneva? Could I possibly document the relentless succession of our miseries? Patience, reader; whether you are a spiritual entity or a human descendant, you will read of this extinct race and wonder if they were as fragile as you. They were indeed, so weep for them; yet attend to this chronicle of your predecessors. The final events of our journey through France were so horrific that I dare not linger. To analyze every moment would be to recount tales so harrowing they would chill your blood. It is my duty to build this monument to the lost race for your instruction, without forcing you to endure the gruesome details of our suffering."
