  "rewritten_text": "The night was dark and stormy. No stars shone above, but heavy thunder rumbled constantly, and lightning flashed through the clouds, seemingly mocking Franklin’s scientific control. The winds roared from every direction, adding to the wild atmosphere. In that dreary moment, I longed for company, and my dear friend—my guide and comfort—appeared beside me. She moved like a vision from a dream, a queen of natural beauty. Her step was silent, and had she not touched me, I might not have noticed her. A strange sadness marked her face as she pointed to the storm outside and asked me to observe the two figures there. This nightmare filled ten pages and ended with a sermon so harsh toward non-Presbyterians that it won first prize. The village mayor praised it highly, claiming even Daniel Webster would be proud of such eloquence. Notably, many other entries relied heavily on clichés like \"beauteous\" and \"life’s page.\" Finally, the schoolmaster, feeling quite tipsy, pushed his chair aside to draw a map of America on the blackboard for his geography class. His shaky hands made a mess of it, causing the audience to giggle. He tried to fix his mistakes, but only made the map look worse, which increased the laughter. He focused intensely, determined to ignore the mockery, unaware that his efforts were only making the situation more ridiculous."
}

