  "rewritten_text": "Next, we built a wire fence. A wire fence? Yes. You suggested it yourself a few years back. I remember now—back when the Church first challenged us and decided to wait for a better opportunity. How did you set it up? I ran twelve heavy, uninsulated wires from a dynamo in the cave. The wires extend from the cave to enclose a hundred-yard circle of flat ground, forming twelve independent, concentric rings spaced ten feet apart, with their ends returning to the cave. The wires are secured to sturdy oak posts, spaced three feet apart and buried five feet deep. That is strong. The wires have no external ground connection. They run from the positive brush, while the negative brush provides the ground. Each wire returns to the cave and is grounded separately. No, that is inefficient. You only need one ground connection at the negative brush. Bring the other ends back to the cave without grounding them. Think of the savings: a cavalry charge hits the fence, and you spend no energy until the horses touch the wire. At that moment, they complete the circuit through the ground and drop dead. The power is stored like a loaded gun, costing nothing until triggered. You are right; I missed that. It is cheaper and safer if the wires break. Especially if we monitor them from the cave. What about the Gatling guns? They are set. In the center of the inner circle, on a six-foot platform, I have mounted thirteen Gatling guns with plenty of ammunition. Perfect. They cover every approach, and when the Church’s knights arrive, we will be ready. And the cliff above the cave? I have a wire fence and a Gatling gun there as well."
