  "request_id": "1e2e6cacce52e3a758e6745e",
  "rewritten_text": "Sir Arnold and Sir Gauter, knights of the castle, clashed with Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay, shattering their spears upon impact. Sir Pertolope then emerged from the castle to face Sir Lionel, whom the green knight promptly unhorsed. Heralds carefully recorded the performance of every combatant. Sir Bleobaris broke his spear against Sir Gareth but was himself thrown to the ground. Seeing this, Sir Galihodin challenged Gareth, only to be struck down as well. Sir Galihud attempted to avenge his brother, but Gareth defeated him just as he had the others, along with Sir Dinadan, La Cote Male Taile, Sir Sagramore, and Sir Dodinas, all of whom he unhorsed with a single spear. King Agwisance of Ireland watched in amazement as the knight shifted colors between green and blue with every pass, making him impossible to identify. Gareth then unhorsed King Agwisance, King Carados of Scotland, King Uriens of Gore, and Sir Bagdemagus. When Bagdemagus’s son, Meliganus, broke a spear against him, Prince Galahault challenged the multicolored knight to a duel. Gareth struck the prince’s helmet so forcefully that he nearly fell, saved only by his men. King Arthur praised the knight’s skill and asked Sir Launcelot to challenge him. Launcelot refused, stating that the knight had already endured enough labor for one day. He argued that it would be dishonorable to interrupt such a display of prowess, especially since the knight was clearly fighting for a lady’s favor. Launcelot declared that he would not deny the man his well-earned glory."
}

