parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f81e0427c635ecc0933ef608",
  "rewritten_text": "Listen! His father fell ill in Rome, and my mother, from whom he had long been separated, traveled from Paris with me to join him. She likely came only to secure his assets, as there was no love between them. He was unconscious when we arrived and died the following day. Among his desk papers were two documents addressed to you, Mr. Brownlow, marked to be delivered only after his death: a letter to Agnes and a will. The letter was a frantic, repetitive confession. He had deceived Agnes, claiming a secret mystery prevented their marriage, leading her to trust him until she was ruined and pregnant. He begged her not to curse his memory or fear their child would suffer for his sins. He recalled the locket and the ring he had given her, asking her to keep them close, writing as if he had lost his mind. The will mirrored the letter. He lamented the misery his wife had caused and the malice of you, his son, whom he claimed had been raised to hate him. He left you and your mother small annuities, while dividing the bulk of his estate equally between Agnes Fleming and their child, provided the infant survived to adulthood."
}

