parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9713a7878553a21ea8b588f4",
  "rewritten_text": "The Attorney-General told the jury that the young prisoner was a veteran in the treasonous activities that now threatened his life. He claimed this communication with the enemy was not recent, but that the prisoner had been traveling between France and England for years on secret, suspicious business. He argued that if treason could ever succeed, the man’s guilt might have remained hidden, but Providence had inspired a courageous patriot to uncover the schemes and report them to the government. This witness, he promised, would appear before them. He described this man as a noble figure who had once been the prisoner’s friend but, upon discovering his treachery, chose to sacrifice that friendship for the good of the country. While Britain did not erect statues to such heroes like the ancients did, he deserved one. The Attorney-General then spoke of the contagious nature of patriotism, noting that this witness’s example had inspired the prisoner’s own servant to search his master’s belongings and steal his private papers. He anticipated that the defense might criticize this servant, but he insisted he respected the man more than his own family and urged the jury to do the same. Finally, he stated that the testimony of these two men, along with the recovered documents, would prove the prisoner had been supplying the enemy with detailed information about British military forces and their readiness, leaving no doubt of his guilt."
}

