parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f4375a54ee96c4733b5c15e4",
  "rewritten_text": "While occupied with my studies and Mr. Pocket, I received a letter that immediately agitated me. Although I had never seen the handwriting, I instinctively recognized the sender. It lacked a formal salutation and simply stated: I am arriving in London the day after tomorrow on the midday coach. I understand you are to meet me? Miss Havisham believes so, and I write accordingly. She sends her regards. Yours, Estella. Had time permitted, I would have purchased new attire, but I had to settle for what I owned. My appetite disappeared, and I found no tranquility until the day arrived. Even then, I was more restless than before, pacing the coach-office in Wood Street long before the coach could have departed from our town. Despite knowing the futility of my actions, I felt compelled to keep the office in view. I had been waiting for half an hour when Wemmick approached me. Hello, Mr. Pip, he remarked. How are you? I would not have expected to find you here. I explained I was meeting a passenger, then asked after his home and his father. Both are doing well, especially the Aged, who is in excellent spirits and turning eighty-two soon, Wemmick replied. I am considering firing my cannon eighty-two times, provided the neighbors do not object and the weapon holds up. But this is not business. Can you guess my destination? To the office? I asked. Nearly, he said. I am heading to Newgate. We are handling a case involving a banker's parcel, and after inspecting the scene, I must consult our client. Did he commit the robbery? I inquired. Good heavens, no, Wemmick answered dryly. But he is accused of it. Just as you or I might be."
}

