parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9de5036cb2c6ddbb093ce599",
  "rewritten_text": "At that moment, we both noticed a scrap of parchment half-buried in the sand. Nearby lay the rotted remains of a ship’s longboat, so decayed that its structure was barely recognizable. Jupiter picked up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. On our way home, we met Lieutenant G——, who asked to take the insect to the fort. I agreed, and he quickly pocketed the beetle, leaving me holding the parchment. He was so eager to secure his prize for his natural history collection that he didn't notice I kept the scrap, which I absentmindedly tucked into my own pocket. Later, when I sat down to sketch the beetle, I couldn't find any paper. While searching my pockets for a scrap to use, I discovered the parchment. I recall these details clearly because they struck me as significant. You might think me imaginative, but I had begun to connect the pieces. A boat on the shore and a parchment marked with a skull—the universal symbol of pirates—seemed linked. I noted that the material was durable parchment rather than paper, which is rarely used for unimportant notes. This suggested the skull had a specific, hidden meaning. Although one corner was torn, the original shape of the parchment was clearly oblong."
}

