parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ca2562b94289016f2f95ae52",
  "rewritten_text": "After settling our friend into his new office, we headed toward Windsor. Because it was so close to London, leaving Raymond and Perdita did not feel like a painful separation. We said our goodbyes at the Protectoral Palace. It was charming to watch my sister embrace her new role, trying to carry herself with appropriate dignity. Her inner pride and humble manner were constantly at odds. Her shyness was genuine, stemming from a fear of being misunderstood and a sensitivity to the world's indifference, much like Raymond. However, Perdita was more considerate of others; her bashfulness came from a desire to ensure those around her did not feel inferior, a thought that never occurred to her. While Idris would have been more naturally suited to formal ceremonies due to her upbringing, she found such routines tedious. In contrast, Perdita clearly enjoyed her new position. She was so occupied with new ideas that she felt little sadness when we left. She said an affectionate goodbye and promised to visit, but she did not mind our departure. Raymond was in high spirits, overwhelmed by his new authority. His mind was full of plans, though he had not settled on any yet. He vowed to his friends and the world that his time as Protector would be marked by some act of great glory. We discussed them as we returned to Windsor Castle with a smaller group. We were relieved to escape the political chaos and returned to our quiet life with renewed appreciation. We kept busy, and I focused my energy on intellectual pursuits; hard study proved to be the perfect remedy for the restless spirit that would have otherwise plagued me. Perdita allowed us to take Clara back to Windsor, and she, along with my two children, provided constant joy. The only thing that troubled us was Adrian's health. He was clearly fading, though he showed no specific symptoms beyond bright eyes, an animated expression, and flushed cheeks, which made us fear consumption. Yet, he felt no pain or fear. He turned to his books with passion and spent his rest periods in the company he loved most: his sister and me."
}

