parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6d7d455d582e8d8459c6c6d3",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Darcy spoke warmly of his sister’s musical skill. I am pleased to hear it, Lady Catherine said, but tell her she cannot expect to excel without frequent practice. I assure you, he replied, she does not need that advice; she practices constantly. So much the better, she continued. I will remind her of it when I write. I often tell young ladies that musical excellence requires constant effort. I have told Miss Bennet repeatedly that she will never play well without more practice. Although Mrs. Collins lacks an instrument, I have often invited her to use the piano in Mrs. Jenkinson’s room at Rosings. She would not be in anyone’s way there. Mr. Darcy, embarrassed by his aunt’s rudeness, remained silent. After coffee, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth of her promise to play, and she sat at the instrument. He pulled a chair close. Lady Catherine listened briefly before resuming her conversation with her other nephew, until he walked away and stood where he could watch Elizabeth play. Noticing him, she paused and said with a playful smile, You intend to intimidate me, Mr. Darcy, by listening so intently, but I will not be frightened, even if your sister plays so well. I am too stubborn to be intimidated; my courage only grows when someone tries to scare me. I will not say you are mistaken, he replied, because you know I have no such intention. I have known you long enough to realize you enjoy expressing opinions you do not actually hold. Elizabeth laughed and told Colonel Fitzwilliam, Your cousin is giving you a poor impression of me and teaching you to doubt my words. It is unfortunate to meet someone so capable of exposing my true character in a place where I hoped to be well-regarded."
}

