parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "36f7a5f87c94aa7d8a707470",
  "rewritten_text": "I would wake up eventually and look for the cause of a noise, perhaps seeing a steamboat chugging upstream so far away that I could only tell if it had a stern or side wheel. Then, for an hour, there would be nothing to see or hear, just pure solitude. Later, I might see a raft drifting in the distance with someone chopping wood. I would see the axe flash and fall, but hear nothing until the axe was raised again, when the sound finally reached us across the water. We spent our days lounging and listening to the silence. Once, a thick fog rolled in, and the passing rafts banged on tin pans to warn off steamboats. A boat passed so close we could hear the crew talking, swearing, and laughing, but we could see nothing. It felt eerie, like ghosts floating in the air. Jim thought they were spirits, but I told him spirits would not complain about the fog. When night fell, we pushed off, letting the current carry us while we smoked our pipes and dangled our legs in the water. We stayed naked day and night whenever the mosquitoes allowed, as I never cared much for clothes. Sometimes we had the river entirely to ourselves. We would watch the distant banks and islands, spotting a candle flickering in a cabin window or a light on a passing raft, sometimes hearing a fiddle or a song drift over the water. Life on a raft is lovely. We would lie on our backs, staring at the star-speckled sky, and argue about whether the stars were created or just happened to exist. Jim believed they were made, but I argued they just happened, as it would have taken too long to create so many."
}

