  "rewritten_text": "The Attorney-General told the jury that the defendant, despite his youth, was a veteran of treasonous activities punishable by death. He argued that this collaboration with the enemy was not a recent development, but part of a long-standing pattern of suspicious travel between France and England. Had these crimes been successful, they might have remained hidden, but Providence led an irreproachable patriot to uncover the plot and report it to the government. This witness, once the defendant’s friend, had courageously chosen to sacrifice their bond for the sake of the nation. While Britain did not erect statues to such heroes as the ancients did, the witness remained a shining example of virtue. This patriotism had even inspired the defendant’s own servant to search his master’s belongings and seize incriminating documents. The Attorney-General anticipated criticism of this servant, yet he personally held the man in higher regard than his own family, urging the jury to do the same. He concluded that the testimony of these two witnesses, supported by the recovered documents, would prove the defendant had systematically provided the enemy with sensitive intelligence regarding British military forces."
}

