parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a27c5b9a7136b728a8d33c91",
  "rewritten_text": "He explained that while traveling from his home through Datchet, he noticed smoke rising from the inn's chimney. Expecting to find fellow travelers, he knocked and was let in. Only Lucy and her mother were there; the mother was paralyzed by rheumatism, and as the other local residents had all departed, they were left behind. Lucy begged the man to stay until her mother recovered enough to travel, warning that they would surely die if abandoned. The man refused, insisting he had to rejoin his wife and children who had already left. As a final hope, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris. He delivered it, and Idris read the following with great distress: Honoured Lady, I hope you will remember and pity me. I am writing in a state of confusion. A month ago, my mother lost the use of her limbs. She is improving, and I am certain she could travel within a month if you could help us as you promised. However, everyone else has left. I tried to ask Samuel Woods to wait for us, but his home was already empty. I have seen no one until this man arrived. What will become of us? My mother is too ill to know our true situation. Please send someone to help. If I move her now, she will die, and even if she recovers, I do not know how we could travel so many miles to the sea. By then, you will all be in France, separated from us by the terrifying ocean."
}

