parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "44d5c4495323bc3807d40fc0",
  "rewritten_text": "“Drat my hat! I need Virginia!” the young Duke exclaimed with a laugh, and they raced toward the train station. There, Mr. Otis asked the station-master if anyone matching Virginia’s description had been spotted, but he learned nothing. The official promised to send inquiries along the tracks and keep a sharp lookout. After purchasing a hat for the Duke from a shopkeeper closing for the night, Mr. Otis rode to Bexley, a nearby village known for gypsy encampments on the adjacent common. They woke the local constable, but he offered no leads. After scouring the common, they returned to the Chase by eleven, exhausted and devastated. Washington and the twins met them at the gate-house with lanterns, as the path was pitch black. No sign of Virginia had surfaced. The gypsies had been located in Brockley meadows, but she was not among them; they claimed they had simply miscalculated the date of Chorton Fair and departed in haste. They were genuinely upset by the news, as they appreciated Mr. Otis letting them camp on his land, and four of them remained to assist in the search. The carp-pond had been searched and the entire estate combed, yet nothing was found. It was clear that Virginia was gone for the night, and Mr. Otis and the boys returned to the house in profound despair, followed by the groom with the horses. Inside, they found the staff terrified, and Mrs. Otis lay on the library sofa, nearly hysterical with worry, while the housekeeper tended to her. Mr. Otis insisted she eat and ordered supper for everyone. It was a somber affair; few words were spoken, and even the twins were uncharacteristically quiet, as they adored their sister. Once finished, Mr. Otis ignored the Duke’s pleas and sent everyone to bed, declaring that nothing more could be achieved until morning, when he would summon detectives from Scotland Yard."
}

