parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ef2d73bbd9b2d8408a8e04cb",
  "rewritten_text": "Lay down your weapons, brothers! Forgiveness, aid, and affection await your change of heart. You are precious to us, for you share our fragile human form; every one of you shall find a friend among these ranks. Why should man war against man, while the plague, our common enemy, looms above us, thriving on our slaughter, more merciless than the disease itself? Both armies hesitated. Our soldiers gripped their weapons tightly, eyeing the enemy with grim resolve. The opposing troops had not discarded their arms, driven more by fear than a desire to fight; they glanced at one another, seeking a lead that never came. Adrian dismounted and approached a fallen soldier. He was a man, he shouted, and now he is gone. Quickly, dress the wounds of the fallen; let no one perish. Do not let another soul depart through your cruel cuts to recount this fratricide before God. Bind their wounds and return them to their kin. Cast aside the savage hearts within you; discard those instruments of malice. In this moment of shared fate, let every man be a brother and protector to his neighbor. Abandon those bloodied weapons and hurry to tend these injuries. As he spoke, he knelt and lifted a man from whom life was rapidly draining. The poor soul gasped, and in the sudden silence of both armies, his moans were clearly audible. Every heart, recently set on total destruction, now beat with anxious hope for this single life. Adrian tore away his scarf to bind the wound, but it was futile. The man sighed deeply, his head slumped, and his body went limp. He is dead, Adrian declared, bowing his head in grief. The destiny of the world felt tied to this one death. On both sides, the soldiers dropped their weapons. Even the hardened veterans wept, and our men reached out to their former foes, a wave of profound affection washing over them. The two armies merged, unarmed and united, discussing only how to aid one another. Repenting for their past violence and cruelty, they followed the General’s command to march toward London."
}

