parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5d8473e6065b3cb868277d23",
  "rewritten_text": "In a certain town—which I shall leave unnamed for discretion—there stands a building common to most municipalities: a workhouse. Within these walls, on a date I need not specify as it holds no immediate relevance, was born the individual whose name titles this chapter. For some time after the parish surgeon brought him into this world of woe, it was uncertain if the infant would survive long enough to be named. Had he perished, these memoirs would either never exist or would have been remarkably brief, serving as a uniquely concise biography. While being born in a workhouse is hardly an enviable start, it proved the best possible outcome for Oliver Twist. He struggled initially to draw breath, a tedious but necessary habit, lying on a mattress while teetering between life and death. Had he been surrounded by doting relatives and expert physicians, he surely would have been killed by their interference. Instead, with only a tipsy pauper and a contract surgeon present, Oliver and Nature settled the matter themselves. After a few efforts, he breathed, sneezed, and announced his arrival to the workhouse by crying as loudly as any infant could after only three minutes of life."
}

