parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e0dc5504f118648fca71491d",
  "rewritten_text": "We finally lightened the box by removing two-thirds of its contents, allowing us to lift it from the hole with some effort. We hid the removed items in the brambles, leaving the dog to guard them with Jupiter’s strict orders not to move or bark until we returned. We hurried home with the chest, reaching the hut safely but exhausted at one in the morning. Too tired to continue, we rested until two, ate, and then headed back to the hills with three sturdy sacks we were lucky to find. Shortly before four, we reached the pit, divided the remaining loot as equally as possible, and returned to the hut with our golden burden just as dawn broke over the treetops. We were completely spent, yet the intense excitement kept us from sleeping. After a restless three or four hours, we rose by mutual agreement to examine our treasure. The chest had been filled to the brim, and we spent the entire day and most of the next night sorting through it. Everything had been tossed in without order. Once organized, we realized our wealth was even greater than expected. We estimated the gold coins to be worth over four hundred and fifty thousand dollars based on current exchange rates. There was no silver, only antique gold from France, Spain, Germany, and a few English guineas, along with some unfamiliar tokens. Several heavy, worn coins were impossible to identify, and there was no American currency. The jewels were harder to value. We found one hundred and ten large, fine diamonds, eighteen brilliant rubies, three hundred and ten beautiful emeralds, twenty-one sapphires, and an opal. These stones had been pried from their settings, which we found hammered flat, likely to prevent them from being identified."
}

