  "request_id": "692942cfff02b66543abacbc",
  "rewritten_text": "What is it? Ten o'clock, sir. What do you mean? Ten at night? Yes, sir. You asked me to wake you. Right, I remember. After failing to fall back asleep while his servant noisily stirred the fire, he rose, grabbed his hat, and left. He walked to the Temple, paced the pavement twice to clear his head, and headed for Stryver’s chambers. Stryver’s clerk had already left, so the man himself opened the door. He wore slippers and a loose robe, his collar open for comfort. He had the strained, bloodshot eyes typical of men who live as hard as he did, a look common to his type throughout history. You are a bit late, Memory, Stryver said. About the usual time, maybe fifteen minutes past. They entered a dim, book-lined room cluttered with papers and warmed by a roaring fire. A kettle steamed on the hearth, and a table was set with wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons. I see you have finished your bottle, Sydney. Two tonight, I think. I was dining with today’s client, or watching him dine—it makes no difference. That was a brilliant point you made regarding the identification. How did you think of it? I just thought he was a handsome guy and that I would have been just like him if I had any luck. Stryver laughed, his belly shaking. You and your luck, Sydney! Get to work. Looking sullen, the jackal loosened his clothes, fetched a jug of cold water, a basin, and some towels from the next room. He soaked the towels, wrung them out, wrapped them around his head, and sat down. Now I am ready. Not much to finish tonight, Memory, Stryver said cheerfully, sorting through his papers. How much? Only two cases. Give me the hardest one first. There you go, Sydney. Get started. Stryver reclined on a sofa by the table, while Sydney sat at his own desk, surrounded by papers, bottles, and glasses."
}

