parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d7d6479454d11687ea89e2e7",
  "rewritten_text": "Perdita’s name went unspoken, yet I knew his indecision stemmed from his love for her. Who was more worthy of his affection than my noble sister? Who better deserved this self-important leader than she, whose regal presence commanded respect and who loved him in return, even as disappointment dampened her feelings and ambition warred with his own? We arrived at the House that evening. Raymond, aware that his future would be decided during the debate, remained lighthearted. A roar like a swarm of bees greeted us in the coffee-room, where anxious politicians gathered in clusters. The aristocratic faction, England’s wealthiest men, seemed calm, as the debate would proceed without their direct interference. Near the fire stood Ryland and his allies. Ryland, a man of humble origins who had amassed a fortune from his father’s manufacturing business, had spent his life championing the democratic shifts that followed the king’s abdication. While he initially welcomed Lord Raymond’s influence as a way to weaken his rivals, the situation had escalated. The lesser nobility, eager to regain their lost power, embraced the return of monarchy. Though some remained steadfast, the term republic had lost its appeal, and many longed for the pageantry of a crown. Ryland, finally moved to action, declared that his patience had ended and he would soon dismantle this royalist movement. When Raymond entered the room, his supporters cheered, gathering around him to tally their votes and discuss the new members who had yet to declare their allegiance."
}

