{
  "request_id": "1108d551d07bf847a9ace911",
  "rewritten_text": "For a moment, I was too stunned to think clearly. I knew my own drawing was quite different in detail, even if the general shape was similar. I took a candle to the other side of the room to examine the parchment more closely. When I flipped it over, I saw my own sketch on the back, exactly as I had drawn it. At first, I was simply amazed by the strange coincidence that a skull, matching my beetle in size and shape, happened to be on the other side of the paper without my knowledge. Such coincidences often leave the mind paralyzed as it struggles to find a logical connection. However, as I recovered, a far more startling realization dawned on me. I clearly remembered that the parchment had been blank when I drew the beetle. I was certain of this because I had checked both sides to find a clean spot. If the skull had been there, I would have noticed it. This mystery felt impossible to explain, yet a faint idea began to form in my mind—a glimmer of the truth that last night’s events would eventually prove. I stood up, put the parchment away, and stopped thinking about it until I could be alone. After you left and Jupiter was asleep, I began a more organized investigation. First, I considered how I had found the parchment. We discovered the beetle on the mainland coast, about a mile east of the island, just above the high-tide mark. When I picked it up, it bit me, causing me to drop it. Jupiter, always cautious, looked for a leaf to grab the insect after it flew toward him."
}

