parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8bf6004d954302973830a7a4",
  "rewritten_text": "Their arrival sparked a sudden tension in Saint Antoine, visible in the faces watching from every doorway. No one followed them, and the wine-shop fell silent as they entered, though every gaze fixed upon them. Monsieur Defarge greeted the room, prompting a collective, hesitant response. When Defarge remarked on the poor weather, the men exchanged uneasy glances and looked away, save for one who promptly left. Defarge then introduced his companion, a road-mender named Jacques, to his wife, asking her to serve him wine. A second man departed. The road-mender drank and ate his coarse bread near the counter, while a third man left the shop. Defarge drank sparingly, waiting for the stranger to finish. Neither he nor his wife, who had returned to her knitting, acknowledged the others. Once the man finished, Defarge led him through the streets, across a courtyard, and up a steep staircase to a small attic room. This was the same place where an old man had once sat making shoes. The old man was gone, but the three men who had left the wine-shop were waiting inside. Defarge shut the door and spoke quietly, introducing the road-mender to the others as the witness he had been sent to find."
}

