parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8a9239f7ea3a35266eedaaa6",
  "rewritten_text": "We could certainly build a thrilling superstition around this. I suppose you will name the bug scarabaeus caput hominis, or something similar, as there are many such names in natural history books. But where are the antennae you mentioned? The antennae! Legrand replied, sounding strangely agitated. I am certain you must see them. I drew them as clearly as they appear on the actual insect, which should be enough. Well, perhaps so, I said, but I still do not see them. I handed him the paper without further comment, not wanting to upset him. I was surprised by his reaction; his bad mood puzzled me, and the drawing truly lacked any visible antennae, looking much like a standard picture of a death's-head moth. He took the paper irritably and was about to crumple it into the fire when a quick look at the design caught his attention. His face turned bright red, then deathly pale. He studied the drawing closely for several minutes. Finally, he stood up, took a candle, and sat on a chest in the corner to examine the paper from every angle. He remained silent, and his behavior shocked me, but I decided not to provoke him further. He soon placed the paper in his wallet and locked it in his desk. He calmed down, but his earlier excitement had vanished. He seemed less angry than distracted, lost in deep thought for the rest of the evening. I had planned to stay the night, but given his mood, I decided to leave. He did not ask me to stay, but he shook my hand with extra warmth as I left. About a month later, while I was in Charleston, his servant Jupiter visited me. I had never seen the old man look so discouraged, and I feared something terrible had happened to my friend."
}

