  "rewritten_text": "By eight o'clock, I had reached seventeen miles above the ground. It was clear my ascent was accelerating, even without the ballast I had released. Though I suffered intermittent, sharp pains in my head and ears and occasional nosebleeds, my condition was better than anticipated. Breathing grew increasingly labored, however, with each breath causing a painful spasm in my chest. I prepared my condensing equipment for use. The earth below looked magnificent. To the west, north, and south stretched an endless, calm ocean, deepening in blue and showing signs of curvature. Far to the east, I could clearly see Great Britain, the Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a sliver of Africa. Human structures were invisible; the world's great cities had vanished. Gibraltar was a mere speck, and the Mediterranean, speckled with islands like a starry sky, reached eastward until it seemed to spill over the horizon's edge, making me strain to hear the roar of a waterfall. Above, the sky was pitch black, filled with bright stars. Seeing the pigeons in distress, I decided to release them. I placed a gray-mottled bird on the wicker rim. It looked around nervously, fluttering and cooing, but refused to leave the car. When I tossed it a few yards away, it did not descend as I had hoped. Instead, it fought desperately to return, crying out shrilly. It managed to land back on the rim, but its head immediately slumped, and it died inside the basket."
}

