  "rewritten_text": "We visited your home, the first man said, but missed you. Hearing you were walking this way, we followed, hoping to catch you. Please, get into the carriage. Their manner was commanding, and they positioned themselves to block my path to the door. They were armed; I was not. Gentlemen, I replied, forgive me, but I usually ask who is seeking my help and the nature of the case. The second man answered, Doctor, your clients are people of status. As for the case, we trust your skill to diagnose it better than we could describe it. That is enough. Get in. I had no choice but to comply, entering in silence. They followed, the last one jumping in after closing the steps. The carriage turned and sped off. I am recounting this conversation exactly as it happened, word for word. I am describing events precisely, keeping my mind focused. Where I leave gaps, I am pausing to hide my notes. The carriage left the city, passed the North Barrier, and reached the country road. About two miles later—a distance I measured later—it turned off the main road and stopped at a secluded house. We all got out and walked along a damp garden path past an overflowing, neglected fountain to the door. When the bell was answered, one of my escorts struck the man who opened it across the face with his heavy glove. I thought little of it, as I had seen commoners treated worse than dogs. However, the other man grew angry and struck him as well. Seeing their identical expressions and movements, I realized they were twin brothers. From the moment we entered the locked gate, I had heard cries coming from an upstairs room."
}

