  "rewritten_text": "Farewell, Uncle George. I must depart or I shall miss lunch. Thank you for the details I requested; I prefer learning about new acquaintances while ignoring the old. Where are you dining, Harry? At Aunt Agatha’s. I invited myself and Mr. Gray, her current favorite. Tell her to stop pestering me with her charitable requests, Harry. I am tired of her assuming I exist only to fund her whims. I will tell her, Uncle, though it will be futile. Philanthropists lack all humanity; it is their defining trait. The old man grunted in agreement and summoned his servant. Lord Henry walked through the arcade toward Berkeley Square. So that was the history of Dorian Gray’s birth. Despite the blunt delivery, the tale of a strange, modern romance intrigued him. A beautiful woman sacrificing all for a reckless passion, followed by brief happiness ended by a treacherous crime. Then, months of silent suffering, a painful birth, the mother’s death, and a boy left to the cold rule of a loveless guardian. It was a compelling history that framed the youth perfectly. Every beautiful thing requires a tragic foundation; the world must suffer for a flower to bloom. How charming he had been the previous evening, his eyes wide and lips parted in delight as he sat across from him, the candlelight deepening the wonder on his face. Conversing with him felt like playing a fine violin, responsive to every movement. The power of influence was deeply captivating. To project one’s soul into another, to hear one’s own thoughts returned with the music of youth, to infuse one’s temperament into another like a delicate perfume—this was a profound joy in an era so vulgar and limited. This lad, discovered by chance in Basil’s studio, was a marvelous specimen, or at least, one that could be molded into such."
}

