  "rewritten_text": "Furthermore, this knot is a specialty of Maltese sailors. I found the ribbon near the lightning rod, and it could not have belonged to the victims. If my deduction that the Frenchman is a sailor from a Maltese ship is wrong, the advertisement does no harm; he will simply assume I was misled. However, if I am correct, I have gained a significant advantage. Even if innocent of the murder, the Frenchman will hesitate to claim his orangutan. He will reason that he is poor and the animal is a fortune, so why lose it to fear? He will tell himself the beast was found far from the crime scene, and it is impossible to prove he is implicated. He will conclude that since the advertiser knows he owns the animal, avoiding it might draw more suspicion. He will decide to claim the creature and keep it hidden until the investigation ends. Just then, we heard footsteps on the stairs. Be ready with your pistols, Dupin whispered, but do not show them until I signal. The visitor had entered the open front door and started up the stairs, then hesitated and began to descend. As Dupin moved toward the door, the man turned and climbed back up, finally knocking on our chamber door. Come in, Dupin said cheerfully. A man entered. He was clearly a sailor—tall, muscular, and possessing a daring, not entirely unpleasant, expression."
}

