  "rewritten_text": "I consider it one of the primary refinements of civilized society. Certainly, sir; and it has the benefit of being popular among the less civilized as well, for every savage can dance. Sir William merely smiled. Your friend performs beautifully, he continued after a pause, watching Bingley join the group; and I have no doubt you are an expert yourself, Mr. Darcy. You saw me dance at Meryton, I believe, sir. Yes, indeed, and I took great pleasure in it. Do you often dance at St. James’s? Never, sir. Do you not think it would be a fitting tribute to the location? It is a tribute I never pay to any place if I can avoid it. You have a home in the city, I assume? Mr. Darcy bowed. I once considered settling in town myself, as I enjoy high society, but I was not certain the London air would suit Lady Lucas. He paused, hoping for a reply, but his companion remained silent. As Elizabeth moved toward them, Sir William decided on a gallant gesture and called out, My dear Miss Eliza, why are you not dancing? Mr. Darcy, you must allow me to introduce this young lady as a wonderful partner. You cannot refuse to dance when such beauty stands before you. He reached for her hand to offer it to Mr. Darcy, who was surprised but not unwilling to accept. Elizabeth immediately pulled back, telling Sir William with some agitation, Truly, sir, I have no intention of dancing. Please do not think I came this way to solicit a partner. Mr. Darcy formally requested the honor of her hand, but to no avail. Elizabeth remained firm, and Sir William’s attempts at persuasion failed. You excel so much at dancing, Miss Eliza, that it is cruel to deny me the pleasure of watching you. Even if this gentleman dislikes the activity, he surely cannot object to obliging us for half an hour. Mr. Darcy is very polite, Elizabeth said with a smile. He is indeed, but given the incentive, we cannot be surprised by his willingness; who would object to such a partner? Elizabeth gave a playful look and turned away. Her refusal had not lowered her in the gentleman’s estimation, and he was thinking of her with some satisfaction when Miss Bingley approached him."
}

