parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dc7b6f317fe238daef0d371b",
  "rewritten_text": "He shivered and returned, shutting the window. At the door, he turned the key and opened it, avoiding any look at the corpse. He felt the key to his survival was to ignore the reality of the situation. The friend who had painted the portrait that caused his misery was gone; that was enough. He then remembered the Moorish lamp, made of silver and steel with turquoise inlays. Fearing his servant might notice it missing, he hesitated, then returned to retrieve it from the table. He could not avoid seeing the body, which lay still with horribly white hands, resembling a grotesque wax figure. After locking the door, he crept downstairs. The floorboards creaked, sounding like cries of pain, but he paused and realized it was only his own footsteps. In the library, he found the bag and coat. He hid them in a secret wall compartment where he kept his disguises, planning to burn them later. Checking his watch, he saw it was twenty minutes to two. He sat to reflect. Men were often executed for such crimes, but what evidence existed against him? Basil Hallward had left at eleven, and no one had seen him return. Most servants were away, and his valet was asleep. Basil had intended to take the midnight train to Paris; his reserved nature meant it would be months before anyone grew suspicious. By then, all evidence would be gone. A sudden idea occurred to him. He put on his coat and hat and went to the hall. Hearing a policeman’s heavy footsteps and seeing a flashlight beam on the window, he held his breath. Moments later, he slipped out, closed the door quietly, and began ringing the bell."
}

