parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "af5936dc40714b2e0cfdd686",
  "rewritten_text": "Watching him in these moods, I often pondered the old theory of the dual soul, imagining a double Dupin—one creative and one analytical. Do not mistake this for a mystery or a work of fiction. What I observed in the Frenchman was simply the product of an intense, perhaps even strained, intellect. An example of his comments during these times will best illustrate my point. We were walking one night down a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. Both lost in thought, we had not spoken for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly, Dupin remarked, \"He is a very small man, it is true, and would be better suited for the Théâtre des Variétés.\" \"There is no doubt of that,\" I replied instinctively, not immediately realizing—so deep was my concentration—how perfectly he had aligned with my own thoughts. A moment later, I caught myself, and my astonishment was profound. \"Dupin,\" I said seriously, \"this is beyond my understanding. I am amazed and can hardly believe my senses. How could you possibly know I was thinking of—?\" I paused, wanting to confirm if he truly knew whom I meant. \"—of Chantilly,\" he said. \"Why do you hesitate? You were thinking that his small stature made him unfit for tragedy.\" This was exactly what I had been considering. Chantilly was a former cobbler from the Rue St. Denis who, obsessed with acting, had attempted the role of Xerxes in Crébillon’s play and was famously mocked for it. \"For heaven's sake, tell me the method—if there is one—by which you read my mind,\" I exclaimed, more startled than I cared to admit. \"It was the fruit seller,\" my friend replied, \"who led you to conclude that the cobbler was not tall enough for Xerxes and his like.\" \"The fruit seller! You surprise me; I do not know any fruit seller.\" \"The man who bumped into you as we entered the street—perhaps fifteen minutes ago.\" I then recalled that a fruit seller carrying a large basket of apples had accidentally nearly knocked me over as we turned onto the street, but I could not fathom what that had to do with Chantilly."
}

