parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8aef23b519b95201ebee2bb3",
  "rewritten_text": "I walked beside a burly man with a cheerful face, hoping to strike up a conversation and learn more, but he immediately began telling that same tired anecdote Sir Dinadan had shared when I first clashed with Sir Sagramor. I excused myself and fell to the back of the group, feeling disheartened and weary of this life of struggle and defeat, yet fearing the finality of death and the prospect of encountering that same story in eternity. By early afternoon, we caught up to another group of pilgrims, but this one was devoid of any joy or laughter. It included people of all ages, from the elderly to infants, yet every face was marked by the hollow hopelessness born of long-term suffering. They were slaves. Shackles bound their hands and feet to waist belts, and the adults were chained together by their collars. They had walked three hundred miles in eighteen days on meager rations, sleeping huddled together like animals. They wore only rags, and their irons had caused festering, maggot-ridden sores on their ankles. Their feet were raw, and every one of them limped. Originally a group of one hundred, half had been sold along the way. The trader in charge rode a horse, carrying a heavy, multi-tailed whip. He never spoke, using the lash to strike the shoulders of anyone who stumbled from exhaustion, forcing them back into line."
}

