parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "709f84e2a74e343cd5efd9ef",
  "rewritten_text": "Must I be borne to heaven on beds of ease, while others struggle for the prize and navigate crimson seas? He was esteemed as an exceptional reader. At church gatherings, he was frequently asked to recite verse; upon finishing, the women would raise their hands, let them drop in their laps, roll their eyes, and shake their heads, signaling that the beauty was beyond mortal expression. Following the hymn, Reverend Sprague acted as a bulletin board, announcing meetings and society events until the list seemed endless—a strange practice that persists in America despite the prevalence of newspapers. Often, the more baseless a tradition, the more difficult it is to abandon. Then, the minister offered a comprehensive prayer. It was a generous petition, covering the church, the village, the county, the State, the nation, the government, sailors at sea, the oppressed under foreign tyrannies, the spiritually blind, and the distant heathen, concluding with a plea that his sermon might bear fruit. Amen. As the congregation sat, the boy whose story this is merely endured the prayer. He was restless, tracking the familiar route of the minister’s petition and resenting any new additions as unfair. During the prayer, a fly landed on the pew ahead, tormenting him by grooming itself with such tranquil, vigorous precision—polishing its head and smoothing its wings like coat-tails—that it seemed entirely indifferent to its surroundings."
}

