parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8e57e63961071f0fb423a615",
  "rewritten_text": "Is that all? That is not much. Many are in the Republic’s bad graces and must face the guillotine. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which one is he? I am he. Naturally, as I am the last. Jarvis Lorry answers these questions while standing by the coach door, addressing a group of officials. They slowly inspect the carriage and its luggage, while local onlookers crowd around to stare. A mother holds her child’s arm out so the infant can touch the wife of an aristocrat destined for execution. Here are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, properly signed. May I leave, citizen? You may. Move on, postilions! Have a safe trip! I salute you, citizens. The first danger is behind us! Jarvis Lorry says this while clasping his hands and looking up. Inside the carriage, there is terror, weeping, and the heavy breathing of the unconscious passenger. Are we moving too slowly? Can they be made to go faster? Lucie asks, clinging to the old man. It would look like we are fleeing, my dear. I cannot push them too hard; it would raise suspicion. Look back and see if we are being followed! The road is clear, my dear. We are not being pursued yet. We pass scattered houses, farms, ruins, and leafless trees. The carriage rattles over hard, uneven pavement, flanked by deep mud. Sometimes we veer into the mud to avoid the jarring stones, or get stuck in ruts. Our impatience is agonizing; in our panic, we want to jump out and run, or hide, anything but stop. We pass more ruins, farms, and trees. Have they tricked us and brought us back the same way? Is this the same place? Thank God, no. A village. Look back to see if we are followed! Quiet! The posting-house. Our four horses are unhitched slowly. The coach sits in the street, motionless. New horses appear one by one, and new postilions follow, fiddling with their whips, while the old ones count their money, miscalculate, and grumble."
}

