parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eb980cf9cb9fd2a5fdfac1a2",
  "rewritten_text": "It was marvelous even to read about the opulence of the deceased. He then shifted his focus to embroideries and the tapestries that served as frescoes in the cold chambers of northern Europe. As he studied these—possessing a unique talent for total immersion in his current interest—he felt a melancholy regarding the decay time inflicts upon exquisite objects. He, however, had avoided such a fate. Seasons passed, flowers withered, and nights of dread recounted their disgrace, yet he remained unaltered. No winter touched his youthful complexion. How starkly this contrasted with physical items. Where had they vanished? Where was the saffron robe depicting the gods battling giants, crafted by dark-skinned maidens for Athena? Where was the massive velarium Nero spanned over the Colosseum, that purple sail showing the starry heavens and Apollo’s chariot? He yearned to behold the intricate napkins of the Priest of the Sun, featuring every imaginable feast delicacy; King Chilperic’s burial shroud with its three hundred golden bees; the eccentric garments that angered the Bishop of Pontus, adorned with beasts and hunters; or the coat of Charles of Orleans, its sleeves embroidered with a song’s lyrics and musical notes fashioned from pearls. He learned of Queen Joan of Burgundy’s Rheims palace chamber, decorated with over a thousand golden parrots and hundreds of butterflies bearing royal crests. Catherine de Medicis owned a mourning-bed of black velvet covered in crescents and suns, draped in damask and pearl-fringed embroidery, set within a room hung with her emblems. Louis XIV featured fifteen-foot-tall gold-embroidered caryatids in his quarters."
}

