  "rewritten_text": "They traveled past desolate brickyards. The mist thinned, revealing odd, bottle-shaped kilns glowing with orange flames. A dog bayed, and a distant gull cried out in the gloom. The horse tripped in a rut, then veered and began to gallop. Eventually, they left the dirt track for uneven cobblestones. Most windows remained dark, though occasionally strange silhouettes danced against illuminated blinds. He observed them with interest, finding their puppet-like movements repulsive. A simmering anger filled him. As they rounded a bend, a woman shouted from a doorway, and two men pursued the carriage briefly until the driver lashed out with his whip. Passion often traps the mind in repetitive cycles. Dorian Gray obsessively turned over the same phrases regarding soul and sense, seeking to justify his turbulent emotions through intellectualization. His desire to live intensified, sharpening his nerves. He began to cherish the ugliness he once despised, finding it more authentic than art or music. Such raw, chaotic reality offered the oblivion he craved. In three days, he would be liberated. The carriage stopped abruptly at the mouth of a dark alley. Beyond the jagged rooftops, ship masts loomed, draped in ghostly white fog. The driver asked if they had arrived. Dorian stepped out, paid the promised fare, and hurried toward the quay. Lanterns flickered on merchant vessels, their light fracturing in the puddles. A steamer nearby cast a red glow, and the damp pavement glistened like oilcloth."
}

