parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c07ab15521ac19103b94ffd8",
  "rewritten_text": "Your father might spare you, provided your mother agrees. Fathers rarely value daughters as highly. If you remain for another full month, I could take one of you to London when I travel there in early June. Since Dawson does not mind the barouche-box, there is space for one, or perhaps both if the weather stays cool and you remain small. You are very kind, madam, but we must stick to our initial schedule. Lady Catherine appeared to accept this. Mrs. Collins, you must provide a servant to accompany them. I always speak plainly, and the thought of two young ladies traveling alone is quite improper. You must arrange for someone to go. I despise such carelessness. Young women require proper protection according to their station. When my niece Georgiana visited Ramsgate, I insisted she have two men-servants. Miss Darcy, daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley and Lady Anne, could not have traveled otherwise. I am extremely careful regarding such matters. You must send John with them, Mrs. Collins. I am pleased I thought of it, as it would reflect poorly on you to let them travel unaccompanied. Our uncle is sending a servant for us. Oh, your uncle? He employs a man-servant, does he? I am relieved someone considers these details. Where will you change horses? Bromley, naturally. Mention my name at the Bell, and you will be well served. Lady Catherine posed numerous other questions about their trip, and because she rarely answered them herself, Elizabeth had to remain attentive. Elizabeth felt this was fortunate, as her distracted mind might otherwise have wandered. She saved her reflections for private moments, finding relief in daily solitary walks where she could dwell on her distressing memories. She nearly knew Mr. Darcy’s letter by heart. She examined every phrase, her emotions toward him fluctuating. Recalling his manner of address still sparked indignation, yet reflecting on her own unjust condemnation of him shifted her anger toward herself, leaving her to pity his disappointment."
}

