parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "381b017880cf427abaf6d874",
  "rewritten_text": "The powerful, unearthly voice—which seemed to come from no instrument—suddenly fell silent. The girl turned to help her elderly companion and finally noticed us. She was the guide for her blind father, a musician from Saxony who had settled in the village years ago. A young German student had joined them shortly before the plague began. It was clear that the nobleman had fallen in love with the musician’s daughter and fled his family to be with her, only to die early in the epidemic. The girl had kept her father alive by hiding the truth of the world’s collapse, letting him believe they were still surrounded by a living audience. On the day we arrived, she fell ill. Terrified of leaving her father alone, she found the strength to lead him to the chapel one last time. Despite her grief, she played a beautiful hymn perfectly, unaware that the earth would soon be her grave. When we appeared, her desperate courage broke. She rushed to Adrian, crying out for him to save her father before collapsing in tears. Sadly, both are now buried beneath the walnut tree where her lover rests, just as she requested. Her father held her hand until she died, refusing to let go, and passed away himself twelve hours later. They rest together under the trees, surrounded by the mountains. Though they are gone, I often imagine the music they loved still drifting through the air, bringing peace to their spirits."
}

