parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "64ed50fbc48f98dcc03919a6",
  "rewritten_text": "Weeks passed, and I became increasingly obsessed with you. I had depicted you as Paris in delicate armor and as Adonis with a huntsman’s cloak. You sat on Adrian’s barge crowned with lotus blossoms, staring across the Nile, or leaned over a Greek woodland pool to admire your own reflection. It was all as art should be—idealized and distant. Then, on a day I now consider fateful, I decided to paint you as you truly are, in your own clothes and era. Whether it was the realism or the sheer intensity of your personality laid bare, I cannot say. But as I worked, every brushstroke seemed to expose my secret. I feared others would discover my devotion, feeling I had poured too much of myself into the canvas. I resolved never to exhibit it. You were annoyed, but you didn't understand what it meant to me. Harry laughed, but I didn't care. Once finished, I felt justified in my decision. Yet, once the painting left my studio and its overwhelming presence faded, I felt foolish for imagining it held anything beyond your beauty and my technical skill. I still believe the passion felt during creation isn't truly visible in the final work. Art is more abstract than we assume; form and color only speak of themselves. It often seems art hides the artist more than it reveals. When I received the offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the centerpiece of the exhibition, never dreaming you would refuse. I see now that you were right. The painting cannot be shown. Please don't be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have confessed."
}

