parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6905b16f7f02c0cc80c369af",
  "rewritten_text": "Such reflections soothed me, and I fell into a deep slumber that afternoon; yet my feverish state precluded any tranquil visions. The ghastly events of the prior day played repeatedly in my mind—the women fleeing and Felix pulling me away from his father. I woke drained and, seeing it was night, emerged from my shelter to find sustenance. Once fed, I walked toward the familiar path leading to the cottage. Everything was quiet. I retreated to my shed, waiting in silence for the family to wake. The time passed and the sun climbed high, yet the residents did not emerge. I shook with dread, fearing some terrible calamity. The cottage remained dark and still; the torment of my uncertainty is beyond words. Soon, two men stopped by the house and spoke with agitated gestures, though I could not grasp their meaning as they used the local tongue rather than my protectors' language. Shortly after, Felix arrived with a stranger. I was startled, knowing he had not left earlier, and listened intently to discern the reason for these strange events. His companion asked if he realized he would forfeit three months' rent and his garden's yield, offering him time to reconsider. Felix insisted it was pointless, stating they could never return. He explained his father’s life was in peril due to the incident he had described, and that his wife and sister remained traumatized. He begged the man to stop arguing, urging him to reclaim the property so he could depart. Felix shook as he spoke. They entered the cottage briefly before leaving. I never saw the De Lacey family again. I spent the rest of the day in my hovel, consumed by a numb, hollow despair. My protectors had vanished, severing my only connection to humanity. For the first time, malice and vengeance flooded my heart, and rather than resist these impulses, I surrendered to them, focusing my thoughts on destruction and death."
}

