  "request_id": "0c87fe06ed7cf398c4c36035",
  "rewritten_text": "I agree. It is the best plan. Will you just join hands and promise that we— Oh no, that will not suffice for this. That is fine for trivial, common matters—especially with girls, because they betray you regardless and gossip if they get angry—but there ought to be a written record for a significant thing like this. And blood. Tom’s entire spirit cheered this notion. It was profound, dark, and ominous; the time, the situation, and the environment were all appropriate. He grabbed a smooth pine shingle resting in the moonlight, retrieved a small piece of red chalk from his pocket, caught the moonlight on his task, and laboriously scratched these words, stressing every deliberate downward stroke by biting his tongue and easing the pressure on the upward strokes. Huck Finn and Tom Sawyer swear they will remain silent about This and They wish They may Drop down dead in Their Tracks if They ever Tell and Rot. Huckleberry was struck with awe at Tom’s writing skill and the grandeur of his prose. He immediately pulled a pin from his lapel and prepared to pierce his skin, but Tom intervened. Wait! Do not do that. A pin is brass. It might have verdigris on it. What is verdigris? It is poison. That is what it is. You just swallow some of it once—you will see. So Tom unspooled thread from one of his needles, and each boy pricked his thumb pad, squeezing out a drop of blood. Eventually, after much squeezing, Tom managed to sign his initials, using his little finger as a pen. Then he instructed Huckleberry on how to form an H and an F, and the vow was finished. They interred the shingle near the wall with somber rituals and chants, and the chains binding their tongues were deemed locked with the key discarded. A figure crept quietly through a gap at the far end of the derelict structure, but they remained unaware. Tom, Huckleberry whispered, does this prevent us from ever telling—always? Certainly it does. It makes no difference what happens, we must stay silent. We would drop down dead—do you not know that? Yes, I suppose that is true. They continued whispering for a while. Suddenly, a dog began a long, mournful howl just outside—within ten feet of them. The boys grabbed each other instantly, paralyzed by terror. Which one of us is he signaling? Huckleberry gasped. I do not know—look through the crack. Quickly! No, you do it, Tom! I cannot—I am unable to, Huck!"
}

