parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "60303cdb5ecd1ec29e06f5e5",
  "rewritten_text": "He said nothing about the matter until their visitor left for the night. Once he was gone, he turned to his daughter and said, “Jane, I congratulate you. You will be a very happy woman.” Jane immediately went to him, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness. “You are a good girl,” he replied, “and I am pleased to think you will be so well settled. I have no doubt you will do well together. Your personalities are quite similar. You are both so agreeable that you will never make a firm decision, so easygoing that your servants will cheat you, and so generous that you will always spend more than you earn.” “I hope not,” Jane replied. “Being reckless with money would be inexcusable for me.” “Spend more than they earn? My dear Mr. Bennet,” his wife cried, “what are you talking about? He has four or five thousand a year, and likely more.” Turning to her daughter, she added, “Oh, my dear Jane, I am so happy! I know I won’t sleep a wink tonight. I knew this would happen. I always said it would. I knew you couldn’t be that beautiful for nothing! I remember thinking you would end up together the moment I saw him arrive in Hertfordshire last year. He is the handsomest young man ever seen!” Wickham and Lydia were forgotten. Jane was clearly her favorite child, and for the moment, she cared for no one else. Her younger sisters soon began asking her for favors she might be able to grant in the future. Mary asked to use the library at Netherfield, and Kitty begged for a few balls there every winter. From that point on, Bingley visited Longbourn daily, often arriving before breakfast and staying until after supper, unless a neighbor forced him to attend a dinner he felt obligated to accept. Elizabeth had little time to talk with her sister, as Jane focused entirely on Bingley when he was present. However, Elizabeth proved useful to both during their brief times apart. When Jane was away, Bingley would talk to Elizabeth about her, and when Bingley left, Jane would seek out Elizabeth to talk about him. “He has made me so happy,” Jane said one evening, “by telling me he had no idea I was in town last spring!”"
}

