parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8b5bea3c412843516a4ad3c4",
  "rewritten_text": "We both loved Adrian, though Perdita, still a child, could not fully appreciate his virtues or share his interests as I did. I was always by his side. His gentle, sensitive nature gave our conversations a tender, ethereal quality. He was as joyful as a singing lark, as ambitious in thought as an eagle, and as innocent as a dove. He could lighten Perdita’s seriousness and soothe my own restless, troubled spirit. I looked back on my past struggles as if they were a fading nightmare, feeling as though I had been reborn into a new body with a different way of perceiving the world. Yet, I remained the same in my strength and my deep need for connection. My masculine drive did not vanish, but it became softened and more human. Adrian taught me more than just history and philosophy; while he helped me tame my reckless spirit, he also revealed his own heart, allowing me to understand his remarkable character. The former queen of England had tried to instill ambition in her son from infancy. Recognizing his genius, she nurtured his talents to serve her own goals. She encouraged his thirst for knowledge and his bold courage, hoping his love of freedom would eventually turn into a desire for power. She wanted him to resent those who had forced his father to abdicate, but she failed. Instead, the stories he heard about a nation claiming its right to self-governance inspired him, and he became a republican by principle. Still, his mother did not give up. She combined his pride of birth with patience and ambition, carefully studying his personality. She used praise and criticism to influence him, and though his reactions never matched her expectations, she relied on his talents and remained confident that she would eventually win him over."
}

