  "rewritten_text": "Men marry out of exhaustion; women, out of curiosity: both end in disappointment. I doubt I will marry, Harry. I am too much in love. I am applying your own aphorism to my life. Who is she? Lord Henry asked. An actress, Dorian Gray replied, blushing. Lord Henry shrugged. That is a common start. You would not say that if you saw her. Who is she? Sibyl Vane. I have never heard of her. No one has yet, but they will. She is a genius. My dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women are merely decorative. They have nothing to say, yet they say it charmingly. They represent the triumph of matter over mind, just as men represent the triumph of mind over morals. Harry, how can you say that? It is true. I am currently analyzing women, so I should know. It is not as complex as I thought. There are only two kinds: the plain and the colorful. The plain are useful for gaining a reputation for respectability. The others are charming but make the mistake of using makeup to look young. Our grandmothers used makeup to appear witty. Now, as long as a woman looks younger than her daughter, she is satisfied. There are only five women in London worth talking to, and two are social outcasts. Tell me about your genius. How long have you known her? Your views terrify me. Never mind that. How long? About three weeks. Where did you meet her? I will tell you, but do not be unsympathetic. It never would have happened if I had not met you. You filled me with a desperate desire to know everything about life. For days, something throbbed in my veins. As I walked through the park or down the street, I looked at everyone and wondered about their lives with a frantic curiosity. Some fascinated me; others terrified me. There was a beautiful poison in the air. I craved sensations."
}

