  "rewritten_text": "That matters little. She answered, I might also ask why you chose to tell me you admired me against your own will, judgment, and nature, while clearly intending to insult me. Was that not justification for my rudeness? Yet I have other grievances. Even if my own heart had not turned against you, would I ever accept the man who destroyed the happiness of my beloved sister? Darcy paled at these words, yet he remained silent as she continued. I have every reason to despise you. No excuse justifies your cruel actions. You cannot deny that you were the primary cause of their separation, exposing them to public judgment and deep misery. She paused, noting with anger that he appeared entirely unrepentant, even offering a skeptical smile. Can you deny it? she asked. He replied calmly, I do not deny that I worked to separate my friend from your sister, and I am glad I succeeded. I acted more kindly toward him than toward myself. Elizabeth ignored his remark, though it only deepened her resentment. My dislike is not based solely on that, she added. Long before, Mr. Wickham revealed your true character to me. How can you defend yourself there? You seem very invested in that gentleman, Darcy said, his voice tightening. Who could know his misfortunes and not care? she countered. His misfortunes! Darcy repeated with scorn. Yes, they have been great indeed. And caused by you, Elizabeth exclaimed. You have reduced him to his current state of poverty."
}

