parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "14b40d9b8311b25057d2d16c",
  "rewritten_text": "We could certainly build a chilling superstition around this. I suppose you will name the beetle scarabaeus caput hominis, or something similar, as those titles appear often in natural histories. But where are the antennae you mentioned? The antennae! Legrand replied, appearing strangely agitated. I am certain you must see them. I drew them as clearly as they appear on the actual insect, which should suffice. Very well, I said, perhaps you did, but I still cannot see them. I handed him the paper silently, not wanting to provoke him. I was surprised by his reaction; his irritability baffled me, as the drawing clearly lacked antennae and looked remarkably like a standard death’s-head moth. He took the paper crossly and nearly crumpled it for the fire, but a sudden look at the sketch froze him. His face flushed crimson, then turned deathly pale. He studied the drawing intently for several minutes before rising to take a candle to a sea-chest in the corner. There, he examined the paper from every angle with intense focus. He remained silent, and though his behavior stunned me, I chose not to aggravate his mood. Eventually, he placed the paper in his wallet and locked it inside a desk. He calmed down, though his earlier enthusiasm had vanished, replaced by a deep, distracted reverie that my attempts at conversation could not break. I had planned to stay the night, but given his state, I decided to leave. He did not ask me to stay, though he shook my hand with unusual warmth as I departed. About a month later, during which time I had not seen Legrand, his servant Jupiter visited me in Charleston. The old man looked so dejected that I feared some terrible misfortune had struck my friend."
}

