{
  "request_id": "9fdde46d174ef3523f776fc5",
  "rewritten_text": "No, that is for tomorrow, and I specifically needed to see Knightley today for that reason. What a sweltering morning! I walked across the fields, which made it even worse. To find him gone! I am not at all pleased. No apology, no message left for me. The housekeeper claimed she knew nothing of my visit. It is very strange, and no one knew where he had gone. Perhaps to Hartfield, the Abbey Mill, or his woods. Miss Woodhouse, this is unlike our friend Knightley. Can you explain it? Emma agreed that it was indeed strange and that she had nothing to say in his defense. I cannot imagine, Mrs. Elton said, feeling the slight as a wife should, how he could treat you this way, of all people. My dear Mr. Elton, he must have left a message. Knightley could not be that eccentric; his servants must have forgotten. That is likely the case with the Donwell staff, who are always awkward and careless. I would not have his servant Harry in our house for anything. As for Mrs. Hodges, Wright thinks very little of her; she promised a recipe and never sent it. I met William Larkins near the house, Mr. Elton continued, and he told me I would not find his master, but I did not believe him. William seemed irritable and said he did not know what had come over his master lately, as he could rarely speak to him. I do not care about William’s issues, but it is vital that I see Knightley today, so this hot walk has been a serious inconvenience. Emma decided she should head home immediately. She was likely expected there, and Mr. Knightley might be spared further conflict with Mr. Elton, if not with William Larkins. She was pleased when Miss Fairfax insisted on walking her out, giving her an opportunity she immediately used to speak."
}

