  "request_id": "db6e1f5d2ee51dfc2a33f22b",
  "rewritten_text": "Keeping in mind the points I have highlighted—the strange voice, the unusual agility, and the baffling lack of motive in such a brutal murder—let us examine the crime itself. A woman was strangled and shoved headfirst up a chimney. Ordinary killers do not commit murder this way, nor do they dispose of bodies like this. You must admit that forcing a corpse into a chimney is bizarre and defies all human logic, even for the most depraved individuals. Consider the immense strength required to shove the body into such a tight space, especially since it took several men working together to pull it back out. Consider the evidence of extraordinary physical power. Thick clumps of grey hair were torn out by the roots. You know how much force is needed to pull out even a few hairs; the roots were still attached to pieces of scalp, proving the massive force used to rip out thousands of hairs at once. The victim’s throat was not just cut; her head was completely severed by a simple razor. Note the savage ferocity of these acts. I will not dwell on the bruises on Madame L’Espanaye’s body. Monsieur Dumas and Monsieur Etienne correctly identified that they were caused by a blunt object, which was clearly the stone pavement where she landed after falling from the window. This simple explanation eluded the police, just as the width of the shutters did, because their obsession with the nails blinded them to the possibility that the windows could have been opened. If you combine these facts with the strange disorder of the room, we have identified a culprit with astounding agility, superhuman strength, brutal ferocity, and no clear motive. We are looking for a horror that defies human nature, accompanied by a voice that sounds foreign to every nationality and lacks any intelligible speech."
}

