parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b3df23a23c2447580b56cb58",
  "rewritten_text": "In my youth, I was never unhappy. If I ever felt bored, the beauty of nature or the study of human achievement could always lift my spirits. Now, however, I am like a blasted tree; my soul has been struck by lightning. I realized then that I would live on only as a broken shell of a person, a miserable sight to others and unbearable to myself. We spent a long time in Oxford, wandering the area and trying to find places connected to important moments in English history. Our trips were often extended by the interesting things we found. We visited the tomb of the famous Hampden and the battlefield where he died. For a moment, my soul rose above my fears to consider the noble ideas of liberty and sacrifice that these sites represented. For an instant, I dared to feel free, but the weight of my suffering returned, and I sank back into hopelessness. We left Oxford for Matlock. The landscape there resembled Switzerland, though on a smaller scale, and the green hills lacked the distant, snow-capped peaks of my homeland. We visited a famous cave and small natural history displays, which were arranged like those in Servox and Chamounix. Hearing the name of that place made me tremble, and I quickly left Matlock to escape the painful memory. Traveling north from Derby, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could almost imagine I was back in the Swiss mountains. The patches of snow on the hills, the lakes, and the rushing streams were all familiar and dear to me. We met people there who almost tricked me into feeling happy. Clerval was even more delighted than I was; his mind grew in the company of talented people, and he discovered strengths in himself he had never realized he possessed."
}

