parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cb04ef16e11b655954dad0b3",
  "rewritten_text": "Are you talking about Mr. Knightley? Of course I am. I never meant anyone else, and I assumed you knew. When we discussed him, it seemed perfectly clear. Not quite, Emma replied, forcing herself to remain calm, because everything you said then seemed to refer to someone else. I could have sworn you named Mr. Frank Churchill. I am certain we spoke of the help he gave you by protecting you from the gypsies. Oh, Miss Woodhouse, how you forget! My dear Harriet, I remember our conversation perfectly. I told you I understood your feelings, given the service he provided, and you agreed, speaking warmly of your gratitude and how you felt when he rescued you. It is vivid in my memory. Oh, I see what you mean now, Harriet exclaimed, but I was thinking of something entirely different. It was not the gypsies or Mr. Frank Churchill. I was thinking of a much more precious moment: when Mr. Knightley asked me to dance after Mr. Elton refused, and no one else would. That was the act of kindness and generosity that made me realize how superior he is to everyone else on earth. Good God, Emma cried, this has been a most unfortunate and deplorable mistake. What can be done? You would not have encouraged me if you had understood? At least I am no worse off than I would have been otherwise, and now, it is possible. She paused. Emma could not speak. I do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse, Harriet continued, that you see a vast difference between the two men. You must think one is infinitely above me compared to the other. But I hope, even if it seems strange, that you will remember your own words about how even more unlikely matches have occurred. If I were so incredibly fortunate that Mr. Knightley did not mind the disparity, I hope you will not oppose it or create difficulties."
}

