parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "46cab6c8659e52a89cb76747",
  "rewritten_text": "After settling our friend into his new office, we turned our attention toward Windsor. Its proximity to London made our departure from Raymond and Perdita feel less like a painful separation. We said our goodbyes at the Protectoral Palace, where it was charming to watch my sister embrace her new role with appropriate dignity. Her internal pride and humble demeanor were in constant conflict. Her shyness was genuine, stemming from a fear of being misunderstood and a sensitivity to public neglect, traits she shared with Raymond. Yet, Perdita was more considerate of others; her bashfulness often arose from a desire to ensure those around her did not feel inferior—a thought that never occurred to her. While Idris’s upbringing might have better suited her for formal ceremonies, she found such routines tedious. Conversely, despite the challenges, Perdita clearly enjoyed her position. She was so preoccupied with new ideas that she felt little pain at our departure; she said an affectionate goodbye and promised to visit, showing no regret over our separation. Raymond was in high spirits, overwhelmed by his new authority. His mind was filled with grand plans, and he vowed to his friends and the world that his time as Protector would be marked by some act of extraordinary glory. We discussed them and reflected on these changes as we returned to Windsor Castle with a smaller group. We felt immense relief to escape political turmoil and embraced our solitude with renewed enthusiasm. We remained busy, and I channeled my energy into intellectual pursuits; rigorous study proved an excellent remedy for the restlessness that idleness would have surely invited. Perdita allowed us to take Clara back to Windsor, and she, along with my two lovely children, provided constant joy. The only shadow over our peace was Adrian’s health. He was clearly declining, though without clear symptoms, unless his bright eyes, animated expression, and flushed cheeks signaled consumption. He remained free of pain or fear, dedicating himself to his books and finding rest in the company of his sister and me."
}

