parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c9202c2a5b319ec50b953243",
  "rewritten_text": "One day, while I was occupied with my studies and Mr. Pocket, I received a letter that immediately unsettled me. Although I had never seen the handwriting, I instinctively knew who had sent it. It lacked any formal salutation, simply stating: I am arriving in London the day after tomorrow on the midday coach. I believe it was arranged that you would meet me. Regardless, Miss Havisham expects it, and I am writing accordingly. She sends her regards. Yours, Estella. Had there been time, I would have ordered new clothes, but I had to settle for what I owned. My appetite disappeared, and I found no peace until the day arrived. Even then, I was more anxious than ever, haunting the coach office in Wood Street long before the coach could have possibly left town. Despite knowing better, I felt I could not leave the office for more than five minutes. I had been waiting for half an hour when Wemmick bumped into me. Hello, Mr. Pip, he said. How are you? I would not have expected to see you here. I explained I was meeting someone arriving by coach and asked after his home and his father. Both are doing well, thank you, Wemmick replied, especially the old man. He is in great spirits and will be eighty-two soon. I am considering firing my cannon eighty-two times, provided the neighbors do not complain and the weapon holds up. But this is not business talk. Where do you think I am headed? To the office? I asked. Something like that, Wemmick said. I am going to Newgate. We are handling a bank robbery case, and after inspecting the scene, I need to speak with our client. Did your client commit the robbery? I asked. Goodness no, Wemmick replied dryly. But he is accused of it. You or I could just as easily be accused of the same thing."
}

