parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d9791593bbbcad2f2ddadbaf",
  "rewritten_text": "The painter suddenly appeared at the studio door, gesturing for them to enter. They exchanged smiles. I am waiting, he called out. Please come in. The light is perfect, and you may bring your drinks. They stood and walked down the path together. Two butterflies fluttered by, and a thrush began to sing in the garden pear tree. You are glad you met me, Mr. Gray, Lord Henry said, watching him. Yes, I am glad now. I wonder if I always will be. Always! That is a terrible word. It makes me shudder. Women love using it, ruining every romance by trying to make it permanent. It is meaningless, too. The only difference between a whim and a lifelong passion is that the whim lasts a little longer. As they entered the studio, Dorian Gray touched Lord Henry’s arm. In that case, let our friendship be a whim, he murmured, blushing at his own boldness, before stepping onto the platform to resume his pose. Lord Henry threw himself into a wicker armchair to watch. The sound of the brush against the canvas filled the silence, broken only when Hallward stepped back to study his work. Dust motes danced in the golden light streaming through the doorway, and the heavy scent of roses hung in the air. After fifteen minutes, Hallward stopped, staring at Dorian and then at the painting, biting his brush and frowning. It is finished, he finally announced, signing his name in vermilion letters on the canvas. Lord Henry approached to examine the work. It was a remarkable piece of art and a perfect likeness. My dear fellow, I congratulate you, he said. It is the finest portrait of our time. Mr. Gray, come see yourself. The young man started as if waking from a dream. Is it really finished? he murmured, stepping down. Quite, said the painter. You sat splendidly today. I am very grateful. That is entirely due to me, Lord Henry interrupted. Isn’t it, Mr. Gray? Dorian did not answer, but walked listlessly toward the portrait."
}

