parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5561842fa2ad5a2dd1c99cc9",
  "rewritten_text": "Raymond paced the room in a fury while I waited to hear his complaints. Adrian leaned against the wall and remarked that our behavior was as ridiculous as that of schoolboys. Raymond insisted that this was part of a tyrannical system he would no longer tolerate. He demanded to know why he, as Protector of England, should be treated like a slave, his privacy violated and his friends insulted. He declared he was finished with it all, removed the star of his office from his chest, and threw it onto the table, announcing his resignation. Adrian challenged him to find anyone else in England capable of taking his place, urging him to recognize his own worth. He reminded Raymond that only months ago, the country viewed his life and the Protectorate as inseparable. He recounted how Raymond had improved their cities, established useful institutions, and protected the weak from the powerful. Adrian pleaded with him to overcome this bad mood and resume his duties, promising that the people would support him if he could only master himself. Raymond replied gloomily that Adrian’s advice would be better suited to himself, as he possessed the wisdom and justice to rule. Raymond admitted that he had taken on a task he was unfit for, as he could not even control his own passions. He swore by God that his resignation was not a temporary outburst but a permanent decision to abandon the burden of power."
}

