  "rewritten_text": "No raising by hand, then. Not at all! Joe offered me more gravy, which I hesitated to accept. He was quite a burden to you, ma’am, Mrs. Hubble remarked to my sister. Burden? my sister repeated, launching into a terrifying list of my various illnesses, sleepless nights, falls from heights and depths, self-inflicted injuries, and the many times I had stubbornly refused her wishes for my demise. I suspect the Romans were constantly irritated by their noses, perhaps explaining their restless nature. Regardless, Mr. Wopsle’s Roman nose so vexed me during this account of my sins that I longed to pull it until he screamed. Yet, my suffering was minor compared to the dread I felt when the silence following her speech left everyone staring at me with visible disgust. Still, Mr. Pumblechook gently steered the conversation back, asking if boiled pork was also rich. Have some brandy, uncle, my sister suggested. Oh heavens, the moment had arrived! He would discover it was diluted, declare it so, and I would be ruined. I gripped the table leg beneath the cloth, bracing for the end. My sister fetched the stone bottle and poured his drink. The wretched man toyed with his glass, prolonging my agony while the others cleared the table. I watched him, still clutching the table leg, as he playfully swirled his drink, smiled, tilted his head back, and swallowed. Suddenly, the room erupted in horror as he leaped up, spun in a spasmodic, whooping dance, and bolted outside. Through the window, I saw him plunging about and spitting, making grotesque faces as if he had lost his senses. I held on tight while Joe and his wife rushed to his aid."
}

