parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7a1863678ae992be5f13faa9",
  "rewritten_text": "Starve to death? Oh, Sir Simon, are you hungry? I have a sandwich in my bag. Would you like it? No, thank you, I do not eat anymore; but that is very kind of you. You are much nicer than the rest of your rude, vulgar, and dishonest family. Stop! Virginia cried, stamping her foot. You are the one who is rude and vulgar. As for dishonesty, you stole my paints to touch up that ridiculous bloodstain in the library. You took all my reds, so I could not paint sunsets, then you took the green and yellow, leaving me only indigo and white for depressing moonlight scenes. I never told on you, even though I was annoyed. Besides, who has ever heard of emerald-green blood? Well, the ghost replied meekly, what was I to do? Real blood is hard to find, and since your brother started this with his detergent, I saw no reason not to use your paints. As for color, that is a matter of taste. The Cantervilles have blue blood, but I know you Americans do not care for such things. You know nothing about it. You should move to America to improve your mind. My father would gladly pay for your passage. There is a tax on spirits, but the customs officers are Democrats, so there will be no trouble. You would be a huge success in New York. I know people who would pay a fortune to have a family ghost. I do not think I would like America. I suppose that is because we have no ruins or curiosities, Virginia said sarcastically. No ruins? No curiosities? the ghost replied. You have your navy and your manners. Good evening. I am going to ask my father to give the twins an extra week of holiday. Please do not go, Miss Virginia, he pleaded. I am so lonely and unhappy. I want to sleep, but I cannot. That is absurd. You just need to go to bed and blow out the candle."
}

