parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a81ef16b16d37d3daf1e8a74",
  "rewritten_text": "The lieutenant once asked why he had traveled so far across the ice in such an unusual sled. The stranger’s face turned deeply somber as he replied, \"I am searching for someone who fled from me.\" When asked if the man he pursued traveled the same way, he confirmed it. I mentioned that we had seen a man on a sled pulled by dogs the day before we rescued him. This caught his attention, and he asked many questions about the path the creature had taken. Later, when we were alone, he noted that I must be curious but was too polite to ask questions. I agreed that prying would be rude, especially after I had saved his life. He then asked if the shifting ice might have destroyed the other sled. I told him I could not be sure, as the ice did not break until midnight, and the traveler might have reached safety by then. This news gave the stranger a new sense of purpose. He was eager to go on deck to watch for the sled, but I convinced him to stay in the cabin, as he was too weak for the cold. I promised to have someone watch for him instead. This is my record of these events. The stranger is slowly recovering, though he remains quiet and uneasy around others. Still, his gentle nature has won over the crew. I have grown to care for him like a brother, and his profound sorrow moves me deeply. He must have been a remarkable man in his prime. I once wrote to you, Margaret, that I would find no friends at sea, yet I have met a man whom I would have been proud to call a brother."
}

