parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b6c9342b54d2cb29a4a22ceb",
  "rewritten_text": "He shuddered and retreated, shutting the window. Upon reaching the door, he unlocked it and stepped out, deliberately avoiding a look at the corpse. He believed the key to his composure was to ignore the reality of the scene. The man who had created the portrait responsible for his suffering was gone; that sufficed. He then recalled the Moorish lamp, crafted of silver and steel with turquoise accents. Fearing his servant might notice its absence, he returned to retrieve it. He could not avoid seeing the body, noting how motionless it lay and how ghastly white the hands appeared, resembling a macabre wax figure. After locking the door, he descended the stairs, the floorboards groaning under his weight. He paused frequently, but the house remained silent. In the library, he gathered the coat and bag, stowing them in a hidden wall compartment where he kept his costumes, intending to burn them later. Checking his watch, he saw it was nearly two. He pondered his situation, reflecting on the frequency of executions for such crimes. Yet, he reasoned, there was no proof. Basil had departed at eleven, and no one had witnessed his return. With the staff away and his valet asleep, he could claim Basil had taken the midnight train to Paris. Given the artist's reclusive nature, months would pass before anyone grew suspicious, providing ample time to erase all traces. A sudden idea occurred to him. Donning his coat and hat, he moved to the hall, pausing as a policeman’s lantern flashed against the window. He held his breath until the officer passed, then quietly unlatched the door, slipped outside, and began to ring the bell."
}

