{
  "request_id": "ff1dbbb9dc9d47fc013a71e3",
  "rewritten_text": "He shivered and retreated, shutting the window. At the door, he turned the key and stepped out, refusing to look at the corpse. He felt that ignoring the reality of the situation was his only path forward. The friend who had painted the portrait responsible for his ruin was gone; that was sufficient. He then remembered the Moorish lamp, a silver piece inlaid with steel and turquoise. Fearing his servant might notice its absence, he returned to retrieve it. He could not avoid seeing the body; it lay perfectly still, its hands a ghastly, waxen white. After locking the door, he crept downstairs. The floorboards groaned under his weight, making him pause repeatedly, though he soon realized the sounds were only his own. In the library, he found the bag and coat. He hid them in a secret wall compartment where he kept his disguises, intending to burn them later. Checking his watch, he saw it was nearly two in the morning. He sat to reflect. Men were frequently executed in England for such crimes, yet what proof existed against him? Basil Hallward had left at eleven, and no one had seen him return. With most servants away and his valet asleep, he reasoned that Basil’s supposed trip to Paris would delay any suspicion for months. That was plenty of time to destroy the evidence. A sudden thought occurred to him. He donned his coat and hat, entering the hall just as a policeman’s lantern flashed against the window. He held his breath until the heavy footsteps faded. Finally, he slipped out, closed the door quietly, and rang the bell."
}

