parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0169748305beddc59163c40e",
  "rewritten_text": "“Never!” the girl replied. “Why?” “For one reason,” she answered resolutely, “which the lady understands and will support—I have her word. Furthermore, despite his wicked life, I have lived poorly too. Many of us have shared the same path, and I will not betray those who could have turned on me but chose not to, however corrupt they may be.” “Then,” the gentleman said, as if reaching his goal, “hand Monks over to me and let me handle him.” “What if he betrays the others?” “I promise that if the truth is extracted, the matter ends there. Oliver’s history contains painful details that should not be public; if the truth emerges, the others will go free.” “And if it does not?” she asked. “Then,” the gentleman continued, “Fagin shall not face justice without your consent. I believe I could then provide reasons to persuade you.” “Do I have the lady’s word?” she inquired. “You do,” Rose answered. “My sincere pledge.” “Monks will never discover how you learned this?” the girl asked after a pause. “Never,” the gentleman replied. “The information will be used in a way he could never suspect.” “I have been a liar among liars since childhood,” she said after another silence, “but I will trust your word.” After receiving their assurances, she spoke in a voice so hushed it was hard to follow, describing the tavern she had left that night. She paused frequently, seemingly while the gentleman took notes. Once she had detailed the location, the best vantage point for surveillance, and the times Monks usually visited, she paused to recall his appearance. “He is tall,” she said, “and well-built but not heavy. He has a furtive gait, constantly glancing over his shoulders as he walks.”"
}

