parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b19ef2164f8814155722874a",
  "rewritten_text": "From the moment we embarked at the stairs, I had kept a vigilant watch for signs of suspicion, yet found none. We were certainly not being shadowed by any vessel; had we been, I would have steered for the bank to force their hand. Instead, we proceeded without interference. Wrapped in his boat-cloak, he appeared entirely at home in the surroundings. It was striking—perhaps a result of his harsh existence—that he remained the most composed among us. He was not unconcerned, expressing a wish to see his gentleman thrive abroad, nor was he merely resigned, yet he refused to anticipate trouble. He met adversity only when it arrived. \"If you knew, dear boy,\" he remarked, \"what it is to sit here with you and smoke, after years confined within four walls, you would envy me. You cannot truly understand.\" I replied that I appreciated the joys of liberty. He shook his head solemnly. \"You cannot know it as I do. You must have been imprisoned to grasp it fully, but I will not be melancholy.\" It seemed contradictory that he would risk his life and liberty for a singular purpose, yet I realized that safety without peril was perhaps too foreign to his nature. He confirmed this after a pause: \"When I was across the world, I constantly looked toward this side. Growing wealthy there felt hollow, for everyone knew Magwitch, and no one cared where he went. They are not so indifferent here, at least if they knew my whereabouts.\" I assured him that if all proceeded as planned, he would be secure and free within hours. He drew a deep breath and replied, \"I hope so.\" I asked, \"And do you believe it?\""
}

