  "request_id": "9ae8b7e1e47e19dda9038ad7",
  "rewritten_text": "Back home, we gathered funds through tariffs, and citizens falsely believed foreign importers bore the burden. In reality, the American public paid, with the cost distributed so precisely that a millionaire and a laborer's infant paid the same six dollars. I find that perfectly equitable. Scotland and Ireland paid tribute to Arthur, with the British Isles holding under a million people. A laborer earned three cents daily, covering his own expenses. Consequently, the government required ninety thousand dollars annually, or roughly two hundred fifty dollars a day. By swapping gold for nickels during the king's healing ceremony, I satisfied everyone while saving four-fifths of the daily budget—a sum equivalent to eight hundred thousand dollars in my own time. I utilized a lesson from my youth: I used to donate buttons to missionaries instead of coins. The buttons satisfied the ignorant, while the coins served me better; everyone benefited. Marinel processed the patients. He screened them, and those who qualified were sent to the king. A priest recited scripture while the monarch touched the sores. Finally, the patient received a nickel, hung around their neck by the king, and was sent away. You might doubt the efficacy of this, but it worked. Any ritual cures if the patient possesses sufficient faith. Near Astolat, a chapel stands where a goose-girl claimed the Virgin appeared. Despite a frightening painting of the event, thousands of sick and crippled pilgrims visit annually and depart fully restored."
}

