  "request_id": "279be710ff78ffd18596a5b5",
  "rewritten_text": "I uncovered the embers, and a light breeze quickly fanned them into flame. I used a branch to keep the fire alive when it began to fade. By night, I realized the fire provided both light and warmth, and it improved my food; the scraps left by travelers tasted far better roasted than the raw berries I gathered. I tried cooking other items, finding that while berries were ruined, nuts and roots were much improved. Food eventually became scarce, and I often spent entire days searching for acorns to satisfy my hunger. I decided to leave my current home to find a place where my needs were more easily met. I deeply regretted losing the fire, as I did not know how to recreate it. After much thought, I gave up on trying to restart it, wrapped myself in my cloak, and headed west. After three days of wandering, I reached open country. A heavy snowfall had covered the fields in white, and the cold, damp ground chilled my feet. Around seven in the morning, desperate for food and shelter, I spotted a small shepherd's hut on a hill. I examined the structure with curiosity and entered through the open door. An old man sat by a fire, preparing breakfast. Upon seeing me, he shrieked and fled across the fields with surprising speed for his frail frame. His reaction and flight puzzled me, but I was captivated by the hut. It was dry and protected from the elements, appearing to me as a divine sanctuary after my long suffering."
}

