parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0428688d9e7cb77cb8639d16",
  "rewritten_text": "It is quite remarkable that she always hears Jane more clearly than she hears me. Jane speaks so distinctly! Still, she will find her grandmother no deafer than she was two years ago, which is saying a lot at my mother’s age. It has been two full years since she was last here. We have never gone so long without seeing her, and as I told Mrs. Cole, we will hardly know how to make enough of her now. Are you expecting Miss Fairfax soon? Oh yes, next week. Indeed, that must be a great pleasure. Thank you, you are very kind. Yes, next week. Everyone is so surprised and says such kind things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends in Highbury as they are to see her. She will arrive Friday or Saturday; she cannot say which because Colonel Campbell will need the carriage himself on one of those days. It is so kind of them to send her the whole way, but they always do. Yes, next Friday or Saturday. That is what she wrote about, which is why she wrote out of turn; normally, we would not have heard from her until next Tuesday or Wednesday. Yes, I imagined as much. I feared there was little chance of hearing anything about Miss Fairfax today. How kind of you! No, we would not have heard if not for this specific circumstance of her coming here so soon. My mother is delighted, as she is to stay with us for at least three months. She says so quite positively, as I am about to read to you. The situation is that the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her parents to visit her immediately. They had not intended to go until summer, but she is impatient to see them again. Until she married last October, she had never been away from them for more than a week, so it must feel very strange to be in different countries. She wrote a very urgent letter—or perhaps Mr. Dixon wrote it, I am not sure, but we shall see in Jane’s letter—to press them to come over immediately. They would meet in Dublin and then travel to their country estate, Ballycraig, which I imagine is a beautiful place."
}

