parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "400cdc16be3a05fa2f21eec0",
  "rewritten_text": "Do not assume I failed to see it as a misfortune in its own right. Rest assured, they were entirely at ease. Perhaps that does not improve my opinion of the situation. Such a crowd of women and children have no business being comfortable at sea. My dear Frederick, you speak foolishly. What would become of us sailors' wives, who frequently need transport to join our husbands, if everyone shared your sentiments? My sentiments did not stop me from transporting Mrs. Harville and her family to Plymouth. I dislike hearing you speak like a dandy, treating women as delicate creatures rather than rational beings. None of us expect calm seas forever. My dear, the Admiral remarked, once he has a wife, he will change his tune. If we survive to see another war, we shall see him act as we have, grateful to anyone who brings his wife to him. Indeed we shall. I am finished, Captain Wentworth declared. Once married people start telling me I will change my mind, I simply insist I will not, and the conversation ends there. He rose and departed. You must have traveled extensively, Mrs. Musgrove remarked to Mrs. Croft. Quite a bit during my fifteen years of marriage, though others have done more. I have crossed the Atlantic four times and made one trip to the East Indies, alongside various domestic ports like Cork, Lisbon, and Gibraltar. I never ventured past the Straits or visited the West Indies; we do not consider Bermuda or the Bahamas part of the West Indies, you know. Mrs. Musgrove had no objection, having never labeled them as such in her life. I assure you, Mrs. Croft continued, that the accommodations on a man-of-war are excellent, particularly on larger vessels. On a frigate, space is limited, yet any sensible woman can be content; I can honestly say my happiest years were spent aboard ship. As long as we were together, there was nothing to fear."
}

