parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1dbfdd82419c45bf2d8bfc46",
  "rewritten_text": "Emma did not regret her decision to visit the Coles. The next day, she enjoyed reflecting on the event, feeling that the popularity she gained was well worth the loss of her usual privacy. She had certainly delighted the Coles, who were good people, and she was sure her presence would be remembered for some time. Perfect happiness is rare, however, and two things troubled her. She worried that she had acted wrongly by sharing her suspicions about Jane Fairfax’s feelings with Frank Churchill. It was a lapse in judgment, yet his willingness to listen to her theories was so flattering that she found it hard to regret speaking. Her second regret was clearer: she was genuinely unhappy that her own musical skills were inferior to Jane’s. She felt ashamed of her lack of practice as a child, so she sat down and played for an hour and a half. Harriet interrupted her, and if praise could have helped, Harriet would have comforted her. Oh, if I could play like you and Miss Fairfax, Harriet said. Do not compare us, Emma replied. My playing is like a lamp, while hers is like sunshine. Oh, I think you play just as well as she does, Harriet insisted. Everyone last night said how well you played. Those who truly understand music know the difference, Emma said. My playing is merely good enough to be praised, but Jane’s is far superior. Harriet disagreed, noting that Mr. Cole and Frank Churchill had both praised Emma’s taste, and that Frank valued taste over technical skill. Emma reminded her that Jane possessed both. Harriet remained unconvinced, arguing that Jane’s skill was only expected since she would eventually have to teach for a living."
}

