parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8bcbf4ca8247ed5be95d8a7d",
  "rewritten_text": "April 7th. I woke early and was thrilled to finally see what I am certain is the North Pole. It lay directly beneath me, though I had climbed to such a height that I could not see it clearly. Based on my altitude readings between April 2nd and the time my barometer stopped, I estimate that by 4:00 A.M. on April 7th, I had reached at least 7,254 miles above sea level. This may seem extreme, but it is likely an underestimate. I could see the entire northern hemisphere spread out like a map, with the equator forming my horizon. While the unexplored Arctic regions were directly below, they were too small and distant to study in detail. Still, the view was fascinating. Beyond the known limits of human discovery, a vast sheet of ice stretches out. It starts flat, becomes a level plane, and eventually curves inward, ending at the Pole in a sharp, circular center. This dark spot, which looked blacker than anything else on the globe, measured about sixty-five seconds in diameter from my perspective. By noon, this circle had shrunk, and by 7:00 P.M., it vanished as my balloon drifted toward the equator. April 8th. The earth appears smaller today, and its color and overall appearance have noticeably changed."
}

