parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8c3fd830678b6725173c7010",
  "rewritten_text": "The refined gentlemen of noble lineage sported small hanging ornaments that chimed with their sluggish movements; these golden chains sounded like tiny bells. Between that tinkling and the soft swish of silk, brocade, and linen, the air held a lightness that kept the starving masses of Saint Antoine at a distance. Attire served as the constant charm to maintain social order. Everyone appeared dressed for an endless masquerade. From the Tuileries Palace, through the Court and legal chambers, down to the common executioner, the costume persisted. Even the hangman was expected to perform his duties while powdered, frizzled, and adorned in a gold-trimmed coat with silk stockings and pumps. At the gallows, Monsieur Paris presided in this elegant garb. In that year of our Lord seventeen hundred and eighty, who could doubt that a society anchored by a daintily dressed executioner would endure forever? After finishing his chocolate, Monseigneur had his inner sanctum doors opened and emerged. The display of groveling, fawning, and total servility was absolute; the courtiers bowed so deeply that they left nothing for Heaven, which perhaps explains why they never sought divine favor. Offering scattered promises and smiles, Monseigneur moved through his chambers before eventually retreating back into his sanctuary, hidden away by his attendants. When the spectacle concluded, the atmosphere grew restless, and the bells jingled as the crowd departed. Only one man remained, walking slowly past the mirrors with his hat tucked away and a snuff-box in hand. Pausing at the final exit, he turned toward the sanctuary and declared, I consign you to the Devil. He flicked the snuff from his fingertips as if shaking off dust and calmly descended the stairs."
}

