  "request_id": "15998d8ad96f61648a044740",
  "rewritten_text": "Keep in mind the points I have highlighted: the strange voice, the unusual agility, and the lack of motive in such a brutal murder. Let us examine the crime itself. A woman was strangled by hand and shoved headfirst up a chimney. Ordinary killers do not commit murder this way, nor do they dispose of bodies like that. You must admit that forcing a corpse into a chimney is bizarre and defies any normal human behavior, even among the most depraved. Consider the strength required to shove the body into that space so tightly that it took several men to pull it back down. Consider the evidence of incredible physical power. On the hearth lay thick clumps of grey hair torn out by the roots. You know how much force is needed to pull out even a few hairs. We both saw the locks, and their roots were still attached to bits of scalp—proof of the immense strength used to rip out so much hair at once. The old lady’s throat was not just cut; her head was completely severed using only a razor. Notice the brutal ferocity of these acts. Regarding the bruises on Madame L’Espanaye’s body, the doctors correctly identified they were caused by a blunt object. That object was simply the stone pavement in the yard, where she landed after falling from the bedroom window. This seems obvious now, but the police missed it because they were so focused on the locked windows that they could not imagine them ever being opened. If you combine these facts with the strange disorder of the room, we have a clear picture: astounding agility, superhuman strength, brutal ferocity, a motiveless crime, a grotesque horror unlike anything human, and a voice that sounded foreign to everyone who heard it, lacking any clear or understandable language."
}

