  "rewritten_text": "I had not yet fully accepted my situation. I did not yet feel in every heartbeat and thought that I was truly the last human left on earth. The sky turned gray, and a light rain began to fall at sunset. I wondered if it was shameful for a man to cry when even the heavens wept. I recalled old stories of people who turned into fountains from their tears and wished that were my fate, as it would connect me to the deaths of Adrian and Clara. Grief is strange; it colors everything around me and finds meaning in every object. I had wandered far from where I landed and found one of the watchtowers that line the Italian coast. I was relieved to find something built by human hands after seeing only the empty wilderness. I climbed the winding stairs to the guardroom. Fortunately, there were no grim reminders of the people who once lived there. I found a simple bed made of planks and dried corn husks, along with a chest containing some stale biscuits. These items triggered a hunger I had not realized I felt. I was also suffering from a parching thirst caused by the seawater I had swallowed and my physical exhaustion. I felt refreshed and calmed as I ate the simple food and drank the sour wine left in a flask. I lay down on the bed, grateful for the shelter. The earthy scent of the dried leaves was a relief after the smell of seaweed. For a moment, I forgot my loneliness. I stopped thinking about the past or the future and drifted into a peaceful sleep, dreaming of the mountain life and simple country scenes from my childhood."
}

