parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7a79ef3c34c54a836e8dd7f4",
  "rewritten_text": "Do you visit often? I have never returned since. Neither have I. As the moon rose, I remembered the calm expression on his face before he passed, and the pressure of his hand when I spoke his final words. Estella broke the silence. I have often intended to return, but circumstances prevented me. This poor, old place. Moonlight caught the mist and the tears falling from her eyes. Unaware I saw them, she composed herself and asked, Were you wondering why it was left like this? Yes, Estella. The land is mine. It is the only thing I kept. Everything else is gone, but I held onto this despite years of misery. Is it being developed? Finally, yes. I came to say goodbye before it changes. And you, she asked with genuine concern, do you still live abroad? Still. And you are doing well, I hope? I work hard for a living, so yes, I am doing well. I have thought of you often, Estella said. Have you? Lately, yes. For a long time, I suppressed the memory of what I discarded before I understood its value. But now that I am free to remember, I have made room for you in my heart. You have always held a place in mine, I replied. We fell silent until she spoke again. I never imagined I would say goodbye to you while saying goodbye to this place, she said. I am glad I did. Glad to part again? Parting is painful for me; the memory of our last farewell has always been sorrowful. But you told me, God bless you, God forgive you, she replied earnestly. If you could say that then, you will say it now, now that suffering has taught me to understand your heart. I have been bent and broken, but I hope I have been reshaped into something better."
}

