parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2c3f36e2141c6612bf3c4bdb",
  "rewritten_text": "Forget my hat, I want Virginia! the young Duke laughed as they galloped toward the station. Mr. Otis asked the station-master if anyone matching Virginia’s description had been seen, but received no information. The official promised to alert the line and keep a watch. After buying the Duke a new hat from a shopkeeper closing for the night, Mr. Otis rode to Bexley, a village four miles away known for its gypsy encampments. They woke the local policeman, but he knew nothing. After searching the common, they returned to the Chase by eleven, exhausted and devastated. Washington and the twins waited at the gate with lanterns, but there was no sign of Virginia. The gypsies had been found in Brockley meadows, but she was not with them; they explained they had simply miscalculated the date of the Chorton Fair. They were genuinely distressed by her disappearance, grateful for the permission to camp on the estate, and four of them remained to assist in the search. The pond had been dragged and the grounds scoured to no avail. It was clear she was lost for the night. Mr. Otis and the boys walked to the house in despair, followed by the groom. Inside, the servants were terrified, and Mrs. Otis lay on the library sofa, hysterical with anxiety, while the housekeeper tended to her. Mr. Otis insisted she eat and ordered supper for everyone. It was a somber meal; even the twins were uncharacteristically quiet. Afterward, despite the Duke’s protests, Mr. Otis sent everyone to bed, promising to contact Scotland Yard for detectives in the morning."
}

