parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a9a11c81d605108551031532",
  "rewritten_text": "I reasoned backward. If the killers escaped through these windows, they could not have locked them from the inside, yet the police found them secured. Since the windows were locked, they had to be self-locking. I approached the clear window, removed the nail, and tried to lift the sash. It would not budge. I suspected a hidden spring, and finding it confirmed my theory, even if the nails remained a mystery. I pressed the spring but left the sash closed. I replaced the nail and studied it. A killer could close the window, triggering the spring, but they could not have replaced the nail from the outside. The killers must have used the other window. Assuming the springs were identical, there had to be a difference in the nails or how they were fixed. I climbed onto the bed to examine the second window. I found the identical spring behind the headboard. The nail looked just as sturdy and well-fitted as the first. You might think I was stumped, but my logic remained sound. I had followed the trail perfectly, and it led directly to this nail. Despite its appearance, the fact that the trail ended here meant something was wrong. I touched the nail, and its head, along with a small piece of the shank, snapped off in my hand."
}

