parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "185ab6ae664e11b2b59d7f5e",
  "rewritten_text": "It is only four o’clock, but winter has already brought an early sunset. The sky is clear and frosty, lacking clouds to catch the light, yet the air holds a faint rose tint that reflects off the snow-covered ground. I live in a solitary cottage on a vast, empty heath where no human voice reaches me. The desolate plain is blanketed in white, broken only by dark patches atop sharp hillocks where the snow is thin. A few birds peck at the ice covering the pools, hardened by a long, relentless frost. I am in a strange state of mind. I am entirely alone in the world, withered by the blight of misfortune. I know I am dying, and I feel a sense of joy. My pulse races, and my thin hand burns against my cheek; the quick spirit within me is emitting its final sparks. I will not see another winter’s snow or feel the warmth of another summer sun. Convinced of this, I begin to record my tragic history. Perhaps it should die with me, but an indefinable urge drives me, and I am too weak to resist. When I was strong, I believed my story was too sacred and horrific to share, but now, facing death, I feel compelled to reveal its terrors. It is like the grove of the Eumenides, which only the dying may enter; I am like Oedipus, nearing my end. What am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know if anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them after I am gone. I do not address them to you alone, as dwelling on our friendship in this way feels more natural than a private letter. I will tell my story as if for strangers. You have often asked about my solitary life, my tears, and my impenetrable silence. In life, I dared not speak; in death, I unveil the mystery. Others may discard these pages, but to you, Woodville, my kind and affectionate friend, they will be precious memorials of a heartbroken girl who, even in death, is warmed by gratitude. I know your tears will fall upon these words, and while I still live, I thank you for your sympathy."
}

