parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "11a47ee1441584753cf45543",
  "rewritten_text": "The small man possessed a disproportionately wide frame, lending his entire appearance a comical roundness. His feet remained hidden, though a suspicious, horny material occasionally poked through a tear in the base of his vehicle, or more accurately, the crown of his hat. His hands were massive. His hair, quite gray, was pulled into a queue at the back. His nose was remarkably long, hooked, and inflamed; his eyes were full, sharp, and bright; his chin and cheeks, while aged and wrinkled, were wide, puffy, and double; yet no trace of ears could be found anywhere on his head. This peculiar gentleman wore a loose sky-blue satin frock coat with matching tight breeches, secured by silver knee buckles. His vest was a vibrant yellow; a white taffeta cap sat rakishly on his head; and to finish his attire, a blood-red silk scarf wrapped his neck, cascading over his chest in a massive, elaborate bow. Having descended to roughly one hundred feet above the ground, the little man was suddenly gripped by anxiety, seemingly unwilling to get any closer to solid earth. He struggled to heave a bag of sand from his craft, causing his ascent to halt immediately. In a flustered state, he pulled a large morocco wallet from his coat pocket. He weighed it suspiciously, appearing shocked by its heft. Finally, he opened it, removed a bulky letter sealed in red wax and bound with red tape, and dropped it right at the feet of the burgomaster, Superbus Von Underduk. His Excellency bent to retrieve it. However, the aeronaut, still deeply rattled and seemingly finished with his business in Rotterdam, began preparing to leave. Needing to shed weight to rise again, he tossed out half a dozen bags without emptying them, which unfortunately landed squarely on the burgomaster, rolling him over twenty-one times before the entire population of Rotterdam. One should not assume, however, that the great Underduk allowed this insolence from the little man to go unpunished."
}

