  "rewritten_text": "Certainly not, Duff replied. And, to explain the term yokel for the ladies, I assume you mean this attempt was not made by a countryman, Mr. Losberne said with a smile. That is it, sir, Blathers replied. This is all about the robbery, is it? All, the doctor confirmed. Now, what about this boy the servants are discussing? Blathers asked. Nothing at all, the doctor replied. A frightened servant assumed he was involved in the break-in, but it is pure nonsense. That is easily dismissed, Duff remarked. Blathers nodded, playing with his handcuffs. Who is the boy? What is his story? Where did he come from? He did not just fall from the sky, did he? Of course not, the doctor replied, glancing nervously at the ladies. I know his history, but we can discuss that later. You would like to see where the thieves made their attempt first, I assume? Certainly, Blathers agreed. We should inspect the premises before questioning the servants. That is standard procedure. Lights were brought, and the group went to the small room at the end of the hall to examine the window. They checked the lawn, inspected the shutter with a candle, traced footprints with a lantern, and poked the bushes with a pitchfork. Afterward, Giles and Brittles reenacted their roles in the previous night’s events six times, contradicting each other more frequently with each performance. Once finished, Blathers and Duff cleared the room for a private, solemn consultation. Meanwhile, the doctor paced the next room anxiously while Mrs. Maylie and Rose watched him. Upon my word, he said, stopping after many rapid turns, I hardly know what to do."
}

