  "request_id": "0825260f77fadcf3cad9ac92",
  "rewritten_text": "Everyone compares the voice to a language they do not know. The Frenchman thinks it sounds Spanish, though he admits he would only recognize words if he knew the language. The Dutchman claims it was French, yet he required an interpreter to testify. The Englishman believes it was German, a language he does not speak. The Spaniard is certain it was English, relying solely on the tone since he knows no English. The Italian thinks it was Russian, despite never having spoken to a Russian. A second Frenchman disagrees with the first, insisting it was Italian, though he also relies only on the intonation because he does not know that language. How incredibly strange that voice must have been for people from across Europe to find nothing familiar in its sound. You might suggest it was the voice of an Asian or African, though such people are rare in Paris. Regardless, consider three points: one witness calls it harsh rather than shrill, two others describe it as quick and uneven, and no one could identify any actual words. Dupin continued, I do not know what you make of this, but I believe the logical conclusions drawn from these descriptions of the voices are enough to form a suspicion that should guide our investigation. I mean that these are the only logical conclusions, and the suspicion follows inevitably. I will not reveal my suspicion yet, but keep in mind that it gave a clear direction to my search of the room. Let us imagine ourselves in that room now. What should we look for first? The means the murderers used to escape. It is safe to say that neither of us believes in the supernatural."
}

