parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1298a429b9664a1c3e3b4bab",
  "rewritten_text": "Since we first boarded the boat, I had been watching carefully for any sign that we were being followed. I saw nothing suspicious. We were definitely not being trailed by another vessel; had we been, I would have steered toward the shore to force them to reveal their intentions. We traveled without any interference. He wore his boat-cloak and looked like a natural part of the surroundings. Surprisingly, he was the least anxious among us, perhaps due to the difficult life he had led. He was not indifferent, as he expressed a hope that his gentleman would thrive abroad, but he refused to worry about danger until it was directly in front of him. If you knew what it was like to sit here with me and smoke after being locked between four walls for so long, you would envy me, he told me. I replied that I understood the value of freedom. He shook his head, insisting that I could not truly appreciate it without having been imprisoned myself, though he refused to let his mood darken. It seemed strange that he would risk his life for his goals, but I realized that for a man like him, a life without danger might feel unnatural. He confirmed this, explaining that while he had grown wealthy on the other side of the world, he had always looked toward England. There, he was just Magwitch, ignored by everyone, whereas here, his presence would cause alarm if he were discovered. I told him that if all went well, he would be safe and free within a few hours. He took a long breath and said he hoped so, and I asked if he truly believed it."
}

