  "rewritten_text": "Jane admitted some surprise at the engagement, yet she spoke more of her sincere wishes for their joy than her shock, nor would she let Elizabeth convince her it was unlikely. Kitty and Lydia felt no jealousy toward Miss Lucas, as Mr. Collins was merely a clergyman, and they viewed the news only as gossip to share in Meryton. Lady Lucas took obvious pleasure in returning the favor to Mrs. Bennet by boasting of a well-married daughter, visiting Longbourn frequently to express her delight, despite Mrs. Bennet’s bitter glares and spiteful comments. A new reserve grew between Elizabeth and Charlotte, leaving them silent on the matter, and Elizabeth felt certain their former intimacy was gone. Disappointed in Charlotte, she turned with deeper affection toward her sister, whose integrity she trusted completely, though she worried increasingly for Jane’s happiness, as a week had passed since Bingley’s departure with no word of his return. Jane had replied promptly to Caroline’s letter and waited anxiously for a response. On Tuesday, Mr. Collins sent a formal letter of gratitude to their father, written with the gravity of a long-term houseguest. Having fulfilled that duty, he wrote ecstatically of his engagement to their neighbor, Miss Lucas, explaining that his eagerness to revisit Longbourn in two weeks was driven by a desire to see her. He noted that Lady Catherine fully supported the union and urged a swift wedding, which he hoped would persuade his dear Charlotte to set an early date. Mrs. Bennet no longer welcomed Mr. Collins’s return to Hertfordshire; she complained of it as loudly as her husband. She found it bizarre and highly inconvenient that he intended to stay at Longbourn rather than Lucas Lodge, resenting the presence of guests while she felt unwell, especially those as tiresome as lovers."
