parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e9a2b494c008d2972e880964",
  "rewritten_text": "I cannot say how long I remained in that state, but it must have been quite a while. When I finally regained consciousness, dawn was breaking. The balloon was at a massive height over a vast ocean, with no land in sight anywhere. Surprisingly, I did not feel the agony one might expect. Instead, a sense of calm bordering on madness took over as I surveyed my situation. I examined my hands, wondering why the veins were swollen and my fingernails were black. I checked my head repeatedly, relieved to confirm it had not grown larger than the balloon itself. I then checked my pockets and felt strangely annoyed to find my notebook and toothpick case missing. I noticed pain in my left ankle, and a vague awareness of my predicament began to dawn on me. Oddly, I felt no horror. If anything, I felt a smug satisfaction at how cleverly I would escape this mess, never doubting my survival. I sat in deep thought for a few minutes, making the thoughtful gestures of a man pondering a complex problem in his armchair. Once I had gathered my thoughts, I carefully reached behind my back to unfasten the large iron buckle on my trousers. The three teeth of the buckle were rusty and stiff, but I managed to set them at a right angle. Holding the buckle in my teeth, I slowly untied my necktie, resting several times before finally succeeding."
}

