parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "841065130e2f260a9d5e7146",
  "rewritten_text": "“Oh, I misunderstood you. I thought you said 'anybody,' and I assumed that meant everyone.” “It does—anybody of noble birth, preferably royal.” “That seems reasonable,” the abbot said, hoping to keep the peace. “It is unlikely such a gift would be used for anyone less than those born to greatness. Our King Arthur—” “Would you like to know about him?” the enchanter interrupted. “Most gladly,” the abbot replied. Everyone was immediately filled with awe again, the foolish people. They watched the magic spells with interest and looked at me with a smug expression when the announcement came: “The king is tired from the hunt and has been sleeping soundly in his palace for two hours.” “God bless him,” the abbot said, crossing himself. “May that sleep refresh his body and soul.” “That would be nice if he were actually sleeping,” I said, “but the king is not sleeping; he is riding.” This caused trouble, as it created a conflict of authority. No one knew who to believe, though I still had some credibility. The magician, feeling insulted, said, “I have seen many prophets and magicians, but never one who could see the truth without using spells.” “You have lived in the woods too long. I use spells myself, as these brothers know, but only when it is truly necessary.” I knew how to hold my own when it came to sarcasm, and that comment made him squirm. The abbot asked about the queen and the court, and the magician replied, “They are all asleep, overcome by fatigue, just like the king.” I countered, “That is another lie. Half of them are busy with their own affairs, and the queen and the others are not sleeping; they are riding. Perhaps you can show off a bit more and tell us where the king, the queen, and their companions are going?” “They are sleeping now, as I said, but tomorrow they will ride toward the sea.” “And where will they be the day after tomorrow at sunset?” “Far to the north of Camelot, with half their journey complete.” “That is another lie, by at least a hundred and fifty miles.”"
}

