parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "abf02c7e421a32e2596311bf",
  "rewritten_text": "Jane admitted she was surprised by the engagement, though she focused more on wishing the couple happiness than on her own shock. Elizabeth could not convince her that the match was unlikely. Kitty and Lydia did not envy Miss Lucas, as Mr. Collins was merely a clergyman; to them, it was simply gossip to share in Meryton. Lady Lucas felt triumphant that she could now boast of a well-married daughter to Mrs. Bennet. She visited Longbourn frequently to share her joy, despite Mrs. Bennet’s bitter reactions. A sense of awkwardness grew between Elizabeth and Charlotte, leaving them unable to discuss the matter. Elizabeth realized their friendship had lost its former intimacy. Disappointed in Charlotte, she turned to her sister Jane with renewed affection, worrying about her future as Bingley had been gone a week with no word of his return. Jane had replied to Caroline’s letter and was anxiously awaiting a response. On Tuesday, Mr. Collins sent a formal letter of thanks to Mr. Bennet, expressing deep gratitude. He then excitedly announced his engagement to Miss Lucas, explaining that he planned to return to Longbourn in two weeks to see her. He noted that Lady Catherine fully supported the marriage and hoped for a quick wedding, which he expected would convince Charlotte to set an early date. Mrs. Bennet was no longer pleased by Mr. Collins’s return. She complained that his visit to Longbourn instead of Lucas Lodge was inconvenient and annoying. She disliked having guests while she felt unwell, and she found lovers to be the most irritating company of all."
}

