parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b2cc8706afc9b59184c2c8a4",
  "rewritten_text": "When I finally caught Tom alone, I asked what his plan had been for the escape—specifically, what he intended to do if we successfully freed a man who was already legally free. He explained that his original vision was to take Jim downriver on the raft, seeking adventures until we reached the mouth of the river. Then, we would reveal his freedom, return him home in style on a steamboat, compensate him for his time, and arrange a grand celebration with a torchlight procession and a brass band to make him a hero. I decided things turned out well enough as they were. We freed Jim quickly, and once the family realized how much he had helped nurse Tom, they treated him with great kindness, providing him with plenty of food and leisure. In the sickroom, Tom gave Jim forty dollars for his patience as our prisoner. Jim was overjoyed, exclaiming that his earlier signs had proven true and that he knew he would be rich again. Tom then suggested the three of us sneak out soon to get gear and head into the Territory for a few weeks of adventure. I agreed, though I worried I lacked the money for supplies, assuming my father had returned to steal my funds from Judge Thatcher. Tom assured me the money was still there, over six thousand dollars, and that my father had not returned."
}

