  "request_id": "b6ac1e8ef84f026c8cfd5283",
  "rewritten_text": "April 12th. The balloon changed direction as I had hoped, turning sharply east at the twentieth parallel south to follow the moon’s path. This shift caused the car to sway noticeably for several hours. April 13th. The loud, crackling noise returned, leaving me baffled. The earth appeared much smaller, covering only twenty-five degrees of my view. The moon was directly overhead and hidden, and I made little eastward progress. April 14th. The earth shrank rapidly. I realized the balloon was traveling directly toward the moon’s closest point to earth. I spent much time condensing the air to breathe. April 15th. The earth’s features were no longer visible. Around noon, that terrifying sound returned, growing louder and more intense. As I braced for disaster, the car shook violently while a massive, flaming object roared past like thunder. I eventually concluded it was a volcanic rock ejected from the moon, similar to the meteoric stones found on earth. April 16th. Looking through the side windows, I was thrilled to see the edge of the moon’s disk peeking out from behind the balloon."
}

