parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0779637af262a33888a0bb9a",
  "rewritten_text": "I turn now to my own history. My early years offer little to recount, yet I must linger on my childhood to show how the loss of one hope rendered my life a void, and how the destruction of my only cherished affection extinguished my spirit. My aunt differed greatly from my father. While not malicious, she possessed the coldest heart imaginable, entirely devoid of warmth. She assumed my guardianship out of duty, yet having lived long in solitude, she would not permit my youthful presence to disrupt her peace. Unmarried, she had spent five years alone on her inherited estate by Loch Lomond in Scotland. Though my father requested she live with me at our family home near Richmond, she refused. Once she settled her brother’s affairs, she left England, taking me to her Scottish residence. Until I turned eight, my mother’s former servant, who had joined us in our seclusion, cared for me. I lived in a distant wing, seeing my aunt only twice daily: once at noon in my nursery, and again after her dinner. She never showed affection, appearing anxious that my childish behavior might disturb her. My nurse coached me thoroughly before these visits, and the dread inspired by my aunt’s icy demeanor ensured I remained perfectly still and obedient. Under my nurse’s guidance, I roamed the park and fields freely. Born of profound love, I was a sensitive child, possessed by an intense passion for everything around me, even the inanimate."
}

