parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2c92a0a99c1f2091249db06d",
  "rewritten_text": "Though it is merely four, the winter sun has vanished. The sky is clear and freezing, lacking clouds to catch the light, yet the air holds a faint pink hue that mirrors onto the snow-dusted earth. I dwell in a solitary cottage upon a vast, empty heath, isolated from all human sound. The barren plain is blanketed in white, broken only by dark spots where the midday sun cleared the sharp hilltops. A few birds forage on the frozen pools, remnants of a long-standing frost. My mental state is peculiar. I am entirely alone, withered by the touch of misfortune. Knowing my end is near, I feel a strange joy. My pulse races, and my thin hand burns against my cheek; the flickering spirit within me is casting its final sparks. I will not witness another winter or feel the summer sun again, and with this certainty, I begin to record my tragic story. Perhaps this history should perish with me, but an indefinable urge compels me, and I lack the strength to resist. When I was vibrant, I believed my tale held a sacred horror that forbade speech, yet now, facing death, I reveal its dark secrets. It is a grove reserved for the dying, and like Oedipus, I approach my end. What am I setting down? I must gather my thoughts. I assume only you, my friend, will read these pages after I pass. I write as if for strangers, though I cherish our bond. You have frequently questioned my isolation, my tears, and my guarded silence. I dared not speak while living, but in death, I disclose the truth. While others may discard these pages, to you, Woodville, they will be precious—a testament from a broken-hearted girl who, even in death, feels warmth from your kindness. I know you will weep over these accounts of my suffering, and for that sympathy, I am grateful while I still draw breath."
}

