parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2e22e2058be3426c02383ba2",
  "rewritten_text": "Enough of this. I will begin my story; it is my final task, and I hope I have the strength to finish it. I have committed no crimes; my mistakes are easily forgiven, as they came from poor judgment rather than malice. I believe few could claim they would have avoided the misfortunes that have claimed me, had they been in my place. My fate was dictated by a cruel necessity—a force stronger than any human power—that bound me, once a person of joy and goodness, to a misery that will only end in death. But I digress. I will pause to compose myself and try to lose my current sorrow in the memories of the past. I was born in England to a nobleman. Having lost his own father early, he was raised by an indulgent mother who gave him everything he wanted. He attended Eton and college, where he was granted large sums of money, gaining the independence typical of a wealthy schoolboy. These circumstances allowed his personality to take deep root. Because he was always permitted to act on his own impulses, his character developed early, showing both potential for virtue and the seeds of future misfortune. His reckless spending, which he mistook for passion, often appeared as great generosity. While he was eager to help others, he never had to sacrifice his own desires to do so. He gave away his time and money freely, but only because he had plenty of both. I cannot say if he would have been selfish had he ever been forced to choose between his needs and those of others, but that test never came. He was raised in constant prosperity, surrounded by people who loved him and sought only to please him."
}

