parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "262f7c0f0c0412d27419b0d2",
  "rewritten_text": "We endured several dismal hours until the eleven o’clock start of the trial. Since my father and family were required to testify, I went with them to the courtroom. Throughout this pathetic charade of justice, I underwent agonizing torment. It was to be determined if my own reckless curiosity and forbidden experiments would lead to the deaths of two people: one an innocent, joyful infant, the other a victim of a murder made uniquely horrific by its circumstances. Justine was a virtuous young woman whose life held great promise; now, all of that was to be erased by a shameful execution, and I was the culprit! I would have gladly confessed to the crime myself, but my absence at the time meant such a claim would be dismissed as insanity, failing to save the woman who suffered because of me. Justine looked composed. Clad in mourning, her naturally charming face held a profound, striking beauty. She seemed secure in her innocence and remained steady, despite the thousands who stared and cursed her, their sympathy replaced by the perceived enormity of her alleged crime. Her calm was clearly forced; having previously been accused of guilt due to her confusion, she now summoned a display of bravery. Upon entering the court, she scanned the room and spotted us. A tear clouded her vision, but she regained her composure, her sorrowful gaze affirming her total innocence. The trial commenced, and after the prosecutor outlined the charges, witnesses were summoned. A series of bizarre facts emerged that might have shaken anyone lacking my absolute certainty of her innocence. She had been out all night when the murder occurred, and a market-woman had spotted her near the location where the child’s body was later discovered. When questioned about her presence there, she had appeared odd and provided only a muddled, incoherent reply. She arrived home around eight o’clock, and when asked about her whereabouts, she claimed to have been searching for the boy, anxiously inquiring if there was any news of him."
}

