parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e7eeea1612b731b938cb4f14",
  "rewritten_text": "My friend carefully drove a peg into the ground exactly where the beetle had landed, then pulled out a tape measure. He attached one end to the nearest point on the tree trunk and stretched it out to the peg, continuing for another fifty feet in the same line while Jupiter cleared away the brush with a scythe. At that spot, he drove a second peg and marked a rough circle about four feet wide around it. He handed spades to Jupiter and me, urging us to start digging immediately. Honestly, I had no desire for this task. Night was falling, and I was exhausted, but I saw no way out and did not want to upset my friend. I considered forcing him home, but I knew Jupiter would never help me fight his master. I assumed Legrand had fallen victim to local superstitions about buried treasure, fueled by the discovery of the beetle and Jupiter’s insistence that it was made of real gold. His talk about the insect being the key to his fortune seemed like the delusions of a troubled mind. Still, I decided to cooperate, hoping that digging would quickly prove his theories wrong. We lit our lanterns and began working with surprising energy. As the light shone on us and our tools, I realized how strange and suspicious we must have looked to any outsider. We dug steadily for two hours."
}

