parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8a6c0755665bcd775134c2a5",
  "rewritten_text": "Smallpox! I rushed to the king and murmured, \"Leave at once, sire! The woman suffers from the same plague that ravaged Camelot two years past.\" He stood firm. \"I shall stay and assist.\" I pressed him again, \"King, you must depart.\" He replied, \"Your intentions are good, but a king cannot show fear, nor a knight ignore those in need. I will not leave. You must go, for the Church forbids your presence here, and they will punish you severely if they learn of your intrusion.\" It was a perilous situation that could claim his life, but arguing was futile. If he felt his knightly honor was involved, he would not budge. I ceased my protests. The woman spoke, \"Kind sir, would you climb the ladder and tell me what you see? Do not fear to speak, for a mother's heart can break no further.\" \"Stay,\" the king commanded, \"and feed the woman. I will go.\" He set down his pack. I turned to move, but the king had already begun. He paused, looking at a man lying in the shadows who had remained silent. \"Is this your husband?\" the king inquired. \"Yes.\" \"Is he sleeping?\" \"Thank God, yes—for three hours. I am so grateful for his rest.\" I added, \"We will be quiet so as not to wake him.\" \"You need not worry, for he is dead.\" \"Dead?\" \"Yes, and what a victory that is! No one can harm or insult him now. He is in heaven, or perhaps hell, where he will find no abbots or bishops to torment him. We were childhood sweethearts and married for twenty-five years, never apart until today. Think of the years we spent loving and suffering together. This morning, he lost his senses and imagined we were children wandering through happy fields again. In that innocent state, he drifted further away, eventually crossing into the unknown and vanishing from mortal sight.\""
}

