parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5f163848af9b0b95f6a7b6b1",
  "rewritten_text": "Sir Walter had secured an excellent residence in Camden Place, a grand and elevated location befitting a man of his standing, and both he and Elizabeth were quite content there. Anne arrived with a heavy heart, dreading a long confinement and silently questioning when she might depart. Yet, the surprisingly warm reception she encountered lifted her spirits. Her father and sister greeted her kindly, eager to display their new home and its appointments, and they appreciated her presence at the dinner table. Mrs. Clay was as charming and smiling as ever, though her manners felt routine. While Anne had anticipated such behavior from her, the genuine pleasantness of her father and sister was unforeseen. They were in high spirits and eager to share their news, showing little interest in Anne’s own experiences. After fishing for compliments about how much they were missed in their former home—which Anne could not provide—they quickly turned the conversation to their own affairs, focusing entirely on Bath rather than Uppercross or Kellynch. They assured her that Bath exceeded their expectations in every way. Their house was clearly the finest in Camden Place, with drawing-rooms that surpassed all others in layout and decor. They were highly sought after, receiving constant visits from strangers despite their efforts to limit introductions. Anne understood why they were so pleased, yet she could not help but sigh at her father’s lack of regret for his former responsibilities and his vanity regarding such trivial urban matters. She watched with a mix of amusement and sadness as Elizabeth proudly paced between the folding doors, boasting of the space in a house that seemed tiny compared to the grandeur of Kellynch Hall. Beyond these comforts, they also had the company of Mr. Elliot."
}

