  "rewritten_text": "Anne soon had enough opportunities to observe the group, though she kept her opinions to herself to avoid conflict at home. While Louisa seemed the favorite, Anne suspected Captain Wentworth was not in love with either sister. The women were infatuated with him, though it was merely a fleeting admiration that might eventually turn into genuine love. Charles Hayter appeared to feel ignored, yet Henrietta seemed torn between the two men. Anne wished she could warn them of the potential consequences, though she believed no one acted with malice. She was relieved that Captain Wentworth seemed unaware of the pain he caused; he showed no arrogance and likely remained ignorant of Charles Hayter’s feelings. He was simply wrong to accept the attentions of two women simultaneously. Eventually, Charles Hayter withdrew, staying away from Uppercross for three days and declining a dinner invitation. When Mr. Musgrove found him studying intensely, the family worried he was overworking himself. Mary hoped Henrietta had rejected him, while her husband expected his return. Anne simply thought Charles was acting wisely. One morning, while the men were out shooting, the Musgrove sisters visited the cottage. It was a beautiful November day, and they stopped by to announce they were going for a long walk, assuming Mary would not want to join. When Mary insisted on coming, clearly offended by the suggestion that she was not a good walker, Anne could tell the sisters did not want her company. She marveled at the family habit of forcing everyone to participate in every activity, regardless of whether it was convenient or desired."
}

