parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ff93169baadf969823f479ff",
  "rewritten_text": "I mentioned he was a poet; at twenty-three, he released his debut poem, which the entire nation greeted with fervent acclaim. Fortune favored him constantly, establishing a reputation with unprecedented speed and universality. The public praised the very verses that captivated scholars in their studies, and not a single critic dissented. During this peak of fame, he met Elinor. She was a beautiful young heiress living with her guardian, and from their first meeting, they seemed destined for each other. While Elinor lacked Woodville’s genius, she possessed a noble, generous spirit, elevated by her youth and the universal affection she inspired to recognize only virtue. She was charming, with candid manners and deep blue eyes shining with the brilliance of combined sensibility and wisdom. They were perfect matches and quickly fell in love. Woodville experienced the joy of romance for the first time, while Elinor was thrilled to hold the heart of such a celebrated man. Could such a union produce anything but pure happiness? Woodville was a poet, courted by every social circle and the focus of every gaze, yet he was merely a clergyman’s son, while Elinor was a wealthy heiress. Her guardian did not object to their bond, as Woodville’s merit was too great to be dismissed for lack of fortune. However, her father’s will forbade her marriage until she reached her majority, a condition tied to her inheritance. Having just turned twenty, the couple had to accept this wait. They remained inseparable, living in near-paradise, studying together and crafting future plans, finding such delight in each other’s presence that they barely lamented the postponement of their wedding. Woodville’s fame continued to grow, and Elinor flourished in wisdom under his guidance. With only two months until Elinor turned twenty-one, all arrangements for their marriage were complete. How can I describe the ruin of such happiness? Yet the world would not be the place of sorrow it is if such angelic beings were permitted to remain together. One could search the globe and never find the perfect bliss their marriage promised; the established order of our miserable existence would have required a total upheaval to allow for such absolute joy."
}

