  "request_id": "df97fe2656bd90829433216a",
  "rewritten_text": "After sharing their distress over the disastrous outcome, which Elizabeth viewed as inevitable and Jane could not deny was possible, Elizabeth pressed for more details. She asked what Colonel Forster had said and if he had suspected anything before the elopement, given how often the couple was seen together. Jane explained that the Colonel had noticed some mutual interest, particularly from Lydia, but felt no alarm. He had been very kind and was already traveling to see them to express his regret before the rumors of their flight to Scotland surfaced. Elizabeth asked if Denny knew of their plans or if he still believed Wickham would not marry her. Jane replied that when questioned, Denny claimed ignorance and refused to share his true thoughts, though he no longer insisted they would not marry, which gave her some hope. Elizabeth asked if they had truly believed the couple was married until the Colonel arrived. Jane admitted they had no reason to suspect otherwise, though she had worried about Lydia’s happiness because of Wickham’s past behavior. Their parents only saw the match as imprudent. Kitty later revealed that Lydia had hinted at this in her last letter, having known of their affection for weeks. When Elizabeth asked if this predated their time in Brighton, Jane said she believed not. Elizabeth then asked if the Colonel understood Wickham’s true character. Jane admitted he no longer spoke well of him, noting that Wickham was considered reckless and was rumored to have left Meryton with significant debts. Elizabeth lamented that if they had only been more open about what they knew of him, this might have been avoided. Jane agreed it might have been better, but noted that exposing someone’s past faults without knowing their current state of mind had felt wrong."
}

