parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "647c2c811f0c0b019ba85937",
  "rewritten_text": "I recall the conditions during our nineteenth-century civil war. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day in gold, while in the South, he received fifty in nearly worthless Confederate currency. Since a pair of overalls cost three dollars in the North and seventy-five in the South, Northern wages held twice the purchasing power. I enjoyed seeing our new coins circulating among the local workers, including milrays, mills, cents, nickels, and some silver. Gold was only found at the goldsmith’s bank. While Marco was arguing with a shopkeeper over salt, I asked the goldsmith to change a twenty-dollar gold piece. After testing the coin and questioning me extensively about my identity and history, the banker seemed stumped. I continued to volunteer absurd details about my life—my dog, my wife’s religion, and an acquaintance with extra thumbs—until the curious man was satisfied, if slightly annoyed. He respected my wealth and refrained from being rude, though he took his frustration out on his staff. Changing the twenty clearly strained the bank’s resources, much like asking a small-town shopkeeper to break a two-thousand-dollar bill. The goldsmith likely wondered how a traveler could carry such a fortune, as he followed me to the door and watched me leave with a look of reverent admiration."
}

