parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0674092b7f20ed45bbc70e3b",
  "rewritten_text": "A few days later, Virginia and her companion were riding through Brockley meadows when she tore her riding habit on a hedge. Returning home, she attempted to sneak in through the back stairs to avoid being seen. Passing the open door of the Tapestry Chamber, she thought she saw her mother’s maid and looked in to request a repair. To her shock, the Canterville Ghost sat by the window, watching the autumn leaves drift by. He leaned his head on his hand, appearing so profoundly miserable that Virginia, despite her initial urge to flee, felt a surge of pity and decided to offer comfort. Her footsteps were so quiet and his sorrow so deep that he did not notice her until she spoke. I am sorry for you, she said, but my brothers return to school tomorrow, and if you behave, no one will bother you. It is ridiculous to ask me to behave, he replied, turning in surprise toward the girl. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and wander at night; that is my purpose. That is no reason to exist, and you know you have been wicked, she countered. Mrs. Umney told us you killed your wife. I admit it, the ghost said petulantly, but it was a private family matter. It is wrong to kill anyone, Virginia insisted with a touch of stern morality. I despise such abstract judgment, he snapped. My wife was plain, could not starch my ruffs, and was a terrible cook. She even ruined a magnificent deer I had hunted. It is all over now, though I still think it was unfair of her brothers to starve me to death in retaliation."
}

