parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4cb6c42341845a8727291e82",
  "rewritten_text": "It was well past noon when he woke. His valet had checked on him repeatedly, puzzled by his master’s late slumber. When the bell finally rang, Victor entered quietly with tea and a stack of letters on a Sevres china tray, pulling back the olive-satin curtains to reveal the tall windows. You slept well this morning, Monsieur, he said with a smile. What time is it, Victor? Dorian Gray asked sleepily. A quarter past one, Monsieur. It was so late. He sat up, sipped his tea, and glanced at his mail. One letter from Lord Henry, delivered by hand, he set aside. He opened the others listlessly: invitations to dinners, private viewings, and charity concerts—the usual flurry of social obligations for a fashionable young man. There was a steep bill for a silver toilet set he dared not show his old-fashioned guardians, who failed to grasp that in this age, luxuries are necessities. There were also polite offers from money-lenders on Jermyn Street. After ten minutes, he rose, donned an embroidered cashmere dressing gown, and entered the onyx bathroom. The cool water refreshed him, and his recent troubles seemed to fade, feeling as unreal as a dream. Once dressed, he went to the library for a light breakfast by the open window. It was a beautiful day, the air heavy with spice. A bee buzzed around a bowl of yellow roses. He felt entirely content. Suddenly, his gaze landed on the screen hiding the portrait, and he flinched. Too cold for you, Monsieur? his valet asked, placing an omelette on the table. Should I close the window? Dorian shook his head. I am not cold, he murmured."
}

