  "rewritten_text": "There was no joy in the small town that quiet Saturday afternoon. The Harper and Aunt Polly families were in deep mourning, filled with grief and tears. An unusual stillness hung over the village, and the residents went about their business distractedly, sighing often. Even the children found the holiday a burden, eventually abandoning their games. Becky Thatcher wandered the empty schoolyard, feeling miserable. Finding no comfort, she whispered to herself, wishing she still had the brass knob to remember him by, and choked back a sob. She stood where they had last spoken, regretting her harsh words and mourning that she would never see him again. Overcome with sadness, she walked away in tears. Soon, a group of Tom and Joe’s friends gathered by the fence. They spoke in hushed, reverent tones about the last things the boys had said or done, pointing out exactly where they had stood. Each child recounted their final interactions, claiming they felt a strange, ominous sensation at the time that they only now understood. A debate broke out over who had seen the boys last, with many children competing for the grim honor. Those who could prove they were the final people to speak to the lost boys gained a sense of sacred importance, envied by the others. One boy even tried to claim status by bragging that Tom Sawyer had once beaten him up, but the others dismissed this, as most of them had shared that experience. The group eventually drifted away, continuing to share memories of their lost friends in hushed, awed voices."
}

