parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f4b33edc7ac80536aae00d18",
  "rewritten_text": "So, what is the result? What impression have I made on your imagination? I felt a chill as Dupin asked me this. A madman, I said, must have committed this crime—some raving lunatic escaped from a nearby asylum. In some ways, he replied, your idea is not irrelevant. But the voices of madmen, even in their worst fits, never match the strange voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to a nation, and their language, however incoherent, always retains the structure of speech. Besides, the hair I now hold is not human. I pulled this tuft from Madame L’Espanaye’s clenched fingers. Tell me what you make of it. Dupin, I said, completely unnerved, this hair is most unusual; it is not human. I have not claimed it is, he said, but before we decide, look at this sketch. It is a copy of what witnesses described as bruises and deep nail indentations on Mademoiselle L’Espanaye’s throat. You will see, my friend continued, spreading the paper, that this drawing shows a firm, fixed hold. There is no slipping. Each finger kept its grip until the victim died. Try to place your fingers into these impressions. I tried and failed. We are not giving this a fair trial, he said. The paper is flat, but a throat is cylindrical. Here is a piece of wood the size of a throat. Wrap the drawing around it and try again. I did, but the difficulty was even more obvious. This, I said, is not the mark of a human hand. Read this passage from Cuvier, Dupin replied. It was a detailed anatomical account of the large, tawny orangutan of the East Indian islands. The giant size, immense strength, agility, ferocity, and imitative nature of these animals are well known. I immediately understood the horror of the murder. The description of the fingers, I said after reading, matches this drawing perfectly."
}

