parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "59c2bc80a394866300e99707",
  "rewritten_text": "Winters near Sullivan’s Island are rarely harsh, and a fire is seldom needed in autumn. However, in mid-October of that year, a day of unusual cold arrived. Just before sunset, I pushed through the evergreens to my friend’s cabin. I had not visited in weeks, as I lived in Charleston nine miles away, and travel was far more difficult then. Finding no answer to my knock, I retrieved the hidden key, unlocked the door, and entered. A fire blazed on the hearth, a welcome surprise. I removed my coat, sat by the logs, and waited for my host. He arrived after dark, greeting me warmly. Jupiter, grinning broadly, hurried to prepare marsh-hens for dinner. Legrand was in one of his typical states of intense excitement. He had discovered a new genus of bivalve and, with Jupiter’s help, had captured a beetle he believed was entirely new, though he wanted my opinion on it the next day. Why not tonight? I asked, warming my hands and wishing the beetle elsewhere. If I had known you were coming, Legrand replied, but it has been so long. I foolishly lent the specimen to Lieutenant G from the fort, so you cannot see it until morning. Stay the night, and I will have Jup fetch it at sunrise. It is the most beautiful thing in existence. What, sunrise? I asked. No, the bug! he insisted. It is brilliant gold, the size of a large hickory nut, with two jet-black spots near one end and a longer one at the other. The antennae are... There is no tin in it, Massa Will, I keep telling you, Jupiter interrupted. The bug is solid gold, inside and out, except for its wings. I have never felt a bug so heavy in my life."
}

