parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f0c2a55900d68e33bd1a0fb2",
  "rewritten_text": "They brought in a distinguished-looking older gentleman and a younger man with his arm in a sling. The crowd cheered and laughed loudly, but I saw nothing funny about it. I expected the duke and the king to look nervous, but they did not. The duke acted perfectly content, while the king looked down at the newcomers with a pained expression, as if the existence of such frauds broke his heart. He played the part perfectly. Many prominent citizens gathered around the king to show their support. The older man looked completely confused. He soon began to speak, and I noticed immediately that he sounded like a genuine Englishman, unlike the king’s imitation. He addressed the crowd, saying, This is an unexpected surprise, and I admit I am unprepared to respond. My brother and I have faced misfortune; he has a broken arm, and our luggage was mistakenly left at a town upriver last night. I am Harvey Wilks, brother of Peter, and this is my brother William, who is deaf and mute and cannot use signs well with only one hand. We are who we claim to be, and I will prove it once our luggage arrives. Until then, we will wait at the hotel. As he and his brother left, the king laughed and mocked them, calling the broken arm and lost luggage convenient excuses for a fraud who had not learned sign language. The king laughed, and most of the crowd joined in. Only a few did not, including the doctor, a sharp-looking lawyer named Levi Bell who had just arrived from the steamboat, and a large, rough man who had been listening intently to both sides."
}

