parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8463bdfeeb06eee3906b2eee",
  "rewritten_text": "Those elderly, frail clerks were always seen bowing to the public, and it was widely believed that once they bowed a customer out, they continued bowing in the empty office until the next one arrived. The barrister realized the banker would not have spoken so firmly without moral certainty. Though the news was difficult to accept, he managed to process it. \"Now,\" Mr. Stryver said, gesturing toward the Temple, \"my strategy is to prove you all wrong.\" It was a classic courtroom tactic that brought him relief. \"You will not make me the one in the wrong, young lady,\" he decided; \"I will do that for you.\" When Mr. Lorry visited at ten that night, Mr. Stryver was surrounded by books and papers, acting as if the morning's topic was the furthest thing from his mind. He even feigned surprise at seeing Lorry, appearing entirely preoccupied. \"Well!\" said Lorry, after thirty minutes of failing to steer the conversation. \"I have been to Soho.\" \"Soho?\" Stryver repeated coldly. \"Oh, right! What was I thinking?\" \"I am certain my previous advice was correct,\" Lorry insisted. \"My opinion is confirmed.\" \"I assure you,\" Stryver replied with forced friendliness, \"I am sorry for your sake and the father's. I know this is a sensitive subject; let us drop it.\" \"I don't understand,\" Lorry said. \"I imagine not,\" Stryver replied dismissively. \"It doesn't matter.\" \"But it does matter,\" Lorry urged. \"It truly doesn't. Having mistakenly assumed there was sense and ambition where there was none, I am glad to be rid of the error. Young women have made such mistakes before, only to regret them in poverty. I am sorry it ended, as it would have been a poor worldly move for me, yet I am relieved it is over for the same reason—it is obvious I could have gained nothing from it.\""
}

