  "rewritten_text": "If he asked why I wanted it or what right I had to it, I would tell him—though he cared little for such dreams—that I had loved Estella long and deeply. Even though I had lost her and faced a lonely life, everything concerning her remained more precious to me than anything else. Seeing Mr. Jaggers stand silent and unmoved, I turned to Wemmick. I know you have a kind heart, I said. I have seen your pleasant home, your father, and the cheerful ways you balance your professional life. Please speak for me to Mr. Jaggers and explain that, given the circumstances, he should be more open. The two men exchanged a strange look. I feared Wemmick might be fired, but Mr. Jaggers relaxed into a smile, and Wemmick grew bolder. What is this? Mr. Jaggers asked. You have a father and playful habits? If I keep them separate from work, what does it matter? Wemmick replied. Pip, Mr. Jaggers said, touching my arm with a smile, this man must be the most cunning impostor in London. Not at all, Wemmick countered, I think you are another. They looked at each other again, both seemingly suspicious. You have a pleasant home? Mr. Jaggers asked. Since it does not interfere with business, let it be, Wemmick said. Looking at you, sir, I wonder if you might plan to have a home of your own one day, when you tire of this work. Mr. Jaggers nodded and sighed. Pip, he said, we will not discuss poor dreams; you have fresher experience with such things than I. But regarding this other matter, I will propose a scenario. Remember, I admit nothing. He waited for me to confirm I understood he admitted nothing. Now, Pip, he continued, consider this case. Suppose a woman in such circumstances kept her child hidden and was forced to tell her lawyer, after he insisted he needed to know the facts to prepare her defense."
}

