parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e10ad771ef09fc0915118574",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Merton was quite upset by the second delay of the wedding. Lady Julia, having already purchased her dress, tried hard to convince Sybil to end the engagement. Despite her deep love for her mother, Sybil had committed her life to Lord Arthur and refused to change her mind. Lord Arthur was devastated for days, his nerves completely shattered. Eventually, his common sense returned, and he quickly decided on his next move. Since poison had failed, he concluded that an explosive was the logical solution. He reviewed his list of relatives and decided to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean was a cultured man who loved collecting clocks, which gave Lord Arthur an idea. Finding an explosive device was difficult. The London Directory offered no help, and he knew the police were useless at tracking such things. He suddenly remembered his friend Count Rouvaloff, a young Russian with revolutionary leanings he had met at a party. While the Count claimed to be researching Peter the Great, many suspected he was a Nihilist agent. Lord Arthur visited his Bloomsbury lodgings to ask for help. When Lord Arthur explained his goal, the Count asked if he was getting into politics. Lord Arthur, disliking pretense, admitted he had no interest in social issues and only needed the explosive for a private family matter."
}

