parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8e00242c222fe562e3b1b3ef",
  "rewritten_text": "Soon, his voice called out from above. How much further do I go? How high are you? Legrand asked. Very far, the man replied. I can see the sky through the treetops. Never mind the sky, Legrand said. Look down the trunk and count the branches you have passed on this side. One, two, three, four, five, the man said. I have passed five big branches. Then go up one more, Legrand instructed. A few minutes later, the man announced he had reached the seventh branch. Now, Jupiter, Legrand shouted, clearly excited, climb out on that branch as far as you can. If you see anything unusual, tell me. By now, I was certain my friend had lost his mind, and I worried about how to get him home. While I considered what to do, Jupiter spoke again. I am afraid to go far on this branch. It is mostly dead wood. Is it really dead? Legrand asked, his voice shaking. Yes, sir, it is completely dead, Jupiter replied. What shall I do? Legrand asked, sounding distressed. Do? I said, happy to speak up. Come home and go to bed. It is getting late, and you promised. Jupiter, Legrand shouted, ignoring me, do you hear me? Yes, Master Will, I hear you clearly. Test the wood with your knife and see if it is very rotten, Legrand said. It is rotten, Jupiter replied after a moment, but not too bad. I might be able to go out a little way. By yourself? What do you mean? I mean the bug, Jupiter said. It is a very heavy bug. If I drop it first, the branch will not break under just my weight. You fool! Legrand cried, sounding relieved. What are you talking about? If you drop that beetle, I will break your neck. Do you hear me? Yes, sir, no need to shout at me like that. Listen, Legrand said. If you go out on the branch as far as is safe without dropping the beetle, I will give you a silver dollar when you come down."
}

