  "request_id": "c20cf2f059b89078cdaa4328",
  "rewritten_text": "No, that is for tomorrow; I specifically needed to speak with Knightley today regarding that matter. What a scorching, miserable morning! I walked across the fields, which only made it worse. To find him absent! I am quite displeased. No note, no message left for me. The housekeeper claimed she was unaware of my visit. It is very strange, and no one knew his destination. Perhaps Hartfield, the Abbey Mill, or his woods. Miss Woodhouse, this is unlike our friend Knightley. Can you account for it? Emma feigned agreement that it was indeed odd, offering no defense for him. I cannot fathom, Mrs. Elton remarked, feeling the slight as a wife should, how he could treat you so, of all people! The last person one would expect him to neglect. My dear Mr. E., he surely left a message; his servants must have forgotten. That is likely the case, as the Donwell staff are notoriously clumsy and negligent. I would never tolerate such a person as his Harry at our sideboard. As for Mrs. Hodges, Wright thinks little of her; she promised a recipe and never delivered it. I encountered William Larkins near the house, Mr. Elton added, and he warned me his master was away, but I doubted him. William seemed irritable, complaining that he could rarely speak with his master lately. I care little for William’s grievances, but it is vital that I see Knightley today; this hot, fruitless walk is a serious inconvenience. Emma decided it was best to depart immediately. She was likely expected at home, and her absence might prevent Knightley from further offending Mr. Elton or William Larkins. She was glad when Miss Fairfax insisted on escorting her out, providing a moment for her to speak."
}

