parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "852e16e86d0bc3c2a1c57f01",
  "rewritten_text": "Those who were once our finest patrons now lacked any time to consider us. They claimed they were fully occupied reading about revolutions and tracking the progress of modern thought. If a fire needed stoking, a newspaper sufficed, and as the government faltered, leather and iron seemed to grow more resilient; soon, no bellows in Rotterdam required repair. This situation was unbearable. I became destitute, and with a family to support, my struggles grew overwhelming, leading me to contemplate how best to end my existence. Meanwhile, creditors left me no peace. My home was constantly besieged, making me pace like a caged tiger. Three men in particular harassed me incessantly, threatening legal action. I silently swore the harshest vengeance against them should I ever gain the upper hand; only the anticipation of this retribution kept me from using a blunderbuss to end my life. I decided to hide my anger, offering them empty promises until fate might grant me a chance for revenge. One day, having evaded my creditors and feeling deeply despondent, I wandered aimlessly through quiet streets until I bumped into a bookseller’s stall. Finding a chair, I sat down and aimlessly opened the nearest volume. It was a pamphlet on Speculative Astronomy by Professor Encke or a Frenchman with a similar name. Possessing some slight knowledge of the subject, I became completely engrossed, reading the entire text twice before realizing where I was."
}

