parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7f1756c8da1722bb7bfcd854",
  "rewritten_text": "The insect’s heft was truly extraordinary, and I could scarcely fault Jupiter for his assessment; yet Legrand’s agreement with that view baffled me entirely. When I finished inspecting the beetle, he declared with grandiosity that he had summoned me to seek my advice and help in advancing the designs of Fate and the insect. I interrupted, insisting he was unwell and needed rest, suggesting I stay with him until he recovered from his apparent fever. He told me to check his pulse, which proved perfectly normal. Despite my insistence that he might be ill without fever and should retire, he maintained that his agitation was the only issue and that I could help by alleviating it. He explained that he and Jupiter were planning a trip into the mainland hills and required a trusted companion, insisting I was the only one. He claimed this would settle his nerves regardless of the outcome. When I asked if the beetle was linked to this excursion, he confirmed it was. I refused to participate in such a ridiculous endeavor, prompting him to say they would proceed alone. Concerned for his sanity, I asked how long they would be gone. He replied they would leave at once and return by sunrise. I made him promise that once this obsession was resolved, he would follow my medical advice. He agreed, and we departed. I joined him, Jupiter, and the dog with a heavy heart around four o’clock. Jupiter carried the scythe and spades, seemingly more out of a desire to keep the tools away from his master than from any genuine industriousness."
}

