parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "df9bbd6a4e6aa468ea731889",
  "rewritten_text": "She was discovered dead in her bed the next morning, seemingly having passed without pain. Sir Mathew Reid was summoned immediately, but nothing could be done, and her funeral was scheduled for the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. Shortly before her death, she had drafted a will leaving Lord Arthur her Curzon Street home, furniture, and personal belongings, excluding her miniatures for Lady Margaret Rufford and an amethyst necklace for Sybil Merton. The estate held little value, yet her solicitor, Mr. Mansfield, urged Lord Arthur to return promptly to settle numerous unpaid bills, as Lady Clementina had never maintained organized records. Lord Arthur was deeply moved by her generosity and blamed Mr. Podgers for the circumstances. Still, his devotion to Sybil eclipsed all else, and the knowledge that he had fulfilled his duty brought him peace. He felt entirely content upon arriving at Charing Cross. The Mertons welcomed him warmly. Sybil made him promise that nothing would ever separate them again, and they set their wedding date for June 7th. Life appeared bright once more, and his former joy returned. One day, while clearing out the Curzon Street house with the solicitor and Sybil—burning old letters and sorting through drawers—Sybil suddenly cried out in delight. \"What have you found?\" Lord Arthur asked with a smile. \"This lovely little silver candy box, Arthur. Isn't it quaint? Please let me have it! I know amethysts won't suit me until I'm eighty.\" It was the container that had held the aconitine. Lord Arthur started, his face flushing. He had nearly forgotten his actions, and it felt like a strange coincidence that Sybil, the very reason for his past torment, should be the one to remind him. \"Of course you can have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\" \"Oh, thank you! And may I have the candy inside, too? I had no idea Lady Clementina liked sweets; I thought she was far too intellectual.\" Lord Arthur turned deathly pale as a horrific realization struck him."
}

