parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8f6c9c742e8e7c84cc372bd5",
  "rewritten_text": "At the top of the stairs, he composed himself to unleash his famous, terrifying laugh. It had proven effective before, turning Lord Raker’s hair white overnight and causing three of Lady Canterville’s governesses to quit early. He let out a chilling shriek that echoed through the vaulted ceiling, but as the sound faded, Mrs. Otis stepped out in a blue robe. She offered him a bottle of medicine, suggesting it would cure his indigestion. Furious, the ghost prepared to transform into a large black dog—a feat that had once caused a man to lose his sanity—but the sound of approaching footsteps made him pause. Instead, he simply glowed with a faint light and vanished with a mournful groan just as the twins arrived. Back in his room, he collapsed in distress. The rudeness of the twins and the practical nature of Mrs. Otis were irritating, but he was most upset that he could not wear his suit of armor. He had hoped the Americans would appreciate a ghost in armor, perhaps out of respect for the poet Longfellow, whose work he enjoyed. It was his own suit, which he had worn to great acclaim at the Kenilworth tournament before Queen Elizabeth I. However, the heavy steel breastplate and helmet had been too much for him; he had fallen hard on the stone floor, bruising his knees and hand. He remained ill for several days, leaving his room only to touch up the bloodstain. Once he recovered, he decided to try once more to frighten the American family."
}

