parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4450e4633ccb319a134bbfe5",
  "rewritten_text": "The thought that once sustained my early career now only drags me into the dirt. My ambitions and hopes are worthless; like the archangel who sought total power, I am bound in an endless hell. My imagination was sharp, matched by my analytical focus, and together these traits allowed me to conceive and create a man. I still recall with intense emotion the daydreams I had while the work was unfinished. I felt divine, exulting in my abilities and the potential impact of my discovery. I was raised with grand expectations, but how far I have fallen! My friend, if you had known me before, you would not recognize me in this ruined state. I rarely felt hopeless; a great destiny seemed to guide me, until I fell, never to rise again. Must I lose this remarkable man? I have long sought a friend who would sympathize with and love me. Here, on these desolate seas, I have found one, yet I fear I have only gained him to realize his worth before losing him. I want to convince him to live, but he rejects the idea. I thank you, Walton, he said, for your kindness toward such a miserable wretch. But when you speak of new bonds, do you think anyone can replace those I have lost? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman another Elizabeth? Even when feelings are not deeply stirred by exceptional character, childhood companions hold a power over our minds that later friends rarely achieve. They understand our early nature, which, though modified by time, is never erased; they can judge our actions with a clearer understanding of our true motives. A sibling can never suspect the other of deceit unless it was evident early on, whereas a new friend, no matter how devoted, may inevitably be viewed with suspicion. I enjoyed friends who were dear not just through habit, but for their own merits. Wherever I go, the gentle voice of my Elizabeth and the conversation of Clerval will echo in my ears. They are dead, and only one purpose could convince me to stay alive in this solitude. If I were committed to a noble goal of great benefit to humanity, I could live to see it through."
}

