parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0c51329124ecf4e7717b52e8",
  "rewritten_text": "Yet upon witnessing you become the focus of another’s affection, or imagining you might be cherished as something less than a divine icon of beauty, or that you might return such passion more fervently than you did mine, the monster stirred inside me. I sent your suitor away, and since that hour, tranquility has eluded me. Sleep fled, my eyes remained open, and my blood surged in constant agitation. I emerged into a new existence, much like a soul dying in hope might awaken in damnation. I shall not stain your mind by detailing my inner battles, my self-loathing, or my misery. Let a shroud cover the indescribable torments of a sinful father; such tortured secrets must not be cheapened. Everything was chaos, transgression, regret, and malice, yet mingled with the deepest tenderness. What finally spurred my resolve to master this passion and return to my daughter as a father was witnessing your own profound, empathetic grief. This brought me here; I believed that by rekindling the sorrow I felt at your mother’s passing, along with the memories dormant for seventeen years, my affection for her child would vanish. In a burst of resolve, I chose to depart alone, leaving you—the very essence of my life—until I could return without guilt. But it failed; I overestimated my strength or underestimated my love. I would surely have perished had you not come to me. Would that I had truly ceased to exist! And now, Mathilda, I offer my final admission. I was wretchedly wrong to think I could overcome my love for you; it is impossible. The sight of this home, these fields and woods where my first love once lived, seems only to have intensified it. In my delirium, I dared to tell myself that Diana died to bring you forth, that her spirit resides within you, and you should be as Diana to me. Despite every attempt to discard it, this affection clings tighter—a forbidden love, more unnatural than hatred, which blights your future and ruins me eternally. Better to have embraced despair or kissed death itself. Neither time nor distance can sever from my soul that which has become part of it. Since arriving, I have not for an instant escaped the hellish passion planted within me, destined to burn until all is cold, rigid, and lifeless."
}

