  "rewritten_text": "I have neglected to mention the most significant point. What is that, Harry? asked the youth apathetically. The obvious comfort, of course. Stealing another person's suitor after losing one's own. In high society, that always redeems a woman. Yet, Dorian, how distinct Sibyl Vane must have been from the women we usually encounter! Her death possesses a genuine beauty to me. I am pleased to live in an era where such marvels occur, as they validate the concepts we merely toy with, like romance, passion, and love. I was dreadfully cruel to her, you forget. I suspect women value cruelty, sheer cruelty, above all else. They possess remarkably primitive instincts. Despite our efforts to emancipate them, they remain slaves seeking masters. They enjoy being controlled. I am certain you were magnificent. I have never witnessed your true rage, but I can imagine how captivating you appeared. Furthermore, you remarked something to me two days ago that I initially dismissed as whimsical, but now recognize as profound; it explains everything. What was that, Harry? You claimed Sibyl Vane embodied every romantic heroine for you—that she was Desdemona one evening and Ophelia the next; that if she perished as Juliet, she revived as Imogen. She shall never revive again, the youth murmured, hiding his face in his hands. No, she will not. Her final performance is over. View her solitary death in that cheap dressing-room as a vivid, dark fragment of a Jacobean tragedy, a striking scene from Webster, Ford, or Tourneur. The girl never truly lived, and thus she never truly died. To you, she remained a dream, a phantom drifting through Shakespeare’s works, enhancing their beauty and enriching the music of his verse. Once she touched reality, she ruined it, and it ruined her, leading to her departure. Grieve for Ophelia if you must. Cover your head in ashes because Cordelia was strangled. Rail against Heaven because Brabantio’s daughter perished. But do not squander tears on Sibyl Vane. She was less real than they. Silence followed. The room grew dim as evening approached. Shadows crept in from the garden with silent, silver steps. The colors slowly drained from the surroundings. Eventually, Dorian Gray raised his head."
}

