parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8f12230356c1b673eaad19dd",
  "rewritten_text": "It was past two when I rounded a bend and spotted a small skiff moored near the lake, close to our home. Seeing my father and aunt on the lawn, I jumped into the boat and rowed with all my might. Dressed in white with my tartan shawl and hair flowing behind me, I moved with such speed that my father later told me I looked more like a spirit than a girl. I reached the shore, and he caught the boat as I leapt into his arms. My life truly began then. Everything shifted from dull monotony to vibrant joy. The happiness of being with my father surpassed my wildest hopes. We were inseparable, our conversations endless. He had spent sixteen years traveling through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, living among locals in ways few Europeans ever could. His stories of their customs and landscapes filled our hours when we weren't discussing our future. The sound of his affection was so new that I cherished every word as he described his feelings during those long years of separation. He told me that while he initially struggled to think of me, hope eventually returned, and my image haunted him in cities and deserts alike. He felt my spirit in the northern breeze and dreamed of taking me to a peaceful island. As he finally traveled home, his anticipation was marred by agonizing fear that he might find my grave instead of me. But he held me close, calling me his consolation and his hope."
}

