parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "07dcb8e103ed8a9bd32769d5",
  "rewritten_text": "“Well, Jupiter,” I remarked, “what is the trouble? How is your master?” “To tell the truth, sir, he is not as well as he might be.” “I am sorry to hear that. What are his symptoms?” “That is the point; he never complains of anything, yet he is quite ill.” “Very ill, Jupiter? Why didn’t you say so? Is he bedridden?” “No, he is not confined at all, and that is exactly what worries me about poor Master Will.” “I don’t follow you. You claim he is sick, but has he not told you what is wrong?” “Sir, please don’t be angry. Master Will insists nothing is the matter, but he walks around with his head down, shoulders hunched, and looking as pale as a ghost. He keeps scribbling ciphers on his slate—the strangest figures I have ever seen. I am frightened and must keep a close watch on his movements. The other day he slipped away before sunrise and was gone all day. I had a heavy stick ready to give him a sound thrashing when he returned, but I am such a fool that I lacked the heart; he looked so wretched.” “Indeed? I think it is best you are not too harsh with him. Do not strike him, Jupiter; he is in no state for it. Can you guess what caused this change in behavior? Has anything unpleasant occurred since I last saw you?” “No, sir, nothing since then. I fear it happened before, on the very day you visited.” “What do you mean?” “I mean the bug, sir.” “The what?” “The bug. I am certain Master Will was bitten on the head by that gold-bug.” “What makes you think that?” “Claws and a mouth, sir. I never saw such a devilish insect; it kicked and bit everything near it. Master Will caught it first but had to drop it quickly, and that must be when he was bitten. I didn’t like the look of its mouth, so I refused to touch it with my fingers, catching it with a piece of paper instead.”"
}

