parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0d164de970bde55f801c442a",
  "rewritten_text": "He remained indoors the following day, largely confined to his room, paralyzed by a frantic fear of death while simultaneously feeling detached from existence. A sense of being pursued and trapped consumed him. The slightest rustle of the tapestry made him tremble, and withered leaves tapping against the windowpanes mirrored his own squandered intentions and bitter regrets. Whenever he shut his eyes, the sailor’s visage appeared behind the glass, and dread gripped his heart once more. Perhaps, however, he had merely conjured vengeance from the darkness. While reality is chaotic, the imagination possesses a grim logic, driving remorse to haunt wrongdoing and forcing every transgression to spawn its own wretched consequences. In the mundane world, virtue and vice rarely meet their just deserts; strength brings success, and weakness invites failure. Surely, had a stranger lurked nearby, the staff would have noticed, or the gardeners would have discovered footprints. It was only a delusion. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned for revenge; he had likely perished at sea. He was safe, for the man could not possibly recognize him behind his youthful facade. Yet, if it were only an illusion, the power of conscience to manifest such terrifying specters was harrowing. What existence awaited him if shadows of his guilt were to haunt his every moment, mocking him from the darkness or waking him with freezing touches? As this realization took hold, he turned pale, and the room seemed to chill. In a moment of frantic insanity, he had murdered his friend, and the memory returned now with unbearable clarity. From the depths of the past, his sin emerged, vivid and stained in crimson."
}

