parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1461eab6d4948dead1c5b976",
  "rewritten_text": "Citizens, butchers, cattle-dealers, peddlers, youths, criminals, and loafers of every sort formed a dense, chaotic mob. The whistling of drovers, barking dogs, bellowing cattle, bleating sheep, squealing pigs, hawkers' cries, and the constant shouting, swearing, and fighting created a deafening, discordant roar. Between the ringing bells, the clamor from taverns, and the shoving, beating, and yelling of the unwashed, disheveled masses surging through the market, the scene was utterly overwhelming and disorienting. Mr. Sikes, pulling Oliver along, pushed through the densest part of the throng, ignoring the sights and sounds that bewildered the boy. He acknowledged a few acquaintances with nods and declined several offers of a drink, moving purposefully until they escaped the chaos, passing through Hosier Lane into Holborn. Now, youngster, Sikes remarked, glancing at St. Andrew’s clock, it is nearly seven. You must hurry. Do not start lagging now, Lazy-legs. Sikes punctuated this with a sharp tug on the boy’s wrist, forcing Oliver to trot to keep pace with the house-breaker’s long strides. They maintained this speed past Hyde Park Corner toward Kensington, where Sikes slowed until an empty cart approached. Noting the sign for Hounslow, he politely asked the driver for a ride to Isleworth. Climb in, the man said, asking if the boy was his. Yes, he is mine, Sikes answered, staring at Oliver while his hand drifted toward his hidden pistol. Your father moves a bit fast for you, does he not? the driver asked, noticing Oliver’s exhaustion. Not at all, Sikes interrupted. He is accustomed to it. Here, grab my hand, Ned. Get in. He helped Oliver into the cart, where the driver told the boy to rest on a pile of sacks. As they passed each milestone, Oliver grew increasingly anxious about their destination. They traveled steadily through Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford, showing no signs of stopping."
}

