  "rewritten_text": "When I first caught Tom alone, I inquired about his original plan for the escape—what he intended to do if we successfully freed a man who was already legally liberated. He explained that his vision had always been to float Jim downriver on the raft, seeking adventures until we reached the river's mouth. Afterward, we would reveal his freedom, return him home in style via steamboat, compensate him for his time, and organize a grand procession with torches and a brass band to welcome him as a hero. I decided, however, that things turned out for the best as they were. We freed Jim from his shackles quickly. Once Aunt Polly, Uncle Silas, and Aunt Sally learned how diligently he had assisted the doctor in nursing Tom, they treated him with great kindness, providing him with excellent food and leisure. We visited him in the sickroom for a long conversation, where Tom gave Jim forty dollars for his patient cooperation. Jim was overjoyed, exclaiming that his earlier predictions on Jackson's Island had come true, proving that his signs were indeed accurate. Tom then suggested that the three of us sneak away soon to acquire gear and embark on wild adventures among the Indians in the Territory for a few weeks. I agreed, though I noted I lacked the funds for supplies and doubted I could get any from home, assuming my father had returned to seize my money from Judge Thatcher. Tom assured me that my six thousand dollars remained untouched and that my father had not returned."
}

