parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5ad0d19ce7aca7b426d53fbf",
  "rewritten_text": "Mrs. Otis, whose experience spending winters in Boston as a girl makes her quite an authority on art, informs me these jewels are highly valuable. Given this, Lord Canterville, I am sure you understand I cannot allow my family to keep them. Such ostentatious trinkets, while perhaps fitting for the British aristocracy, are entirely inappropriate for those raised on the strict, enduring principles of republican simplicity. Virginia, however, is very eager to keep the box as a memento of your unfortunate ancestor. Since it is quite old and damaged, I hope you might grant her request. I am surprised my daughter shows such sympathy for medievalism, though I suppose it stems from her being born in a London suburb shortly after her mother returned from Athens. Lord Canterville listened gravely, tugging his grey moustache to hide a smile. When Mr. Otis finished, he shook his hand warmly. My dear sir, your daughter did my ancestor, Sir Simon, a great service, and my family owes her a debt for her courage. The jewels are hers. If I were heartless enough to take them, I suspect the old ghost would rise from his grave within a fortnight to make my life miserable. They are not legal heirlooms, as their existence was unknown and they were never mentioned in any will. I have no more claim to them than your butler. When Virginia grows up, she will surely enjoy wearing them. Furthermore, you purchased the furniture and the ghost at a valuation; legally, anything belonging to the ghost became your property the moment you bought the estate."
}

