  "request_id": "4986ef2be369e59b4ef63886",
  "rewritten_text": "In that harsh critique of Chantilly published in yesterday’s Musée, the writer made shameful remarks about the cobbler changing his name for the stage, citing a Latin line we have discussed: Perdidit antiquum litera prima sonum. I had explained that this referred to Orion, once spelled Urion, and I knew you remembered it. It was obvious you would connect Orion with Chantilly. Your smile confirmed you had, and I saw you thinking of the poor cobbler’s demise. You straightened your posture, clearly reflecting on Chantilly’s small stature. I interrupted your thoughts to suggest that, given his size, he would be better suited for the Théâtre des Variétés. Shortly after, we were reading the evening Gazette des Tribunaux when these paragraphs caught our eye: Extraordinary Murders. At three this morning, residents of the Quartier St. Roch were awakened by terrifying screams from the fourth floor of a house in the Rue Morgue, occupied by Madame L’Espanaye and her daughter, Camille. After failing to gain entry, neighbors broke down the door with a crowbar and entered with two police officers. The screams had stopped, but as they climbed the stairs, they heard two rough voices arguing above. By the second landing, silence had returned. The group searched the rooms, eventually forcing open a locked back chamber on the fourth floor. The scene inside horrified and astonished them. The room was in total disarray, with furniture smashed and scattered. The bed had been stripped, its mattress thrown into the center of the floor. A blood-stained razor lay on a chair."
}

