parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8adcff1ec08d52221fd412ca",
  "rewritten_text": "I hope to introduce my son to you soon, Mr. Weston said. Mrs. Elton, assuming this was a special compliment, smiled graciously. You have heard of Frank Churchill, I assume, and know he is my son, even if he does not use my name. Oh yes, I look forward to meeting him. Mr. Elton will certainly visit him, and we would love to host him at the Vicarage. You are very kind, and Frank will be delighted. He arrives in town next week, or perhaps sooner. We received a letter today; I saw his handwriting and opened it, even though it was addressed to Mrs. Weston. She is his main correspondent, and I rarely hear from him. You actually opened her mail? Mrs. Elton laughed affectedly. I must protest! That is a dangerous precedent. I hope our neighbors do not follow your lead. We married women will have to be more careful! I could not have believed it of you. We men are troublesome, Mr. Weston replied. You must be careful, Mrs. Elton. The letter is brief, just to let us know they are moving south immediately because Mrs. Churchill has been unwell all winter and finds Enscombe too cold. Indeed, from Yorkshire, I believe? Yes, it is about one hundred and ninety miles from London, quite a long trip. It is significant, certainly. Sixty-five miles further than Maple Grove is from London. But distance means little to the wealthy. You would be amazed at how my brother, Mr. Suckling, travels. He and Mr. Bragge once went to London and back twice in one week with four horses. The trouble with the distance from Enscombe, Mr. Weston noted, is that Mrs. Churchill has been unable to leave her sofa for a week. In his last letter, Frank mentioned she was too weak to reach her conservatory without help from both him and his uncle."
}

