parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "75a4fc050f63341bb0b7b065",
  "rewritten_text": "My father stayed by my side to comfort me, replacing bitter memories with hopeful prospects; I found a strange sweetness in my grief, knowing he was there to dry my tears. He further distracted me by comparing my sorrow to his own despair after losing my mother. I shuddered at his vivid descriptions; he possessed a poet’s imagination, and his account of that emotional whirlwind was so lifelike that I trembled as I listened. I marveled that he could ever return to ordinary life after such intense experiences, which seemed to transcend human capacity. His emotions felt better suited to a spirit dwelling in earthquakes and volcanoes than to a mortal man. Yet, he had changed; he was now entirely gentle and loving, his smile revealing a heart filled with kindness. Two months after my aunt died, we moved to London, where my father encouraged me to pursue more rigorous studies. He delighted in my progress, assisting me with every lesson. We socialized often, and he worked to make every day enjoyable. Our mutual love and respect made every moment charming. We lived so fully that a single week felt like months, with every new experience adding zest to our lives. We frequently traveled together to see beautiful scenery or art, or simply to find amusement. I was always happiest when near him. I regretted when others joined us, but a single tender look from my father would instantly restore my joy. Those hours of intense delight were brief, yet in retrospect, they feel like a lifetime, now obscured by the mist of grief that soon followed to hide them from my sight."
}

