  "rewritten_text": "For an instant, I was too stunned to reason clearly. While my own design differed in detail, the general shape bore a striking resemblance. I took a candle to the far side of the room to examine the parchment more carefully. Flipping it over, I discovered my own sketch on the back, exactly as I had drawn it. Initially, I was simply astonished by the remarkable similarity of the outlines and the strange coincidence that a skull, unknown to me, existed on the reverse side directly beneath my scarab, matching it in both size and form. This oddity left me momentarily paralyzed, as the mind often struggles to find a causal link in such events and falters when it cannot. Yet, as my shock faded, a far more unsettling realization emerged. I distinctly recalled that the parchment was blank when I drew the beetle. I was certain of this, as I remembered checking both sides for a clean surface. Had the skull been present, I surely would have seen it. This mystery felt inexplicable, though a faint, distant intuition of the truth—which last night’s events would later confirm—began to stir in my mind. I stood up, secured the parchment, and set aside further thought until I could be alone. After you left and Jupiter fell asleep, I began a systematic investigation. I first considered how the parchment came into my hands. We found the scarab on the mainland coast, a mile east of the island, just above the high-tide line. When I picked it up, it bit me, causing me to drop it. Jupiter, with his usual wariness, searched for a leaf to grasp the insect after it flew toward him."
}

