parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "32e5d44f10562599acbe328d",
  "rewritten_text": "I had tucked my handkerchief and other items inside my helmet, forgetting that I could not remove the headpiece myself. The knowledge that the cloth was so near yet completely unreachable became an unbearable obsession. As everyone knows, the things we cannot possess are exactly what we desire most. My focus narrowed entirely to that trapped handkerchief, and for miles, I fixated on it while salty sweat stung my eyes. Though it sounds trivial, this was genuine misery. I resolved that in the future, I would carry a handbag regardless of what the knights of the Round Table thought; I preferred comfort over their rigid standards of style. We continued our journey, occasionally hitting dusty patches that filled my nose and forced me to sneeze and curse, though I admit my language was far from perfect. We encountered no one in that desolate land, not even an ogre. Given my state of mind, it was fortunate for any ogre that he lacked a handkerchief, as I would have gladly traded his armor for one. Meanwhile, the sun heated my iron suit until I felt like I was cooking. Every sensation became an irritation. When I trotted, the rattling of my armor annoyed me, and the shifting shield banged against my chest and back. If I slowed to a walk, my joints groaned like a rusty wheelbarrow, and the lack of airflow made the heat stifling. Furthermore, moving slowly only made the heavy iron feel as though it were crushing me with increasing weight every passing minute."
}

