parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "64833add98a4790a5086f646",
  "rewritten_text": "The lieutenant eventually asked why he had traveled so far across the ice in such an unusual craft. His face darkened instantly, and he replied, \"I am searching for someone who fled from me.\" When asked if the man he pursued traveled the same way, he confirmed it. The lieutenant mentioned seeing a man in a dog-drawn sledge crossing the ice the day before they rescued him. This caught the stranger's attention, and he began asking many questions about the route taken by the creature he called a demon. Later, alone with me, he remarked that he had surely piqued our curiosity, though we were too polite to pry. I assured him that it would be rude and heartless to question him, especially after we had saved his life. He then asked if the ice breaking might have destroyed the other sledge. I told him I could not be certain, as the ice did not break until near midnight, and the traveler might have reached safety by then. Since that conversation, a new energy has filled the stranger. He is desperate to go on deck to watch for the sledge, but I have insisted he stay in the cabin, as he is too weak for the freezing air. I promised to have someone watch for him. This is my record of these events. The stranger is slowly recovering but remains quiet and anxious when others enter his cabin. His gentle, winning manner has charmed the crew, despite their limited contact. I have grown to love him like a brother; his profound grief moves me deeply. He must have been a remarkable man in his prime, for even in his broken state, he is captivating. I once wrote to you, Margaret, that I would find no friend at sea, yet I have met a man who, had he not been crushed by sorrow, would have been the brother of my heart."
}

