  "request_id": "f77feb623241bab404d041e1",
  "rewritten_text": "Are you talking about Mr. Knightley? Of course I am. I never meant anyone else, and I assumed you knew. When we discussed him, it seemed perfectly clear. Not quite, Emma replied, trying to stay calm. Everything you said seemed to point to someone else. I could have sworn you named Mr. Frank Churchill, especially when you mentioned how he saved you from the gypsies. Oh, Miss Woodhouse, how you forget! My dear Harriet, I remember our conversation perfectly. I told you that I understood your feelings because of his kindness, and you agreed, speaking warmly about how he rescued you. I remember it clearly. Oh, Harriet cried, now I understand, but I was thinking of something else entirely. It was not the gypsies or Mr. Frank Churchill. I was thinking of a much more meaningful moment: when Mr. Knightley asked me to dance after Mr. Elton refused, and when no one else would. That was the kind, generous act that made me realize how superior he is to everyone else. Good heavens, Emma exclaimed, this has been a terrible, unfortunate mistake! What can we do? You would not have encouraged me if you had understood? At least I am no worse off than I would have been otherwise, and now, it is possible. She paused, and Emma could not speak. I am not surprised, Miss Woodhouse, that you see a huge difference between the two men, Harriet continued. You must think one is infinitely above me. But I hope, even if it seems strange, that you will not oppose it. You said yourself that more unlikely matches have happened than one between Mr. Frank Churchill and me. If I am lucky enough that Mr. Knightley does not mind the difference in our status, I hope you will not try to stand in our way."
}

