parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7f08ca2cfe2f516500cecc92",
  "rewritten_text": "Hear me! You! Monks replied. When his father fell ill in Rome, my mother, from whom he had long been estranged, traveled from Paris with me to join him. She likely came to secure his assets, as there was little love between them. He remained unconscious until his death the following day. Within his desk, we discovered two documents dated from the onset of his sickness, addressed to you, Mr. Brownlow. They were accompanied by a note instructing that they only be delivered after his passing. One was a letter to Agnes, the other a will. What of the letter? Mr. Brownlow inquired. It was a page filled with remorseful confessions and pleas for divine mercy. He had deceived the girl, claiming a secret mystery prevented their marriage, leading her to trust him until she suffered an irreparable loss. She was then nearing her delivery. He detailed his plans to conceal her disgrace had he survived, begging her not to curse his memory or fear their child would suffer for his sins. He recalled the locket and ring he had given her, inscribed with her name and a space for the one he hoped to provide, urging her to keep them close. He wrote frantically, as if losing his mind, which I believe he was. The will, Mr. Brownlow prompted as Oliver wept. Monks remained quiet. The will, Mr. Brownlow continued, mirrored the letter. He spoke of the misery his wife caused, the malice and vice of you, his son, whom he claimed was raised to despise him. He bequeathed you and your mother annuities of eight hundred pounds each. The remainder of his estate was split equally between Agnes Fleming and their child, provided the infant survived and reached maturity."
}

