parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "80f4ab60ecb0e918216b2165",
  "rewritten_text": "The gauge showed I had reached an altitude of 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, allowing me to see about one three-hundred-twentieth of the Earth's total surface. By nine o'clock, the land to the east had vanished, and I noticed the balloon drifting quickly to the north-northwest. The curve of the ocean below was clearly visible, though clouds often blocked my view. I noted that even the thinnest vapors never rose more than ten miles above sea level. At nine-thirty, I dropped a handful of feathers through the valve to test the air. Instead of floating, they fell straight down like bullets, disappearing in seconds. I was confused at first, unable to believe my ascent had suddenly accelerated so much. I soon realized the air was too thin to support the feathers, and their rapid fall combined with my own upward speed made them vanish instantly. By ten o'clock, I had little to do. Everything was going well, and I felt the balloon rising faster every moment, though I could no longer measure the exact speed. I felt no pain or discomfort and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I spent my time checking my equipment and refreshing the air inside the chamber every forty minutes, mostly as a health precaution. Meanwhile, my mind wandered to the moon. Unshackled by reality, my imagination roamed through a dreamlike landscape. I pictured ancient forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls crashing into bottomless pits. Then, I imagined silent, midday solitudes where no wind blew, and vast fields of poppies and lilies stretched out, perfectly still and quiet forever."
}

