{
  "request_id": "ecaddf9176b429388ff4aeaf",
  "rewritten_text": "It was the best of times and the worst of times; an age of wisdom and foolishness; a period of belief and skepticism; a season of light and darkness; a spring of hope and a winter of despair. We had everything before us, yet we had nothing. We were all heading straight to Heaven or straight the other way. In short, that era was so similar to the present that some of its loudest leaders insisted it be viewed only in the most extreme terms. A king with a large jaw and a plain-faced queen sat on the English throne, while a king with a large jaw and a beautiful queen ruled France. In both nations, the powerful elite were certain that their status was secure forever. It was 1775. England was filled with spiritual claims, much like today. Mrs. Southcott had just turned twenty-five, and a prophetic soldier had announced that London and Westminster were destined to be destroyed. The Cock-lane ghost had only been silenced twelve years prior, after tapping out messages much like the unoriginal spirits of the past year. Meanwhile, messages of great importance had arrived for the English Crown from British subjects in America—events far more significant than any ghostly warnings. France, less focused on spiritual matters, slid smoothly toward ruin, printing and spending paper money. Guided by their religious leaders, the French also engaged in brutal acts, such as sentencing a young man to have his hands cut off, his tongue torn out, and his body burned alive simply for failing to kneel before a passing religious procession. It is likely that in the forests of France and Norway, trees were already growing that Fate would eventually harvest to build a certain wooden frame with a sack and a blade, destined to become a terrifying symbol in history."
}

