parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "90541193a15c90095b3465f9",
  "rewritten_text": "He stood and left the chamber with my nurse, and my father walked in immediately. Nothing could have delighted me more than his arrival. I reached out my hand and asked if he, Elizabeth, and Ernest were safe. My father comforted me by confirming their well-being, hoping to lift my spirits, yet he soon realized a prison is no place for joy. He gazed sadly at the barred windows and dismal room, remarking on the misfortune that seemed to follow me, and mentioned poor Clerval. The mention of my murdered friend overwhelmed my fragile state, and I wept. I told him that some terrible fate hung over me, and I had to survive to meet it, or I would have perished beside Henry. We could not speak long, as my health required absolute rest. Mr. Kirwin entered and insisted I avoid overexertion. Still, my father’s presence acted like a guardian angel, and I slowly regained my strength. As my illness faded, a dark, impenetrable melancholy took hold. Clerval’s murdered image haunted me, and my distress often worried my friends that I might relapse. I wondered why they kept me alive, concluding it was only to fulfill a destiny nearing its end. Soon, death would silence my suffering, and I would find peace through justice. At that time, death felt distant, though I constantly desired it, often sitting motionless for hours and praying for a catastrophe to destroy both me and my enemy. The assizes drew near. Having spent three months in jail, I was still frail, yet I had to travel nearly a hundred miles to the county town for my trial. Mr. Kirwin took charge of gathering witnesses and preparing my defense."
}

