parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ba551ae640af6fa487d0cb1b",
  "rewritten_text": "I vow, he shouted, by the sun, the azure heavens, and the passion consuming my soul, that if you fulfill my request, you will never see me again as long as they live. Go home and begin your work; I will observe your progress with profound concern, and rest assured that I will return when you are prepared. With these words, he vanished, perhaps fearing I might change my mind. I watched him descend the mountain faster than an eagle, soon disappearing into the icy expanse. His story had taken the entire day, and the sun was setting as he left. I knew I needed to hurry down to the valley before darkness fell, yet my spirit was burdened and my pace sluggish. Navigating the narrow mountain trails while lost in the day's intense emotions proved difficult. It was late when I reached the halfway point and sat by the fountain. Stars flickered between passing clouds, and the dark pines and fallen timber created a solemn, unsettling atmosphere. I wept in anguish, crying out to the stars, clouds, and winds to either end my consciousness and memory or leave me in total darkness. These were desperate, chaotic thoughts, yet I cannot convey how the constant starlight oppressed me or how every gust of wind felt like a scorching gale meant to destroy me. Dawn broke as I reached Chamounix, but I did not rest, traveling straight to Geneva. My internal turmoil was indescribable, pressing down with crushing weight. Upon returning home, my disheveled and frantic state alarmed my family, but I remained silent, unable to speak. I felt ostracized, as though I had forfeited my right to their comfort or any future connection with them."
}

