parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "febad1f4c65377d05ad79561",
  "rewritten_text": "What is wrong? I replied, Pap, and mam, and sis, and— Then I faltered. He said, Oh, hang it, do not carry on so; we all face hardships, and this will resolve itself. What is wrong with them? They are—are you the boat's watchman? Yes, he said, sounding quite pleased. I am the captain, owner, mate, pilot, watchman, and head deck-hand; sometimes I am the freight and passengers too. I am not as wealthy as old Jim Hornback, nor can I be as generous to every Tom, Dick, and Harry, throwing money around as he does; but I have told him many times I would not trade places with him. A sailor's life is for me, and I would not live two miles outside of town, where nothing ever happens, not for all his riches and more. I interrupted, saying, They are in terrible trouble, and— Who is? Why, pap, mam, sis, and Miss Hooker; if you would take your ferry-boat up there— Up where? Where are they? On the wreck. What wreck? Why, there is only one. You do not mean the Walter Scott? Yes. Good heavens! What are they doing there, for mercy's sake? Well, they did not go there on purpose. I bet they did not! Great goodness, there is no hope for them if they do not get off quickly! How on earth did they get into such a mess? It was easy. Miss Hooker was visiting up at the town— Yes, Booth's Landing—go on. She was visiting Booth's Landing, and just at dusk she started across with her servant in the horse-ferry to stay the night at a friend's house—I forget the name—and they lost their steering-oar. They drifted downstream, stern first, for two miles and became wedged on the wreck. The ferryman, the servant, and the horses were lost, but Miss Hooker managed to grab hold and climb onto the wreck. About an hour after dark, we arrived in our trading-scow, and it was so dark we did not see the wreck until we were right upon it; so we became wedged too. Everyone was saved except Bill Whipple—and oh, he was the best creature! I almost wish it had been me, I truly do. My word! It is the most astonishing thing I have ever encountered."
}

