parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3d1e76e312fa23ff7a0dc4d0",
  "rewritten_text": "No imagination could capture how magnificent this sight would have been in the dark of night; it would have looked like hell itself. Even in the daylight, my hair stood on end as I stared into the deep, fiery abyss, picturing myself wandering through those strange, glowing caverns. I had narrowly escaped disaster. If I had stayed in the cloud a moment longer—or if I had not decided to drop my ballast to avoid the dampness—I would have certainly perished. These dangers are often overlooked, yet they are the most significant risks of ballooning. By now, however, I had reached such a height that I no longer worried about that. By seven o'clock, my barometer showed I had reached an altitude of nine and a half miles. I began to struggle for air. My head throbbed, and I soon realized the moisture on my cheeks was blood leaking from my ears. My eyes felt painful and seemed to bulge from their sockets, making everything in the balloon look distorted. These symptoms alarmed me. In a moment of poor judgment, I threw out fifteen pounds of ballast. This caused me to rise too quickly into the thin upper atmosphere, nearly killing me. I suffered a violent spasm lasting five minutes, and even afterward, I could only gasp for air while bleeding heavily from my nose, ears, and eyes. The pigeons were clearly distressed, and the cat staggered around the car, mewing piteously with its tongue hanging out as if it had been poisoned. I realized too late how reckless I had been to drop the ballast, and I was terrified, expecting to die within minutes."
}

