  "rewritten_text": "Basil, this is incredible! I have to meet Dorian Gray. Hallward stood up and paced the garden before returning. Harry, he said, Dorian Gray is simply an artistic inspiration to me. You might see nothing in him, but I see everything. He is present in my work even when his image is absent; he suggests a new style. I find him in the curves of certain lines and the subtlety of specific colors. That is all. Then why won't you exhibit his portrait? Lord Henry asked. Because I have unintentionally poured my own artistic obsession into it, something I have never mentioned to him. He knows nothing of it, and he never will. But the public might guess, and I refuse to expose my soul to their shallow scrutiny. I will not let them put my heart under a microscope. There is too much of myself in that painting, Harry. Poets are less careful, Lord Henry replied. They know passion sells. A broken heart often leads to many editions these days. I despise them for that, Hallward cried. An artist should create beauty without injecting his own life into it. We live in an era that treats art as autobiography, having lost the sense of abstract beauty. One day I will show the world what art truly is, and for that reason, they will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray. I think you are mistaken, Basil, but I will not argue. Only the intellectually lost argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray fond of you? The painter paused. He likes me, he said. I flatter him terribly, and I find a strange pleasure in saying things I later regret. Usually, he is charming, and we talk for hours in the studio. Occasionally, however, he is thoughtless and seems to enjoy hurting me. At those times, Harry, I feel I have surrendered my soul to someone who treats it like a flower for his lapel—a mere decoration to feed his vanity for a summer day."
}

