parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "49088181d54f890858eeac03",
  "rewritten_text": "Dorian, these rumors must concern you. Every gentleman cares about his reputation. You surely do not want to be viewed as someone corrupt. While you have status and wealth, those things are not everything. I do not believe these stories, at least not when I look at you. Sin leaves a mark on a man’s face that cannot be hidden. There is no such thing as a secret vice; it reveals itself in the lines of the mouth, the eyelids, or even the hands. Last year, a man I will not name asked me to paint him. I refused, despite his high offer, because I despised the shape of his fingers. I later learned my instincts were correct; his life is truly wretched. But you, with your innocent face and youthful appearance, seem above reproach. Yet, I rarely see you, and when I hear the terrible things people whisper, I am at a loss. Why does the Duke of Berwick leave a club room when you arrive? Why do so many men in London avoid your company? Lord Staveley recently told me that while you have artistic taste, you are someone no decent woman should be near. He said this openly, and it was horrifying. Why is your friendship so destructive? Think of the young soldier who killed himself, or Sir Henry Ashton, who was forced to flee England in disgrace."
}

