{
  "request_id": "0d19459f7e1d84e4c43e46a9",
  "rewritten_text": "I lack the nerves for such matters. But could you truly feel at ease spending the entire evening away from the poor child? Yes; his father manages it, so why shouldn't I? Jemima is quite attentive, and she could update us hourly. I really believe Charles should have told his father we would all attend. I am no more worried about little Charles now than he is. I was terribly frightened yesterday, but today the situation is quite changed. Well, if you do not think it too late to send word, why not go along with your husband? Leave the boy in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove could not object while I am here with him. Are you in earnest? Mary exclaimed, her eyes lighting up. How wonderful! That is a brilliant idea. I might as well go, as I am of no use here, and it only distresses me. You, lacking a mother's sensibilities, are far better suited for this. You can make little Charles obey instantly. It will be much better than leaving him with only Jemima. I shall certainly go; I ought to, just as much as Charles, since they are so eager for me to meet Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left alone. A marvelous suggestion, Anne. I will inform Charles and prepare at once. You can summon us immediately if anything happens, though I am certain there will be no cause for alarm. I would not dream of going if I were not perfectly comfortable regarding my dear child. Moments later, she was knocking at her husband’s door, and as Anne followed her upstairs, she overheard the entire exchange, which began with Mary declaring triumphantly, I intend to accompany you, Charles, for I am no more useful here than you are. If I remained shut up with the child forever, I could never persuade him to do anything against his will. Anne will stay; she has offered to remain and look after him. It was her own proposal, so I shall join you, which is much better, as I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday. This is very kind of Anne, her husband replied, and I would be glad to have you come, but it seems rather unfair that she should be left alone to nurse our sick child."
}

