parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cf04ad6b0ad7cee3274bca65",
  "rewritten_text": "On the morning scheduled for Admiral and Mrs. Croft to tour Kellynch Hall, Anne felt it best to walk to Lady Russell’s house to avoid the encounter, only to regret missing the chance to meet them afterward. The meeting was a success, settling the matter immediately. Both women were eager to agree, viewing each other with mutual favor. The Admiral’s genuine, open-hearted nature impressed Sir Walter, who had been further flattered by Mr. Shepherd’s claims that the Admiral considered him a paragon of refinement. The house, grounds, and terms were all accepted without a single dispute, and the legal paperwork began immediately. Sir Walter declared the Admiral the most presentable sailor he had ever encountered, noting that with a better barber, he would be quite acceptable in public. Meanwhile, the Admiral remarked to his wife that the Baronet was harmless, if not particularly brilliant. These mutual assessments were equally balanced. The Crofts were to take possession at Michaelmas, and since Sir Walter planned to move to Bath the month before, preparations began in earnest. Lady Russell, knowing Anne would have no say in choosing their new home, hoped she might stay behind until after Christmas. However, Lady Russell had her own travel plans, and Anne, despite dreading the heat of Bath and mourning the loss of the autumn season in the country, ultimately felt it was best to leave."
}

