parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d78e364d6c0785c0c367a062",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Elliot gazed upward with profound respect. How charming! Mrs. Clay exclaimed, though she dared not glance at Anne. It is just like a father and son! My dear Miss Elliot, may I not call them father and son? I place no restrictions on what people say, but I hardly feel his attentions exceed those of any other gentleman. My dear Miss Elliot! Mrs. Clay cried, raising her hands and eyes before falling into a convenient, astonished silence. Well, Penelope, you need not be so concerned. I invited him, after all. When I learned he was visiting friends at Thornberry Park tomorrow, I took pity on him. Anne marveled at her friend’s performance, managing to display such delight at the arrival of the very man who hindered her own ambitions. Mrs. Clay surely loathed the sight of Mr. Elliot, yet she maintained a placid, obliging expression, appearing content to spend only half as much time with Sir Walter as she might have otherwise. For Anne, Mr. Elliot’s entrance was distressing, and his approach was painful. She had previously suspected his sincerity, but now she perceived falsehood in everything. His deferential behavior toward her father, so contrary to his earlier remarks, was repulsive. Recalling his heartless treatment of Mrs. Smith, she could barely endure his mild smiles or his hollow, virtuous sentiments. She hoped to avoid any change in demeanor that might invite his questioning. Her goal was to evade any scene or scrutiny, yet she intended to remain as cool as their relationship allowed, quietly withdrawing from the unnecessary intimacy she had been drawn into. Consequently, she was more guarded and distant than the previous evening. He attempted to pique her curiosity once more regarding where he had heard her praised, hoping for further encouragement, but the spell had vanished. He realized that the excitement of a public setting was required to stir his cousin’s vanity, or at least that his current efforts were ineffective amidst the competing demands of the others."
}

