parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ff9c5722ee3d5c9e91cadc77",
  "rewritten_text": "It is truly nothing, a mere pleasure. I do not labor for profit; my existence is devoted solely to my craft. Lord Arthur placed four pounds, two shillings, and sixpence upon the table, expressed his gratitude to the German, and after politely refusing an invitation to join Anarchists for a Saturday meal, departed for the Park. Over the following two days, he remained in a fever of anticipation, arriving at the Buckingham by noon on Friday to await word. Throughout the afternoon, the porter displayed telegrams regarding horse races, divorce rulings, and weather reports, while the ticker tape provided tedious updates on parliamentary sessions and market fluctuations. At four, the evening editions arrived, and Lord Arthur retreated to the library with several papers, much to the annoyance of Colonel Goodchild, who sought to read his own Mansion House address on South African Missions and the merits of black Bishops. None of the journals mentioned Chichester, leading Lord Arthur to conclude his attempt had failed. Devastated, he found his nerves shattered. When he visited Herr Winckelkopf the next day, the man offered profuse apologies and proposed a replacement clock or discounted nitro-glycerine bombs. Having lost confidence in explosives, Lord Arthur listened as the German lamented the modern adulteration of even pure dynamite. The artisan remained optimistic that the clock might yet detonate, citing a barometer he once sent to an Odessa Governor that failed to explode for three months. While that device eventually killed only a housemaid because the Governor had departed weeks prior, it served as proof that dynamite, when mechanized, remained a potent, if tardy, instrument of destruction."
}

