parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c094bdcddedee9f7be497294",
  "rewritten_text": "You do not know me unless you have read the book The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, but that does not matter. Mr. Mark Twain wrote it, and he mostly told the truth, though he exaggerated a few things. Everyone lies sometimes, except perhaps Aunt Polly, the widow, or Mary, who are all featured in that book. At the end of that story, Tom and I found the robbers' hidden money in a cave, making us both rich. We each received six thousand dollars in gold. Judge Thatcher invested it for us, earning us a dollar a day, which was more money than we knew how to spend. The Widow Douglas adopted me and tried to civilize me, but living in her house was difficult because she was so strict and proper. Eventually, I could not stand it anymore, so I ran away, put on my old rags, and lived in my sugar barrel, feeling free and happy. However, Tom Sawyer found me and promised that if I returned to the widow and acted respectable, I could join his new band of robbers. So, I went back. The widow cried over me and called me a poor lost lamb, though she meant no harm. She forced me back into those new clothes, which made me sweat and feel restricted. The old routine started again. She rang a bell for supper, and I had to be on time. Before eating, I had to wait for her to say grace over the food, even though there was nothing wrong with it, other than everything being cooked separately. I prefer when food is mixed together so the flavors blend. After dinner, she read to me from the Bible about Moses. I was interested at first, but when she mentioned that Moses had been dead for a long time, I lost interest because I do not care about dead people."
}

