  "rewritten_text": "He does not understand English, but judges by tone. Alberto Montani, a confectioner, testified that he was among the first to climb the stairs. He heard the voices in question; the gruff one was French and seemed to be arguing. He could not understand the shrill voice, which spoke quickly and unevenly; he assumes it was Russian, though he has never spoken to a Russian. He confirms the general testimony. Several witnesses confirmed that the fourth-floor chimneys were too narrow for a person. The brushes used to clean them were passed through every flue, and there was no back exit for anyone to escape while the group ascended. Mademoiselle L’Espanaye’s body was wedged so tightly in the chimney that it took several men to pull it down. Paul Dumas, a physician, testified that he examined the bodies at daybreak. The young woman’s corpse was bruised and scraped, likely from being forced up the chimney. Her throat was chafed, with deep scratches under the chin and livid finger-shaped bruises. Her face was discolored, her eyes protruded, and her tongue was bitten. A bruise on her stomach suggested pressure from a knee. Dr. Dumas concluded she was strangled by unknown persons. The mother’s body was horribly mutilated, with shattered bones in her right leg and arm, a splintered left tibia, and broken ribs. Her entire body was bruised. Such injuries could only be inflicted by a very powerful man using a heavy, blunt object like a club, iron bar, or chair. A woman could not have caused such damage. The victim’s head was severed and shattered, and her throat had been cut with a sharp instrument, likely a razor."
