parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "49a4b65941e3c82c82c3afc0",
  "rewritten_text": "We finished our investigation at dusk and left. On the way home, my companion stopped briefly at a newspaper office. I have mentioned that my friend had many quirks, which I always humored. He chose not to discuss the murder until the following afternoon, when he suddenly asked if I had noticed anything peculiar at the crime scene. His emphasis on that word made me shudder for no apparent reason. I told him I saw nothing beyond what the papers reported. He dismissed the newspaper's account, arguing that the police found the case impossible only because its strange details made it seem difficult. They were confused by the lack of motive for such brutality and the impossibility of the suspects escaping the room. The chaotic state of the apartment, the body shoved up the chimney, and the horrific mutilation had completely baffled the authorities. They made the common mistake of confusing the unusual with the complex. However, it is through these strange deviations that reason finds the truth. In such cases, we should ask not what happened, but what has happened that is truly unprecedented. The ease with which I will solve this mystery is directly proportional to how impossible it seems to the police. I stared at him in silence, stunned. He looked toward our door and added that he was expecting someone who, even if not the killer, was certainly involved in the crime."
}

