parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "00fcb35d9292cba1b90ecad3",
  "rewritten_text": "“I must point out, Sir Walter,” Mr. Shepherd said one morning at Kellynch Hall as he set down his newspaper, “that the current situation works in our favor. The peace will bring many wealthy naval officers home, and they will all need a place to live. There could not be a better time to find reliable tenants. Many have made fortunes during the war. If a rich admiral were to come our way—” “He would be a very lucky man,” Sir Walter interrupted. “Kellynch Hall would be a prize for him, the greatest prize he has ever taken, wouldn’t it, Shepherd?” Mr. Shepherd laughed as expected, then continued, “I believe naval officers are excellent to deal with. They have generous ideas and make ideal tenants. I suggest that if rumors of your plans get out—and it is hard to keep such things secret when you are a man of your status—we should prioritize applications from wealthy naval commanders. I can come over at any time to handle the details so you do not have to worry.” Sir Walter simply nodded. Soon after, he stood and paced the room, remarking sarcastically, “I imagine few naval officers would not be surprised to find themselves in a house like this.” “They would look around and bless their good fortune,” added Mrs. Clay, who was visiting. “But I agree with my father that a sailor would be a very desirable tenant.”"
}

