  "request_id": "9d61a0f79c994a34cb396908",
  "rewritten_text": "Ten minutes later, the tea bell rang. When Virginia failed to appear, Mrs. Otis sent a footman to find her, but he returned reporting she was nowhere to be found. Initially unconcerned, Mrs. Otis grew anxious by six o'clock. She sent her sons to search while she and Mr. Otis checked every room. By half-past six, the boys returned empty-handed. In a panic, Mr. Otis recalled granting a group of travelers permission to camp in the park days earlier. He immediately set out for Blackfell Hollow with his eldest son and two farmhands. The young Duke of Cheshire begged to join them, but Mr. Otis refused, fearing a confrontation. Upon arrival, they found the camp abandoned in haste, with the fire still smoldering and dishes left behind. After sending his son and the men to search the area, Mr. Otis returned home to wire police inspectors across the county regarding a suspected kidnapping. He then ordered his horse and, after insisting his family eat dinner, rode toward the Ascot Road with a groom. Two miles out, he heard galloping behind him and saw the Duke on his pony, hatless and flushed. The boy gasped that he could not eat while Virginia was missing and pleaded not to be sent back, suggesting their engagement would have prevented this crisis. Touched by the young man's devotion, Mr. Otis smiled, patted his shoulder, and agreed to let him join the search, provided they stopped in Ascot for a hat."
}

