  "rewritten_text": "We eventually agreed to reside together for the duration of my city visit. Since my financial situation was slightly more stable than his, I took on the cost of leasing and decorating a decaying, eccentric mansion in a quiet, abandoned corner of the Faubourg St. Germain, a place long avoided due to local superstitions we chose not to investigate. If our daily habits had been public knowledge, we would have been labeled harmless lunatics. We lived in total isolation, receiving no guests. My former acquaintances were unaware of our location, and Dupin had long since vanished from Parisian society. We lived entirely within our own private world. My friend possessed a strange obsession with the night, a quirk I adopted with complete devotion. Since true darkness could not always be with us, we mimicked it. At dawn, we sealed the shutters of our home and lit scented candles that cast only faint, ghostly light. We spent our hours dreaming, reading, writing, or talking until the clock signaled the arrival of real night. We would then walk the streets together, discussing our thoughts or wandering the city to find the mental stimulation that comes from observing the shifting shadows of the crowd. During these walks, I often marveled at Dupin’s analytical prowess. He clearly relished exercising this skill and openly admitted his enjoyment. With a quiet chuckle, he claimed he could see into the hearts of others as if they had windows in their chests, often proving his deep insight into my own mind. At these times, his demeanor became cold and detached, his gaze unfocused, and his voice shifted from a rich tenor to a high, distinct treble that sounded almost petulant."
}

