parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "90025decce14d2543fa168ad",
  "rewritten_text": "The storm raged all night, but nothing unusual happened. The next morning, however, the bloodstain had returned to the floor. Washington remarked that the detergent must not be at fault, suggesting it had to be the ghost. He cleaned it again, but the stain reappeared the following morning. It returned a third time, even though Mr. Otis had locked the library and kept the key. The family became intrigued; Mr. Otis reconsidered his skepticism, Mrs. Otis planned to join a psychical society, and Washington wrote a detailed letter about the persistence of bloodstains linked to crimes. That night, all doubt regarding ghosts vanished. The day had been warm, and the family went for a drive, returning at nine for a light supper. They did not discuss ghosts, avoiding any atmosphere of expectation. As Mr. Otis later noted, they talked about typical topics for cultured Americans: the acting skills of Fanny Davenport versus Sarah Bernhardt, the lack of American food like corn and buckwheat cakes in England, the global importance of Boston, the efficiency of the baggage check system, and the pleasantness of the New York accent. No one mentioned the supernatural or Sir Simon de Canterville. The family went to bed by eleven, and the house was dark by half-past. Later, Mr. Otis was woken by a strange metallic clanking in the hallway that grew louder. He stood up, lit a match, and checked the time; it was exactly one o’clock. Remaining calm and checking his steady pulse, he listened to the noise and the sound of footsteps. He put on his slippers, grabbed a small bottle from his dressing case, and opened the door."
}

