parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2c4172541730d39669fa7e9d",
  "rewritten_text": "The ringmaster gave a brief speech, asking for no further disruptions and promising to let the man ride if he could stay mounted. The crowd laughed, and the man climbed on. Immediately, the horse began bucking and rearing while two circus workers struggled to hold the bridle. The drunk man clung to the horse’s neck, his legs flailing as the audience roared with laughter. Eventually, the horse broke free and galloped around the ring with the man swaying dangerously, his limbs dangling. I felt terrified for him, but soon he pulled himself upright, grabbed the reins, and stood tall on the galloping horse as if he were perfectly sober. He began shedding layers of clothing, tossing seventeen suits into the air until he stood slim and handsome in a brilliant, ornate costume. He whipped the horse into a frenzy, then leaped off, bowed, and danced away while the crowd cheered in disbelief. The ringmaster, realizing he had been tricked by one of his own performers, looked utterly humiliated. I felt foolish for being deceived, but I would not have traded places with that ringmaster for anything. It was the best circus I have ever seen, and I will certainly return whenever I find it again."
}

