parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7159e492401167c076f07611",
  "rewritten_text": "The refined gentlemen of high society wore small hanging trinkets that chimed as they moved. These golden ornaments, combined with the rustle of silk and fine linen, created an atmosphere that kept the starving people of Saint Antoine at a distance. Fashion was the primary tool used to maintain social order. Everyone dressed as if they were attending a never-ending costume party. From the royal palace and the court down to the legal system and even the public executioner, everyone followed this trend. The executioner himself was expected to perform his duties while wearing a powdered wig, a gold-trimmed coat, and white silk stockings. In 1780, it seemed impossible that a system built on such absurd formality could ever collapse. After finishing his chocolate, Monseigneur opened the doors to his private chambers and emerged. The crowd responded with extreme submission and fawning, bowing so low that they seemed to have no devotion left for God, whom they largely ignored. Monseigneur moved through the rooms, offering empty promises and smiles to his followers before retreating back into his sanctuary. Once the display ended, the crowd dispersed, their jewelry jingling as they left. Only one man remained, walking slowly past the mirrors with his hat and snuffbox. Stopping at the final door, he looked toward the sanctuary and muttered, I devote you to the Devil. He brushed the snuff from his fingers and calmly walked downstairs."
}

