parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e581b5b8681bcab4af5723ec",
  "rewritten_text": "Every normal boy eventually feels a burning urge to hunt for buried treasure. This impulse struck Tom one day. He looked for Joe Harper but couldn't find him, and Ben Rogers had gone fishing. Finally, he found Huck Finn. Tom pulled him aside to share his secret plan. Huck was interested, as he was always ready for any activity that was fun and cost nothing, since he had plenty of time and no money. Where should we dig, Huck asked. Oh, almost anywhere, Tom replied. Is it hidden everywhere, Huck wondered. No, Tom explained, it is hidden in specific spots like islands, in rotting chests beneath dead trees where shadows fall at midnight, or under the floorboards of haunted houses. Who hides it, Huck asked. Robbers, of course, Tom said. Who else would it be? I would just spend it, Huck noted. Robbers do not do that, Tom insisted. They hide it and leave it. Do they ever come back, Huck asked. No, they usually forget the markers or die. Eventually, someone finds a cryptic map covered in symbols and hieroglyphics that takes a week to decode. What are those, Huck asked. Pictures that do not seem to mean anything, Tom explained. Do you have one, Huck asked. No, Tom admitted. Then how will you find it, Huck pressed. I do not need a map, Tom said. We can check Jackson’s Island, the old haunted house, and all the dead trees. Is it under all of them, Huck asked. Of course not, Tom replied. Then how will you know which one, Huck asked. We will try them all, Tom declared. That will take all summer, Huck argued. But imagine finding a brass pot with a hundred dollars or a chest full of diamonds, Tom countered. Huck’s eyes lit up. That sounds great, he said. Just give me the hundred dollars; I do not need the diamonds."
}

