parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "743ed6d1bf0aeb4f6db41157",
  "rewritten_text": "The stars were still bright when I woke at midnight, shaken by a nightmare. I had dreamt of a feast where Raymond, transformed into a monstrous, plague-bearing phantom, chased me while throwing foul-smelling vessels. The vision grew until it seemed to threaten the very sky. Shaking off the terror, I regained my senses and thought only of Perdita. I knew I had to return to her, to comfort her broken heart and guide her through her grief with both duty and compassion. Guided by the stars, I escaped the ruins of the Golden City. Outside the walls, I borrowed a horse from a group of soldiers and rode toward my sister. The landscape had changed; the army was gone, and the scattered soldiers I passed looked stunned and fearful. I entered the palace with a heavy heart, dreading what I would find. Perdita sat on the marble floor, her hair messy and her face pale with agony. When she looked up, a flicker of hope crossed her eyes, which only deepened my pain. I could not speak, and my forced smile told her everything. She bowed her head again, her fingers twisting nervously. When I finally found my voice, it frightened her; she did not want to hear the confirmation of her tragedy spoken aloud. Instead, she stood up and whispered, \"Hush, Clara is finally sleeping after all her crying; we must not wake her.\""
}

