  "request_id": "02498494d8c43ae4a99957c1",
  "rewritten_text": "I thanked him and apologized. He replied, \"Not at all,\" and continued. \"Miss Havisham was now an heiress, and naturally considered a great catch. Her half-brother had regained his wealth, but he quickly squandered it again through debts and reckless behavior. There was intense conflict between them, and it is believed he held a deep grudge against her for influencing their father’s anger. Now, I reach the cruel part of the story—though I must pause, my dear Handel, to note that a dinner napkin will not fit inside a glass.\" I have no idea why I was trying to force mine into the tumbler, only that I was working with strange determination to make it fit. I thanked him and apologized again, and he cheerfully replied, \"Not at all!\" before resuming. \"A man appeared—perhaps at the races or a public ball—who began courting Miss Havisham. I never met him, as this was twenty-five years ago, but my father described him as a flashy man, well-suited for his schemes. My father insisted he was no gentleman, arguing that true manners must come from the heart. He believed that no amount of polish can hide a person's true nature; in fact, the more you try to cover it, the more obvious it becomes. This man pursued Miss Havisham relentlessly, and she, having shown little interest in anyone before, fell passionately in love with him. She truly idolized him. He manipulated her feelings so effectively that he extracted large sums of money from her. He even convinced her to buy out her brother’s share of the brewery at an inflated price, claiming that as her husband, he needed full control. Your guardian was not involved in her affairs at the time, and she was far too proud and infatuated to listen to anyone else."
}

