parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b4f98f644a7941d367b2c7cf",
  "rewritten_text": "Once our friend was settled in his new office, we looked toward Windsor. Its proximity to London softened the sting of parting from Raymond and Perdita, whom we left at the Protectoral Palace. It was charming to watch my sister embrace her role, striving to inhabit her position with appropriate grace. Her inner pride and humble demeanor were in constant conflict. Her shyness was genuine, stemming from a fear of being misunderstood and a disregard for public opinion, much like Raymond. Yet, Perdita was more considerate of others; her reserve was partly an attempt to spare those around her from feeling inferior—a sentiment she herself never harbored. While Idris’s upbringing might have better suited her for formal duties, she found such rituals tedious, whereas Perdita clearly relished her new status. Occupied by fresh ideas, she bid us a warm farewell, promising a visit without lingering on the sadness of our departure. Raymond was brimming with boundless energy, his mind racing with ambitious plans to mark his Protectorship with some glorious achievement. We discussed them as we returned to Windsor Castle, relieved to leave political strife behind and eager for our quiet life. I focused my restless energy on intellectual pursuits, finding that rigorous study calmed the spirit. Perdita allowed us to bring Clara to Windsor, and she, along with my two children, provided constant joy. The only shadow on our peace was Adrian’s failing health. Though he showed no clear symptoms, his bright eyes and flushed cheeks suggested consumption, yet he remained free of pain or anxiety. He devoted himself to his books, finding rest in the company of his sister and me."
}

