  "rewritten_text": "To look at him was to imagine he had been struck by a divine curse. He stood frozen, his face a mask of marble sorrow. Having spent his youth in privileged, carefree luxury, he was suddenly confronted by the crushing weight of fate. It felt monstrous and insane. Was his future truly written in the lines of his palm, a hidden record of sin he could not decipher? Was he merely a pawn moved by unseen forces, a vessel shaped by a potter’s whim? His mind rebelled, yet he felt a looming tragedy, an unbearable burden placed upon him. Actors are lucky; they choose their roles, whether to laugh or weep. In reality, people are forced into parts they are ill-equipped to play. The world is a stage, but the casting is disastrous. Suddenly, Mr. Podgers entered. Seeing Lord Arthur, he recoiled, his face turning a sickly, jaundiced hue. They locked eyes in a tense silence. The Duchess left her glove, and I have come to retrieve it, Podgers said, spotting it on the sofa. Good evening. Mr. Podgers, I insist you answer my question directly. Another time, Lord Arthur; the Duchess is waiting. You are not leaving. The Duchess can wait. Ladies are often impatient, Podgers replied with a nauseating smile. Lord Arthur curled his lip in disdain; the Duchess meant nothing to him now. He approached Podgers and held out his hand. Tell me what you saw, he demanded. Tell me the truth. I am not a child."
}

