  "rewritten_text": "When the morning arrived for Admiral and Mrs. Croft to inspect Kellynch Hall, Anne felt it best to walk to Lady Russell’s to avoid the encounter, though she later regretted missing the chance to meet them. The meeting went perfectly, settling the matter immediately. Both ladies were eager to agree, and the Admiral’s genuine, open-hearted nature impressed Sir Walter, who had been further softened by Mr. Shepherd’s flattery regarding his reputation for refinement. The property, the tenants, and the terms were all accepted without dispute, and the legal documents were prepared immediately. Sir Walter even complimented the Admiral’s appearance, suggesting he would be presentable with a better haircut, while the Admiral remarked to his wife that the Baronet, though unremarkable, seemed harmless. The Crofts were scheduled to move in at Michaelmas, and with Sir Walter planning to relocate to Bath the month prior, preparations began at once. Lady Russell, knowing Anne would have no say in their new home, hoped to keep her at Kellynch until after Christmas. However, due to her own upcoming travel, she could not extend a formal invitation. Anne, despite dreading the heat of Bath and mourning the loss of the autumn season in the country, ultimately felt it was best to depart."
}

