  "rewritten_text": "If you are asking for my patience and the mercy of those you have hurt, sit in that chair without another word. It has been waiting for you for two days. Monks muttered something unclear, still hesitating. You must be quick, Mr. Brownlow said. One word from me, and your chance is gone forever. The man still wavered. I have no desire to argue, Mr. Brownlow continued, and since I am protecting the interests of others, I do not have the right to. Is there no other way? Monks asked, his voice shaking. None. Monks looked at the old man, but seeing only stern resolve, he walked into the room and sat down with a shrug. Lock the door from the outside, Mr. Brownlow told the attendants, and return when I ring. The men obeyed, leaving the two alone. This is fine treatment from my father’s oldest friend, Monks said, throwing down his hat and cloak. It is precisely because I was your father’s oldest friend, Mr. Brownlow replied. It is because my hopes were tied to him and to the woman he loved, who died young and left me alone. It is because he stood with me at his sister’s deathbed when we were boys—a woman who would have been my wife had heaven willed it. My heart stayed loyal to him through all his mistakes until he died. Because of these memories, and because even seeing you brings him to mind, I am moved to treat you gently, Edward Leeford, even though I am ashamed of how you carry that name. What does the name matter? Monks asked, watching the older man’s distress with stubborn confusion. What is the name to me? Nothing to you, Mr. Brownlow replied. But it was hers, and even now, it brings back the joy I once felt just hearing it. I am very glad you changed it. This is all very dramatic, Monks said after a long silence, during which he shifted in sullen defiance while Mr. Brownlow covered his face with his hand."
}

