parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1a037dde46b13ae67e8ef3ac",
  "rewritten_text": "Smallpox! I rushed to the king and whispered, \"Leave at once, sire! The woman is dying of the same disease that devastated Camelot two years ago.\" He did not move. \"I will stay and help,\" he insisted. I urged him again, \"King, you must go.\" He replied, \"You mean well, but it would be shameful for a king to show fear or for a knight to ignore those in need. I will not leave. You should go, as the Church forbids your presence here, and they will punish you severely if they find out.\" It was a dangerous situation that could cost him his life, but I knew arguing was useless. If he felt his honor was at stake, he would not budge. I let the matter drop. The woman spoke, \"Kind sir, would you climb the ladder and tell me what you find? Do not fear to tell me, for a mother's heart can break only once.\" The king set down his bag and said, \"Stay and feed her. I will go.\" He started up, then paused to look at a man lying in the shadows who had not spoken. \"Is this your husband?\" the king asked. \"Yes,\" she replied. \"Is he asleep?\" \"Thank God, yes, for three hours now. I am so grateful for his rest.\" I promised, \"We will be careful not to wake him.\" She answered, \"You cannot, for he is dead. It is a relief to know he is safe from harm and insult. He is in heaven now, or perhaps in hell, where he will at least be free from bishops and abbots. We were together for twenty-five years and never parted until today. This morning, he was delirious, imagining we were children wandering in happy fields, and in that state, he slipped away from this life.\""
}

