  "rewritten_text": "The two ladies were thrilled to see their friend, remarking that it had been ages since they last met and repeatedly asking how she had been. They ignored the rest of the family, avoiding Mrs. Bennet, saying little to Elizabeth, and nothing to the others. They soon left, rising with a sudden energy that surprised their brother, and hurried away as if eager to escape Mrs. Bennet’s politeness. The upcoming Netherfield ball delighted every woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet felt it was held in honor of her eldest daughter and was flattered that Mr. Bingley had invited them personally. Jane looked forward to spending the evening with her friends and their brother, while Elizabeth anticipated dancing with Mr. Wickham and observing Mr. Darcy’s behavior. Catherine and Lydia were excited simply because it was a ball, and even Mary agreed to attend, noting that she found social recreation acceptable in moderation. Elizabeth was in such high spirits that she asked Mr. Collins if he planned to attend and dance. She was surprised to learn he had no objections, fearing no criticism from his superiors for participating. He assured her that a ball hosted by a respectable man was perfectly fine and even asked for the first two dances with her, hoping Jane would not take offense at his preference."
}

