parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "03d1b5527c34ccdca331a373",
  "rewritten_text": "He was attired as Jonas the Graveless, the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn, a role that had famously sparked the Cantervilles' feud with Lord Rufford. It was past two in the morning, and the house seemed silent. While heading to the library to check for the bloodstain, two figures suddenly sprang from the shadows, flailing their arms and shouting 'BOO!' in his ear. Terrified, he bolted for the stairs, only to find Washington Otis waiting with a garden syringe. Cornered, he escaped into an unlit iron stove, crawling through the flues to reach his room, covered in soot and misery. He never ventured out at night again. Despite the twins' efforts to trap him by scattering nutshells, he remained in hiding, his pride deeply injured. Mr. Otis returned to his history of the Democratic Party, Mrs. Otis hosted a successful clam-bake, the boys played American games, and Virginia rode her pony with the visiting Duke of Cheshire. Believing the ghost had departed, Mr. Otis informed Lord Canterville, who expressed his relief. Yet, the ghost remained, though weakened. He was particularly agitated by the presence of the young Duke, whose great-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once bet he could play dice with the spirit, only to be discovered the next day paralyzed and capable of uttering nothing but 'Double Sixes.'"
}

