{
  "request_id": "71bec474f85de34fd81265d2",
  "rewritten_text": "He could not help it. It infuriated him to believe that Becky Thatcher did not even realize he was alive. In truth, she saw him perfectly well; she knew she was winning and felt satisfied to see him suffer as she had. Amy’s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom made excuses about urgent tasks, but the girl kept talking. He thought, \"I wish she would leave.\" Finally, he had to go, and she innocently mentioned she would be around after school, which made him despise her. \"Anyone but that arrogant boy from Saint Louis!\" Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \"I beat you once, and I will do it again! Just wait until I catch you.\" He punched and kicked the air, finishing his imaginary fight with satisfaction. Tom rushed home at noon, unable to stand Amy’s happiness or his own jealousy. Becky continued looking at pictures with Alfred, but as Tom failed to appear, her triumph faded into sadness. She listened for his footsteps, but he never came. Eventually, she felt miserable for pushing things too far. When Alfred tried to show her another picture, she snapped, \"Leave me alone! I don't care about them!\" and walked away crying. Alfred tried to follow, but she shouted, \"Go away! I hate you!\" He stopped, confused, while she walked off in tears. Alfred entered the empty schoolhouse, feeling humiliated and angry. He realized the truth: she had only used him to get back at Tom Sawyer."
}

