parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c1baea842f9ce35573a36d71",
  "rewritten_text": "I considered feigning death; my satisfied heirs would claim my fortune, granting me my liberty. Yet, the scheme required careful planning; I needed to ensure I was not left penniless. Alas, what wretched measures I was forced to adopt! Otherwise, a lifetime of deceit awaited me. Whenever remorse for my planned deception made me hesitate, the arrival of an aunt or cousin would solidify my resolve by reminding me that death is inevitable. They would claim my father had lost his senses since my mother passed, calling him insane and suggesting I was lucky he had not killed me during one of his episodes. They spoke with delicate hints, whispering in dark, soft tones with sympathetic expressions, while I listened calmly, my nerves fraying, unable to challenge their blasphemy. What a delightful life, entirely devoid of honesty! I wore a dove’s expression while harboring a fox’s heart, feeling only the shame of my lies rather than any sense of righteous innocence. Having once worn the bright cloak of sincerity, I now had to adopt a multicolored one; it felt awkward initially, but practice allowed me to drape it elegantly. I might stain my soul with falsehood until its true nature was hidden. Oh, beloved father, accept your daughter’s pure heart; let me join you untainted, or you will not recognize me. Just as grief transformed Constance, deceit would alter me until, in heaven, you would fail to know me. To remain happy now and in our future reunion, I must escape this mocking existence. Only in solitude can I be myself and belong to you. Even now, I look back with loathing upon the schemes and struggles I endured to effect my departure. I could detail the methods I employed to secure a modest living and convince others of my death, but I choose not to."
}

