parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b36d26ef8c319a92ef06f338",
  "rewritten_text": "I decided to get off as soon as we reached town to avoid him, and I succeeded. My small suitcase was under my feet; I simply unlatched it, dropped it, and climbed down, standing on the first paved street under a lamp. The convicts continued on the coach toward the river. In my mind, I pictured the boat waiting at the muddy stairs, heard the harsh command to row, and saw the dark, ominous vessel on the black water. I could not name my fear, but it was intense and vague. Walking to the hotel, I felt a dread far beyond the worry of being recognized, a sudden, shapeless return of childhood terror. The Blue Boar’s dining room was empty, and I was already eating before the waiter recognized me. After apologizing for his lapse in memory, he asked if he should fetch Mr. Pumblechook. I refused firmly. The waiter, who had served me during my apprenticeship, seemed surprised and quickly placed a stained local newspaper in front of me. I read a paragraph noting that the recent rise in fortune of a local blacksmith—a subject for the poet Tooby—was guided by a respected figure in the corn and seed trade located near the High Street. The article proudly claimed this man as the mentor to our young local success story."
}

