parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "11a10cc6f70f5ce222fc0d19",
  "rewritten_text": "I do not understand why she wanted to be close to me, but I would likely be fooled again if the situation repeated itself. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday, and I heard nothing from her in the meantime. When she finally arrived, it was clear she did not enjoy the visit. She offered a brief, formal apology for the delay, said nothing about wanting to see me again, and acted so differently that I decided to end our acquaintance. I pity her, even though I blame her. She was wrong to single me out, as she initiated every step toward our friendship. I pity her because she must know she acted wrongly, and I am certain her anxiety about her brother is the cause. I need not explain further; while we know her worry is unnecessary, it explains her behavior. Given how much she loves her brother, her concern is natural. Still, I am surprised she has these fears, because if he cared for me, we would have met long ago. She let slip that he knows I am in town, yet she talks as if she wants to convince herself he prefers Miss Darcy. I cannot understand it. If I were not afraid of being too harsh, I would call her behavior deceitful. I will try to set aside these painful thoughts and focus on what makes me happy: your affection and the kindness of our aunt and uncle. Write to me soon. Miss Bingley mentioned he might never return to Netherfield and might give up the house, but she was not certain. We should not mention it. I am glad you have good news from our friends at Hunsford. Please visit them with Sir William and Maria; I am sure you will be comfortable. This letter troubled Elizabeth, but she felt better knowing Jane would no longer be deceived by the sister. Any hope regarding the brother was now completely gone."
}

