parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1aaf867053a3c488f4b4f686",
  "rewritten_text": "The area seemed to be a maze of back alleys, ditches, and small gardens, offering a rather dull, secluded atmosphere. Wemmick’s home was a tiny wooden cottage surrounded by garden plots, its roof carved and painted to resemble a battery equipped with cannons. My own work, Wemmick said. Looks nice, doesn't it? I praised it highly. It was the smallest house I had ever seen, featuring odd gothic windows—mostly fake—and a door so narrow it was difficult to enter. That is a real flagpole, Wemmick noted. On Sundays, I raise a real flag. And look here. Once I cross this bridge, I lift it up—like this—and cut off access. The bridge was just a plank spanning a four-foot-wide, two-foot-deep trench. It was charming to see the genuine pride he took in hoisting it, smiling with real satisfaction rather than just going through the motions. At nine o'clock sharp, Greenwich time, the gun fires, Wemmick explained. There it is! When you hear it, I think you will agree it packs a punch. The cannon sat in its own lattice-work fortress, shielded from the elements by a clever umbrella-like tarp. In the back, out of sight so it doesn't ruin the fortification theme—because I believe if you have a concept, you should commit to it, don't you agree? I told him I certainly did. In the back, I keep a pig, chickens, and rabbits. I also built my own cold frame to grow cucumbers, and you can judge my salad skills at supper. So, sir, Wemmick said, smiling seriously, if you imagine this place under siege, it could hold out for a long time with these provisions. He then led me to a garden bower a dozen yards away, reached by such winding paths that it took a while to arrive. Our glasses were already waiting. Our punch was chilling in an ornamental pond beside the bower. This circular pool featured a small island—perhaps the future salad—and a fountain he had built. When you started the small mill and unplugged the pipe, it sprayed with enough force to dampen the back of your hand."
}

