parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f8606876aed85014cd541ae9",
  "rewritten_text": "He let her know that Frank admired her greatly, finding her both beautiful and charming. Given his many positive qualities, she felt she could not judge him too severely. As Mrs. Weston noted, all young people have their quirks. However, one person in Surrey was not so forgiving. While most people in Donwell and Highbury were generous toward such a handsome, polite young man, Mr. Knightley remained unmoved by his charm. When he heard the news at Hartfield, he stayed silent at first, but Emma soon heard him mutter to himself while reading the paper, \"Just the silly, trifling fellow I thought he was.\" She considered taking offense, but realized he was merely venting his own frustration rather than trying to provoke her, so she let it go. Although the Westons brought some disappointing news, their visit was timely. Emma needed advice on a matter, and they provided exactly the guidance she sought. The Coles, a friendly and unpretentious family, had lived in Highbury for years. Though they came from a modest background in trade, their recent business success had significantly increased their wealth. They expanded their home, hired more staff, and began living in a style second only to the Hartfield family. Their new dining room and social ambitions suggested they would soon start hosting dinner parties. Emma doubted they would dare invite the area's most prominent families, such as those from Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls. She was determined not to attend if invited, though she worried her father’s predictable habits would make her refusal seem less significant than she intended."
}

