parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "326f6d2823594d4e3e1f25fc",
  "rewritten_text": "Without pausing to think, and feeling a jumble of emotions, Elizabeth finished the first letter and immediately tore open the second, which had been written a day later. My dearest sister, you have likely received my first hurried note by now. I hope this one is clearer, though my mind is so overwhelmed that I cannot promise to be coherent. I have terrible news that cannot wait. As foolish as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now desperate for it to be true, because we fear they never went to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday from Brighton. Although Lydia’s note to Mrs. Forster suggested they were heading to Gretna Green, a comment from Mr. Denny made the Colonel suspect that Wickham never intended to marry her at all. The Colonel immediately set out to track them. He followed them as far as Clapham, but there they switched to a public carriage and vanished. They were last seen heading toward London. I do not know what to think. After searching near London, the Colonel came to Hertfordshire to check the roads, but he found no trace of them. He then came to Longbourn to share his fears with us, acting with great kindness. I feel for him and his wife, but they are not to blame. We are in deep distress. Mother and Father fear the worst, but I cannot think so poorly of Wickham. Perhaps they chose to marry privately in London instead. Even if Wickham were capable of such a scheme, I cannot believe Lydia would be so foolish. Still, I am saddened that the Colonel does not share my hope; he shook his head and admitted he does not trust Wickham."
}

