parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7eada23f6943d3b3a7f80f4e",
  "rewritten_text": "It was Lady Windermere’s final gathering before Easter, and Bentinck House was packed beyond its usual capacity. Six Cabinet Ministers arrived from the Speaker’s Levée adorned in their ribbons and stars, the most attractive women displayed their finest gowns, and at the gallery’s edge, Princess Sophia of Carlsrühe—a stout, Tartar-featured woman with small dark eyes and magnificent emeralds—spoke broken French loudly, laughing excessively at every remark. It was a remarkable gathering. Splendid peeresses conversed kindly with radical agitators, famous clergymen mingled with noted doubters, a group of bishops trailed a stout opera singer, and Royal Academicians stood on the stairs, while the supper-room was reportedly filled with geniuses. It was truly a highlight for Lady Windermere, and the Princess remained until nearly eleven-thirty. Once she departed, Lady Windermere went back to the gallery, where a famous economist was gravely detailing the science of music to an annoyed Hungarian virtuoso, and she began speaking with the Duchess of Paisley. She appeared stunning, with her ivory neck, large blue eyes, and thick golden hair. It was true gold, not the pale straw shade often called gold today, but the hue of sunbeams or ancient amber, lending her face the aura of a saint mixed with the allure of a sinner. She was a fascinating psychological subject. Early on, she realized that nothing mimics innocence quite like an indiscretion; through various bold, mostly harmless adventures, she earned the status of a unique personality. She had married three times, according to Debrett, yet because she kept the same lover, society had stopped gossiping about her. Now forty, childless, and possessing that insatiable thirst for pleasure that preserves youth, she suddenly scanned the room and asked in her clear contralto, Where is my palmist? Your what, Gladys? the Duchess asked, startled. My palmist, Duchess; I cannot do without him right now. Dear Gladys, you are always so unique, the Duchess whispered, struggling to recall if a palmist was the same as a foot doctor. He examines my hand twice every week, Lady Windermere added, and he is quite fascinating."
}

