parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "18d90393e1180a3334bf9083",
  "rewritten_text": "For years, Dorian Gray remained under the spell of that book, or rather, he made no effort to escape it. He acquired nine first-edition copies from Paris, binding them in various hues to match his shifting moods and the erratic impulses of a spirit he often struggled to govern. The protagonist, a remarkable youth who fused romantic and scientific sensibilities, served as a mirror for his own existence, as if his life had been chronicled before he lived it. Unlike the novel’s hero, Dorian never suffered the absurd fear of mirrors or reflective surfaces that plagued the Parisian after his beauty faded. He read the tragic conclusion with a sense of cruel delight, finding a dark satisfaction in the despair of a man who had forfeited the very beauty he prized above all else. His own exquisite appearance, which had captivated Basil Hallward and others, remained untouched by time. Despite the scandalous rumors circulating through London clubs, people found it impossible to believe ill of him upon seeing his face. His youthful purity silenced even the most vulgar men, reminding them of the innocence they had lost. They marveled that someone so graceful could remain untainted by a sordid, sensual era. Frequently, after returning from his long, mysterious excursions, he would retreat to his locked room. With his key in hand, he would stand before the portrait, comparing the decaying, wicked visage on the canvas with the radiant, laughing reflection staring back at him from the glass."
}

