  "rewritten_text": "Sir Walter had rented a fine house in Camden Place, a grand and elevated location befitting a man of his status, and both he and Elizabeth were quite pleased with their new home. Anne arrived with a heavy heart, dreading a long stay and wondering when she might finally escape. However, the unexpectedly warm welcome she received lifted her spirits. Her father and sister were genuinely glad to see her, if only to show off their new residence and furnishings, and they treated her with kindness. They even appreciated having a fourth person at the dinner table. Mrs. Clay was pleasant and smiling, though her manners felt routine. Anne had expected such performative politeness from her, but the friendliness of her father and sister was a surprise. They were in high spirits and eager to share their news, showing no interest in hearing about Anne’s life. After fishing for compliments about how much they were missed in their old neighborhood—which Anne could not provide—they quickly turned the conversation back to themselves. Uppercross and Kellynch held no interest for them; they only cared about Bath. They assured her that Bath exceeded all their expectations. Their house was clearly the best in Camden Place, with drawing rooms that surpassed any others in layout and decor. They were highly sought after, with strangers constantly leaving calling cards despite their efforts to limit new acquaintances. Anne understood why they were happy, yet she sighed to see her father feel no shame in his diminished circumstances. He seemed to have forgotten the responsibilities of a landowner, finding vanity instead in the trivialities of city life. She could only sigh and smile as Elizabeth proudly paced between the folding doors, boasting about the space of rooms that were barely thirty feet apart—a woman who had once been the mistress of Kellynch Hall. Yet, their happiness did not end there; they also had Mr. Elliot."
}

