parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0e5fffc524ad9f237e1488d7",
  "rewritten_text": "Only after some time did I regain my composure enough to manage the balloon. Upon inspection, I was relieved to find it undamaged. My tools remained secure, and I had lost neither provisions nor ballast, as I had fastened them too firmly for such a mishap. Checking my watch, I noted it was six o’clock. I continued to climb, with my barometer indicating an altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Below, a small, dark, oblong object floated on the ocean, resembling a domino. Through my telescope, I identified it as a British ninety-four-gun vessel, sailing close-hauled and tossing in the waves, headed west-southwest. Beyond this ship, only the sea, the sky, and the risen sun were visible. It is time to clarify the purpose of my dangerous journey. You must understand that financial ruin in Rotterdam had driven me to contemplate suicide. I did not loathe life itself, but I was exhausted by the hardships of my condition. Seeking to live yet weary of existence, I found inspiration in a bookstall treatise. I decided to depart the world while remaining alive—in short, I resolved to attempt a voyage to the moon. To avoid appearing entirely insane, I will explain why I believed this difficult and perilous feat was possible for a bold spirit. First, I considered the moon’s distance. The average interval between the centers of the two bodies is about 237,000 miles. However, because the moon’s orbit is an ellipse with an eccentricity of 0.05484, if I could intercept the moon at its perigee, that distance would be significantly reduced."
}

