parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "64b37919b70900838c98bb40",
  "rewritten_text": "After I submitted the petitions, I could not stay away from the places where I had left them, feeling as though my presence offered some small comfort. Driven by this restless anxiety, I spent my evenings wandering the streets, passing the offices and homes where I had delivered my appeals. Even now, the cold, dusty streets of western London on a spring night, with their rows of stern, closed mansions and flickering lamps, fill me with sadness because of those memories. My daily visits were now shorter, and he was kept under stricter guard. Suspecting that I might be accused of trying to smuggle poison to him, I asked to be searched before sitting by his bed, telling the guard I would do anything to prove my intentions were honest. No one treated us cruelly; they were simply doing their duty. The guard and the other prisoners acting as nurses—criminals, yet capable of kindness—always told me he was growing weaker. As days passed, I noticed him lying quietly, staring at the ceiling with a dull expression that only brightened for a moment when I spoke. Often unable to talk, he would squeeze my hand, and I learned to understand him perfectly. By the tenth day, I saw a significant change. His eyes brightened as I entered, turning toward the door. Dear boy, he said as I sat down, I thought you were late, but I knew you couldn't be. It is exactly the right time, I replied. I waited at the gate. You always wait at the gate, don't you, dear boy? Yes, so I wouldn't lose a moment. Thank you, dear boy. God bless you. You have never abandoned me. I squeezed his hand in silence, unable to forget that I had once intended to leave him. And the best part of all, he added, is that you have been more comfortable with me since I have been in trouble than you were when things were going well."
}

