parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dec1f694f34014c5192945f8",
  "rewritten_text": "She watched me closely for a moment before asking quietly, \"What do you want for them?\" I replied, \"Only that you do not group them with the others. They may share the same blood, but they are not the same in nature.\" Miss Havisham repeated her question, still studying me. I felt myself blush as I answered, \"I am not clever enough to hide that I do want something. If you would provide the money to help my friend Herbert in a way he will never know, I could show you how.\" She rested her hands on her cane to focus on me. \"Why must he not know?\" she asked. \"Because I began this help two years ago without his knowledge, and I do not want to be exposed. I cannot explain why I cannot finish it myself; it is someone else's secret.\" She turned her gaze to the fire. After a long silence, the coals shifted, and she looked back at me with renewed focus. Estella continued knitting throughout. When Miss Havisham looked at me again, she asked, \"What else?\" I turned to Estella, my voice shaking. \"Estella, you know I love you. I have loved you for a long time.\" Estella looked up, her expression unchanged as her fingers kept moving. Miss Havisham glanced between us. \"I should have said this sooner, but I was mistaken. I hoped Miss Havisham intended us for each other. I stayed silent while I thought you had no choice in the matter, but I must speak now.\" Estella shook her head without stopping her work. \"I know,\" I said. \"I have no hope that you will ever be mine, Estella.\""
}

