  "request_id": "39247346defac1f6e71a38d4",
  "rewritten_text": "I swear by the sun, the blue sky, and the love in my heart, that if you grant my request, you will never see me again as long as they live. Go home and begin your work. I will watch your progress with intense anxiety, and you need not fear; I will appear when you are ready. He left suddenly, perhaps afraid I might change my mind. I watched him descend the mountain faster than an eagle, quickly vanishing into the icy landscape. His story had taken all day, and the sun was setting as he left. I knew I should hurry down to the valley before dark, but my heart was heavy and my pace slow. Navigating the mountain paths was difficult while I was distracted by the day's events. It was late when I reached the halfway point and sat by the fountain. The stars flickered through passing clouds, and the dark pines and fallen trees created a solemn, unsettling scene. I wept bitterly, clutching my hands in agony, and cried out to the stars, clouds, and winds to either end my suffering and memory or leave me in darkness. These were desperate thoughts, but the constant twinkling of the stars felt like a burden, and every gust of wind sounded like a destructive force coming to consume me. Morning arrived before I reached Chamounix. I did not rest, returning immediately to Geneva. I could not express my feelings; they weighed on me like a mountain, crushing my spirit. When I arrived home and saw my family, my wild, haggard appearance alarmed them, but I could not speak or answer their questions. I felt like an outcast, unworthy of their sympathy and unable to ever enjoy their company again."
}

