  "rewritten_text": "I do not rush at my opponent, risking a clumsy failure. Instead, I move to the side and approach slowly, so he never suspects I plan to strike. Then, in a flash, he is on his back, completely bewildered by how it happened. That is how I handled brother Dowley. I began speaking in a relaxed, casual tone, as if just passing the time. No one could have guessed where my argument was heading. Boys, there are many strange things about laws, customs, and the way human opinion evolves. Written laws fade, but unwritten ones are eternal. Consider the unwritten law of wages: they must rise gradually over the centuries. We know current wages, and we know what they were one or two hundred years ago. That history reveals the rate of progress, allowing us to estimate wages from centuries past. We do not stop there; we apply this law to the future. I can predict wages for hundreds of years to come. What, goodman, what! Yes. In seven hundred years, wages here will be six times higher; farmhands will earn three cents a day, and mechanics six. I wish I could die now and live then, interrupted Smug the wheelwright, his eyes glowing with greed. And that is not all; they will get their meals, too, though they will not be filling. Two hundred and fifty years later, pay attention, a mechanic’s wages will be twenty cents a day. The group gasped in astonishment, and Dickon the mason murmured, eyes and hands raised to the sky."
}

