parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eeca9762cb021c6a9f5d596f",
  "rewritten_text": "After I sent them in, I could not stay away, feeling they were less desperate when I was near. Driven by this restless anxiety, I wandered the evening streets, passing the offices where I had left the petitions. To this day, the cold, dusty streets of western London, with their stern, closed mansions and rows of lamps, remain melancholy to me because of those memories. My daily visits were now shortened, and he was more strictly guarded. Suspecting they thought I might bring him poison, I asked to be searched before sitting by his bed, telling the ever-present officer I would do anything to prove my intentions were pure. No one was cruel to us; they simply performed their duty. The officer and the other prisoners acting as nurses—criminals, yet capable of kindness—always told me he was worsening. As days passed, he lay staring at the ceiling, his face dull until my voice brightened it for a moment. When he could not speak, he squeezed my hand, and I learned to understand him. By the tenth day, I saw a significant change. His eyes lit up as I entered. \"Dear boy,\" he said as I sat down, \"I thought you were late, but I knew you couldn't be.\" \"It is exactly the right time,\" I replied. \"I waited at the gate.\" \"You always wait at the gate, don't you?\" \"Yes, so I don't lose a moment.\" \"Thank you, dear boy. God bless you! You never abandoned me.\" I squeezed his hand silently, haunted by the memory that I had once intended to desert him. \"And the best part,\" he added, \"is that you have been more comfortable with me since I have been under this dark cloud than when the sun was shining.\""
}

