  "request_id": "79f63e0fa5beb4332847e33e",
  "rewritten_text": "“What is wrong?” Oliver asked. “Quiet!” the Dodger answered. “Do you notice that elderly man at the bookstand?” “The gentleman across the street?” Oliver asked. “Yes, I see him.” “He will serve,” the Dodger said. “An excellent target,” remarked Charley Bates. Oliver stared at them in bewilderment, but before he could question them, the two boys crept across the street and slipped behind the man. Oliver followed a few steps, then paused, watching in stunned silence. The gentleman appeared quite respectable, with powdered hair, gold spectacles, a bottle-green coat with a velvet collar, and white trousers, carrying a bamboo cane. He was so absorbed in a book from the stall that he seemed to believe he was in his own study, oblivious to his surroundings as he read with intense focus. Oliver watched in horror as the Dodger reached into the man’s pocket, pulled out a handkerchief, passed it to Charley Bates, and then both boys sprinted around the corner. Suddenly, the truth about the stolen goods and the old man dawned on Oliver. Terrified, he felt as though his blood were boiling, and he fled in a panic, running as fast as he could. This all occurred in a moment. As Oliver bolted, the gentleman reached for his pocket, realized his handkerchief was gone, and turned around. Seeing the boy racing away, he assumed Oliver was the thief and, shouting “Stop thief!” at the top of his lungs, chased after him with his book still in hand."
}

