  "request_id": "62fac965dbbfa0af7bbfeaa6",
  "rewritten_text": "“So I am the owner of knights,” I remarked as we departed. “Who would have guessed I would count such things as property? I have no idea what to do with them, perhaps I will raffle them away. How many are there, Sandy?” “Seven, if it pleases you, sir, along with their squires.” “A decent haul. Who are they and where do they reside?” “Where do they reside?” “Yes, where do they live?” “Ah, I did not grasp your meaning. I shall inform you presently.” She mused softly, savoring the words: “Where do they reside—the phrase has a charming grace and is quite elegantly put. I shall repeat it often in my spare time to master it. Where do they reside. It already rolls off my tongue quite easily.” “Do not forget the knights, Sandy.” “Knights?” “Yes, the ones you were about to describe. Proceed with your report and do not waste time with such lengthy introductions.” “I shall begin at once. The two of them departed and rode into a vast forest. And—” “Good heavens!” I realized my error immediately. I had started her narrative engine, and she would take a month to reach the facts. She typically began without context and ended without purpose. Interrupting her was useless, as she would either ignore me or restart her sentence, yet I had to intervene to avoid being driven mad by her monotonous drone. “Good heavens!” I cried in frustration. She simply restarted: “The two of them departed and rode into a vast forest. And—” “Which two?” “Sir Gawaine and Sir Uwaine. They arrived at a monastery and were well accommodated. The next morning, after hearing mass, they rode until they reached a great forest, where Sir Gawaine spotted twelve beautiful maidens and two armed knights near a tower in a valley, with the women walking back and forth by a tree.”"
}

