parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e625c8b5f160fb2d22173c92",
  "rewritten_text": "“Come on, Tom,” I said, “you could at least tell me. What is a bar sinister?” “I don’t know. But he has to have one. All the nobility does.” That was just how he was. If he didn’t want to explain something, he wouldn’t. You could pester him for a week and it wouldn’t matter. With the coat of arms settled, he started planning a mournful inscription, insisting Jim needed one just like the others. He wrote several on a piece of paper and read them aloud: 1. Here a captive heart burst. 2. Here a poor prisoner, forsaken by the world and friends, fretted out his sorrowful life. 3. Here a lonely heart broke, and a worn spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity. 4. Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV. Tom’s voice trembled as he read, and he nearly broke down. When he finished, he couldn’t decide which one Jim should scratch onto the wall, so he decided Jim should scratch them all. Jim argued it would take him a year to carve all that into the logs with a nail, and he didn’t even know how to form letters, but Tom promised to outline them so Jim only had to follow the lines. Then he added, “Actually, logs won’t work; dungeons don’t have log walls. We need to carve the inscriptions into a rock. We’ll get one.” Jim complained that a rock was even worse, saying it would take so long he would never get out. Tom offered to let me help. He then checked our progress on the pens. It was incredibly tedious, slow work that kept my hands from healing, and we were barely making any progress, so Tom said, “I know how to fix this. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the inscriptions, so we can kill two birds with one stone.”"
}

