parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "aa18eb701f97ab63c98fe862",
  "rewritten_text": "Dorian, you must care about your reputation. Every gentleman does. You wouldn't want people calling you vile. Wealth and status aren't everything. I don't believe these rumors, especially when I look at you. Sin always leaves a mark on a man's face; it cannot be hidden. There is no such thing as a secret vice. If a man is corrupt, it shows in his mouth, his eyes, or even his hands. Last year, a man I didn't know offered me a fortune to paint him, but I refused because I hated the shape of his fingers. I later learned his life was truly dreadful. But you, with your innocent face and youthful glow, seem incapable of such things. Yet, I rarely see you now, and when I hear these terrible whispers, I don't know what to think. Why does the Duke of Berwick leave the room when you enter? Why do so many gentlemen avoid you? Lord Staveley told me recently that while you have artistic taste, no decent woman should be allowed to know you. He said it right in front of everyone, and it was horrible. Why is your friendship so destructive? Think of the young soldier who killed himself, or Sir Henry Ashton, who had to flee England in disgrace."
}

