parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3afd6d7aef37544f08505830",
  "rewritten_text": "I cannot say how long I remained in that state, but it must have been a significant duration. When I regained consciousness, dawn was breaking, and the balloon drifted at a tremendous altitude over an endless ocean, with no land visible on the horizon. Surprisingly, I felt little agony. A sense of budding madness colored my calm assessment of the situation. I studied my hands, wondering why the veins were swollen and the fingernails blackened. I examined my head, shaking and feeling it carefully to ensure it had not, as I feared, grown larger than the balloon itself. I checked my pockets, and finding my notebook and toothpick case missing, I felt inexplicably annoyed. I noticed pain in my left ankle, and the reality of my predicament began to dawn on me. Yet, I felt no horror. Instead, I experienced a smug satisfaction at the ingenuity I would soon display to escape. I never doubted my survival. I sat in deep thought, making the gestures of a man contemplating a complex problem from the comfort of an armchair. Finally, I reached behind me to unfasten the large iron buckle on my waistband. The three rusty teeth were difficult to move, but I managed to set them at a right angle. Holding this makeshift tool in my teeth, I began the exhausting process of untying my cravat, resting frequently until the task was finished."
}

