parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f2b2fa15f09acfeb10248fe9",
  "rewritten_text": "Her complexion shifted, and she fell silent. Mrs. Gardiner stood slightly behind her, and when she paused, he asked for the honor of an introduction to her companions. This gesture of courtesy caught her off guard, and she barely suppressed a smile, noting his eagerness to meet the very people his pride had once rejected. She wondered at his reaction once he realized their true identity, assuming he mistook them for high society. The introduction proceeded, and as she identified them as her relatives, she glanced at him, expecting him to flee from such embarrassing company. He was clearly surprised, yet he maintained his composure. Instead of leaving, he turned to walk with them and began speaking with Mr. Gardiner. Elizabeth felt a sense of triumph; it was satisfying to know she had family members who required no apology. She listened intently, taking pride in her uncle’s intelligence and refined manners. The discussion shifted to fishing, and Darcy politely invited him to fish on the estate whenever he liked, offering his own equipment and advice on the best spots. Mrs. Gardiner exchanged a look of amazement with Elizabeth. Though Elizabeth remained silent, she was deeply gratified, sensing the gesture was meant for her. She was bewildered by his transformation, questioning how his behavior could have softened so significantly. She doubted her past criticisms could have caused such a shift, refusing to believe he still harbored feelings for her. After walking for some time, the group paused by the river to examine a water plant. When they resumed, Mrs. Gardiner, tired from the morning’s walk, found Elizabeth’s support insufficient and switched to walking with her husband."
}

