parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fc2184062da224552bc8ca69",
  "rewritten_text": "Mary never wrote to Bath herself; the burden of maintaining a sluggish and frustrating correspondence with Elizabeth fell entirely to Anne. Breakfast had barely concluded when Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick arrived, as they had planned to take a final walk around Lyme. They were due to depart for Uppercross by one o'clock, intending to spend the interim outdoors together. Once they reached the street, Captain Benwick gravitated toward Anne. Their conversation the previous evening had encouraged him to seek her out again, and they walked for a while, debating the merits of Scott and Byron with the same inconclusive results as before, until the group shifted and Captain Harville took his place beside her. Miss Elliot, he said quietly, you have done a kindness by drawing that poor man out. I wish he had such company more often. It is unhealthy for him to remain so isolated, but we cannot leave him. No, Anne agreed, I can see that is impossible; but in time—we know what time does for grief, and you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend is still a recent mourner—only since last summer, I understand. True enough, he replied with a heavy sigh, only since June. And perhaps he did not learn of it until later. Not until the first week of August, when he returned from the Cape, having just been promoted on the Grappler. I was in Plymouth, dreading the news; he sent letters, but the Grappler was ordered to Portsmouth. The news had to reach him there, but who would break it? Not I. I would rather have been hanged. No one could do it but that good man, he said, gesturing toward Captain Wentworth. The Laconia had arrived in Plymouth the week before, safe from immediate deployment. Wentworth took the risk, requested leave, and without waiting for a reply, traveled day and night to Portsmouth. He rowed out to the Grappler immediately and stayed with him for a week. That is what he did; no one else could have saved poor James. You can imagine, Miss Elliot, how dear he is to us. Anne considered this with absolute certainty and replied with as much warmth as her feelings allowed, or as he seemed able to bear, for he was too moved to continue the subject, and when he spoke again, it was of something entirely different."
}

