parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "12f0e2ecabbbf6a638f7ee5b",
  "rewritten_text": "If I cannot survive on that, I will not ask for help. Is that acceptable? A massive roar of applause erupted, and the king’s voice rose above it: Remove his bonds and set him free! Let everyone, high and low, pay him homage, for he is now the king’s right hand, vested with authority and seated at the highest level of the throne! Now, banish this creeping night and restore the light so the world may bless you. I replied: It is nothing for a common man to be shamed, but it would dishonor the king if those who saw his minister humiliated did not also see him vindicated. If I might request that my clothes be returned. They are not suitable, the king interrupted. Bring him different attire; dress him like a prince! My plan worked. I needed to maintain the status quo until the eclipse was total, or they would pressure me to end the darkness, which I could not do. Sending for the clothes bought some time, but not enough, so I made another excuse. I suggested it would be natural for the king to reconsider his impulsive decision; therefore, I would let the darkness persist, and if he remained resolute after a reasonable time, I would dismiss it. No one was satisfied, but I held my ground. It grew increasingly dark while I struggled with those awkward sixth-century garments. Eventually, it became pitch black, and the crowd groaned in horror as cold, eerie breezes blew and stars appeared in the sky. When the eclipse finally became total, I was relieved, though everyone else was miserable. I announced: The king, by his silence, upholds the terms. I raised my hands, stood still for a moment, and declared with solemnity: Let the enchantment dissolve and pass away! There was no response in the graveyard silence of the darkness. But when the sun’s silver rim emerged moments later, the crowd erupted in a massive shout, rushing toward me with blessings and gratitude; Clarence was certainly among the first to reach me."
}

