  "request_id": "2721ed9389989cd2fef98151",
  "rewritten_text": "“Story!” He appeared to intentionally misinterpret her word, quickly adding, “Yes, clients; in banking, we refer to our associates as clients. He was a French gentleman, a man of science and great learning—a doctor.” “Not from Beauvais?” “Indeed, from Beauvais. Like your father, Monsieur Manette, he was a man of standing in Paris. I had the privilege of knowing him there. Our connection was professional, yet private. I was stationed at our French branch for twenty years.” “At that time—may I ask when, sir?” “I refer to twenty years past, miss. He wed an Englishwoman, and I served as a trustee. His matters, like those of many French families, were managed by Tellson’s. I have acted as trustee for countless clients in similar fashion. These are strictly professional ties, miss; devoid of friendship, interest, or sentiment. I move from one to the next throughout my career just as I do during a workday; in essence, I possess no emotions; I am merely a machine. To continue—” “But this is my father’s story, sir; and I suspect,” she said, her brow furrowed with focus, “that when I was orphaned after my mother survived my father by only two years, it was you who escorted me to England. I am nearly certain it was you.” Mr. Lorry accepted her hesitant hand, raising it to his lips with formal grace. He guided the young woman back to her seat, and, gripping the chair-back with his left hand while using his right to stroke his chin, adjust his wig, or emphasize his points, he gazed down at her as she looked up at him. “Miss Manette, it was I. You will see how accurately I described myself as emotionless, and how my connections are purely professional, when you consider that I have not seen you since. You have remained a ward of Tellson’s, and I have been occupied with the firm’s affairs ever since. Feelings! I have neither the time nor the opportunity for them. I spend my entire existence, miss, operating a vast financial mangle.”"
}

