  "rewritten_text": "On that matter, I shall remain silent, and you may be certain that I will never speak unkindly of you once we are married. Elizabeth had to interrupt him. You are moving too quickly, sir, she exclaimed. You forget that I have not yet answered. Let me do so now. Thank you for the compliment, and I appreciate the honor of your proposal, but I must decline. Mr. Collins waved his hand formally. I know it is common for young women to reject a man at first even when they intend to accept him, and sometimes they refuse several times. I am not discouraged and hope to lead you to the altar soon. Elizabeth replied that his hope was strange given her clear answer. I am not one of those women who risk their happiness by playing games. I am serious. You could not make me happy, and I am the last woman who would make you happy. Even Lady Catherine would agree that I am ill-suited for the role. Mr. Collins replied gravely that he doubted Lady Catherine would disapprove. When I see her again, I will speak highly of your modesty and other fine qualities. Mr. Collins, please do not praise me. You must let me judge for myself and believe what I say. I wish you happiness and wealth, and by refusing you, I am doing my best to ensure you find both. You have fulfilled your duty to your feelings regarding my family, so you may take the Longbourn estate when it falls to you without guilt. This matter is settled. She stood to leave, but Mr. Collins spoke again. When I address this subject again, I hope for a more favorable answer. I do not accuse you of cruelty, as I know it is the custom for women to reject a man at first, and you have likely said as much as is proper to encourage me."
}

