parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a4e74aadcc7041a96c285098",
  "rewritten_text": "Charles Evrémonde, known as Darnay, was finally brought to trial. The judges wore feathered hats, but the rough red cap and tricolor cockade were the standard attire. Observing the jury and the rowdy crowd, he felt as if the natural order had inverted, with criminals judging the innocent. The city's most vicious elements dominated the room, shouting, cheering, and dictating the outcome without restraint. Many men carried weapons, while some women brandished knives or daggers, and others ate or knitted. Among the knitters sat a woman beside Defarge, a man Darnay recognized from his arrival. She whispered to her husband, yet neither ever glanced at the prisoner, staring only at the jury with grim resolve. Below the President sat Doctor Manette, who, along with Mr. Lorry, remained dressed in ordinary clothes, unlike the others who had adopted the coarse style of the revolution. The prosecutor charged Darnay as an emigrant, subject to the death penalty under a decree that banished all such individuals. It mattered little that the law postdated his return; he was in France, and they demanded his life. The crowd roared for his execution, branding him an enemy of the Republic. When the President silenced the room, he questioned Darnay about his years in England. Darnay admitted to living there but denied being an emigrant in the legal sense. He argued that he had voluntarily abandoned his title and status, leaving France before the term was even defined, choosing to support himself through his own labor rather than exploit the burdened people of his homeland."
}

