parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ca826e1c8486a00f818f4655",
  "rewritten_text": "When Tom woke up, he was momentarily disoriented. He sat up, rubbed his eyes, and realized where he was. It was a cool, gray dawn, and the woods were filled with a profound, peaceful stillness. Nothing moved; the forest seemed lost in meditation. Dew clung to the grass, and the campfire was reduced to white ash with a thin trail of blue smoke rising into the air. Joe and Huck were still asleep. Soon, birds began to call to one another, followed by the rhythmic tapping of a woodpecker. As the morning light brightened, the forest came to life. Tom watched in wonder as nature stirred. A green inchworm crawled over a leaf, measuring the distance as it moved. Tom sat perfectly still, hoping the creature would crawl onto him, which would signify he was destined for a new suit of clothes—specifically, a flashy pirate costume. When the worm finally climbed onto his leg, he was delighted. Nearby, a line of ants marched past, one struggling to carry a dead spider up a tree. Tom whispered a rhyme to a ladybug, and when it flew away, he smiled, knowing how easily it was fooled. A tumblebug rolled its ball, curling up to play dead when Tom touched it. By now, the birds were active. A catbird sang from above, mimicking its neighbors, while a blue jay landed nearby to inspect the boys with curiosity. A gray squirrel and a large fox squirrel scurried past, pausing to chatter at the intruders, likely having never encountered humans before."
}

