parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6794a005c941c6266960fdda",
  "rewritten_text": "I don't rush him, risking a clumsy failure. Instead, I approach from the side, moving gradually so he never suspects my intent. Then, in a flash, he is on his back, completely bewildered by how it happened. That is how I handled brother Dowley. I began speaking in a relaxed, casual tone, as if merely passing time. No one could have predicted where my argument would lead. \"Friends, there are many strange things about law, custom, and the evolution of human opinion. Written laws fade, but unwritten ones are eternal. Consider the unwritten law of wages: they must rise gradually over the centuries. We know current wages, and we know what they were one or two hundred years ago. That history reveals the law of progress, allowing us to estimate wages from centuries past. We do not stop there; we apply this law to the future. I can predict wages for hundreds of years to come.\" \"What do you mean?\" \"In seven hundred years, wages here will be six times higher. Farmhands will earn three cents a day, and mechanics six.\" \"I wish I could live then!\" interrupted Smug the wheelwright, his eyes gleaming with greed. \"And that is not all; they will receive board as well, though it will be meager. Two hundred and fifty years later, mark my words, a mechanic's wages will be twenty cents a day. This is law, not guesswork.\" The group gasped in awe, and Dickon the mason murmured, his eyes and hands raised in disbelief."
}

