  "rewritten_text": "My father stayed by my side to comfort me, replacing my bitter memories with hopeful thoughts. It felt sweet to grieve simply because he was there to dry my tears. He distracted me from my sorrow by comparing it to the despair he felt when he lost my mother. I shuddered at his description of that time; he had a poet's imagination, and his account of his emotional turmoil was so vivid that I trembled as I listened. I marveled that he could ever return to normal life after experiencing such intense, almost supernatural feelings. His emotions seemed too vast for a human heart, better suited to a spirit of fire and earth than a mortal man. Yet, he had changed. He was now entirely gentle and loving, and his warm smile showed that his heart was filled with kindness. Two months after my aunt died, we moved to London, where my father encouraged me to pursue more serious studies. He took great joy in my progress, assisting me with every lesson. We socialized often, and my father worked to make every day enjoyable. Our deep mutual love and respect made every moment special. We lived so fully that a single week felt like months, with every new experience adding excitement to our lives. We frequently went on excursions together, whether to see beautiful landscapes, admire art, or simply find amusement. I was always happiest when I was with him. I disliked it when others joined us, but if I looked at my father with concern, his tender gaze would instantly restore my happiness. Those hours of intense joy were brief, yet looking back on them through the fog of later grief, they feel as long as a lifetime."
}

