  "request_id": "7d99a986bb667b7f1e1a263a",
  "rewritten_text": "They brought in a distinguished-looking older gentleman and a younger man with his arm in a sling. The crowd cheered and mocked them, but I saw no humor in it, and I doubted the duke and the king would either. I expected them to turn pale, but they didn't. The duke acted oblivious, looking as content as a jug pouring buttermilk, while the king gazed sorrowfully at the newcomers, as if the existence of such frauds pained him deeply. He played the part perfectly. Local leaders gathered around the king to show their support. The older man looked completely bewildered. Soon, he began to speak, and I immediately noticed he sounded like a genuine Englishman, unlike the king's decent imitation. He addressed the crowd: This is an unexpected surprise, and I admit I am ill-prepared to respond. My brother and I have faced misfortune; he has a broken arm, and our luggage was mistakenly left at a town upriver last night. I am Harvey Wilks, brother of Peter, and this is his brother William, who is deaf and mute and cannot easily communicate with only one hand. We are who we claim to be, and I will prove it once our luggage arrives. Until then, we will wait at the hotel. As he and his brother left, the king laughed and scoffed, calling the broken arm and lost luggage convenient excuses for a fraud who had not learned to sign. The crowd laughed along, except for a few, including the doctor, a sharp-looking lawyer named Levi Bell who had just arrived from Louisville, and a large, rugged man who had been listening intently to both sides."
}

