  "rewritten_text": "He was a poet who published his first work at twenty-three, earning immediate and universal acclaim. His success was unprecedented, celebrated by both the public and the intellectual elite without a single critic. During this peak of fame, he met Elinor, a beautiful young heiress living with her guardian. They seemed perfectly matched. While Elinor lacked his poetic genius, she was noble, generous, and possessed a natural wisdom reflected in her deep blue eyes. They fell in love instantly, finding pure joy in their connection. Woodville, the son of a poor clergyman, was a celebrated figure, while Elinor was wealthy. Her guardian approved of their relationship, recognizing Woodville’s immense talent, but her father’s will prohibited her from marrying until she came of age. Though they had to wait until she turned twenty-one, they spent their time together in blissful anticipation, studying and planning their future. Woodville’s reputation continued to grow, and Elinor flourished under his guidance. With only two months left until her birthday, everything was ready for their wedding. Yet, such perfect happiness is rarely permitted in this world of sorrow. To allow a couple as ideal as they were to find complete fulfillment would have required a fundamental change in the nature of human existence."
}

