  "request_id": "cb183e95ef3cf9b6bdb191e8",
  "rewritten_text": "Count Rouvaloff stared at him in astonishment for a moment, then realizing his sincerity, scribbled an address on a slip of paper, initialed it, and passed it over. Scotland Yard would pay dearly for this information, he remarked. Lord Arthur laughed, declaring they would never obtain it. After shaking the Russian’s hand, he hurried out, checked the address, and instructed his driver to head for Soho Square. Dismissing the carriage, he wandered down Greek Street until he reached Bayle’s Court. Passing through the archway, he entered a strange dead-end street filled with a French laundry, where clotheslines crisscrossed the space, fluttering with white linen. He reached the end and knocked at a small green house. Following a pause, during which curious faces appeared in every window, a disheveled foreigner opened the door and demanded his business in broken English. Lord Arthur presented the paper from Count Rouvaloff. The man bowed and ushered him into a worn parlor. Shortly after, Herr Winckelkopf entered, wearing a wine-spotted napkin and holding a fork. Lord Arthur introduced himself as Robert Smith and requested an explosive clock. The German laughed, greeting him as Lord Arthur and mentioning he had seen him at Lady Windermere’s. He invited his guest to join him for breakfast, offering pâté and fine Rhine wine. Before Lord Arthur could recover from the shock of being identified, he was seated in the back room, enjoying excellent wine and conversing with the notorious conspirator. Herr Winckelkopf noted that explosive clocks were poor exports, as unreliable train schedules often caused them to detonate prematurely."
}

