parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "02af32b89a6871a4c63da6de",
  "rewritten_text": "“Tom!” Silence. “TOM!” Silence. “Where has that boy gone? You, TOM!” Still no reply. The elderly woman adjusted her glasses to peer around the room, though she rarely used them for spotting a mere boy; they were for show, not utility, and she could have seen just as clearly through iron lids. Puzzled, she muttered, loud enough for the room to hear, “If I catch you, I’ll—” She left the threat unfinished, busy jabbing her broom beneath the bed. She only managed to retrieve the cat. “I have never seen the likes of that child!” She stepped to the doorway to survey the garden of tomato plants and weeds. Tom was nowhere to be seen. She raised her voice to carry across the yard: “Y-o-u-u TOM!” A rustle behind her made her spin around, catching a small boy by his jacket just as he tried to bolt. “I should have checked the closet. What were you doing in there?” “Nothing.” “Nothing? Look at your hands and mouth. What is that?” “I don’t know, Aunt.” “I know. It is jam. I have told you forty times to leave it alone or I would tan your hide. Give me that switch.” As the rod hung in the air, the danger peaked. “Oh! Look behind you, Aunt!” The woman turned quickly, pulling her skirts aside. The boy vanished instantly, scaling the tall fence and leaping over. Aunt Polly stood stunned, then began to chuckle. “Confound the boy, will I never learn? Has he not tricked me enough that I should expect this? Yet old fools are the worst. You cannot teach an old dog new tricks. Still, he never repeats the same prank, so how is one to anticipate him? He seems to know exactly how far to push me before I lose my temper, and he knows that if he can distract me or make me laugh, my anger vanishes and I cannot strike him.”"
}

