parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e9894fa59290d3b939a32989",
  "rewritten_text": "You have fostered an artificial spirit in him, ma'am, which is inappropriate for his station. As the board, who are practical philosophers, would tell you, what business do paupers have with soul or spirit? It is enough that we provide them with living bodies. If you had fed the boy only gruel, this would never have occurred. Dear me, Mrs. Sowerberry exclaimed, piously looking toward the ceiling, this is the result of being generous. Her generosity to Oliver had consisted of giving him the scraps no one else would eat, so there was a certain irony in her accepting Mr. Bumble’s harsh accusation, of which she was entirely innocent. Ah, Mr. Bumble said, once she looked down, the only solution now is to leave him in the cellar until he is sufficiently starved, then keep him on gruel throughout his apprenticeship. He comes from bad stock. His mother arrived here having endured hardships that would have killed any decent woman. Hearing his mother mentioned, Oliver began kicking with such violence that no other sound could be heard. Sowerberry returned, and after the ladies explained the offense with calculated exaggerations, he unlocked the cellar door and dragged his rebellious apprentice out by the collar. Oliver’s clothes were torn, his face bruised, and his hair disheveled, yet he scowled at Noah, entirely undismayed. You are a fine young man, aren't you? Sowerberry said, shaking him and boxing his ears. He insulted my mother, Oliver replied. What if he did, you ungrateful wretch? Mrs. Sowerberry said. She deserved it and worse. She did not, Oliver insisted. She did, she countered. It is a lie, Oliver shouted. Mrs. Sowerberry burst into tears. This left Mr. Sowerberry no choice. Had he hesitated to punish Oliver severely, he would have been branded a brute and a terrible husband, according to the established precedents of marital disputes."
}

