parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "abc1b6456051b526275e1cbd",
  "rewritten_text": "Indeed, and I would have claimed him had I been larger; I spotted him initially. Who captured him? It was an elderly stranger who traded his opportunity for forty dollars because he had to travel upriver immediately. Imagine that! I would have waited seven years for such a prize. I agreed, though I wondered if the deal was legitimate given the low price. It is perfectly honest, he insisted. I saw the flyer myself; it describes him precisely, including the plantation he escaped from near New Orleans. There is no doubt about the legality of this venture. Could you spare some chewing tobacco? I had none, so he departed. I retreated to the raft and sat in the shelter to ponder, but I reached no conclusion. I exhausted my mind trying to find a solution, yet everything seemed ruined. After our long journey and all our efforts for those villains, it was devastating that they would betray Jim for forty miserable dollars, condemning him to lifelong slavery among strangers. I briefly considered writing to Tom Sawyer to inform Miss Watson of Jim's location, thinking it better for him to be enslaved near his family. I quickly dismissed this, realizing she would be furious at his perceived ingratitude and likely sell him further down the river. Furthermore, people despise an ungrateful runaway, and Jim would suffer constant humiliation. Then there was my own reputation; if word spread that Huck Finn assisted a slave in escaping, I would be utterly shamed if I ever encountered anyone from home. That is the way of it: a person commits a shameful act and then avoids the consequences, believing secrecy prevents disgrace. That was my exact situation. The more I contemplated this, the more my conscience tormented me, leaving me feeling increasingly wicked and degraded."
}

