  "rewritten_text": "No sympathy was offered to Ireland. This oversight was returned in kind; when Lady Elliot passed away, Kellynch received no condolences, suggesting the Dalrymples considered the family tie severed. Restoring this connection was a priority for both Lady Russell and Mr. Elliot. Family ties were worth keeping, and Lady Dalrymple, having rented a home in Laura Place for three months, promised a sophisticated social life. Lady Russell had heard her described as charming, and they hoped to renew the acquaintance without sacrificing their dignity. Sir Walter insisted on handling it himself, sending a grand, overly explanatory letter to his cousin. Though Lady Russell and Mr. Elliot found the letter embarrassing, it succeeded in eliciting a brief, scrawled reply from the Dowager Viscountess, expressing her pleasure at the prospect of meeting. The effort paid off. They visited Laura Place and displayed the calling cards of the Viscountess and Miss Carteret prominently, frequently boasting of their high-status cousins to everyone they met. Anne felt embarrassed. Even if the women had been pleasant, the fuss was humiliating, but they were entirely unremarkable. Lady Dalrymple was only considered charming because she was polite to everyone, while Miss Carteret was so dull and awkward that her social standing was the only reason she was tolerated. Lady Russell admitted they were disappointing, yet insisted the connection held value. When Anne questioned Mr. Elliot, he conceded they lacked personal merit but argued their social status and ability to host others made them valuable. Anne smiled and replied that she defined good company as clever, well-informed people who could hold an engaging conversation."
}

