parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "14adbe59a8a73a9118ba932d",
  "rewritten_text": "I cannot determine how long I stayed in that condition. It was certainly a significant duration, for when I regained consciousness, dawn was arriving, the balloon soared at an immense altitude above an endless sea, and no land appeared anywhere along the horizon. My feelings upon waking were not as painful as one might expect. In fact, a touch of budding insanity colored the detached way I assessed my predicament. I lifted my hands to my face, one by one, pondering why my veins were swollen and my fingernails turned a ghastly black. I then inspected my head, shaking it and touching it thoroughly until I felt certain it had not grown larger than the balloon, as I had briefly feared. With a knowing air, I searched my pockets, and upon finding my tablets and toothpick case missing, I felt strangely annoyed by my inability to explain their loss. I then noticed a sharp discomfort in my left ankle, and a vague awareness of my circumstances began to dawn on me. Curiously, I felt neither shock nor terror. If I experienced any emotion, it was a smug satisfaction regarding the ingenuity I would soon employ to escape this trap; I never once doubted my eventual survival. I sat in deep thought for several minutes, recalling how I pressed my lips together and touched my nose, mimicking the gestures of a man pondering complex matters from the comfort of his chair. Having gathered my thoughts, I carefully reached behind me to unfasten the heavy iron buckle on my trousers. The three teeth were rusted and stiff, but I managed to pivot them to a right angle and keep them steady. Gripping this tool between my teeth, I began to untie my cravat, a task that required several pauses before I finally succeeded."
}

