parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "07b2f09395149dcfca8fb835",
  "rewritten_text": "Mary never wrote to Bath herself, leaving the tedious task of corresponding with Elizabeth entirely to Anne. Shortly after breakfast, Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick joined them for a final walk around Lyme before the group departed for Uppercross at one o'clock. Once they reached the street, Captain Benwick gravitated toward Anne. They continued their previous discussion about Scott and Byron, finding themselves just as unable to agree on the poets' merits as before, until the group shifted and Captain Harville took his place beside her. Speaking quietly, he told Anne that she had done a kindness by drawing Benwick out of his shell. He worried about his friend's isolation but noted they could not bear to be apart. Anne suggested that time would eventually ease his grief, noting that he had only lost his partner the previous summer. Harville sighed, confirming it was only June. He explained that Benwick did not learn of the tragedy until August, when he returned from the Cape. Harville admitted he could not bring himself to deliver the news, so Captain Wentworth had taken on the burden. Wentworth traveled day and night to Portsmouth to reach Benwick, staying by his side for a week to help him through the shock. Harville emphasized how much this proved Wentworth's loyalty. Anne agreed wholeheartedly, though her own emotions made it difficult to say much more. Harville, clearly moved by the memory, soon changed the subject to something else."
}

