parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "504cb9fdf8199438377ecdae",
  "rewritten_text": "Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall in Somersetshire read only one book for pleasure: the Baronetage. It occupied his idle hours and comforted him in distress. It stirred his admiration for the oldest titles and allowed him to view the newer, more common creations of the last century with pity and disdain. Most of all, he never tired of reading his own entry. The book always fell open to this page: ELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL. Walter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, Gloucestershire. By this lady (who died in 1800), he had issue: Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; and Mary, born November 20, 1791. Sir Walter had updated the original text, adding the date of his wife’s death and noting that Mary married Charles, heir to Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, Somerset, on December 16, 1810. The entry continued with the family’s history: their origins in Cheshire, their service as high sheriffs, their parliamentary seats, and their loyalty rewarded by a baronetcy in the first year of Charles II. It listed the various marriages and concluded with their coat of arms, motto, and the location of Kellynch Hall. In his own hand, Sir Walter had added: Heir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great-grandson of the second Sir Walter. Vanity defined Sir Walter’s character, both regarding his appearance and his social standing. He had been a handsome youth and remained a striking man at fifty-four. Few women were as obsessed with their looks as he was, and he took as much pride in his social position as any servant of a newly titled lord. He believed beauty was second only to a baronetcy, and he held himself in the highest regard for possessing both. His looks and rank were his greatest assets, having secured him a wife far superior to anything he deserved."
}

