parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "aa6367008f4dbdd2b50d69c2",
  "rewritten_text": "It was past two when I suddenly found myself near the lake, where a small boat was tied up. Seeing my father and aunt on the lawn nearby, I jumped into the skiff. Used to such things, I pushed off and rowed with all my might. Dressed in white with only a tartan shawl over me and my hair flowing, I moved so quickly that my father later told me I looked more like a spirit than a girl. As I reached the shore, he caught the boat, and I leaped into his arms. My life truly began then. Everything shifted from dullness to pure joy. The happiness of being with my father surpassed my wildest hopes. We were inseparable, and our conversations never ended. During his sixteen years away, he had traveled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, living among people few Europeans ever saw. His stories about their customs and the landscapes he visited filled our hours when we weren't discussing our own future. The sound of his affection was so new that I listened intently as he described his feelings during those long years of silence. He told me that at first, he could not bear to think of me, but as his grief faded and hope returned, he constantly imagined my small form. He felt as though the northern breeze carried my spirit, and he dreamed of taking me to a peaceful island. As he traveled home, his excitement was mixed with painful fear that I might be dead. But he told me, no, it was not so; he had his Mathilda, his comfort, and his hope."
}

