parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e0a20c9e6875ffbc19211e20",
  "rewritten_text": "The intensifying intensity of Mr. Wopsle’s gaze and his evident mental turmoil were so puzzling that I remained baffled long after he had vanished into the clouds within a giant watch-case. I was still pondering the matter an hour later when I exited the theater and discovered him waiting by the entrance. We shook hands and walked down the street together. I remarked that I had noticed him observing me. He replied that he had indeed seen me, but asked who else was present. When I pressed him, he appeared dazed, insisting he could swear to the identity of a man he had seen. Alarmed, I urged him to clarify. He mused that he might have noticed the man even without my presence. I instinctively scanned my surroundings, feeling a sudden chill. He assured me the man had departed before he did. Given my own suspicions, I grew wary of the actor, fearing he might be trying to trick me into a confession, so I remained silent. He then mentioned a strange notion that the man had been with me, sitting behind me like a phantom, until he realized I was unaware of him. This renewed my unease, yet I stayed quiet, suspecting he might be attempting to link these comments to Provis, though I was certain Provis had not been there. He noted my confusion and remarked on the strangeness of the situation. When I prompted him, he asked if I recalled that Christmas Day from my childhood when he dined at Gargery’s and soldiers arrived to have handcuffs repaired. I confirmed I remembered it clearly. He asked if I also recalled the pursuit of the two convicts, during which we joined the hunt, Gargery carried me, and I struggled to keep pace with him as he led the way."
}

