parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6d862c1404233133dd163c4d",
  "rewritten_text": "Please consider, ma’am, Oliver pleaded, tears welling despite his resistance. Think of how young and kind she is, and the joy she brings to everyone. I am certain that for your sake, for her own, and for those she makes happy, she will not perish. Heaven would never allow her to die so young. Hush, Mrs. Maylie replied, resting a hand on his head. You speak like a child, yet you remind me of my duty. I had momentarily forgotten it, but I hope for forgiveness; I am old and have witnessed enough suffering to understand the pain of losing those we love. I also know that the youngest and best are not always spared. This should comfort us, for Heaven is just, and such trials remind us that a brighter world exists, reached quickly. God’s will be done; He knows how deeply I love her. Oliver was surprised to see Mrs. Maylie suppress her grief with a single effort, standing tall and composed. He was even more astonished that this resolve held throughout the following days of care, as she performed her duties steadily and seemingly cheerfully. He was young and did not yet understand the strength of a resolute mind under pressure, a strength often unknown even to its possessor. A restless night followed. By morning, Mrs. Maylie’s fears were confirmed; Rose had fallen into a dangerous fever. We must act, Oliver, and avoid useless sorrow, Mrs. Maylie said, looking firmly at him. This letter must be sent immediately to Mr. Losberne. Take it to the market town four miles away by the footpath, and have it dispatched by horseback to Chertsey. The innkeepers will handle it, and I know I can trust you to ensure it is done. Oliver could not speak, but his expression showed his eagerness to depart."
}

