parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e578b03e5b2b9e8f34ad6d4c",
  "rewritten_text": "Further chances to observe them were inevitable. Anne spent enough time with the quartet to form her own conclusions, though she kept them to herself, knowing they would please neither spouse. While Louisa appeared to be the favorite, Anne suspected Captain Wentworth was not truly in love with either sister. Their feelings were merely a fleeting infatuation, though they might eventually lead to genuine affection. Charles Hayter seemed to sense his own neglect, yet Henrietta remained indecisive. Anne wished she could warn them of the potential consequences, though she believed none of them acted with malice. It comforted her to think the Captain was oblivious to the distress he caused; he showed no arrogance and likely remained ignorant of Charles Hayter’s prior claims. His only fault lay in welcoming the dual attentions of both young women. Eventually, Charles Hayter retreated. Three days passed without a visit, and he even declined a dinner invitation. When Mr. Musgrove found him buried in books, the family worried he was overworking himself. Mary hoped Henrietta had rejected him, while her husband expected his return daily. Anne simply thought Charles Hayter was acting prudently. One morning, while the men were out shooting, the sisters from the Great House visited the cottage. It was a beautiful November day, and the Miss Musgroves arrived merely to announce a long walk, assuming Mary would decline. When Mary insisted on joining them out of pride, Anne could see the sisters were disappointed. She marveled at the family’s rigid habit of sharing every activity, regardless of whether it was truly welcome or convenient."
}

