parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ca468d97240352074a0d8afd",
  "rewritten_text": "Recent reports from Rotterdam suggest the city is in a state of intense intellectual agitation. Events have occurred there so unexpected, so novel, and so contrary to established beliefs, that I am certain all of Europe is currently in an uproar, with the fields of physics, reason, and astronomy thrown into total disarray. It appears that on a certain day, a large crowd gathered in the Exchange square of Rotterdam for reasons not clearly stated. The weather was unusually warm for the season with little wind, and the people were in good spirits, occasionally cooled by brief showers falling from white clouds scattered across the blue sky. Around noon, however, a noticeable stir rippled through the assembly. A sudden clamor of voices erupted, and instantly, ten thousand faces turned toward the heavens. Ten thousand pipes were removed from ten thousand mouths, and a roar, comparable only to the sound of Niagara Falls, echoed loudly throughout the city. The cause of this commotion soon became clear. Emerging from behind a large cloud into the clear blue sky was a strange, solid object, shaped so whimsically and constructed so oddly that the sturdy citizens below could neither comprehend nor sufficiently admire it. What could it be? What could it possibly mean? No one knew, and no one could guess. Even the mayor, Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk, had no clue to solve the mystery. Lacking a better course of action, everyone replaced their pipes, tilted their heads to study the phenomenon, puffed, paused, paced about, and grunted significantly before repeating the process. Meanwhile, the object of such curiosity and smoke descended lower toward the city. Within minutes, it was close enough to be clearly identified. It was, without a doubt, a type of balloon, yet surely no such craft had ever been seen in Rotterdam before."
}

