parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0330b2410b10359fd74b843e",
  "rewritten_text": "The stars were still bright when I woke, and Taurus high in the southern sky marked midnight. I had been troubled by nightmares. I dreamt I was at Timon’s final feast, arriving hungry only to find the dishes filled with nothing but steam. I fled from my host, who took the form of Raymond, while the objects he threw at me seemed to reek of decay. His figure distorted into a massive phantom marked by the sign of plague, growing until it threatened to shatter the very sky. The nightmare became unbearable, and I forced myself awake to regain my senses. My first thought was of Perdita; I had to return to her, to support her and guide her through her grief with both duty and tenderness. Guided by the stars, I turned away from the ruins of the Golden City and managed to escape. Outside the walls, I borrowed a horse from a group of soldiers. The landscape had shifted; the encampment was gone, and the scattered remnants of the army moved about with faces full of shock and despair. With a heavy heart, I entered the palace, afraid to move or speak. Perdita sat on the marble floor, her head bowed, her hair wild, and her fingers twisting together. She was pale, her face twisted in agony. When she looked up, the brief spark of hope in her eyes was heartbreaking. I could not speak, and my forced smile only confirmed her fears. She understood, and her head dropped again. When I finally found my voice, it frightened her; she did not want her misery confirmed by words. Instead, she tried to distract me, rising from the floor and whispering, “Hush, Clara is finally sleeping after so much crying; we must not wake her.”"
}

