parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e5829b950fd3b5563594c422",
  "rewritten_text": "With these screams, many women, driven into a blind frenzy, spun around, striking and tearing at their own companions until they collapsed in a passionate swoon, saved from being trampled only by their men. Yet, not a moment was wasted. Foulon was at the Hotel de Ville and might be released. Not if Saint Antoine remembered its own suffering and insults. Armed people poured out of the district so quickly, dragging even the stragglers with them, that within fifteen minutes, only a few elderly women and crying children remained in Saint Antoine. By then, the crowd was choking the Hall of Examination where the wicked old man was held, spilling into the surrounding streets. The Defarges, The Vengeance, and Jacques Three were in the front, near him in the hall. Look, Madame cried, pointing with her knife. See the old villain tied with ropes. Tying a bundle of grass to his back was well done. Let him eat it now. She tucked her knife away and clapped as if at a theater. The people behind her explained her satisfaction to those further back, and soon the neighboring streets echoed with applause. During hours of tedious debate, Madame Defarge’s impatience was mirrored by the crowd, relayed by men who had climbed the building’s exterior to watch through the windows. Finally, the sun rose high, casting a ray of light onto the prisoner’s head. The crowd could no longer be contained; the barrier of dust and tension vanished, and Saint Antoine seized him. The news spread instantly. Defarge leaped over a railing and table to grab the wretch, while Madame Defarge twisted her hand into his ropes. Before the others could even reach him, a cry went up across the city: Bring him out!"
}

