parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c49328078c8371f2a70224d6",
  "rewritten_text": "We eventually decided to live together during my time in the city. Since my finances were more stable than his, I paid to rent and furnish a decaying, grotesque mansion in a desolate part of the Faubourg St. Germain, a place long abandoned due to superstitions we chose not to investigate. If our daily routine had been public knowledge, people would have considered us mad, though perhaps harmlessly so. We lived in total seclusion, admitting no visitors. We kept our location secret from my former acquaintances, and Dupin had long since cut ties with Parisian society. We lived entirely within our own private world. It was a strange whim of my friend to be obsessed with the night, and I surrendered to his eccentricities completely. Since the darkness could not always be with us, we faked it. At dawn, we would close the heavy shutters of our old home and light a pair of scented candles that cast only a ghastly, dim glow. By their light, we spent our time reading, writing, or talking until the clock signaled the arrival of true night. Then, we would walk the streets arm in arm, discussing our earlier topics or wandering until late, seeking the mental stimulation that comes from observing the city’s shifting lights and shadows. During these walks, I admired Dupin’s remarkable analytical ability. He clearly took great pleasure in exercising it, often boasting with a low chuckle that most people had windows in their chests, allowing him to see right through them. He would follow such claims with startling proofs of his intimate knowledge of my own thoughts. At these moments, his manner was cold and detached, his eyes vacant, and his voice, usually a rich tenor, rose to a sharp treble that would have seemed petulant if not for his deliberate, precise way of speaking."
}

