parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "759873e9e84d85e43700b0f0",
  "rewritten_text": "Though Perdita’s name went unspoken, I knew his indecision stemmed from his love for her. Who was more worthy of his affection than my noble sister? She possessed the regal grace of a queen and loved him deeply, even as disappointment dampened her feelings and ambition conflicted with his own. We arrived at the House that evening. Raymond seemed lighthearted and carefree, despite knowing his future would be decided during the upcoming debate. A roar like a thousand swarms of bees greeted us as we entered the coffee-room. Politicians gathered in groups, their faces anxious and their voices intense. The aristocratic party, representing England’s wealthiest and most influential men, seemed calm, as the debate would proceed without their direct interference. Near the fireplace stood Ryland and his followers. Ryland, a man of humble origins who had amassed a fortune from his father’s manufacturing business, had spent his life strengthening the power of the people. Initially, he had not minded Lord Raymond’s influence, as it weakened his political rivals. Now, however, things had gone too far. The lesser nobility welcomed the return of a monarchy, hoping to regain their lost status. The spirit of royalty was reviving, and many people were eager to submit to it once more. While some stood firm as pillars of the republic, the term itself had lost its appeal to the masses, many of whom craved the glitter of a crown. Ryland was ready to fight back, declaring that he would no longer tolerate this movement and intended to sweep away the influence Raymond had gained. When Raymond entered the room, his supporters cheered. They crowded around him, calculating their strength and discussing the new members who had yet to declare their allegiance."
}

