parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f025e002c011868c8d2434ea",
  "rewritten_text": "Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall in Somersetshire was a man who read only the Baronetage for his own entertainment. It provided him with distraction during idle moments and comfort when troubled. His mind was stirred to admiration by the oldest titles, while domestic worries shifted to pity and disdain as he scanned the numerous creations of the previous century. If all else failed, he could always find his own history, which held his unwavering interest. The book naturally fell open to this entry: ELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL. Walter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, Gloucestershire. By this lady, who passed away in 1800, he had issue: Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; and Mary, born November 20, 1791. This was the original printed text, but Sir Walter had updated it for his family’s benefit by adding Mary’s marriage to Charles Musgrove of Uppercross on December 16, 1810, and precisely noting the date of his wife’s death. The text continued with the standard account of the family’s origins and rise: their early settlement in Cheshire, mentions in Dugdale, service as high sheriffs, parliamentary representation, loyalty, and the baronetcy granted in the first year of Charles II, along with the names of all the women they had married. This spanned two elegant pages, ending with the family arms and motto, the location of Kellynch Hall, and Sir Walter’s own handwritten addition: Heir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great-grandson of the second Sir Walter. Vanity defined Sir Walter Elliot’s entire character, both regarding his appearance and his social standing. He had been strikingly handsome in his youth and remained a very fine-looking man at fifty-four. Few women were as preoccupied with their looks as he was, and no valet to a newly minted lord could be more pleased with his social position. He viewed beauty as a blessing second only to his baronetcy, and as the man who possessed both, he was the constant subject of his own deepest devotion. His handsome features and high rank were well worth his affection, as they had surely secured him a wife far superior to anyone he deserved."
}

