parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a01f11570477d4f73023db0c",
  "rewritten_text": "April 7th. I woke early and, to my delight, finally saw what must be the North Pole. It was directly beneath me, yet I had ascended so high that I could see nothing clearly. Based on my altitude readings between April 2nd and the moment my barometer failed, I estimate that by 4:00 A.M. on April 7th, I had reached at least 7,254 miles above sea level. This figure may seem vast, but it is likely an underestimate. Regardless, I could see the Earth’s entire diameter; the northern hemisphere appeared like a flat map, with the equator forming my horizon. You can imagine that the unexplored Arctic regions, while directly below, were too small and distant to examine in detail. Still, what I saw was remarkable. North of the known world, an unbroken sheet of ice stretches out. It flattens, then levels into a plane, and finally becomes concave, ending at the Pole in a sharp, circular center. This spot, which spanned about sixty-five seconds of arc from my vantage point, was darker than the rest of the hemisphere, sometimes appearing as an impenetrable black. By noon, this circle had shrunk, and by 7:00 P.M., it vanished as my balloon drifted toward the equator. April 8th. The Earth’s apparent size has noticeably decreased, and its color and overall appearance have shifted significantly."
}

