{
  "request_id": "450e5c48bd4612e3383c252c",
  "rewritten_text": "With their agreement settled, they toasted the contract with another cup of peppermint tea, which helped calm the lady’s nerves. As she drank, she informed Mr. Bumble that the old woman had passed away. Very good, he said, sipping his drink. I will stop by Sowerberry’s on my way home and tell him to collect the body tomorrow. Was that what upset you, my dear? It was nothing in particular, she replied evasively. It must have been something, Mr. Bumble insisted. Won’t you tell your own B.? Not now, she said. One of these days, after we are married. After we are married! Mr. Bumble exclaimed. It was not some rude pauper, was it? No, no, my love, she quickly interrupted. If I thought any of them had dared to look at your lovely face, he continued, clenching his fist. They would not have dared, she replied. They had better not, he said. Let any man, local or otherwise, try it, and he will not do it twice. Though his words were not exactly romantic, his aggressive gestures convinced her of his devotion, and she admiringly called him a dove. The dove then turned up his collar, put on his hat, and after an affectionate embrace, stepped out into the cold night. He paused briefly in the men’s ward to insult the paupers, just to ensure he still had the necessary harshness to be a workhouse master. Satisfied with his temperament, he walked toward the undertaker’s shop, dreaming of his future promotion. Mr. and Mrs. Sowerberry were out, and Noah Claypole was too lazy to close the shop, even though it was late. Mr. Bumble tapped his cane on the counter, but received no answer. Seeing a light in the back parlor, he peeked inside and was quite surprised by what he saw."
}

