  "request_id": "1dbf759047242f94a9cdc555",
  "rewritten_text": "I wish we had a donkey. It would be perfect for Jane, Miss Bates, and me to ride, with my dear husband walking alongside. I really must discuss buying one with him. In the country, I consider it a necessity; no matter how many interests a woman has, she cannot stay indoors forever, and long walks are difficult—there is always dust in summer and mud in winter. You will find neither between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and it is perfectly dry right now. Still, come by donkey if you prefer; you can borrow Mrs. Cole’s. I want everything to be exactly to your liking. I am sure you do. I truly appreciate you, my friend. Beneath your dry, blunt exterior, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you are quite the character. Believe me, Knightley, I am fully aware of your thoughtfulness in planning this. You have suggested exactly what I wanted. Mr. Knightley had another reason to avoid sitting in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse and Emma to join, and he knew that eating outdoors would inevitably make Mr. Woodhouse ill. He could not let him be tempted into misery under the guise of a pleasant morning drive. He was invited in good faith, with no hidden traps. He agreed. He had not visited Donwell in two years. He thought that on a fine morning, he, Emma, and Harriet could go; he could sit with Mrs. Weston while the girls walked the gardens. He doubted the ground would be damp in the middle of the day. He would love to see the old house again and meet the Eltons and other neighbors. He saw no objection to the trip. He thought Mr. Knightley’s invitation was kind and sensible—much better than dining out, which he disliked. Mr. Knightley was fortunate that everyone agreed so readily. The invitation was so well received that everyone seemed to take it as a personal compliment. Emma and Harriet expressed great excitement, and Mr. Weston promised to bring Frank along if possible—a gesture of approval that Mr. Knightley could have done without. He was forced to say he would be glad to see him, and Mr. Weston vowed to write immediately to persuade him."
}

