parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "df3db120a617cdf5e801702e",
  "rewritten_text": "In that harsh attack on Chantilly published in yesterday’s Musée, the writer made shameful comments about the cobbler changing his name when he became an actor. He also quoted a Latin line we have discussed before: Perdidit antiquum litera prima sonum. I had explained that this line refers to Orion, once spelled Urion, and I knew you remembered it. It was obvious you would connect Orion with Chantilly. I saw by your smile that you did, and that you were thinking of the poor cobbler’s death. You had been walking with a stoop, but then you stood up straight, and I knew you were considering Chantilly’s small stature. I interrupted your thoughts to say that since he was such a tiny man, he would be better suited for the Théâtre des Variétés. Shortly after, we were reading the evening Gazette des Tribunaux when a report caught our attention. Extraordinary Murders. At three this morning, residents of the Quartier St. Roch were woken by terrifying screams from the fourth floor of a house on the Rue Morgue, where Madame L’Espanaye and her daughter, Camille, lived. After failing to enter normally, neighbors and two police officers broke down the door with a crowbar. The screaming had stopped, but as they climbed the stairs, they heard two rough voices arguing loudly. By the second landing, those sounds also ceased, and the house went silent. The group searched the rooms and forced open a locked back bedroom on the fourth floor. The scene inside shocked everyone. The room was in total chaos, with furniture smashed and scattered. The bed had been stripped and thrown into the middle of the floor, and a blood-stained razor lay on a chair."
}

