parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cf51663f145c944ca29a52ed",
  "rewritten_text": "No raising him by hand then. Not at all! Joe offered me more gravy, but I was afraid to accept. He was a great burden to you, ma'am, Mrs. Hubble said, sympathizing with my sister. Burden? my sister repeated. She then listed every illness I had caused, every sleepless night I had forced on her, every fall I had taken, and every time she had wished me dead only for me to stubbornly survive. I think the Romans must have annoyed each other with their noses, which perhaps made them such a restless people. Regardless, Mr. Wopsle’s Roman nose irritated me so much during my sister’s lecture that I wanted to pull it until he screamed. Yet, my suffering was nothing compared to the dread I felt when the silence following her speech left everyone staring at me with disgust. Still, Mr. Pumblechook said, steering the conversation back, boiled pork is rich, isn't it? Have some brandy, uncle, my sister offered. It had finally happened. He would realize the brandy was weak, and I would be ruined. I gripped the table leg under the cloth and waited for my fate. My sister brought the stone bottle and poured his drink. The man toyed with his glass, looking at it against the light and prolonging my agony while the others cleared the table. I could not look away. Holding the table leg tightly, I watched him smile, tilt his head back, and swallow the drink. Suddenly, the room erupted in shock as he jumped up, performed a frantic, coughing dance, and bolted out the door. Through the window, I saw him gasping and making hideous faces as if he had lost his mind. I held on tight while my sister and Joe ran to help him."
}

