  "rewritten_text": "Summer arrived, and under the sun’s intense heat, the plague unleashed its lethal reach across the world. Nations succumbed and perished. Abundant crops rotted in the fields, while the desperate parent, seeking food for their children, collapsed dead in the furrows. Majestic forests swayed above the dying, whose cries disrupted the peaceful woods. Birds flew and deer rested undisturbed, while livestock wandered from abandoned stables to graze in the wheat; death claimed only humanity. As summer deepened, so did our terror. My beloved Idris and I looked at each other and our children. I promised, “We will save them, Idris. Years from now, we will tell them of these fears, long since passed. Even if they are the only ones left, they will survive, their health and spirits intact.” Our eldest son sensed the surrounding tragedy and occasionally asked about the widespread ruin, yet his youthful joy soon eclipsed his worries. Evelyn, a cheerful, playful toddler, filled our halls with laughter, his innocence untouched by sorrow. Clara, our gentle, lovely daughter, was our comfort. She dedicated herself to nursing the sick, aiding the elderly, and cheering the young. She moved through our home like a celestial spirit, brightening our darkest moments. Though she was modest and playful, we marveled at the heroism and wisdom hidden within her grace. The summer dragged on, as we prayed winter would at least slow the disease. We dared not hope it would vanish entirely; to speak such a thought aloud was to invite a flood of tears, revealing how profound our fear had become and how fragile our hope remained."
}

