parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "35a5ff7515d44a5b36c85b34",
  "rewritten_text": "Upon my return, I found this letter from my father: My dear Victor, you have likely been waiting for me to set a date for your homecoming. I considered writing only a brief note, but that would be a cruel kindness. I cannot bear to imagine your shock if you arrived expecting a joyful welcome only to find us in tears. How can I tell you of our tragedy? I know you still care for our family, and I cannot spare you this pain. I wish I could prepare you, but I know it is impossible; even now, your eyes are racing across the page to find the terrible news. William is dead. That sweet, gentle, happy child has been murdered. I will not try to comfort you, but will simply explain what happened. Last Thursday, May 7th, I went for a walk in Plainpalais with my niece and your brothers. The evening was warm, so we stayed out longer than usual. By dusk, we realized William and Ernest were missing. We waited on a bench until Ernest returned alone, asking if we had seen his brother. He explained that they had been playing hide-and-seek, but William had vanished and never returned. We were alarmed and searched until dark, when Elizabeth suggested he might have gone home. He was not there. We went back out with torches, as I could not bear the thought of my boy lost in the cold night. Elizabeth was in agony. At five in the morning, I found my beautiful boy, who had been so full of life the day before, lying dead on the grass. The marks of a killer’s fingers were on his neck. We brought him home, and my expression revealed the truth to Elizabeth. She insisted on seeing the body. I tried to stop her, but she pushed into the room, examined the victim’s neck, and cried out, O God!"
}

