parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a30a2d8a5ac6bb98278f21e6",
  "rewritten_text": "“Indeed, Mr. Bumble,” he finally remarked, “it is true that under the current dietary regulations, coffins have become somewhat slimmer and shallower than before; yet we require a margin of gain, Mr. Bumble. Quality wood is costly, and the iron fittings arrive by canal from Birmingham.” “True,” Mr. Bumble agreed, “every business has its difficulties. A reasonable profit is certainly justified.” “Naturally,” the undertaker answered, “and if I miss a gain on one item, I recover it eventually—he! he! he!” “Quite so,” said Mr. Bumble. “Still,” the undertaker added, returning to his previous point, “I face a significant challenge: the stouter individuals perish the fastest. Those who were once prosperous and paid taxes are the first to succumb upon entering the workhouse; and let me assure you, Mr. Bumble, a few extra inches beyond my estimate significantly diminishes my earnings, especially with a family to support.” As Mr. Sowerberry spoke with the righteous frustration of an aggrieved man, and Mr. Bumble sensed a slight against the parish’s integrity, the latter decided to shift the conversation. Oliver Twist being on his mind, he introduced the subject. “By the way,” Mr. Bumble inquired, “do you know anyone in need of a boy? A parish apprentice who is currently a burden, a millstone around the parish’s neck? The terms are generous, Mr. Sowerberry.” As he spoke, Mr. Bumble tapped his cane against the sign above, striking the words “five pounds” printed in large Roman letters. “Goodness!” the undertaker exclaimed, grasping the gold-trimmed lapel of the beadle’s coat, “that is precisely what I wished to discuss. You know—my word, what a refined button this is, Mr. Bumble! I had not noticed it previously.” “Yes, I find it quite handsome,” the beadle replied, looking down with pride at the large brass buttons adorning his coat. “The design matches the parish seal—the Good Samaritan tending to the wounded man. The board gifted it to me on New Year’s morning, Mr. Sowerberry. I recall wearing it for the first time to attend the inquest for that impoverished tradesman who passed away in a doorway at midnight.”"
}

