parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "95dc5a6df7e0bedb2feab02a",
  "rewritten_text": "You have not been in Bath long enough to enjoy the evening parties, he remarked. Oh no, she replied. They do not interest me, as I do not play cards. I know you did not like them before, he said, but time changes many things. I have not changed that much, Anne said, then stopped, worried she might be misunderstood. After a pause, he added as if speaking from the heart, It is a long time. Eight and a half years is a long time. Anne wondered if he would have continued, but Henrietta interrupted, eager to leave before more visitors arrived. They had to move. Anne claimed she was ready, but she felt a deep reluctance to leave her seat. If Henrietta understood Anne’s regret, she might have pitied her. Their departure was halted by the arrival of Sir Walter and Miss Elliot, whose presence chilled the room. The comfort and joy of the gathering vanished, replaced by forced politeness and silence to match her father and sister’s cold elegance. Anne was mortified. She noticed that Captain Wentworth was acknowledged by both, with Elizabeth being surprisingly gracious. Elizabeth had a plan. After some small talk, she invited the Musgroves to a small gathering the next evening. She spoke gracefully and placed her invitation cards on the table, offering a special smile and an extra card to Captain Wentworth. Elizabeth had learned that in Bath, a man of his appearance was worth cultivating."
}

