parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "92bfac95bac1cad7a8ce2391",
  "rewritten_text": "The artist suddenly emerged at the studio entrance, gesturing sharply for them to approach. They exchanged smiles. I am waiting, he called out. Please enter. The lighting is ideal, and you are welcome to bring your refreshments. They stood and walked down the path together. A pair of green-and-white butterflies drifted by, and a thrush began singing in the garden pear tree. You are pleased to have met me, Mr. Gray, Lord Henry remarked, observing him. Yes, I am pleased now. I wonder if I shall always feel this way. Always! That is a terrible word. It makes me shiver. Women love using it, ruining every romance by insisting it endure forever. It is also empty. The sole distinction between a whim and a lifelong passion is that the whim persists slightly longer. As they walked into the studio, Dorian Gray touched Lord Henry’s arm. In that case, let our friendship be a whim, he whispered, blushing at his own audacity, before climbing onto the platform to resume his pose. Lord Henry collapsed into a wicker armchair to watch. The rhythmic strokes of the brush against the canvas were the only sounds in the quiet, save for Hallward occasionally stepping back to study his progress. Dust motes danced like gold in the slanted light pouring through the doorway. The thick fragrance of roses seemed to hang over the room. After fifteen minutes, Hallward ceased painting, staring intently at Dorian and then at the portrait, chewing his brush and frowning. It is finished, he finally announced, leaning down to sign his name in bright red letters in the corner. Lord Henry approached to inspect the work. It was truly a remarkable piece of art and an excellent likeness. My dear fellow, I offer my sincere congratulations, he said. It is the greatest portrait of our time. Mr. Gray, come see yourself. The young man started as if roused from a dream. Is it truly done? he asked, stepping down. Quite finished, the painter replied. You sat wonderfully today. I am very grateful. That is entirely my doing, Lord Henry interjected. Is it not, Mr. Gray? Dorian did not reply, but walked listlessly toward the painting and turned to face it."
}

