parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e9eed38b3d79582cb422a728",
  "rewritten_text": "Reading about the luxuries buried with the dead was fascinating. He then turned his focus to embroideries and the tapestries that served as wall decorations in cold northern European rooms. As he studied these, he possessed a unique ability to become completely immersed in his interests, yet he felt a touch of sadness regarding how time eventually ruins all beautiful things. He, however, had avoided that fate. Seasons passed, flowers bloomed and withered, and nights of terror came and went, but he remained the same. No winter touched his face or faded his youthful appearance. How different this was from physical objects. Where had they all gone? He wondered about the golden robe depicting the gods fighting giants, crafted for Athena, or the massive purple canopy Nero stretched over the Roman Colosseum, showing the starry sky and Apollo’s chariot. He longed to see the intricate napkins made for the Priest of the Sun, featuring every imaginable feast dish; the burial cloth of King Chilperic, covered in three hundred golden bees; or the strange robes that angered the Bishop of Pontus, decorated with lions, bears, hunters, and forests. He thought of the coat worn by Charles of Orleans, with song lyrics embroidered on the sleeves in gold thread, each musical note formed by four pearls. He read about Queen Joan of Burgundy’s room at Rheims, decorated with over thirteen hundred embroidered parrots and five hundred butterflies, all featuring royal emblems in gold. Catherine de Medici owned a mourning bed of black velvet covered in crescents and suns, with damask curtains and pearl fringe, displayed in a room hung with black velvet symbols on silver cloth. Even Louis XIV had fifteen-foot-tall gold embroidered figures decorating his private chambers."
}

