parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3819e8190b7cd13ae7222064",
  "rewritten_text": "My companion carefully drove a stake into the earth exactly where the beetle had landed, then retrieved a tape measure from his coat. He secured one end to the tree trunk nearest the stake, extending the tape to the marker and continuing fifty feet further along the line formed by the tree and the first peg, while Jupiter hacked away the undergrowth with his scythe. A second stake was placed at this location, and a rough circle four feet wide was traced around it. Legrand handed us each a spade and insisted we begin excavating immediately. Honestly, I had little appetite for such labor, especially as evening approached and I was already exhausted, but I saw no way to refuse without upsetting my friend. I considered forcing the supposedly deranged man home, but I knew Jupiter would never help me oppose his master. I assumed Legrand had succumbed to local superstitions regarding buried treasure, likely fueled by the discovery of the beetle and Jupiter’s insistence that it was made of solid gold. Such notions easily sway a fragile mind, particularly when they align with existing obsessions, and I recalled his earlier claim that the insect would lead to his fortune. I felt frustrated but decided to comply, hoping that digging would quickly prove the folly of his beliefs. We lit our lanterns and began working with misplaced enthusiasm. As the light illuminated our group and tools, I reflected on how peculiar and suspicious our activities would seem to any unexpected witness. We labored diligently for two hours."
}

