parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "946045dea9b9d70efecc4864",
  "rewritten_text": "I thanked him and apologized. He replied, \"Not at all,\" and continued. \"Miss Havisham was an heiress, and naturally considered a great catch. Her half-brother had regained his wealth, only to squander it again through debt and reckless behavior. Their relationship was far more strained than his had been with their father, and it is believed he held a lethal grudge against her for influencing their father's resentment. Now, I reach the cruel part of the story—pausing only to note, my dear Handel, that a dinner napkin will not fit inside a glass.\" I have no idea why I was trying to force mine into the tumbler, only that I was working with absurd persistence to cram it inside. I thanked him and apologized again, to which he cheerfully replied, \"Not at all!\" and resumed. \"A man appeared—perhaps at the races or a public ball—and began courting Miss Havisham. I never met him, as this occurred twenty-five years ago, before you or I were around, but my father described him as a flashy individual, perfectly suited for his scheme. My father insisted he was no gentleman, maintaining that one who lacks a gentleman's heart can never truly possess a gentleman's manners. He argued that no amount of polish can hide the wood's grain; in fact, the more you apply, the more the grain shows. Regardless, this man pursued Miss Havisham relentlessly, claiming to be devoted to her. She had not been particularly romantic before, but she fell deeply and passionately in love. She idolized him. He manipulated her affections so systematically that he extracted large sums of money from her and convinced her to buy out her brother's share of the brewery at an exorbitant price, claiming that as her husband, he needed full control. Your guardian was not involved in Miss Havisham's affairs at the time, and she was far too proud and infatuated to accept advice from anyone.\""
}

