parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3b738528af937006ca33f7e6",
  "rewritten_text": "Count Rouvaloff stared at him in disbelief. Realizing he was serious, he wrote an address on a slip of paper, initialed it, and handed it across the table. Scotland Yard would pay a fortune for this address, he remarked. Lord Arthur laughed and promised they would never get it. After shaking the Russian’s hand, he hurried downstairs, checked the address, and ordered his coachman to drive to Soho Square. He dismissed the carriage there and walked down Greek Street until he reached Bayle’s Court. Passing under the archway, he entered a dead-end alley filled with clotheslines and fluttering white laundry. He walked to the end and knocked on a small green house. After a delay, during which curious neighbors peered from every window, a rough-looking man opened the door and asked his business in broken English. Lord Arthur handed him the paper. The man bowed and led him into a shabby front room. Moments later, Herr Winckelkopf bustled in, wearing a wine-stained napkin around his neck and holding a fork. Lord Arthur bowed and introduced himself as Robert Smith, explaining he needed an explosive clock. The German laughed and said he was charmed to meet Lord Arthur, noting he had seen him at Lady Windermere’s. He invited Lord Arthur to join him for breakfast, offering him pâté and excellent Rhine wine. Surprised to be recognized, Lord Arthur sat in the back room, sipping wine from a fine glass and chatting with the famous conspirator. Explosive clocks are poor exports, Winckelkopf explained, because even if they clear customs, the unreliable train schedules usually cause them to detonate before reaching their destination."
}

