parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "59a9273902f8a4e5d753b485",
  "rewritten_text": "Back home, we collected taxes through tariffs, and citizens liked believing foreign importers paid them. In reality, the American public paid, with the burden distributed so evenly that a millionaire and a laborer’s infant paid the exact same six dollars. I suppose that is as fair as it gets. Scotland and Ireland paid tribute to Arthur, with the British Isles holding under a million people. A mechanic earned three cents daily, covering his own expenses. National costs were ninety thousand dollars annually, or two hundred fifty daily. By swapping gold for nickels during the king’s healing ceremonies, I satisfied everyone while saving four-fifths of the daily budget, equivalent to eight hundred thousand dollars in my time. I drew on childhood wisdom, realizing that a true statesman values all insight; I used to donate buttons to missionaries instead of coins, keeping the money for myself. Everyone was satisfied, and no one suffered. Marinel processed the patients. If they qualified, they went to the king. A priest recited scripture while the king touched the ulcers. Finally, the patient received a nickel, hung around their neck by the king, and left. You might doubt the efficacy, but it worked. Any ritual cures if the patient has faith. Near Astolat, a chapel stood where a goose-girl claimed to see the Virgin. Despite a disturbing painting of the event, thousands of sick and lame visited annually, leaving fully recovered, while even the healthy remained unharmed."
}

