parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0d9f33d178244bac209143b2",
  "rewritten_text": "Upon returning, I discovered this note from my father: My dear Victor, you have surely been awaiting word regarding your homecoming. I considered sending a brief note with a date, but that would be a heartless mercy. How could I surprise you with sorrow when you anticipated a joyful reunion? I cannot shield you from this tragedy, though I know your eyes are already racing to find the terrible truth. William is dead. That dear, cheerful boy has been murdered. I will not offer hollow comfort, but will explain what occurred. Last Thursday, May 7th, I went to Plainpalais with my niece and your brothers. The evening was pleasant, and we stayed out longer than intended. As dusk fell, we realized William and Ernest were missing. We waited, but only Ernest returned, explaining that William had run off to hide and failed to reappear. Alarmed, we searched until nightfall, then checked the house. He was gone. We searched with torches through the night, fearing for his safety. At five in the morning, I found my beautiful boy, who had been so vibrant, lying lifeless on the grass, his neck marked by the killer’s grip. We brought him home, and my grief revealed the truth to Elizabeth. Despite my protests, she insisted on seeing him. Upon viewing the body and the marks on his throat, she clasped her hands and cried out, O God!"
}

