  "request_id": "3cfabcb194db0bf4b24ae80d",
  "rewritten_text": "If you seek my patience and the mercy of those you have harmed, sit in that chair without speaking. It has been waiting for you for two days. Monks muttered something indistinct, still hesitating. You must be quick, Mr. Brownlow said. One word from me and your chance is gone forever. The man still wavered. I have no desire to negotiate, Mr. Brownlow continued, and since I represent the vital interests of others, I have no right to do so. Is there no middle ground? Monks asked, his voice trembling. None. Monks searched the old man’s face, but finding only stern resolve, he walked into the room and sat down with a shrug. Lock the door from the outside, Mr. Brownlow told the attendants, and return when I ring. They obeyed, leaving the two men alone. This is fine treatment from my father’s oldest friend, Monks said, tossing aside his hat and cloak. It is precisely because I was your father’s oldest friend, Mr. Brownlow replied, that I treat you with such restraint. My hopes were tied to him and to the woman who died young, leaving me alone. I stood with him at his sister’s deathbed when we were boys, the woman who would have been my wife had fate allowed. My heart clung to him through all his struggles until his death. Because those memories remain, and because even seeing you brings him to mind, I am moved to be gentle—even now, Edward Leeford—though I am ashamed of your unworthiness to bear that name. What does the name matter? Monks asked, watching his companion’s agitation with sullen confusion. What is the name to me? Nothing to you, Mr. Brownlow replied. But it was hers, and even now, it stirs the same thrill in me that I felt hearing it long ago. I am very glad you changed it. This is all very dramatic, Monks said after a long silence, during which he shifted in sullen defiance while Mr. Brownlow covered his face with his hand."
}

