{
  "request_id": "edab86395d4b4231884aaabd",
  "rewritten_text": "He kept one secret from his dear friends, nurtured since childhood. Despite his affection for his peers, he dared not trust them with his feelings, fearing they would mock the intensity of a passion he considered the very essence of his life. A man of modest means lived near his family estate with three lovely daughters. The eldest was the most beautiful, possessing a sharp intellect and an angelic temperament. She and my father had been childhood playmates; his mother had always favored her, ensuring they spent every vacation together. Influenced by novels that introduced the concept of romance to the impressionable, he was deeply affected. By age eleven, he spoke the language of love to her. Though she was two years his senior, her sheltered upbringing left her naive; she accepted his declarations innocently, unaware of their weight. She had read no novels and knew nothing of the distinction between love and friendship. By the time she understood the nature of their bond, her heart was already his, and she feared only that his interest might eventually fade. Their love grew more ardent and tender with time, becoming inseparable from his very being. They kept their secret, and though he feared his companions might judge him for loving someone of lower social standing, he remained steadfast in his resolve to marry her as soon as he could overcome the inevitable obstacles. Diana was truly worthy of his devotion."
}

