parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "70ce201a7bde1b5daf22fe9b",
  "rewritten_text": "The line of flickering streetlights stretched far into the distance, where a lone taxi waited outside a small walled house, its driver asleep. He walked quickly toward Portland Place, glancing back occasionally as if fearing pursuit. At the corner of Rich Street, two men studied a poster on a wooden fence. Driven by sudden curiosity, he crossed the street. As he approached, the word 'Murder' in bold black letters caught his eye. He flinched, his face flushing deeply. It was a reward notice for a man of medium height, aged thirty to forty, wearing a bowler hat, black coat, and plaid trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it repeatedly, wondering if the man would be caught and how he had earned his scar. Perhaps one day his own name would be plastered on London’s walls, with a price on his head. The thought sickened him. He turned and hurried into the night. He wandered aimlessly through a maze of grim houses and dark streets, eventually reaching Piccadilly Circus at dawn. Walking toward Belgrave Square, he passed wagons heading to Covent Garden. Carters in white smocks, their faces sunburnt and hair curly, strode along cracking whips and calling to one another. A chubby boy sat atop a massive grey horse, laughing as he gripped the mane, a bunch of primroses tucked into his battered hat. The piles of vegetables appeared like jade against the morning sky. Lord Arthur felt strangely moved, finding the delicate beauty of the dawn deeply pathetic. He reflected on how many days begin in beauty only to end in storms. He wondered what these simple, good-humored workers saw in this city—a pale, ghost-like place, free from the night's sins and the day's smoke. He questioned if they understood London’s hidden splendor and shame, its intense joys, its desperate hunger, and all it creates or destroys from sunrise to sunset."
}

