parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "87a18cae14d0cf4cb81694a2",
  "rewritten_text": "He explained that his current location was a waste of time and that associates in London were urging his return to finalize their Indian business venture. Unable to postpone his departure any longer, and anticipating that his trip to London might soon lead to a much longer voyage, he begged me to spend as much time with him as possible. He asked me to abandon my isolated island and join him in Perth so we could travel south together. This request revived my spirits, and I resolved to leave the island in two days. Before leaving, however, I faced a daunting task: I had to pack my chemical equipment, which required entering the room where I had performed my repulsive work and touching the tools that now sickened me. At dawn the next day, I gathered my resolve and opened the laboratory. The remnants of the unfinished creature I had destroyed were strewn across the floor, and I felt as though I had mutilated a living person. After steadying myself, I entered the room. With shaking hands, I removed the instruments, realizing I could not leave the remains of my work to alarm the local villagers. I placed the scraps in a basket weighted with stones, intending to cast them into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the shore, cleaning and organizing my apparatus. My perspective had shifted entirely since the night the demon appeared. Previously, I had viewed my promise with hopeless despair, believing it had to be fulfilled regardless of the outcome. Now, it felt as if a veil had been lifted, allowing me to see clearly for the first time. The thought of resuming my work never crossed my mind; although the creature's threat weighed on me, I no longer believed my own actions could prevent it. I decided that creating another such monster would be an act of profound selfishness, and I dismissed any thought that might suggest otherwise."
}

