  "rewritten_text": "This knot is one only sailors can tie, and it is unique to the Maltese. I found the ribbon near the lightning rod, and it could not have belonged to either victim. If my deduction that the Frenchman is a sailor from a Maltese ship is wrong, my advertisement does no harm. If I am mistaken, he will simply assume I was misled. However, if I am right, I have gained a significant advantage. Even if he is innocent of the murder, the Frenchman will hesitate to claim his orangutan. He will think: I am innocent and poor, and this animal is worth a fortune. Why lose it to groundless fear? It was found far from the crime scene. How could anyone suspect a beast committed such a murder? The police have no leads. Even if they find the animal, they cannot prove I was involved. Most importantly, I am known. The advertiser knows I own the beast. If I do not claim such valuable property, I only invite suspicion. It is safer to claim the animal and keep it hidden until this blows over. Just then, we heard footsteps on the stairs. Be ready with your pistols, Dupin said, but do not show or use them until I signal. The front door was open, and the visitor had entered without ringing. He hesitated on the stairs, then started to descend, but soon turned and walked up with purpose, knocking on our door. Come in, Dupin said cheerfully. A man entered. He was clearly a sailor—tall, muscular, and possessing a bold, daring look that was not entirely unpleasant."
}

