parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "486c5acc54c0a9b3377edc3b",
  "rewritten_text": "I encountered several funerals, sparsely attended and viewed by onlookers as grim omens. Some watched with frantic intensity, others fled in fear, and some wept openly. Adrian’s primary goal, beyond immediate medical aid, was to hide the plague's progression from London’s residents. He understood that fear and despair fueled the disease, making people more vulnerable to infection. Consequently, he kept shops open and maintained a semblance of normal traffic to avoid visible signs of decay. Yet, to me, the city felt profoundly altered. Carriages were absent, grass grew in the streets, and houses looked desolate with shuttered windows. The people I passed wore a look of haunted terror, a stark contrast to the usual industrious nature of Londoners. My lone carriage drew attention as I traveled toward the Protectoral Palace, where the elite streets seemed even more abandoned. I found Adrian’s waiting room packed. Unwilling to interrupt, I watched the petitioners—mostly working-class people whose livelihoods had vanished with the collapse of trade. Their anxiety and terror contrasted sharply with the calm, satisfied expressions of those leaving after meeting him. I could see his influence in their renewed hope. When the clock struck two, he stopped taking visitors, and I entered his office. I was struck by Adrian’s improved health. He no longer appeared fragile or weighed down; his eyes were bright, his expression composed, and he radiated a focused energy. He sat with secretaries, organizing petitions and notes. I admired his patience and fairness as he advised those who could afford to leave London to do so immediately, providing them with the necessary resources."
}

