  "rewritten_text": "I am going, Master Will, I truly am, the man replied quickly, nearly at the tip now. At the tip? Legrand shouted, are you at the end of that branch? Almost there, master, oh my, Lord have mercy, what is this on the tree? Well, Legrand exclaimed, clearly pleased, what is it? It is just a skull, someone left their head in the tree and the crows have picked it clean. A skull? Very well, how is it attached to the limb? Look closely, master, it is a strange situation, there is a large nail pinning the skull to the wood. Jupiter, listen carefully, do you hear me? Yes, master. Pay attention, find the skull's left eye. Oh, that is difficult, there is no eye left at all. You fool, do you know your right from your left? Yes, I know that, it is my left hand I use to chop wood. Exactly, you are left-handed, and your left eye is on that same side. Now, can you find the left eye or where it used to be? After a long silence, the man asked, is the skull's left eye on the same side as its left hand? It has no hands, but never mind, I have found the left eye. What should I do? Drop the beetle through it as far as the string goes, but do not let go of the line. That is done, Master Will, it was easy to pass the bug through the hole, watch for it below. During this exchange, Jupiter remained hidden, but the beetle appeared at the end of the string, shining like polished gold in the final light of the setting sun. The scarab hung free of the branches and would have landed at our feet if released. Legrand used the scythe to clear a circular area several yards wide directly under the insect, then told Jupiter to release the string and descend."
}

