parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0df73d11d548bce331324e2e",
  "rewritten_text": "I fail to grasp why she desired my company, yet I would surely be misled again under identical conditions. Caroline delayed her return visit until yesterday, offering no message in the interim. Her arrival was clearly joyless; she offered a brief, stiff excuse for the delay, said nothing of future meetings, and appeared so transformed that I decided to end our acquaintance upon her departure. I blame her, yet I pity her. She was mistaken to target me, as she initiated every step toward closeness. I pity her because she must realize her error, driven by concern for her brother. I need not elaborate; while we know her worry is groundless, it explains her conduct, and her devotion to him is natural. Still, I wonder at her fears, for if he cared for me, we would have met long ago. She inadvertently confirmed he knows I am in town, yet she seems to convince herself he prefers Miss Darcy. It is baffling. Were I not wary of harsh judgment, I might suspect duplicity. I shall strive to discard these troubling thoughts and focus on your affection and the kindness of our aunt and uncle. Write soon. Miss Bingley hinted he might abandon Netherfield, though she was uncertain. Let us not speak of it. I am delighted by the good news from Hunsford. Do visit them with Sir William and Maria; you will find it pleasant. Yours, etc. This letter troubled Elizabeth, though she felt relieved that Jane would no longer be deceived by the sister. Any hope regarding the brother had vanished."
}

