{
  "request_id": "4c74aec3b3cd9f9a4b2ff74d",
  "rewritten_text": "Your father might spare you, if your mother agrees. Daughters matter less to a father. If you stay another full month, I could take one of you to London when I go in early June. Since Dawson does not mind the carriage box, there is room for one, or even both if the weather is cool and you remain small. You are very kind, but we must stick to our original plan. Lady Catherine accepted this. Mrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. I always speak my mind, and it is highly improper for two young women to travel alone. You must arrange for someone to go. I dislike such things; young women should always be properly attended based on their status. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate, I insisted she have two men-servants. Miss Darcy could not have traveled otherwise. You must send John with them; it would be shameful for you to let them go alone. My uncle is sending a servant for us. Oh, your uncle has a man-servant? I am glad someone is thinking of these things. Where will you change horses? Bromley, of course. Mention my name at the Bell, and they will take care of you. Lady Catherine asked many questions about the trip, and Elizabeth found it fortunate that she had to pay attention, or she might have forgotten where she was. She saved her thoughts for her solitary walks, where she could dwell on her unpleasant memories. She nearly knew Mr. Darcy’s letter by heart. She studied every sentence, and her feelings toward him shifted. She felt indignant remembering his tone, but when she considered how unfairly she had judged him, her anger turned toward herself, and she felt pity for his disappointment."
}

