parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4f5269cd9f35cc3bbe23e13e",
  "rewritten_text": "The area seemed to be a mix of back alleys, ditches, and small gardens, creating a rather quiet, dull atmosphere. Wemmick’s home was a tiny wooden cottage surrounded by garden plots, with a roof carved and painted to resemble a fortress equipped with cannons. My own work, Wemmick said, looking pleased. It looks nice, doesn't it? I praised it highly. It was the smallest house I had ever seen, featuring strange gothic windows—mostly fake—and a door so small it was difficult to enter. That is a real flagpole, Wemmick explained, and I raise a real flag on Sundays. Look here. Once I cross this bridge, I pull it up like this and cut off access. The bridge was just a plank spanning a four-foot-wide, two-foot-deep ditch. It was charming to see the genuine pride he took in lifting it, smiling with real enjoyment rather than just going through the motions. At nine o'clock sharp, Greenwich time, the gun fires, Wemmick said. There it is! When you hear it, I think you will agree it is quite a powerful blast. The cannon sat in its own small fortress made of lattice, protected from the rain by a clever umbrella-like cover. In the back, out of sight so it does not ruin the fortification theme—because I believe if you have an idea, you should commit to it—there is a pig, along with chickens and rabbits. I also build my own frames to grow cucumbers, and you can judge my salad at supper. So, if you imagine this place under siege, it could hold out for a long time with all these supplies, he added with a serious, thoughtful smile. He then led me to a garden arbor about twelve yards away, reached by such winding paths that it took a while to arrive. Our glasses were already waiting there. Our punch was chilling in a small ornamental pond next to the arbor. This pond, which featured a tiny island that might have served as our salad, had a fountain he had built. When you started a small mill and pulled a cork from a pipe, it sprayed water with enough force to dampen the back of your hand."
}

