parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3e5efcf0575d9e19ef97afd6",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Otis was troubled by Lord Canterville’s refusal and urged him to reconsider, but the peer remained firm. He eventually convinced the Minister to let his daughter keep the ghost’s gift. In the spring of 1890, when the young Duchess of Cheshire was presented at the Queen’s first drawing-room following her wedding, her jewels were universally admired. Virginia had received the coronet, the reward for all good American girls, and married her childhood sweetheart once he came of age. They were so charming and devoted that everyone was delighted, except for the Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had unsuccessfully tried to secure the Duke for one of her seven daughters, and, oddly, Mr. Otis himself. While fond of the young Duke, Mr. Otis theoretically opposed titles, fearing that the influence of a pleasure-seeking aristocracy might erode republican simplicity. His objections were overruled, and as he walked his daughter down the aisle at St. George’s, Hanover Square, he was surely the proudest man in England. After their honeymoon, the couple visited Canterville Chase. The day after arriving, they walked to the secluded churchyard near the pine woods. After much debate, Sir Simon’s tombstone was engraved only with his initials and the verse from the library window. Virginia scattered roses on the grave before they wandered into the ruined abbey. She sat on a fallen pillar while her husband lounged at her feet, smoking. He soon discarded his cigarette, took her hand, and asked her to share the secrets she had kept from him. He pressed her to reveal what occurred while she was locked away with the ghost. Virginia replied solemnly that she had never told anyone."
}

