parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3568b531a732c6efe4c51a97",
  "rewritten_text": "I rushed to her, cradling her in my arms while showering her with affection and pleading, \"Speak to me, dear, what is wrong?\" Her head slumped against my chest as she whispered barely audibly, \"Hello-Central!\" I yelled to Clarence to summon the king's homeopath immediately. Within moments, I was at the child's bedside while Sandy sent servants scurrying throughout the palace. I recognized the ailment instantly: membranous croup. I leaned in and murmured, \"Wake up, sweetheart. Hello-Central.\" She opened her eyes slowly and managed to say, \"Papa.\" That brought me relief; she was still fighting. I ordered sulfur treatments and prepared the croup-kettle myself, as I never wait for physicians when my family is ill. I was experienced in nursing them, having held this little one for much of her life, often soothing her tears when even her mother could not. Sir Launcelot, dressed in his finest armor, marched through the hall toward the stock-board. As president, he occupied the Siege Perilous, which he had purchased from Sir Galahad for the Knights of the Round Table to conduct their business. Though he was a bear currently cornering a market and preparing to squeeze the shorts, he abandoned his financial schemes the moment he saw his favorite was unwell. He tossed his helmet aside and immediately began tending the alcohol lamp for the croup-kettle. Sandy had already constructed a blanket canopy over the crib. Launcelot and I added unslaked lime, carbolic acid, and a dash of lactic acid to the kettle, filled it with water, and directed the steam spout under the canopy. With everything prepared, we sat on either side of the crib to begin our vigil."
}

