parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5591dac5d33988b947587bb3",
  "rewritten_text": "Winters on Sullivan’s Island are rarely harsh, and fires are seldom needed in the autumn. However, in mid-October of that year, a day arrived with a sudden, biting chill. Just before sunset, I pushed through the evergreens to reach my friend’s hut. I had not visited in weeks, as I lived in Charleston nine miles away, and travel was much more difficult then than it is now. I knocked, but receiving no answer, I found the hidden key, unlocked the door, and entered. A fire blazed on the hearth—a welcome surprise. I removed my coat, sat in an armchair by the logs, and waited for my hosts. They arrived shortly after dark and greeted me warmly. Jupiter, grinning broadly, hurried to prepare marsh-hens for dinner. Legrand was in one of his typical states of intense excitement. He had discovered a new type of shellfish and, with Jupiter’s help, had captured a beetle he believed was a unique species. He wanted my opinion on it the next day. Why not tonight? I asked, warming my hands and wishing the beetle were elsewhere. Legrand explained that he had foolishly lent the bug to Lieutenant G from the fort earlier that day. He insisted I stay the night so Jupiter could retrieve it at sunrise. He described the creature as a brilliant, gold-colored beetle the size of a hickory nut, marked with three black spots. Jupiter interrupted, insisting that the bug was made of solid gold, noting how unusually heavy it felt."
}

