parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7b164006a6a543e6e9413de2",
  "rewritten_text": "Oh, bother my hat! I want Virginia! the little Duke laughed as they galloped toward the station. Mr. Otis asked the station-master if anyone matching Virginia’s description had been seen, but he had no news. The official promised to alert the line and keep a watch. After buying the Duke a new hat from a nearby shop, Mr. Otis rode to Bexley, a village four miles away known for its gypsy camps. They woke the local policeman, but he offered no help. After searching the common, they returned home by eleven o’clock, exhausted and heartbroken. Washington and the twins met them at the gate with lanterns, but they had found no trace of her. The gypsies had been located in a nearby meadow, but Virginia was not with them; they explained they had left in a hurry to reach a fair on time. They were genuinely upset to hear she was missing and had even left four men behind to help search. The pond had been dragged and the entire estate scoured, but to no avail. It was clear that Virginia was truly lost for the night. Mr. Otis and the boys walked to the house in deep despair, followed by the groom. Inside, the servants were terrified, and Mrs. Otis lay on the library sofa, nearly hysterical, while the housekeeper tended to her. Mr. Otis insisted she eat and ordered supper for everyone. It was a somber meal; even the twins were quiet and subdued. Once finished, Mr. Otis sent everyone to bed despite the Duke’s protests, promising to contact Scotland Yard for detectives in the morning."
}

