parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "72157bef06feab7238a5fd19",
  "rewritten_text": "Two years elapsed in this manner. Though my external circumstances remained static, my internal state shifted as I drifted toward my end. I immersed myself in study and literature, finding connection in historical narratives and losing my sense of self among the ghosts of the past. As the sharpness of my immediate agony faded, I grew more human. Solitude, once appealing, began to feel hollow; I craved companionship, not from the masses, but from a single devoted friend. You might assume this prepared me for society, but I disagree. The purity of the connection I sought could not exist in a world tainted by coarse, worldly concerns. I was, in truth, less prepared for human interaction than ever. Previously, my torment was external, like a sickness I longed to shed. Now, I yearned for a soul to bind to my own, a desire that would have only invited further misery, for I had become fragile and hypersensitive. I did not seek intellectual validation, but rather tender affection—a heart to receive my confessions, hoping that even from my wretched state, something beautiful might bloom. Yet, such friendship is elusive, often born of shared youth or mutual hardship. It descends unbidden like dew upon the chosen, but flees from those who actively pursue it. I understood this and refrained from searching, yet in my desolate isolation, a glimmer of connection arrived like a winter sunbeam upon the snow. Alas, it illuminated only decay; I was too far broken to be revived by its warmth."
}

