parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "402a052ddd826110a79aa65f",
  "rewritten_text": "What kind of life would they have while fate kept this terrifying fortune hanging in the balance? The wedding had to be delayed at any cost. He was firm on this. As much as he loved her—and the simple touch of her hand sent thrills through him—he saw his duty clearly. He had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. Once that was done, he could stand at the altar with Sybil Merton and offer her his life without fear of guilt. He could hold her, knowing she would never have to feel shame because of him. It had to be done, and the sooner, the better. Many men might have chosen an easy life over duty, but Lord Arthur was too principled to put pleasure first. His love was more than just passion; Sybil represented everything noble. Though he felt a brief, natural revulsion toward the task, it quickly faded. His heart told him it was a sacrifice, not a sin, and his reason confirmed there was no other way. He had to choose between his own desires and the needs of others. Though the task was horrific, he refused to let selfishness win. Everyone faces this choice eventually, but it came to him early, before cynicism or vanity could corrupt him. He did not hesitate. Fortunately, he was not a dreamer or a dilettante. Had he been, he might have wavered like Hamlet. Instead, he was practical. To him, life meant action, not just thought; he possessed the rare gift of common sense. The chaotic emotions of the previous night had vanished, and he looked back on his frantic wandering and agony with a sense of shame. His past suffering now felt unreal, and he wondered how he could have been so foolish as to despair over the inevitable."
}

