parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2d061c1ae9f5d07c27fa9c23",
  "rewritten_text": "Do not misunderstand; I was never truly insane. I remained aware of my condition even when frantic thoughts pushed me toward madness, keeping them hidden in silence and solitude. Those around me noticed nothing. They saw only a broken-spirited girl who spoke softly, her eyes often downcast to hide tears. I was someone who craved isolation, avoided attention, and never smiled—that was all. Eventually, I escaped. I left my guardian’s home and vanished; based on the letters I left behind, people assumed I had taken my own life. Consequently, the search for me was half-hearted, and I was soon forgotten. I departed London on a small ship bound for northern England. Once I succeeded, peace returned. As the ship glided over calm seas, I sat on deck beneath the open sky, feeling transformed. I was no longer the raving, miserable Mathilda, but a young hermit dedicated to seclusion, determined to keep my heart free from turmoil and despair. My simple, nun-like attire, the secret of my survival, and the prospect of a solitary future soothed my wounded spirit. The breeze refreshed me, and I watched the sun glinting off the waves and birds skimming the water. I slept without dreams and woke to enjoy my quiet freedom. After four days, we reached port. I moved inland immediately, seeking a solitary house on a vast plain, far from others, where I could view the horizon and wander undisturbed. I was not a misanthrope, but I knew my peace required solitude. I settled in a desolate, stony heath, where short grass grew and rushes clustered around a small pool."
}

