parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3fb9910a597b153912cf65bc",
  "rewritten_text": "Few possessed a heart so untainted or a spirit so genuinely humble, paired with such steadfast faith in her own virtue and that of others. Raised in seclusion, she lost her mother early, leaving her father to oversee her upbringing. His unconventional methods left her well-versed in the legends of antiquity and English history, yet largely unaware of contemporary affairs. While she had read little from the last half-century, her broader literary knowledge was profound. Though she seemed less worldly than he, her wisdom was grounded in deeper truths; even had her beauty not captivated him, her intellect would have commanded his devotion. He viewed her as his mentor, finding joy in the sense of inferiority she occasionally inspired. Following his mother's death when he was nineteen, he left university and withdrew from his peers, finding solace in her presence. This brief isolation emboldened him to claim his independence. Believing his friends would not openly mock a marriage already finalized, he secured his guardian's reluctant consent and her father's ready approval. By his twentieth birthday, he had wed Diana in secret. He adored her with intense passion, finding her affection so enchanting that he could focus on nothing else. Though he initially invited college acquaintances to visit, their superficiality repelled him. Diana had stripped away his youthful illusions; he had matured into a man, marveling that he had ever adopted the trivial habits of his peers or feared their judgment. He abandoned those old connections not out of caprice, but because he recognized they were beneath him."
}

