parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0e8df4497ffd0adc239a6e8a",
  "rewritten_text": "The next morning, the Otis family discussed the ghost over breakfast. The American Minister felt annoyed that his gift had been rejected. He stated that while he meant the ghost no harm, throwing pillows at him was rude, given how long the spirit had lived there. The twins laughed at this, but their father insisted that if the ghost refused the lubricant, they would have to confiscate his chains to ensure a quiet night. The rest of the week remained peaceful, save for the recurring bloodstain in the library. It was baffling, as Mr. Otis kept the room locked and the windows barred. The stain’s shifting colors—ranging from dull red to vermilion, purple, and even emerald-green—became a source of amusement and betting for the family. Only young Virginia felt distressed by the sight, nearly crying when the stain turned green. The ghost reappeared Sunday night. A loud crash in the hall drew the family downstairs, where they found a suit of armor collapsed on the floor and the ghost sitting in a chair, clutching his knees in pain. The twins fired their pea-shooters with practiced precision, while the Minister leveled his revolver and ordered the spirit to raise his hands. With a furious shriek, the ghost vanished through them like mist, blowing out Washington’s candle and plunging the hall into darkness."
}

