parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "700f7f12edf0ea34880b43e9",
  "rewritten_text": "Spirits of the storm, take me! Powers of destruction, open your arms and hold me forever, unless a kinder fate has decreed that after long suffering I might find my reward and feel my heart beat against another’s once more. The Tiber, nature’s own path across the continent, lay at my feet, lined with tethered boats. I planned to take a few books, supplies, and my dog, then drift down the current into the sea. I would hug the coast, sailing past the beautiful shores of the Mediterranean, Naples, and Calabria, braving Scylla and Charybdis. With nothing to lose, I would head toward Malta and the Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople, a place belonging to a past life, and sail along Asia Minor and Syria. Passing the Nile, I would head north, leaving Carthage and Libya behind to reach the Pillars of Hercules. Eventually, I might find a watery grave, succumb to illness, or perhaps find the companion I seek. If not, I will wander forever, an old man mourning his lost youth, sailing from one bay to the next, crossing the ocean, leaving Europe for Africa, and finally mooring my boat in the fragrant islands of the Indian Ocean. These are wild dreams, yet they have dominated my thoughts since I stood atop St. Peter’s a week ago. I have chosen my boat and packed my meager supplies. I have selected a few books, primarily Homer and Shakespeare, though I can restock at any port. I do not expect things to improve, but the monotony of my current life is unbearable."
}

