parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ec68d98dd87829985dfccbd1",
  "rewritten_text": "The surrounding oaks housed a colony of nightingales. I am there now, with Idris by my side in the prime of youth. Remember, I am twenty-two, and she has seen barely seventeen summers. The river, swollen by autumn rains, flooded the lowlands, and Adrian was busy in his boat, dangerously plucking a branch from a submerged oak. I wondered if he was tired of life to risk such a stunt. He secured his prize and navigated the flood, but the current carried him away. He had to land far downstream and walk back to us. Idris watched him land and waved, relieved, suggesting we wait for him. We were alone as the sun set and the nightingales began to sing. The evening star shone brightly in the fading western light. Idris gazed at it, remarking on its flickering glow, which she felt mirrored the wavering, fearful, and loving nature of our own lives. I begged her not to look at distant stars but to listen to me. I had been silent for so long, desperate to offer her my entire soul. I told her that my love for her was as constant as the star’s light, and it would last until my end. The joy of that moment is mine alone to keep. I still feel her against me and recall the intensity of that first kiss. We walked in silence to meet Adrian. Later, after he took his sister home, I asked him to return. Walking along the moonlit paths, I confessed my feelings and hopes to him. He looked troubled for a moment and said he should have seen this coming, noting the conflict that would surely follow."
}

