  "rewritten_text": "These representatives were sent to Adrian, the chosen mediator, traveling from Paris to Calais across empty towns and ruined landscapes while harboring intense mutual animosity, and they now argued their respective positions with fierce partisanship. Through separate interviews and thorough inquiry, we discovered the actual situation in Paris. Since parliament named him Ryland’s deputy, the remaining English had deferred to Adrian as their leader, legislator, and protector for their journey to foreign lands. Initially, our emigration plan assumed no prolonged separation, with the Earl of Windsor holding ultimate authority. However, unexpected events forced our groups to remain apart from the supreme leader for nearly two months, leading to conflict upon their arrival in Paris. They discovered Paris to be a wasteland. While early plague reports reached us, communication eventually ceased as mortality rates climbed. Even within England, travel became infrequent. Few vessels crossed the channel, and those who attempted the journey often perished at sea or succumbed to the infection before sharing news of France’s ruin. We had hoped to find many survivors abroad, but the plague had devastated France even more thoroughly than England. The road from Calais to Paris was entirely devoid of life. In Paris, only about a hundred people remained, wandering the streets or gathering to discuss the past with their characteristic, persistent spirit. The English occupied the lifeless city without resistance. A few pale residents lingered at the Tuileries, questioning why the islanders had come to their doomed city, as those suffering in misery often believe their own agony is the most severe, wishing only to trade their specific torment for any other."
}

