  "request_id": "6a0f5676a4ff15c84ad2174a",
  "rewritten_text": "No, that is for tomorrow, and I specifically needed to see Knightley today for that reason. It is such a scorching morning, and I walked across the fields, which made it even worse. To find him gone is unacceptable. There was no apology and no message. The housekeeper claimed she did not know I was expected, which is very strange. Nobody knew where he went—perhaps to Hartfield, the Abbey Mill, or his woods. Miss Woodhouse, this is unlike our friend Knightley. Can you explain it? Emma pretended to agree that it was very strange and that she had no excuse for him. I cannot imagine, Mrs. Elton said, feeling insulted on her husband's behalf, how he could treat you this way, especially you. My dear Mr. Elton, he must have left a message. Knightley could not be that rude; his servants must have forgotten. That is typical of the Donwell staff, who are always clumsy and careless. I would never allow someone like his servant Harry in our house. As for Mrs. Hodges, Wright thinks very little of her; she promised a recipe and never sent it. I met William Larkins near the house, Mr. Elton continued, and he told me his master was out, but I did not believe him. William seemed grumpy and said he could hardly ever speak to his master lately. I do not care about William's problems, but it is very important that I see Knightley today, so this hot, wasted walk is a serious inconvenience. Emma decided it was best to go home immediately. She was likely expected there, and Mr. Knightley might be spared further criticism from Mr. Elton, if not from William Larkins. As she left, she was pleased that Miss Fairfax followed her out of the room and downstairs, giving her a chance to speak privately."
}

