parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "15b694428add710706d74749",
  "rewritten_text": "That was all the encouragement she needed. My dear, you must know that Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been rented by a wealthy young man from the north of England. He arrived on Monday in a carriage to see the property, liked it so much that he signed the lease immediately, and plans to move in before Michaelmas. Some of his staff will arrive by the end of next week. What is his name? Bingley. Is he married or single? Oh, he is single, of course! A wealthy bachelor with four or five thousand a year. What a wonderful opportunity for our daughters! How so? How does that concern them? My dear Mr. Bennet, how can you be so annoying? You know I am thinking of him marrying one of them. Is that his plan for moving here? Nonsense, how can you say that? But it is very likely he might fall in love with one of them, so you must visit him as soon as he arrives. I see no reason for that. You and the girls can go, or you can send them on their own, which might be even better. Since you are as pretty as any of them, Mr. Bingley might prefer you. My dear, you flatter me. I once had my share of beauty, but I do not claim to be anything special now. When a woman has five grown daughters, she should stop worrying about her own looks. In such cases, a woman rarely has much beauty left to worry about. But you really must visit Mr. Bingley when he arrives. I cannot promise that, I assure you. Think of your daughters. Consider what a great match it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are going for that very reason, even though they usually do not visit newcomers. You must go, because we cannot visit him unless you do. You are being overly cautious. I am sure Mr. Bingley will be happy to see you, and I will write a note giving my full consent for him to marry whichever girl he chooses, though I must put in a good word for my favorite, Lizzy. I forbid you to do that. Lizzy is no better than the others, and she is certainly not as pretty as Jane or as cheerful as Lydia."
}

