parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9430d2c183dc0effcebc8463",
  "rewritten_text": "As summer progressed, the sun intensified, and the plague spread its deadly reach across the world. Nations suffered and perished. Crops rotted in the fields while those who went to harvest them collapsed, struck down by the sickness. The forests remained lush, providing shade for the dying, whose cries disrupted the peaceful woods. While birds and animals thrived, death claimed only humanity. Our fear grew alongside the summer heat. Looking at my wife, Idris, and our children, I vowed to protect them. I promised that one day we would tell them of these dark times, ensuring they would survive and remain healthy. Our eldest son occasionally asked about the widespread suffering, but his youth allowed him to quickly return to his play. Little Evelyn remained a joyful, innocent child, filling our home with laughter. Our daughter, Clara, was our greatest comfort. She dedicated herself to helping the sick, the elderly, and the grieving, moving through our home like a guardian angel. Despite her gentle nature and simple kindness, she possessed remarkable courage and wisdom. We endured the long summer, praying that winter would slow the disease. We dared not hope it would vanish entirely, for even mentioning such a possibility brought forth tears and overwhelming grief from those who had lost all sense of security."
}

