parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "241373c975898371379030fd",
  "rewritten_text": "I call upon the spirits of the dead and the agents of vengeance to help me in my mission. Let that cursed monster suffer deeply; let him feel the same despair that now torments me. I began my plea with a solemnity that made me feel as if my murdered friends were listening and approving, but by the end, my rage was so intense that I could barely speak. The silence of the night was broken by a loud, demonic laugh. It echoed through the mountains, and I felt as if hell itself were mocking me. I might have lost my mind and ended my life right then, but I knew my vow had been heard and that I was destined for revenge. The laughter faded, and a familiar, hated voice whispered near my ear, \"I am satisfied, you miserable wretch! You have chosen to live, and that is enough for me.\" I lunged toward the sound, but the devil escaped my grasp. Suddenly, the moon rose, illuminating his distorted figure as he fled with superhuman speed. I chased him, and this has been my life for many months. I followed his trail along the Rhone, but in vain. When I reached the Mediterranean, I saw him board a ship heading for the Black Sea. I followed on the same vessel, yet he still managed to slip away. I have tracked him through the wilderness of Russia and Tartary. Sometimes frightened peasants told me which way he went, and sometimes he left clues himself, fearing that if I lost his trail, I might give up and die. As snow fell, I followed his massive footprints across the plains. To you, who are young and have not yet known true suffering, how can I explain what I have endured? Cold, hunger, and exhaustion were the least of my pains. I felt cursed, carrying my own personal hell with me. Yet, a sense of goodness still guided my steps, and whenever I felt hopeless, I was miraculously saved from impossible situations. Sometimes, when I was near death from starvation, I would find food waiting for me in the wilderness, giving me the strength to continue."
}

