parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0e5770ac2741a06b7df27e0a",
  "rewritten_text": "“I look forward to presenting my son to you before long,” Mr. Weston remarked. Mrs. Elton, pleased to interpret this as a personal tribute, offered a gracious smile. “You are likely familiar with Frank Churchill,” he added, “and aware that he is my son, despite his different surname.” “Indeed, I shall be delighted to meet him. Mr. Elton will certainly call upon him promptly, and we would both be pleased to welcome him to the Vicarage.” “You are very kind. Frank will be equally pleased. He is expected in town by next week, if not sooner. We received word today; I intercepted the mail this morning and, seeing his handwriting, took the liberty of opening the letter addressed to Mrs. Weston. She is his primary correspondent, I fear; I rarely receive one myself.” “You actually opened her mail! Oh, Mr. Weston,” she said with a playful laugh, “I must object. That sets a dangerous precedent! I hope your neighbors do not follow suit. If this is the standard, we wives must be on our guard! I never would have suspected you of such a thing.” “We men are indeed troublesome. You must be cautious, Mrs. Elton. This brief note informs us that they are traveling to town immediately for Mrs. Churchill’s sake. She has been unwell all winter and finds Enscombe too chilly, so they are relocating south without delay.” “From Yorkshire, I believe? Is Enscombe in Yorkshire?” “Yes, it is roughly one hundred and ninety miles from London—a significant trip.” “Quite significant, indeed. Sixty-five miles further than the distance from Maple Grove to London. Yet, what is distance to the wealthy? You would be astonished by how my brother, Mr. Suckling, travels. It is hard to believe, but he and Mr. Bragge once made the round trip to London twice in a single week using four horses.” “The trouble with the distance from Enscombe,” Mr. Weston noted, “is that Mrs. Churchill has reportedly been confined to her sofa for a week. In his recent letter, Frank mentioned she was too frail to reach her conservatory without the support of both his arm and his uncle’s.”"
}

