parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "38dc91856a15c24f8e209c7d",
  "rewritten_text": "Well, Mr. Bumble, he finally said, there is no denying that since the new feeding system began, the coffins are narrower and shallower than before. Still, we must make a profit. Quality wood is expensive, and the iron handles are shipped by canal from Birmingham. Mr. Bumble agreed that every trade has its downsides and a fair profit is acceptable. Of course, the undertaker replied, if I lose money on one item, I make it up elsewhere. He chuckled. Mr. Bumble nodded. The undertaker continued, noting a major disadvantage: the stoutest people die the fastest. Those who were once better off and paid taxes are the first to fail when they enter the workhouse. A few extra inches beyond my estimate ruins my profit, especially with a family to support. Sowerberry spoke with the indignation of a wronged man. Sensing a slight against the parish, Mr. Bumble changed the subject to Oliver Twist. By the way, do you know anyone who needs a boy? A parish apprentice who is currently a burden? We offer generous terms. Bumble tapped his cane against the sign advertising five pounds. The undertaker grabbed Bumble’s lapel, saying that was exactly what he wanted to discuss. He then complimented Bumble’s elegant button. It is quite nice, the beadle said, looking at the brass buttons on his coat. The design matches the parish seal, showing the Good Samaritan helping the injured. The board gave it to me on New Year’s morning. I remember wearing it for the first time at the inquest for that bankrupt tradesman who died in a doorway."
}

