  "request_id": "549076708134d2f7c3d959cb",
  "rewritten_text": "After mourning the terrible outcome that Elizabeth felt was certain and Jane could not deny was possible, Elizabeth asked for more details. She wanted to know what Colonel Forster had said and if anyone had suspected the elopement, given how often the couple was seen together. Jane explained that the Colonel had noticed Lydia’s interest in Wickham but had not been alarmed. He felt terrible about the situation and had come to reassure them of his concern before realizing the couple had not actually gone to Scotland. When asked if Denny knew of their plans, Jane said that Denny had denied knowing anything when questioned by the Colonel. He had not repeated his earlier claim that they would not marry, which gave Jane some hope. Elizabeth asked if they had assumed the couple was married, and Jane admitted that such a thought had never occurred to them. She had been worried about Lydia’s happiness because of Wickham’s past behavior, though her parents only saw the match as imprudent. Kitty then revealed that Lydia had hinted at this in her last letter, having known about their romance for weeks. When Elizabeth asked if the Colonel thought poorly of Wickham, Jane admitted he no longer spoke of him favorably. He considered Wickham reckless and in debt, though Jane hoped the rumors of his financial troubles were untrue. Elizabeth lamented that if they had been more open about what they knew of Wickham, this might have been avoided. Jane agreed it might have been better, but noted that exposing someone’s past mistakes without knowing their current intentions had felt wrong."
}

