parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d8fb6dfc48b8dd678662bbf5",
  "rewritten_text": "One more word, Rose. Dearest Rose, just one! Harry cried, blocking her path. If I had been less fortunate—if a quiet, obscure life had been my fate—if I were poor, sick, or helpless, would you have turned away? Or has my potential for wealth and status caused this hesitation? Do not force me to answer, Rose replied. That situation does not exist and never will. It is unfair, even cruel, to ask. If your answer is what I hope, Harry insisted, it would bring light to my lonely path. It is not a small thing to offer such comfort to someone who loves you above all else. In the name of my enduring devotion and all I have suffered for you, please answer. If your life had been different, Rose said, if you were only slightly above my station, and I could have been a comfort to you in a humble, peaceful life rather than a burden in your ambitious circles, I would have been spared this pain. I have every reason to be happy now, but I admit I would have been happier then. Memories of long-cherished girlhood hopes flooded her mind, bringing tears that offered a strange relief. I cannot help this weakness, but it strengthens my resolve, Rose said, extending her hand. I must go. I only ask one promise, Harry said. Within a year, I may speak to you on this subject one last time. Not to pressure me into changing my mind, Rose replied with a sad smile, for that would be useless. No, Harry said, only to hear you confirm your decision. I will lay whatever status or fortune I have at your feet, and if you still refuse, I will never ask again. Then let it be so, Rose agreed. It is only one more moment of pain, and by then, I may be better able to bear it. She reached out her hand once more."
}

