  "rewritten_text": "I recall the conditions during our nineteenth-century civil war. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars daily in gold, while in the South, he received fifty in nearly worthless Confederate currency. Since a pair of overalls cost a day's pay in the North but two days' wages in the South, Northern earnings effectively held double the purchasing power. During my time in the village, I was pleased to see our new currency circulating among the common people, including mills, cents, nickels, and silver. Gold remained at the goldsmith's shop. While Marco was busy arguing over salt prices, I requested change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. The staff scrutinized the coin, testing its weight and purity, before subjecting me to an exhaustive interrogation about my identity and travels. When their questions ceased, I provided unsolicited details about my dog, my late wife's religious background, and an acquaintance with extra thumbs, until the inquisitive clerk seemed both satisfied and annoyed. Though he dared not challenge a man of my means, he vented his frustration on his subordinates. They eventually broke my twenty, though the transaction clearly taxed their resources, much like asking a small-town merchant to change a two-thousand-dollar bill. The goldsmith likely wondered how a traveler possessed such wealth, as he stood at the door watching me depart with a look of awestruck respect."
}

