  "rewritten_text": "I bored a hole in the bottom of the keg and inserted a tapered wooden plug. After a few trials, I adjusted the plug so that water dripped into the pitcher below, filling it exactly in one hour. I determined this by checking how much water collected over a short period. My bed was positioned on the floor so my head rested directly beneath the pitcher. Once the pitcher filled, it would overflow from its spout, which sat lower than the rim. Falling from over four feet, the water would land on my face and wake me instantly, no matter how deeply I slept. By eleven o'clock, I finished these preparations and went to bed, confident in my device. It worked perfectly. Every hour, the water woke me, and I would empty the pitcher back into the keg before returning to sleep. These interruptions bothered me less than expected. When I finally woke for the day, it was seven o'clock, and the sun was high. On April 3rd, I found the balloon at a massive altitude, and the curve of the earth was clearly visible. Below, black specks in the ocean were surely islands. To the north, a thin, bright white line on the horizon appeared to be the ice of the Polar Sea. I was eager to travel further north, hoping to eventually fly directly over the Pole. However, I worried that my extreme height would prevent me from observing the area as clearly as I wanted."
}

