  "rewritten_text": "We visited your home, the first man stated, but missing you there and hearing you had walked this way, we followed to catch you. Please step into the carriage. Their demeanor was commanding, and they positioned themselves to block my path to the door. They carried weapons; I did not. Gentlemen, I replied, forgive me, but I typically ask who seeks my help and what the situation entails. The second man answered, Doctor, your clients are of high standing. Regarding the case, our faith in your expertise convinces us you will determine the details better than we could explain. Enough. Please enter the carriage. I had no choice but to obey and climbed in silently. Both men followed, the last one pulling up the steps. The carriage turned and resumed its pace. I recount this dialogue precisely as it happened, word for word. I describe events exactly as they occurred, keeping my focus steady. Where I insert these breaks, I am pausing to conceal my notes. The carriage exited the city, passed the North Barrier, and reached the country road. About two miles from the barrier—a distance I calculated later—it left the main route and stopped at a secluded house. We all stepped out and walked along a damp garden path past an overflowing, neglected fountain to the entrance. The door did not open instantly when the bell rang, and one of my escorts struck the servant who answered across the face with his heavy riding glove. This violence did not surprise me, as I had often seen commoners treated worse than animals. However, the second man, also irritated, struck the servant in the same fashion. Their identical expressions and movements revealed they were twin brothers. From the moment we arrived at the locked outer gate, which they had unlocked and secured behind us, I had heard cries coming from an upstairs room."
