  "rewritten_text": "This marriage is right. I doubted it at first, but I see it now. The gods created Sibyl Vane for you; without her, you would be incomplete. Thanks, Basil, Dorian Gray replied, pressing his hand. I knew you would understand. Harry is so cynical he scares me. But the orchestra is starting. It is terrible, but it only lasts five minutes. Then the curtain rises, and you will see the girl to whom I have dedicated my life and everything good in me. Fifteen minutes later, amidst loud applause, Sibyl Vane appeared on stage. She was lovely, one of the most beautiful creatures Lord Henry had ever seen. She possessed a shy, fawn-like grace, and her cheeks flushed as she looked at the enthusiastic crowd. Basil Hallward stood up to applaud, while Dorian Gray sat motionless, staring at her like a man in a dream. Lord Henry peered through his glasses, whispering that she was charming. The scene was the Capulet house, and Romeo had entered with his friends. As the music played, Sibyl moved through the group of poorly dressed actors like a creature from another world. She swayed like a plant in water, her throat curved like a lily, and her hands looked like cool ivory. Yet, she seemed strangely listless. She showed no joy when she looked at Romeo. The lines she spoke were delivered in a completely artificial way. Her voice was beautiful, but the tone was false, draining the life from the verse and making the passion feel unreal. Dorian Gray turned pale as he watched her, feeling puzzled and anxious. His friends remained silent, as she appeared completely incompetent to them."
