parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a6a154aaaeaad32ddb2e4777",
  "rewritten_text": "The following morning, the Otis family gathered for breakfast and spoke at length about the specter. The American Minister felt slightly vexed that his gift had been rejected. He stated that he had no desire to harm the spirit, yet he found it discourteous to pelt him with pillows, given his long tenure in the residence. This reasonable observation prompted the twins to erupt in laughter. He added that if the ghost refused the lubricant, they would be forced to confiscate his chains, as the racket outside their rooms made sleep impossible. The remainder of the week passed peacefully, save for the recurring bloodstain in the library. This was peculiar, as Mr. Otis kept the room locked and the windows secured each night. The stain’s shifting hues also drew notice, varying from dull red to vermilion, deep purple, and even a vibrant emerald-green during morning prayers. These transformations entertained the family, who placed bets on the color, though young Virginia remained troubled by the sight, nearly weeping when the stain turned green. The ghost reappeared on Sunday night. A loud crash in the hall startled the family from their beds. Downstairs, they discovered a suit of armor had toppled from its stand, and the Canterville ghost sat in a high-backed chair, nursing his knees in obvious pain. The twins immediately fired their pea-shooters with practiced precision, while the Minister leveled his revolver and ordered the spirit to raise his hands, per Californian custom. With a furious cry, the ghost vanished through them like mist, blowing out Washington’s candle and plunging the group into darkness."
}

