parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d7e4d4be6b2182399f326998",
  "rewritten_text": "They were her life, more precious than any sanctuary of love or anything else on earth. The boys were delighted by the news of our departure, and Clara asked if we were moving to Athens. When I told her it was possible, her face lit up with joy. She longed to see her parents' tomb and the land where her father had earned his fame. She had spent so much time quietly thinking about those places, and those memories had turned her childhood playfulness into a serious, restless ambition. We had many humble friends we could not leave behind, including the spirited horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter, Alfred’s dog, and an aging pet eagle. Yet, making this list of favorites only reminded us of the heavy losses we had suffered and the many things we were forced to abandon. Idris wept, while Alfred and Evelyn brought me a favorite rose tree and a carved marble vase, insisting they must come with us and lamenting that we could not take the entire castle, forest, and wildlife. I told them they were being foolish, as we had already lost far greater treasures and were leaving these behind to protect what truly mattered. I urged them to focus on our goal, which would help ease their regret over such trifles. The children were easily distracted and soon returned to their excitement. Idris, however, had disappeared to hide her sadness. I found her in the park, clinging to an old oak tree and sobbing uncontrollably. It broke my heart to see her in such pain. I pulled her close, and as she felt my arms around her, she remembered what she still had. She thanked me for not scolding her, admitting that while her grief was sharp, she remained happy because I and our children were still by her side, unlike so many others who had lost their entire families."
}

