  "rewritten_text": "Should you require one for personal use, I can provide a superior item and promise your complete satisfaction. May I inquire as to the recipient? If it is for the police or anyone associated with Scotland Yard, I cannot assist. English detectives are our greatest allies; their incompetence allows us to act with impunity, and I would hate to lose them. Lord Arthur replied that the police were not involved, explaining that the clock was meant for the Dean of Chichester. Herr Winckelkopf expressed surprise at such religious devotion in a young man, though Lord Arthur blushed and admitted he knew nothing of theology. Confirming the matter was strictly private, the man left and returned with a small dynamite disc and an elegant French clock topped with an ormolu figure of Liberty. Lord Arthur was delighted and asked how it functioned. Winckelkopf kept his methods secret, offering to calibrate the device for any desired moment. Lord Arthur requested it be set for Friday at noon, when the Dean would be home. The maker noted this in his ledger. When asked for the cost, Winckelkopf dismissed the labor, charging only for the materials and shipping, noting his pleasure in helping a friend of Count Rouvaloff. Lord Arthur then asked about compensation for his effort."
}

