parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1d0d30d2b283f81d70dd6df4",
  "rewritten_text": "My friend carefully drove a peg into the ground exactly where the beetle had landed, then pulled a tape measure from his pocket. He attached one end to the nearest point on the tree trunk, extended it to the peg, and continued fifty feet further in that same line while Jupiter cleared the brush with a scythe. At that spot, he drove a second peg and marked a rough four-foot circle around it. He handed spades to Jupiter and me, urging us to start digging immediately. Honestly, I had no desire for this task; I was exhausted, night was falling, and I wanted to decline. Yet, I saw no way out and feared upsetting my friend. I considered forcing him home, but I knew Jupiter would never help me oppose his master. I assumed Legrand had fallen victim to local superstitions about buried treasure, reinforced by the beetle and Jupiter’s insistence that it was made of solid gold. Such ideas easily sway a fragile mind, especially when they align with existing obsessions like his belief that the insect was his key to fortune. Frustrated and confused, I decided to make the best of it, hoping that digging would quickly prove his theory wrong. We lit our lanterns and began working with misplaced intensity. As the light illuminated our group and tools, I realized how strange and suspicious we must have looked to any accidental observer. We dug steadily for two hours."
}

