parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7b86ab92ceb32e355e5e4f8d",
  "rewritten_text": "I would wake up eventually to investigate the cause of a sound, perhaps spotting a steamboat chugging upstream so far away that I could only distinguish if it had a stern or side wheel. Then, for an hour, there was nothing to see or hear—just pure solitude. Later, a raft would drift by in the distance, and I might see a man chopping wood; the axe would flash, and only after it rose again would the dull thud finally reach us across the water. We spent our days idling in the silence. Once, a dense fog rolled in, and the passing rafts beat tin pans to warn off steamboats. A vessel passed so close we could clearly hear the crew talking, swearing, and laughing, yet we saw nothing. It felt eerie, like ghosts haunting the air. Jim thought they were spirits, but I argued that ghosts wouldn't curse the fog. Once night fell, we pushed off, letting the current carry us while we smoked and dangled our legs in the water. We stayed naked day and night whenever the mosquitoes allowed, as I never cared for clothes and the ones Buck’s family gave me were too restrictive. Sometimes the river was entirely ours. We watched the distant banks and islands, occasionally spotting a candle flickering in a cabin window or a light on a passing raft, sometimes accompanied by the faint sound of a fiddle or a song. Life on a raft is beautiful. We would lie on our backs, gazing at the star-speckled sky, debating whether the stars were created or simply happened. Jim insisted they were made, but I argued they just happened, as it would have taken too long to create so many."
}

