parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7af5606bef26fe7dd8a4faed",
  "rewritten_text": "Ten minutes later, his face drained of color and eyes wide with sorrow, Lord Arthur Savile bolted from Bentinck House, pushing past the liveried servants beneath the striped awning as if oblivious to his surroundings. The air was freezing, and the streetlamps wavered in the sharp gale, yet his skin burned with a feverish heat. He stumbled forward with the unsteady rhythm of a man intoxicated. A constable watched him with suspicion, and a beggar emerging from a doorway recoiled, intimidated by a despair deeper than his own. Pausing beneath a light, he inspected his hands, imagining he saw bloodstains, and a soft moan escaped his shaking mouth. Murder was what the palmist had discovered. The word haunted the night, carried by the howling wind and lurking in every shadowed corner and rooftop. He reached the Park, drawn to its gloomy woods. Leaning against the cold, damp railings, he whispered the word repeatedly, as if repetition might soften its dread. His own voice startled him, yet he longed for the city to wake and hear his confession. He wandered toward Oxford Street and into squalid lanes. Painted women jeered at him, and from a dark yard came the sounds of violence and screaming. Seeing the bent, impoverished figures on a doorstep, he felt a sudden, strange sympathy. Were they, like him, merely fated pawns in a cruel performance? It was the absurdity of their suffering that struck him—its utter pointlessness and lack of logic. The world felt disjointed and discordant, a stark contrast to the superficial cheerfulness of his social circle. He was, after all, still very young. Eventually, he arrived at Marylebone Church, where the empty street stretched out like a silver ribbon, patterned by the shifting silhouettes of trees."
}

