  "rewritten_text": "“So, I own a few knights now,” I remarked as we rode away. “Who would have thought I’d end up with assets like these? I have no idea what to do with them, unless I raffle them off. How many are there, Sandy?” “Seven, sir, along with their squires.” “That’s a decent haul. Who are they, and where are they based?” “Where are they based?” “Yes, where do they live?” “Ah, I didn’t understand you. I will tell you shortly.” She repeated the phrase to herself, savoring the sound. “Where are they based? It has a charming, elegant ring to it. I shall practice it while I’m idle so I might learn it. Where are they based? It already rolls off my tongue quite easily.” “Don’t forget the knights, Sandy.” “Knights?” “Yes, you were going to tell me about them. Let’s get to the point.” “I will begin,” she said. “So, the two of them departed and rode into a vast forest. And—” “Good grief!” I realized my mistake immediately. I had started her off, and it was my own fault; she would take a month to reach the facts. She usually started without an introduction and finished without a conclusion. If I interrupted, she would either ignore me or repeat the sentence from the beginning. Interruptions were counterproductive, yet I had to make them to survive; the sheer monotony of her storytelling was unbearable. “Good grief!” I exclaimed in frustration. She simply started over. “So, the two of them departed and rode into a vast forest. And—” “Which two?” “Sir Gawaine and Sir Uwaine. They arrived at an abbey of monks, where they were well lodged. The next morning, they heard mass and rode until they reached a great forest. There, Sir Gawaine noticed twelve beautiful damsels and two armed knights on large horses in a valley near a tower, with the damsels walking back and forth by a tree.”"
}

