parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "41f58e78a2e130d43b873ee5",
  "rewritten_text": "Mrs. Musgrove seized a moment alone with Anne to remark, \"Oh, Miss Anne, I truly wish Mrs. Charles possessed your way with those children. They behave like entirely different beings when they are with you! They are generally so coddled, it is a shame you cannot teach your sister how to handle them. They are such robust, healthy little things, but Mrs. Charles has no idea how to manage them. They can be so vexing that I hesitate to invite them over as often as I might. I suspect Mrs. Charles is annoyed by my reluctance, but it is exhausting to constantly correct children or bribe them with excessive sweets just to keep them quiet.\" Mary also shared her own grievances, claiming, \"Mrs. Musgrove believes her servants are paragons of virtue, but her housemaid and laundry-maid are constantly wandering the village instead of working. I see them everywhere, and they are always trying to lead Jemima astray. If she were not so reliable, they would certainly ruin her.\" Conversely, Mrs. Musgrove confided to Anne, \"I never interfere in my daughter-in-law's affairs, but I must tell you that I distrust her nursery-maid. She is always out and about, and her obsession with fine clothing is a bad influence on the other staff. Mrs. Charles trusts her implicitly, but I wanted to warn you so you might keep a watchful eye and speak up if you notice anything wrong.\" Finally, Mary complained that Mrs. Musgrove frequently failed to grant her the social precedence she deserved during dinners at the Great House, failing to see why her status as family should cost her proper recognition."
}

