parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3d40431d07beee5014b3fbe5",
  "rewritten_text": "Look, Merlin said, there is the sword I mentioned. They saw a woman walking on the lake. Who is she? Arthur asked. That is the Lady of the Lake, Merlin replied. Within that lake is a rock containing a beautiful dwelling, and she will approach you soon. Speak kindly to her so she might grant you the blade. The woman soon reached Arthur and greeted him. Arthur asked about the sword held by the arm above the water, expressing his desire for it since he lacked one. King Arthur, the woman said, that sword is mine, but if you promise me a future favor, it shall be yours. Arthur agreed to her terms. She told him to row out to the sword, take it and the scabbard, and she would claim her gift later. Arthur and Merlin tied their horses to trees and boarded a boat. Arthur took the sword by the hilt, and the arm vanished beneath the water. Returning to land, they rode on until they saw a lavish tent. Merlin explained it belonged to Sir Pellinore, the knight Arthur had previously fought. Pellinore was away, having chased one of Arthur's knights, Egglame, toward Carlion. Arthur declared he would use his new sword to seek vengeance, but Merlin advised against it. He warned that Pellinore was exhausted from battle, making a fight dishonorable, and that the knight was too skilled to be easily defeated. Merlin urged Arthur to let him pass, promising that Pellinore and his sons would eventually serve him well, and that Arthur would one day be happy to give the knight his sister in marriage. Arthur agreed to follow his counsel."
}

