parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5d7cbeaeec7b740f698e546f",
  "rewritten_text": "These deputies had been sent to Adrian, the chosen arbiter. They traveled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and desolate landscapes, harboring intense mutual hatred, and now they argued their cases with fierce partisanship. By questioning the deputies separately, we discovered the true situation in Paris. Since parliament named him Ryland’s deputy, the remaining English had deferred to Adrian as their leader, lawgiver, and protector. Originally, we intended to stay together under the Earl of Windsor’s command, but unforeseen events forced us to split for nearly two months. Upon arriving in Paris in two separate groups, conflict erupted. They found Paris a wasteland. While early plague reports reached us, communication eventually ceased as mortality rates climbed. Even within England, travel became rare. No ships crossed from Calais to Dover; those who tried were often lost at sea or arrived already infected, dying before they could describe the devastation. We had hoped to find many survivors on the continent, but the plague had been even more destructive there. France was empty; we saw no one on the road from Calais to Paris. In the capital, perhaps a hundred people remained, wandering the streets or gathering to discuss the past with their characteristic, resilient spirit. The English took over the silent city. A few pale figures at the Tuileries wondered why we had come to their doomed home, for in deep misery, people always believe their own suffering is the worst, wishing only to trade their specific agony for any other."
}

