parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "44de8005e9388614250f3c2d",
  "rewritten_text": "April 12th. The balloon shifted its course, a change I had anticipated and which brought me immense relief. After reaching the twentieth parallel south, it veered sharply eastward, maintaining a path aligned with the lunar ellipse. This maneuver caused a noticeable, persistent swaying of the car that lasted for several hours. April 13th. The loud, crackling noise from the tenth returned, causing me great alarm. I pondered its cause without success. The earth’s apparent size has shrunk significantly, now spanning an angle of just over twenty-five degrees. The moon is hidden directly above me. I remain on the elliptical path but have made little eastward progress. April 14th. The earth is shrinking rapidly. I am convinced the balloon is traveling along the line of apsides toward the perigee, heading directly for the moon’s closest point to earth. The moon remains obscured overhead. I have spent considerable effort condensing the atmosphere. April 15th. The earth’s continents and seas are no longer distinguishable. Around noon, I heard that terrifying sound for the third time. It grew in intensity until the car shook violently. A massive, flaming object roared past me like thunder. Once my shock faded, I concluded it was a volcanic fragment ejected from the moon—likely the same type of substance known on earth as a meteoric stone. April 16th. Looking through the side windows, I was delighted to see the edges of the moon’s disk finally emerging from behind the balloon’s massive frame."
}

