parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e612553ff895ba3298386928",
  "rewritten_text": "My best customers no longer had time for me. They claimed they were too busy reading about revolutions and keeping up with the latest intellectual trends. If a fire needed fanning, a newspaper worked just fine, and as the government weakened, my leather and iron goods seemed to become indestructible; soon, no bellows in Rotterdam needed repairs. This situation was unbearable. I became penniless, and with a wife and children to support, my life felt like an impossible burden. I spent hours considering how to end it. Meanwhile, debt collectors gave me no peace. My house was constantly surrounded, making me pace like a caged tiger. Three men in particular harassed me, threatening legal action. I vowed to take bitter revenge on them if I ever had the chance, and only the hope of that vengeance kept me from shooting myself. I decided to hide my anger and appease them with empty promises until fate offered me a way to strike back. One day, after slipping away from my creditors, I wandered the backstreets in despair until I stumbled upon a bookseller’s stall. I sat in a nearby chair and aimlessly opened the first book I found. It was a pamphlet on speculative astronomy by Professor Encke or a French author with a similar name. Having some interest in the subject, I became completely absorbed, reading the entire volume twice before I finally noticed my surroundings again."
}

