  "rewritten_text": "Four days later, a funeral procession departed Canterville Chase at eleven o’clock at night. Eight black horses pulled the hearse, their heads adorned with nodding ostrich plumes, while the leaden coffin lay beneath a purple pall embroidered with the golden Canterville crest. Servants carrying torches walked alongside the carriages, creating a somber and striking scene. Lord Canterville, having traveled from Wales, served as chief mourner, riding with young Virginia. The American Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the boys, with Mrs. Umney in the final carriage; having endured the ghost’s presence for fifty years, she was deemed entitled to witness his final rest. The Reverend Augustus Dampier conducted the service at a grave beneath the churchyard’s ancient yew tree. As the coffin was lowered, Virginia placed a cross of white and pink almond blossoms upon it. Suddenly, the moon emerged from the clouds, bathing the churchyard in silver light as a nightingale sang from a nearby thicket. Recalling the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death, Virginia wept silently and remained quiet for the journey home. The following morning, Mr. Otis met with Lord Canterville to discuss the jewels the ghost had gifted Virginia. They were magnificent, particularly a sixteenth-century Venetian ruby necklace, and their immense value caused Mr. Otis to hesitate about his daughter keeping them. He told Lord Canterville that he believed the jewels should be considered family heirlooms and requested that he take them to London as recovered property. He added that his daughter was merely a child with no interest in such luxuries."
}

