parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "07a6d6c799790f87a7bab262",
  "rewritten_text": "He thought so, at least, but who could be sure? No, there was nothing more. He had spared her out of vanity and acted the part of a good man out of hypocrisy. He had practiced self-denial only to satisfy his curiosity. He understood that now. But would this murder haunt him forever? Was he destined to be burdened by his past? Confess? Never. Only one piece of evidence remained: the portrait itself. He had to destroy it. Why had he kept it so long? It once brought him pleasure to watch it age, but lately, it only caused him distress and kept him awake. When away, he lived in terror that someone might see it. It had cast a shadow over his passions and ruined his joy. It had become his conscience. He would destroy it. He spotted the knife he had used to kill Basil Hallward. He had polished it until it shone. Just as it had killed the painter, it would now destroy the painter’s work and everything it represented. It would kill the past, and with that dead, he would be free. It would end this monstrous existence, and without its hideous warnings, he would finally find peace. He grabbed the blade and stabbed the canvas. A cry of agony rang out, followed by a crash. The sound was so horrific that the terrified servants woke and crept from their rooms. Two men passing through the square below stopped to look at the mansion. They found a policeman and brought him back. He rang the bell repeatedly, but there was no answer. Aside from a light in an upper window, the house remained dark. Eventually, the officer moved to a nearby portico to watch. Whose house is that, Constable? the older man asked. It belongs to Mr. Dorian Gray, the officer replied. The men exchanged a sneer as they walked away. One was the uncle of Sir Henry Ashton. Inside, the servants whispered to one another in their nightclothes. Mrs. Leaf wept and wrung her hands while Francis looked deathly pale. After fifteen minutes, he gathered the coachman and a footman and crept upstairs."
}

