parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "11b63804a4a4e40f313ad1aa",
  "rewritten_text": "Years ago, I became close with William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and was once wealthy, but a string of misfortunes left him destitute. To escape the shame of his losses, he left New Orleans, his ancestral home, and moved to Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina. This island is unique, consisting mostly of sea sand, three miles long and less than a quarter-mile wide. It is separated from the mainland by a narrow creek winding through reeds and mud, a haven for marsh hens. Vegetation is sparse and stunted. Aside from the western tip near Fort Moultrie, where some modest summer cottages for Charleston residents stand among palmettos, the island is covered in dense thickets of sweet myrtle. These shrubs grow up to fifteen feet high, forming nearly impenetrable groves that fill the air with scent. Deep within these thickets, near the island's eastern end, Legrand built a small hut where he lived when I first met him by chance. Our acquaintance quickly grew into friendship, as the recluse was a man of deep intellect and character. However, he was prone to misanthropy and erratic shifts between intense enthusiasm and deep melancholy. Though he owned many books, he rarely read them, preferring to hunt, fish, or wander the beach and myrtle groves searching for shells and insects. His collection of specimens would have impressed any expert. He was usually accompanied by an elderly man named Jupiter, a formerly enslaved person who had been freed before the family's decline. Jupiter refused to leave his young master’s side, regardless of threats or promises. It is likely that Legrand’s relatives, fearing he was mentally unstable, had encouraged Jupiter’s stubborn loyalty to ensure the wanderer was watched over."
}

