parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "30596cf2a04bf10c90fa210e",
  "rewritten_text": "I recited the beautiful passage describing Dante’s arrival in the terrestrial Paradise, imagining how wonderful it would be to wander those banks and see the chariot of light descend, bringing my long-lost father back to me. I pictured myself weaving a wreath from the lovely flowers there to celebrate our reunion. I would sing my father’s favorite song, 'sul margine d’un rio,' and my voice, drifting through the still air, would tell him I had arrived. The marks of my sorrow would vanish, and I would look fearlessly into his eyes, which always shone with innocent love. Thinking of his gaze made me weep, though I kept my sobs quiet to avoid breaking the spell. I was so lost in this daydream that I didn't notice where I was walking until I reached down to pick a flower on that barren plain where nothing grew. I woke from my reverie, realizing I was completely lost. The sun had set, and the pink glow of the clouds had faded. A cold wind blew across the plain, and I could not find my way in the gathering dark. Eventually, night fell completely. Knowing my servant was staying in a nearby village and would not worry, and feeling safe from intruders in this wild place, I decided to stay where I was. I was too exhausted to continue. The air was cold, but I was used to the elements after two years of solitary wandering. I lay on the grass in total darkness. The night was silent, as even the insects were still in this desolate place devoid of trees or shrubs. This profound silence calmed my senses while stirring my soul, and my mind raced through endless thoughts."
}

