parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b6ba366170916903ebfbc14e",
  "rewritten_text": "Story! He seemed to deliberately misunderstand her, quickly adding, Yes, clients; in banking, we refer to our connections as clients. He was a French gentleman, a man of science and great learning—a doctor. Not from Beauvais? Yes, from Beauvais. Like your father, Monsieur Manette, he was well-regarded in Paris. I had the honor of knowing him there. Our relationship was professional, though confidential. I was working at our French branch then, as I had been for twenty years. At that time—may I ask when that was, sir? I am speaking of twenty years ago, miss. He married an Englishwoman, and I served as one of his trustees. His affairs, like those of many French families, were managed entirely by Tellson’s. I have acted as trustee for many clients in similar ways. These are strictly business matters; there is no friendship, no sentiment involved. I move from one to another just as I do throughout my workday. In short, I have no feelings; I am merely a machine. To continue— But this is my father’s story, sir; and I am beginning to think, she said, her brow furrowed in concentration, that when I was orphaned after my mother died two years after my father, it was you who brought me to England. I am almost certain it was you. Mr. Lorry took her hesitant hand, kissed it with formal grace, and led her back to her chair. Holding the back of the chair, he rubbed his chin and adjusted his wig, looking down at her as she gazed up at him. Miss Manette, it was I. You will see how truthfully I described myself as having no feelings, and that my connections to others are purely professional, when you consider that I have not seen you since. You have been a ward of Tellson’s, and I have been occupied with the firm’s other affairs. Feelings! I have no time or opportunity for them. I spend my entire life, miss, operating a vast financial machine."
}

