parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "176b096847076446bd50320e",
  "rewritten_text": "The cycle of suffering that dictates existence must have been interrupted, yet the cruel destiny governing it would not tolerate such a violation of her ancient decrees. Why should I complain? Sorrow was my natural state, and only the wretched could draw near; had Woodville been content, our paths would never have crossed. Can I, who subsisted on tears and grew under the shadow of sorrow, hesitate to recount a narrative of grief and demise? Woodville was compelled to travel to the countryside, where he was held back for days, away from his beloved wife. He received a note from her mentioning a minor illness, yet urging him to return, claiming his presence would be her most effective cure. Delayed for three more days, he finally rushed to her. An inexplicable dread filled his heart; having heard nothing further, he feared her condition had worsened. This anxiety made him desperate to see her restored to health, even as a dark intuition murmured that he would never see her as she once was. Upon reaching her home, he found it deathly quiet. Passing through the rooms, he discovered a servant sobbing. Trembling with terror, he barely managed to ask if she had passed, only to hear the chilling reply, \"Not yet.\" These words felt less devastating than he had anticipated; knowing she still lived offered a glimmer of hope. Recalling her letter, he clung to the desperate belief that his loving touch would restore her spirit and that she could not perish while he remained by her side. He hurried to her chamber, where she lay feverish and unresponsive. Embracing her, he pressed frantic kisses to her hot lips and whispered her name in agony, begging her to return to him. He pleaded for her to revive, insisting she could not die in his presence, for to lose her was to lose his own identity, his past, and his future."
}

