  "rewritten_text": "Smallpox! I rushed to the king and whispered, \"Leave immediately, sire! The woman is dying of the same plague that devastated Camelot two years ago.\" He did not move. \"I shall stay and help,\" he insisted. I urged him again, \"King, you must go.\" He replied, \"You mean well, but it would be shameful for a king to show fear or for a knight to withhold aid. I will not leave. You must go, as the Church forbids your presence here, and they will punish you severely if they find out.\" It was a dangerous situation that could cost him his life, but I knew arguing was useless once he felt his honor was at stake. I dropped the subject. The woman spoke, \"Kind sir, would you climb that ladder and tell me what you find? Do not fear to tell me, for a mother's heart can break only once.\" The king set down his pack and said, \"Stay and feed her. I will go.\" He started up, then paused, looking down at a man lying in the shadows who had not spoken. \"Is this your husband?\" the king asked. \"Yes,\" she replied. \"Is he asleep?\" \"Thank God, yes, for three hours. My heart is full of gratitude for his rest.\" I promised, \"We will be careful not to wake him.\" She answered, \"You cannot, for he is dead.\" \"Dead?\" I asked. \"Yes, and what a triumph that is! No one can harm or insult him now. He is in heaven, or if he is in hell, he is content, for he will find no bishops there. We were together for twenty-five years, never separated until today. Think of how long we loved and suffered. This morning he was delirious, imagining we were children wandering in happy fields, and in that innocent conversation, he wandered into the afterlife and left my sight.\""
}

