  "rewritten_text": "Yet, no joy existed in the small town that peaceful Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly’s household were draped in mourning, filled with profound sorrow and weeping. An uncommon stillness hung over the village, which was typically quiet enough as it was. The residents went about their business distractedly, speaking little but sighing frequently. The Saturday break felt like a weight to the children, who lost interest in their games and eventually abandoned them. In the afternoon, Becky Thatcher wandered the empty schoolyard, feeling deeply despondent. Finding no solace, she mused aloud, wishing she still possessed the brass andiron knob to remember him by, stifling a sob. She paused, reflecting on the past, and lamented that she would never see him again, which caused her to break down in tears. Soon, a group of Tom and Joe’s playmates gathered by the fence, speaking in hushed, reverent tones about the last things the boys had said or done, which now seemed prophetic. Each child pointed out where they had stood, recalling small gestures and the strange feelings they had experienced at the time. A debate arose over who had last seen the boys alive, with many claiming the grim honor and offering embellished proof. Once the final witnesses were identified, they assumed a solemn importance, envied by the others. One boy, lacking any other claim to fame, proudly noted that Tom Sawyer had once beaten him up, but this failed to impress, as most of the others could say the same. The group drifted away, continuing to share memories of the lost boys in hushed voices."
}

