  "rewritten_text": "No, the girl insisted. Tell me why. For one reason, she replied firmly, which the lady knows and will support, as she promised. Also, despite his wicked life, I have lived a bad one too. Many of us have shared the same path, and I will not betray those who could have turned on me but chose not to. Then, the gentleman said, hand Monks over to me and let me handle him. What if he betrays the others? I promise that if the truth is forced from him, the matter ends there. Oliver’s history contains painful details that should not be public; if the truth is revealed, the others will go free. And if it is not? Then, the gentleman continued, Fagin will not be prosecuted without your consent. I believe I could then convince you to agree. Do I have the lady’s word? You do, Rose replied. My sincere pledge. Monks will never know how you learned this? the girl asked. Never, the gentleman answered. The information will be used in a way he could never guess. I have been a liar since childhood, the girl said after a silence, but I will trust your word. After receiving their assurances, she spoke in a voice so quiet it was hard to follow, identifying the pub she had been followed from. She paused frequently, as if the gentleman were taking notes. Once she had detailed the location, the best vantage point, and the times Monks usually visited, she paused to recall his appearance. He is tall and well-built, but not stout, she said. He walks with a furtive gait, constantly glancing over his shoulders."
}

