parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "289cb11aa1a209866e202b6b",
  "rewritten_text": "So you will not, will you? You precious fool! It is wicked, is it not, Oliver? asked Charley Bates. He will end up hanged, will he not? I do not understand that term, Oliver answered. It means this, my friend, said Charley. He grabbed his neckerchief, held it high, let his head loll, and made a sharp noise with his teeth, demonstrating through pantomime that scragging meant hanging. That is what it means, Charley noted. Look at his expression, Jack! This boy is such excellent company; he will be the death of me. Charley laughed until he wept, then returned to his pipe. You have been raised poorly, the Dodger remarked, admiring his polished boots. Fagin will shape you, or you will be his first failure. You should start immediately, as you will join the trade sooner than you expect; you are wasting time, Oliver. Master Bates added his own moral advice, and then he and Mr. Dawkins described the joys of their lifestyle, urging Oliver to win Fagin’s favor quickly. Remember this, Nolly, the Dodger said as the Jew unlocked the door, if you do not steal handkerchiefs and watches— What is the point of that? Charley interrupted; he does not understand you. If you do not take them, the Dodger explained, someone else will, so the victims lose out, you lose out, and nobody gains anything except the thieves—and you have as much right to them as anyone. Certainly, certainly! said the Jew, who had entered unnoticed. It is simple, my dear; take the Dodger’s word for it. He knows his trade well. The old man rubbed his hands, delighted by his pupil’s logic. The discussion ended there, as the Jew arrived with Miss Betsy and a stranger named Tom Chitling, who had paused on the stairs to flirt with the lady before entering."
}

