parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "32af21f53685ccdd34f635c3",
  "rewritten_text": "He chose Friday, August 17th, for his appearance, spending the day reviewing his wardrobe before selecting a wide-brimmed hat with a red feather, a ruffled burial shroud, and a corroded dagger. As evening fell, a fierce rainstorm erupted, rattling the house’s doors and windows—the exact weather he preferred. His plan was to slip into Washington Otis’s room, gibber from the foot of the bed, and stab his own throat three times to the accompaniment of slow music. He held a grudge against Washington for repeatedly scrubbing away the Canterville bloodstain with detergent. After terrifying the youth, he intended to visit the Minister and his wife, placing a cold hand on her forehead while whispering graveyard secrets into her husband’s ear. He remained undecided about Virginia, who had been gentle and never insulted him; he thought a few hollow groans from her wardrobe or clawing at her bedsheets would suffice. He was resolved to punish the twins by sitting on their chests to induce nightmares, then standing between their beds as a frozen, green corpse before shedding his shroud to crawl about as the 'Suicide’s Skeleton,' a role he had perfected. At ten-thirty, he heard the family retiring. The twins’ laughter delayed him, but by eleven-fifteen, silence fell. As midnight struck, he emerged. An owl struck the glass, a raven croaked, and the wind moaned like a lost soul, yet the Otis family slept on, oblivious, while the Minister’s steady snoring rose above the storm."
}

