  "rewritten_text": "Dorian, these matters must concern you. Every gentleman cares for his reputation. You surely do not wish to be branded as base or corrupt. While you possess status and fortune, those things are not absolute. I do not credit these rumors, especially when I look at you. Sin inevitably marks a man’s countenance; it is impossible to hide. There is no such thing as a secret vice. If a man is corrupt, it manifests in his mouth, his eyelids, or even his hands. Last year, a man I will not name sought a portrait. I refused him despite his generous offer because I detested the shape of his fingers. I later learned my instincts were correct; his life is truly wretched. Yet, looking at your innocent, youthful face, I cannot believe these accusations. Still, I see you rarely, and you avoid my studio. When I am apart from you and hear these vile whispers, I am at a loss. Why does the Duke of Berwick exit a club room upon your arrival? Why do so many London gentlemen shun you? You were once close with Lord Staveley. When your name arose at dinner last week regarding your miniatures, he sneered, claiming that while you possess artistic taste, you are unfit for the company of virtuous women. When I demanded an explanation, he spoke plainly before everyone. It was appalling. Why is your companionship so ruinous to young men? Consider the poor boy in the Guards who took his own life, or Sir Henry Ashton, who fled England in disgrace."
}

