parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "512e6561c70bbee5590a67a6",
  "rewritten_text": "There was plenty of cold water to finish their meal. Soon, Becky suggested they keep moving, but Tom hesitated. Becky, can you handle some bad news? he asked. Her face went pale, but she nodded. We have to stay here near the water, Tom explained. That little piece of candle is all we have left. Becky began to cry and wail. Tom tried to comfort her, but it did little good. Eventually, Becky asked if people would search for them. Tom agreed they certainly would. She wondered if they were looking now, and Tom hoped they were. When would they realize we are gone? she asked. Tom guessed it would be when they returned to the boat. Becky worried it might be dark by then, but Tom assured her that her mother would notice her absence as soon as she got home. Seeing the fear on her face, Tom realized his mistake; Becky was not supposed to be home that night. They fell silent, both realizing that it might be Sunday morning before anyone noticed Becky was missing. They watched their candle burn down until the flame flickered and died, leaving them in total darkness. They eventually fell into a deep, exhausted sleep. When they woke, they had no idea how much time had passed. Tom guessed it could be Sunday or even Monday. He tried to talk to Becky, but she was too hopeless to respond. Tom suggested they shout for help, but the terrifying echoes in the dark cave made him stop. As time dragged on, hunger returned. They shared the last of Tom’s cake, but it only made them hungrier."
}

