parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6525cda2464e15ba78e75607",
  "rewritten_text": "On Saturday at noon, I visited the well to watch. Merlin was still burning powders, waving his arms, and muttering nonsense, though he looked discouraged since he had not even dampened the well. I asked him how things were going. He replied that he was using the most powerful Eastern enchantment and demanded silence until he finished. He created a massive cloud of smoke that drifted toward the hermits, followed by more chanting and wild gestures. After twenty minutes, he collapsed, exhausted. Soon, the abbot, hundreds of monks and nuns, and a large crowd of pilgrims and orphans arrived, drawn by the smoke. When the abbot asked for the results, Merlin claimed he had used his strongest magic and failed. He declared that a powerful, nameless spirit had cursed the well, making it impossible for any mortal to restore the water. He asked to leave, claiming he had done all he could. The abbot was distressed and turned to me, asking if it was true. I told him part of it was. When he asked which part, I confirmed that the spirit had indeed cursed the well. The abbot cried out in despair, but I suggested that Merlin was not necessarily right about the spell being unbreakable. I explained that under certain conditions, there might still be a small chance of success."
}

