  "rewritten_text": "Emma felt great satisfaction from her conversation with Mr. Knightley. As she walked the lawn the next morning, she savored the memory of the ball. She was pleased they agreed on the Eltons and felt gratified by his praise of Harriet. The Eltons' rudeness, which had briefly threatened to spoil her evening, had instead led to these pleasant outcomes, and she hoped Harriet’s infatuation was finally cured. Harriet’s comments at the ball suggested she finally saw Mr. Elton for who he truly was. With Harriet becoming rational, Frank Churchill’s affections in check, and Mr. Knightley on good terms with her, Emma anticipated a wonderful summer. She did not mind that Frank Churchill would not visit that morning, as he had to return home. Having settled her thoughts, she prepared to return to her family duties when the gate opened. To her shock, Frank Churchill entered with Harriet leaning on his arm. Harriet looked pale and terrified, and Frank was attempting to comfort her. They quickly reached the hall, where Harriet promptly fainted. Emma soon learned the cause. Harriet and her friend Miss Bickerton had gone for a walk on the Richmond road. Though it seemed safe, the path became secluded and shaded by elms. A short distance past Highbury, they suddenly encountered a group of gypsies."
}

