  "rewritten_text": "I desire to see you suitably settled, and for that purpose, it is wise to limit your casual associations. Should you remain in this area when Mr. Martin weds, I hope your closeness to his sisters does not lead you to befriend his wife, who will likely be an uneducated farmer’s daughter. Certainly. I do not believe Mr. Martin would marry anyone lacking a proper upbringing, yet I will not oppose your judgment. I value the Miss Martins, particularly Elizabeth, and would regret losing them, as they are as well-schooled as I. Still, if he marries a vulgar, ignorant woman, I shall avoid her if possible. Emma observed her throughout this reply, finding no signs of affection. The young man had been the first to admire her, but she hoped there was no deeper attachment and that Harriet would not resist her own plans. They encountered Mr. Martin the following day on the Donwell road. He was walking and regarded Emma with respect, then looked at her companion with genuine delight. Emma took the chance to observe him, stepping ahead to study Mr. Robert Martin. He appeared tidy and sensible, but lacked other distinctions; she felt he would suffer by comparison to true gentlemen. Harriet appreciated refined manners, yet Mr. Martin seemed entirely unaware of them. They spoke only briefly, as Miss Woodhouse could not be delayed. Harriet rejoined her, smiling and visibly excited, a state Miss Woodhouse intended to calm. To think we met him! How strange! He said it was pure luck he had not taken the Randalls path, as he assumed we walked there daily."
}

