parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ee5d71cbf690e367a9bbb03c",
  "rewritten_text": "He believed so, at least, though who could be certain? No, nothing further had occurred. He had spared her out of vanity and donned a mask of virtue through hypocrisy. He had practiced self-denial merely to satisfy his curiosity, a truth he now acknowledged. Yet, would this murder haunt him forever? Must he always carry the weight of his history? Confess? Never. Only one piece of proof remained: the portrait. He had to destroy it. Why had he preserved it? It once delighted him to witness its decay, but lately, it only brought him sleepless nights. In his absence, he feared prying eyes would discover it. It had cast a shadow over his desires and poisoned his joy; it had become his conscience. He would end it. He spotted the blade that had slain Basil Hallward. He had polished it until it shone, free of any stain. Just as it had ended the artist, it would now destroy the artist’s creation and all it represented. It would slay the past, granting him freedom. It would extinguish this grotesque existence, and without its grim reminders, he would finally find rest. He grabbed the weapon and plunged it into the canvas. A harrowing cry of agony rang out, followed by a crash. The sound terrified the servants, who emerged from their rooms. Two gentlemen passing the square paused to gaze at the mansion. They summoned a policeman, who rang the bell repeatedly to no avail. The house remained dark, save for a single light in an upper window. Eventually, the officer retreated to a nearby portico to keep watch. Whose residence is this, Constable? the older gentleman inquired. It belongs to Mr. Dorian Gray, the officer replied. The men exchanged sneering glances as they departed; one was the uncle of Sir Henry Ashton. Within the servants' quarters, the staff whispered in hushed tones. Mrs. Leaf wept, wringing her hands, while Francis stood deathly pale. After fifteen minutes, he gathered the coachman and a footman to ascend the stairs."
}

