parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "71793e8eea8d7ddd6b4f2dcd",
  "rewritten_text": "He thought so, at least, but who could be sure? There had been nothing more. He had spared her out of vanity and acted good only to hide his hypocrisy. He realized now that his self-denial had been mere curiosity. Would this murder haunt him forever? Must he always carry this burden? Confess? Never. Only one piece of evidence remained: the portrait. He had to destroy it. He had once enjoyed watching it age, but lately, it only brought him dread. It had become a conscience he could not escape. He grabbed the knife he had used to kill Basil Hallward. It was clean and bright. Just as it had killed the painter, it would destroy the painting and his past. Once the past was dead, he would finally be free. He stabbed the canvas. A terrible cry rang out, followed by a crash. The servants woke in terror. Two men passing by noticed the house, alerted a policeman, and waited. The officer rang the bell, but the house remained dark, save for a single light upstairs. When the officer left, the men walked away, mocking the owner, Dorian Gray. Inside, the servants whispered in fear. Mrs. Leaf wept while Francis stood pale. Eventually, he gathered the coachman and a footman to head upstairs."
}

