parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1a37fa0f8402ef7a195a6560",
  "rewritten_text": "His only concern was selecting a victim, as he understood that murder, like ancient pagan rituals, required both a priest and a sacrifice. Lacking the imagination to have enemies, and recognizing that his grave mission left no room for petty grudges, he drafted a list of friends and family. After careful thought, he chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, his elderly second cousin living on Curzon Street. He was fond of her, and since he had inherited Lord Rugby’s fortune, he stood to gain nothing financially from her death. Convinced she was the perfect choice and unwilling to delay for Sybil’s sake, he began his preparations. First, he settled his debt with the palmist, writing a check for 105 pounds to Mr. Septimus Podgers and sending it via his valet. He then ordered his carriage and dressed to leave. Before departing, he glanced at Sybil Merton’s photograph, vowing to keep his self-sacrificing secret hidden forever. On his way to the club, he sent Sybil a basket of narcissus. Upon arrival, he went to the library and ordered a lemon-and-soda along with a book on toxicology. He decided poison was the most discreet method; he found physical violence distasteful and wanted to avoid the public scandal of a sensational murder investigation, fearing the unwanted attention of society columns."
}

