parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "42f339d75ccec0be5d6d6a7e",
  "rewritten_text": "I reasoned backward. The killers fled through these windows, yet the sashes were found locked from within, a fact that baffled the police. Since the windows had to lock themselves, I examined the clear casement, removed the nail, and tried to lift the sash. It held firm, confirming my theory of a hidden spring. I soon located the mechanism, pressed it, and left the window closed. I replaced the nail and studied it. A killer could close the window, but could not replace the nail from outside. Thus, they must have exited through the other window. Assuming the springs were identical, I climbed onto the bed to inspect the second casement. I found the matching spring behind the headboard, but the nail appeared identical to the first, driven deep into the frame. You might assume I was stumped, but my logic remained sound. I had followed the trail to the nail itself. Despite its appearance, the nail was the key to the mystery. I touched it, and the head, along with a small portion of the shank, snapped off in my hand."
}

