  "request_id": "2fcd08faed2cacecf59eff5e",
  "rewritten_text": "Three weeks later, St. Peter’s was packed with fashionable guests for the wedding. The Dean of Chichester performed an impressive ceremony, and everyone remarked that the bride and groom were a stunning couple. More importantly, they were truly happy. Lord Arthur never regretted his sacrifices for Sybil, and she offered him her complete devotion and love. Reality did not destroy their romance; they remained forever young at heart. Years later, with two children, they hosted Lady Windermere at Alton Priory, the Duke’s wedding gift. While sitting under a lime tree watching the children play, Lady Windermere asked Sybil if she was happy. Sybil confirmed she was and asked if Lady Windermere felt the same. Lady Windermere admitted she lacked time for happiness, noting that she grew bored with people once she knew them well. When asked if her famous guests satisfied her, she complained that they were only interesting for a short time and often behaved poorly. She mentioned the fraud Mr. Podgers, noting that while she forgave his financial requests, his romantic advances ruined palmistry for her; she now preferred telepathy. Sybil warned her not to criticize palmistry, as Arthur took it very seriously. When Lady Windermere questioned if he truly believed in it, Sybil directed her to Arthur, who was approaching with their children and a bouquet of roses. Lady Windermere asked him directly if he believed in palmistry. Arthur smiled and confirmed he did, explaining that he owed all his happiness to it."
}

