parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9b9041922d5ff432fe96fae5",
  "rewritten_text": "I am certain she would! The Widow Douglas’ generous welcome was an alluring prospect. Between that and Tom’s arguments, the matter was settled. They agreed to keep their plans for the evening secret. It soon struck Tom that Huck might arrive that very night to signal him, which dampened his excitement. Yet, he could not bring himself to abandon the Widow’s party. He reasoned that since no signal had appeared the previous night, it was unlikely to occur now. The guaranteed enjoyment of the evening surpassed the vague promise of treasure, so, with a boy’s typical resolve, he chose to follow his immediate desire and dismiss thoughts of the money for the rest of the day. Three miles downriver, the ferry docked at a wooded hollow. The group hurried ashore, filling the craggy woods with their shouts and laughter. After exhausting themselves with various games, the wanderers returned to camp with hearty appetites to consume their provisions. Following the meal, they rested and conversed under the shade of large oaks. Eventually, someone called out to ask who was prepared to explore the cave. Everyone agreed. They gathered bundles of candles and raced up the hill toward the A-shaped entrance. The heavy oak door stood open. Inside, the chamber was as cold as an icehouse, its limestone walls damp with moisture. Standing in the shadows, looking out at the sunlit valley, felt romantic and mysterious. However, the novelty faded quickly, and the play resumed. Whenever a candle was lit, a scuffle ensued until it was extinguished, sparking more laughter and chasing. Eventually, the group moved down the main passage, their dim lights illuminating the towering rock walls sixty feet above. This main path was narrow, and every few paces, even tighter crevices branched off, as McDougal’s cave was a sprawling maze of winding, interconnected tunnels that led to nowhere."
}

