{
  "request_id": "eb70bb35fe0022ed45757fda",
  "rewritten_text": "I was disappointed to hear Jim say that; it lowered my opinion of him. My conscience bothered me more than ever, until I finally told myself, \"Stop it—it isn't too late—I will paddle to shore at dawn and report him.\" I felt light and happy immediately, as if my troubles had vanished. I began watching for a light, humming to myself. Soon, one appeared. Jim shouted, \"We are safe, Huck! We are safe! Jump up and celebrate! That is Cairo at last, I know it!\" I replied, \"I will take the canoe and check, Jim. It might not be the right place.\" He quickly prepared the canoe, placing his coat inside for me to sit on, and handed me the paddle. As I pushed off, he said, \"Soon I will be shouting for joy, and I will say it is all because of Huck. I am a free man, and I never could have been free if it weren't for you. Jim will never forget you, Huck; you are the best friend I have ever had, and the only friend I have left.\" I was paddling away, eager to turn him in, but his words drained all my resolve. I slowed down, unsure if I was glad I had left. When I was fifty yards away, Jim called out, \"There you go, the true Huck; the only white gentleman who ever kept his promise to old Jim.\" I felt sick. Still, I told myself I had to do it. Just then, a skiff with two armed men approached. They stopped, and I did too. One asked, \"What is that over there?\" \"A piece of a raft,\" I said. \"Are you on it?\" \"Yes, sir.\" \"Is there anyone else?\" \"Only one man, sir.\" \"Well, five enslaved men ran away tonight up the river. Is your man white or black?\" I hesitated. I tried to speak, but the words would not come. I tried to find the courage, but I lacked the nerve. Realizing I was failing, I finally said, \"He is white.\" \"We will go see for ourselves,\" the man said. \"I wish you would,\" I replied, \"because it is my father on the raft, and maybe you could help me tow it to the shore where the light is.\""
}

