parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "42e1e4cbc3b78a05592737d7",
  "rewritten_text": "The congratulatory letter Elizabeth received from Lydia regarding her marriage revealed that Lydia, if not her husband, held high expectations. The letter read: My dear Lizzy, I wish you joy. If you love Mr. Darcy half as much as I love my dear Wickham, you must be very happy. It is a great comfort that you are so wealthy; when you have time, please think of us. I am sure Wickham would love a government position, as we will not have enough money to live on without help. Any role paying three or four hundred a year would suffice, though do not ask Mr. Darcy if you would rather not. Yours, etc. Since Elizabeth preferred not to ask, she firmly discouraged such requests in her reply. However, she frequently sent them financial aid, saved from her own personal allowance. She knew their income was insufficient for two such extravagant, shortsighted people, and whenever they relocated, Jane or Elizabeth were inevitably asked to help settle their debts. Their lifestyle remained chaotic even after they settled down; they constantly moved in search of cheaper housing while overspending. Wickham’s affection for Lydia soon faded into indifference, though hers lasted longer. Despite her youth and behavior, she maintained the social standing her marriage provided. While Darcy would never welcome Wickham to Pemberley, he helped advance his career for Elizabeth’s sake. Lydia occasionally visited when her husband traveled to London or Bath, and they stayed with the Bingleys so often that even Bingley’s patience wore thin, leading him to consider asking them to leave. Miss Bingley was deeply bitter about Darcy’s marriage, but to maintain her access to Pemberley, she suppressed her resentment. She became more attached to Georgiana, remained attentive to Darcy, and was unfailingly polite to Elizabeth."
}

