parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4e2aeba8fbba8ba2dbe3bf41",
  "rewritten_text": "Following a shared mourning over the grim outcome of this affair—which Elizabeth deemed nearly inevitable and Miss Bennet could not claim was entirely impossible—the former pressed for more details. \"Tell me everything I have not yet heard. What did Colonel Forster say? Did they have no suspicions before the elopement? They must have been seen together constantly.\" \"Colonel Forster admitted he had often noticed a mutual fondness, particularly from Lydia, but nothing that caused him alarm. I feel so sorry for him; his conduct was remarkably kind. He was already traveling to see us to express his regret before he realized they were not headed for Scotland; the rumor of their flight only accelerated his arrival.\" \"Was Denny certain Wickham would not marry? Did he know of their plans? Did the Colonel speak to him directly?\" \"Yes, but when questioned, Denny claimed ignorance and refused to share his true thoughts. He did not reiterate his belief that they would not wed, which gives me some hope he was previously misunderstood.\" \"And until the Colonel arrived, none of you doubted they were actually married?\" \"How could such a thought occur to us? I felt some unease regarding my sister’s future happiness, knowing his past behavior was questionable. My parents were unaware of that; they simply viewed it as an imprudent match. Kitty then revealed, with a touch of pride, that Lydia’s final letter had hinted at this, as she had known of their affection for weeks.\" \"But not before they left for Brighton?\" \"No, I believe not.\" \"Did Colonel Forster seem to think poorly of Wickham? Is he aware of his true character?\" \"I must admit he spoke less favorably of him than before. He considered him reckless and spendthrift; since this tragedy, rumors suggest he departed Meryton with significant debts, though I hope that is untrue.\" \"Oh, Jane, had we been less discreet and shared what we knew, this might have been avoided!\" \"Perhaps it would have been better,\" her sister answered. \"But exposing someone's past faults without knowing their current state of mind felt wrong.\""
}

