  "request_id": "d0531f2c1430631a795f9cb7",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Bennet stayed quiet during dinner, but once the servants left, he decided to engage his guest by mentioning how lucky he was to have such a patroness. Mr. Collins responded with great enthusiasm, praising Lady Catherine de Bourgh’s kindness and status. He spoke with solemn importance, claiming he had never seen such grace from someone of her rank. He noted that she had praised his sermons, invited him to dine at Rosings twice, and even included him in her card games. While some considered her proud, he insisted she was always friendly, treating him like any other gentleman. She encouraged him to marry wisely, allowed him to visit his relatives, and even visited his home to suggest improvements, such as adding shelves to his closets. Mrs. Bennet agreed that she sounded like a lovely woman and asked if she lived nearby. Mr. Collins explained that his home was just a lane away from her estate, Rosings Park. When Mrs. Bennet asked about her family, he mentioned she had one daughter who would inherit her vast fortune. Mrs. Bennet sighed, wondering if the girl was beautiful. Mr. Collins replied that she was charming, noting that Lady Catherine considered her superior to all other women because her features clearly displayed her noble birth."
}

