parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "321614eb32d3d02dcfd08027",
  "rewritten_text": "Daylight arrived as I finished the manuscript. The rain had nearly stopped, leaving the world gray and somber as the storm faded away. I went to the stranger's room and listened at his door, which stood slightly open. Hearing him speak, I knocked, but received no reply. I peeked inside to see him lying on his back, talking feverishly and waving his arms in the restless manner of the delirious. I entered quietly and leaned over him. When I spoke his name, his glassy eyes and pale face lit up with sudden joy and relief. Oh, Sandy, you have finally come. I have missed you so much. Sit by me and never leave again. Give me your hand; let me hold it. Now everything is peaceful and I am happy once more. You look like a faint mist, but you are here, and that is enough. Don't pull away; I won't need you for long. Was that the child? Hello, Central? She doesn't answer. Perhaps she is asleep. Bring her when she wakes so I can say goodbye. Sandy, are you still there? I drifted off for a moment. Have I been sick for a long time? It feels like months. I have had such strange, terrifying dreams that felt completely real. I dreamt the king was dead, that you were trapped in Gaul, and that a revolution had broken out. In my delirium, I imagined that Clarence and I fought and destroyed all the knights of England. Even stranger, I felt like a man from a distant, future age. I seemed to have traveled back and forth through time, feeling like a lonely stranger separated from you and my home by thirteen centuries of history."
}

