parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1effd715d1cf7079f21301ff",
  "rewritten_text": "“Joseph! Joseph!” He shook his head and tapped it, signaling his disappointment in Joseph. “My dear young friend,” Mr. Pumblechook said, “you must be hungry and exhausted. Sit down. I have brought chicken, tongue, and other treats from the Boar that I hope you will enjoy. But am I truly looking at the boy I used to play with during his happy childhood? May I—may I?” This meant he wanted to shake my hand. I agreed, and he shook it enthusiastically before sitting down again. “Here is wine,” he said. “Let us drink to Fortune, and may she always choose her favorites wisely! Yet I cannot look at you and drink without again asking—may I—may I?” I gave him permission, and he shook my hand again, then drained his glass. I did the same, and the wine went straight to my head. Mr. Pumblechook served me the best parts of the chicken and tongue, barely eating anything himself. “Ah, poultry! You never thought when you were a chick that you would provide a meal under this humble roof for someone who—call it a weakness if you like—but may I? May I?” He kept asking to shake my hand so often that I stopped answering, and he simply did it anyway. I am surprised he did not cut himself with my knife. “And your sister,” he continued after eating, “who had the honor of raising you! It is sad to think she can no longer appreciate that honor. May—” I saw he was about to reach for me again, so I interrupted. “Let us drink to her health,” I said. “Ah!” Mr. Pumblechook cried, leaning back in his chair, overwhelmed with admiration. “That is how you recognize them, sir! That is how you know the noble-minded, sir!”"
}

