parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f0b445c6cd19fdeefc8e4802",
  "rewritten_text": "With their business settled, the agreement was sealed with another cup of peppermint, which the lady required to calm her nerves. As she drank, she informed Mr. Bumble of the old woman’s passing. That gentleman, sipping his drink, promised to notify Sowerberry to collect the body the next day, asking if that news had caused her distress. She evaded the question, promising to explain after their wedding. When Mr. Bumble suggested a male pauper might have insulted her, she quickly denied it. He grew indignant, threatening to punish any man who dared look at her, a display of devotion that moved the lady to call him a dove. The dove then donned his hat and coat, embraced his bride-to-be, and ventured into the night. He stopped briefly in the paupers’ ward to berate the residents, confirming his own capacity for the necessary harshness of a workhouse master. Satisfied, he headed toward the undertaker’s shop, his mind filled with dreams of advancement. Finding the shop open and the Sowerberrys away, he tapped his cane on the counter. Receiving no response, he peered into the back parlor and was quite astonished by what he witnessed."
}

