  "rewritten_text": "We finally lightened the box by removing two-thirds of its contents, which allowed us to lift it from the hole with some effort. We hid the items in the bushes and left the dog to guard them, with Jupiter strictly ordering him not to move or bark until we returned. We hurried home with the chest, reaching the hut safely but exhausted at one in the morning. Too tired to continue, we rested until two, ate, and then headed back to the hills with three sturdy sacks we found at the hut. Shortly before four, we reached the pit, divided the remaining treasure, and returned to the hut with our heavy loads just as dawn broke over the trees. We were exhausted, but our excitement kept us from sleeping. After a restless nap of a few hours, we woke up to examine our treasure. The chest had been packed to the top, and we spent the entire day and most of the next night sorting through it. Everything had been thrown in without order. Once organized, we realized we were even wealthier than we had imagined. We estimated the gold coins to be worth over four hundred and fifty thousand dollars. There was no silver, only antique gold from France, Spain, Germany, and England, along with some unfamiliar coins. Some large, heavy pieces were too worn to identify, and there was no American currency. The jewels were harder to value. We found one hundred and ten large, high-quality diamonds, eighteen brilliant rubies, three hundred and ten beautiful emeralds, twenty-one sapphires, and an opal. These stones had been pried from their settings, which we found hammered flat, likely to hide their origin."
}

