parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eaf30229b2665d3098433f40",
  "rewritten_text": "Ten minutes later, his face pale with terror and eyes wild with grief, Lord Arthur Savile rushed from Bentinck House, pushing past the crowd of footmen under the striped awning, oblivious to his surroundings. The night was freezing, and the streetlights flickered in the sharp wind, yet his hands were feverish and his forehead burned. He walked with the unsteady gait of a drunkard. A policeman watched him with curiosity, and a beggar emerging from an archway retreated, intimidated by a misery deeper than his own. Stopping under a lamp, he examined his hands, imagining he saw bloodstains, and a faint cry escaped his lips. Murder. That was what the palmist had seen. The night seemed to know it, and the wind howled the word. It lurked in dark corners and grinned from the rooftops. He reached the Park, drawn to its somber woods. Leaning against the wet metal railings to cool his brow, he whispered the word repeatedly, as if repetition could dull its horror. The sound of his own voice made him shudder, yet he longed for an echo to wake the sleeping city. He felt a desperate urge to stop a stranger and confess everything. He wandered across Oxford Street into narrow, squalid alleys. Two women with heavy makeup mocked him as he passed. From a dark courtyard came the sound of swearing and violence, followed by shrill screams, and he saw the hunched figures of the elderly poor huddled on a damp doorstep. A strange pity stirred within him. Were these people destined for their fate, just as he was for his? Were they, like him, merely puppets in a grotesque performance? It was the absurdity of suffering that struck him—its utter pointlessness and lack of meaning. Everything felt incoherent and discordant. He was stunned by the gap between the day's shallow optimism and the harsh reality of existence. He was still very young. Eventually, he found himself before Marylebone Church. The silent road stretched out like a ribbon of polished silver, patterned by the dark, swaying shadows."
}

