  "rewritten_text": "When we reached the tree, he said, Jupiter, come here. Was the skull nailed to the branch facing out, or facing the wood? The face was out, master, so the crows could get at the eyes easily, Jupiter replied. Well, then, was it this eye or that one through which you dropped the beetle? Legrand touched each of Jupiter’s eyes. It was this eye, master—the left—just as you told me, Jupiter said, pointing to his own right eye. That will do, we must try again. I began to see a strange logic in my friend’s behavior. He moved the peg marking where the beetle fell three inches to the west. He measured fifty feet from the trunk in a straight line, marking a new spot several yards from where we had previously dug. We drew a larger circle around this new spot and began digging again. I was exhausted, yet I no longer minded the work. I felt strangely interested and even excited. Perhaps Legrand’s deliberate, thoughtful manner had affected me. I dug eagerly, finding myself actually expecting to find the treasure that had driven my friend mad. After about an hour and a half, the dog began howling violently again. This time, his distress was serious. When Jupiter tried to quiet him, the dog resisted, jumped into the hole, and frantically clawed at the dirt. Within seconds, he uncovered human bones, two complete skeletons mixed with metal buttons and rotted fabric. A few more spade strokes revealed a large Spanish knife, followed by several loose gold and silver coins."
}

