  "request_id": "9739f609bc0f9e47c6e0d47f",
  "rewritten_text": "I wish we had a donkey. Jane, Miss Bates, and I could ride them, with my dear husband walking alongside. I really must talk to him about buying one. In the country, I consider it a necessity. No matter how many hobbies a woman has, she cannot stay home forever, and long walks are difficult when it is dusty in summer or muddy in winter. You will find neither between Donwell and Highbury, as the lane is never dusty and is currently dry. Still, come by donkey if you prefer; you can borrow Mrs. Cole’s. I want everything to be exactly to your liking. I am sure you do. I truly appreciate you, my friend. Despite your blunt manner, I know you have a warm heart. I tell Mr. Elton that you are quite a character. Believe me, Knightley, I am grateful for your thoughtfulness regarding this plan. You have suggested exactly what I wanted. Mr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse to join the party, knowing that sitting outdoors to eat would make the older man ill. He had to ensure Mr. Woodhouse was not tempted into discomfort under the guise of a pleasant morning drive. The invitation was made in good faith, and Mr. Woodhouse agreed. He had not visited Donwell in two years. He decided that he, Emma, and Harriet could go on a fine morning. He could sit with Mrs. Weston while the girls walked in the gardens, provided it was not damp. He wanted to see the old house again and meet the Eltons and his other neighbors. He saw no objection to the trip and thought it was kind and sensible of Mr. Knightley to invite them, as it was much better than dining out, which he disliked. Mr. Knightley was fortunate that everyone agreed so readily. The invitation was so well received that everyone felt it was a personal compliment. Emma and Harriet were excited, and Mr. Weston promised to bring Frank along if possible. Although Mr. Knightley would have preferred otherwise, he politely agreed that he would be glad to see him, and Mr. Weston promised to write immediately to convince him to come."
}

