parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3ea6e7f5810ed8941ec5cbf5",
  "rewritten_text": "She felt a sudden urge to check the weather at the door, dismissing any suspicion of hidden intentions. Captain Wentworth had surely departed. She rose, determined to stop her mind from constantly second-guessing itself. Before she could reach the door, however, Captain Wentworth arrived, accompanied by a group of his friends. He appeared visibly startled and flustered upon seeing her, his face flushing. For the first time since their reunion, she felt more composed than he, having had a moment to brace herself against the initial shock. Yet, she remained deeply moved, experiencing a complex blend of pain and pleasure. He addressed her briefly before turning away, his demeanor clearly awkward rather than cold or friendly. Soon, he returned to exchange polite, trivial remarks, though neither truly absorbed the conversation. Anne noted his unusual lack of composure; he seemed unable to maintain the practiced indifference they had recently adopted. Whether due to time or Louisa, he was clearly unsettled. Though he appeared healthy and spoke of the Musgroves and Louisa with a fleeting touch of his old playfulness, he remained visibly ill at ease. Anne was saddened, though not surprised, to see Elizabeth pointedly ignore him. She witnessed their mutual recognition and knew he expected a greeting, making it all the more painful to watch her sister turn away with icy indifference. Finally, the servant arrived to announce that Lady Dalrymple’s carriage had arrived, much to Elizabeth’s relief."
}

