parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "54f3696755105a396ebdd0b4",
  "rewritten_text": "He did not try again. The boys lay there, counting the agonizing seconds until it felt as if time itself had stalled and eternity was fading; they were relieved when the sun finally began to set. One snore stopped. Injun Joe sat up, looked around, and smiled grimly at his companion, whose head rested on his knees. He nudged him with his foot and said, \"Hey! You're supposed to be keeping watch, aren't you? It's fine, though—nothing happened.\" \"Wow, did I fall asleep?\" \"Partly, I suppose. It's almost time to move, partner. What should we do with the rest of our loot?\" \"I don't know—leave it here like we always have, I guess. No point in carrying it until we head south. Six hundred and fifty in silver is a lot to haul around.\" \"Fair enough. It won't hurt to come back one more time.\" \"True, but I'd suggest coming at night as usual; it's safer.\" \"Maybe, but listen; it might be a while before I get the right opening for that job. Accidents happen, and this isn't the best spot. We should just bury it properly—deep.\" \"Good idea,\" his partner agreed. He walked over, knelt, lifted a hearthstone, and pulled out a jingling bag. He took twenty or thirty dollars for himself and the same for Injun Joe, then handed the bag to Joe, who was already on his knees in the corner, digging with his knife. The boys instantly forgot their fear and misery. They watched every move with wide, greedy eyes. Luck! The sheer brilliance of it was unimaginable. Six hundred dollars was enough to make half a dozen boys rich. This was treasure hunting under perfect conditions—no guessing where to dig. They nudged each other constantly, a silent communication that meant, \"Aren't you glad we're here now?\" Joe’s knife hit something. \"Hello!\" he said. \"What is it?\" his partner asked. \"A rotting plank—no, I think it's a box. Here, give me a hand and we'll see what's inside. Never mind, I've broken through.\" He reached in and pulled out a handful of gold coins. The boys above were just as thrilled and delighted as the men. Joe’s partner said, \"Let's make quick work of this. There's a rusty pick in the weeds by the corner near the fireplace—I saw it a moment ago.\""
}

