  "request_id": "3891b4d2e135b6524e8af806",
  "rewritten_text": "His only real dilemma was choosing a victim, as he understood that murder, much like ancient religious rituals, required both a killer and a sacrifice. Lacking any enemies and viewing his task as a solemn duty rather than a personal vendetta, he listed his friends and relatives. After careful thought, he selected Lady Clementina Beauchamp, his elderly second cousin living in Curzon Street. He was fond of her, and since he had inherited Lord Rugby’s vast fortune, he had no financial motive for her death. Convinced she was the perfect choice and eager to avoid delaying his plans for Sybil, he decided to act immediately. First, he wrote a check for 105 pounds to the palm reader, Mr. Septimus Podgers, and sent it to his address. After calling for his carriage, he paused to look at a photograph of Sybil Merton, vowing to keep his self-sacrificing secret hidden forever. On his way to his club, he stopped to send Sybil a basket of narcissus flowers. Once at the club, he ordered a drink and requested a book on toxicology. He had decided that poison was the most discreet method. He found physical violence repulsive and wanted to avoid any public scandal that might turn him into a celebrity or subject him to unwanted gossip in the society papers."
}

