parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3246dcba3eb2660aec5f15a9",
  "rewritten_text": "It is strange, but everyone who goes missing is supposedly seen in San Francisco. It must be a wonderful city, possessing all the charms of the afterlife. What do you think happened to Basil? Dorian asked, holding his wine to the light and wondering why he could discuss it so calmly. I have no idea. If Basil wants to hide, it is not my concern. If he is dead, I prefer not to think about it. Death is the only thing that terrifies me. I hate it. Why? the younger man asked wearily. Because, Lord Henry said, sniffing his perfume box, one can survive anything today except that. Death and vulgarity are the only two facts of the nineteenth century that cannot be explained away. Let us have coffee in the music room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran off with played it beautifully. Poor Victoria! I was fond of her. The house is lonely without her. Marriage is just a habit, a bad one. But one regrets losing even the worst habits. Perhaps one regrets them most, as they are essential to one's personality. Dorian said nothing, rose, and went to the piano, letting his fingers drift over the keys. After coffee arrived, he stopped and looked at Lord Henry. Harry, did it ever occur to you that Basil was murdered? Lord Henry yawned. Basil was popular and wore a cheap watch. Why would he be murdered? He was not clever enough to have enemies. He had a genius for painting, but a man can paint like a master and still be dull. Basil was dull. He only interested me once, when he told me years ago that he adored you and that you were the inspiration for his art. I was fond of Basil, Dorian said sadly. But do people not say he was murdered? Some newspapers do, but it seems unlikely. I know there are dangerous places in Paris, but Basil was not the type to go there. He lacked curiosity, which was his main flaw. What would you say, Harry, if I told you that I murdered Basil? the younger man asked."
}

