parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f5b526d0b4a2741c99e9542d",
  "rewritten_text": "It is only four o’clock, but winter has already brought an early sunset. The sky is clear and frosty, casting a soft pink glow over the snow-covered ground. I live in a lonely cottage on a vast, empty heath where no human voice ever reaches me. The desolate plain is white, except for a few dark patches on the hilltops where the snow is thinner. A few birds peck at the ice covering the pools, hardened by a long, bitter frost. I am in a strange state of mind. I am completely alone in the world, withered by misfortune. I know I am dying, and I feel strangely happy. My pulse races, and my thin hand feels hot against my cheek. My inner spirit is burning out its final sparks. I will not live to see another winter or feel the warmth of another summer. Believing this, I begin to write my tragic story. Perhaps it should die with me, but I am too weak to resist the urge to tell it. While I was strong, I felt my story was too dark to share, but now that I am dying, I feel compelled to reveal its terrors. I must gather my thoughts. I do not know who will read these pages besides you, my friend, who will receive them after my death. I am writing as if for strangers, though I address you. You have often asked about my solitary life, my tears, and my silence. In life, I could not speak, but in death, I reveal the mystery. Others may discard these pages, but to you, Woodville, my kind friend, they will be precious. They are the memories of a heartbroken girl who, even in death, is warmed by gratitude for your sympathy."
}

