  "rewritten_text": "Yes, it is, and I could have had it if I were big enough; I saw him first. Who caught him? It was an old stranger who sold his chance for forty dollars because he had to travel up the river and could not wait. Imagine that! I would have waited seven years. I agreed, but wondered if the chance was worth so little because something was wrong. No, it is perfectly legitimate. I saw the flyer myself. It describes him exactly, including the plantation he is from near New Orleans. There is no doubt about that deal. Give me some chewing tobacco, will you? I had none, so he left. I went to the raft and sat in the shelter to think, but I could not find a solution. I worried until my head ached, but saw no way out. After our long journey and all we did for those scoundrels, everything was ruined because they could betray Jim for forty dirty dollars and make him a slave for life among strangers. I briefly thought it would be better for Jim to be a slave back home with his family, so I considered writing to Tom Sawyer to tell Miss Watson where he was. I quickly dismissed this for two reasons: she would be furious at his escape and sell him further down the river, and everyone would despise him for being ungrateful, making his life miserable. Then I thought of myself. It would get out that Huck Finn helped a slave escape, and I would be so ashamed that I would want to hide from anyone I knew. That is how it goes; a person does something wrong and then tries to avoid the consequences, thinking that as long as it stays hidden, it is not shameful. That was my situation. The more I thought about it, the more my conscience bothered me, and the more wicked and low-down I felt."
}

