parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fda76606578f551f1ee2eab1",
  "rewritten_text": "We finished our inspection by nightfall and left. On the way home, my companion stopped briefly at a newspaper office. I have mentioned that my friend had many quirks, and I always humored them. He chose not to speak of the murder until the following afternoon, when he suddenly asked if I had noticed anything peculiar at the crime scene. His emphasis on the word peculiar made me shudder for no apparent reason. I replied that I had seen nothing beyond what the papers reported. He dismissed the press, arguing that the police found the case impossible only because its bizarre nature should have made it easy to solve. They were baffled by the lack of motive for such brutality, the conflicting accounts of voices heard, and the fact that no one else was found in the room. The chaotic state of the apartment, the body shoved up the chimney, and the horrific mutilations had paralyzed the authorities. They made the common mistake of confusing the unusual with the complex. Yet, it is through these very deviations from the ordinary that reason finds the truth. In such cases, one must ask not what happened, but what has happened that is unprecedented. The ease with which I will solve this mystery is directly proportional to how impossible it seems to the police. I stared at him in silence. He looked toward our door and added that he was waiting for someone who, while perhaps not the killer, was certainly involved in the crimes."
}

