  "rewritten_text": "Stop the starboard! Ting-a-ling-ling! Stop the port! Come ahead on the starboard! Stop her! Let the outside turn over slowly! Ting-a-ling-ling! Chow-ow-ow! Get that headline out! Lively now! Come on, get the spring-line out—what are you doing? Wrap the loop around that stump! Stand by the stage—let her go! Engines finished, sir! Ting-a-ling-ling! Ssh! Ssh! Ssh! Tom kept whitewashing, ignoring the steamboat. Ben stared for a moment and said, Hey! You are in a tough spot, aren't you? Tom did not answer. He examined his last stroke with an artist's eye, brushed again, and inspected the result. Ben walked up beside him. Tom wanted the apple, but he kept working. Ben said, Hello, friend, you have to work, huh? Tom turned suddenly and said, Oh, it is you, Ben! I did not notice you. Say, I am going swimming. Do you not wish you could? But of course you would rather work, right? Naturally! Tom looked at the boy and asked, What do you call work? Why, is that not work? Tom resumed painting and replied casually, Well, maybe it is, and maybe it is not. All I know is that it suits Tom Sawyer. Oh come on, you do not mean to say you like it? The brush kept moving. Like it? Well, I do not see why I should not. Does a boy get a chance to whitewash a fence every day? That changed things. Ben stopped eating his apple. Tom swept his brush back and forth, stepped back to check the effect, and added a few touches. Ben watched every move, becoming increasingly absorbed. Finally, he said, Say, Tom, let me whitewash a little. Tom considered it, then changed his mind. No, I do not think that would work, Ben. You see, Aunt Polly is very particular about this fence, since it faces the street. If it were the back fence, I would not mind, and neither would she. She is very strict about this one; it has to be done carefully. I doubt one boy in a thousand, maybe two thousand, can do it the right way. Is that so? Oh, come on, let me try. Just a little. I would let you if you were me, Tom. Ben, I would like to, honestly, but Aunt Polly—well, Jim wanted to do it, but she would not let him; Sid wanted to do it, and she would not let him either."
}

