parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b331355c24d83440c1c54a92",
  "rewritten_text": "We declared our freedom so boisterously that the liberty-obsessed orator and his followers took notice, surrounding us with a menacing air. The speaker declared that if we were truly free, Britain’s divine protections would shield us, and demanded we present evidence. When I hesitated, the king erupted, calling the man mad and insisting the burden of proof lay with the accuser. The king understood the law only in theory, not in practice, as people often do until it affects them personally. The crowd grew disinterested as the orator shifted to a practical tone, explaining that since we were strangers, the law required us to prove we were not slaves. I pleaded for time to send for verification, but he dismissed the request as an unreasonable inconvenience to our master. When the king began to shout that he had no master, I silenced him, fearing we would be branded lunatics. Ultimately, the earl auctioned us off. This same cruel law had existed in the American South centuries later, yet it only felt truly demonic once I was the one on the block. We were sold like livestock; because the market was stagnant, we fetched a price so low it still shames me to recall."
}

