  "rewritten_text": "In the middle of the gap, I noticed a white object, though I could not initially identify it. After adjusting the telescope, I realized it was a human skull. I felt confident the mystery was solved. The instructions regarding the seventh limb and the east side clearly referred to the skull's position, while the mention of the left eye suggested a specific method for locating the treasure. I understood that dropping a bullet through that eye socket would create a line of sight; extending that path fifty feet from the tree trunk would mark the exact spot where the treasure might be buried. I remarked that the logic was clever yet straightforward, and asked what happened after he left the Bishop's Hotel. He explained that once he stepped away from the ledge, the circular gap disappeared and could not be seen from any other vantage point. He had been accompanied by Jupiter, who had been watching him closely, but he managed to slip away the next morning to find the tree. He eventually located it, though his valet threatened to punish him for his disappearance. I suggested that their initial failure to find the treasure was due to Jupiter dropping the beetle through the wrong eye. He agreed, noting that the small error in the starting position caused a significant deviation over the fifty-foot distance, leading them completely off course."
}

