  "rewritten_text": "However, I must caution you that I intend to inform Sybil of everything. She joins me for lunch tomorrow to discuss millinery, and should Mr. Podgers discover you possess a volatile temper, a predisposition to gout, or a spouse residing in Bayswater, I shall surely disclose it all to her. Lord Arthur offered a smile and a shake of his head. I have no fear, he replied. Sybil understands me as thoroughly as I understand her. Oh, I regret hearing that. A successful marriage relies on mutual misunderstanding. No, I am not cynical, merely experienced, which amounts to much the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is eager for a palm reading. Do not mention his engagement to one of London's loveliest women, as that was reported in the Morning Post a month back. Dear Lady Windermere, the Marchioness of Jedburgh exclaimed, please let Mr. Podgers remain. He just suggested I pursue acting, and I am quite intrigued. If he told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I shall certainly whisk him away. Come here at once, Mr. Podgers, and examine Lord Arthur’s hand. Well, Lady Jedburgh said, pouting as she stood, if I am forbidden from the stage, I must at least be permitted to join the audience. Certainly, we shall all be spectators, Lady Windermere agreed; now, Mr. Podgers, do tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is a particular favorite of mine. Yet, upon viewing Lord Arthur’s palm, Mr. Podgers turned noticeably pale and remained silent. A tremor appeared to seize him, and his thick, bushy brows twitched in that peculiar, vexing manner they adopted when he was perplexed. Large beads of sweat emerged on his sallow forehead like a toxic dew, and his plump fingers turned cold and damp. Lord Arthur observed these odd displays of distress, and for the first time, he felt a sense of dread. His instinct was to flee the room, but he held his ground. It was preferable to face the worst than to endure this agonizing uncertainty. I am waiting, Mr. Podgers, he stated. We are all waiting, Lady Windermere added in her brisk, impatient style, but the palmist offered no response. I suspect Arthur intends to take up acting, Lady Jedburgh remarked, and after your reprimand, Mr. Podgers is hesitant to confirm it."
}

