parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b3aca537cc196249e2b81291",
  "rewritten_text": "At the top of the stairs, he composed himself, ready to unleash his signature demonic laugh. He had found it effective before; it had turned Lord Raker’s hair white overnight and caused three of Lady Canterville’s governesses to quit within a month. He laughed his most terrifying laugh, echoing through the vaulted ceiling, but as the sound faded, Mrs. Otis opened a door in a light blue robe. She offered him a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture, suggesting it would cure his indigestion. Furious, the ghost prepared to transform into a large black dog—a feat that had once left Lord Canterville’s uncle permanently mentally disabled. Hearing footsteps, he hesitated and instead chose to glow faintly, vanishing with a mournful groan just as the twins arrived. Back in his room, he collapsed in distress. The twins’ rudeness and Mrs. Otis’s practicality were irritating, but he was most upset that he could not wear his suit of armor. He had hoped the Americans would respect a Spectre in Armor, perhaps even out of appreciation for Longfellow’s poetry, which he had enjoyed during his time with the Cantervilles. It was his own suit, worn successfully at the Kenilworth tournament and praised by the Queen herself. Yet, the weight of the breastplate and helmet had been too much; he had fallen on the stone floor, bruising his knees and knuckles. He remained ill for several days, rarely leaving his room except to maintain the bloodstain. Once recovered, he prepared a third attempt to terrify the American minister and his family."
}

