  "rewritten_text": "The gauge showed my altitude at 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, meaning I could see one three-hundred-twentieth of the Earth's total surface. By nine o'clock, the eastern land had vanished, and I noticed the balloon moving swiftly toward the north-northwest. The Earth's curvature was clearly visible, despite frequent interference from drifting clouds. I noted that even the thinnest mists never ascended beyond ten miles above sea level. At nine-thirty, I tested dropping a handful of feathers through the valve. Instead of floating, they plummeted straight down like lead weights, vanishing instantly. I was initially baffled, unable to grasp such a sudden increase in my ascent rate. I soon realized the air was too thin to support the feathers, and their rapid fall was compounded by my own swift upward movement. By ten o'clock, I had little to do. Everything proceeded smoothly, and I felt the balloon accelerating, though I lacked the means to measure it. I felt no discomfort and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam, alternating between checking my equipment and refreshing the cabin's air. I performed this ventilation every forty minutes, primarily for health. Meanwhile, my mind wandered to the moon. Unrestrained, my imagination explored a dreamlike landscape of ancient forests, jagged cliffs, and roaring, bottomless waterfalls. Then, I drifted into silent, windless noonday plains where vast fields of poppies and lilies stretched out, forever still."
