  "rewritten_text": "Men wed out of weariness, women out of curiosity; both end in disappointment. I doubt I shall marry, Harry, for I am too deeply in love. I am simply applying one of your own maxims. Who is the object of your affection? Lord Henry inquired. Dorian Gray flushed as he replied, An actress. Lord Henry shrugged, calling it a trite beginning. You would feel differently if you saw her, Dorian insisted. Who is she? Sibyl Vane. I have never heard of her. No one has yet, but they will; she is a genius. My dear boy, women are merely decorative; they possess no genius, only a charming way of saying nothing. They are the victory of matter over mind, just as men are the victory of mind over morals. Harry, how can you say that? It is the truth, Dorian. I am currently studying women and find them simple: there are only the plain, who are useful for appearances, and the painted, who try to look young rather than speak brilliantly. Nowadays, a woman is content if she looks younger than her daughter. Tell me, how long have you known this genius? Your perspectives frighten me, Harry. Never mind that; how long? About three weeks. Where did you meet her? I will tell you, but do not be unkind. It is your fault, really; you sparked a frantic desire in me to understand life. After we met, I felt a pulse in my veins. While walking through the park or Piccadilly, I watched passersby with a wild curiosity, wondering about their lives. Some fascinated me, others terrified me. The air felt poisoned, and I developed a craving for sensation."
