  "rewritten_text": "For years, Dorian Gray remained under the spell of that book, or perhaps he simply chose not to break free. He ordered nine copies from Paris, binding them in various colors to match his shifting moods and his increasingly erratic nature. The protagonist, a young man who blended romanticism with science, felt like a mirror of his own life, as if his story had been written before he lived it. In one way, Dorian was luckier than the book’s hero. He never suffered from the strange fear of mirrors or reflective surfaces that plagued the character after his beauty faded. Dorian read the tragic end of the book with a cruel sense of satisfaction, finding pleasure in the despair of someone who had lost the beauty he valued most. Yet, the beauty that had captivated Basil Hallward never left Dorian. Even when scandalous rumors about his lifestyle circulated through London clubs, people could not believe the gossip once they saw him. He always appeared untouched by the world. His face was so pure that it silenced vulgar men, reminding them of the innocence they had lost. They wondered how someone so charming could remain untainted by such a sordid, sensual age. Often, after returning from his mysterious and long absences, Dorian would sneak upstairs to his locked room. He would unlock the door, stand before the portrait Basil had painted, and hold up a mirror. He would compare the aging, wicked face on the canvas to the youthful, laughing reflection staring back at him."
}

