            "text": "{\n  \"request_id\": \"f56cdc3ca45bacedd852cd50\",\n  \"rewritten_text\": \"He also considered Sybil’s traditional parents, who might oppose the union if scandal arose, though he believed they would understand his motives if fully informed. Poison seemed the ideal choice; it was secure, certain, and discreet, sparing him the unpleasant confrontations that most Englishmen naturally detest. Lacking any knowledge of toxicology, he searched the library himself after the waiter failed to assist. He discovered a fine edition of the Pharmacopoeia and Erskine’s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid—a man whose membership in the Buckingham club resulted from a clerical error that led the committee to reject the actual physician out of spite. Confused by the technical jargon and wishing he had studied harder at Oxford, Lord Arthur eventually found a clear description of aconitine in Erskine’s second volume. It was the perfect substance: rapid, painless, and easily ingested via a gelatine capsule. He noted the lethal dosage on his cuff, returned the books, and walked to Pestle and Humbey’s pharmacy. Mr. Pestle, accustomed to serving the elite, initially hesitated and requested a medical certificate. Once Lord Arthur claimed the poison was for a rabid Norwegian mastiff that had bitten his coachman, the chemist was satisfied, praised his toxicological expertise, and prepared the order. Lord Arthur placed the capsule into a silver bonbonnière purchased on Bond Street, discarded the pharmacy’s container, and departed for Lady Clementina’s.\"\n}",
