parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "64ef6852b051885dc382cd95",
  "rewritten_text": "They were her existence, more precious than any sanctuary of love or anything else on earth. The boys received news of our departure with youthful joy, while Clara inquired if we were headed to Athens. I answered that it was possible, and her face lit up with delight. There, she would finally see her parents' grave and the lands steeped in her father’s renown. She had long contemplated these places in quiet, a preoccupation that had replaced her childhood playfulness with a grave, ambitious spirit. We had many humble friends we could not abandon, including the spirited horse Lord Raymond had gifted his daughter, Alfred’s dog, and an aging pet eagle. Yet, listing these favorites brought sorrow for our greater losses and the many things we were forced to leave behind. Idris wept, while Alfred and Evelyn insisted on packing rose bushes and carved vases, lamenting that we could not bring the castle, the forest, and all our familiar treasures. I told the foolish ones that we had already lost far greater things and were leaving these behind to protect what truly mattered. I urged them to focus on our purpose to stem their regret for such trifles. The children were soon distracted by thoughts of future fun, but Idris had vanished. She had fled to the park to hide her distress, and I found her embracing an old oak tree, sobbing uncontrollably. My heart broke to see her so consumed by grief. I pulled her close, and as she felt my embrace, she remembered what she still possessed. She thanked me for my kindness, admitting that while her heart was torn by sorrow, she remained happy because I and our children were still by her side."
}

