  "rewritten_text": "I mistook your meaning, thinking anybody meant everyone. It applies to those of noble birth, preferably royal. The abbot intervened to ease the tension, suggesting such a gift was surely meant for the high-born. When he asked about King Arthur, the enchanter offered to reveal his status. The crowd watched with renewed fascination as the magician claimed the king was resting in his palace. The abbot offered a blessing, but I countered that the king was actually riding. This created a dilemma for the onlookers. The magician mocked my lack of incantations, but I retorted that I reserve my magic for significant moments. Stung by my sarcasm, the magician claimed the queen and court were also asleep. I dismissed this as another falsehood, noting they were active. I challenged him to predict their destination, and he claimed they would travel toward the sea by morning. When he predicted they would be far north of Camelot by the following evening, I declared his statement a lie by a hundred and fifty miles."
}

