parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "66669d851249a5a75f8d7438",
  "rewritten_text": "Meanwhile, gravity would steadily weaken in proportion to the square of the distance, and I would eventually reach those distant regions where the moon's pull overcomes the earth's, accelerating rapidly. Given this, I saw no reason to pack more than forty days of supplies. Another concern troubled me. Balloonists at high altitudes often suffer from respiratory distress, physical pain, and nosebleeds, which worsen as they climb. I wondered if these symptoms would prove fatal. I concluded they would not. These issues stem from the loss of atmospheric pressure causing blood vessels to swell, rather than a fundamental biological failure like oxygen deprivation. Since breathing is a muscular action, I believed life could persist even in a vacuum. I assumed my body would adapt to the lack of pressure and that my physical resilience would see me through the initial discomfort. Your Excellencies, these are some of the factors that inspired my lunar voyage. I will now describe the outcome of this unprecedented endeavor. After reaching an altitude of three and three-quarters miles, I tossed out some feathers and continued to rise quickly, so I kept my ballast. I wanted to retain as much weight as possible for reasons I will explain later."
}

