parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0063d0036603ff65de20b717",
  "rewritten_text": "Please consider, ma'am, Oliver said, tears welling despite his efforts. Think of how young and kind she is, and the joy she brings to everyone. I am certain that for your sake, and for hers, she will not die. Heaven would never let her pass so young. Hush, Mrs. Maylie said, resting a hand on his head. You speak like a child, but you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it, but I hope to be forgiven; I am old and have seen enough death to know the pain of losing those we love. I also know that the youngest and best are not always spared. Yet this brings comfort, for Heaven is just, and these trials remind us of a brighter world beyond. God's will be done. I love her, and He knows how deeply. Oliver was surprised to see Mrs. Maylie suddenly compose herself, her grief replaced by firm resolve. He was even more astonished that this strength held throughout the following days; she remained collected and diligent, performing her duties with a steady, almost cheerful demeanor. He was young and did not yet understand what strong minds can endure under pressure. An anxious night passed, and by morning, Mrs. Maylie's fears were confirmed. Rose had fallen into a dangerous fever. We must be active, Oliver, and avoid useless grief, Mrs. Maylie said, looking him in the eye. This letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne immediately. Take it to the market town four miles away by the footpath, and have them send an express rider to Chertsey. The inn staff will handle it, and I know I can trust you to see it done. Oliver could not speak, but his expression showed his eagerness to leave at once."
}

