parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ded2920b70aaaeee2a8af4c2",
  "rewritten_text": "The studio smelled of roses, and as a light summer breeze moved through the garden, the scent of lilacs and flowering thorns drifted in through the open door. From his spot on the Persian divan, where he lay smoking his usual endless cigarettes, Lord Henry Wotton could see the golden blossoms of a laburnum tree, its branches heavy with vibrant beauty. Occasionally, the shadows of birds flew across the silk curtains over the large window, creating a fleeting, artistic effect that reminded him of Japanese painters who capture motion in still art. The steady buzzing of bees in the tall grass made the quiet feel even heavier, while the distant hum of London sounded like a far-off organ. In the center of the room, a full-length portrait of an exceptionally handsome young man stood on an easel. The artist, Basil Hallward—whose sudden disappearance years ago had caused such a stir—sat nearby. As Basil looked at the beautiful figure he had captured on canvas, he smiled with satisfaction. Suddenly, he stood up and covered his eyes, as if trying to hold onto a dream he was afraid of losing. It is your best work, Basil, the best thing you have ever done, Lord Henry said lazily. You must send it to the Grosvenor next year. The Academy is too big and common. I either cannot see the paintings because of the crowds, or I cannot see the people because of the paintings. The Grosvenor is the only place. I do not think I will send it anywhere, Basil replied, tossing his head back in the peculiar way that used to amuse his friends at Oxford."
}

