parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5a7fbdddc5e6713f5cc0a6ef",
  "rewritten_text": "The doctor was a kind, elderly man. I woke him up and explained that my brother and I had been hunting on Spanish Island yesterday. I told him my brother accidentally shot himself in the leg with his gun while dreaming, and we needed him to treat the wound secretly so we could surprise our family back home that evening. When he asked who our family was, I told him the Phelpses. He asked again how the accident happened, and I repeated that it was a strange dream. He grabbed his lantern and medical bag, but he hesitated when he saw my canoe, worried it was too small for two people. I quickly claimed it had carried the three of us—my brother, myself, and the guns—without trouble. He remained skeptical and looked for a larger boat, but since all others were locked, he took mine. He told me to wait or go home, but I insisted on staying. I gave him directions to the raft and he left. I worried that if the injury took days to heal, he might reveal our secret. I decided that if he had to return to the raft, I would follow him, tie him up if necessary, and keep him until my brother was ready to leave, at which point we would pay him and let him go. I fell asleep in a pile of lumber, and when I woke, the sun was high. I rushed to the doctor’s house, but was told he had left during the night and had not yet returned."
}

