  "rewritten_text": "After dinner, the group headed to the sandbar to hunt for turtle eggs. They poked sticks into the sand, and upon finding soft spots, knelt to dig with their hands. Sometimes they retrieved fifty or sixty eggs from a single hole; the eggs were perfectly round, white, and slightly smaller than walnuts. They enjoyed a grand feast of fried eggs that night and again on Friday morning. After breakfast, they ran out onto the bar, shouting and chasing each other. They shed their clothes until they were naked, continuing their game in the shallow water against a strong current that frequently knocked them over, adding to the excitement. Occasionally, they gathered to splash water at one another, shielding their faces from the spray until they grappled and wrestled. The strongest would dunk his opponent, leaving them all in a tangled heap of limbs, surfacing to gasp and laugh. Once exhausted, they sprawled on the hot, dry sand, covering themselves with it before returning to the water to repeat the cycle. Eventually, they decided their bare skin resembled flesh-colored tights, so they drew a ring in the sand and held a circus, with three boys insisting on being clowns. Next, they played marbles until the game lost its appeal. Joe and Huck went for another swim, but Tom refused, having realized he had lost his string of rattlesnake rattles while undressing. Fearing he might suffer a cramp without his charm, he searched until he found it. By then, the others were tired and restless. They drifted apart, feeling melancholy as they gazed across the river at the sleepy village. Tom found himself tracing the name BECKY in the sand with his toe. He angrily erased it, ashamed of his sentimentality, yet he wrote it again, unable to resist. Finally, he cleared the sand and rejoined the other boys to distract himself."
}

