  "request_id": "9ccc50b81a7654a9241aea20",
  "rewritten_text": "P.S. Do inform me regarding the bows. Jennings maintains they are currently in vogue. Lord Arthur appeared so grave and distressed by the note that the Duchess erupted in laughter. My dear Arthur, she exclaimed, I shall never share a young lady’s correspondence with you again! However, what shall I reply concerning the clock? I consider it an excellent device and would enjoy owning one myself. I do not think highly of them, Lord Arthur replied with a melancholy smile, and after kissing his mother, he departed. Upon reaching his room, he collapsed onto a sofa, his eyes brimming with tears. He had exerted every effort to execute this murder, yet he had failed twice, through no fault of his own. He had attempted to fulfill his duty, but it appeared as though Destiny had betrayed him. He felt burdened by the fruitlessness of good intentions and the pointlessness of striving for nobility. Perhaps it would be wiser to dissolve the engagement entirely. Sybil would suffer, certainly, but such pain could not truly damage a spirit as fine as hers. As for himself, what did it signify? There is always a war where a man might perish or a cause to which he might dedicate his life, and since existence held no joy for him, death possessed no fear. He would let Fate determine his end; he would no longer intervene. At seven-thirty, he dressed and proceeded to the club. Surbiton was present with a group of young men, and he was compelled to dine with them. Their superficial chatter and aimless jokes failed to engage him, and he departed as soon as coffee arrived, fabricating an excuse to leave. As he exited, the hall-porter delivered a letter. It was from Herr Winckelkopf, requesting he visit the following evening to inspect an explosive umbrella that detonated upon opening. It was the newest innovation, recently arrived from Geneva. He shredded the letter. He had resolved to cease his experiments. He then wandered to the Thames Embankment, sitting by the water for hours. The moon gazed through a mass of tawny clouds like a lion’s eye, and countless stars adorned the hollow sky like gold dust scattered across a purple dome. Occasionally, a barge drifted into the murky current, carried away by the tide, while railway signals shifted from green to crimson as trains shrieked across the bridge."
}

