  "rewritten_text": "He was a poet who published his first work at twenty-three, earning immediate, nationwide acclaim. His success was unprecedented, with both the public and intellectuals praising his work without reservation. At the peak of his fame, he met Elinor, a beautiful young heiress living with her guardian. They seemed destined for each other. While Elinor lacked Woodville’s creative genius, she was noble and virtuous, possessing a natural grace and deep, expressive eyes that reflected both wisdom and sensitivity. They fell in love instantly, finding profound joy in their mutual devotion. Woodville, the son of a modest clergyman, was a celebrated figure, while Elinor was wealthy. Her guardian approved of their relationship, acknowledging Woodville’s stature, but her father’s will prohibited her from marrying until she came of age. Though only twenty, they accepted the wait, spending their time together in blissful anticipation, studying and planning their future. As Woodville’s reputation grew, Elinor flourished under his guidance. With her twenty-first birthday only two months away, their wedding preparations were underway. It is painful to recount the tragedy that followed, yet perhaps it was inevitable; in a world defined by sorrow, such perfect happiness as theirs could not be permitted to endure. To allow them their union would have required a fundamental change in the nature of our existence."
}

