  "request_id": "e2efd908094ebb4e8f758b89",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Merton felt quite distressed by the second delay of the wedding, and Lady Julia, having already purchased her gown, urged Sybil to end the engagement. Despite her deep love for her mother, Sybil had committed her life to Lord Arthur and remained steadfast. Lord Arthur struggled for days with his disappointment, his nerves frayed, but his practical nature soon prevailed. Since poison had failed, he concluded that an explosive was the logical next step. He reviewed his list of relatives and decided to target his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean was a cultured man with a vast collection of antique clocks, which Lord Arthur realized provided the perfect opportunity for his plan. Finding an explosive device proved difficult; the directory offered no help, and he doubted the police would be useful, as they rarely anticipated such events. He then recalled his acquaintance, Count Rouvaloff, a young Russian with revolutionary leanings he had met at a party. While the Count claimed to be researching Peter the Great, many suspected he was a Nihilist agent. Lord Arthur visited his Bloomsbury lodgings to seek his help. When asked if he was becoming involved in politics, Lord Arthur, disliking pretense, admitted he had no interest in social issues and only required the device for a private family matter."
}

