parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "647ac2dcfe43f972bcb968f9",
  "rewritten_text": "Reading about the opulence of the deceased was truly remarkable. He then focused on the embroideries and tapestries that served as wall hangings in the cold northern European palaces. As he researched—possessing a unique talent for total immersion in his current interest—he felt a melancholy reflection on how time inevitably destroys beautiful objects. He, however, had avoided such a fate. Seasons passed, flowers bloomed and withered, and nights of terror repeated their shameful tales, yet he remained untouched. No winter aged his face or faded his youthful glow. How different it was for material things. Where had they vanished? Where was the saffron robe depicting the gods battling giants, crafted by artisans for Athena? Where was the massive purple canopy Nero stretched over the Colosseum, featuring a starry sky and Apollo driving his chariot? He yearned to see the intricate napkins made for the Priest of the Sun, displaying every imaginable feast; King Chilperic’s burial shroud with its three hundred golden bees; the scandalous robes adorned with lions, bears, and hunters that angered the Bishop of Pontus; or the coat of Charles of Orleans, its sleeves embroidered with song lyrics and musical notes formed from pearls. He read of Queen Joan of Burgundy’s room at Rheims, decorated with over thirteen hundred embroidered parrots and five hundred butterflies, all bearing royal crests in gold. Catherine de Medici owned a mourning bed of black velvet covered in crescents and suns, with damask curtains and pearl-fringed embroidery, set in a room draped with her emblems. Louis XIV even possessed fifteen-foot-tall gold-embroidered caryatids in his private chambers."
}

