parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "742b2652d16d04b1b0a08ad0",
  "rewritten_text": "This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis had agreed to retain at Lady Canterville’s urgent insistence. She greeted them with a deep curtsey as they arrived, stating in a formal, archaic style, ‘I welcome you to Canterville Chase.’ They followed her through the Tudor hall into the library, a long, dark-paneled room featuring a large stained-glass window. Tea was prepared there, and after removing their coats, they sat and surveyed the room while Mrs. Umney served them. Mrs. Otis soon noticed a dull red mark on the floor near the hearth and, unaware of its meaning, remarked, ‘I fear something has been spilled there.’ ‘Yes, madam,’ the housekeeper whispered, ‘blood has been spilled on that spot.’ ‘How dreadful,’ Mrs. Otis exclaimed; ‘I dislike bloodstains in a living room. It must be cleaned immediately.’ The woman smiled, replying in a hushed, cryptic tone, ‘It is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, murdered there by her husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon lived nine more years before vanishing under mysterious circumstances. His remains were never found, but his spirit haunts the Chase. The stain is admired by visitors and cannot be removed.’ ‘That is nonsense,’ Washington Otis declared; ‘Pinkerton’s Champion Stain Remover will clear it instantly.’ Before the horrified housekeeper could stop him, he knelt and scrubbed the floor with a black stick. Soon, the stain vanished. ‘I knew Pinkerton would succeed,’ he boasted to his family, but as he spoke, a brilliant flash of lightning illuminated the room, a loud crack of thunder startled them, and Mrs. Umney fainted. ‘What a terrible climate!’ the American Minister noted, lighting a cigar. ‘I suppose England is so crowded they lack sufficient weather for everyone. Emigration is the only solution.’ ‘My dear Hiram,’ Mrs. Otis asked, ‘what shall we do with a woman who faints?’ ‘Bill her for it like broken china,’ the Minister replied; ‘she will not faint again.’ Shortly after, Mrs. Umney regained consciousness."
}

