parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d711898ecdaa5f1fd09beaee",
  "rewritten_text": "These deputies traveled from Paris to Calais to meet Adrian, who had been chosen as the arbitrator. Throughout their journey through empty towns and desolate landscapes, they harbored intense hatred for one another, and they continued to argue their cases with fierce partisanship. By questioning the deputies separately, we discovered the true situation in Paris. Since parliament had named him Ryland’s deputy, all surviving English people had accepted Adrian as their leader, lawgiver, and protector. Originally, we had not planned for our group to be separated, and the Earl of Windsor was intended to hold supreme command. However, unforeseen events forced us to divide for nearly two months. Upon arriving in Paris in two separate groups, conflict broke out between them. They found Paris to be a ghost town. While we had once received regular news about the plague’s progress on the continent, communication eventually ceased as the death toll rose. Even within England, travel became rare. Few ships crossed the channel, and those who did often perished at sea or arrived already infected, dying before they could share news of France’s destruction. We had hoped to find many survivors abroad, but the plague had been even more devastating there. France was empty; we saw no one on the road from Calais to Paris. In the capital, only about a hundred people remained, wandering the streets and discussing the past with a surprising, lingering cheerfulness. The English took over the city, which was filled with lifeless, narrow streets. A few pale survivors gathered at the Tuileries, wondering why we had come to their doomed city. In their misery, they believed their own suffering was the worst, much like a person in pain who imagines that any other form of agony would be easier to bear."
}

