parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "356d4c48fb5f1645cfa383b4",
  "rewritten_text": "Many American women abroad pretend to be sickly, thinking it looks sophisticated, but Mrs. Otis avoided this mistake. She was healthy and energetic. In many ways, she was quite English, proving that America and England share everything except language. Her eldest son, Washington, was a handsome, fair-haired man who prepared for diplomacy by dancing at Newport. He was sensible, though he loved flowers and the nobility. Virginia, his fifteen-year-old sister, was graceful and lovely with bright blue eyes. A skilled rider, she once beat Lord Bilton in a race, which so impressed the young Duke of Cheshire that he proposed to her immediately, only to be sent back to school by his guardians. Finally, there were the twins, nicknamed the Stars and Stripes because they were constantly in trouble. They were the only true republicans in the family besides their father. Since Canterville Chase was seven miles from the train station, Mr. Otis hired a carriage. They set off in high spirits on a beautiful July evening, enjoying the scent of pine and the sight of wildlife. As they reached the estate, however, the sky turned dark, the air grew still, and birds flew silently overhead. Rain began to fall just before they arrived. An elderly woman in a black silk dress, white cap, and apron stood on the steps to greet them."
}

