parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5353c072bd9b90cd38e0f224",
  "rewritten_text": "We went to your home, the first man said, but we missed you. Hearing you were walking this way, we followed, hoping to catch you. Please, get into the carriage. Both men were demanding, and they moved to block my path to the carriage door. They were armed, and I was not. Gentlemen, I said, excuse me, but I usually ask who is requesting my help and what the situation is. The second man replied, Doctor, your clients are people of status. As for the case, we trust your skill to figure it out better than we could explain. That is enough. Get in. I had no choice but to obey and climbed in silently. They followed, the last one jumping in after closing the steps. The carriage turned and drove off quickly. I am repeating this conversation exactly as it happened, word for word. I am describing everything just as it occurred, keeping my focus steady. Where I leave gaps, I am stopping for now to hide my notes. The carriage left the city, passed the North Barrier, and reached the country road. About two miles later, it turned off the main path and stopped at a lonely house. We all got out and walked along a damp, overgrown garden path to the door. When the bell was rung, the door did not open right away, so one of my escorts struck the man who answered across the face with his heavy glove. I did not think much of it, as I had seen common people treated worse than dogs. However, the other man grew angry and struck the servant as well. Their expressions and movements were so identical that I realized then they were twin brothers. From the moment we arrived at the locked gate, I had heard cries coming from an upstairs room."
}

