parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "74ea828507bdfe2210d7425b",
  "rewritten_text": "Charles Evrémonde, known as Darnay, was finally brought to trial. The judges wore feathered hats, but the rough red caps and tricolor badges of the revolutionaries were everywhere else. Looking at the jury and the rowdy crowd, Darnay felt as if the world had turned upside down, with criminals judging honest men. The city’s most brutal citizens dominated the room, shouting their opinions and cheering for a conviction without restraint. Many men carried weapons, while some women held knives or daggers. Others ate, drank, or knitted as they watched. One woman, knitting steadily, sat near a man Darnay recognized as Defarge. She whispered to her husband, yet neither of them ever looked at Darnay. They stared only at the jury, waiting with grim focus. Doctor Manette sat quietly below the President, dressed in his usual clothes, appearing alongside Mr. Lorry as one of the few people not wearing the coarse revolutionary uniform. The prosecutor accused Darnay of being an emigrant, a crime punishable by death under the Republic’s new laws. It did not matter that the law was passed after he had returned to France; he was there, and they wanted his head. The crowd screamed for his execution, calling him an enemy of the Republic. The President rang his bell for order and asked if Darnay had lived in England for many years. Darnay admitted he had. The President then asked if that made him an emigrant. Darnay replied that he hoped not, as he had voluntarily given up his title and status, leaving France to earn his own living in England rather than profiting from the labor of the French people."
}

