parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8bfed9aa61ef30825ba25fb4",
  "rewritten_text": "Once I had submitted the petitions, I found myself drawn to the locations where they were held, feeling as though my presence offered them some small measure of hope. Driven by this anxious agitation, I would pace the streets at night, passing the offices and homes where I had left my appeals. To this day, the bleak western districts of London on a chilly, dusty spring evening, with their imposing, closed residences and endless lines of streetlamps, evoke a profound sadness in me due to those memories. My daily visits were now curtailed, and he was under tighter guard. Suspecting that I might be accused of smuggling poison to him, I requested to be searched before sitting by his bed, assuring the ever-present officer of my honest intentions. No one treated us with cruelty; they simply performed their duties without malice. The officer and the other prisoners acting as nurses—criminals, yet capable of compassion—consistently informed me that his condition was worsening. As time passed, I observed him gazing quietly at the ceiling, his expression dull until my voice briefly animated his features. Often unable to speak, he would communicate through gentle squeezes of my hand, which I learned to interpret clearly. By the tenth day, a significant shift occurred. His eyes brightened as I walked in, fixed upon the entrance. Dear boy, he greeted me, I feared you were late, though I knew that was impossible. It is exactly the right time, I replied, as I waited at the gate. You always wait at the gate, don't you? Yes, so I would not miss a single moment. Thank you, dear boy, God bless you, you have never abandoned me. I squeezed his hand, unable to forget that I had once intended to do just that. And the best part, he added, is that you have been more at ease with me since I fell into this misfortune than when things were bright."
}

