parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "455ddff5d775d0a6cc810f48",
  "rewritten_text": "“You don’t mean to say—” my sister began. “Yes, I do,” Pumblechook interrupted. “Go on, Joseph.” “What would you say to twenty pounds?” Joe asked. “That would be generous,” my sister replied. “Well,” Joe continued, “it’s more than twenty.” Pumblechook nodded, laughing patronizingly. “It’s more than that, indeed. Keep going, Joseph!” “To finish it,” Joe said, handing her the bag, “it’s twenty-five pounds.” “Twenty-five pounds,” Pumblechook echoed, standing to shake her hand, “and you deserve every penny. I wish you joy!” The villain then made things worse by seizing my arm with an air of ownership that eclipsed his previous behavior. “Now, Joseph and wife,” he said, gripping my elbow, “I always finish what I start. This boy must be apprenticed immediately. That’s my way.” “We are deeply grateful, Uncle Pumblechook,” my sister said, clutching the cash. “Don’t mention it,” the man replied. “But we must get this boy bound. I promised I would see to it.” We went to the nearby Town Hall to finalize my apprenticeship. Pumblechook shoved me along as if I had just committed a crime. The crowd clearly assumed I was guilty; some asked what I had done, while others remarked on my supposedly wicked appearance. A kind-looking person even handed me a pamphlet titled To Be Read In My Cell, featuring an illustration of a young criminal in chains. The courtroom felt strange, with high pews and onlookers peering down. The Justices sat back, some reading newspapers or dozing, while dark portraits hung on the walls, appearing to my eyes like strange, hardened lumps."
}

