  "rewritten_text": "Rose faced a difficult dilemma. She desperately wanted to uncover the mystery of Oliver’s past, yet she felt bound to protect the secret shared by the troubled woman she had met. That woman’s plea had moved Rose deeply, and she genuinely wanted to help the outcast find redemption. They planned to stay in London for only three more days before leaving for the coast. It was already midnight of the first day, leaving her little time to act without raising suspicion. Mr. Losberne was staying with them, but Rose knew his impulsive temper. She feared he would react with such fury that he would ruin any chance of helping the girl. For the same reason, she could not tell Mrs. Maylie, who would immediately consult the doctor. Legal help seemed equally impossible. She briefly considered asking Harry for advice, but the memory of their last goodbye stopped her. She worried that calling him back would be unfair, especially if he had finally moved on and found happiness without her. Troubled by these thoughts, Rose spent a restless night. By the next day, she reached a desperate decision: she would contact Harry. She worried about the pain of seeing him again, but hoped he might simply write back or visit without forcing a personal encounter. As she began to write, she set down her pen and turned away, unable to let even the paper witness her tears."
