  "request_id": "ebc088dd2aa046d48f2068e8",
  "rewritten_text": "Your father can surely spare you, if your mother agrees. Daughters matter less to fathers. If you stay another full month, I could take one of you to London when I go in early June. Since Dawson does not mind the carriage box, there is room for one, or even both if the weather is cool and you remain small. You are very kind, but we must stick to our original plan. Lady Catherine accepted this. Mrs. Collins, you must send a servant. I speak my mind, and it is improper for two young women to travel alone. You must arrange for someone to go. I despise such things; young women require proper protection. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate, I insisted on two men-servants. Miss Darcy could not have traveled otherwise. I am very particular. You must send John with them, Mrs. Collins; it would reflect poorly on you to let them go alone. My uncle is sending a servant for us. Oh, your uncle has a man-servant? I am glad someone considers these things. Where will you change horses? Bromley, of course. Mention my name at the Bell and you will be well served. Lady Catherine asked many questions about the trip, and Elizabeth found it fortunate she had to pay attention, or she might have lost track of her surroundings. She saved her reflections for solitary walks, where she could dwell on her unpleasant memories. She nearly knew Mr. Darcy’s letter by heart. She analyzed every sentence, her feelings shifting constantly. She felt indignant at his tone, yet when she considered how unfairly she had judged him, her anger turned inward, and she felt compassion for his disappointment."
}

