parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a37d66bbda88d1a902c77cca",
  "rewritten_text": "Sandy and I were back on the road early the next morning. It felt wonderful to breathe in the fresh, dew-kissed, woodland air after suffocating for two days in the filth of that miserable place. Of course, the castle had been fine for Sandy, who was accustomed to high society. The poor girl had been quiet for a while, and I knew she was eager to talk. Since she had supported me so well in the castle with her elaborate stories, I decided she had earned the right to speak, and I didn't mind when she began. Now we turn to Sir Marhaus, who rode south with a thirty-year-old damsel. Are you going to pick up the trail of the cowboys again, Sandy? Indeed, my lord. Go ahead then. I won't interrupt this time. Start over, tell the whole story, and I will smoke my pipe and listen. Now we turn to Sir Marhaus, who rode south with a thirty-year-old damsel. They entered a deep forest and were caught by nightfall. Eventually, they reached a courtyard where the Duke of South Marches lived and asked for shelter. In the morning, the Duke challenged Sir Marhaus to a fight. Sir Marhaus armed himself, attended mass, ate breakfast, and mounted his horse in the courtyard. The Duke and his six sons were already waiting, each holding a spear. They charged, and the Duke and two of his sons broke their spears against Sir Marhaus, who did not even lower his weapon. Then the other four sons attacked in pairs, breaking their spears as well, while Sir Marhaus still refused to strike. Finally, Sir Marhaus charged the Duke and struck him with such force that both man and horse fell to the ground."
}

