  "request_id": "c3fd32d3052ce9ab9abbb23c",
  "rewritten_text": "Good heavens! Had I for a moment suspected the diabolical purpose of my enemy, I would have chosen a life of lonely exile over agreeing to this wretched union. Yet, the monster used some dark art to obscure his true motives; in my attempt to prepare for my own demise, I inadvertently accelerated the destruction of someone far more precious. As the wedding date approached, I felt my spirit falter, whether from fear or intuition. I masked my dread with a forced cheerfulness that pleased my father, though it failed to fully convince the perceptive Elizabeth. She anticipated our marriage with calm hope, tempered by the lingering fear that such happiness might prove fleeting. Preparations proceeded, and I hid my inner turmoil, participating in my father's arrangements as if they were not merely the trappings of my own ruin. Through his efforts, Elizabeth regained some of her inheritance, including a property on Lake Como, where we planned to spend our honeymoon. Meanwhile, I armed myself with pistols and a dagger, remaining vigilant against any overt assault. This vigilance brought me some peace, and as the day neared, the threat seemed less substantial, making our union feel inevitable. Elizabeth appeared content, and my composed manner helped soothe her, yet on our wedding day, she was somber, haunted by a sense of impending doom and perhaps the weight of the secret I had vowed to share with her the following day."
}

