parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9689042acb73b3f1fd48338b",
  "rewritten_text": "“I must remark, Sir Walter,” Mr. Shepherd stated one morning at Kellynch Hall while setting aside his paper, “that current circumstances favor us. This peace will bring many wealthy naval officers back to land, all seeking residences. It is an ideal moment to secure reliable tenants. Many have amassed great fortunes during the war. If a wealthy admiral were to present himself, Sir Walter—” “He would be a fortunate man, Shepherd,” Sir Walter answered. “Kellynch Hall would be his greatest prize, regardless of what he captured at sea; eh, Shepherd?” Mr. Shepherd laughed as expected, then continued, “I find naval gentlemen are excellent to deal with. They possess liberal views and make desirable tenants. I suggest that if rumors of your intentions spread—which is likely, as your actions are always under scrutiny, unlike my own—we should prioritize applications from wealthy naval commanders. I can arrive within two hours to handle any inquiries, sparing you the effort.” Sir Walter merely nodded. Soon, he stood and paced, remarking dryly, “I imagine few naval officers would not be astonished to inhabit a house of this caliber.” “They would certainly look about and count their blessings,” added Mrs. Clay, who was present, as a drive to Kellynch always benefited her health. “Yet I agree with my father that a sailor could prove a very suitable tenant.”"
}

