parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d502d34505e8e40162003121",
  "rewritten_text": "Every properly fashioned boy eventually feels an intense urge to search for buried treasure. This impulse struck Tom suddenly. He went to find Joe Harper but failed, and his search for Ben Rogers also proved fruitless as the boy had gone fishing. Eventually, he encountered Huck Finn the Red-Handed, who proved receptive. Tom brought him to a secluded spot to share his secret plan. Huck agreed, as he was always ready for any venture that promised amusement without requiring money, possessing an excess of time that held no monetary value. Where shall we dig, Huck asked. Oh, almost anywhere, Tom replied. Is it hidden everywhere, Huck wondered. Certainly not, Tom explained. It is buried in specific locations, like islands, inside decaying chests beneath the boughs of dead trees where shadows fall at midnight, or under the floorboards of haunted houses. Robbers hide it, Tom clarified, dismissing Huck's suggestion of Sunday-school superintendents. Huck mused that he would rather spend such wealth, but Tom insisted robbers always leave it behind. They often forget the markers or perish, leaving the treasure to rust until someone discovers an old, cryptic map filled with hieroglyphics that require weeks to decipher. When Huck asked if Tom possessed such a map, Tom admitted he did not. He planned to search haunted houses, islands, and dead trees with protruding limbs. When Huck pointed out that checking every tree would take all summer, Tom countered by describing the potential reward of a brass pot filled with rusty coins or a chest of diamonds. Huck’s eyes brightened, declaring that a hundred dollars would be plenty for him."
}

