parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "65c9317f6600cfe6d88a75fb",
  "rewritten_text": "Three or four months passed, and it was deep into winter. I had been attending school regularly, learning to read, write, and spell a little. I could recite the multiplication table up to six times seven is thirty-five, but I doubt I could ever learn more, even if I lived forever. I never cared much for math anyway. At first, I hated school, but eventually, I grew used to it. When I felt tired, I would skip class, and the punishment I received the next day actually cheered me up. As time went on, school became easier. I was also getting accustomed to the Widow’s rules, which didn't bother me as much anymore. Living indoors and sleeping in a bed felt restrictive, but before the weather turned cold, I would occasionally sneak out to sleep in the woods for a break. I still preferred my old ways, but I was starting to enjoy the new ones too. The Widow said I was making slow but steady progress and that she was proud of me. One morning at breakfast, I accidentally knocked over the salt shaker. I reached to throw some over my left shoulder to ward off bad luck, but Miss Watson stopped me, scolding me for making a mess. The Widow tried to defend me, but I knew that wouldn't stop the bad luck from coming. I left the house feeling anxious, wondering what kind of trouble was headed my way. Since there was no way to prevent this specific misfortune, I just walked along, feeling gloomy and alert. I went to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the high board fence. There was an inch of fresh snow on the ground, and I noticed someone’s footprints. They had come from the quarry, lingered by the stile, and then walked around the garden fence. It was strange that they hadn't come inside after waiting there so long. I couldn't understand it. It seemed very odd, so I knelt down to examine the tracks more closely."
}

