  "rewritten_text": "That was all the encouragement she needed. \"My dear, you must hear this. Mrs. Long says that Netherfield Park has been rented by a wealthy young man from the north of England. He arrived on Monday in a carriage to inspect the property, liked it so much that he signed the lease with Mr. Morris immediately, and plans to move in before late September, with his staff arriving by the end of next week.\" \"What is his name?\" \"Bingley.\" \"Is he married or single?\" \"Single, of course! A wealthy bachelor with four or five thousand a year. What a wonderful opportunity for our daughters!\" \"How so? How does that concern them?\" \"My dear Mr. Bennet,\" his wife replied, \"how can you be so difficult? You know I am thinking of him marrying one of them.\" \"Is that his intention in moving here?\" \"Intention? Don't be ridiculous. But it is very likely he might fall in love with one of them, so you must visit him as soon as he arrives.\" \"I see no reason for that. You and the girls can go, or send them on their own, which might be better; since you are as attractive as any of them, Mr. Bingley might prefer you.\" \"You flatter me, my dear. I certainly had my share of beauty, but I don't claim to be anything special now. When a woman has five grown daughters, she should stop worrying about her own looks.\" \"In such cases, a woman rarely has much beauty left to worry about.\" \"But you really must visit Mr. Bingley when he arrives.\" \"I can make no such promise, I assure you.\" \"But think of your daughters. Consider what a match it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are going for that reason alone, even though they usually don't visit newcomers. You must go, because we cannot visit him if you don't.\" \"You are being overly cautious. I am sure Mr. Bingley will be happy to see you, and I will send a note with you giving my full consent for him to marry whichever girl he chooses—though I must put in a good word for my little Lizzy.\" \"I forbid you to do any such thing. Lizzy is no better than the others; she is not half as pretty as Jane, nor half as cheerful as Lydia.\""
}

