parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8eee5c0e8a941b06014bb292",
  "rewritten_text": "It was an era of extremes: simultaneously the finest and most wretched of times, an age of profound sagacity and utter folly, a period of faith and deep skepticism, a season of illumination and shadow, a spring of optimism and a winter of hopelessness. We possessed everything and yet nothing; we were destined for paradise or its opposite. In essence, that era so closely resembled our own that its most vocal leaders demanded it be viewed only in the most extreme terms. England was ruled by a king with a prominent jaw and a plain-faced queen, while France had a king with a similar jaw and a beautiful queen. To the ruling classes in both nations, it seemed perfectly evident that the existing order was permanent. The year was 1775. England experienced spiritual manifestations much like our own; Mrs. Southcott had just turned twenty-five, and a prophetic soldier had foretold the destruction of London. The Cock-lane ghost had been silenced only twelve years prior, having communicated much like the unoriginal spirits of the previous year. Meanwhile, messages of far greater consequence arrived from British subjects in America, proving more significant to humanity than any spectral warnings. France, though less spiritually inclined, slid effortlessly toward ruin, printing and squandering currency. Under the watch of her clergy, she amused herself with brutal acts, such as torturing a youth for failing to kneel before a passing religious procession. It is probable that in the forests of France and Norway, trees were already being marked by Fate to be fashioned into the grim, historical apparatus of execution that would eventually claim its victims."
}

