parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ca3ab30ca52527c86ac9eaca",
  "rewritten_text": "I told him I required the watermelon, but he argued that I did not need it for a prison escape, which was the point of contention. He claimed it would be acceptable if I intended to conceal a blade inside to help Jim slay the jailer. I dropped the subject, though I found little benefit in playing the part of a prisoner if I had to debate such trivialities whenever I spotted a watermelon. As I mentioned, we waited that morning until the yard was deserted. Tom carried the sack into the shed while I kept watch. He soon emerged, and we sat on the woodpile to confer. He stated that everything was prepared except for the tools. When I asked what tools were for, he explained we needed them to dig, as we could not simply gnaw Jim out. I suggested the old, broken picks in the shed should suffice, but he looked at me with such pity that it was nearly heartbreaking. He asked if I had ever heard of a prisoner being provided with modern digging equipment, noting that such tools would ruin any chance of him being a hero. He insisted that even a king would not be given picks and shovels. When I asked what we should use instead, he specified a pair of case-knives. I protested that using knives to dig out a cabin foundation was absurd, but he maintained it was the correct and standard method. He claimed to have read every relevant book on the subject, asserting that prisoners always dig through solid rock with case-knives, a process that takes ages. He asked how long I thought a prisoner in the Castle d'If took to dig his way out, and when I could not guess, he pressed me for an answer. I replied that I had no idea, perhaps a month and a half."
}

