parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8fe6cbed4b13b32d515e6284",
  "rewritten_text": "Sir Walter had rented a grand house in Camden Place, a location befitting a man of his status, and both he and Elizabeth were quite pleased with their new home. Anne arrived feeling discouraged, dreading the months ahead and wondering when she might leave. However, the unexpectedly warm welcome she received lifted her spirits. Her father and sister were happy to see her, mostly to show off their new residence and furnishings, and they treated her with kindness. They even appreciated her presence at the dinner table. Mrs. Clay was pleasant and smiling, though her friendliness felt routine. Anne had expected this, but the genuine warmth from her father and sister was a surprise. They were in high spirits and eager to talk about themselves, showing little interest in Anne’s news. After fishing for compliments about how much they were missed back home—which Anne could not provide—they quickly turned the conversation to Bath. They insisted that the city exceeded their expectations. They claimed their house was the finest in Camden Place, boasting that their drawing rooms and decor were superior to any others. They also bragged that everyone in town wanted to meet them, noting that they had already turned down many invitations while still receiving constant visits from strangers. Anne understood why they were happy, yet she felt saddened that her father saw no loss in leaving his responsibilities as a landowner, instead finding vanity in the trivialities of city life. She could only sigh and smile as Elizabeth proudly paced between the rooms, boasting about the space, amazed that the former mistress of Kellynch Hall now took pride in a house only thirty feet wide. Yet, there was one more reason for their happiness: Mr. Elliot."
}

