parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "efc620bccda62a0a6d187f12",
  "rewritten_text": "I decided to get off as soon as we reached town to avoid him. I managed this easily. My small bag was under my feet, so I simply opened the latch, grabbed it, and stepped onto the pavement by the first streetlamp. The convicts continued on the coach toward the river. In my mind, I pictured the boat waiting for them at the muddy stairs, heard the harsh command to row, and saw the dark, ominous ship on the black water. I could not explain my fear, but it was intense and vague. As I walked to the hotel, I felt a dread far worse than just worrying about being recognized. It was formless, like a sudden return of a childhood terror. The Blue Boar’s coffee room was empty, and I had already started my dinner before the waiter recognized me. After apologizing for his poor memory, he asked if he should fetch Mr. Pumblechook. I told him absolutely not. The waiter, who had served me when I was first apprenticed, looked surprised. He quickly placed a dirty local newspaper in front of me. I picked it up and read a paragraph about my recent rise in fortune. It claimed that my earliest mentor was a respected local man in the corn and seed trade, whose business was located near the High Street. The article praised him as the guide behind my success, clearly taking pride that our town had produced the man who started my career."
}

