  "rewritten_text": "While reaching through the valve with water for the cat, my shirt sleeve caught the loop supporting the basket, instantly detaching it. The basket vanished from view as if it had dissolved into thin air. Less than a tenth of a second passed between the release and its total disappearance. I wished them well, though I held no expectation that the cat or her kittens would survive the fall. By six o'clock, I noticed a large section of the earth to the east shrouded in shadow, which spread quickly until the entire visible surface was cloaked in night by five minutes to seven. The setting sun continued to light the balloon long after this, a predictable yet deeply satisfying sight. It was clear that I would witness the sunrise hours before the people of Rotterdam, despite their more easterly position, and that my increased altitude would grant me progressively longer days. I resolved to record my journey, counting days as continuous twenty-four-hour periods regardless of darkness. At ten, feeling tired, I prepared to sleep, but a glaring problem occurred to me: how would the air in the cabin be refreshed while I rested? Breathing the same air for more than an hour or so would be impossible, potentially leading to fatal results. This realization caused me significant alarm; it may seem strange that after surviving so many perils, I would view this issue so gravely that I considered abandoning my mission and descending. Yet, this doubt lasted only a moment."
}

