parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8d288513aadf91c4f75e58f3",
  "rewritten_text": "Mary avoided writing to Bath, leaving the burden of a tedious, unproductive correspondence with Elizabeth entirely to Anne. Shortly after breakfast, Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick arrived to join them for a final stroll through Lyme, as the group intended to depart for Uppercross by one o'clock and wished to spend their remaining time outdoors. Once they reached the street, Captain Benwick gravitated toward Anne. Their previous evening's discussion had encouraged him to seek her out again, and they walked for a while, debating the works of Scott and Byron with the same lack of consensus common to all readers. Eventually, the party shifted, and Captain Harville took Benwick's place at her side. Miss Elliot, he murmured, you have done a kindness by drawing that poor man into conversation. I wish he had such company more frequently. It is unhealthy for him to remain so isolated, yet we cannot bear to part. Anne agreed that separation seemed impossible, though she suggested that time might eventually ease his suffering, reminding him that his friend was still a recent mourner, having lost his partner only last summer. Indeed, he sighed, it was only June. And perhaps he did not learn of it immediately, she added. Not until August, when he returned from the Cape newly promoted to the Grappler, Harville explained. I was in Plymouth, dreading the task of informing him. He sent letters, but the Grappler was bound for Portsmouth. I could not bring myself to break the news; no one could have done it but that fine fellow, he said, gesturing toward Captain Wentworth. The Laconia had arrived in Plymouth, so he was free from immediate duty. He requested leave but, without waiting for a reply, traveled day and night to Portsmouth, rowed out to the Grappler, and stayed by his friend's side for a week. That is what he did; no one else could have saved poor James. You can imagine, Miss Elliot, how dear he is to us. Anne reflected on this with absolute certainty and offered a reply that matched her own sentiments and his capacity to hear them, for he was too moved to continue the topic, and their subsequent conversation turned to entirely different matters."
}

