parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0c9a8d3e129546e0a472f0f6",
  "rewritten_text": "It is strange, but everyone who vanishes is supposedly spotted in San Francisco. It must be a charming city, possessing all the allure of the afterlife. What do you think happened to Basil? Dorian asked, holding his wine to the light and wondering how he could speak of it so calmly. I have no idea. If Basil wants to hide, that is his business. If he is dead, I prefer not to dwell on it. Death is the only thing that truly terrifies me. I despise it. Why? the younger man asked wearily. Because, Lord Henry said, sniffing his scented vinaigrette, one can survive anything these days except that. Death and vulgarity are the only two facts of this century that cannot be explained away. Let us have coffee in the music room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran off with played it beautifully. Poor Victoria! I was fond of her. The house is lonely without her. Marriage is just a habit, a bad one, but one misses even the worst habits. Perhaps one misses them most, as they are such a core part of one's personality. Dorian said nothing, but rose and went to the piano, letting his fingers drift over the keys. Once the coffee arrived, he looked at Lord Henry and asked, Harry, did it ever occur to you that Basil was murdered? Lord Henry yawned. Basil was popular and carried a cheap watch. Why would he be murdered? He was not clever enough to have enemies. He had a genius for painting, but one can paint like Velasquez and still be dull. Basil was dull. He only interested me once, when he confessed his obsession with you and that you were the inspiration for his art. I was fond of Basil, Dorian said sadly. But don't people say he was murdered? Some newspapers do, but it seems unlikely. I know Paris has dangerous spots, but Basil was not the type to go there. He lacked curiosity; it was his greatest flaw. What would you say, Harry, if I told you I murdered Basil? the younger man asked."
}

