parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8fb421646c7a7fc641501e84",
  "rewritten_text": "The tempest howled throughout the night, yet nothing unusual happened. By morning, however, the gruesome bloodstain had returned to the floor. Washington, having exhausted his Paragon Detergent, concluded it must be the ghost. He scrubbed it away, but it reappeared the following day, and again the third, despite Mr. Otis locking the library and keeping the key. The family grew intrigued; Mr. Otis reconsidered his skepticism, Mrs. Otis planned to join the Psychical Society, and Washington drafted a treatise on the persistence of bloodstains linked to crimes. That night, all skepticism regarding ghosts vanished. After a sunny day, the family enjoyed an evening drive, returning at nine for a light supper. They avoided any talk of spirits, focusing instead on typical topics for refined Americans: the acting prowess of Fanny Davenport, the scarcity of American staples like hominy in England, the cultural significance of Boston, the efficiency of baggage checks, and the merits of the New York accent. No one mentioned the supernatural or Sir Simon de Canterville. By eleven-thirty, the house was dark. Later, Mr. Otis was roused by a metallic clanking in the hallway. He rose, lit a match, and noted it was one o’clock. Remaining composed, he checked his steady pulse. As the clanking and footsteps persisted, he donned his slippers, grabbed a small phial from his kit, and stepped into the corridor."
}

