  "request_id": "f2fdfea0057ef51215f64efb",
  "rewritten_text": "Then, we constructed a wire barrier. A wire barrier? Indeed. You suggested it yourself a few years back. Ah, I recall—when the Church first challenged us and decided to delay for a better opportunity. How did you set it up? I ran twelve heavy, uninsulated wires from a cave dynamo, which lacks brushes save for a positive and negative one. Correct. The wires exit the cave to enclose a hundred-yard circle of flat land, forming twelve separate rings spaced ten feet apart, returning to the cave at their ends. Proceed. The wires are secured to sturdy oak posts spaced three feet apart, buried five feet deep. That is quite robust. Yes. The wires lack external ground connections. They emerge from the positive brush, while the negative brush provides the ground; the wire ends return to the cave and are grounded separately. No, that is inefficient. Why? It wastes power. You only need the ground connection via the negative brush. Each wire must return to the cave independently without grounding. Consider the savings: a cavalry charge hits the fence, yet you expend no power until the horses touch the wire, completing the circuit through the ground and falling instantly. Your energy remains stored like a loaded gun, costing nothing until triggered. Of course, I missed that. It is cheaper and more effective, as broken wires cause no issues. Especially with a cave monitor to disconnect damaged lines. Continue. The Gatlings? They are ready. I have mounted thirteen Gatling guns on a six-foot platform in the center, fully stocked with ammunition. Perfect. They cover every angle, and when the Church’s knights appear, it will be quite a spectacle. And the cliff above the cave? I have installed a wire fence and a Gatling gun there."
}

