parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7d8b5022334529f3f2a633e2",
  "rewritten_text": "To see a star clearly, one must look at it indirectly, using the outer edges of the retina, which are more sensitive to faint light. Looking directly at a star makes it appear dimmer. While more light hits the eye when staring straight on, the peripheral view offers a sharper perception. Excessive focus can weaken thought, just as staring too intently at Venus can make the planet disappear from the sky. Regarding these murders, let us investigate them ourselves before forming an opinion. It will be an interesting diversion, and Le Bon once did me a favor I have not forgotten. We will inspect the scene. I know the Prefect of Police and can easily get permission. Permission granted, we went to the Rue Morgue, a dreary street between Rue Richelieu and Rue St. Roch. It was late when we arrived, as it was far from our home. The house was easy to find, as a crowd still lingered across the street, staring at the closed shutters. It was a typical Parisian building with a concierge booth by the gate. Before entering, we walked around the block, with Dupin examining the neighborhood and the house with a level of detail I found inexplicable. Returning to the front, we rang the bell, showed our credentials, and were admitted by the police. We went upstairs to the room where the body of Mademoiselle L’Espanaye had been discovered and where the victims still lay. The room remained in its original state of disarray. I saw nothing that had not already been reported in the newspapers. Dupin examined everything, including the bodies. We then inspected the other rooms and the yard, accompanied by a gendarme throughout."
}

