parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "35c9cfc27ff46fdfaa66401f",
  "rewritten_text": "I must warn you that I intend to tell Sybil everything. She is joining me for lunch tomorrow to discuss hats, and if Mr. Podgers discovers you have a short temper, gout, or a secret wife in Bayswater, I will certainly inform her. Lord Arthur smiled and shook his head. I am not afraid, he replied. Sybil knows me as well as I know her. I am sorry to hear that. A successful marriage requires mutual misunderstanding. I am not being cynical, merely experienced, which is essentially the same. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile wants his palm read. Do not mention his engagement to one of London's most beautiful women, as that was in the Morning Post a month ago. Dear Lady Windermere, the Marchioness of Jedburgh cried, let Mr. Podgers stay. He just told me I should be an actress, and I am fascinated. If he said that, I am taking him away. Come here, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur’s hand. Lady Jedburgh pouted as she rose. If I cannot be an actress, I must at least be in the audience. We all will be, Lady Windermere said. Now, Mr. Podgers, tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is a favorite of mine. When Mr. Podgers examined Lord Arthur’s hand, he turned pale and fell silent. A shudder passed through him, and his bushy eyebrows twitched in that strange, irritating way they did when he was confused. Beads of sweat broke out on his yellow forehead like poisonous dew, and his fingers turned cold and clammy. Lord Arthur noticed this agitation and felt fear for the first time. He wanted to flee the room but forced himself to stay. It was better to know the worst than to endure this agonizing uncertainty. I am waiting, Mr. Podgers, he said. We are all waiting, Lady Windermere added impatiently, but the palmist remained silent. I suspect Arthur is going on the stage, Lady Jedburgh remarked, and Mr. Podgers is afraid to tell him after your scolding."
}

