  "rewritten_text": "What is the matter? Ten o’clock, sir. What do you mean? Ten at night? Yes, sir. You told me to wake you. Oh, I remember. Very well. After failing to fall back asleep while his servant noisily stirred the fire, he got up, grabbed his hat, and left. He walked to the Temple, paced the pavement twice to wake himself up, and headed to Stryver’s chambers. The clerk had already left, so Stryver opened the door himself, wearing slippers and a loose robe. He had the strained, bloodshot eyes common to men who live as hard as he did. You are a bit late, Memory, Stryver said. About the usual time, maybe fifteen minutes late. They entered a messy, book-lined room with a roaring fire. A kettle steamed on the stove, and a table was covered with wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons. I see you have had your bottle, Sydney. Two tonight, I think. I was dining with today’s client, or watching him eat, which is the same thing. That was a clever point you made about the identification, Sydney. How did you think of it? I just thought he was a handsome fellow and that I would be like him if I had any luck. Stryver laughed, shaking his belly. You and your luck, Sydney! Get to work. Looking annoyed, the jackal loosened his clothes, went to the next room, and returned with a jug of cold water, a basin, and some towels. He soaked the towels, wrung them out, wrapped them around his head, and sat down. Now I am ready! Not much to do tonight, Memory, Stryver said cheerfully while sorting his papers. How much? Only two sets. Give me the worst one first. There they are, Sydney. Get to it! Stryver relaxed on a sofa by the table, while Sydney sat at his own desk, surrounded by papers and bottles."
}

