parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ae15685109ff5c0cdf085709",
  "rewritten_text": "The studio smelled of roses, and as a summer breeze moved through the garden, the heavy scent of lilacs and the delicate fragrance of flowering thorn drifted through the open door. From his spot on the Persian divan, where he lay smoking his usual endless cigarettes, Lord Henry Wotton could see the honey-colored laburnum blossoms, their branches swaying under the weight of such vibrant beauty. Occasionally, the shadows of birds passed over the silk curtains covering the large window, creating a fleeting, artistic effect that reminded him of Japanese painters who capture motion through static art. The low hum of bees in the tall grass made the silence feel heavier, while the distant sound of London resembled the deep tone of an organ. In the center of the room, a full-length portrait of a remarkably handsome young man stood on an easel. Nearby sat the artist, Basil Hallward, whose mysterious disappearance years ago had once caused such public fascination. As Basil looked at the graceful figure he had captured, he smiled with satisfaction. Suddenly, he stood up and covered his eyes, as if trying to hold onto a dream he feared might vanish. It is your finest work, Basil, Lord Henry said lazily. You must send it to the Grosvenor next year. The Academy is too crowded and common; I can never see the art for the people, or the people for the art. The Grosvenor is the only suitable choice. I do not think I will send it anywhere, Basil replied, tossing his head back in the peculiar way that used to amuse his friends at Oxford."
}

