parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f16662189ad8cd12a18667ce",
  "rewritten_text": "Jane admitted she was surprised by the engagement, yet she spoke less of her shock than of her sincere hope for their happiness, refusing to let Elizabeth convince her it was unlikely. Kitty and Lydia felt no envy toward Miss Lucas, as Mr. Collins was merely a clergyman; to them, it was simply gossip to share in Meryton. Lady Lucas could not hide her triumph in finally being able to boast to Mrs. Bennet about a daughter well-married. She visited Longbourn more frequently than usual to express her joy, despite Mrs. Bennet’s bitter glares and spiteful comments. A new reserve grew between Elizabeth and Charlotte, leaving them silent on the matter, and Elizabeth felt certain their friendship could never regain its former intimacy. Disappointed in Charlotte, she turned with deeper affection toward her sister, whose integrity she trusted completely, though she grew increasingly anxious for Jane’s happiness, as Bingley had been gone a week with no word of his return. Jane had replied promptly to Caroline’s letter and was counting the days until she might hear back. On Tuesday, Mr. Collins sent a letter of thanks to their father, written with the heavy formality of someone who had lived with them for a year. Having fulfilled that duty, he wrote rapturously of his joy in winning the affection of their neighbor, Miss Lucas. He explained that he had accepted their invitation to return to Longbourn on Monday fortnight specifically to enjoy her company. He added that Lady Catherine heartily approved of the marriage and wished it to happen quickly, which he hoped would convince his dear Charlotte to set an early wedding date. Mr. Collins’s return to Hertfordshire no longer pleased Mrs. Bennet; she was as eager to complain about it as her husband. She found it bizarre and highly inconvenient that he would visit Longbourn rather than Lucas Lodge. She detested having guests while her health was poor, and she found lovers to be the most tiresome people of all."
}

