parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dbd720308a1e458f22b65e90",
  "rewritten_text": "It was after two when a sudden turn brought me to the lake, near a cove where a small skiff lay anchored. Near our home, I spotted my father and aunt strolling on the grass. I climbed into the boat, and being practiced in such things, shoved off and rowed with all my might. Clad in white with only my tartan rachan draped over me, my hair flowing loose, I propelled the craft with such surprising speed that my father later remarked I resembled a spirit rather than a girl. As I neared the bank, he caught the boat, and I sprang out into his embrace. My life truly began then. Everything shifted from monotonous gloom to vibrant joy. The happiness of being with my father surpassed my wildest hopes. We were inseparable, our conversations endless. Having spent sixteen years away in lands largely unknown to Europe—wandering through Persia, Arabia, and northern India—he had moved among the locals with rare freedom. His tales of their customs, his stories, and his vivid descriptions filled our hours when we were not discussing our own future. The sound of affection was so novel that I listened raptly as he spoke of his feelings during those long years of seeming neglect. He explained that while he initially could not bear to think of me, his grief eventually gave way to hope. Amidst deserts and cities, he imagined my small, fairy-like form. The northern breeze felt sweeter, as if it carried my spirit. He had often dreamed of returning to take me to some peaceful, fertile island. As he finally traveled home, his fervent hopes were plagued by agonizing fears; he dreaded that the sun and moon might shine upon my grave rather than my living self. Yet, he concluded, he had found his Mathilda, his comfort and his hope."
}

