parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d219be0e17439af74ea3ac28",
  "rewritten_text": "I bored a hole into the keg above the pitcher and inserted a tapered wooden plug. After some testing, I adjusted the fit so that water would drip into the pitcher, filling it exactly in one hour. This was easily verified by checking the water level over time. My bed was positioned so that my head lay directly beneath the pitcher’s spout. Once the pitcher filled, the overflow would spill from the spout, falling four feet onto my face and waking me from even the deepest sleep. By eleven, everything was ready, and I went to bed confident in my device. It worked perfectly; every hour, my reliable alarm woke me. I would empty the pitcher back into the keg and return to sleep. These interruptions were less bothersome than expected, and when I finally woke for the day at seven, the sun was already high. April 3rd. The balloon had reached a massive altitude, and the earth’s curve was clearly visible. Below, a cluster of black specks in the ocean appeared to be islands. To the north, a brilliant white line on the horizon suggested the ice of the Polar Sea. I was eager to travel further north, perhaps even directly over the Pole, though I regretted that my extreme height would hinder a detailed survey."
}

