parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cd2d9ea80f9b83d2df3dfd97",
  "rewritten_text": "Rising early and once more evading my creditors, I hurried to the bookstore to spend my remaining cash on texts regarding mechanics and practical astronomy. Back home, I studied them constantly, quickly gaining the expertise needed for my plan. Meanwhile, I worked to appease my three persistent creditors. I finally succeeded by selling half my furniture to pay part of the debt and promising the rest upon the completion of a project for which I requested their help. Being simple men, they were easily persuaded. Once settled, I secretly sold my remaining property with my wife's help and borrowed significant sums under various pretenses, ignoring how I would ever repay them. With these funds, I bought fine cambric muslin, twine, rubber varnish, a custom wicker basket, and other materials for a massive balloon. I instructed my wife on how to assemble it while I wove the netting and prepared the rigging. I also purchased a quadrant, compass, telescope, modified barometer, and two obscure astronomical tools. Under cover of night, I transported five fifty-gallon iron-bound casks, a larger one, six ten-foot tin tubes, a specific semi-metal, and twelve jugs of common acid to a secluded spot east of Rotterdam. The gas produced from these materials is unique to my work, or at least never used this way before. I would gladly reveal the secret, but it rightfully belongs to a man in Nantes, France, who shared it with me under specific conditions."
}

