parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4787646b970b5014cc775fe7",
  "rewritten_text": "He jumped up and grabbed her arm. \"Show him to me. Which one is he? Point him out. I have to see him!\" he demanded. Just then, the Duke of Berwick’s carriage blocked their view, and by the time it passed, the other vehicle had vanished. \"He is gone,\" Sibyl murmured sadly. \"I wish you had seen him.\" \"I wish I had, because as surely as there is a God, if he ever hurts you, I will kill him.\" She stared at him in horror. He repeated the threat, his words sharp as a blade. Bystanders began to stare, and a woman nearby giggled. \"Come away, Jim,\" she whispered. He followed her through the crowd, feeling satisfied with his warning. By the Achilles Statue, she turned to him. Her eyes held pity, though her lips curved into a smile. She shook her head. \"You are foolish, Jim; just a bad-tempered boy. How can you say such horrible things? You are just jealous and unkind. I wish you would fall in love; it makes people good, and what you said was wicked.\" \"I am sixteen,\" he replied, \"and I know what I am doing. Mother is no help to you; she doesn't know how to look after you. I wish I weren't going to Australia. I would quit if my papers weren't already signed.\" \"Don't be so serious,\" she said. \"You sound like a hero from one of Mother’s silly melodramas. I won't fight with you. Seeing him is perfect happiness. You wouldn't harm anyone I love, would you?\" \"Not as long as you love him, I suppose,\" he muttered. \"I will love him forever!\" she cried. \"And he?\" \"Forever, too!\" \"He had better.\" She pulled away, then laughed and touched his arm, dismissing him as just a boy. At Marble Arch, they caught a bus toward their shabby home on Euston Road. It was past five, and Sibyl needed to rest before her performance. Jim insisted on it, wanting to say goodbye without their mother present to cause a scene, which he detested."
}

