parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2a6c0855aa425517c2c1d762",
  "rewritten_text": "There was no joy in the small town that quiet Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly’s family were in deep mourning, filled with grief and tears. An unusual silence hung over the village, which was already peaceful enough. The residents went about their business distractedly, speaking little and sighing often. The Saturday holiday felt like a burden to the children, who lost interest in their games and eventually stopped playing altogether. That afternoon, Becky Thatcher wandered around the empty schoolyard, feeling deeply depressed. Finding no comfort there, she whispered to herself, \"If only I still had that brass andiron knob. Now I have nothing to remember him by.\" She choked back a sob. She paused and added, \"It happened right here. If I could do it over, I would never say that—not for anything. But he is gone, and I will never see him again.\" Overcome, she walked away with tears streaming down her face. Soon, a group of Tom and Joe’s friends gathered by the fence, speaking in hushed tones about the last time they saw the boys. They pointed out exactly where the missing lads had stood, recalling small, prophetic details. Each child described their own position, claiming they felt a strange, chilling sensation at the time, which they only now understood. A debate broke out over who had seen the boys last. Those who could prove they had spoken to them last gained a sense of solemn importance, envied by the others. One boy, lacking any other claim to fame, said proudly, \"Well, Tom Sawyer beat me up once.\" The attempt at glory failed, as most of the boys could say the same. The group drifted away, still whispering memories of the lost boys."
}

