parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "783d76600b31afb87f3bebb5",
  "rewritten_text": "“Tom!” No answer. “TOM!” No answer. “Where has that boy gone? You, TOM!” No answer. The old lady lowered her glasses to peer around the room, then pushed them up to look underneath. She rarely looked through them for something as small as a boy; they were for show, not for seeing. She looked confused, then said, loud enough for the furniture to hear, “Well, if I catch you, I’ll—” She stopped to poke under the bed with her broom, needing her breath for the effort. She found only the cat. “I have never seen the likes of that boy!” She went to the door and looked out at the garden. No Tom. She shouted, “Y-o-u-u TOM!” A noise behind her made her turn just in time to grab a small boy by his jacket. “I should have thought of the closet. What were you doing in there?” “Nothing.” “Nothing! Look at your hands and your mouth. What is that?” “I don’t know, Aunt.” “Well, I know. It’s jam. I’ve told you forty times to leave that jam alone. Hand me that switch.” As the switch hovered in the air, the boy cried, “Look behind you, Aunt!” The old lady spun around, and the boy immediately scrambled over the high fence and vanished. Aunt Polly stood surprised, then laughed softly. “Darn the boy, will I never learn? Hasn’t he played enough tricks for me to be ready? But old fools are the biggest fools. You can’t teach an old dog new tricks. Still, he never plays the same trick twice, so how is a person to know what’s coming? He seems to know exactly how long he can annoy me before I get angry, and he knows that if he can distract me or make me laugh, my anger fades and I can’t hit him.”"
}

