  "rewritten_text": "Yes, burglar’s tools. That seems to put you at ease. But what startled you? What were you expecting us to find? Huck was in a tight spot. The man’s gaze was fixed on him, and he would have given anything for a believable excuse. Nothing came to mind, and as the man’s eyes bored into him, he blurted out a nonsensical reply: Sunday-school books, maybe. Huck was too anxious to smile, but the old man laughed heartily, shaking with mirth, and remarked that such a laugh was worth a fortune for the health benefits. He added, Poor boy, you look pale and exhausted. You aren’t well at all, which explains why you seem a bit rattled. You will recover soon. Rest and sleep will set you right. Huck felt foolish for showing such suspicious nerves. He had stopped believing the parcel from the tavern was the treasure after hearing the conversation at the widow’s gate, but he hadn’t been certain. The mention of the bundle had caught him off guard. Still, he was relieved to know for sure that the package was not the treasure, which put his mind at ease. Everything seemed to be falling into place; the gold must still be in room number two, the men would be arrested that day, and he and Tom could claim the treasure that night without fear. Just as breakfast ended, there was a knock at the door. Huck scrambled to hide, wanting no part in the recent events. The Welshman let in several guests, including the Widow Douglas, and saw crowds of people climbing the hill to look at the gate. The news had spread. The Welshman recounted the night’s events to the visitors. The widow expressed her deep gratitude for being saved. Don’t mention it, madam, he replied. There is someone else you owe more to than me and my boys, but he doesn’t want his name revealed. We wouldn’t have been there if not for him."
}

