parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a4c76fb4b0e29c97664c4686",
  "rewritten_text": "I hurried toward my college, suddenly chilled by the realization that the creature I had abandoned in my rooms might still be roaming there. Terrified of encountering the monster—and even more fearful that Henry might see him—I asked my friend to wait downstairs while I rushed up. My hand gripped the lock before I hesitated, overcome by a freezing tremor. I flung the door open with the frantic energy of a child expecting a ghost, but the room was vacant. My bedroom was likewise deserted. Scarcely daring to believe in such luck, I confirmed my enemy had vanished and raced back down to Clerval, clapping my hands in relief. We went up to my room, but as the servant served breakfast, I could not compose myself. Overwhelmed by a frantic sensitivity and a racing pulse, I paced restlessly, leaping over furniture and laughing with a hollow, wild intensity. Clerval initially mistook my behavior for excitement at his arrival, but my erratic eyes and jarring laughter soon filled him with alarm. My dear Victor, he pleaded, what is wrong? You are clearly unwell. Do not laugh like that. I covered my eyes, screaming for him to save me as I hallucinated the monster entering the room. I struggled against an imagined grasp until I collapsed in a fit. Poor Clerval; the reunion he had anticipated with such delight had turned into a nightmare. I remained unconscious, oblivious to his distress, for a long time. This marked the start of a nervous fever that kept me bedridden for months. Throughout my illness, Henry acted as my sole caretaker. I later discovered that he had shielded my father and Elizabeth from the truth of my condition, sparing them the agony of knowing how severe my breakdown had become."
}

