  "rewritten_text": "If you want one for personal use, I can provide an excellent model and guarantee your satisfaction. May I ask who it is for? If it is for the police or anyone at Scotland Yard, I cannot help. English detectives are our best allies; their incompetence allows us to act freely, and I would hate to lose them. Lord Arthur replied that it had nothing to do with the police, as the clock was intended for the Dean of Chichester. Herr Winckelkopf remarked that he was surprised by Lord Arthur's religious devotion, which was rare for a young man. Lord Arthur blushed and admitted he knew nothing of theology. When the man asked if it was a private matter, Lord Arthur confirmed it was. Winckelkopf left and returned with a small cake of dynamite and a French clock topped with an ormolu figure of Liberty. Lord Arthur was pleased and asked how it functioned. Winckelkopf explained that he would set the timer for the exact moment of explosion. Lord Arthur requested it be set for Friday at noon, when the Dean would be home. Winckelkopf noted this in his ledger. When Lord Arthur asked for the cost, Winckelkopf replied that the dynamite was seven shillings and sixpence, the clock three pounds ten, and shipping five shillings, noting he was happy to help a friend of Count Rouvaloff. Lord Arthur asked about his labor costs."
}

