parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f9829ffe20dc1015e9824598",
  "rewritten_text": "The ringmaster gave a short speech, asking for no more trouble and agreeing to let the man ride if he could stay on. The crowd laughed, and the man climbed on. Immediately, the horse began bucking and leaping. Two circus workers tried to hold the bridle while the drunk man clung to the horse's neck, his legs flying wildly. The audience stood and cheered until they were crying with laughter. Eventually, the horse broke free and galloped around the ring with the man flopping against its neck. I felt terrified for him, but soon he pulled himself upright, grabbed the reins, and stood tall on the horse's back. He looked perfectly steady, as if he had never been drunk. He began stripping off his clothes, tossing seventeen suits into the air. Underneath, he was slim, handsome, and wearing a brilliant costume. He whipped the horse to a faster pace before leaping off, bowing, and dancing away while the crowd roared with delight. The ringmaster realized he had been tricked by one of his own performers, who had planned the stunt in secret. The ringmaster looked miserable, and I felt foolish for being fooled, though I would not have traded places with him for anything. It was the best circus I had ever seen, and I would gladly pay to see it again."
}

