parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "57858955f6be185ec9fe2ee4",
  "rewritten_text": "Daylight arrived as I set the manuscript down. The rain had nearly stopped, leaving the world gray and somber as the spent storm sighed into silence. I approached the stranger's room and listened at his partially open door. Hearing him speak, I knocked, but received no reply. I peered inside to find him lying on his back, rambling with feverish intensity while his arms moved restlessly. I entered quietly and leaned over him. When I spoke his name, his glassy eyes and pale face suddenly brightened with joy and relief. Oh, Sandy, you have finally arrived; I have missed you so much. Stay beside me and never leave again. Give me your hand; holding it brings me peace and happiness. You appear as a faint mist, yet your presence is enough. Do not pull away, for I will not need it for long. Was that the child? Hello, Central? She does not answer. Bring her to me when she wakes so I may say goodbye. Sandy, are you still there? I drifted off and feared you had vanished. Have I been ill for a long time? It feels like months. I have had such terrifyingly vivid dreams. In my delirium, I imagined the king had died, you were trapped in Gaul, and a revolution had broken out. I even dreamt that Clarence and I, with a few cadets, wiped out the entire English chivalry. Stranger still, I felt like a man from a distant, future age. I seemed to have traveled back and forth across time, stranded as a lonely outsider in that strange England, separated from you and everything I hold dear by thirteen centuries."
}

