parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "348b1b1160253a26eec9e8fe",
  "rewritten_text": "I am not eating at home, so I will not need you. Thank you, sir, the man replied, leaving the room. Now, Alan, we must hurry. This chest is heavy, so I will carry it. You bring the rest. He spoke quickly and with authority. Campbell felt pressured by him, and they left together. When they reached the landing, Dorian unlocked the door, then hesitated, looking troubled and shivering. I do not think I can go in, Alan, he whispered. It makes no difference to me, Campbell said coldly. Dorian opened the door slightly. He saw his portrait leering in the sunlight, with the torn curtain lying on the floor. He realized he had forgotten to cover the painting the night before and started to rush forward, but pulled back in horror. A wet, red substance glistened on the hand of the portrait, as if it were sweating blood. It was more terrifying than the silent body on the table, whose distorted shadow on the carpet showed it had not moved. Taking a deep breath, Dorian opened the door wider and walked in with his eyes half-closed and his head turned away, determined not to look at the corpse. He picked up the gold-and-purple cloth and threw it over the painting. He stood there, afraid to turn around, staring at the fabric pattern. He heard Campbell bring in the heavy chest and the tools needed for his grim task. He wondered if Campbell and Basil Hallward had ever met. Leave me now, a stern voice said behind him. Dorian turned and hurried out, catching a glimpse of the body pushed back into the chair and Campbell staring at a pale, yellow face. As he went downstairs, he heard the lock turn. It was well past seven when Campbell returned to the library. He looked pale but calm. I have done what you asked, he muttered."
}

