parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e78c2758ecdf910958e4e8a6",
  "rewritten_text": "I considered faking my death; my satisfied heirs would claim my fortune, and I would finally be free. Yet, the plan required careful execution; I could not be left penniless, so I had to secure funds. What wretched measures I was forced to take! A life of deception seemed my only path. Whenever guilt made me hesitate, a visit from an aunt or cousin would solidify my resolve. They would claim death is inevitable, then suggest my father had lost his mind since my mother’s passing. They called him insane and told me I was lucky he had not killed me during one of his episodes. They spoke with delicate, whispered hints, downcast eyes, and sympathetic whimpers. I listened calmly while trembling inside, unable to challenge their cruel words. What a delightful life, entirely devoid of honesty! I wore a gentle expression while harboring a cunning heart, feeling only the shame of my lies rather than the comfort of innocence. I, who once valued sincerity, now had to adopt a deceptive persona. It felt awkward at first, but I learned to lie with grace, staining my soul until its true nature was hidden. Oh, beloved father, accept your daughter’s pure heart; let me join you untainted, or you will not recognize me. Just as grief transformed Constance, deceit has changed me, until you might say in heaven, “This is not my child.” To be happy now and when we reunite, I must escape this mocking life. Only in solitude can I be myself and belong to you. Even now, I look back with disgust at the schemes I used to secure my independence and stage my own death. I could detail the painful struggles I endured to ensure my survival and convince the world I had perished, but I will not."
}

