  "rewritten_text": "The two ladies expressed great joy at reuniting with their friend, remarking on how long it had been and inquiring about her recent activities. They largely ignored the rest of the family, steering clear of Mrs. Bennet, speaking little to Elizabeth, and saying nothing to the others. They departed quickly, rising with a suddenness that startled their brother and rushing away as if desperate to avoid Mrs. Bennet’s hospitality. The upcoming Netherfield ball delighted every woman in the house. Mrs. Bennet viewed it as a tribute to her eldest daughter, feeling honored that Mr. Bingley had extended the invitation personally. Jane looked forward to a pleasant evening with her friends and their brother, while Elizabeth anticipated dancing with Mr. Wickham and observing Mr. Darcy’s conduct. Catherine and Lydia were excited simply by the prospect of a ball, intending to dance with Mr. Wickham but remaining open to other partners. Even Mary expressed her approval, noting that she found occasional social engagements acceptable as long as her mornings remained private. Elizabeth, in high spirits, asked Mr. Collins if he planned to attend and dance. She was surprised to learn he had no moral objections and feared no disapproval from his patrons. He assured her that a ball hosted by a respectable gentleman was perfectly appropriate, adding that he intended to dance with all his cousins and specifically requested the first two dances with Elizabeth, hoping Jane would understand his choice."
}

