  "rewritten_text": "Next, we built a wire fence. A wire fence? Yes. You suggested it yourself a few years ago. I remember now, back when the Church first challenged us and decided to wait for a better opportunity. How did you set it up? I ran twelve heavy, uninsulated wires from a large dynamo in the cave. The wires extend from the cave to enclose a flat area one hundred yards across. They form twelve separate circular fences, spaced ten feet apart, with both ends returning to the cave. The wires are attached to sturdy oak posts, spaced three feet apart and buried five feet deep. That sounds strong. The wires have no ground connection outside the cave. They run from the positive terminal, while the negative terminal is grounded. Each wire returns to the cave and is grounded separately. No, that is inefficient. You should only use the ground connection at the negative terminal. Bring the other ends back to the cave without any ground connection. This way, you save power. When a cavalry charge hits the fence, you are not wasting energy. The electricity waits like a loaded gun, costing nothing until the horses touch the wire and complete the circuit through the ground, killing them instantly. I see, that is much cheaper and more effective, especially if the wires break. Exactly, and we can monitor them from the cave. What about the Gatling guns? I have placed thirteen of them on a six-foot platform in the center of the inner circle, with plenty of ammunition. Perfect. They cover every approach, and when the Church’s knights arrive, they will face a deadly surprise. What about the cliff above the cave? I have installed a wire fence and a Gatling gun there as well."
