  "rewritten_text": "I cannot express the immense joy I felt returning from political turmoil and foreign hardships to the sanctuary of my own home, a place defined by goodness, love, and shared empathy. Had I never left Windsor, these feelings might be less profound, but my time in Greece was marked by fear and the tragic loss of two individuals who embodied greatness. Such misery could not touch the domestic life we cultivated in our secluded forest. While time inevitably left its mark on our expectations, life remained tranquil. Idris, a devoted wife and sister, was a deeply passionate mother. We had three children, but the loss of our second child while I was in Greece shattered her sense of security. Previously, she viewed our children as guaranteed, but now she lived in constant terror that death would claim the others. Any minor illness triggered panic, and she was miserable whenever separated from them, guarding her happiness like a treasure against a thief. Fortunately, her fears were largely unfounded. Alfred, now nine, was an upright, manly boy with a radiant expression and a gentle, independent spirit. Our youngest was still an infant, but he was healthy and his constant energy filled our home with laughter. Clara had outgrown the age that caused Idris such anxiety. She was cherished by everyone for her rare combination of intelligence, innocence, and grace; she was truly the jewel of our family. At the start of winter, nine-year-old Alfred began his studies at Eton. He viewed this as a significant step toward manhood and was appropriately excited. Engaging in shared studies and play further developed his character, highlighting his perseverance, generosity, and steady resolve."
}

