  "request_id": "8059cc0da999bff030e29185",
  "rewritten_text": "Regarding that matter, I shall remain silent, and you may rest assured that no unkind words will escape me once we are wed. Elizabeth felt compelled to interject. You are moving too quickly, sir, she exclaimed. You overlook the fact that I have not yet responded. Let me do so immediately. I thank you for your flattering offer, but I must decline. Mr. Collins waved his hand formally and replied that he was well aware young women often reject suitors they intend to accept, sometimes doing so repeatedly. Consequently, he remained undeterred and expected to marry her soon. Elizabeth insisted that his hope was strange given her clear refusal. She assured him she was not the type of woman to gamble her happiness on a second proposal. She was entirely serious; he could not make her happy, and she was certain she would be a poor match for him. Furthermore, she believed Lady Catherine would find her unsuitable. Mr. Collins solemnly disagreed, stating he doubted Lady Catherine would disapprove and promised to praise Elizabeth's modesty and virtues when he next saw her. Elizabeth urged him to cease such praise and trust her word. She wished him wealth and happiness, noting that by declining his hand, she was ensuring he would not be miserable. She added that he had fulfilled his duty to her family and could inherit Longbourn without guilt, considering the matter closed. As she rose to leave, Mr. Collins spoke again, stating he expected a more favorable response when he next addressed the subject. He claimed he did not view her refusal as cruelty, as he understood it was customary for women to decline an initial proposal, and he believed she had already offered as much encouragement as propriety allowed."
}

