parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5e3b4ba5484bac466ede5767",
  "rewritten_text": "I told myself I did not want to be involved in any of this. When I went downstairs the next morning, the parlor was closed and the people keeping watch were gone. Only the family, the Widow Bartley, and our group remained. I studied their faces for clues, but I could not tell if anything had happened. By midday, the undertaker and his assistant arrived. They placed the coffin on chairs in the center of the room and arranged our chairs in rows, borrowing more from neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were filled. I noticed the coffin lid was positioned as it had been before, but I was too afraid to look inside while others were present. People began to arrive, and the con artists and the girls sat in the front row near the coffin. For half an hour, the guests walked by in a single line to look at the dead man’s face. Some shed tears, and the room remained quiet and solemn, save for the girls and the men sobbing into their handkerchiefs. The only other sounds were the shuffling of feet and the blowing of noses, which always happens more at funerals than anywhere else except church. Once the room was packed, the undertaker moved about in his black gloves, making final adjustments with a quiet, soothing manner. He was as silent as a cat, guiding people and making space with only nods and hand gestures. He stood against the wall, the most stealthy and humorless man I have ever seen. They had borrowed a wheezy old organ, and when a young woman began to play it, the sound was squeaky and unpleasant. Everyone joined in to sing, though I thought Peter was the only one who sounded good. Then, Reverend Hobson began to speak slowly and solemnly, but suddenly, a terrible noise erupted from the cellar. It was only one dog, but he made such a powerful racket that he would not stop. The preacher had to stand there over the coffin and wait, as the noise made it impossible to hear anything else."
}

