  "request_id": "78f7d42f9a62a2f53f3f233c",
  "rewritten_text": "He also considered Sybil’s old-fashioned parents, who might oppose the marriage if a scandal occurred. Still, he felt sure they would understand his motives if he explained everything. He decided that poison was the best choice; it was safe, certain, and quiet, avoiding the painful scenes that he, like most Englishmen, deeply disliked. Knowing nothing about poisons, he searched the library himself after the waiter failed to help. He found a medical textbook and a copy of Erskine’s Toxicology. The technical language confused him, and he regretted not studying harder at Oxford, but he eventually found a clear description of aconitine. It was fast, painless, and easy to take in a gelatin capsule. He noted the lethal dose on his shirt cuff, returned the books, and walked to a prominent chemist. The owner, Mr. Pestle, was surprised by the request and mentioned needing a medical certificate. Lord Arthur explained that the poison was for a Norwegian mastiff showing signs of rabies that had already bitten his coachman. Satisfied by this excuse, the chemist praised Lord Arthur’s knowledge and prepared the dose. Lord Arthur placed the capsule into a silver candy box he bought on Bond Street, discarded the chemist’s container, and drove to Lady Clementina’s."
}

