parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b51e58add2f8aedbd3043290",
  "rewritten_text": "The shock and confusion I felt at this sudden shift in circumstances were perhaps the most difficult parts of the experience to explain. The reversal was natural and inevitable, long anticipated for the moment my balloon reached the point where the moon’s gravity overcame the earth’s. Waking from a deep sleep, I was startled by a phenomenon I knew would happen, though not exactly when. The transition must have been gradual; even had I been awake, I doubt I would have felt any physical sensation of the inversion. Once I regained my senses and moved past the initial terror, I focused entirely on the moon’s appearance. It lay below like a map, and though still distant, its surface features were remarkably clear. The total lack of oceans, lakes, or rivers was the most striking geological detail. Strangely, I saw vast, flat alluvial plains, though most of the visible hemisphere was covered in countless conical volcanic mountains that looked almost artificial. The tallest rose less than four miles high, but a map of the Phlegraean Fields would give you a better sense of the terrain than I could describe. Most were actively erupting, and I was terrified by the frequent, thunderous roar of volcanic debris rushing past my balloon with increasing intensity."
}

