  "request_id": "dc0c3ff9f0278f5e5c5b4493",
  "rewritten_text": "What kind of life would they lead while his future remained so uncertain? He was determined that the wedding must be delayed. Although he loved Sybil deeply—and the mere touch of her hand thrilled him—he clearly understood his duty. He knew he had no right to marry her until he had committed the murder. Once that was done, he could stand at the altar with her, free from the fear of wrongdoing. He could hold her, knowing she would never have to feel ashamed of him. It had to be done, and the sooner, the better. Many men might have chosen pleasure over duty, but Lord Arthur was too principled for that. His love for Sybil was more than just passion; she represented everything good and noble. Though he felt a brief moment of natural hesitation, it quickly faded. His heart told him this was a sacrifice, not a sin, and his reason confirmed there was no other way. He had to choose between his own desires and the needs of others. While the task was terrible, he refused to let selfishness win. Everyone faces such choices eventually, but it came to him early, before his character could be hardened by cynicism or vanity. He did not hesitate. Fortunately, he was a practical man of action rather than a dreamer. He possessed the rare gift of common sense. The intense, chaotic emotions of the previous night had vanished. He looked back on his frantic wandering and emotional agony with a sense of shame. His past suffering now seemed unreal, and he wondered how he could have been so foolish as to despair over the inevitable."
}

