parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "895c642bdbe78c9dc932fe53",
  "rewritten_text": "Jupiter could barely contain his joy at the sight, but his master looked deeply disappointed. He urged us to keep digging, and I had barely started again when I tripped over a large iron ring half-buried in the loose soil. We began working in earnest, and I have never experienced ten minutes of such intense excitement. We unearthed an oblong wooden chest, preserved so well and hardened by some mineral process that it remained perfectly intact. The box measured three and a half feet long, three feet wide, and two and a half feet deep. It was reinforced with riveted iron bands forming a trellis pattern. Six iron rings, three on each side, allowed for a firm grip, yet our combined strength barely budged the heavy coffer. We realized moving it was impossible. Fortunately, the lid was held only by two sliding bolts. We pulled them back, trembling with anxiety. Instantly, a treasure of immense value was revealed. As our lantern light hit the pit, a dazzling glow erupted from a chaotic pile of gold and jewels. I cannot describe my feelings; amazement was the primary emotion. Legrand seemed exhausted and spoke little. Jupiter’s face turned as pale as possible, and he looked completely stunned. He fell to his knees, plunged his bare arms into the gold up to his elbows, and let them rest there as if bathing. Finally, he sighed and muttered to himself, \"And this all came from the gold-bug! The pretty gold-bug! The poor little gold-bug I treated so roughly! Aren't you ashamed of yourself? Answer me that!\" Eventually, I had to remind them that we needed to move the treasure. It was getting late, and we had to secure everything before dawn. We were all so disoriented that we spent a long time debating what to do next."
}

