  "rewritten_text": "He bowed and left. He saw no one in the street, which was a quiet, deserted side road. William Bird, a tailor, testified that he was among those who entered the house. An Englishman living in Paris for two years, he was one of the first to climb the stairs. He heard voices arguing. The gruff voice was French, and he clearly heard the words sacre and mon Dieu. He also heard sounds of a struggle, like scraping and scuffling. The shrill voice was louder than the gruff one and did not sound English; he thought it might be German or perhaps a woman's voice, though he does not speak German. Four witnesses confirmed that the door to Mademoiselle L.'s room was locked from the inside when they arrived. Everything was silent, with no groans or other noises. When they forced the door, the room was empty. The windows in both rooms were shut and locked from the inside. The door between the rooms was closed but unlocked, while the door to the hallway was locked with the key inside. A small, cluttered storage room at the end of the hall was searched thoroughly, as was every other part of the house. Chimneys were checked, and the roof trapdoor was found nailed shut and undisturbed for years. Witnesses estimated that between three and five minutes passed between hearing the argument and breaking down the door. Alfonzo Garcio, an undertaker living on the Rue Morgue, testified that he was part of the group but stayed downstairs due to his nervous disposition. He heard the argument and confirmed the gruff voice was French, but he was certain the shrill voice belonged to an Englishman."
}

