  "request_id": "377a964bca4d0db6284be7da",
  "rewritten_text": "She left everything behind, except the memory that such things had occurred. Anne had not visited Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell’s home in September. It had not been necessary, and she had managed to avoid the few opportunities she had to go to the Hall. Her return was meant to resume her place in the elegant rooms of the Lodge and please its mistress. Lady Russell felt some anxiety along with her joy at seeing Anne. She knew who had been visiting Uppercross. Fortunately, Anne looked healthier, or at least Lady Russell thought so. As Anne accepted these compliments, she amused herself by linking them to her cousin’s silent admiration, hoping she might enjoy a renewed sense of youth and beauty. As they talked, Anne realized her own perspective had shifted. The concerns that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, which she had felt ignored and forced to hide among the Musgroves, now seemed less important. She had recently lost interest in her father, her sister, and their life in Bath. Their affairs had been overshadowed by those at Uppercross. When Lady Russell discussed their old hopes and fears, mentioning her satisfaction with the house in Camden Place and her regret that Mrs. Clay was still with them, Anne felt ashamed of how much more she was thinking about Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and her new acquaintances there. The home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick felt far more interesting to her than her father’s house or her sister’s friendship with Mrs. Clay. She had to force herself to show equal concern for topics that should have been her priority. Their conversation felt slightly awkward when they turned to the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had heard the full story shortly after arriving the day before, but they still had to discuss it. She asked questions, expressed regret over the recklessness, and lamented the outcome. Both women had to mention Captain Wentworth’s name. Anne felt she handled it less naturally than Lady Russell. She could not say his name while looking directly at her until she decided to briefly share her thoughts on the attachment between him and Louisa."
}

