parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f4759f08a271f1f56d663873",
  "rewritten_text": "For years, Dorian Gray remained under the spell of that book, or perhaps he simply never wanted to escape it. He ordered nine large-paper copies from Paris, binding them in various colors to match his shifting moods and the impulses of a nature he often struggled to control. The protagonist, a fascinating Parisian who blended romanticism with science, felt like a mirror of his own future. The novel seemed to tell the story of his life before he had even lived it. In one respect, he was luckier than that fictional hero. He never suffered the strange fear of mirrors, polished metal, or still water that plagued the Parisian after his once-remarkable beauty began to fade. Dorian read the tragic conclusion with a sense of cruel delight, finding a dark satisfaction in the despair of a man who had lost what he valued most. Yet, the beauty that had captivated Basil Hallward and so many others never abandoned Dorian. Even those who heard scandalous rumors about his lifestyle in London clubs found it impossible to believe them when they saw him. He always appeared untouched by the world. Crude men fell silent in his presence, rebuked by the purity of his face, which reminded them of the innocence they had lost. They marveled at how someone so graceful could remain untainted by such a sordid, sensual age. Often, after returning from mysterious, long absences that fueled gossip among his supposed friends, he would sneak upstairs to his locked room. Using the key he kept constantly with him, he would stand before Basil’s portrait with a mirror, comparing the aging, wicked face on the canvas to the fair, laughing youth reflected in the glass."
}

