parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eab3ee2233955dd4aa895f87",
  "rewritten_text": "Meanwhile, gravitational force would steadily decrease following the inverse square law, causing my velocity to increase rapidly until I reached the regions where lunar attraction overcomes the earth's pull. Consequently, I deemed it unnecessary to carry more than forty days of provisions. Another concern troubled me slightly. It is well known that during high-altitude balloon flights, travelers suffer from respiratory distress, bodily discomfort, and nosebleeds, which worsen as they ascend. This was a daunting thought. Could these symptoms prove fatal? I ultimately concluded they would not. Their cause lies in the diminishing atmospheric pressure on the body, which distends superficial blood vessels, rather than any fundamental biological failure. Provided the blood is properly oxygenated, I saw no reason why life could not persist in a vacuum, as breathing is merely a muscular action. I believed that as my body adjusted to the lack of pressure, the pain would subside, and I trusted my robust constitution to endure the initial discomfort. Thus, your Excellencies, I have outlined some of the reasoning behind my lunar voyage. I shall now describe the outcome of this bold and unprecedented endeavor. Upon reaching an altitude of three and three-quarters miles, I released some feathers and observed that I continued to rise quickly, making it unnecessary to discard any ballast. I was pleased, as I intended to keep as much weight as possible for reasons I will explain later."
}

