parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7223f482ca370669942f7743",
  "rewritten_text": "My father was gone, and his memory remained the center of my existence. While I could feel gratitude toward others, I could no longer love or hope as I once had. Everything was suffering; even my moments of pleasure were merely endured rather than enjoyed. I felt like a lonely valley surrounded by steep, black cliffs, where no sunlight could reach and no path led to brighter lands. Although friendship offered temporary comfort, it could not heal me. It arrived like a gentle visitor, and when it left, I barely noticed the loss. My inner spirit was dead, so it should not surprise you that I did not welcome the gift of a friend with joy, nor did I mourn deeply when that friendship faded. My friend was named Woodville. I will briefly share his story so you can understand how cold my heart must have been to remain unmoved by his eloquence and sympathy, and how we might have comforted each other had my misery not turned me to stone. Woodville’s troubles were not as deep as mine; his grief was a natural sorrow that purified rather than destroyed, and he would eventually emerge from its shadow brighter than before. Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and was well-educated. He was one of the rare people favored by fortune, gifted with intellect and charm, and seemingly protected from even the smallest disappointment. His mind was pure, and his understanding was flawless. He was a brilliant poet, though that title hardly does him justice. He resembled the poets of antiquity, blessed from birth with divine inspiration. As he moved among others, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly light that set him apart. His striking beauty, the intensity of his gaze, and his captivating words left everyone around him in silent wonder, making them seem like mere attendants to his greatness."
}

