parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "269c588089192b002096ee05",
  "rewritten_text": "We both loved Adrian, though she, still a child, could not fully grasp his merits or share my deep connection to his ideas. I was constantly by his side. His gentle, sensitive nature lent a tender, ethereal quality to our talks. He was as joyful as a singing bird, as ambitious as an eagle, and as innocent as a dove. He could lighten Perdita’s seriousness and soothe my own restless, tortured spirit. I viewed my past struggles as a troubled dream, feeling as transformed as if I had entered a new body that perceived the world differently. Yet, I remained the same in my strength and my craving for connection. My masculine virtues endured, softened and humanized by his influence. Adrian taught me more than just history and philosophy; while helping me tame my reckless spirit, he revealed his own heart, allowing me to understand its remarkable depth. The former queen of England had tried since his infancy to instill ambition in her son. Recognizing his genius, she nurtured it to serve her own ends. She encouraged his thirst for knowledge and his courage, hoping his love of freedom would eventually turn into a desire for power. She tried to foster resentment toward those who had forced his father’s abdication, but she failed. Despite her biased accounts, he admired the nation’s choice to govern itself and became a republican by principle. Still, she did not despair. Combining his pride of birth with her own patience and ambition, she studied his character closely. She used praise and criticism to manipulate him, and though the results disappointed her, she relied on his talents, certain she would eventually prevail."
}

