  "request_id": "360b75a18a94f96b5d2b55d9",
  "rewritten_text": "Those elderly and frail individuals were constantly observed by the public bowing, and it was widely assumed that after escorting a client out, they continued to bow in the vacant office until the next visitor arrived. The barrister was perceptive enough to realize the banker would not have spoken so definitively without absolute moral certainty. Though the news was difficult to accept, he managed to process it. \"Now,\" Mr. Stryver declared, gesturing toward the Temple, \"my strategy is to prove you all mistaken.\" It was a classic maneuver for an Old Bailey strategist, providing him significant comfort. \"You will not make me the one in the wrong, young lady,\" he remarked; \"I shall handle that for you.\" Consequently, when Mr. Lorry visited at ten that night, Mr. Stryver, surrounded by a clutter of books and documents, appeared entirely unconcerned with the morning's topic. He even feigned surprise at seeing Mr. Lorry, acting distracted and preoccupied. \"Well!\" the good-natured messenger noted, after thirty minutes of futile attempts to steer the conversation. \"I have been to Soho.\" \"To Soho?\" Mr. Stryver asked coldly. \"Oh, of course! My mind was elsewhere!\" \"I am certain,\" Mr. Lorry continued, \"that our previous discussion was accurate. My view remains unchanged, and I repeat my counsel.\" \"I assure you,\" Mr. Stryver replied cordially, \"that I regret this for your sake and for the father's. I realize this is a sensitive family matter; let us drop it.\" \"I do not follow you,\" Mr. Lorry stated. \"I imagine not,\" Stryver replied, nodding dismissively; \"it is of no consequence.\" \"But it does matter,\" Mr. Lorry insisted. \"It truly does not. Having assumed there was wisdom where there is none, and ambition where there is none, I am relieved to be rid of the error. Young women have made such mistakes before, often ending in poverty and obscurity. Unselfishly, I regret the matter is closed, as it would have been disadvantageous for me; selfishly, I am glad it is over, as it would have been a poor worldly prospect—it goes without saying I would have gained nothing from it.\""
}

