{
  "request_id": "804c0670b4a89174b35fe682",
  "rewritten_text": "Enough of this. I will begin my story; it is my final task, and I hope I have the strength to finish it. I have committed no crimes; my mistakes are easily forgiven, as they stemmed from poor judgment rather than malice. I believe few could claim they would have avoided these misfortunes through better wisdom. My fate was dictated by a cruel necessity—a force stronger than any human power, which shattered the unbreakable chains that once bound me to joy and goodness, dragging me into a misery that will only end in death. But I digress. I will pause, wipe my eyes, and try to trade my current, heavy unhappiness for the sharper emotions of the past. I was born in England to a man of high rank. Having lost his own father early, he was raised by an indulgent mother who granted him every luxury. He attended Eton and university, enjoying the independence that comes with wealth and privilege from a young age. These circumstances allowed his character to take root, growing into either virtues or vices. Because he was always free to act on his own, his personality became distinct, revealing both potential for greatness and for ruin. His reckless spending, which he justified as passion, often manifested as boundless generosity. Yet, while he focused on the needs of others, he never denied his own desires. He gave away money and time he did not value, and offered affections that he was glad to express. I cannot say if he would have been selfish had his needs conflicted with others, but he was never tested. He was raised in prosperity, surrounded by people who loved him and sought only to please him."
}

