  "rewritten_text": "Following supper, the entire group ventured out to search for turtle eggs along the sandbar. They probed the sand with sticks, and upon locating a soft patch, knelt to excavate with their hands. Occasionally, they retrieved fifty or sixty eggs from a single nest. These were perfectly spherical, white objects, slightly smaller than an English walnut. They enjoyed a grand feast of fried eggs that evening and again the following Friday morning. After breakfast, they dashed onto the bar, shouting and leaping, discarding their garments as they ran until they were entirely unclothed. They continued their play in the shallow water against the strong current, which frequently knocked them off balance, adding to their amusement. Periodically, they gathered to splash water at one another, approaching with heads turned to shield their faces from the spray, until they grappled and wrestled. The victor would dunk his companion, leaving them all submerged in a chaotic knot of limbs before they surfaced, sputtering and laughing for air. Once exhausted, they collapsed onto the sun-baked sand, burying themselves in it before eventually returning to the water to repeat the cycle. Eventually, realizing their bare skin resembled flesh-toned tights, they drew a ring in the sand and staged a circus, with three boys insisting on the role of clown. Next, they played marbles until the game lost its appeal. Joe and Huck went for another swim, but Tom declined, having realized he lost his string of rattlesnake rattles while undressing. Fearing the loss of his protective charm, he refused to enter the water until he recovered it. By then, the others were weary and sought rest. They drifted apart, feeling melancholy, and stared across the river toward the sleepy village. Tom began tracing the name BECKY in the sand with his toe, then angrily erased it, frustrated by his own sentimentality. Yet, he wrote it again, unable to resist, before finally smoothing it over and gathering the other boys to distract himself."
}

