parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fe015e46dfe563e5a8a7db58",
  "rewritten_text": "I went to the plantation and questioned the older residents. Eventually, an elderly woman mentioned she knew of Bessop’s Castle, though she insisted it was merely a tall rock rather than a building. I offered her payment, and she reluctantly agreed to lead me there. After we arrived, I dismissed her and began my inspection. The site was a cluster of cliffs, one of which was strikingly tall and appeared almost man-made. I climbed to the top, uncertain of my next move. While pondering, I noticed a narrow ledge on the eastern face, about a yard below me. It was small and projected from a niche that resembled an antique chair. I was certain this was the devil’s seat mentioned in the manuscript, and the solution to the riddle began to take shape. I realized the good glass referred to a telescope, a common term among sailors. The specific coordinates and compass heading were clearly instructions for aiming it. Excited, I rushed home to retrieve a telescope and returned to the rock. I lowered myself onto the ledge, finding I could only sit in one specific position, which confirmed my theory. I used the telescope, interpreting the degrees as an elevation above the horizon and the compass heading as the horizontal direction. After setting my compass, I aimed the glass at roughly forty-one degrees of elevation and scanned the area until I spotted a circular opening in the foliage of a distant, towering tree."
}

