parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "db7bac192cb48ce99f85aa98",
  "rewritten_text": "How would they live while Fate kept this grim outcome in the balance? The wedding had to be delayed at any price. He was certain of this. Despite his deep love for her—where even the brush of her hand sent thrills of joy through him—he saw his duty clearly. He knew he had no right to wed until he had carried out the murder. Once finished, he could approach the altar with Sybil Merton, offering his life to her without fear of sin. Then, he could hold her, certain she would never need to feel shame on his account. It had to be done, and the sooner, the better for them both. Many might have chosen an easier path, but Lord Arthur was too principled to prioritize pleasure. His love held more than passion; Sybil represented everything noble. Though he felt a brief, natural aversion to his task, it vanished. His heart framed it as a sacrifice, not a sin, and his reason confirmed there was no alternative. He had to choose between self-interest and the welfare of others, and he would not let selfishness conquer love. This choice faces everyone eventually, but it arrived early for Lord Arthur, before cynicism or vanity could corrupt him. He did not hesitate. He was no idle dreamer; he was practical. To him, life demanded action over contemplation, and he possessed the rare gift of common sense. The turbulent emotions of the night before had faded, and he looked back on his frantic wandering and agony with a sense of embarrassment. The very intensity of his past suffering now made it seem like a dream, and he wondered how he could have been so foolish as to struggle against the inevitable."
}

