parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "860bcf5131c0e9b613a4f2ee",
  "rewritten_text": "April 19th. This morning, to my immense relief, the moon’s surface appeared alarmingly close by nine o’clock, and my condenser pump finally signaled a shift in the air. By ten, I sensed the density rising. By eleven, the equipment required minimal effort, and at noon, I cautiously loosened the tourniquet. Feeling no ill effects, I opened the gum-elastic chamber and detached it from the car. Predictably, this hasty, risky experiment brought on spasms and a severe headache. Yet, since these breathing troubles were not life-threatening, I resolved to endure them, knowing I was rapidly descending into the moon’s thicker atmospheric layers. My descent remained dangerously swift, and it became clear that while the moon possessed an atmosphere, its density was insufficient to support the balloon’s weight, contrary to my expectations based on terrestrial gravity. My rapid fall confirmed this, likely due to the geological anomalies I previously mentioned. As I plummeted toward the surface, I frantically jettisoned my ballast, water casks, condenser, chamber, and every other item in the car. It was futile; I continued to drop at a terrifying speed. With only half a mile remaining, I discarded my coat, hat, and boots, then cut the heavy car loose. Clinging to the netting, I barely glimpsed a landscape filled with tiny dwellings before crashing into the center of a bizarre city. I landed amidst a crowd of grotesque little beings who stood like simpletons, arms akimbo, grinning foolishly and staring at me and my balloon without offering any help or speaking a word."
}

