parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "78bf0da5d6c4e0bd0465f9bf",
  "rewritten_text": "Sydney Carton halted in the street, uncertain of his destination. “Tellson’s at nine,” he mused. “Should I make an appearance meanwhile? Yes, it is a wise precaution for them to know I am present. But I must be careful.” He paced the dimming street, weighing the implications of his choice. Convinced of his plan, he headed toward Saint Antoine. Defarge had identified his wine-shop in that district, and Carton found it easily. After dining and sleeping soundly—abstaining from strong spirits for the first time in years—he awoke at seven. He tidied his cravat, collar, and hair in a shop mirror before entering the wine-shop. Only Jacques Three, the juror with the nervous hands, was present with the Defarges and The Vengeance. Carton sat and requested wine in poor French. Madame Defarge scrutinized him, then approached to confirm his order. When she asked if he were English, he stared as if struggling with the language before replying in a thick, foreign accent."
}

