parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6fe573f09b2f6b2dfe092dfa",
  "rewritten_text": "The note of congratulation Elizabeth got from Lydia regarding her wedding revealed that, at least in the eyes of his wife, such an expectation existed. The message read: My dear Lizzy, I wish you happiness. If you adore Mr. Darcy even half as much as I love my dear Wickham, you must be truly joyful. It is a great relief that you are so wealthy; when you have spare time, please remember us. I am certain Wickham would appreciate a government position, and I doubt we will have sufficient funds to manage without assistance. Any role paying three or four hundred a year would suffice, though please do not mention it to Mr. Darcy if you prefer not to. Yours, etc. Since Elizabeth certainly preferred not to, she attempted in her reply to discourage any such requests or hopes. She did, however, provide what aid she could by practicing frugality with her own funds. It was clear to her that their income, managed by two such spendthrift and shortsighted individuals, would never be enough. Whenever they relocated, either Jane or Elizabeth was inevitably asked for help to settle their debts. Their lifestyle remained chaotic even after peace allowed them to settle down. They constantly moved in search of affordable housing while consistently overspending. His love for her quickly faded into apathy, while hers persisted slightly longer; despite her youth and behavior, she kept the social standing her marriage provided. Although Darcy would never welcome him at Pemberley, he helped him advance his career for Elizabeth’s sake. Lydia visited occasionally when her husband traveled to London or Bath for his own amusement. They stayed with the Bingleys so often that even Bingley’s patience wore thin, leading him to consider suggesting they depart. Miss Bingley felt deeply humiliated by Darcy’s marriage, but deciding it was wise to maintain her access to Pemberley, she set aside her bitterness. She became more devoted to Georgiana, remained nearly as attentive to Darcy as before, and offered every possible courtesy to Elizabeth."
}

