  "rewritten_text": "I recited the beautiful verses describing Dante’s arrival in the terrestrial Paradise, imagining how delightful it would be to walk those shores and witness the radiant chariot bringing my long-lost father back to me. I envisioned weaving a garland from the local blossoms to crown myself in celebration. I would sing my father’s preferred melody, sul margine d’un rio, letting my voice carry through the still air to signal my arrival at his resting place. The sorrow would vanish from my face, and I would gaze into his eyes, which always shone with pure, tender affection. Recalling that magical expression brought me to gentle tears, which I suppressed to keep from breaking the spell. I was so absorbed in this fantasy that I continued walking, only realizing I was on a barren, flowerless plain when I bent down to pick a bloom. The realization snapped me out of my reverie, leaving me disoriented. The sun had vanished, and the pink glow of twilight was fading. A breeze stirred, and I found myself in an unfamiliar landscape, unable to retrace my steps. As darkness deepened, every landmark disappeared. Eventually, night swallowed everything. Exhausted and knowing my servant was staying in a nearby village, I decided to remain where I was, feeling secure in the wilderness. My two years of solitary wandering had hardened me against the cold. I lay on the grass in absolute darkness. The night was perfectly still, with no insects or creatures to break the silence. This profound quiet calmed my body while stirring my spirit, allowing my mind to race through endless thoughts and visions."
}

