parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e94808c8bfb80cdee2b4c882",
  "rewritten_text": "What outcome has followed? What impression have I left on your imagination? I felt a shiver as Dupin posed the query. A lunatic, I suggested, committed this act—some raving soul fled from a nearby asylum. In some ways, he answered, your notion is not off base. Yet the cries of madmen, even in their most frantic states, never match that strange voice heard on the stairs. Lunatics belong to a nation, and their speech, however garbled, maintains the consistency of syllables. Furthermore, the hair of a madman is not like this tuft I hold, which I pulled from the tightly clenched fingers of Madame L’Espanaye. Tell me what you think of it. Dupin, I said, shaken, this hair is highly strange; it is not human. I did not claim it was, he replied, but before we conclude, look at this sketch I have drawn. It is a copy of what witnesses described as bruises and deep nail indentations on the throat of Mademoiselle L’Espanaye, or as livid spots left by fingers. You will see, my friend continued, laying the paper down, that this drawing suggests a firm, unmoving grip. There is no sign of slipping. Each finger held its position until the victim died. Try to place your own fingers into these marks. I tried and failed. We are perhaps not testing this fairly, he noted. The paper is flat, but a throat is cylindrical. Here is a wooden rod with a similar circumference. Wrap the drawing around it and try again. I did so, but the impossibility was even clearer. This, I said, is not the mark of a human hand. Read this passage from Cuvier, Dupin replied. It was a detailed anatomical description of the large fulvous orangutan of the East Indian Islands. The immense size, incredible strength, wild ferocity, and imitative nature of these animals are well known. I grasped the full horror of the murder immediately. The description of the fingers, I said, finishing the text, matches this drawing perfectly."
}

