parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "62eaafbe8385f0208e8b5f42",
  "rewritten_text": "Ten minutes later, his face pale with terror and his eyes wild with grief, Lord Arthur Savile rushed out of Bentinck House. He pushed through the crowd of footmen by the entrance, seeing and hearing nothing. The night was freezing, and the gas lamps flickered in the sharp wind, but his hands were feverish and his forehead burned. He walked like a drunkard. A policeman watched him with curiosity, and a beggar who stepped out to ask for money grew frightened, seeing misery worse than his own. Once, he stopped under a lamp to look at his hands. He imagined he saw bloodstains on them and let out a soft cry. Murder. That was what the palm reader had seen. The night seemed to know it, and the wind howled it in his ears. The dark corners of the streets were full of it, and it seemed to grin from the rooftops. He reached the park, where the dark trees drew him in. He leaned against the wet metal railings to cool his brow, listening to the quiet of the woods. He kept repeating the word murder, as if saying it over and over could lessen the horror. The sound of his own voice made him shudder, yet he almost wanted to wake the city and tell everyone. He wandered across Oxford Street into narrow, grim alleys. Two women with heavy makeup mocked him as he passed. From a dark courtyard came the sound of swearing and fighting, followed by screams. On a damp doorstep, he saw the hunched figures of the poor and elderly. A strange pity filled him. Were these people destined for their suffering just as he was for his? Were they, like him, just puppets in a cruel play? Yet it was not the mystery of suffering that struck him, but its comedy—its total uselessness and lack of meaning. Everything seemed disconnected and chaotic. He was amazed by the gap between the shallow optimism of society and the harsh reality of life. He was still very young. Eventually, he found himself in front of Marylebone Church. The empty road looked like a long ribbon of polished silver, marked by the dark, waving shadows of the trees."
}

