  "rewritten_text": "On that point, I will remain silent, and you can be certain that I will never speak unkindly of you once we are married. Elizabeth had to interrupt him. You are moving too fast, sir, she exclaimed. You forget that I have not answered you yet. Let me do so now without delay. Thank you for the compliment, and I appreciate the honor of your proposal, but I must decline. Mr. Collins waved his hand formally. I know that young women often reject a man they intend to marry when he first proposes, sometimes refusing him repeatedly. I am not discouraged by your words and hope to lead you to the altar soon. Elizabeth replied that his hope was extraordinary given her clear refusal. I am not one of those women who would risk their happiness by waiting for a second request. I am serious. You could not make me happy, and I am the last woman who would make you happy. Even Lady Catherine would agree that I am ill-suited for the role. Mr. Collins replied gravely that he doubted Lady Catherine would disapprove, and he would be sure to praise her modesty and character to her. Elizabeth insisted that his praise was unnecessary. You must allow me to judge for myself and believe what I say. I wish you happiness and wealth, and by refusing you, I am doing my best to ensure you find both. You have fulfilled your obligations to my family, so you may take possession of the Longbourn estate without guilt. This matter is settled. She stood to leave, but Mr. Collins stopped her. When I speak to you about this again, I hope for a more favorable answer. I do not accuse you of cruelty, as I know it is customary for women to reject a man at first, and you have likely said as much as is appropriate for a lady to encourage my suit."
}

