  "request_id": "4a53efdec21677a5e81254d2",
  "rewritten_text": "Well, Jupiter, what is the matter? How is your master? To tell the truth, sir, he is not as well as he could be. Not well? I am sorry to hear that. What is he complaining of? That is the problem. He never complains of anything, yet he is very sick. Very sick, Jupiter? Why didn't you say so? Is he in bed? No, he is not. That is exactly what worries me. I am very concerned about poor Master Will. Jupiter, I need to understand you. You say he is sick, but hasn't he told you what is wrong? Sir, don't get angry. Master Will says nothing is wrong, but he walks around with his head down, his shoulders hunched, and he is as pale as a ghost. He also keeps drawing symbols on a slate. He draws the strangest figures I have ever seen. I am getting scared. I have to watch him closely. The other day, he slipped away before sunrise and was gone all day. I had a big stick ready to give him a good beating when he returned, but I am such a fool that I didn't have the heart to do it. He looked so unwell. I think you should not be too harsh with him. Do not hit him, Jupiter; he cannot handle it. Do you have any idea what caused this change in his behavior? Has anything unpleasant happened since I last saw you? No, sir, nothing unpleasant has happened since then. I fear it happened before that, on the very day you were there. What do you mean? I mean the bug. The what? The bug. I am certain Master Will was bitten on the head by that gold bug. What makes you think that? It had plenty of claws and a mouth, sir. I have never seen such a bug. It kicked and bit everything near it. Master Will caught it first but had to let it go quickly. That must be when he was bitten. I didn't like the look of the bug's mouth, so I wouldn't touch it with my fingers. I caught it with a piece of paper instead."
}

