parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4afaeae7d60c3134d9128479",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Wopsle’s eyes were glaring in such a strange, confused way that I could not understand him. I pondered this long after he had finished his performance, and I was still thinking about it when I left the theater and found him waiting for me outside. We shook hands and walked down the street together. I mentioned that I had seen him looking at me, but he seemed preoccupied. He asked if I had noticed anyone else in the audience. When I asked who he meant, he looked lost and insisted he could swear he saw someone. I grew uneasy and asked him to explain. He rambled on, saying he might not have noticed the man if I had not been there. I looked around nervously, feeling a chill. He assured me the man was gone, having left before the show ended. Because of my own secrets, I suspected Mr. Wopsle might be trying to trick me into saying something incriminating, so I remained silent. He continued, saying he had imagined the man was with me until he realized I was unaware of him sitting behind me like a ghost. I felt cold again but stayed quiet, fearing he was trying to link me to Provis. I knew for certain Provis had not been there. He told me I would find his story hard to believe. He asked if I remembered that Christmas Day from my childhood when soldiers came to the Gargery house to fix handcuffs. I told him I remembered it well. He asked if I recalled the hunt for the two convicts, when Gargery carried me on his back and I struggled to keep up with him as he led the way."
}

