parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "db9346908093ddd8e4056615",
  "rewritten_text": "It is nothing, truly. I do not work for money; I live for my art. Lord Arthur placed four pounds and two shillings on the table, thanked the German for his help, and after declining an invitation to a Saturday gathering of anarchists, left for the park. For two days, he was restless. On Friday at noon, he went to the Buckingham to wait for news. The hall porter posted telegrams about horse races, divorce cases, and the weather, while the ticker tape reported on parliamentary sessions and stock market fluctuations. At four, the evening papers arrived. Lord Arthur retreated to the library with several copies, much to the annoyance of Colonel Goodchild, who wanted to read his own speech on South African missions and the appointment of black bishops. None of the papers mentioned Chichester, and Lord Arthur realized the attempt had failed. He was devastated. When he visited Herr Winckelkopf the next day, the man offered a replacement clock or nitro-glycerine bombs at cost. Lord Arthur had lost faith in explosives, and Winckelkopf admitted that even dynamite was often impure these days. Still, the German remained hopeful, citing a barometer he once sent to a governor in Odessa. It was meant to explode in ten days but took three months. When it finally detonated, it killed a housemaid, as the governor had left weeks earlier. It proved that dynamite, while unpunctual, remained a powerful force when controlled by machinery."
}

