parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7468bf67c4f43cee7072bae6",
  "rewritten_text": "He explained that while traveling from home through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke rising from the inn's chimney. Expecting to find fellow travelers, he knocked and was let in. Only Lucy and her mother were there; the mother was bedridden with rheumatism, and as the remaining locals departed one by one, they were left behind. Lucy begged the man to stay until her mother recovered in a week or two so they could leave together, warning that they would otherwise perish. The man refused, insisting he had to rejoin his wife and children among the emigrants. As a final plea, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, which he successfully delivered. Idris read it with deep emotion: Honoured Lady, I hope you remember and pity me, for I have no other hope. Please forgive my confused writing. A month ago, my mother lost the use of her limbs. She is improving and would be ready to travel in a month with the help you promised, but everyone else has abandoned us. Three days ago, I went to Samuel Woods, hoping his family might wait for us, but their house was empty. I have seen no one until this man arrived. What will become of us? I have hidden our situation from my mother. Will you send someone? We will surely perish here. If I moved her now, she would die on the road, and even if she recovered, I have no way to navigate the long journey to the sea, only to find you have already crossed the ocean to France."
}

