parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f2b76109553356095bfc6abb",
  "rewritten_text": "The area seemed a cluster of alleys, trenches, and small garden patches, suggesting a rather unexciting retreat. Wemmick’s home was a modest wooden cottage surrounded by garden plots, its roof carved and painted to resemble a battery equipped with cannons. My own work, Wemmick remarked, asking if I found it attractive. I praised it warmly, noting it was perhaps the tiniest dwelling I had ever encountered, featuring odd gothic windows—mostly decorative—and a gothic entrance barely large enough for entry. That is a genuine flagpole, Wemmick explained, and I raise a real flag on Sundays. Furthermore, once I cross this bridge, I lift it—like this—to sever all access. The bridge was merely a plank spanning a four-foot-wide, two-foot-deep ditch. Yet, it was delightful to witness the genuine pride and enjoyment with which he raised and secured it. Every night at nine, Greenwich time, the cannon fires, he noted. You will see it there, and when it goes off, I believe you will call it a Stinger. The weapon sat within its own lattice-work fortress, shielded from the elements by a clever umbrella-like tarpaulin. In the back, hidden to maintain the fortification theme—since I believe in fully committing to an idea—there is a pig, along with chickens and rabbits. I build my own frames to grow cucumbers, and you can judge my salad at supper. So, if this place were ever under siege, it could hold out for quite a while regarding supplies, he added with a serious smile. He then led me to a nearby arbor, reached by such winding paths that it took some time to arrive. Our glasses were prepared there, and our punch chilled in an ornamental pond beside the bower. This circular pool, featuring a central island that might serve as our salad, included a fountain that, once a small mill was activated and a pipe uncorked, sprayed with enough force to dampen the back of one's hand."
}

