parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e09175d9de5641de34fcf7e1",
  "rewritten_text": "With a racing heart and an unexplained dread, I dismissed my servant, locked the door, and sat to read my father’s letter. It read as follows: My dear child, I have betrayed your trust. I have tried to corrupt your mind and exposed your innocent heart to the language of monstrous, illicit passion. I must atone for these crimes and ensure my punishment matches my guilt. You are surely prepared for what I must announce: we must separate forever. I am depriving you of your parent and only friend, casting you out into the world without shelter. Your hopes are ruined, and the peace of your pure mind is destroyed. Memory will haunt you with images of my guilt and the pain of betrayed love. Yet I, who have brought this misery upon you, who have remorselessly branded my own child with agony and replaced your beauty with the deformity of sin, I beg you in my own anguish to forgive me. I do not ask for pity, for you must abhor me. But pardon me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts follow me into exile with unrelenting anger. I must never see you or hear your voice again, but the soft whisper of your forgiveness might cool my disordered brain and heart, even in the grave. I dare to make this request by explaining how I was trapped in this web of anguish and how I struggled to escape. If your soul were less pure, I would not attempt to justify myself, fearing that by lessening your hatred for me, you might hate vice less. But in addressing you, I feel I am appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness, which I must gain or fall into despair. I implore you to listen; if guilt can be mitigated by sharp agony and remorse that borders on madness, perhaps you might think I have some claim to your compassion. I entreat you to remember our happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had arrived after sixteen years of weary wandering, during which, despite many dangers, my affections had remained a blank."
}

