parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e319381a6e9bf2089d74575c",
  "rewritten_text": "He always pretended Peter wrote those letters, but that was a lie; he got all the information from that young fool we took to the steamboat. Mary Jane brought out the letter her father left, and the king read it aloud, pretending to cry. It left the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls, and the profitable tanyard, other property worth seven thousand, and three thousand in gold to Harvey and William. It also mentioned where the six thousand dollars in cash was hidden in the cellar. The two frauds told me to bring a candle so they could retrieve it and appear honest. We locked the cellar door, and when they found the bag, they dumped the gold on the floor. It was a beautiful sight. The king’s eyes lit up as he slapped the duke on the shoulder and exclaimed that this was better than their previous scams. The duke agreed. They played with the gold coins, letting them jingle through their fingers. The king remarked that acting as brothers to a wealthy dead man and representatives for foreign heirs was their best strategy, proving that trusting in Providence always pays off. Most people would have been satisfied with the money, but they insisted on counting it. They found it was four hundred and fifteen dollars short. The king cursed, wondering where the missing money went. After searching in vain, the duke suggested the sick man had simply made a mistake and they should let it go. The king insisted he did not care about the loss, but he was worried about the count. He wanted to be completely transparent and count the money in front of everyone to avoid suspicion, but he was concerned about the discrepancy between their count and the amount the dead man claimed was there."
}

