parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a7264c50710a3f140aeb2b35",
  "rewritten_text": "He was brought to you. You see, I am aware of the details. That is true, Defarge replied. His wife had nudged him while she knitted, signaling him to keep his answers brief. The spy continued, It was to you that his daughter came, and from your care that she took him, along with that man from Tellson’s Bank, Mr. Lorry, to England. That is true, Defarge repeated. Very interesting, the spy said. I have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England. Do you hear much about them now? No, Defarge said. Actually, Madame Defarge added, looking up from her knitting, we never hear about them. We heard they arrived safely and received a letter or two, but since then, we have gone our separate ways. Quite right, the spy replied. She is going to be married. Going to be? Madame asked. She was pretty enough to have been married long ago. You English seem cold. You know I am English, the spy said. I can tell by your accent, Madame replied, and I assume the man matches the tongue. He did not take this as a compliment but laughed it off. After finishing his drink, he added, Yes, Miss Manette is getting married, but not to an Englishman. She is marrying a Frenchman, the nephew of the Marquis for whom Gaspard was executed. He lives in England under the name Charles Darnay, though his mother’s family name was D’Aulnais. Madame Defarge kept knitting, but the news clearly affected her husband. As he tried to light his pipe, his hands shook. The spy noticed his reaction immediately. Having made his point, and with no other customers arriving, Mr. Barsad paid for his drink and left, politely mentioning that he looked forward to seeing the Defarges again."
}

