  "rewritten_text": "To observe him was to imagine Nemesis had seized Pallas's shield, revealing the Gorgon’s visage. He appeared petrified, his countenance marble-like in its sorrow. Having enjoyed a refined existence of wealth and privilege, free from common worries and marked by youthful ease, he now grasped the grim enigma of Fate and the dire significance of Doom. How irrational and grotesque it appeared! Was some dark secret of transgression or a crimson mark of guilt inscribed upon his palm, legible only to others? Was there no flight? Were humans merely pawns shifted by an invisible hand, or clay molded by a potter for glory or disgrace? His intellect rebelled, yet he sensed an impending tragedy and an unbearable weight placed upon him. Actors are lucky; they select their roles, whether tragic or comic. In reality, however, people often play parts for which they are ill-suited. Our minor characters act as leads, and our leads must play the fool. The world is a stage, but the casting is flawed. Suddenly, Mr. Podgers walked in. Upon seeing Lord Arthur, he recoiled, his bloated face turning a sickly greenish-yellow. Their gazes locked in a silent moment. The Duchess left a glove, and I am here to retrieve it, Mr. Podgers eventually stated, spotting it on the sofa. Good evening. Mr. Podgers, I demand a direct answer to my question. Another time, Lord Arthur; the Duchess is waiting. You shall not leave. The Duchess can wait. Ladies should not be delayed, Mr. Podgers replied with a nauseating grin. They are prone to impatience. Lord Arthur’s lips curled in scorn. The Duchess seemed trivial to him then. He approached Mr. Podgers and extended his hand. Tell me what you saw, he commanded. Speak the truth. I must know. I am no child."
}

