parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "26af2de3a284f05b14853ba4",
  "rewritten_text": "It is quite notable that she consistently understands Jane more clearly than she does me. Jane articulates so plainly! Still, she will find her grandmother no harder of hearing than she was two years ago, which is quite an achievement at my mother’s age. It has been a full two years since her last visit. We have never gone so long without seeing her, and as I mentioned to Mrs. Cole, we shall struggle to show her enough attention now. Are you anticipating Miss Fairfax’s arrival soon? Oh yes, next week. Indeed, that must be a wonderful delight. Thank you, you are very kind. Yes, next week. Everyone is so surprised and offers such gracious remarks. I am certain she will be just as pleased to see her Highbury friends as they are to see her. She will arrive Friday or Saturday; she cannot specify because Colonel Campbell requires the carriage himself on one of those days. It is so generous of them to provide her transportation the entire way, but they always do. Yes, next Friday or Saturday is what she mentions in her letter. That is why she wrote out of turn; normally, we would not have heard from her until next Tuesday or Wednesday. Yes, I assumed as much. I feared there was little chance of hearing news of Miss Fairfax today. How kind of you! No, we would not have received word if not for this specific reason of her visiting so soon. My mother is thrilled, as she will stay with us for at least three months. She states that duration quite firmly, as I shall soon have the pleasure of reading to you. The situation is that the Campbells are traveling to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has convinced her parents to visit her immediately. They had not planned to go until summer, but she is so eager to see them again. Until her marriage last October, she had never been separated from them for more than a week, making it quite unusual to be in different countries. She sent an urgent letter to her parents—or perhaps her father, I cannot recall, but we shall see in Jane’s note—writing on behalf of herself and Mr. Dixon to insist they come over at once. They plan to meet in Dublin and then travel to their estate, Baly-craig, which I imagine is a lovely residence."
}

