parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d08bb675c5a6e549cd2e7b22",
  "rewritten_text": "Weeks passed, and I became increasingly obsessed with you. I had painted you as Paris in armor, as Adonis with a spear, and as a figure on an ancient barge. Those works were what art should be: ideal and detached. One day, I decided to paint a portrait of you as you truly are, in your own clothes and time. I am not sure if it was the realism or your own striking personality, but as I worked, every brushstroke seemed to expose my secret. I feared others would discover my devotion. I felt I had revealed too much of myself, so I decided never to show the painting. You were annoyed, but you did not understand. Harry laughed at my concerns, but I ignored him. Once the painting was finished, I felt justified in my decision. However, after the portrait left my studio, I felt foolish for thinking it contained anything more than a handsome subject and my own technical skill. I now believe that the passion an artist feels is rarely visible in the final work. Art is more abstract than we imagine; it often hides the artist rather than revealing them. When I received an offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the centerpiece of my exhibition. I never expected you to refuse, but I see now that you were right. The picture must not be shown. Please do not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have confessed."
}

