parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8f9f3a9ee3c50510eb61f755",
  "rewritten_text": "I reasoned backward. The killers escaped through one of these windows, yet the sashes were found locked from the inside. This obvious fact had stopped the police from investigating further, but I knew the windows must be capable of locking themselves. I went to the first window, removed the nail with effort, and tried to lift the sash. It would not budge. I realized a hidden spring must exist, and finding it confirmed my theory. I pressed the spring but left the window closed. I replaced the nail and studied it. A killer could close the window so the spring would catch, but they could not have replaced the nail from the outside. This meant they must have escaped through the other window. Assuming the springs were identical, there had to be a difference in the nails or how they were secured. I climbed onto the bed to examine the second window. I found the identical spring behind the headboard, but the nail looked just as sturdy and well-fitted as the first. You might think I was confused, but I was not. My logic remained sound, and I had followed the trail directly to the nail. Despite its appearance, the fact that the trail ended here meant something was wrong. I touched the nail, and its head, along with a small piece of the shank, snapped off in my hand."
}

