parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e3ba3a2df4027e3d94333714",
  "rewritten_text": "I considered faking my death; my happy heirs would take my fortune, and I would finally be free. My plan had to be clever, though, as I needed to secure enough money to survive. What disgusting schemes I was forced to adopt! I would have lived a life of lies, yet whenever I felt guilty about the deception, a visit from an aunt or cousin would harden my resolve. They would tell me that death comes for everyone, then claim my father had lost his mind since my mother died. They called him insane and told me I was lucky he had not killed me during one of his episodes. They said this with soft whispers, downcast eyes, and fake sympathy, while I sat quietly, trembling inside, unable to challenge their cruel words. What a wonderful life of honesty this was! I wore a gentle expression while hiding a cunning heart. I felt only the shame of lying, not the comfort of innocence. I had to learn to weave complex webs of deceit until I could lie gracefully, masking my true self entirely. Oh, beloved father, accept your daughter’s pure heart; let me return to you untainted, or you will not recognize me. Just as grief changed Constance, deceit would change me until you would not know me in heaven. To be happy now and when we meet again, I must escape this mocking life. Only in solitude can I be myself and be yours. Even now, I look back with disgust at the schemes I used to escape after many painful struggles. I could explain how I secured my living and faked my death, but I will not."
}

