parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f4b7a1f36c397a3850944756",
  "rewritten_text": "Put down your weapons, brothers! Forgiveness and friendship await your change of heart. You are precious to us because we share the same fragile humanity. Why should man fight man when a deadly plague already threatens us all, thriving on our violence? Both armies hesitated. Our soldiers held their weapons tightly, watching the enemy with stern eyes. The opposing troops had not dropped their arms, mostly out of fear rather than a desire to fight, waiting for a leader to show them what to do. Adrian jumped from his horse and knelt by a fallen soldier. He is a man, and he is dead, he cried. Quickly, tend to the wounded. Do not let another soul die or go before God to tell the story of this brotherly slaughter. Replace your cruel hearts with kindness and discard your weapons. In this moment of shared fate, let us be guardians to one another. As he spoke, he knelt and lifted a man whose life was bleeding away. The soldier gasped, and in the sudden silence, his moans were heard by everyone. Hearts that had been set on killing now beat with anxious hope for this one life. Adrian used his scarf to bind the wound, but it was too late. The man sighed and went limp. He is dead, Adrian said, bowing his head in grief. The fate of the world seemed to rest on that single death. Soldiers on both sides dropped their weapons. Even the hardened veterans wept, and our men reached out to their former enemies with love. The two armies merged, unarmed and holding hands, discussing how to help one another. Regretting their past violence, they followed the General’s orders and began the journey toward London."
}

