  "rewritten_text": "Raymond paced the room in a fury. I stood prepared to face his accusations. Adrian leaned against the wall. \"This is absurd,\" he cried. \"If you were schoolboys, you could not act more unreasonably.\" \"You do not understand,\" Raymond said. \"This is part of a system of tyranny I will never accept. Because I am Protector of England, must I be the only slave in the empire? My privacy is invaded, my actions criticized, and my friends insulted. I am finished with it all.\" He took the star, the symbol of his office, from his chest and threw it onto the table. \"I renounce my position. I abdicate my power. Let whoever wants it take it.\" \"Let someone take it,\" Adrian exclaimed, \"if they can prove themselves your superior. No one in England has the arrogance to try. Know yourself, Raymond, and your anger will fade. Months ago, we prayed for the country's prosperity and for the Protector's welfare as one. You dedicated your time to our benefit and sought our approval. You improved our towns, built useful institutions, and made the land fertile. The powerful feared your judgment, and the poor thrived under your protection. Can you wonder that we are shocked and grieving now that this has changed? This bad mood has passed; resume your duties. Your supporters will cheer, your enemies will be silenced, and we will show you our love and respect again. Master yourself, Raymond, and the world is yours.\" \"That would be sensible if said to someone else,\" Raymond replied moodily. \"Learn the lesson yourself, and you, the highest peer in the land, could become its sovereign. You are good, wise, and just; you could rule all hearts. But I realize, too late for England, that I took on a task I cannot handle. I cannot rule myself. My passions are my masters. Do you think I renounced the Protectorate in a fit of temper? I swear by the living God that I will never pick up that trinket again, nor burden myself with the weight of the misery it represents.\""
}

