parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b429b99d95aa1914496d532e",
  "rewritten_text": "I consider it one of the first refinements of civilized society. Certainly, sir, and it has the advantage of being popular among less polished societies as well; every savage can dance. Sir William smiled. Your friend performs delightfully, he said after a pause, watching Bingley join the group. I have no doubt you are an expert yourself, Mr. Darcy. You saw me dance at Meryton, I believe, sir. Yes, and I enjoyed it. Do you often dance at St. James’s? Never, sir. Do you not think it would be a proper compliment to the place? It is a compliment I avoid paying whenever I can. You have a house in town, I assume? Mr. Darcy bowed. I once thought of settling in London myself, as I enjoy high society, but I was not sure the air would suit Lady Lucas. He paused, hoping for a reply, but his companion remained silent. Just then, Elizabeth walked toward them. Sir William had a sudden idea and called out, My dear Miss Eliza, why are you not dancing? Mr. Darcy, you must allow me to introduce this young lady as a wonderful partner. You cannot refuse to dance when such beauty is before you. He took her hand to give it to Mr. Darcy, who was surprised but not unwilling. Elizabeth immediately pulled back and said to Sir William, I have no intention of dancing. Please do not think I walked over here to find a partner. Mr. Darcy politely asked for the honor of her hand, but she refused. Elizabeth remained firm, and Sir William’s attempts to persuade her failed. You dance so well, Miss Eliza, that it is cruel to deny me the pleasure of watching you. Even if this gentleman dislikes dancing, he surely would not mind obliging us for half an hour. Mr. Darcy is very polite, Elizabeth said with a smile. He is indeed, but given the incentive, his willingness is no surprise; who would object to such a partner? Elizabeth gave a playful look and turned away. Her refusal had not lowered her in Mr. Darcy’s estimation, and he was thinking of her with some satisfaction when Miss Bingley approached him."
}

