parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "56c679d518eb1ea8ddbdfc97",
  "rewritten_text": "It was some time before I regained my composure enough to check the balloon. To my relief, it was undamaged. My equipment was secure, and I had lost neither ballast nor supplies, as I had fastened them too well for such a mishap. Checking my watch, I saw it was six o'clock. I was still climbing rapidly, and my barometer indicated an altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly below, a small, dark, oblong object floated on the ocean, looking much like a child's domino. Through my telescope, I identified it as a British ninety-four-gun ship, sailing close-hauled and pitching heavily, headed west-southwest. Beyond that, there was only the sea, the sky, and the sun, which had long since risen. It is time to explain the purpose of my dangerous journey. You must understand that financial ruin in Rotterdam had driven me to consider suicide. I did not hate life itself, but I was exhausted by the misery of my circumstances. Caught between the desire to live and the weariness of existence, a book I found at a stall sparked my imagination. I decided to leave the world while continuing to live; in short, I resolved to attempt a voyage to the moon. Lest you think me entirely mad, I will explain why I believed this difficult and perilous feat was possible for a bold spirit. First, I considered the moon's distance. The average interval between the centers of the two bodies is about 237,000 miles. However, because the moon's orbit is elliptical and the earth sits at its focus, I realized that if I could intercept the moon at its perigee, that distance would be significantly reduced."
}

