parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8455898cb9d7c45ec0d8b568",
  "rewritten_text": "Those elderly, frail clerks were always seen bowing to the public. People joked that after showing a customer out, they kept bowing in the empty office until the next person arrived. The lawyer realized the banker would not have spoken so firmly without being certain. Though the news was hard to accept, he managed to process it. Shaking his finger at the room, Mr. Stryver decided his best move was to prove everyone else wrong. It was a classic courtroom tactic that brought him comfort. He told himself he would be the one to reject the young lady before she could reject him. When Mr. Lorry visited that night at ten, Stryver acted as if he were busy with legal papers and had forgotten their earlier talk. He even feigned surprise at seeing Lorry. After half an hour of trying to bring up the topic, Lorry finally said he had been to Soho. Stryver responded coldly, pretending to have forgotten. Lorry insisted his previous advice was correct and still held true. Stryver replied in a friendly tone that he felt sorry for Lorry and the girl’s father, suggesting they drop the painful subject. When Lorry protested that it did matter, Stryver dismissed him with a wave. He claimed he had simply made a mistake in judgment. He remarked that many young women had made similar foolish choices before, only to regret them later in poverty. He concluded that while he was sorry for the family's sake, he was relieved to be finished with the matter, as it would have been a poor choice for his own career."
}

