parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "50f58413fb1bf370739ae7e9",
  "rewritten_text": "While these events unfolded at the workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in his old hideout—the same place Oliver had been taken from—staring into a dim, smoky fire. He held a pair of bellows on his lap, having tried to stir the flames, but he had drifted into deep thought, resting his chin on his hands while staring blankly at the rusty grate. Behind him, the Artful Dodger, Charley Bates, and Mr. Chitling played a game of whist, with the Dodger playing against the other two. The Dodger, always sharp, watched the game closely, frequently sneaking glances at Mr. Chitling’s cards to adjust his own strategy. Because it was cold, the Dodger kept his hat on, as he often did indoors. He also kept a clay pipe in his teeth, removing it only to take sips of gin-and-water from a large pot on the table. Charley Bates was also focused, but being more excitable, he drank more of the gin and made constant jokes that were inappropriate for a serious game. The Dodger tried to lecture him on his behavior, but Charley just brushed him off with witty insults, which Mr. Chitling found hilarious. Interestingly, Chitling and his partner always lost, yet Charley found this incredibly funny, laughing loudly after every hand and claiming he had never had so much fun. That is two doubles and the rubber, Mr. Chitling said with a long face, pulling half a crown from his pocket. I have never seen anyone like you, Jack; you win everything."
}

