  "request_id": "cbfaad013b3f5044ab41fa8f",
  "rewritten_text": "May I ask you two questions? You may ask, Monks replied with surprise, but whether I answer is another matter. That makes three, Mr. Bumble joked. Is that what you expected from me? the matron asked. It is, Monks replied. What is your other question? What do you plan to do with it? Can it be used against me? Never, Monks said. Nor against me. Look here, but do not step forward, or your life is forfeit. He shoved the table aside, pulled an iron ring, and opened a trapdoor at Mr. Bumble’s feet, causing him to scramble back. Look down, Monks said, lowering his lantern. Do not fear me. I could have dropped you in while you were sitting over it if I had wanted. The matron approached the edge, and Mr. Bumble followed, driven by curiosity. Below, the rain-swollen water rushed rapidly, its roar drowning out all other sounds as it churned against slimy wooden piles. Once a water-mill, the site now featured only rotting stakes and broken machinery that the river surged past. If you threw a body down there, where would it be by morning? Monks asked, swinging the lantern. Twelve miles downriver and shredded, Mr. Bumble replied, recoiling. Monks pulled the packet from his coat, tied it to a lead weight, and dropped it into the stream. It vanished into the water without a sound. The three looked at each other, breathing easier. There, Monks said, slamming the trapdoor shut. If the sea ever gives up its dead, it will keep its gold and silver, and that trash along with it. We are finished here. By all means, Mr. Bumble agreed quickly. You will keep your mouth shut, won't you? Monks asked with a threatening glare."
}

