  "request_id": "b04c7087fca58e9316a3f276",
  "rewritten_text": "The cramped alleys and courtyards eventually opened into a wide expanse containing animal pens and other signs of a livestock market. Sikes slowed down here, as the girl could no longer maintain their previous brisk pace. He turned to Oliver and harshly ordered him to grasp Nancy’s hand. When the boy hesitated and glanced around, Sikes growled at him to obey. They stood in a shadowed corner, hidden from any passersby. Realizing that fighting back was useless, Oliver extended his hand, which Nancy gripped firmly. Sikes grabbed Oliver’s other hand and called for his dog. Bull’s-Eye looked up and snarled. Sikes pressed his hand against Oliver’s throat, warning him that if he uttered even a whisper, the dog would attack. The animal growled and stared at Oliver, clearly eager to strike. Sikes praised the dog’s readiness with grim satisfaction, telling Oliver that he knew the consequences if he tried to call for help. The dog wagged its tail and led the way forward. They were crossing Smithfield, though it was indistinguishable to Oliver in the dark, foggy night. The shop lights barely pierced the dense mist, which cloaked the surroundings in gloom and deepened Oliver’s sense of confusion and despair. After they had walked a short distance, a deep church bell tolled. His captors paused, listening to the sound. Nancy noted it was eight o’clock, to which Sikes retorted that he was perfectly capable of hearing it himself. Nancy wondered aloud if others could hear it, and Sikes confirmed they could, recalling how the noise from a fair had been audible even from inside his jail cell, making the silence of his confinement feel unbearable."
}

