  "request_id": "b532ebda5a6247930ead3dfd",
  "rewritten_text": "If Lydia had known her sister wanted to stop her from such a bright future, she would have been devastated. Only her mother could have truly understood her feelings. Lydia’s trip to Brighton was the only thing comforting her mother, who was saddened that her husband refused to go. The family remained unaware of the truth, and their excitement continued until the day Lydia left. Elizabeth was about to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Having spent time with him since returning home, her nerves had settled, and her old feelings for him were gone. She now found his gentle manner artificial and tiresome. Furthermore, she was annoyed that he tried to renew his flirtatious behavior toward her. She felt insulted that he assumed she would be flattered by his attention after he had ignored her for so long. On the regiment's final day in Meryton, Wickham dined at Longbourn. Elizabeth, feeling no desire to be kind, mentioned that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had spent three weeks at Rosings, then asked if he knew the Colonel. Wickham looked surprised and uneasy, but quickly recovered and smiled. He admitted he had known him well and called him a gentleman. When Elizabeth praised the Colonel, Wickham asked how long he had stayed at Rosings. After she confirmed it was nearly three weeks and that they had met daily, he noted that the Colonel’s manners differed from his cousin’s. Elizabeth replied that Mr. Darcy improved the more one knew him. Wickham looked suspicious and asked, with a forced smile, if it was his social skills that improved."
}

