parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "19580ab37e892c9ad23de362",
  "rewritten_text": "“Tom!” No answer. “TOM!” No answer. “Where has that boy gone? You, Tom!” No answer. The old woman lowered her glasses to peer around the room, then pushed them up to look beneath them. She rarely looked through them for something as small as a boy; they were her formal pair, kept for style rather than utility—she could have seen just as well through stove lids. She looked confused for a moment, then said, not angrily but loudly enough for the furniture to hear: “Well, if I get my hands on you, I will—” She did not finish, as she was busy poking under the bed with a broom, needing her breath to punctuate the jabs. She pulled out nothing but the cat. “I have never seen the likes of that boy!” She went to the open door and scanned the tomato vines and weeds in the garden. No Tom. She raised her voice to carry and shouted: “Y-o-u-u TOM!” A slight noise behind her made her turn just in time to grab a small boy by his jacket and stop his escape. “There! I should have thought of that closet. What have you been doing in there?” “Nothing.” “Nothing! Look at your hands. And look at your mouth. What is that stuff?” “I don’t know, Aunt.” “Well, I know. It’s jam. I’ve told you forty times that if you didn’t leave that jam alone, I’d tan your hide. Hand me that switch.” The switch hovered in the air—the danger was immediate— “Oh! Look behind you, Aunt!” The old woman spun around, pulling her skirts out of the way. The boy fled instantly, scrambled up the high board fence, and vanished over it. His Aunt Polly stood surprised for a moment, then began to laugh softly. “Darn the boy, will I never learn? Hasn’t he played enough tricks like that for me to be ready for him by now? But old fools are the biggest fools of all. You can’t teach an old dog new tricks. But goodness, he never plays the same one twice, so how is a person to know what is coming? He seems to know exactly how long he can torment me before I lose my temper, and he knows that if he can distract me for a moment or make me laugh, my anger vanishes and I cannot bring myself to hit him.”"
}

