parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c75c484fb53bbc18009ec02e",
  "rewritten_text": "I went to the plantation and questioned the older workers. Finally, an elderly woman mentioned she knew of Bessop’s Castle, though she insisted it was actually a tall rock rather than a building. I offered to pay her to lead me there, and she eventually agreed. We found the site easily, and after she left, I examined the area. The 'castle' was a collection of cliffs, one of which stood out for its height and unnatural shape. I climbed to the top, unsure of my next move. While thinking, I noticed a narrow ledge on the eastern side, just below where I stood. It was small and shaped like an old-fashioned chair. I was certain this was the 'devil’s seat' mentioned in the manuscript, and I felt I had finally solved the mystery. I realized the 'good glass' referred to a telescope, a common term among sailors. The specific coordinates and directions were clearly instructions for aiming it. Excited, I hurried home to get my telescope and returned to the rock. I lowered myself onto the ledge and found I could only sit in one specific position, which confirmed my theory. I used my pocket compass to face northeast by north and adjusted the telescope to an elevation of forty-one degrees. As I scanned the area, my gaze was caught by a circular opening in the leaves of a distant, towering tree."
}

