  "request_id": "3346a5c8f32abb9f97cbb55c",
  "rewritten_text": "Soon his voice called out in a shout. How much further must I go? How high are you? Legrand inquired. Ever so far, the man answered; I can see the sky through the treetops. Ignore the sky and listen to me. Look down the trunk and count the branches beneath you on this side. How many have you passed? One, two, three, four, five—I have passed five large limbs, master, on this side. Then climb one limb higher. Shortly, the voice returned, stating the seventh limb was reached. Now, Jup, Legrand shouted, clearly agitated, I need you to crawl out on that branch as far as possible. If you spot anything unusual, tell me. By then, any lingering doubt regarding my friend's sanity vanished. I felt certain he was suffering from madness and grew worried about returning him home. As I considered my options, Jupiter spoke again. I am quite afraid to venture far on this limb; it is mostly dead wood. Did you say it was a dead limb? Legrand asked, his voice trembling. Yes, master, it is dead as a doornail—certainly finished—it has departed this life. What on earth shall I do? Legrand asked, appearing deeply distressed. Do? I said, seizing the chance to speak, come home and go to bed. Come now, be a good fellow. It is getting late, and you recall your promise. Jupiter, he called, ignoring me entirely, do you hear me? Yes, Master Will, I hear you clearly. Test the wood well with your knife and see if it seems very rotten. It is rotten, master, for sure, the man replied after a moment, but not as bad as it could be. I might venture a little way out on the limb myself, that is true. Yourself? What do you mean? I mean the bug. It is a very heavy bug. Suppose I drop it first, then the limb will not break under just the weight of one man. You scoundrel! Legrand cried, seemingly relieved, what do you mean by such nonsense? If you drop that beetle, I will break your neck. Listen, Jupiter, do you hear me? Yes, master, no need to shout at me like that. Well, listen! If you venture out on the limb as far as you feel is safe, without dropping the beetle, I will give you a silver dollar once you return."
}

