parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "08f02c0a3c286857e8622a52",
  "rewritten_text": "I had tucked my handkerchief inside my helmet, along with a few other items, forgetting that I could not remove the helmet on my own. The realization that the cloth was so close yet completely inaccessible made my situation unbearable. It is a universal truth that we desire most what we cannot reach. My entire focus narrowed to that handkerchief, and as salty sweat dripped into my eyes, I was powerless to wipe it away. While it sounds trivial, it was genuine misery. I decided that in the future, I would carry a bag regardless of what the knights of the Round Table thought; comfort would take precedence over their rigid standards of style. As we traveled, clouds of dust forced me to sneeze and weep, leading me to curse in frustration. We encountered no one in that desolate landscape, which was fortunate for any ogre who might have crossed my path, as I would have gladly traded his life for a simple cloth. Meanwhile, the sun heated my iron armor until I felt like I was being baked alive. Every movement was an irritation; trotting caused a clattering sound like a crate of dishes, while the shield banged incessantly against my body. If I slowed to a walk, my joints screeched like a rusted wheelbarrow, and the lack of airflow made the heat stifling. The slower I moved, the heavier the armor felt, pressing down on me with the weight of a thousand tons."
}

