parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "531b693abae83b859577c449",
  "rewritten_text": "As I reached through the valve with a cup of water for the cat, my shirt sleeve caught on the loop holding the basket, instantly detaching it from the bottom. The basket vanished from sight as if it had dissolved into thin air. Less than a second passed between the basket coming loose and its total disappearance along with its contents. I wished them well, though I held no hope that the cat or her kittens would survive the fall. By six o'clock, I saw a large shadow covering the earth to the east, which spread rapidly until the entire view was swallowed by night at five minutes before seven. The setting sun continued to light my balloon long after this, which, while expected, brought me great joy. It was clear that I would see the sunrise hours before the people of Rotterdam, despite their location to the east. As I climbed higher, I would enjoy sunlight for longer periods each day. I decided to keep a journal, counting days as continuous twenty-four-hour cycles regardless of the darkness. At ten o'clock, feeling tired, I planned to sleep, but a problem I had overlooked suddenly occurred to me. If I slept, how would the air in the cabin be refreshed? Breathing the same air for more than an hour or so would be impossible, and staying longer could be fatal. This dilemma worried me deeply. It may seem strange that after all I had endured, this issue made me consider giving up my mission and descending. However, my hesitation lasted only a moment."
}

