  "rewritten_text": "The storm raged all night, but nothing notable happened. The next morning, however, the bloodstain had returned to the floor. Washington remarked that the detergent clearly was not to blame, suggesting it must be the ghost. He cleaned it again, but it reappeared the following morning. It returned a third time, despite Mr. Otis locking the library and keeping the key. The family grew intrigued; Mr. Otis reconsidered his skepticism, Mrs. Otis planned to join the Psychical Society, and Washington drafted a report on the persistence of bloodstains linked to crimes. That night, all doubt regarding the existence of ghosts vanished. After a warm, sunny day, the family went for an evening drive. They returned at nine for a light supper, discussing mundane topics like American actresses, the scarcity of specific American foods in England, the cultural importance of Boston, the efficiency of baggage checks, and the merits of the New York accent. No one mentioned the supernatural or Sir Simon de Canterville. The family retired at eleven, and by eleven-thirty, the house was dark. Later, Mr. Otis was awakened by a metallic clanking in the hallway that grew louder. He rose, lit a match, and checked the time: exactly one o’clock. Remaining calm and noting his steady pulse, he heard the noise continue alongside distinct footsteps. He put on his slippers, grabbed a small bottle from his dressing case, and opened the door."
}

