  "rewritten_text": "Unless you call Miss Havisham a man. And I doubt you would go that far. Miss Havisham, in town? Joe asked. Is there any Miss Havisham out of town? my sister replied. She wants this boy to go and play there. Of course he is going. And he had better play there, she said, shaking her head at me to encourage me to be lively and cheerful, or I will deal with him. I had heard of Miss Havisham in town—everyone for miles around had—as an incredibly wealthy and stern woman who lived in a large, gloomy house barricaded against thieves, living a life of total seclusion. Well, I am amazed! Joe said. I wonder how she came to know Pip! Idiot! my sister cried. Who said she knew him? Some individual, Joe politely hinted, mentioned that she wanted him to go and play there. Could she not ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there? Is it not just possible that Uncle Pumblechook is her tenant, and that he might sometimes—we will not say quarterly or half-yearly, as that would be asking too much of you—go there to pay his rent? And could she not then ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there? And could Uncle Pumblechook, always being considerate and thoughtful for us—though you may not think so, Joseph, she said with deep reproach, as if he were the most unfeeling of nephews—then mention this boy, standing here prancing—which I solemnly declare I was not doing—that I have always been a willing slave to? Good again! Uncle Pumblechook cried. Well put! Nicely stated! Good indeed! Now Joseph, you understand the situation. No, Joseph, my sister said, still reproachfully, while Joe apologetically wiped his nose with the back of his hand, you do not yet—though you may think you do—understand the situation. You may believe you do, but you do not, Joseph. For you do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, realizing that for all we know this boy’s fortune might be made by going to Miss Havisham’s, has offered to take him into town tonight in his own cart, keep him overnight, and take him personally to Miss Havisham’s tomorrow morning. And good heavens! my sister cried, throwing off her bonnet in sudden desperation, here I am talking to simpletons, with Uncle Pumblechook waiting, the horse catching a cold at the door, and the boy covered in soot and dirt from head to toe!"
}

