  "rewritten_text": "The two women were thrilled to see their friend, remarking that it had been ages since they last met and repeatedly asking how she had spent her time. They ignored the rest of the family, avoiding Mrs. Bennet, speaking little to Elizabeth, and saying nothing to the others. They soon departed, rising with a suddenness that surprised their brother and hurrying away as if desperate to escape Mrs. Bennet’s politeness. The prospect of the Netherfield ball delighted every woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet viewed it as a tribute to her eldest daughter, feeling flattered that Mr. Bingley had extended the invitation personally rather than by card. Jane looked forward to a pleasant evening with her friends and their brother, while Elizabeth anticipated dancing with Mr. Wickham and observing Mr. Darcy’s behavior. Catherine and Lydia were excited simply by the event itself, as any ball was welcome, and even Mary claimed she did not mind attending. She noted that as long as her mornings remained private, she was happy to participate in social engagements, believing recreation was healthy for everyone. Elizabeth was in such high spirits that she asked Mr. Collins if he planned to attend and whether he felt dancing was appropriate. She was surprised to learn he had no objections and feared no criticism from his patrons for participating. He assured her that a ball hosted by a respectable man was perfectly acceptable. He added that he enjoyed dancing and hoped to partner with all his cousins, specifically requesting the first two dances with Elizabeth, hoping Jane would understand his choice was not meant as a slight."
}

