            "text": "{\n  \"request_id\": \"57c786cf2edc84cb5a773690\",\n  \"rewritten_text\": \"Since my father’s surname was Pirrip and my given name Philip, my baby speech could manage nothing more distinct than Pip. Thus, I named myself Pip, and that is how I became known. I cite Pirrip as my father’s name based on his headstone and the word of my sister, Mrs. Joe Gargery, the blacksmith’s wife. Having never seen my parents or any portrait of them—as they lived long before photography—my initial notions of their appearance were absurdly formed by their graves. The lettering on my father’s stone suggested a stout, square, dark man with curly hair. From the phrasing regarding Georgiana, his wife, I childishly inferred my mother was sickly and freckled. Five small stone markers nearby, dedicated to my five brothers who died in infancy, led me to believe they were born lying on their backs with hands in their pockets, never removing them throughout their lives. We lived in the marshlands near the river, roughly twenty miles from the sea. My first clear understanding of the world came on a raw, late afternoon. That was when I realized this nettle-choked spot was the churchyard, where my parents and brothers lay buried. I saw the dark, flat wilderness of the marshes, the leaden river, the distant sea, and realized the small, shivering, weeping child was myself. \\\"Quiet!\\\" a harsh voice shouted as a man emerged from behind the church porch. \\\"Stay still, you little wretch, or I will slit your throat!\\\" He was a frightening figure, dressed in coarse grey, with a heavy iron shackle on his leg.\"\n}",
