parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "08e72eb117d7bd21f2cad9f1",
  "rewritten_text": "With that finished, I unfastened a few more loops from the rim, fed in more fabric, and reattached the loops to their corresponding buttons. This allowed me to slide the entire upper section of the bag between the netting and the hoop. The hoop now rested inside the car, while the weight of the car and its contents hung entirely from the buttons. While this seemed precarious, it was perfectly secure; the buttons were robust and spaced closely enough that each bore only a fraction of the load. Even if the car had been three times heavier, I would have felt no concern. I raised the hoop back inside the rubberized covering, propping it near its original height with three light poles to keep the bag distended and the netting properly positioned. Finally, I sealed the enclosure by gathering the fabric folds and twisting them tightly from the inside with a stationary tourniquet. Three circular panes of thick, clear glass were set into the sides of the covering, providing a full horizontal view. A fourth window in the bottom, aligned with a small hole in the floor, allowed me to look straight down. I could not install a window overhead due to the way the fabric bunched at the closure, but this was irrelevant, as the balloon itself would have blocked any upward view. About a foot below one side window, I placed an eight-inch circular opening with a threaded brass rim, into which I screwed the condenser tube, keeping the machine itself inside the rubberized chamber."
}

