parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "82ce69142aa470703115783a",
  "rewritten_text": "Everyone lives like that, Huck. Tom, it makes no difference. I am not everyone, and I cannot stand it. Being tied down is miserable. Food comes too easily, so I have no appetite for it. I have to ask permission to go fishing or swimming; I have to ask to do everything. I had to speak so politely that it was no comfort at all. I had to go up to the attic and curse just to feel like myself, or I would have died. The widow would not let me smoke, yell, yawn, stretch, or scratch in front of people. And she prayed constantly! I have never seen such a woman. I had to leave, Tom; I just had to. Besides, school is starting, and I would have been forced to go. I would not stand for that. Look, Tom, being rich is not what people claim. It is just constant worry and sweat, and wishing you were dead. These clothes suit me, and this barrel suits me, and I am never giving them up. I would never have gotten into this mess if not for that money. Take my share along with yours, give me a dime now and then—not often, because I do not care for things unless they are hard to get—and you go convince the widow to let me be. Oh, Huck, you know I cannot do that. It is not fair, and if you try it a little longer, you will grow to like it. Like it? Yes, the way I would like a hot stove if I sat on it long enough. No, Tom, I will not be rich, and I will not live in those suffocating houses. I like the woods, the river, and these barrels, and I am sticking to them. Just when we had guns, a cave, and everything ready to start robbing, this foolishness has to ruin it all! Tom saw his chance. Look, Huck, being rich will not stop me from becoming a robber. Really? Are you serious, Tom? As serious as I am sitting here. But Huck, we cannot let you into the gang if you are not respectable. Huck’s joy vanished. You cannot let me in, Tom?"
}

