parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d7b62bb12cd6affc419a7930",
  "rewritten_text": "Seeing the creature, the orangutan bolted through the chamber door, down the stairs, and out an open window into the street. The Frenchman followed in despair, while the ape, razor still gripped in its hand, paused occasionally to gesture at its pursuer before darting away again. The chase continued through the silent, early morning streets. Passing an alley behind the Rue Morgue, the beast noticed a light shining from an open fourth-story window in Madame L’Espanaye’s home. It rushed to the building, scaled the lightning rod with incredible speed, grabbed the open shutter, and swung itself onto the bed’s headboard in less than a minute, kicking the shutter further open as it entered. The sailor felt both relief and dread. He hoped to trap the animal, as it could only escape by descending the rod, yet he feared what it might do inside. Driven by this anxiety, he followed. Though a sailor could easily climb the rod, he could only reach far enough to peer into the room once he neared the window. What he saw nearly caused him to fall in terror, triggering the horrific screams that woke the neighborhood. Madame L’Espanaye and her daughter, dressed for bed, had been organizing papers from an open iron chest in the center of the room. With their backs to the window, they likely did not notice the beast at first, assuming the shutter had been blown by the wind. As the sailor watched, the massive animal grabbed Madame L’Espanaye by her loose hair and brandished the razor near her face, mimicking the motions of a barber."
}

