parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c1d5a3ea22b4842db19ddb6b",
  "rewritten_text": "I spent two years this way. The days passed without external change, yet they slowly altered my mind as I drifted toward death. I studied more, empathized with the thoughts found in books, read history, and lost my sense of self among those who came before. As my immediate suffering faded, I became more human. Solitude lost its appeal; I began to crave connection. I did not want to join the masses, but I longed for one friend. You might think I was becoming ready to rejoin society, but I disagree. The sympathy I craved had to be so pure and free from external influence that I would inevitably have been disappointed by the crude realities of the world. I was less prepared for human interaction than ever. When I first left society, people tormented me like a sickness—something external I wanted to discard. Now, I would have sought a deep soul-connection, only to invite further suffering, for I had become as sensitive as a raw nerve. I did not want help with ambition or wisdom, but simple, mutual affection—smiles and gentle words. I wanted one heart to receive my complaints, hoping something good might grow from such sorrow. Yet, how could I find it? True friendship is a delicate spirit, rarely found unless two people are bonded from youth or through shared hardship. It arrives unbidden to the fortunate, like dew on barren ground, but it flees when pursued; it mocks those who beg for it. I understood this and did not go looking for it. Yet, there on my lonely heath, under my humble roof in that desolate place, it arrived like a winter sunbeam, illuminating the snow even as it melted it. Alas, the light shone on withered fruit; I could not revive under its warmth, for I was too broken to feel its kindness."
}

