parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cf8dc8ae1d6ceb4a45049ab0",
  "rewritten_text": "Yes, burglar’s tools. That seems to relieve you. But what scared you? What were you expecting us to find? Huck was in a tight spot. The man’s searching gaze was fixed on him, and he desperately needed a believable answer. Nothing came to mind, and as the man’s eyes bored into him, he blurted out a foolish reply: Sunday-school books, maybe. Huck was too anxious to smile, but the old man laughed heartily, shaking with mirth. He remarked that such a laugh was good for one’s health, then added, Poor boy, you look pale and exhausted. You aren’t well, which explains why you seem a bit rattled. Rest and sleep will set you right. Huck felt annoyed at himself for acting so suspicious. He had stopped believing the package from the tavern was the treasure after hearing the conversation at the widow’s gate, but he hadn't been certain. Now, he was relieved to know for sure that the bundle was not the treasure. Everything seemed to be falling into place; the gold must still be in room number two, the men would be arrested, and he and Tom could claim the loot that night without fear. Just as breakfast ended, a knock came at the door. Huck scrambled to hide, wanting no part in the recent events. The Welshman let in several guests, including the Widow Douglas, and saw crowds of people climbing the hill to look at the scene. The news had spread. The Welshman recounted the night’s events to the visitors. The widow expressed her deep gratitude for being saved. Don’t mention it, madam, the man replied. There is someone else you owe more to than me and my boys, but he won’t let me reveal his name. We wouldn’t have been there if not for him."
}

