parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e9d52a5e02fde38d79d96213",
  "rewritten_text": "I rushed to her, held her close, and pleaded, \"Speak to me, darling! What is wrong?\" Her head slumped against my chest as she whispered, \"Hello-Central!\" I shouted to Clarence, \"Quick! Call the king's doctor!\" Within moments, I was at the child's crib while Sandy sent servants racing through the palace. I recognized the symptoms immediately: membranous croup. I leaned down and whispered, \"Wake up, sweetheart.\" She opened her eyes weakly and murmured, \"Papa.\" That gave me hope. I gathered sulfur and prepared the croup-kettle myself; I never wait for doctors when my family is ill. I had nursed this child often, and I knew how to soothe her when even her mother could not. Sir Launcelot, dressed in his finest armor, strode past on his way to the stock exchange. He was the board's president, holding the seat he had purchased from Sir Galahad, as the Knights of the Round Table now used their table for business. Despite his intense focus on market maneuvers, he stopped when he saw his favorite child was ill. He tossed his helmet aside, immediately helping to light the lamp and prepare the kettle. Sandy built a blanket canopy over the crib, and we were ready. Launcelot and I added lime, carbolic acid, and lactic acid to the water, positioning the steam spout under the canopy. With everything prepared, we sat on either side of the crib to begin our watch."
}

