parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7d3f70a1e2d0e5198676c3a3",
  "rewritten_text": "Upon returning to the raft, the king tallied his earnings at eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He also brought back a three-gallon jug of whisky discovered beneath a wagon while walking through the woods. The king remarked that, all things considered, this surpassed any day he had spent in missionary work, noting that heathens were no match for pirates when it came to exploiting a camp meeting. The duke had felt satisfied with his own efforts until the king revealed his haul. He had earned four dollars printing horse bills for farmers and collected ten dollars for newspaper advertisements by offering a discount for advance payment. He also secured three subscriptions at half a dollar each, insisting on cash instead of the usual payment of wood and onions, claiming he had slashed prices after taking over the business. He even composed a melancholy three-verse poem titled \"Yes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart\" to include in the paper for free. In total, he had collected nine dollars and a half, which he considered a fair day's labor. He then presented a handbill he had printed for our benefit, featuring an illustration of a runaway slave with a bundle on a stick and a two-hundred-dollar reward notice. The text described Jim perfectly, stating he had fled a plantation near New Orleans and was likely heading north. The duke explained that this would allow us to travel by day. If approached, we could bind Jim, hide him in the wigwam, and claim we had captured him upriver, using the handbill to explain that we were taking him back to collect the reward because we lacked the funds for steamboat passage."
}

