parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "37def0f9c6c3131a47b354ec",
  "rewritten_text": "This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis had agreed to retain at Lady Canterville’s request. She curtsied as they arrived and said, 'Welcome to Canterville Chase.' They followed her through the Tudor hall into a library paneled in black oak, featuring a large stained-glass window. Tea was prepared, and they sat to look around while Mrs. Umney served them. Suddenly, Mrs. Otis noticed a dull red stain by the fireplace. Unaware of its history, she remarked, 'I fear something has been spilled there.' The housekeeper replied quietly, 'Blood has been spilled on that spot.' 'How horrid,' Mrs. Otis exclaimed. 'I dislike bloodstains in a sitting room. It must be removed.' The old woman smiled mysteriously. 'It is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, murdered there by her husband, Sir Simon, in 1575. Sir Simon vanished nine years later under strange circumstances. His body was never found, but his spirit haunts the house. The stain is famous and cannot be removed.' 'Nonsense,' Washington Otis declared. 'Pinkerton’s Champion Stain Remover will clean it instantly.' Before the terrified housekeeper could stop him, he knelt and scrubbed the floor with a cleaning stick. The stain vanished. 'I knew Pinkerton would work,' he said triumphantly. Immediately, a flash of lightning illuminated the room, a thunderclap made them jump, and Mrs. Umney fainted. 'What a monstrous climate!' the American Minister remarked, lighting a cigar. 'England is so crowded they lack decent weather. Emigration is the only solution.' 'My dear Hiram,' Mrs. Otis cried, 'what can we do with a woman who faints?' 'Charge it to her like breakages,' the Minister replied. 'She won’t faint again.' Shortly after, Mrs. Umney regained consciousness."
}

