parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "14025cbd15c94d471aaf3c1d",
  "rewritten_text": "I walked quickly to my college, suddenly shivering at the thought that the creature I had left in my apartment might still be there. I dreaded seeing the monster, but I feared even more that Henry might encounter him. I asked Henry to wait at the bottom of the stairs while I rushed up to my room. My hand was on the lock before I paused, trembling. I threw the door open, expecting a ghost, but the room was empty. Relieved that my enemy had fled, I clapped my hands and ran back down to Clerval. We went up to my room, but I could not control my excitement. My skin tingled, my pulse raced, and I could not sit still; I jumped over chairs and laughed loudly. Clerval initially thought I was just happy to see him, but my wild eyes and strange, heartless laughter soon frightened him. My dear Victor, he cried, what is wrong? Do not laugh like that. You look ill. What is the cause of this? Do not ask me, I shouted, covering my eyes as I imagined the monster entering the room. He can tell. Save me! I felt the creature grab me, struggled violently, and collapsed. Poor Clerval. He had looked forward to our meeting, only for it to turn into a nightmare. I did not see his distress, as I lost consciousness for a long time. This began a nervous fever that kept me bedridden for months. Henry was my only nurse. I later learned that he had kept the severity of my illness a secret to spare my elderly father and Elizabeth from unnecessary grief."
}

