parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2cfe0bf0aa7e15b5308caeb2",
  "rewritten_text": "You have nurtured an artificial soul and spirit in him, ma’am, unsuitable for his station; the board, Mrs. Sowerberry, being practical philosophers, will confirm this. What business have paupers with soul or spirit? It is sufficient that we permit them living bodies. Had you fed the boy only gruel, ma’am, this would not have occurred. Dear, dear! exclaimed Mrs. Sowerberry, piously lifting her eyes to the ceiling: this is the result of being generous! Mrs. Sowerberry’s generosity to Oliver had involved giving him all the scraps no one else would consume; thus, there was significant humility in her accepting Mr. Bumble’s harsh accusation, of which she was entirely innocent. Ah! said Mr. Bumble, once the lady looked down again; the only remaining option is to leave him in the cellar until he is sufficiently starved, then keep him on gruel throughout his apprenticeship. He comes from a bad family. Excitable natures, Mrs. Sowerberry! Both the nurse and doctor noted that his mother reached this place despite hardships that would have killed any decent woman weeks earlier. At this, Oliver, hearing enough to realize his mother was being discussed, resumed kicking with such force that all other sounds were drowned out. Sowerberry returned at this moment. After the ladies explained Oliver’s offense with exaggerations meant to provoke him, he quickly unlocked the cellar door and dragged his rebellious apprentice out by the collar. Oliver’s clothes were torn from the beating; his face was bruised and scratched, and his hair hung over his forehead. The angry flush remained, however, and as he was pulled from his prison, he glared defiantly at Noah, appearing entirely undaunted. Now, you are a fine young man, aren’t you? said Sowerberry, shaking Oliver and boxing his ear. He insulted my mother, Oliver replied. Well, what if he did, you ungrateful wretch? said Mrs. Sowerberry. She deserved what he said, and worse. She did not, said Oliver. She did, said Mrs. Sowerberry. It is a lie! said Oliver. Mrs. Sowerberry began to weep profusely. This outburst left Mr. Sowerberry no choice. Had he hesitated to punish Oliver severely, it would be clear to any experienced reader that he would have been, by all established marital precedents, a brute, an unnatural husband, and a base imitation of a man."
}

