parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2aa89ea67084f46e59b94e36",
  "rewritten_text": "Good God! If I had realized for even a second what my monstrous enemy was planning, I would have chosen to live as a lonely exile forever rather than agree to this wretched marriage. The creature had used some dark power to blind me to his true motives; I thought I was only preparing for my own death, but instead, I was rushing toward the destruction of someone far more precious. As our wedding date approached, I felt my heart sink, whether from fear or a dark premonition. I hid my dread behind a fake cheerfulness that pleased my father, though it barely fooled Elizabeth. She looked forward to our marriage with quiet hope, tempered by the fear that our happiness might vanish like a dream, leaving only regret. We made preparations and received guests, all while I hid my inner turmoil and acted as if I were excited for a future that felt like a trap. My father managed to recover some of Elizabeth’s inheritance, including a small property on the shores of Lake Como, where we planned to spend our honeymoon. Meanwhile, I took every precaution to defend myself, carrying a pistol and dagger at all times. This vigilance brought me some peace, and as the day neared, the monster’s threats began to feel like a delusion. Elizabeth seemed happy, and my calm behavior helped settle her nerves. Yet, on the day of our wedding, she was melancholy and filled with a sense of impending doom, perhaps because she was thinking of the terrible secret I had promised to reveal to her the following day."
}

