  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Otis was troubled by Lord Canterville’s refusal and urged him to reconsider, but the peer remained firm. He did, however, convince the Minister to let his daughter keep the gift the ghost had given her. In the spring of 1890, when the young Duchess of Cheshire was presented at the Queen’s drawing-room following her wedding, her jewels were widely admired. Virginia had received the coronet, the traditional reward for good American girls, and married her young suitor once he came of age. They were a charming couple, and everyone was happy for them, except for the Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had hoped to secure the Duke for one of her own daughters, and, surprisingly, Mr. Otis. While Mr. Otis liked the young Duke, he held strong republican views and worried that his daughter might lose her simple values among the aristocracy. Nevertheless, his concerns were set aside, and as he walked his daughter down the aisle at St. George’s, Hanover Square, he was surely the proudest man in England. After their honeymoon, the Duke and Duchess visited Canterville Chase. The day after arriving, they walked to the quiet churchyard near the pine woods. After some debate, they had decided to mark Sir Simon’s grave with only his initials and the verse from the library window. Virginia scattered roses over the grave before they wandered into the ruined abbey. As she sat on a fallen pillar and her husband rested at her feet, he asked her to share the secret of what happened when she was locked away with the ghost. Virginia replied solemnly that she had never told anyone."
}

