  "rewritten_text": "She would make such a charming friend for Emma. Mr. Woodhouse concurred, yet added, Our young friend Harriet Smith is also a lovely sort of girl. You will enjoy Harriet. Emma could not ask for a better companion. I am delighted to hear it, but Jane Fairfax is known to be exceptionally talented and refined, and she is precisely Emma’s age. This subject was discussed quite pleasantly, followed by other topics of equal importance that passed with similar accord. However, the evening did not conclude without a brief moment of distress. The arrival of the gruel prompted extensive commentary, including praise for its health benefits and harsh criticism of households that failed to prepare it properly. Unfortunately, the daughter cited her own cook at South End as a prime example of such incompetence, noting the woman could never grasp the concept of a smooth, perfectly thin basin of gruel. This remark created a precarious situation. Ah, Mr. Woodhouse sighed, shaking his head with sympathetic concern. To Emma, his tone implied that the consequences of her trip to South End were truly endless and best left unmentioned. She hoped he would remain silent, allowing him to return to his own meal, but after a few minutes, he began again. I shall always regret that you chose the seaside this autumn rather than visiting us. Why should you be sorry, sir? It was very beneficial for the children. Furthermore, if you had to go to the coast, South End was a poor choice. It is considered unhealthy, and Perry was surprised you selected it. I am aware that many hold that opinion, but it is a misconception. We were perfectly healthy there and experienced no issues with the mud. Mr. Wingfield confirms the location is not unhealthy; he understands the climate well, and his own brother has visited repeatedly. You ought to have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you had to travel at all. Perry spent a week there once and considers it the finest of all the seaside resorts."
}

