parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e15764dbc6a2eeff2ae88196",
  "rewritten_text": "“Surely you don’t mean—” my sister started. “Indeed I do, Mum,” Pumblechook interrupted, “but hold on. Continue, Joseph. Well done! Proceed!” “What would everyone here say,” Joe continued, “to twenty pounds?” “I would call it generous,” my sister replied. “Well then,” Joe said, “it is more than twenty pounds.” That wretched hypocrite Pumblechook nodded, adding with a condescending chuckle, “It is more than that, Mum. Excellent! Keep going, Joseph!” “To finish it,” Joe said, happily handing the bag to my sister, “it is twenty-five pounds.” “It is twenty-five pounds, Mum,” that deceitful swindler Pumblechook repeated, standing to shake her hand, “and it is no more than you deserve, as I stated when consulted, and I congratulate you on the funds!” Had the villain stopped there, his conduct would have been bad enough, but he worsened his offense by seizing me with a sense of ownership that eclipsed his previous behavior. “Now, Joseph and wife,” Pumblechook declared, gripping my arm, “I am a man who finishes what he starts. This boy must be apprenticed immediately. That is my way. Bound at once.” “Heaven knows, Uncle Pumblechook,” my sister said, clutching the cash, “we are very grateful.” “Think nothing of it, Mum,” the wicked corn-merchant replied. “A pleasure is a pleasure everywhere. But this boy, you see; he must be bound. I promised to arrange it, to be honest.” The magistrates were holding court nearby, so we went immediately to have me apprenticed to Joe before them. I say we went, but Pumblechook shoved me along as if I had just committed a crime. The crowd clearly assumed I was guilty; as he pushed me through, people whispered, “What did he do?” and “He is young, but looks like trouble, doesn't he?” One kind-looking person even handed me a pamphlet featuring a drawing of a wicked youth in heavy chains, titled To Be Read In My Cell. The hall seemed strange, with pews taller than those in church, onlookers leaning over the railings, and powerful magistrates—one with a powdered wig—lounging in chairs, taking snuff, dozing, or reading papers, while dark portraits hung on the walls that appeared to my untrained eyes like a mix of candy and adhesive."
}

