parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f4a3c9ee7d2db81c33941c92",
  "rewritten_text": "Yes, it is, and I could have had him if I were big enough; I saw him first. Who caught him? It was an old stranger who sold his claim for forty dollars because he had to head upriver and could not wait. Imagine that! I would have waited seven years. I agree, but perhaps his claim is not worth more if he sold it so cheaply. Maybe something is not right. It is perfectly legitimate. I saw the handbill myself. It describes him exactly, including the plantation he is from below New Orleans. There is no doubt about that deal. Give me a chew of tobacco, will you? I had none, so he left. I went to the raft and sat in the shelter to think, but I could not reach a conclusion. I thought until my head ached, but I saw no way out. After our long journey and all we did for those scoundrels, everything was ruined because they had the heart to betray Jim, making him a slave for life among strangers for forty dirty dollars. I briefly thought it would be better for Jim to be a slave back home with his family, so I considered writing to Tom Sawyer to tell Miss Watson where he was. I dismissed that idea for two reasons: she would be furious at his ingratitude and sell him down the river anyway, and everyone would despise him, making him feel disgraced. And then there was me. It would get out that Huck Finn helped a slave gain his freedom, and if I ever saw anyone from home again, I would be so ashamed I would have to grovel. That is how it goes; a person does something low and then wants to avoid the consequences, thinking that as long as it is hidden, it is not a disgrace. That was my situation. The more I thought about it, the more my conscience gnawed at me, and the more wicked and low I felt."
}

