parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "aafd61e81d389ceb484fa57e",
  "rewritten_text": "Furthermore, this specific knot is rarely tied by anyone but a sailor, and it is characteristic of the Maltese. I discovered the ribbon beneath the lightning-rod; it could not have been owned by either victim. Even if my deduction that the Frenchman is a sailor from a Maltese ship is incorrect, my advertisement remains harmless. If I am mistaken, he will simply assume I was misled by circumstances he need not investigate. However, if I am correct, a significant advantage is achieved. Though innocent of the crime, the Frenchman will naturally be wary of answering the notice to reclaim his orangutan. He will think: I am innocent and poor, and this animal is a fortune. Why lose it to groundless fear? It was found far from the murder scene in the Bois de Boulogne. How could a beast be suspected of such violence? The police have no leads. Even if they find the animal, they cannot prove my involvement. Most importantly, I am known. The advertiser identifies me as the owner. I do not know how much he truly understands. If I fail to claim such valuable property, I only invite suspicion. It is safer to retrieve the creature and keep it hidden until the affair subsides. Just then, footsteps sounded on the stairs. Be prepared with your pistols, Dupin whispered, but do not draw or display them until I signal. The front door was unlocked, and the visitor had entered without ringing, pausing on the stairs before turning back. Then, he resumed his ascent, finally reaching our door and knocking firmly. Come in, Dupin called out cheerfully. A man entered. He was clearly a sailor—tall, robust, and muscular, with a bold, reckless expression that was not entirely unappealing."
}

