  "rewritten_text": "She now wished the encounter had not occurred. Assuming he had consumed too much of Mr. Weston’s wine, she expected him to talk nonsense. Hoping to keep him composed, she prepared to discuss the weather with calm gravity. However, as soon as they passed the gate and joined the other carriage, he interrupted her, grabbed her hand, and began declaring his love. He spoke of his adoration, his fear of rejection, and his intense passion, clearly expecting her to accept him immediately. Mr. Elton, who she thought loved Harriet, was now professing his love for her. She tried to stop him, but he persisted. Though angry, she decided to remain restrained, hoping his behavior was merely the result of intoxication. She replied with a mix of seriousness and humor, hoping to address his state: \"I am astonished, Mr. Elton. You forget yourself. You must mistake me for my friend. I will gladly deliver any message to Miss Smith, but please, no more of this to me.\" He repeated her words with a feigned, boastful amazement that forced her to respond sharply: \"Mr. Elton, this is extraordinary. I can only assume you are not yourself, or you would not speak this way to me or about Harriet. Please stop, and I will try to forget this.\" But Mr. Elton was not too drunk to understand his own actions. He denied her accusations, dismissed his interest in Miss Smith, and continued to press her urgently for a favorable answer."
}

