parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2ec4de406d0e321dd05c4847",
  "rewritten_text": "I traveled to the plantation and resumed my questioning of the elderly laborers. Eventually, one aged woman mentioned she knew of Bessop’s Castle, though she clarified it was actually a tall rock rather than a building. I promised her payment, and after some hesitation, she agreed to lead me there. Once we arrived, I sent her away and began my inspection. The site was a cluster of crags, one of which stood out for its height and unnatural, isolated shape. I climbed to the top, uncertain of my next move. While pondering, I noticed a narrow shelf on the eastern side, about a yard below where I stood. It was roughly eighteen inches long and a foot wide, and a nearby niche gave it the look of an antique chair. I felt certain this was the 'devil’s seat' mentioned in the manuscript, and the mystery began to unravel. I realized 'good glass' referred to a telescope, a common term among sailors. It was clear I needed to use a telescope from this exact spot. I concluded that the specific degrees and compass directions were instructions for aiming the instrument. Thrilled, I rushed home to retrieve my telescope and returned to the rock. I lowered myself onto the ledge, finding I could only sit in one specific way, which reinforced my theory. I began using the glass, interpreting the degrees as the elevation above the horizon, while the compass provided the horizontal bearing. After setting the direction, I adjusted the elevation until I spotted a circular gap in the leaves of a distant, towering tree."
}

