parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ae82d07b82b6d83a889e301d",
  "rewritten_text": "She felt a sudden urge to check the front door to see if it was raining. Why question her own motives? Captain Wentworth had to be gone. She stood up, determined to go; she was tired of her mind constantly second-guessing itself. She would simply check the weather. However, she was immediately forced back by the arrival of Captain Wentworth himself, accompanied by a group of friends he must have met nearby. He seemed more visibly startled and flustered by her presence than she had ever seen him; his face flushed red. For the first time since they had reconnected, she felt she was handling the encounter with more composure than he was. She had the benefit of a few moments to prepare, and the initial, overwhelming shock had passed. Still, she felt a turbulent mix of agitation, pain, and pleasure. He spoke to her briefly before turning away, his manner clearly awkward. It was neither cold nor friendly; it was simply embarrassed. After a short while, he approached her again. They exchanged polite, meaningless pleasantries, though Anne noticed he was far less at ease than usual. While they had recently managed to speak with a veneer of indifference, he could not maintain it now. Whether due to time or Louisa, he was clearly unsettled. He looked healthy and spoke of the Musgroves and even Louisa with a fleeting hint of his old playfulness, yet he remained visibly uncomfortable and unable to hide it. It saddened, though did not surprise, Anne to see Elizabeth refuse to acknowledge him. She saw the mutual recognition between them and knew he was prepared to be greeted, yet she had to watch her sister turn away with icy indifference. Finally, the servant arrived to announce that Lady Dalrymple’s carriage had pulled up, much to Elizabeth’s relief."
}

