parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c776d4f8c4a223a488195648",
  "rewritten_text": "With a racing heart and a sense of dread I could not explain, I sent the servant away, locked my door, and sat down to read my father’s letter. It read as follows: My dear child, I have betrayed your trust. I have tried to corrupt your mind and exposed your innocent heart to the language of forbidden and monstrous passion. I must pay for these crimes, and I must try to make my punishment fit my guilt. I am sure you are prepared for what I am about to say: we must separate and be divided forever. I am taking away your parent and only friend. You are cast out into the world without shelter; your hopes are ruined, and the peace of your pure mind is destroyed. Memory will bring you frightening images of guilt and the pain of innocent love betrayed. Yet I, who have brought all this misery upon you, who have cast you out and cruelly marked my own child with distrust and agony, who have tried to steal your beauty and replace it with the ugliness of sin—I, in the depths of my suffering, beg you to forgive me. I do not ask for your pity, for you must hate me. But pardon me, Mathilda, and do not follow me into exile with constant anger. I must never see you again or hear your voice, but the soft whisper of your forgiveness will reach me and soothe my troubled mind. I am sure I would feel it even in my grave. I dare to make this request by explaining how I was trapped in this web of agony and how I struggled to escape it. If your soul were less pure, I would not try to justify myself, as I would fear that making you hate me less might make you hate evil less. But in speaking to you, I feel as if I am appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness, and I must try to earn it or I will despair. I beg you to listen to my words. If guilt can be lessened by sharp agony and the remorse that drives one to madness, perhaps you might think—though I dare not—that I deserve some compassion. I ask you to remember our first happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had arrived after sixteen years of weary wandering, during which, despite many dangers and misfortunes, my heart had remained empty."
}

