parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7aa91bfb46a7895d61cadd0d",
  "rewritten_text": "Eventually, the Westminster tower clock struck twelve, the deep resonance making the night seem to shudder. The railway lights extinguished, leaving only one lamp glowing like a ruby on a mast, while the city's roar faded. At two, he rose and walked toward Blackfriars. Everything appeared surreal, like a strange dream. The houses across the river seemed constructed of darkness, as if silver and shadow had remade the world. St. Paul’s dome loomed like a bubble in the dusky air. Near Cleopatra’s Needle, he spotted a man leaning over the parapet. As he approached, the man looked up, the gaslight illuminating his face. It was Mr. Podgers, the palmist. His flabby face, gold-rimmed glasses, weak smile, and sensual mouth were unmistakable. Lord Arthur paused, a sudden idea striking him, and crept up behind. He grabbed Mr. Podgers by the legs and threw him into the Thames. A coarse oath and a heavy splash followed, then silence. Lord Arthur peered over, seeing only a top hat spinning in the moonlit water before it sank. He briefly imagined he saw the bulky figure swimming toward the bridge stairs, feeling a sickening sense of failure, but it was merely a reflection. When the moon emerged from the clouds, the vision vanished. Believing he had fulfilled his destiny, he sighed with relief, whispering Sybil’s name. A voice suddenly asked if he had dropped something. He turned to see a policeman with a lantern. He smiled, replied that it was nothing important, hailed a passing cab, and headed to Belgrave Square. For the next few days, he fluctuated between hope and fear. Sometimes he expected Mr. Podgers to walk in; other times, he felt Fate could not be so cruel. He visited the palmist’s West Moon Street address twice but could not bring himself to ring the bell, torn between a longing for certainty and a fear of it. Finally, the news arrived. He was in the club smoking room, listening wearily to Surbiton discuss a song from the Gaiety, when a waiter entered with the evening papers."
}

