parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f6d1fe585e6187312af934b3",
  "rewritten_text": "I rushed to her, held her close, and pleaded, \"Speak to me, darling! What is wrong?\" Her head slumped against my chest as she whispered, \"Hello-Central!\" I shouted to Clarence to summon the king's doctor immediately. Within moments, I was at the child's crib while Sandy sent servants racing through the palace. I recognized the illness instantly: croup. I leaned down and whispered, \"Wake up, sweetheart.\" She opened her eyes slowly and murmured, \"Papa.\" That gave me hope. I gathered sulfur and prepared the croup kettle myself; I never wait for doctors when my family is ill. I had plenty of experience nursing them, and I could often soothe this child when even her mother could not. Sir Launcelot soon strode past the hall, dressed in his finest armor on his way to the stock exchange. He served as president of the board, which was now comprised of the Knights of the Round Table conducting business. Despite his busy schedule and his aggressive financial dealings, he stopped when he saw his favorite child was sick. He tossed his helmet aside and immediately helped prepare the croup kettle. Sandy built a tent over the crib with blankets, and we were soon ready. Launcelot and I filled the kettle with lime, carbolic acid, and lactic acid, added water, and directed the steam under the canopy. Everything was set, and we sat on either side of the crib to begin our watch."
}

