parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "58305c1d6ec29b898292fa79",
  "rewritten_text": "If you look at a star indirectly—using the outer edges of your retina, which are more sensitive to faint light—you see it more clearly. Its brightness actually fades if you stare directly at it. While more light hits your eye when looking straight on, the indirect view allows for better perception. Thinking too deeply can confuse and weaken the mind; even Venus can disappear from the sky if you study it with too much intensity. Regarding these murders, let us investigate for ourselves before forming an opinion. It will be an interesting exercise, and Le Bon once did me a favor I have not forgotten. We will visit the scene. I know the Prefect of Police and can easily get permission. We received authorization and went to the Rue Morgue, a dreary street between Rue Richelieu and Rue St. Roch. It was late afternoon when we arrived. The house was easy to find because a crowd was still gathered across the street, staring at the closed shutters. It was a typical Parisian building with a gateway and a small concierge booth. Before entering, we walked around the block and through an alley to examine the back of the house. Dupin studied the entire neighborhood and the building with a level of detail I found puzzling. We returned to the front, rang the bell, and were admitted by the police. We went upstairs to the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye’s body was found and where the victims still lay. The room remained in its original state of disarray. I saw nothing that had not already been reported in the newspaper, but Dupin examined everything, including the bodies. Accompanied by a guard, we then inspected the other rooms and the yard."
}

