parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3b567144423405ac395d5b8f",
  "rewritten_text": "Please consider, ma'am, Oliver said, tears filling his eyes despite his efforts to stop them. Think of how young and kind she is, and the joy she brings to everyone. I am certain that for your sake, and for her own, she will not die. Heaven would never let her pass away so young. Hush, Mrs. Maylie said, resting her hand on Oliver's head. You think like a child, but you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it for a moment, but I hope to be forgiven. I am old and have seen enough illness to know the pain of losing those we love. I also know that the youngest and best are not always spared, but this should comfort us. Heaven is just, and these trials remind us that there is a better world than this. God's will be done. I love her, and He knows how much. Oliver was surprised to see Mrs. Maylie suddenly stop her grieving and stand tall, appearing calm and firm. He was even more amazed that this strength lasted. Throughout the long care that followed, she remained composed and ready, performing her duties steadily and even cheerfully. He was young and did not understand what strong minds can endure during difficult times. A restless night followed. By morning, Mrs. Maylie’s fears were confirmed. Rose was suffering from a severe and dangerous fever. We must be active and not give in to useless grief, Mrs. Maylie said, looking firmly at him. This letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne immediately. Take it to the market town four miles away by the footpath, and have it sent by horseback to Chertsey. The people at the inn will handle it, and I know I can trust you to see it done. Oliver could not speak, but his expression showed his eagerness to leave at once."
}

