parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a258c666fd8626689037e2f6",
  "rewritten_text": "With a racing heart and an inexplicable dread, I sent away the servant, bolted my door, and sat to read my father’s letter. Its contents were as follows: My dear Child, I have violated your trust; I have sought to corrupt your thoughts, exposing your innocent heart to the gaze and speech of forbidden, monstrous desire. I must atone for these sins, attempting to match my penance to my transgression. You are surely ready for my announcement: we must part, separated forever. I strip you of your father and sole companion. You are abandoned, homeless; your prospects ruined, the tranquility of your pure spirit shattered. Memory will haunt you with visions of guilt and the pain of betrayed innocence. Yet I, who have brought this misery upon you, who have cast you out and cruelly branded my own child with agony, who with wicked flippancy tried to replace your beauty with the ugliness of sin—I, in my profound sorrow, beg your forgiveness. I do not seek your pity; you must loathe me. But pardon me, Mathilda, and do not pursue me into exile with unceasing wrath. I must never see you or hear your voice again, yet the gentle murmur of your pardon might soothe my fevered mind and heart; I believe I would feel it even in death. I venture to make this plea by explaining how wretchedly I was ensnared in this trap of torment and my efforts to escape. If your soul were less radiant, I would not try to justify myself, fearing that lessening your abhorrence might diminish your hatred of vice. However, speaking to you feels like addressing an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your absolution, or I shall fall into despair. I implore you to hear me; if guilt can be lessened by intense suffering and the madness of remorse, perhaps you might consider, though I dare not, that I deserve your compassion. I beg you to recall our early, joyful life by Loch Lomond. I had returned from sixteen years of weary travel, during which, despite many perils and hardships, my heart had remained entirely empty."
}

