parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c36579e28eb74717df228b7c",
  "rewritten_text": "I want to see you settled in a good social position, so it is best to avoid unusual acquaintances. If you are still here when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your friendship with his sisters does not force you to know his wife, who will likely be an uneducated farmer’s daughter. Certainly. I do not think Mr. Martin would marry someone without an education, but I will not argue with you. I will not seek out his wife. I care for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and would hate to lose them, as they are as well-educated as I am. But if he marries someone ignorant and vulgar, I will avoid her if I can. Emma watched her reaction and saw no signs of love. The young man had been interested first, but she hoped Harriet felt no deep attachment that would block her own plans. They met Mr. Martin the next day on the Donwell road. He was on foot and looked at Harriet with genuine joy. Emma walked ahead to observe him. He looked neat and sensible, but lacked any other distinction. She felt he would seem inferior when compared to true gentlemen. Harriet noticed his lack of polish, as she had previously admired her father’s refined manners. Mr. Martin seemed to have no manners at all. They spoke for only a few minutes so as not to delay Miss Woodhouse. Harriet returned with a smile and a flutter of excitement that Emma hoped to calm. Just think of meeting him! How strange! He said it was pure luck he did not take the other road. He did not think we walked this way, as he assumed we usually headed toward Randalls."
}

