parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "71e271d2878a8d847118cbcc",
  "rewritten_text": "I told myself I wanted no part in such affairs. Descending the stairs the next morning, I found the parlor closed and the vigil keepers departed. Only the family, Widow Bartley, and our group remained. I scanned their expressions for signs of recent events, but found none. By midday, the undertaker and his assistant arrived, placing the coffin on chairs in the center of the room. They arranged our chairs in rows, supplementing them with borrowed ones until the hall, parlor, and dining room were crowded. I noticed the coffin lid remained as it had been, yet I dared not peek inside while others were present. As guests arrived, the con artists and the girls occupied the front row. For thirty minutes, mourners filed past in a single line, pausing to gaze at the deceased. Some shed tears in the heavy silence, broken only by the sobbing of the girls and the shuffling of feet or blowing of noses—a common funeral occurrence. Once the room was filled, the undertaker moved about in black gloves, his manner soothing and feline. He managed the crowd and the arrangements with silent gestures and nods, eventually taking his place against the wall. He was the most stealthy, humorless man I had ever encountered. A wheezing melodeon was brought in, and after a young woman played it, the congregation sang, though in my opinion, only Peter sounded decent. Reverend Hobson began his solemn address, but was immediately interrupted by a tremendous commotion from the cellar. A single dog created such a deafening racket that the parson was forced to pause, unable to compete with the noise."
}

