parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6c674d23ecbe7ed0b2ea2b88",
  "rewritten_text": "She studied me intently for a moment before asking quietly, \"What do you want for them?\" I replied, \"Only that you do not group them with the others. They may share the same blood, but they are not the same in nature.\" She persisted, \"What do you want for them?\" Feeling myself blush, I admitted, \"I am not clever enough to hide that I do want something. Miss Havisham, if you would provide the funds to help my friend Herbert in a way he must never discover, I could show you how.\" She rested her hands on her cane to watch me more closely. \"Why must he not know?\" she asked. \"Because I began helping him two years ago without his knowledge, and I do not want to be exposed. I cannot explain why I cannot finish the task; it is someone else's secret.\" She turned her gaze to the fire. After a long silence, the coals shifted, and she looked back at me with renewed focus. Estella continued knitting. Miss Havisham asked, \"What else?\" I turned to Estella, my voice shaking. \"Estella, you know I love you. I have loved you for a long time.\" Estella looked up, her expression indifferent as her needles kept moving. Miss Havisham watched us both. \"I would have said this sooner, but I was mistaken in thinking Miss Havisham intended us for each other. I held back while I believed you had no choice in the matter, but I must speak now.\" Estella kept knitting and shook her head. I responded to her gesture, \"I know. I have no hope that you will ever be mine, Estella.\""
}

