  "rewritten_text": "It is just a tick. Where did you find him? In the woods. What will you take for him? I do not know. I do not want to sell him. Fine. It is a very small tick anyway. Anyone can catch a tick that is not theirs. I am happy with this one. It is good enough for me. There are plenty of ticks. I could have a thousand if I wanted. Well, why do you not? Because you know you cannot. This is an early tick. It is the first one I have seen this year. Huck, I will give you my tooth for him. Let me see it. Tom unwrapped a piece of paper. Huckleberry looked at it longingly. The temptation was strong. Finally, he asked if it was real. Tom lifted his lip to show the gap. All right, it is a trade. Tom put the tick in the small box that used to hold a beetle, and the boys parted, both feeling richer. When Tom arrived at the schoolhouse, he walked in quickly, acting as if he had hurried. He hung up his hat and sat down. The teacher, sitting in his large chair, was dozing off to the sound of the students studying. The noise woke him. Thomas Sawyer! Tom knew that being called by his full name meant trouble. Sir! Come here. Why are you late again? Tom was about to lie when he saw two yellow braids hanging down a back he recognized. That girl was sitting in the only empty seat on the girls' side. He immediately said, I stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn! The teacher stared in shock. The room went silent. The students wondered if Tom had lost his mind. The teacher asked what he had done. I stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn. Thomas Sawyer, this is the most shocking confession I have ever heard. A simple punishment will not do for this. Take off your jacket. The teacher whipped him until his arm was tired and the switches were worn down. Then he ordered, Go and sit with the girls! Let this be a warning. The laughter that spread through the room seemed to embarrass Tom, but in reality, he was overwhelmed by his awe for the girl and the thrill of his good fortune."
}

