parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "964655a8eb0560d8ba56fc48",
  "rewritten_text": "Ten minutes later, the tea bell rang. When Virginia failed to appear, Mrs. Otis sent a servant to find her, but he returned saying she was nowhere to be found. Initially, Mrs. Otis was not worried, as Virginia often gathered flowers in the garden. However, by six o'clock, she grew truly anxious. She sent her sons to search while she and Mr. Otis checked every room. By half-past six, the boys returned empty-handed. In a panic, Mr. Otis remembered allowing a group of gypsies to camp in the park days earlier. He immediately set off for Blackfell Hollow with his eldest son and two farmhands. The young Duke of Cheshire begged to join them, but Mr. Otis refused, fearing a confrontation. Upon arrival, they found the camp abandoned; the fire still smoldered and plates remained on the grass, suggesting a hasty departure. After sending his son and the men to search the area, Mr. Otis rushed home to wire the local police about a kidnapped girl. He then ordered his horse, insisted his family eat dinner, and rode toward the Ascot Road with a groom. He had traveled only two miles when he heard hoofbeats. The young Duke was galloping after him on a pony, hatless and flushed. The boy gasped that he could not eat while Virginia was missing and pleaded to stay, suggesting that if they had been allowed to marry, this would not have happened. He refused to turn back. Mr. Otis, touched by the boy's devotion, smiled and agreed to let him join the search, provided they stopped in Ascot to find him a hat."
}

