parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "28f148f669778c2d693b6aeb",
  "rewritten_text": "This marriage is the right choice. I doubted it initially, but I concede the point now. Sibyl Vane was created for you; without her, you would remain incomplete. Dorian Gray squeezed his friend's hand in gratitude. I knew you would understand. Harry is so cynical he frightens me. But the orchestra is starting. It is terrible, but it only lasts five minutes. Then the curtain rises, and you will see the girl to whom I have dedicated my life and all the goodness within me. Fifteen minutes later, amidst thunderous applause, Sibyl Vane appeared. She was undeniably beautiful, perhaps the most lovely creature Lord Henry had ever seen. Her shy grace and startled expression gave her the air of a fawn. A faint blush touched her cheeks as she looked out at the enthusiastic crowd, and her lips trembled. Basil Hallward stood to applaud, while Dorian Gray remained motionless, staring at her as if in a trance. Lord Henry peered through his glasses, whispering that she was charming. The scene was the Capulet hall, and Romeo had entered with his friends. As the music played, Sibyl moved through the group of poorly dressed actors like a being from a higher realm. Her movements were fluid, her throat curved like a lily, and her hands appeared to be carved from cool ivory. Yet, she seemed strangely detached. She showed no joy when she looked at Romeo. Her lines were delivered with a hollow, artificial tone. While her voice was exquisite, the delivery was completely false, draining the life from the verse and rendering the passion unreal. Dorian Gray turned pale, watching her with growing confusion and anxiety. His friends remained silent, both realizing she was utterly incompetent."
}

