parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e95beabf2d62913e38a019e6",
  "rewritten_text": "Upon arriving at the stairhead, he composed himself and prepared his famous demonic cackle. This performance had proven quite effective previously, having reportedly whitened Lord Raker’s hair overnight and caused three of Lady Canterville’s governesses to resign prematurely. He unleashed his most terrifying laugh, causing the vaulted ceiling to vibrate, but the echo had barely faded when Mrs. Otis emerged in a blue dressing-gown. She offered him a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture, suggesting it would cure his apparent indigestion. Enraged, the ghost prepared to transform into a large black dog—a feat credited with causing the permanent mental decline of the Hon. Thomas Horton—but the sound of footsteps forced him to reconsider. Instead, he merely glowed with a faint phosphorescence and vanished with a mournful groan just as the twins arrived. Back in his chamber, he collapsed in a state of intense agitation. While the twins’ rudeness and Mrs. Otis’s materialism were irritating, he was most distressed by his failure to wear his suit of mail. He had expected even Americans to be impressed by a Spectre in Armour, perhaps out of deference to Longfellow, whose poetry had often occupied his lonely hours. Furthermore, it was his own armor, worn successfully at the Kenilworth tournament and praised by the Virgin Queen. Yet, the weight of the breastplate and helmet had proven too much, causing him to collapse and suffer bruised knees and knuckles. He remained unwell for several days, rarely leaving his room except to maintain the bloodstain. Eventually, he recovered and determined to attempt a third time to terrify the American Minister and his family."
}

