parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "da65b06ac6858d31983361d3",
  "rewritten_text": "What is wrong? Oliver asked. Hush, the Dodger replied. Do you see that old man at the bookstand? The gentleman across the street? Oliver said. Yes, I see him. He will do, the Dodger said. A perfect target, Charley Bates added. Oliver looked between them in confusion, but they did not let him ask questions. The two boys crept across the road and slipped behind the man. Oliver followed a few steps, unsure whether to stay or leave, watching in silent shock. The gentleman looked respectable, with powdered hair and gold spectacles. He wore a dark green coat with a velvet collar, white trousers, and carried a bamboo cane. He had picked up a book and was reading as intently as if he were in his own study. He seemed completely absorbed, unaware of the street or the boys, focused entirely on the pages he turned with eager interest. Oliver watched in horror as the Dodger reached into the man's pocket and pulled out a handkerchief. He handed it to Charley Bates, and both boys sprinted around the corner. Suddenly, the truth about the handkerchiefs, watches, jewels, and the old man dawned on Oliver. Terror surged through him like fire. Confused and terrified, he panicked and began to run as fast as he could. It all happened in a minute. As Oliver fled, the gentleman reached for his pocket, realized his handkerchief was gone, and turned around. Seeing the boy running away, he assumed Oliver was the thief. He shouted Stop thief! at the top of his lungs and chased after him, still clutching his book."
}

