parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "94ba1c8263f24974e0fe3a14",
  "rewritten_text": "Lay down your weapons, brothers! Forgiveness, help, and friendship await your change of heart. You are precious to us because you share our fragile human form; each of you will find a friend among these ranks. Why should man fight man while the plague, our common enemy, looms over us, thriving on our slaughter and proving more cruel than we are? Both armies hesitated. On our side, soldiers gripped their weapons tightly, eyeing the enemy with stern resolve. The opposing troops had not dropped their arms, driven more by fear than a desire to fight; they looked to one another for a lead, but they had no commander. Adrian dismounted and approached a fallen soldier. He is a man, he cried, and he is dead. Quickly, bind the wounds of the fallen. Do not let another soul escape through your brutal cuts to tell God of this fratricide. Cast aside your savage hearts and drop these tools of hate. In this moment of shared destiny, let every man be a brother and guardian to the next. Discard those blood-stained weapons and help treat these wounds. As he spoke, he knelt and lifted a man whose lifeblood was pouring out. The poor soul gasped, and in the sudden silence of both armies, his moans were clearly heard. Every heart, recently set on massacre, now beat with anxious hope for this one man. Adrian tore off his scarf to bind the wound, but it was too late. The man sighed, his head fell back, and his body went limp. He is dead, Adrian said, bowing his head in sorrow. The fate of the world seemed tied to this single death. On both sides, soldiers dropped their weapons. Even the veterans wept, and our men reached out to their foes, filled with a sudden, profound sense of unity. The two forces merged, unarmed and hand in hand, discussing how to help one another. Regretting their past cruelty and recent violence, they followed the General’s orders to march toward London."
}

