  "request_id": "5f21fa68e5705e0861d80eb4",
  "rewritten_text": "She left everything behind, save for the memory that it had once existed. Anne had not stepped foot in Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell’s home in September. She had avoided the Hall whenever possible, but now she returned to the elegant Lodge to delight its mistress. Lady Russell felt a mix of joy and anxiety, aware of Anne’s time at Uppercross. Fortunately, she thought Anne looked healthier, and Anne privately hoped this meant she was regaining her youthful bloom. As they talked, Anne realized her priorities had shifted. The concerns that once consumed her at Kellynch now felt secondary. Even her father and sister in Bath seemed distant compared to her experiences at Uppercross. When Lady Russell discussed their family affairs, Anne felt guilty for being far more preoccupied with her friends in Lyme, particularly the Harvilles and Captain Benwick. She had to force herself to show interest in her own family’s matters. Discussing the accident at Lyme was initially awkward. Lady Russell had already heard the details, but they still needed to process the event, the imprudence involved, and the mention of Captain Wentworth. Anne struggled to remain composed while speaking his name, only finding relief once she explained her perspective on his attachment to Louisa Musgrove."
}

