parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6358e53c833fa7694e9e995f",
  "rewritten_text": "I agree. It is for the best. Will you hold hands and swear that we— Oh no, that will not work for this. That is fine for trivial matters, especially with girls, because they always go back on their word if they get upset. For something this important, we need to write it down. And use blood. Tom loved the idea. It felt serious and dark, fitting the time and place perfectly. He picked up a clean pine shingle in the moonlight, took a piece of red chalk from his pocket, and carefully wrote, pressing his tongue between his teeth as he worked. Huck Finn and Tom Sawyer swear they will keep quiet about this, and they wish they may drop dead in their tracks if they ever tell and rot. Huckleberry admired Tom’s writing and his grand language. He pulled a pin from his lapel to prick his finger, but Tom stopped him. Wait, don't do that. The pin is brass and might have verdigris on it. What is verdigris? It is poison. Swallow some and you will see. Tom used a needle to prick their thumbs, squeezing out drops of blood. After some effort, Tom signed his initials using his finger, then showed Huckleberry how to write his. The oath was complete. They buried the shingle near the wall with solemn rituals, sealing their secret forever. A figure crept through the ruined building, but they did not notice. Tom, Huckleberry whispered, does this mean we can never tell, no matter what? Of course. It does not matter what happens; we must stay silent or we will drop dead. Do you understand? Yes, I suppose so. They whispered for a while until a dog let out a long, mournful howl just feet away. The boys grabbed each other in terror. Which one of us is he howling for? Huckleberry gasped. I do not know. Look through the crack. Quick! No, you look, Tom! I cannot, Huck!"
}

