parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "096a29dac2eaba4b08647a7b",
  "rewritten_text": "After dinner, the group went to the sandbar to hunt for turtle eggs. They poked sticks into the sand, and whenever they found a soft spot, they knelt down to dig with their hands. Sometimes they pulled fifty or sixty eggs from a single hole. The eggs were perfectly round, white, and slightly smaller than walnuts. They enjoyed a large feast of fried eggs that night and again on Friday morning. After breakfast, the boys ran out onto the sandbar, shouting and chasing each other. They shed their clothes until they were naked, continuing their game in the shallow water against the strong current, which frequently knocked them over and added to the fun. Occasionally, they gathered in a group to splash water at each other, turning their faces away to avoid the spray. Eventually, they would grab and wrestle until someone was dunked, leaving them all in a tangled heap of limbs, surfacing to laugh and catch their breath. Once exhausted, they sprawled on the hot, dry sand to bury themselves before returning to the water to repeat the cycle. Eventually, they decided their bare skin looked like flesh-colored tights, so they drew a ring in the sand and held a circus, with all three boys insisting on being clowns. Next, they played marbles until they grew bored. Joe and Huck went for another swim, but Tom refused, realizing he had lost the rattlesnake rattle he wore as a protective charm. He worried he had avoided cramps only because of the charm, so he refused to swim until he found it. By the time he recovered it, the other boys were tired. They wandered off, feeling homesick as they gazed across the river at the sleepy village. Tom found himself writing \"BECKY\" in the sand with his toe. He quickly scratched it out, annoyed by his own sentimentality, but he soon wrote it again. Unable to stop himself, he finally erased it once more and rejoined the other boys to distract himself."
}

