parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2249ab7a8db162b0ab10d5b1",
  "rewritten_text": "Few possessed a heart as pure or a spirit as humble, paired with such steadfast integrity and faith in others. Raised in seclusion after losing her mother early, she was educated by her father according to his unconventional theories. She knew the heroes of ancient Greece, Rome, and early England intimately, yet remained oblivious to contemporary affairs, having read almost nothing published in the last half-century. While she seemed less worldly than her suitor, her knowledge was deeper and more grounded. Had her beauty not captivated him, her intellect surely would have. He viewed her as his guide, finding joy in the sense of inferiority she occasionally inspired. Following his mother's death when he was nineteen, he left college and withdrew from his social circle, finding solace in her presence. This brief isolation gave him the courage to claim his independence. Believing his friends would not dare mock a marriage once it was finalized, he secured permission from his guardian and her father. By his twentieth birthday, he had married Diana without informing anyone else. He loved her passionately, and her tenderness consumed his thoughts. When he invited college friends to visit, their frivolity repulsed him. Diana had stripped away his youthful naivety; he was now a man, bewildered that he had ever adopted the shallow jargon of his peers or feared their judgment. He abandoned those old friendships, not out of fickleness, but because he had outgrown them."
}

