  "request_id": "2f827dd2bbe36fb47fc425a4",
  "rewritten_text": "Our investigation lasted until nightfall, at which point we left. While returning, my associate briefly stopped at a newspaper office. I have noted his many eccentricities and my tendency to indulge them. He chose to remain silent regarding the homicide until the following noon, when he abruptly inquired if I had noticed anything peculiar at the crime scene. His emphasis on that word made me shiver inexplicably. I replied that I had seen nothing beyond what the papers reported. He dismissed the press, arguing that the mystery was deemed unsolvable precisely because of its bizarre nature, whereas that should make it simple. The police are baffled by the lack of motive for such brutality, the conflicting accounts of voices heard, and the impossibility of the killer escaping unseen. The chaotic room, the body shoved up the chimney, and the gruesome mutilations have paralyzed the authorities. They err by confusing the unusual with the complex. Yet, it is through these departures from the ordinary that reason finds the truth. In such cases, one should ask not what happened, but what has never happened before. The ease with which I solve this mystery is proportional to its apparent difficulty for the police. I stared at him in silence. He looked toward our door and added that he was expecting someone who, while perhaps not the killer, was surely involved in the crimes."
}

