parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "751e02ef44179944869b965b",
  "rewritten_text": "Goodbye, Uncle George. I will be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thanks for the information. I prefer to learn everything about my new friends and nothing about my old ones. Where are you eating, Harry? At Aunt Agatha’s. I invited myself and Mr. Gray, who is her latest favorite. Tell your aunt to stop bothering me with her charity requests. I am tired of them. She thinks I exist only to write checks for her silly causes. I will tell her, but it won’t matter. Philanthropic people lose all sense of humanity; it is their defining trait. The old man grunted in agreement and rang for his servant. Lord Henry walked toward Berkeley Square. So that was the story of Dorian Gray’s parents. Even told crudely, it was a strange, modern romance. A beautiful woman risked everything for a passionate affair, followed by a few weeks of happiness cut short by a treacherous crime. After months of agony, a child was born, the mother died, and the boy was left to the care of a cold, loveless man. It was an interesting background that made the boy seem more perfect. Every beautiful thing has a tragic history; the world must suffer for the smallest flower to bloom. How charming he had been at dinner, sitting across from him with wide, wonder-filled eyes. Talking to him was like playing a violin; he responded to every touch. There was something thrilling about influencing others. To project one’s soul into another person, to hear one’s own ideas echoed back with the passion of youth, and to share one’s temperament like a perfume—that was a true joy in this vulgar, common age. Dorian was a marvelous subject, or at least, he could be molded into one."
}

