parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "899ccf7198a5ca873ea69519",
  "rewritten_text": "“Joseph! Joseph!” He shook his head and tapped his temple, signaling his low opinion of Joseph’s intellect. “My dear young friend,” Mr. Pumblechook declared, “you must be famished. Sit. I have brought chicken, tongue, and other delicacies from the Boar, which I hope you will enjoy. But do I truly see before me the boy I once played with in his happy youth? May I—may I?” This request meant a handshake, which I granted. He shook my hand fervently before sitting once more. “Here is wine,” he continued. “Let us toast to Fortune and her wise choices! Yet, I cannot look upon you and drink without again asking—may I—may I?” I agreed, and he shook my hand again, draining his glass and inverting it. I followed suit, and the wine went straight to my head. Mr. Pumblechook served me the best portions of chicken and tongue, neglecting his own plate. “Ah, poultry! You little imagined as a fledgling what fate awaited you—to provide sustenance under this humble roof for one who—call it a weakness, but may I? May I?” He did not wait for my permission again. I marveled that he did not cut himself with my knife during these frequent interruptions. “And your sister,” he added after a pause, “who raised you by hand! It is tragic that she can no longer appreciate that honor. May—” Seeing he was about to lunge for my hand again, I interrupted. “Let us drink to her health,” I suggested. “Ah!” Mr. Pumblechook exclaimed, leaning back in admiration, “that is how you recognize them, sir! That is how you know the noble-minded, sir!”"
}

