parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c220f4327dadb737132909ad",
  "rewritten_text": "When we reached the tree, he asked, Jupiter, come here. Was the skull nailed to the branch facing out or toward the wood? The face was out, master, so the crows could easily reach the eyes. Legrand touched Jupiter’s eyes and asked, Was it this eye or that one through which you dropped the beetle? It was this eye, master, the left one, just as you told me, Jupiter said, pointing to his own right eye. That will do, we must try again. I began to see a method in my friend’s madness as he moved the peg three inches to the west. He measured fifty feet from the trunk in a straight line, marking a spot several yards from our previous excavation. We drew a larger circle and began digging again. Despite my exhaustion, I felt a strange, growing excitement. Legrand’s deliberate, thoughtful manner had affected me. I dug eagerly, finding myself genuinely expecting to uncover the treasure that had obsessed my companion. After an hour and a half, the dog began to howl violently. This time his distress was serious. When Jupiter tried to restrain him, the dog resisted, leaped into the hole, and frantically clawed at the dirt. Within seconds, he unearthed two human skeletons mixed with metal buttons and remnants of decayed wool. A few more spade strokes revealed a large Spanish knife, followed by several loose gold and silver coins."
}

