  "request_id": "78b8df7252036fa4b0ddccce",
  "rewritten_text": "They know how clever he is; he will be sent away for life. They will make sure the Artful Dodger gets a life sentence. What do you mean by lagging and a lifer? asked Mr. Bolter. Why talk to me like that? Why not speak so I can understand you? Fagin was about to explain that these terms meant transportation for life, but the conversation was interrupted by Master Bates, who entered with his hands in his pockets and a look of mock misery on his face. It is all over, Fagin, Charley said after being introduced to the new companion. What do you mean? They found the man who owns the snuffbox, and others are coming to identify him. The Artful Dodger is definitely being sent away, Master Bates replied. I need a full mourning suit and a hatband to visit him before he leaves. To think of Jack Dawkins—the great Dodger—being sent away for a cheap, two-penny snuffbox! I never thought he would go for anything less than a gold watch and chain. Why didn't he rob a rich man of all his valuables and go out like a gentleman, instead of a common thief with no honor? With this lament for his friend, Master Bates sat down, looking dejected. Why do you say he has no honor? Fagin snapped, glaring at his pupil. Was he not always the best among you? Is there one of you who could match him? Not one, Master Bates replied, his voice thick with regret. Then what are you complaining about? Fagin demanded. Why are you crying? Because it will not be on the record, Charley said, emboldened by his grief. It cannot be in the indictment, and no one will ever know how great he truly was. How will he be remembered in the Newgate Calendar? Maybe he will not be there at all. Oh, what a blow this is! Ha! ha! Fagin laughed, turning to Mr. Bolter while shaking with excitement. See how much pride they take in their profession, my dear."
}

