  "rewritten_text": "He bowed and left. The street was empty at that hour; it is a secluded, quiet lane. William Bird, a tailor, testified that he was among those who entered the residence. He is English and has resided in Paris for two years. He was among the first to climb the stairs and heard voices arguing. The gruff voice belonged to a Frenchman; he recalled hearing 'sacré' and 'mon Dieu.' He also noted sounds of a struggle, like scraping and scuffling. The shrill voice was louder than the gruff one and did not sound English; he suspected it was German, though he does not speak the language, or perhaps it was a woman's. Four witnesses confirmed that the door to Mademoiselle L.'s room was locked from the inside when they arrived. Everything was quiet, with no groans or other sounds. Upon breaking the door open, they found no one. The windows in both rooms were shut and secured from within. The door connecting the rooms was closed but unlocked, while the door to the hallway was locked with the key inside. A small, cluttered room at the top of the passage was found with its door ajar, but a thorough search of the entire house, including the chimneys, revealed nothing. The building had four stories and an attic; the roof trapdoor was nailed shut and had clearly not been opened in years. Estimates of the time between hearing the argument and forcing the door varied from three to five minutes, and the door itself proved difficult to open. Alfonzo Garcio, an undertaker living on the Rue Morgue, testified that he was part of the group but stayed downstairs due to his nervous disposition. He heard the argument and identified the gruff voice as French, but he was certain the shrill voice belonged to an Englishman."
}

