parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0971872029d22027340750d0",
  "rewritten_text": "The cycle of suffering that dictates my life must have been interrupted, yet the cruel fate governing it would not allow such a violation of her laws. Why should I complain? Misery is my natural state; only the wretched could ever reach me. Had Woodville been happy, we would never have met. Can I, who have lived for years on tears and sorrow, hesitate to tell this story of death? Woodville had to travel to the country, kept away from his lovely bride for days. He received a letter from her mentioning a minor illness, urging him to return quickly, as his presence would be her best medicine. Delayed for three more days, he finally rushed to her. An inexplicable dread filled his heart; having heard nothing further, he feared she had worsened. He grew restless, desperate to see her healthy again, despite a dark voice whispering that he would never see her as she once was. Arriving at her home, he found it silent. Passing through the rooms, he saw a servant weeping and, trembling with fear, managed to ask if she was dead. The reply, \"Not yet,\" hit him with less force than he had anticipated. Knowing she still lived offered him a sliver of hope. Recalling her letter, he clung to the desperate belief that his love could revive her, that she could not die while he was near. He hurried to her room, where she lay feverish and unconscious. He wrapped her in his arms, kissing her burning lips and calling her name in hushed agony. \"Come back, Elinor; I am here. You promised me you would recover. Let your spirit return; you cannot die while I am with you. What is death? To lose you is to lose a part of myself, leaving me without a past or a future.\""
}

