parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b3f7748098237f77bc4242d6",
  "rewritten_text": "I uncovered the embers, and a soft wind soon coaxed them into a blaze. I fashioned a fan from branches to revive the dying coals. By nightfall, I realized the fire provided both illumination and warmth, and it proved useful for my meals; I discovered that discarded scraps left by travelers tasted far better roasted than the raw berries I collected. I attempted to cook my own food on the embers, finding that while berries were ruined, roots and nuts were greatly enhanced. Food eventually grew scarce, and I spent entire days hunting for acorns to satisfy my hunger. I decided to abandon my home to find a place where my needs might be better met. I deeply regretted leaving behind the fire, as I had no idea how to rekindle it. After much fruitless contemplation, I wrapped myself in my cloak and headed west. After three days of wandering, I reached open country. A heavy snowfall had blanketed the fields in white, and the freezing, damp ground chilled my feet. Around seven in the morning, desperate for shelter and sustenance, I spotted a small shepherd's hut on a hill. Intrigued, I approached and entered the open door. An old man sat by the fire preparing breakfast; upon seeing me, he let out a sharp cry and fled across the fields with surprising agility. His strange appearance and sudden departure puzzled me, yet I was captivated by the hut. It offered a dry, sheltered refuge that seemed as divine to me as Pandemonium must have appeared to the demons escaping their fiery lake."
}

