  "request_id": "d67e978e724ad89892b5be89",
  "rewritten_text": "Good heavens, the Duchess thought, he is a foot doctor after all. How dreadful. I hope he is a foreigner; that would be slightly better. I must introduce him to you. Introduce him? the Duchess cried. You do not mean he is here? She began searching for her fan and shawl, ready to leave immediately. Of course he is here; I would never host a party without him. He says I have a psychic hand and that if my thumb were shorter, I would be a pessimist living in a convent. Oh, I see, the Duchess said, feeling relieved. He tells fortunes, I suppose? And misfortunes, Lady Windermere replied. Next year, I am in danger by land and sea, so I plan to live in a balloon and pull my dinner up in a basket. It is written on my finger or palm, I forget which. But surely that is tempting fate, Gladys. My dear Duchess, surely fate can resist temptation by now. Everyone should have their palms read monthly to know what to avoid. We do it anyway, but it is pleasant to be warned. If someone does not fetch Mr. Podgers, I shall go myself. Let me go, said a handsome young man standing nearby with an amused smile. Thanks, Lord Arthur, but I fear you would not recognize him. If he is as wonderful as you say, I cannot miss him. Describe him, and I will bring him to you. He does not look like a palm reader. He is not mysterious or romantic. He is a short, stout man with a bald head and gold-rimmed glasses, looking like a mix between a doctor and a lawyer. I am sorry, but it is not my fault. People are annoying. My pianists look like poets, and my poets look like pianists. Last season, I invited a dangerous conspirator who had blown up many people and carried a dagger, yet he arrived looking like a nice clergyman and told jokes all evening."
}

