parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f5ffe7ba587c0d28b395ea90",
  "rewritten_text": "“Darn it, Tom,” I remarked, “you could at least explain. What is a bar sinister?” “I haven't a clue. But he requires one. Every nobleman has them.” That was his typical manner. If he chose not to clarify something, he simply wouldn't. You could question him for days to no avail. With the coat of arms settled, he began drafting a melancholy epitaph, insisting Jim needed one just like the others. He composed several, reading them aloud: 1. Here a captive heart burst. 2. Here a poor prisoner, abandoned by the world and his friends, fretted away his sorrowful life. 3. Here a lonely heart broke, and a weary spirit found rest after thirty-seven years of solitary confinement. 4. Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, the natural son of Louis XIV. Tom’s voice shook as he read, nearly overcome with emotion. Unable to choose, he decided Jim should carve them all. Jim protested that carving such a volume of text into logs with a nail would take a year, and he lacked the skill to form letters. Tom promised to outline them so Jim only had to trace the lines. Then he added, “Actually, logs won't suffice; dungeons don't have log walls. We must carve these into stone. We will bring in a rock.” Jim argued that stone was even worse, fearing the labor would keep him trapped indefinitely. Tom offered to let me assist. He then inspected our progress with the pens. The work was incredibly tedious and slow, preventing my hand sores from healing while showing little progress, so Tom declared, “I have a solution. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the inscriptions, so we can accomplish both tasks with a single stone.”"
}

