parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b11d9240e1be3d79612427cd",
  "rewritten_text": "I bored a hole into the keg's base above the pitcher and inserted a tapered wooden plug. By adjusting its fit, I ensured that water trickling from the hole would fill the pitcher exactly within an hour, a rate easily verified by checking the water level over time. The rest of the design was straightforward. My bed was positioned so my head rested directly beneath the pitcher's spout. Clearly, after an hour, the overflowing water would drop from over four feet above, landing on my face and jolting me awake from even the deepest sleep. By eleven, my preparations were finished, and I went to bed, trusting my device. I was not let down. Every hour, my reliable timer woke me; I would empty the pitcher back into the keg, attend to the condenser, and return to sleep. These interruptions proved less bothersome than expected, and when I finally woke for the day at seven, the sun was well above the horizon. April 3d. The balloon had reached a vast altitude, and the earth's curvature appeared significantly more pronounced. Below, dark specks in the ocean were surely islands. To the north, a thin, brilliant white line on the horizon suggested the edge of the Polar Sea's ice. I was eager to travel further north, perhaps even directly over the Pole, though I regretted that my extreme height would hinder a detailed observation."
}

