  "rewritten_text": "I am quite unlearned, and it worries me, if only slightly. Tell me your trouble. I have a cousin, my only kin and an orphan like me, whom I cherish deeply. She is five years my junior, residing on a southern farm. Financial hardship separated us, and she remains unaware of my situation, as I cannot write, and even if I could, how would I explain it? It is best this way. Indeed, it is better so. As we traveled, and as I gaze upon your kind, resolute face that offers me such strength, I have been wondering: if the Republic truly aids the impoverished, reducing their hunger and suffering, she might live a long life, perhaps even to old age. What then, my gentle sister? Do you think, she asks, her enduring eyes filling with tears and her lips trembling, that the wait will feel long for me in that better land where I hope we shall both find mercy? It cannot be, my child; there is no time or sorrow there. You bring me such comfort. I am so ignorant. Should I kiss you now? Is the time at hand? Yes. She kisses him, he kisses her, and they offer solemn blessings. His thin hand remains steady as he lets her go; her patient face shows only a sweet, bright resolve. She precedes him and is gone; the knitters count twenty-two. I am the Resurrection and the Life, says the Lord; he who believes in me, though he die, shall live, and whoever lives and believes in me shall never die. The murmuring voices, the upturned faces, and the pressing crowd, surging like a great wave, all vanish. Twenty-three. That night, people in the city remarked that his face was the most peaceful they had ever seen, with many calling it sublime and prophetic. Another notable victim of the same blade, a woman, had recently asked at the scaffold to record her final thoughts. Had he spoken his own, and were they prophetic, they would have been these: I see Barsad, Cly, Defarge, The Vengeance, the juror, the judge, and the long lines of new oppressors who rose from the ruins of the old, all meeting their end by this same retributive machine before it finally falls into disuse."
}

