  "request_id": "560372c779e3c2c59c1d7833",
  "rewritten_text": "Elizabeth was deeply depressed, finding no joy in her usual activities. She felt that any happiness was a betrayal of the dead, believing that endless grief was the only proper response to such a tragic loss. She was no longer the cheerful girl who once walked by the lake with me, dreaming of our future. This first great sorrow had arrived, dimming her spirit and stealing her smiles. She told me, \"When I think of Justine Moritz’s miserable death, my view of the world changes. I used to think of injustice as something distant or fictional, but now that misery has struck so close, people seem like monsters hungry for blood. I know I am being unfair, as everyone believed she was guilty. If she had actually murdered the child she helped raise for a few jewels, she would have been truly depraved. I could never support an execution, but I would have thought such a person unfit for society. Yet, I know she was innocent, and your agreement confirms it. Victor, when lies look so much like the truth, how can anyone be happy? I feel like I am walking on the edge of a cliff, surrounded by people trying to push me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, yet the killer remains free and perhaps even respected. Even if I were to face the scaffold for these crimes, I would never trade places with such a monster.\" I listened in agony, knowing that while I did not commit the act, I was the true cause of these deaths. Elizabeth saw my pain and took my hand, saying, \"My dear friend, you must calm yourself. These events have hurt me deeply, but I am not as miserable as you are.\""
}

