  "request_id": "9a006344e4ca8aa05344ea9f",
  "rewritten_text": "So, let's hear the whole story. Well, it happened like this. Miss Watson was always picking on me and treating me poorly, but she insisted she would never sell me down to New Orleans. Still, I noticed a slave trader hanging around the property lately, which made me uneasy. One night, I crept to the door and heard her telling the widow that she intended to sell me for eight hundred dollars. She claimed she did not want to, but the money was too much to resist. The widow tried to talk her out of it, but I did not wait to hear the rest. I left immediately. I hurried down the hill, intending to steal a skiff along the shore, but people were still active, so I hid in an old, dilapidated cooper shop on the bank. I stayed there all night. By six in the morning, boats began passing by, and by eight or nine, everyone was talking about how your father had come to town claiming you were murdered. I heard all the details from the people stopping at the shore. I was truly sorry you were killed, Huck, but I am not anymore. I stayed hidden under the wood shavings all day. I was hungry, but not afraid, because I knew Miss Watson and the widow would be at a camp meeting all day. They assumed I was out with the cattle, so they would not miss me until dark. The other servants would be gone as well. When night fell, I headed up the river road for two miles to where there were no houses. I had decided on my plan. If I kept traveling on foot, the dogs would track me; if I stole a skiff to cross, they would notice it missing and know exactly where to find my trail."
}

