  "rewritten_text": "Despite these efforts, the boy grew increasingly somber, pale, and despondent. She introduced hot baths, sitz baths, showers, and plunges, yet he remained as gloomy as a funeral carriage. She supplemented the water with a meager oatmeal diet and blister-plasters, measuring his intake like a vessel and dosing him with various patent medicines. Tom had grown numb to her ministrations, a change that deeply alarmed his aunt. Determined to shatter this apathy, she discovered Pain-killer and promptly acquired a supply. After tasting the fiery liquid, she abandoned her previous treatments in favor of this new remedy. She administered a teaspoonful to Tom, watching anxiously for a reaction. Her relief was immediate when the boy responded with a vigorous, frantic display of energy, as if he had been set ablaze. Sensing that his current situation lacked sufficient sentiment and offered too much chaotic variety, Tom decided to feign a fondness for the medicine. He requested it so frequently that he became a pest, eventually prompting his aunt to let him serve himself. While she would have trusted Sid, she secretly monitored the bottle with Tom. She noticed the level dropping, never suspecting he was using the liquid to mend a floorboard crack. One day, as Tom prepared to dose the floor, the aunt’s yellow cat approached, begging for a taste. Tom warned Peter that he should only partake if he truly desired it, but the cat insisted. After Tom cautioned him once more, he pried the cat’s jaws open and administered the dose. Peter leaped high into the air, let out a piercing cry, and raced around the room, colliding with furniture and overturning plants in a chaotic frenzy."
}

