parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fe2004c0950169e44d484bf6",
  "rewritten_text": "April 12th. A distinct shift occurred in the balloon’s trajectory, which, while expected, brought me immense satisfaction. After reaching the twentieth parallel south, it veered sharply eastward, maintaining that heading all day along the lunar ellipse. Notably, this maneuver caused a persistent, unsettling swaying of the car that lasted for several hours. April 13th. The loud, crackling sound from the tenth returned, causing me great alarm. I pondered its cause to no avail. The earth’s apparent size has shrunk significantly, now spanning an angle of barely twenty-five degrees. The moon remains hidden directly above me. My eastward movement has slowed, though I remain on the elliptical path. April 14th. The earth’s diameter is diminishing rapidly. I am convinced the balloon is ascending the line of apsides toward perigee, following a direct route to the moon’s closest point to earth. The moon is still obscured overhead. I have spent considerable effort condensing the air. April 15th. Earth’s continents and oceans are no longer discernible. Around noon, that terrifying noise returned, growing louder and more intense. As I stood paralyzed by dread, the car shook violently, and a massive, flaming object roared past with a thunderous boom. Once my shock faded, I concluded it was a volcanic fragment ejected from the world I am approaching—likely a meteoric stone. April 16th. Gazing through the side windows, I was thrilled to see the moon’s edge peeking out from behind the balloon’s vast frame."
}

