parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "718be21b9a2ec40188273976",
  "rewritten_text": "His sun-darkened face was mostly obscured by a thick beard and mustache. He carried a heavy oak club but otherwise seemed unarmed. He gave an awkward bow and greeted us in French, which, despite a regional accent, clearly marked him as a Parisian. Sit down, my friend, Dupin said. I assume you are here about the orangutan. I almost envy you owning such a fine, valuable creature. How old do you think he is? The sailor exhaled deeply, as if a heavy weight had been lifted, and answered confidently: I cannot say for sure, but he is likely no more than four or five. Do you have him here? Oh no, we lack the space. He is at a nearby stable on Rue Dubourg. You can collect him tomorrow. I assume you are prepared to prove ownership? Certainly, sir. I will be sorry to lose him, Dupin remarked. I do not expect you to go to this trouble for nothing, the man replied. I am happy to pay a reasonable reward for his recovery. That is fair, Dupin said. Let me think. My reward shall be this: you will tell me everything you know about the murders on Rue Morgue. Dupin spoke softly. He walked to the door, locked it, and pocketed the key. He then calmly placed a pistol on the table. The sailor turned red, gasping as if suffocating. He jumped up and grabbed his club, but immediately collapsed back into his chair, trembling and pale as a corpse. He remained silent. I felt deeply sorry for him. My friend, Dupin said kindly, you are unnecessarily alarmed. We mean you no harm. I give you my word as a gentleman and a Frenchman that we intend no injury. I know you are innocent of the crimes on Rue Morgue, but you cannot deny that you are involved in some way."
}

