parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c8741193932fd184e4123307",
  "rewritten_text": "From the moment we embarked, I had watched anxiously for signs that we were being watched. I saw none. We were certainly not being followed by any boat. Had we been, I would have steered for shore to force them to reveal their intentions. Instead, we traveled without interference. He wore his boat-cloak and seemed a natural part of the surroundings. Remarkably, he was the least anxious among us. He was not indifferent, as he spoke of hoping to see his gentleman thrive abroad, nor was he resigned, but he refused to worry about danger until it arrived. If you knew what it was to sit here with my dear boy and smoke after years behind four walls, you would envy me, he told me. You do not know what it is. I think I understand the joy of freedom, I replied. He shook his head. You do not know it as I do. You must have been locked away to truly understand, but I will not be gloomy. It seemed contradictory that he would risk his life for an ideal, yet I realized that freedom without danger was foreign to his experience. He soon added, When I was on the other side of the world, I always looked toward this side. It felt hollow there, even as I grew rich. Everyone knew Magwitch, and no one cared where he went. They are not so indifferent here, at least not if they knew where I was. If all goes well, I said, you will be safe and free in a few hours. He took a long breath. I hope so. And do you think so?"
}

