  "request_id": "bd0f467a7761f2560a619d6e",
  "rewritten_text": "It was early February, and after a month in Bath, Anne was anxious for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She had not heard from Mary in three weeks and wanted more detail. She knew Henrietta had returned home and Louisa was recovering in Lyme, but little else. One evening, she received a thick letter from Mary, accompanied by the compliments of Admiral and Mrs. Croft. The Crofts were in Bath, which immediately captured her interest. Sir Walter demanded to know why they had sent her a letter, and Anne explained it was from Uppercross. He remarked that he would have visited the Admiral regardless, as he knew his duties to a tenant. Anne ignored him, focused entirely on her letter, which began on February 1st. Mary wrote that she had not apologized for her silence, assuming Anne was too busy enjoying Bath to care about dull Uppercross. She complained about a boring Christmas, noting that the Musgroves had hosted no dinner parties. The holidays had finally ended, and the house was quiet, save for the Harville children, who had not yet gone home. Mary found this strange and disliked the children, noting that Mrs. Musgrove seemed to prefer them to her own grandchildren. She also complained about the terrible weather and the lack of visitors, save for Charles Hayter, whose frequent calls were unwelcome. She added that it was a shame Henrietta had not stayed in Lyme longer to avoid him."
}

