parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "362425a598ed206b47b7fee8",
  "rewritten_text": "He explained that he was wasting his time where he was, and that friends in London were urging his return to finalize the negotiations for his Indian venture. He could no longer delay his departure, but since his trip to London might soon be followed by a much longer voyage, he begged me to spend as much time with him as possible. He asked me to leave my isolated island and meet him in Perth so we could travel south together. This letter brought me back to life, and I decided to leave my island in two days. Before I left, however, I had to face a task that made me shudder: I needed to pack my chemical equipment. This required entering the room where I had performed my repulsive work and handling tools that now sickened me. At daybreak the next morning, I gathered my courage and unlocked the laboratory. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered on the floor, and I felt as if I had mutilated a living human being. After composing myself, I entered the room. With trembling hands, I removed the instruments, realizing I could not leave these remnants to horrify or alarm the local villagers. I placed them in a basket with heavy stones, intending to dump them into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach, cleaning and organizing my equipment. My perspective had shifted completely since the night the creature appeared. Previously, I had viewed my promise with gloomy despair, believing it had to be fulfilled regardless of the consequences. Now, it felt as if a veil had been lifted, and I saw things clearly for the first time. The thought of resuming my work never crossed my mind. Although the creature's threat weighed on me, I no longer believed my actions could prevent it. I had decided that creating another monster would be an act of the most atrocious selfishness, and I dismissed any thought that might lead me to a different conclusion."
}

