  "rewritten_text": "The daughter lay still, having fainted. The elderly woman’s frantic screams and resistance, which resulted in her hair being ripped out, transformed the orangutan’s likely peaceful intent into violent rage. With a single powerful stroke, the beast nearly decapitated her. The sight of blood drove it into a frenzy. Gnashing its teeth and with eyes ablaze, it attacked the girl, sinking its claws into her throat until she died. Its wild gaze then landed on the bed’s headboard, where its master’s face, frozen in terror, was visible. The creature’s fury, tempered by the memory of the whip, shifted to fear. Aware of its guilt, it sought to hide the carnage, pacing the room in a nervous panic, smashing furniture and pulling the bed from its frame. Finally, it shoved the daughter’s body up the chimney and hurled the old woman’s corpse out the window. As the ape neared the window with the body, the sailor recoiled in horror, slid down the lightning rod, and fled home, abandoning the animal in his terror. The noises heard on the stairs were the Frenchman’s cries of shock mixed with the beast’s demonic chattering. I have little to add. The orangutan must have escaped down the rod just before the door was broken, closing the window behind it. Its owner later recaptured it and sold it for a high price to the Jardin des Plantes. Le Bon was released immediately after we explained the events to the Prefect. Though the Prefect was friendly toward my companion, he could not hide his annoyance at the outcome, offering a few sarcastic remarks about people minding their own affairs. Let him talk, Dupin remarked, choosing not to respond. It eases his conscience. I am content to have outwitted him in his own domain. That he failed to solve this mystery is not as surprising as he believes; in reality, our friend the Prefect is far too clever to be truly profound."
}

