parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d9597aa4efc95ff5653b6061",
  "rewritten_text": "Roughly ten minutes later, the tea bell sounded, and since Virginia failed to appear, Mrs. Otis dispatched a footman to summon her. He soon returned, reporting that Miss Virginia was nowhere to be found. Initially, Mrs. Otis felt no alarm, as her daughter frequently gathered garden flowers for the table, but by six o’clock, she grew truly distressed. She sent the boys to search while she and Mr. Otis combed the house. By half-past six, the boys returned empty-handed. In their mounting panic, Mr. Otis recalled granting a group of gypsies permission to camp in the park days earlier. He immediately departed for Blackfell Hollow with his eldest son and two farmhands. The young Duke of Cheshire, frantic with worry, pleaded to join them, but Mr. Otis refused, fearing potential violence. Upon arrival, they discovered the gypsies had fled in haste, leaving behind a smoldering fire and scattered plates. After sending Washington and the men to search the area, Mr. Otis hurried home to wire police inspectors throughout the county regarding a kidnapped girl. He then ordered his horse, insisted his wife and sons dine, and rode toward Ascot Road with a groom. He had traveled only two miles when he heard hoofbeats; the young Duke was galloping after him on his pony, hatless and flushed. The boy gasped that he could not eat while Virginia remained missing and begged not to be sent away, suggesting their engagement might have prevented this crisis. Amused by the spirited youth and moved by his loyalty, the Minister leaned down to pat his shoulder, agreeing to let him join the search provided they stopped in Ascot for a hat."
}

