parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9e0882c762124ad004858adc",
  "rewritten_text": "Get up! What are you doing? I opened my eyes, disoriented. It was past sunrise, and I had been fast asleep. Dad stood over me, looking miserable and ill. He asked, What are you doing with this gun? Realizing he had no memory of his actions, I replied, Someone tried to break in, so I was waiting for him. Why didn't you wake me? I tried, but I couldn't move you. Fine. Stop talking and go check the fishing lines for breakfast. I will be there shortly. He unlocked the door, and I headed up the riverbank. I saw branches and bark floating by, signaling the river was rising. I thought about how much fun I would have if I were in town; the June rise always brought good luck, as logs and firewood drifted down for me to catch and sell. I walked along the bank, watching for Dad and any debris. Suddenly, a beautiful canoe, about thirteen feet long, drifted by. I dove into the water, clothes and all, and swam toward it. I expected someone to be hiding inside to play a trick, but it was empty. I paddled it to shore. I thought Dad would be pleased, as it was worth ten dollars. However, he was nowhere to be seen. As I hid the boat in a vine-covered creek, I had a new idea: I would keep it hidden and use it to escape fifty miles downriver instead of traveling on foot. I worked quickly, constantly fearing Dad would appear. Once the canoe was concealed, I peeked through the willows and saw him down the path, aiming his gun at a bird."
}

