parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fd9225d9b0acb52238681a7d",
  "rewritten_text": "Count Rouvaloff stared at him in disbelief, then realizing he was sincere, wrote an address on a slip of paper, initialed it, and passed it across the table. Scotland Yard would pay dearly for this address, he remarked. They will not get it, Lord Arthur laughed. After shaking the Russian’s hand, he hurried downstairs, checked the note, and instructed his driver to head to Soho Square. He dismissed the carriage there and walked down Greek Street until he reached Bayle’s Court. Passing under the archway, he entered a strange dead-end street dominated by a French laundry, where clotheslines crisscrossed the space, fluttering with white linen. He reached the end and knocked on a small green house. After a pause, during which neighbors peered from every window, a rough-looking man opened the door and asked in broken English what he wanted. Lord Arthur presented the paper. The man bowed and led him into a shabby parlor. Moments later, Herr Winckelkopf entered, wearing a wine-stained napkin and holding a fork. Count Rouvaloff recommended me, Lord Arthur said, bowing. I need to discuss business. My name is Robert Smith, and I require an explosive clock. Delighted to meet you, Lord Arthur, the cheerful German laughed. Do not be alarmed; it is my job to know everyone. I recall seeing you at Lady Windermere’s. I hope she is well. Would you mind joining me for breakfast? The pâté is excellent, and my friends claim my Rhine wine surpasses what is served at the German Embassy. Before Lord Arthur could recover from the shock of being recognized, he was seated in the back room, sipping fine wine from a monogrammed glass and chatting easily with the notorious conspirator. Explosive clocks, Winckelkopf explained, are difficult to export. Even if they clear customs, the train schedules are so unreliable that they often detonate before reaching their intended target."
}

