  "rewritten_text": "Master Charles Bates, horrified by Sikes’s crime, began to wonder if an honest life was truly better. Deciding that it was, he left his past behind, determined to start fresh. He struggled and suffered for a while, but thanks to his cheerful nature and clear goals, he eventually succeeded. After working as a farmhand and a delivery boy, he is now the happiest young cattle farmer in all of Northamptonshire. My hand trembles as I reach the end of this story, wishing I could continue the narrative a little longer. I would love to stay with these characters and describe their happiness. I would show Rose Maylie in her graceful womanhood, bringing warmth and light to everyone around her. I would depict her as the heart of her family, following her through sunny fields and listening to her gentle voice on moonlit walks. I would watch her kindness toward others and her dedication at home, seeing her and her sister’s child share their love and memories of those they lost. I would bring back those happy children gathered at her knee and recall her clear laughter and compassionate tears. I wish I could capture every one of those moments. How Mr. Brownlow educated his adopted son, growing closer to him as the boy’s character blossomed, and how he saw reminders of his old friend in the boy’s actions—bringing him both sadness and comfort—is clear. How the two orphans, shaped by hardship, practiced mercy, love, and gratitude toward the One who protected them, are all things that need not be explained."
}

