  "rewritten_text": "Saturday nights in our village brought a pleasant feeling of tidying up and pausing before starting fresh, which emboldened Joe to stay out thirty minutes past his usual time. As his rum and water finished, Joe stood to leave, taking my hand. Wait a moment, Mr. Gargery, the stranger said. I believe I have a shiny new shilling, and if I do, the boy shall have it. He found it among his change, wrapped it in crumpled paper, and handed it to me, insisting it was mine. I thanked him, staring rudely while clutching Joe. He bid Joe and Mr. Wopsle goodnight, then gave me a strange look with his one good eye, or rather, he winked it in a way that felt significant. On the walk home, Mr. Wopsle left us, and Joe walked with his mouth open to air out the taste of rum. I was too stunned by this encounter with a ghost from my past to speak. My sister was in a decent mood, so Joe told her about the shilling. She immediately suspected it was counterfeit, but when I unwrapped it, it was genuine. Then she noticed two one-pound notes inside the paper, damp and smelling of livestock. Joe grabbed his hat and rushed back to the pub to return them. I sat on my stool, certain the man would be gone. Joe soon returned, confirming the man had vanished, though he had left word about the money."
}

