parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d66cc4040cccd87b4da0c9f5",
  "rewritten_text": "She was an incredible talker. It would have been rude to mention that it was late and I was exhausted. I regretted not going to bed when I had the chance, but I had to endure it. She kept chattering in the silent, ghostly castle until a distant, muffled shriek rose from deep below, filled with such agony that my skin crawled. The queen paused, her eyes brightening with pleasure as she tilted her head like a listening bird. The sound pierced the stillness once more. I asked what it was. She replied that it was a stubborn soul who had been enduring the rack for hours. I asked what he was enduring, and she invited me to witness a cheerful sight, promising that if he did not yield his secret, I would see him torn apart. She was a smooth monster, perfectly serene while my own legs ached in sympathy with the victim. Escorted by armored guards with torches, we walked through echoing halls and down damp, moldy stone stairs. The journey was long and chilling, made worse by her casual talk about the prisoner's crime: killing a stag in the royal forest based on an anonymous tip. I suggested that anonymous testimony was unfair and that the accused should face his accuser. She dismissed this as unimportant, noting that the masked accuser had vanished after speaking to the forester. I asked if anyone else saw the killing, and she admitted no one had, only that this stranger saw the man near the carcass. I pointed out that the masked stranger might be the killer himself, but she ignored my logic, insisting that the man must confess to save his soul. She declared that while he would certainly be executed for his crime, she had to ensure he confessed and received absolution to protect her own soul."
}

