parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "28d6b2d4701bef5320f00de5",
  "rewritten_text": "I do not have the nerves for this sort of thing. But could you really be comfortable spending the whole evening away from the poor boy? Yes, his father can, so why shouldn't I? Jemima is very careful and can send us updates every hour. I think Charles should have told his father we were all coming. I am no more worried about little Charles now than he is. I was terrified yesterday, but today is different. Well, if you do not think it is too late, why not go with your husband? Leave little Charles in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove will not think it wrong if I am with him. Are you serious? Mary asked, her eyes brightening. That is a wonderful idea. I might as well go, as I am of no use at home and it only stresses me out. You, who do not have a mother's feelings, are the best person for this. You can make little Charles do anything; he always listens to you. It will be much better than leaving him with only Jemima. I will certainly go. They want me to meet Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being alone. What an excellent thought, Anne. I will tell Charles and get ready immediately. You can send for us if anything happens, but I am sure there will be no trouble. I would not go if I were not completely at ease about my dear child. A moment later, she was knocking at her husband's door. As Anne followed her upstairs, she heard Mary say excitedly, I am going with you, Charles, because I am no more useful at home than you are. If I stayed with the child forever, I could not make him do anything he disliked. Anne will stay; she offered to look after him. It is her own idea, so I will go with you, which is much better since I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday. This is very kind of Anne, her husband replied, and I would be glad to have you go, but it seems unfair that she should be left alone to nurse our sick child."
}

