parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ab18a79f17ab19413de89665",
  "rewritten_text": "With these cries, many women, driven into a blind frenzy, spun around, striking and tearing at their own friends until they collapsed in a faint, saved from being trampled only by their own men. Still, not a moment was wasted. Foulon was at the Hotel de Ville and might be released. If Saint Antoine remembered its own suffering and insults, that would never happen. Armed men and women poured out of the district so quickly, pulling even the stragglers along, that within fifteen minutes, only a few old women and crying children remained in Saint Antoine. By then, the crowd was choking the Hall of Examination where the wicked old man was held, spilling out into the nearby streets. The Defarges, The Vengeance, and Jacques Three were at the front, close to him. Look, Madame cried, pointing with her knife. See the old villain tied with ropes. Tying a bundle of grass to his back was a good touch. Let him eat it now! She tucked her knife away and clapped like she was at a theater. The people behind her explained her satisfaction to those further back, and soon the surrounding streets echoed with applause. During the long hours of waiting and endless talk, Madame Defarge’s impatience was quickly mirrored by the crowd, aided by men who had climbed the building’s exterior to watch through the windows and signal her reactions to those outside. Finally, the sun rose high enough to cast a ray of light directly onto the prisoner’s head. The crowd could no longer be held back; the barrier of people broke, and Saint Antoine had him. The news spread instantly. Defarge jumped over a railing and a table to grab the miserable man, and Madame Defarge followed, twisting her hand into his ropes. Before the others could even reach him, a cry went up across the entire city: Bring him out!"
}

