parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d01bf64c5974f8c1a9dcceb2",
  "rewritten_text": "My once-loyal customers no longer spared me a thought. They claimed they were too busy reading about revolutions and keeping pace with the spirit of the age. If a fire needed stoking, a newspaper sufficed, and as the government faltered, my leather and iron goods seemed to grow indestructible; soon, not a single pair of bellows in Rotterdam required repair. This situation was unbearable. I became destitute, and with a family to support, my burdens grew so heavy that I spent hours contemplating suicide. Meanwhile, creditors gave me no peace. My home was besieged from dawn until dusk, leaving me pacing like a caged tiger. Three men in particular harassed me constantly, threatening legal action. I vowed to take bitter revenge if I ever caught them, and only the anticipation of that retribution kept me from ending my life with a blunderbuss. I decided to hide my rage, offering them empty promises until fate provided a chance for vengeance. One day, after slipping past my creditors, I wandered the backstreets in despair until I stumbled upon a bookseller’s stall. I collapsed into a customer’s chair and aimlessly opened the first book I found. It was a pamphlet on speculative astronomy by a professor from Berlin or a Frenchman with a similar name. Having some background in the subject, I became completely absorbed, reading the entire text twice before finally noticing my surroundings."
}

