parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "628d34252d2c99838cff5b2d",
  "rewritten_text": "“What is it?” “It is ten o’clock, sir.” “What? Ten at night?” “Yes, sir. You asked to be awakened.” “Ah, I recall. Very well.” Failing to drift back to sleep while his servant noisily tended the fire, he rose, donned his hat, and departed. He wandered through the Temple, pacing King’s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings to clear his head, before heading to Stryver’s chambers. The clerk had departed, so Stryver himself answered the door, dressed in slippers and a loose robe, his throat exposed. He possessed the strained, haggard look around the eyes common to men of his indulgent habits, a trait visible in portraits of heavy drinkers throughout history. “You are slightly late, Memory,” Stryver remarked. “About the usual time, perhaps fifteen minutes past.” They entered a dim, book-lined room cluttered with documents and warmed by a roaring fire. A kettle hissed on the hearth, and a table was set with wine, spirits, sugar, and lemons. “I see you have finished your bottle, Sydney.” “Two tonight, I believe. I dined with today’s client, or watched him dine—it makes no difference.” “That was a brilliant point you made regarding the identification. How did you discover it?” “I thought he was a handsome man, and that I might have been just like him, had I possessed any luck.” Stryver laughed, his belly shaking. “Always with the luck, Sydney! Get to work.” The jackal, looking sullen, fetched a basin of cold water and some towels. He soaked the cloths, wrung them out, wrapped them around his head in a grotesque fashion, and sat down. “I am ready.” “Not much to condense tonight, Memory,” Stryver said cheerfully, sorting through his papers. “How much?” “Only two cases.” “Give me the most difficult one first.” “Here you are, Sydney. Proceed.” The lion reclined on a sofa beside the table, while the jackal took his place at the desk, surrounded by papers and the waiting bottles."
}

