parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e43dcc68bcac761e65a138bc",
  "rewritten_text": "We could certainly build a thrilling superstition around this. I suppose you will name the bug Scarabaeus caput hominis, or something similar—there are many such titles in natural history books. But where are the antennae you mentioned? The antennae! Legrand said, becoming strangely agitated. I am certain you must see them. I drew them as clearly as they appear on the actual insect, and I assume that is enough. Well, perhaps you did, I replied, but I still do not see them. I handed him the paper without further comment, not wanting to upset him. I was surprised by his reaction; his bad temper puzzled me. As for the drawing, there were definitely no antennae visible, and the whole thing looked remarkably like a standard illustration of a death’s-head moth. He took the paper peevishly and was about to crumple it to throw into the fire when a casual glance at the design suddenly caught his attention. His face turned bright red, then deathly pale. For several minutes, he scrutinized the drawing intently. Finally, he stood up, took a candle, and sat on a sea-chest in the corner to examine the paper again, turning it in every direction. He said nothing, which astonished me, but I thought it best not to provoke his moodiness. Eventually, he placed the paper in his wallet and locked it in his desk. He became calmer, but his earlier enthusiasm had vanished; he seemed less sulky than deeply distracted. As the evening progressed, he sank into a reverie I could not break. I had intended to stay the night, but seeing his state, I decided to leave. He did not ask me to stay, though he shook my hand with unusual warmth as I departed. About a month later, during which time I had not seen Legrand, his servant Jupiter visited me in Charleston. I had never seen the old man look so discouraged, and I feared some serious disaster had befallen my friend."
}

