parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "38350597f090005633e8f83d",
  "rewritten_text": "I cannot express the joy I felt returning from political turmoil and foreign hardships to the peace and love of my own home. Had I never left Windsor, these feelings would not be so strong, but my time in Greece was filled with fear and the loss of two people who represented greatness and virtue. Such misery could not touch the domestic life we enjoyed in our secluded forest. Time eventually left its mark on our happiness. Idris, my devoted wife, was a deeply loving mother. We had three children, but our second child died while I was in Greece, which deeply affected her. Before that, she felt her children were safe, but afterward, she lived in constant fear that death would take the others. Any minor illness caused her terror, and she was miserable whenever she was away from them. Fortunately, her fears were mostly unfounded. Alfred, now nine, was a strong, upright boy with a gentle nature. Our youngest was still an infant, but he was healthy and his laughter filled our home. Clara had grown past the age that caused Idris such worry. She was dear to us all, possessing a rare combination of intelligence, innocence, and beauty that made her a true treasure. At the start of winter, nine-year-old Alfred began school at Eton. He saw this as his first step toward manhood and was quite excited. Studying and playing with others helped develop his character, highlighting his perseverance, generosity, and steady nature."
}

