parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a5b63b62022e8395e7cbb0e6",
  "rewritten_text": "You will be happy to hear that my journey has begun without the disasters you feared. I arrived yesterday, and my first priority is to assure my dear sister that I am well and increasingly confident in my success. I am already far north of London. As I walk the streets of St. Petersburg, a cold northern breeze brushes my cheeks, bracing my nerves and filling me with joy. Do you understand this feeling? This wind, blowing from the regions I am heading toward, gives me a taste of those icy lands. Inspired by this promising breeze, my daydreams grow more vivid. I cannot convince myself that the North Pole is merely a place of frost and desolation; I imagine it as a region of beauty and delight. There, Margaret, the sun is always visible, its broad disk skimming the horizon and casting perpetual light. Trusting the accounts of earlier explorers, I believe that snow and frost are banished there. Sailing over a calm sea, we may reach a land more wonderful and beautiful than any yet discovered. Its features may be unique, just as the stars appear different in those unexplored solitudes. What might we find in a land of eternal light? I may discover the mysterious force that attracts the compass needle and clarify celestial observations that currently seem inconsistent. I will satisfy my curiosity by seeing a part of the world never before visited and walking on land never touched by human feet. These goals are enough to overcome any fear of danger or death, making me start this difficult voyage with the joy of a child setting out on an adventure. Even if my theories are wrong, you cannot deny the immense benefit I will provide to all mankind by discovering a shortcut to distant countries or uncovering the secret of the magnet. These thoughts have calmed my initial nerves, and I feel an enthusiasm that lifts my spirits. Nothing settles the mind like a steady purpose upon which the soul can focus."
}

