parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "682fabb9514daeb02319fac2",
  "rewritten_text": "He had been entirely unaware of his own influence. He followed her into the garden without any plan to test it. Anxious to see how she handled Frank Churchill’s engagement, he arrived with no selfish motive, hoping only to comfort or advise her if she allowed it. Everything else happened in the moment, driven by his reaction to her words. The wonderful news that she felt nothing for Frank Churchill and was completely free gave him hope that he might eventually win her love. He had not expected immediate success; he only hoped she would let him try to win her heart. The greater hope that followed was even more enchanting: the affection he had hoped to earn was already his. In half an hour, he had moved from deep distress to near-perfect happiness. Her transformation was just as profound. This short time gave them both the precious certainty of being loved, clearing away their mutual ignorance, jealousy, and doubt. He had been jealous of Frank Churchill since the man first arrived. Loving Emma and resenting his rival had happened at the same time, each feeling revealing the other. His jealousy had driven him to leave the country, and the trip to Box Hill convinced him to go. He wanted to avoid watching those attentions again and hoped to learn indifference. However, he chose the wrong place. His brother’s home was filled with domestic happiness, and his sister Isabella reminded him too much of Emma, only highlighting Emma’s superior brilliance. He stayed as long as he could, until the morning mail brought news of Jane Fairfax. Feeling relieved—and not ashamed to be glad, as he never thought Frank Churchill deserved Emma—he felt such intense concern for her that he could not stay away. He rode home through the rain and walked over immediately after dinner to see how this wonderful woman, perfect despite her flaws, was handling the news."
}

