parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8c866a99045304b2bcbd81f6",
  "rewritten_text": "This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis had agreed to keep on at Lady Canterville’s request. She curtsied as they arrived and said in an old-fashioned way, 'I welcome you to Canterville Chase.' They followed her through the Tudor hall into the library, a long room with black oak panels and a stained-glass window. Tea was waiting, and they sat down to look around while Mrs. Umney served them. Suddenly, Mrs. Otis noticed a dull red stain on the floor near the fireplace. Not knowing what it was, she said, 'I am afraid something has been spilled there.' 'Yes, madam,' the housekeeper replied quietly, 'blood has been spilled on that spot.' 'How horrid,' Mrs. Otis cried. 'I do not want bloodstains in a sitting room. It must be removed.' The old woman smiled and said in a mysterious voice, 'It is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, murdered there by her husband, Sir Simon, in 1575. Sir Simon lived nine more years before disappearing under mysterious circumstances. His body was never found, but his guilty spirit still haunts the house. The stain is admired by tourists and cannot be removed.' 'That is nonsense,' Washington Otis declared. 'Pinkerton’s Stain Remover will clean it up immediately.' Before the terrified housekeeper could stop him, he knelt and scrubbed the floor with a cleaning stick. In moments, the stain was gone. 'I knew Pinkerton would work,' he said triumphantly. Just then, a flash of lightning lit the room, a loud thunderclap made everyone jump, and Mrs. Umney fainted. 'What a terrible climate,' the American Minister said calmly, lighting a cigar. 'I suppose this country is so crowded they do not have enough good weather for everyone. Emigration is the only solution for England.' 'My dear Hiram,' Mrs. Otis cried, 'what can we do with a woman who faints?' 'Charge it to her like broken items,' the Minister replied. 'She will not faint again.' Soon after, Mrs. Umney regained consciousness."
}

