parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d1ee493366256b8eadab3511",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Bennet remained largely silent throughout dinner, but once the staff departed, he sought to engage his visitor by remarking on his good fortune regarding his patroness. He noted that Lady Catherine de Bourgh’s kindness and concern for his welfare were truly exceptional. Mr. Collins responded with fervent praise, his demeanor growing unusually grave as he insisted he had never encountered such grace and humility in a noblewoman. He recounted how she had kindly commended his sermons, invited him to dine at Rosings on two occasions, and even summoned him to join her card games. While some deemed her haughty, he maintained she was nothing but affable, treating him as an equal and encouraging his social life and family visits. She had even advised him to marry wisely and had personally visited his parsonage to approve his renovations, even suggesting the addition of upstairs shelving. Mrs. Bennet expressed her approval, remarking that it was a shame more noblewomen were not so pleasant, and inquired if she lived nearby. Mr. Collins explained that his garden was separated from Rosings Park only by a lane. When Mrs. Bennet asked if the widow had any children, he mentioned her only daughter, the heiress to a vast estate. Mrs. Bennet sighed, noting the girl’s good fortune, and asked if she were beautiful. Mr. Collins described her as a charming young lady, adding that Lady Catherine considered her superior to all other women in beauty, as her features clearly reflected her noble lineage."
}

