parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4b9c7bb40229e9adc34174b2",
  "rewritten_text": "Jupiter could barely contain his delight at the sight, though his master looked deeply disappointed. He pressed us to keep digging, and I had barely started again when I tripped over a large iron ring partially hidden in the dirt. We then labored with renewed intensity, experiencing ten minutes of profound excitement. We uncovered a rectangular wooden chest, remarkably preserved and hardened, likely by a mineral treatment. Measuring three and a half feet long, three feet wide, and two and a half feet deep, the box was reinforced with riveted iron bands in a trellis pattern. Six iron rings, three on each side, allowed for a firm grip, yet our combined strength barely shifted the heavy coffer. Realizing we could not move it, we slid back the two bolts on the lid with trembling hands. A fortune of immense value was revealed. As our lantern light hit the pit, the pile of gold and jewels shimmered with a brilliance that blinded us. I cannot describe my reaction, though amazement was foremost. Legrand was drained by the intensity, barely speaking. Jupiter’s face turned as pale as possible, and he appeared completely stunned. He knelt in the pit, plunging his bare arms into the gold as if bathing in it. Finally, he sighed and muttered to himself about the gold-bug, expressing shame for how he had treated the creature. Eventually, I had to urge them to focus on moving the treasure. It was getting late, and we needed to secure everything before dawn. We spent considerable time deliberating, our thoughts too scattered to decide on a plan."
}

