  "rewritten_text": "When we arrived at the base, he asked, \"Jupiter, come here! Was the skull fastened to the branch facing outward, or toward the wood?\" \"The face was out, master, so the crows could easily reach the eyes,\" Jupiter replied. \"Very well, and was it this eye or that one through which you dropped the beetle?\" Legrand asked, touching each of Jupiter's eyes. \"It was this one, master—the left eye—just as you instructed,\" Jupiter said, pointing to his own right eye. \"That suffices; we must attempt it once more.\" I began to perceive a certain logic in my friend's apparent madness as he shifted the peg three inches westward. Measuring fifty feet from the trunk in a straight line, he marked a new spot several yards from our previous excavation. We drew a larger circle and resumed digging. Despite my exhaustion, I felt a strange, growing interest and excitement, perhaps stirred by Legrand's air of deliberate purpose. I shoveled eagerly, finding myself half-expecting the treasure that had obsessed my companion. After ninety minutes, the dog began to howl violently. Unlike his earlier playfulness, his distress was now intense. When Jupiter tried to restrain him, the dog resisted, jumped into the pit, and clawed at the earth. He quickly unearthed human remains—two complete skeletons mixed with metal buttons and remnants of rotted wool. A few spade strikes revealed a large Spanish knife blade, followed by several scattered gold and silver coins."
}

