  "request_id": "0851110f9363b2b7f4dd3f82",
  "rewritten_text": "Biddy wept, and the darkening garden, the lane, and the emerging stars blurred in my own vision. Nothing was ever discovered, Biddy? Nothing. Do you know what became of Orlick? Based on his clothing, I suspect he works in the quarries. You have seen him, then? Why are you staring at that dark tree? I saw him there the night she died. That was not the last time, was it? No, I have seen him there since we began walking here. It is useless, Biddy said, touching my arm as I moved to run out, you know I would not lie to you; he was only there for a moment and is gone. My indignation flared to learn she was still pursued by him, and I felt a deep hatred. I told her I would spend any amount or effort to drive him from the area. Gradually, she calmed me, speaking of how Joe loved me and never complained—she did not say of me, but I understood—always doing his duty with a strong hand, quiet tongue, and gentle heart. It is hard to praise him enough, I said, and Biddy, we must discuss these things often, as I will be here frequently now. I will not leave poor Joe alone. Biddy said nothing. Biddy, do you hear me? Yes, Mr. Pip. Aside from you calling me Mr. Pip, which seems in poor taste, what do you mean? What do I mean? Biddy asked timidly. Biddy, I said with self-righteous insistence, I must know what you mean by this. By this? Biddy repeated. Do not echo me, I retorted. You never used to echo. Used not! Biddy exclaimed. Oh, Mr. Pip! Used! I decided to drop that point. After another silent walk, I returned to my main concern. Biddy, I said, I mentioned coming here often to see Joe, which you met with silence. Please tell me why. Are you certain you will come to see him often? Biddy asked, stopping in the garden path and looking at me under the stars with clear, honest eyes."
}

