parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "04bc26877935b55beb0127f5",
  "rewritten_text": "Is that everything? That is not much. Many fall under the Republic's wrath and must peer through the small window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which one is he? I am he, as I am the last. Jarvis Lorry answers these inquiries while standing by the carriage door, addressing the officials. They inspect the vehicle and its roof luggage at a slow pace, while local onlookers crowd around to stare. A mother holds her child forward so the infant might touch the wife of an aristocrat destined for the guillotine. Here are your signed papers, Jarvis Lorry. May one leave, citizen? One may leave. Move on, postilions! Safe travels! I salute you, citizens. The first danger is behind us! Jarvis Lorry speaks these words, clasping his hands and looking up. Inside the carriage, there is weeping, terror, and the labored breathing of the unconscious passenger. Are we moving too slowly? Can they be made to go faster? Lucie asks, clutching the old man. It would appear like fleeing, my dear. I cannot press them too hard, or it will invite suspicion. Look back and see if we are followed! The road is empty, my dearest. We are not pursued yet. We pass scattered houses, farms, decaying structures, tanneries, and barren trees. The carriage rattles over uneven stones, occasionally veering into deep mud to avoid the jolting road, or becoming stuck in ruts. Our impatience is agonizing, and in our panic, we long to flee on foot. We pass through more ruins and desolate farms. Have they tricked us and returned us to the same spot? Thankfully, no. A village. Look back to see if we are pursued! Quiet! The posting-house. Our four horses are unhitched at a leisurely pace, leaving the coach motionless in the street. New horses arrive slowly, followed by postilions who idly toy with their whips, while the previous drivers count their coins, miscalculating and grumbling at the totals."
}

