parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ef41d5444e67c46102ed56c5",
  "rewritten_text": "Sydney Carton stood in the street, unsure of his next move. \"Tellson's at nine,\" he mused. \"Should I show myself before then? It is a wise precaution for these people to know I am here. I must be careful.\" He paced the darkening street, weighing the consequences of his plan. Convinced it was the right choice, he headed toward Saint Antoine. Defarge owned a wine shop in that district, and Carton found it easily without asking for directions. After locating the shop, he ate a meal and fell into a deep sleep. For the first time in years, he avoided strong spirits, having poured his brandy away the previous night. He woke at seven, feeling refreshed. On his way to Saint Antoine, he stopped at a mirror to straighten his messy cravat, collar, and hair. He then walked straight into Defarge’s shop. Only Jacques Three, the man he had seen on the jury, was there, drinking and talking with the Defarges and The Vengeance. Carton sat down and ordered a small wine in broken French. Madame Defarge glanced at him, then looked again with sharper interest. She approached him and asked what he wanted. He repeated his order. \"English?\" she asked, raising her eyebrows. He looked at her as if struggling to understand, then replied with a heavy foreign accent."
}

