parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6502e520faf13331143dcad6",
  "rewritten_text": "I have not mentioned the most important point. What is that, Harry? asked the boy listlessly. The obvious comfort of taking someone else’s admirer when you lose your own. In high society, that always clears a woman’s reputation. But Dorian, Sibyl Vane was so different from the women we usually meet. There is something beautiful about her death. I am glad to live in an age where such wonders occur, as they make romance, passion, and love feel real. I was terribly cruel to her, you forget that. I suspect women appreciate cruelty above all else. They have primitive instincts; even though we have freed them, they remain slaves looking for masters. They love being dominated. I am sure you were magnificent. You told me something the other day that seemed fanciful then, but I now see it is the key to everything. What was that? You said Sibyl Vane represented all the heroines of romance to you—that she was Desdemona one night and Ophelia the next. She will never come to life again, the boy muttered, hiding his face in his hands. No, she has played her final part. Think of her lonely death in that cheap dressing room as a dark scene from a classic tragedy. She never truly lived, so she never truly died. To you, she was always a dream, a phantom that moved through Shakespeare’s plays. The moment she touched real life, she ruined it, and it ruined her. Mourn for Ophelia if you wish, or cry over Cordelia, but do not waste tears on Sibyl Vane. She was less real than they were. Silence filled the room as evening darkened. Shadows crept in from the garden, and the colors faded. After a while, Dorian Gray looked up."
}

