  "rewritten_text": "I hope to introduce my son to you soon, Mr. Weston said. Mrs. Elton, eager to interpret this as a personal compliment, smiled graciously. You have heard of Frank Churchill, I assume, and know he is my son, even if he does not carry my name. Oh, yes, and I look forward to meeting him. Mr. Elton will certainly call on him promptly, and we would be delighted to host him at the Vicarage. You are very kind. Frank will be thrilled. He is arriving in town next week, or perhaps sooner. We received word in a letter today. I collected the mail this morning and, seeing my son’s handwriting, opened it, even though it was addressed to Mrs. Weston. She is his primary correspondent. I rarely receive letters myself. You actually opened her mail? Mr. Elton laughed affectedly. I must protest. That is a dangerous precedent! I hope our neighbors do not follow your lead. If this is what I can expect, we married women must stand our ground. I never would have believed it of you! We men are terrible, Mr. Weston admitted. You must be careful, Mrs. Elton. The letter is brief, just to give us notice. It says they are all coming to town immediately because of Mrs. Churchill. She has been unwell all winter and finds Enscombe too cold, so they are moving south quickly. Indeed, from Yorkshire? Yes, about one hundred and ninety miles from London, quite a journey. It is significant, certainly. Sixty-five miles further than from Maple Grove to London. But distance means little to the wealthy. You would be amazed at how my brother, Mr. Suckling, travels. He and Mr. Bragge once went to London and back twice in a single week with four horses. The trouble with the distance from Enscombe, Mr. Weston noted, is that Mrs. Churchill has reportedly been unable to leave her sofa for a week. In his last letter, Frank mentioned she was too weak to reach her conservatory without the support of both his arm and his uncle’s."
}

