parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7955649ba10e349356897733",
  "rewritten_text": "I had no concerns for her. She was a capable woman who managed her affairs well without me. In truth, she likely viewed me as an idle dreamer and was relieved to see me go. It was a dark night when I said goodbye, taking the three creditors who had plagued me as my assistants. We transported the balloon and its gear to the station where the rest of my equipment was stored. Finding everything undisturbed, I began my work. It was April first. The night was dark and starless, with a cold, drizzling rain that made us miserable. My main worry was the balloon, which was becoming heavy with moisture despite its varnish, and the risk to my gunpowder. I kept my three creditors busy packing ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. They constantly pestered me about my plans, complaining about the grueling labor and getting soaked for what they called these dark rituals. I grew anxious, working frantically, as I suspected these fools believed I was in league with the devil. Fearing they might abandon me, I calmed them by promising to pay all my debts in full once the task was finished. They interpreted this as a sign that I would soon possess vast sums of money; as long as they were paid, they clearly did not care about my soul or my safety. After four and a half hours, the balloon was sufficiently inflated. I attached the basket and loaded my equipment, including the condensing unit, plenty of water, and compact, nutrient-dense provisions like pemmican."
}

