parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3682849c2bd65e2126a206f1",
  "rewritten_text": "Suddenly, Mr. Podgers dropped Lord Arthur’s right hand and grabbed his left, leaning so close that his glasses nearly touched the palm. His face turned deathly pale for a moment, but he quickly regained his composure. Looking up at Lady Windermere, he forced a smile and said, \"He has the hand of a charming young man.\" \"Of course he does,\" Lady Windermere replied, \"but will he be a charming husband? That is what I need to know.\" \"All charming young men are,\" Mr. Podgers said. \"I don't think a husband should be too fascinating,\" Lady Jedburgh murmured thoughtfully. \"It is quite dangerous.\" \"My dear, they are never too fascinating,\" Lady Windermere insisted. \"But I want details. Details are the only things that matter. What will happen to Lord Arthur?\" \"Well, within the next few months, Lord Arthur will take a voyage—\" \"His honeymoon, of course!\" \"And he will lose a relative.\" \"Not his sister, I hope?\" Lady Jedburgh asked anxiously. \"Certainly not his sister,\" Mr. Podgers replied with a dismissive wave. \"Merely a distant relative.\" \"I am terribly disappointed,\" Lady Windermere said. \"I have nothing to tell Sybil tomorrow. No one cares about distant relatives anymore; they went out of style years ago. Still, she should probably keep a black silk dress on hand; it is always appropriate for church. Now, let us go to supper. They have likely eaten everything, but we might find some hot soup. François used to make excellent soup, but he is so distracted by politics lately that I am never certain of his work. I wish General Boulanger would stay quiet. Duchess, you must be tired?\" \"Not at all, dear Gladys,\" the Duchess replied, waddling toward the door. \"I have enjoyed myself immensely, and the palm reader is fascinating. Flora, where is my fan? Oh, thank you, Sir Thomas. And my lace shawl? Thank you, Sir Thomas, you are very kind.\" The woman finally managed to reach the stairs, dropping her perfume bottle only twice. Throughout this, Lord Arthur Savile stood by the fireplace, gripped by a sickening sense of impending doom. He smiled sadly at his sister as she passed on Lord Plymdale’s arm, looking beautiful in pink brocade and pearls, and he barely heard Lady Windermere calling him to follow. He thought of Sybil Merton, and the fear that anything could come between them filled his eyes with tears."
}

