parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5647020a84877af389203130",
  "rewritten_text": "Will you? His eyes brightened, and he declared, I will help you steal him! I was stunned, feeling as if I had been struck. It was the most shocking thing I had ever heard, and I must admit my opinion of Tom Sawyer dropped significantly. I simply could not believe it; Tom Sawyer, a person who would steal a slave? Oh, nonsense, I replied, you are teasing. I am not teasing at all. Well, I said, whether you are or not, if you hear any talk about a runaway, remember that neither of us knows anything about him. We loaded the trunk into my wagon, then parted ways. I was so preoccupied and elated that I forgot to drive slowly, arriving home much faster than expected. The old man met me at the door, exclaiming, This is incredible! Who would have thought the mare had it in her? I wish we had timed her. She is not even sweating. I would not take a hundred dollars for her now, even though I would have sold her for fifteen earlier. He was the most guileless, kindhearted man I have ever known. This was not surprising, as he was both a farmer and a preacher who maintained a small log church on his property, providing services and schooling for free. Such men were common in the South. About thirty minutes later, Tom’s wagon pulled up to the stile. Aunt Sally spotted it from the window and called out, Someone has arrived! I believe it is a stranger. Jimmy, tell Lize to set another plate. Everyone hurried to the door, as visitors were rare and far more exciting than an epidemic. Tom hopped over the stile and headed toward the house while his wagon sped off toward the village. We gathered at the entrance, watching Tom approach in his fine clothes, clearly relishing the attention."
}

