            "text": "{\n  \"request_id\": \"ece14b5ab620e2ea29fbedb8\",\n  \"rewritten_text\": \"I know she would! The Widow Douglas’ generous invitation was hard to resist. Between that and Tom’s convincing, they decided to keep their plans secret. Tom worried that Huck might show up tonight to give the signal, which dampened his excitement. Still, he couldn't bear to miss the fun at the Widow’s house. He reasoned that since the signal hadn't come the night before, it was unlikely to happen tonight. The certain fun of the evening outweighed the uncertain treasure, so he decided to follow his desires and stop thinking about the money. Three miles downstream, the ferryboat stopped at a wooded hollow. The crowd rushed ashore, filling the forest with shouts and laughter. After exhausting themselves with games, they returned to camp hungry and devoured the food. Following the feast, they rested in the shade of oak trees. Soon, someone shouted, Who is ready for the cave? Everyone was. They grabbed candles and raced up the hill to the cave entrance, which was shaped like the letter A. The heavy oak door was unlocked. Inside, the small chamber felt like an icebox, with limestone walls covered in cold moisture. It felt mysterious to stand in the gloom and look out at the sunny green valley, but the novelty soon faded, and the play resumed. Whenever someone lit a candle, the others would rush them. After a brief struggle, the candle would be knocked over or blown out, leading to more laughter and chasing. Eventually, the group moved down the main path, their flickering lights revealing rock walls that rose sixty feet high. This main tunnel was only eight to ten feet wide. Every few steps, narrow crevices branched off in both directions, as McDougal’s cave was a vast maze of winding paths that led nowhere.\"\n}",
