parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fd208e7ac3753755d62c7d87",
  "rewritten_text": "Nearly a year had passed since my father returned, and it was now late May. The woods were lush and green, and the scent of fresh grass filled the air. I hoped the beauty of nature would soften his heart and help me gain his trust. I chose one of these evenings for my attempt. I invited him for a walk, leading him to a nearby beech grove where the trees shielded us from the setting sun. After walking in silence, we sat on a mossy mound. I can still see that spot clearly: the smooth, slender trunks wrapped in dark ivy, the white bark, and the young leaves. The ground was covered in moss and scattered autumn leaves, with a few old stumps nearby. The breeze stirred the canopy, revealing the blue sky, and as evening approached, the sun turned the distant trees a deep red. The wind died down, and birds flew past toward their nests. We sat together in this peaceful place, which could have been a paradise if not for our inner turmoil. I remember it vividly, hoping its stillness would give me the courage and the right words to speak. My heart raced as I prepared to address him, determined not to be turned away, yet trembling at how he might react. Finally, I began with hesitation: Your kindness and the deep affection you showed me when you first returned give me the courage to speak to you now, not just as a daughter, but with the openness of a friend."
}

