parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "bc0839717a254048c7752c6b",
  "rewritten_text": "When I saw you become the object of another’s affection, imagining you loved as something other than a sacred symbol of excellence, or that you might love someone else more deeply than me, a monster awoke inside. I dismissed your suitor, and since then, I have known no peace. Sleep eluded me; my blood was in constant turmoil. I woke to a new life, like someone dying in hope only to wake in Hell. I will not stain your mind by detailing my inner battles, my self-loathing, or my despair. Let a veil cover the unimaginable sensations of a guilty father; the secrets of such an agonized heart should not be cheapened. Everything was chaos, crime, remorse, and hate, yet beneath it all remained the tenderest love. What first drove me to resolve to conquer my passion and restore a father to my child was the sight of your bitter, sympathetic sorrow. That brought me here. I thought if I could reawaken the grief I felt at your mother’s death—memories dormant for seventeen years—my love for her child would vanish. In a fit of heroism, I decided to leave you, the life of my life, and never see you again until I could do so without guilt. But it failed; I overestimated my strength or underestimated my love. I would have died had you not come to me. I wish I had truly perished. Now, Mathilda, I must make my final confession. I was miserably mistaken to think I could conquer my love for you; I never can. The sight of this house, these fields and woods where my first love lived, seems to have intensified it. In my madness, I dared tell myself that Diana died to give you birth, that her spirit transferred into your frame, and you should be Diana to me. Every effort to cast it off only makes this guilty, unnatural love cling closer, withering your hopes and destroying me forever. It would have been better to love despair and kiss her instead. No time or distance can tear from my soul what has become a part of it. Since arriving here, I have not for a moment ceased to feel the hell of this passion, which will burn until I am cold, stiff, and dead."
}

