parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e9f5c6f3803ae2b731b02538",
  "rewritten_text": "The stars remained brilliant when I roused, and the position of Taurus indicated it was midnight. My sleep had been plagued by restless visions. I dreamt I attended Timon’s final banquet, arriving hungry only to find the dishes filled with nothing but steam. My host, taking the form of Raymond, chased me away, hurling vessels that emitted foul vapors. His figure warped into a towering specter marked by the plague, growing until it threatened to shatter the very sky. This nightmare became unbearable, forcing me to wake and regain my composure. My immediate concern was Perdita; I needed to return to her, offering support to her broken spirit by balancing the demands of duty with the gentleness of shared sorrow. Guided by the stars, I departed the ruins of the Golden City with great effort. Outside the walls, I secured a horse from a group of soldiers and hurried toward my sister. The landscape had shifted; the army’s camp was dismantled, and the scattered, demoralized remnants of the troops wandered about in shock. With a heavy heart, I entered the palace, hesitating to approach. Perdita sat upon the marble floor, her head bowed, hair untidy, and hands wringing in distress. She was deathly pale, her face etched with pain. When she looked up, the brief flicker of hope in her eyes was heartbreaking. I could not speak, my face twisting into a grimace. She grasped the meaning of my silence, bowing her head once more. When I finally found my voice, the sound startled her; she dreaded hearing the confirmation of her tragedy spoken aloud. Instead, she stood and whispered, urging me to be quiet because Clara had finally fallen asleep after much weeping."
}

