parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2ad1304bdea501b708dda1c3",
  "rewritten_text": "The following day, the ghost felt exhausted and frail. The intense strain of the past month had taken its toll, leaving his nerves frayed and his disposition jumpy. After remaining in his quarters for five days, he decided to abandon the library bloodstain. Since the Otis family lacked the refinement to appreciate such symbolic phenomena, they were clearly unworthy of it. While he could not control his astral manifestations, he felt bound by duty to haunt the corridor weekly and wail from the oriel window twice a month. Despite his wicked past, he remained diligent regarding his supernatural obligations. For the next three Saturdays, he crept through the hall between midnight and three, taking great care to remain undetected. He discarded his boots, stepped softly on the creaking floorboards, donned a velvet cloak, and used the Rising Sun Lubricator to silence his chains. Though initially humiliated, he eventually recognized the utility of the oil. Nevertheless, he faced constant harassment. The twins frequently rigged the corridor with tripwires, and he suffered a painful tumble after stepping on a butter-slide they had placed near the Tapestry Chamber while he was disguised as Black Isaac. Enraged by this indignity, he vowed to reclaim his status by terrorizing the boys the following night as Reckless Rupert, the Headless Earl. He had not donned this costume in seventy years, not since he had frightened Lady Barbara Modish so severely that she abandoned her engagement to Lord Canterville’s grandfather and eloped with Jack Castleton, refusing to marry into a family plagued by such a ghastly specter."
}

