parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ba0cf36608a7aeaffad83ab9",
  "rewritten_text": "“Darn it, Tom,” I said, “you could at least tell me. What is a bar sinister?” “Oh, I don’t know. But he has to have one. All the nobility does.” That was just how he was. If he did not feel like explaining something, he would not do it. You could pester him for a week and it would not make a difference. He had the coat of arms figured out, so he started planning a mournful inscription, insisting Jim needed one just like the others. He wrote several on a piece of paper and read them aloud: 1. Here a captive heart broke. 2. Here a poor prisoner, abandoned by the world and friends, spent his sorrowful life. 3. Here a lonely heart broke, and a weary spirit found rest after thirty-seven years of solitary confinement. 4. Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV. Tom’s voice shook as he read them, and he nearly broke down. When he finished, he could not decide which one Jim should carve into the wall because they were all so good, so he decided Jim should carve them all. Jim complained it would take him a year to scratch all that onto the logs with a nail, and he did not know how to write letters anyway. Tom promised to outline them so Jim only had to follow the lines. Then he said, “Actually, logs will not work; dungeons do not have log walls. We need to carve the inscriptions into a rock. We will get a rock.” Jim argued that a rock was even worse than logs, saying it would take so long to carve that he would never get out. Tom said he would let me help. He then checked on how Jim and I were doing with the pens. It was incredibly tedious, slow work that kept my hands from healing, and we were barely making progress, so Tom said, “I know how to fix this. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the inscriptions, so we can kill two birds with one stone.”"
}

