parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "118b21195449cd8cec614faa",
  "rewritten_text": "He was powerless to stop it. It infuriated him to believe that Becky Thatcher was unaware he was even alive. Yet she saw him clearly, aware that her strategy was succeeding, and she took pleasure in his pain. Amy’s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom suggested he had urgent matters to attend to, but the girl kept talking. He silently cursed her, desperate to escape. When he finally left, she innocently promised to be nearby after school, and he departed, resenting her presence. He seethed, wishing it were any boy other than that arrogant Saint Louis dandy. He recalled beating the newcomer before and vowed to do it again, acting out a violent thrashing against the empty air to satisfy his rage. Tom hurried home at midday, unable to tolerate Amy’s joy or his own jealousy. Meanwhile, Becky’s interest in the pictures faded as Tom failed to appear. Her triumph turned to gloom and then misery, regretting her actions. When Alfred tried to show her another drawing, she snapped at him, weeping as she walked away. Alfred attempted to comfort her, but she told him to leave her alone, declaring her hatred for him. Confused and hurt, Alfred watched her go, eventually realizing he had merely been a tool for her to spite Tom Sawyer."
}

