  "rewritten_text": "“Do you play?” The spy turned to Mr. Lorry with a humble air. “Sir, I appeal to a man of your age and kindness. Please ask this younger gentleman if he can justify playing the Ace he mentioned. I admit I am a spy, a disreputable job that someone must do, but he is not. Why should he lower himself to act like one?” “I will play my Ace, Mr. Barsad,” Carton replied, checking his watch, “without hesitation in a few minutes.” The spy tried to involve Mr. Lorry again. “I had hoped, gentlemen, that your respect for my sister—” “I could show no greater respect for your sister than by finally removing her brother from her life,” Sydney Carton interrupted. “You think so, sir?” “I have fully decided.” The spy’s smooth tone, which clashed with his rough clothes, faltered against Carton’s mysterious demeanor. Carton looked at his cards and said, “Thinking again, I recall another card. That fellow who claimed to be working in the country prisons; who was he?” “He was French. You don’t know him,” the spy said quickly. “French, eh?” Carton mused, echoing him. “Perhaps.” “He is, I assure you, though it is not important,” the spy insisted. “Though it is not important,” Carton repeated mechanically. “Yet I know the face.” “I think not. I am sure not. It cannot be,” the spy said. “It cannot be,” Sydney Carton muttered, swirling his glass. “It cannot be. He spoke good French, yet like a foreigner, I thought?” “Provincial,” the spy suggested. “No, foreign!” Carton cried, striking the table as he realized the truth. “Cly! He was disguised, but it is the same man. We saw him at the Old Bailey.” “Now, you are being hasty, sir,” Barsad said with a crooked smile. “You are really giving me an advantage over you.”"
}

