parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6261c728b9fd02b91995ab5a",
  "rewritten_text": "The following day, the ghost felt exhausted. The stress of the past month had shattered his nerves, leaving him jumpy at every sound. After staying in his room for five days, he decided to stop trying to maintain the bloodstain in the library. Since the Otis family did not want it, they clearly did not deserve it; they were far too practical to appreciate such symbolic displays. While he could not control his ghostly appearances, he felt a duty to haunt the corridor weekly and moan from the window twice a month. Despite his wicked past, he took his supernatural responsibilities seriously. For the next three Saturdays, he crept through the halls between midnight and three in the morning, taking great care to remain unseen. He removed his boots, walked softly on the creaky floorboards, wore a velvet cloak, and even used the Rising Sun Lubricator to silence his chains. Though he felt humiliated using the oil, he eventually admitted it was practical. Nevertheless, the family continued to harass him. They stretched strings across the hallway to trip him, and the twins once created a butter-slide that caused him to fall while he was dressed as the Huntsman of Hogley Woods. Furious at this final insult, he decided to reclaim his dignity by appearing the next night as his most famous persona: Reckless Rupert, the Headless Earl. He had not used this disguise in seventy years, not since he had terrified Lady Barbara Modish so badly that she broke off her engagement to the current Lord Canterville’s grandfather and eloped with Jack Castleton, refusing to marry into a family haunted by such a creature."
}

