  "request_id": "30d3e11c7293ba797ad25caf",
  "rewritten_text": "He sprang up and gripped her arm harshly. \"Point him out. Which one is he? I must see him!\" he cried, but the Duke of Berwick’s coach blocked their view, and by the time it passed, the carriage had vanished from the park. \"He is gone,\" Sibyl whispered mournfully. \"I wish you had seen him.\" \"I wish I had, for as surely as God exists, if he ever harms you, I will kill him.\" She stared at him, terrified. He repeated the threat, his words sharp as a blade. Bystanders began to stare, and a nearby woman giggled. \"Come away, Jim,\" she urged. He followed her stubbornly through the throng, satisfied with his declaration. Near the Achilles Statue, she turned to him, her eyes filled with pity that soon turned to a smile. She shook her head. \"You are foolish, Jim; just a bad-tempered boy. How can you say such awful things? You are merely jealous and unkind. I wish you would fall in love; it makes people good, and your words were wicked.\" \"I am sixteen,\" he replied, \"and I know my mind. Mother cannot help you; she does not know how to protect you. I regret leaving for Australia. I would abandon the plan if my papers were not already signed.\" \"Do not be so serious, Jim. You sound like the heroes in the melodramas Mother used to act in. I will not fight with you. Seeing him is perfect happiness. You would never harm someone I love, would you?\" \"Not while you love him, I suppose,\" he muttered. \"I shall love him forever!\" she declared. \"And he?\" \"Forever, too!\" \"He had better.\" She recoiled, then laughed and touched his arm, dismissing him as a mere boy. At Marble Arch, they boarded an omnibus toward their modest Euston Road home. It was past five, and Jim insisted Sibyl rest before her performance. He preferred to say goodbye without their mother present, as he loathed the scenes she was prone to creating."
}

