  "request_id": "6089c3c9670b65e7c636f515",
  "rewritten_text": "Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by ghosts. The perpetrators were physical beings who made a physical escape. How? Fortunately, there is only one logical way to approach this, and it must lead to a firm conclusion. Let us examine every possible exit. The killers were clearly in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or the adjacent one, when the witnesses climbed the stairs. We need only search those two rooms. The police have inspected the floors, ceilings, and walls thoroughly; no hidden exits could have escaped them. Still, not trusting their eyes, I checked myself. There were no secret passages. Both doors leading to the hallway were locked from the inside with the keys in the locks. As for the chimneys, while they are wide near the hearth, they narrow so much that even a large cat could not pass through. Since these exits are impossible, we must look to the windows. No one could have escaped the front windows without being seen by the crowd below. The killers must have used the back windows. Having reached this conclusion, we cannot reject it simply because it seems impossible. We must prove that these apparent impossibilities are not impossible at all. There are two windows in the room. One is unobstructed and fully visible. The other is partially blocked by the head of a heavy bed pushed against it. The first window was locked from the inside and resisted all attempts to open it. A large hole had been drilled into the frame, and a sturdy nail was driven in. The second window had an identical nail, and it also refused to budge. The police were satisfied that no one had escaped through them, so they saw no reason to remove the nails and open the windows. My own investigation was more thorough, precisely because I knew that the apparent impossibilities had to be explained."
}

