parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "daa46abc73c0fc6975d12585",
  "rewritten_text": "Unless you call Miss Havisham a man. And I doubt you would go that far. Miss Havisham, in town? Joe asked. Is there any other Miss Havisham? my sister replied. She wants this boy to go play there. He is going, and he had better play, she said, shaking her head at me to encourage me to act cheerful, or I will punish him. I had heard of Miss Havisham; everyone for miles around knew of the wealthy, grim woman who lived in a large, gloomy house, barricaded against thieves and hidden from the world. Well, I am surprised, Joe said, amazed. I wonder how she knows Pip! Fool! my sister cried. Who said she knew him? Joe politely suggested that someone must have mentioned he should go play there. Could she not ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew a boy? Is it not possible that Pumblechook is her tenant and visits to pay his rent? Could she not ask him then? And could Pumblechook, always thoughtful of us—though you might not think so, Joseph—then suggest this boy, who stands here prancing—I was certainly not prancing—whom I have always served so faithfully? Well said! Uncle Pumblechook cheered. Very well put! Now, Joseph, you understand the situation. No, Joseph, my sister said reproachfully, while Joe wiped his nose apologetically, you still do not understand. You may think you do, but you do not. You do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, realizing this boy’s fortune might be made by visiting Miss Havisham, has offered to take him to town tonight, keep him, and bring him to her tomorrow morning. Good heavens! my sister cried, throwing off her bonnet in frustration, here I am talking to idiots while Uncle Pumblechook waits, the horse catches a cold outside, and the boy is covered in dirt from head to toe!"
}

