parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "92d536e72557f01d424a5277",
  "rewritten_text": "He could not help it. It infuriated him to think that Becky Thatcher did not even realize he was alive. In truth, she saw him perfectly well; she knew she was winning her battle and felt satisfied to see him suffer just as she had. Amy’s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom made excuses about urgent tasks he had to finish, but the girl kept talking. He thought, \"I wish she would leave me alone.\" Finally, he had to walk away, and she innocently mentioned she would be nearby when school ended. He hurried off, resenting her presence. \"Any other boy!\" Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \"Anyone but that show-off from Saint Louis who thinks he is so fancy! I beat you once, and I will do it again. Just wait until I catch you!\" He began shadowboxing, punching and kicking the air until he felt satisfied with the imaginary fight. Tom ran home at noon. He could not stand Amy’s happiness or his own jealousy any longer. Becky went back to looking at pictures with Alfred, but as time passed without Tom appearing to witness her triumph, she lost interest. She grew sad and distracted, listening for his footsteps, but he never came. Eventually, she felt miserable and regretted taking things so far. When Alfred kept trying to show her pictures, she snapped, \"Leave me alone! I don't care about them!\" and walked away crying. Alfred tried to follow and comfort her, but she shouted, \"Go away! I hate you!\" Alfred stopped, confused and hurt. He soon realized the truth: she had only used him to make Tom Sawyer jealous."
}

