parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "98f33abb2f142b57d57bcf73",
  "rewritten_text": "Spirits of the tempest, take me! Forces of ruin, embrace me eternally, unless a gentler fate has ordained that after long suffering I might find my recompense and once more feel my pulse quicken beside a kindred soul. The Tiber, nature’s own path across the continent, lay before me, lined with moored vessels. I resolved to board one with my books, supplies, and dog, drifting down the river to the sea. I would sail the Mediterranean, skirting the lovely coasts of Naples and Calabria, braving Scylla and Charybdis, and venturing toward Malta and the Cyclades. I would bypass Constantinople, a place belonging to a past life, and navigate past Asia Minor, Syria, and the Nile, eventually reaching the pillars of Hercules. Whether I perish in the ocean depths, succumb to illness, or find the companion I seek, I will continue. If I remain alone, I will sail on, aged and weary, forever navigating new bays and promontories, leaving Europe behind to traverse the African coast and the Cape, until I finally anchor in some remote, fragrant island of the Indian Ocean. These are frantic visions, yet they have dominated my thoughts since I stood atop St. Peter’s a week ago. I have secured my boat, gathered my provisions, and chosen my books—Homer and Shakespeare—though I can replenish my library at any port. I do not anticipate improvement, but I can no longer endure the monotony of my current existence."
}

