parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1eb03673802689e2c1fbc31d",
  "rewritten_text": "Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by ghosts. The perpetrators were physical beings who made a physical escape. How, then? Fortunately, there is only one logical path to follow, and it leads to a certain conclusion. Let us consider every possible exit. It is certain the killers were in the room where the daughter was discovered, or the adjacent one, when the group climbed the stairs. We need only search those two spaces. The police inspected the floors, ceilings, and walls thoroughly; no hidden passages could have eluded them. Still, I verified this myself. There were no secret exits. Both doors opening into the hallway were locked from the inside. As for the chimneys, while wide at the base, they narrow too much to allow even a large cat to pass. Since these routes are impossible, we must look to the windows. The front windows were observed by the street crowd, so the killers must have used the back ones. Having reached this conclusion, we must not discard it due to perceived impossibilities; instead, we must demonstrate that these obstacles are not truly impossible. There are two windows in the room. One is clear and fully visible. The other is partially blocked by the head of a heavy bed. The first was locked from within and resisted all attempts to open it. A stout nail had been driven through a gimlet-hole in the frame. The second window had an identical nail, and it also refused to budge. The police, satisfied that no one had escaped that way, saw no reason to remove the nails. My own inspection was more thorough, precisely because I knew that any apparent impossibility had to be proven false."
}

