parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e16d8f3873471885e4f152cb",
  "rewritten_text": "We eventually decided to live together during my time in the city. Since my finances were more stable than his, I paid to rent and furnish a decaying, strange old mansion in a quiet, lonely part of the Faubourg St. Germain. The house had been abandoned for years due to local superstitions we chose not to investigate. If people knew how we lived, they would have thought us mad, though harmlessly so. We lived in total isolation, admitting no visitors. I kept our location secret from my old friends, and Dupin had long since cut ties with everyone in Paris. We lived entirely within our own private world. Dupin had a strange obsession with the night, and I easily adopted his eccentric habits. Since we could not have darkness all the time, we faked it. At dawn, we closed the heavy shutters and lit scented candles that cast only a faint, ghostly light. We spent our days reading, writing, or talking until the clock signaled the arrival of true night. Then, we would walk the streets arm in arm, discussing our earlier topics or wandering the city to find the mental stimulation that comes from observing the crowds. During these walks, I admired Dupin’s remarkable analytical skills. He clearly enjoyed using them and admitted as much. He would chuckle and claim he could see right through people, often proving it by revealing intimate details about my own thoughts. At these moments, his manner became cold and detached, his eyes looked vacant, and his voice shifted from a rich tenor to a high, sharp pitch that remained precise and deliberate."
}

