  "rewritten_text": "He conveyed that Frank held her in high regard, finding her both beautiful and charming. Given his many positive qualities, she decided not to judge him too severely, echoing Mrs. Weston’s sentiment that young people are prone to occasional whims. However, not everyone in Surrey shared this leniency. While most residents of Donwell and Highbury were forgiving of a handsome young man with a pleasant smile and polite manners, Mr. Knightley remained unmoved. Upon hearing the news at Hartfield, he initially stayed quiet, only to mutter to himself while reading the paper, “Hum! Just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.” Emma considered taking offense, but realizing he was merely venting his own thoughts rather than trying to provoke her, she chose to ignore it. Though the Westons brought mixed news, their visit proved timely, as Emma required their counsel on a matter, and their advice aligned perfectly with her own inclinations. The Coles, a friendly and unpretentious family of modest origins and trade backgrounds, had recently seen their fortunes rise significantly. With their newfound wealth, they expanded their home and lifestyle, now rivaling all but the most prominent families in the area. Their recent social gatherings for local bachelors signaled their intent to host larger dinner parties. Emma doubted they would dare invite the elite families of Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls, but she resolved that nothing would induce her to attend if they did, regretting only that her father’s predictable habits might obscure the significance of her refusal."
}

