  "rewritten_text": "Well, Jupiter, what is the matter? How is your master? To tell the truth, sir, he is not as well as he could be. Not well? I am sorry to hear that. What are his symptoms? That is the problem. He never complains of anything, yet he is very sick. Very sick, Jupiter? Why didn't you say so? Is he bedridden? No, he is not confined at all, and that is where the trouble lies. I am very worried about poor Master Will. Jupiter, I need to understand you. You say he is sick, but has he told you what is wrong? Sir, please do not get angry. Master Will says nothing is wrong, but he walks around with his head down, his shoulders hunched, and he is as pale as a ghost. He also keeps scribbling on a slate. Keeps what? He keeps scribbling figures on a slate, the strangest figures I have ever seen. I am getting scared. I have to keep a close eye on his movements. The other day he slipped away before sunrise and was gone all day. I had a big stick ready to give him a good beating when he returned, but I am such a fool that I did not have the heart to do it because he looked so poorly. I see. I think it is best you do not be too severe with him. Do not hit him, Jupiter; he cannot handle it. Do you have any idea what caused this change in behavior? Has anything unpleasant happened since I last saw you? No, sir, nothing has happened since then. I fear it was before that, the very day you were there. What do you mean? I mean the bug, sir. The what? The bug. I am certain Master Will was bitten on the head by that gold bug. What makes you think that? It had plenty of claws and a mouth, sir. I have never seen such a bug. It kicked and bit everything near it. Master Will caught it first but had to let it go quickly. That must be when he was bitten. I did not like the look of the bug's mouth, so I would not touch it with my fingers, but I caught it with a piece of paper I found."
}

