parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ce9ad655c7b350d6ed42a108",
  "rewritten_text": "I told him I needed the watermelon, but he argued that I did not need it to escape prison. He claimed it would only be acceptable if I were hiding a knife to help Jim kill the guard. I dropped the subject, though I found it annoying to debate such fine points whenever I just wanted a snack. That morning, we waited until the yard was empty. Tom took the sack into the shed while I kept watch. When he returned, we sat on the woodpile to talk. He said everything was ready except for the tools. I asked what tools he meant, and he explained we needed them to dig Jim out, since we could not just gnaw through the walls. I asked if the old, broken picks in the shed would work. Tom looked at me with pity and asked if I had ever heard of a prisoner using modern tools to escape. He argued that using shovels would ruin the heroics of the situation; they might as well just give him the key. He insisted we needed a couple of table knives instead. I told him it was foolish to try to dig out a cabin foundation with knives. Tom replied that it did not matter if it seemed foolish because it was the proper, traditional way. He claimed he had read all the books on the subject, and prisoners always used knives to dig through solid rock, a process that took weeks or even years. He asked me how long I thought a prisoner in the Castle of If had spent digging his way out. When I said I did not know, he told me to guess, and I suggested a month and a half."
}

