parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3c75e511f081803daf243361",
  "rewritten_text": "Good God! Had I realized for a moment the hellish intent of my monstrous enemy, I would have exiled myself forever, wandering the earth as a friendless outcast, rather than agreeing to this wretched marriage. Yet, as if by magic, the creature blinded me to his true motives; while I believed I was merely preparing for my own death, I was actually hastening the demise of a far more precious victim. As our wedding date approached, whether from cowardice or intuition, my heart sank. I hid my dread behind a facade of cheerfulness that pleased my father, though it barely fooled the observant Elizabeth. She anticipated our union with calm contentment, tempered by a lingering fear that this tangible happiness might vanish like a dream, leaving only lasting regret. Preparations continued, and we received well-wishers, all while I suppressed the anxiety consuming me. I participated in my father’s plans, even as they felt like decorations for my own tragedy. Through his efforts, the Austrian government restored part of Elizabeth’s inheritance, including a small property on the shores of Lake Como. We agreed to spend our honeymoon at Villa Lavenza. Meanwhile, I took every precaution against an open attack, carrying a pistol and dagger, constantly alert for treachery. This vigilance brought me some peace. As the day neared, the threat seemed like a delusion, and our marriage felt increasingly inevitable. Elizabeth appeared happy, and my calm demeanor helped settle her nerves. Yet, on the day that was to seal my fate, she grew melancholy, filled with a sense of impending doom—perhaps because she was thinking of the terrible secret I had promised to reveal to her the following day."
}

