parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6f0b5ec235c73ec4ea092325",
  "rewritten_text": "He was dressed as Jonas the Graveless, a character from one of his most famous performances that had once caused a major feud between the Cantervilles and their neighbor, Lord Rufford. It was past two in the morning, and he believed the house was quiet. As he walked toward the library to check for the bloodstain, two figures jumped from the shadows, waving their arms and shouting 'BOO!' in his ear. Terrified, he ran for the stairs, only to find Washington Otis waiting with a large garden syringe. Trapped, he escaped into a cold iron stove and crawled through the chimneys, reaching his room covered in soot and deeply humiliated. He did not venture out again. The twins tried to lure him out by scattering nutshells in the halls, but he remained hidden, his pride clearly wounded. Mr. Otis returned to his history of the Democratic Party, Mrs. Otis hosted a successful clam-bake, the boys played American games, and Virginia rode her pony with the young Duke of Cheshire, who was visiting. Everyone assumed the ghost had left, and Mr. Otis even wrote to Lord Canterville to confirm it, much to the Lord's relief. However, the family was mistaken. The ghost remained in the house, and though weak, he was determined to act, especially after learning that the young Duke of Cheshire was a guest. The Duke’s great-uncle had once bet he could play dice with the ghost, only to be found the next morning paralyzed and unable to speak anything but 'Double Sixes' for the rest of his life."
}

