parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ee03639ae4414a637ba54714",
  "rewritten_text": "Colonel Grangerford was a true gentleman, as was his entire family. He came from a good background, which the Widow Douglas considered as valuable in a man as in a horse, a sentiment even my father agreed with, despite his own lack of refinement. The Colonel was tall and slender with a pale complexion, free of any flush. He shaved his thin face daily, revealing sharp features: thin lips, narrow nostrils, a prominent nose, and heavy eyebrows over deep-set, dark eyes that seemed to peer from caverns. His hair was black, straight, and shoulder-length. He wore pristine white linen suits daily and a blue tail-coat with brass buttons on Sundays, always carrying a silver-headed mahogany cane. He was serious, quiet, and genuinely kind, which made people trust him. While his smile was pleasant, his stern, upright posture and flashing eyes were intimidating enough to make anyone seek safety. He never had to demand respect; his presence naturally encouraged good behavior. He brought a sense of warmth to any room, and his rare moments of anger were brief yet effective in restoring order. Each morning, the family stood when he and his wife entered, remaining standing until the couple was seated. His sons, Tom and Bob, would prepare drinks at the sideboard. They would wait for the Colonel to join them, offering a formal toast before they all drank. Even we boys, Buck and I, were included in this ritual, receiving a small portion of the drink to toast the elders."
}

