  "request_id": "c87dc88b09842e8d94fbc813",
  "rewritten_text": "When I finally caught Tom alone, I asked what his plan had been for Jim if the escape succeeded, especially since Jim was already free. He explained that he had intended for us to take Jim down the river on the raft, have adventures all the way to the mouth, and then reveal his freedom. We would have returned him home on a steamboat in style, paid him for his time, and organized a grand parade with torches and a brass band to make him a hero. I decided things turned out just as well as they were. We freed Jim from his chains quickly. Once Aunt Polly, Uncle Silas, and Aunt Sally learned how well Jim helped the doctor care for Tom, they treated him wonderfully, giving him plenty of food and rest. We visited him in the sickroom, where Tom gave Jim forty dollars for his patience. Jim was overjoyed and exclaimed that his earlier signs about becoming rich again had finally come true. Tom then suggested that the three of us sneak away soon to find supplies and head into the Territory for a few weeks of adventures among the Indians. I agreed, but mentioned I had no money for supplies and doubted I could get any from home, assuming my father had returned and spent it all. Tom assured me the money was still there, over six thousand dollars, and that my father had not been back."
}

