  "rewritten_text": "Certainly not, Duff replied. And, to clarify for the ladies, I assume you mean this attempt was not made by a local, Mr. Losberne said with a smile. That is it, sir, Blathers answered. This is all about the robbery, then? All of it, the doctor replied. Now, what is this about the boy the servants are discussing? Blathers asked. Nothing at all, the doctor said. One of the panicked servants decided he had something to do with the break-in, but it is complete nonsense. That is easily dismissed, if so, Duff remarked. He is right, Blathers agreed, nodding while fiddling with his handcuffs like castanets. Who is the boy? What is his story? Where is he from? He did not just fall from the sky, did he? Of course not, the doctor replied, glancing nervously at the ladies. I know his entire history, but we can discuss that later. I assume you would like to see where the thieves made their attempt first? Certainly, Blathers said. We should inspect the premises before questioning the servants. That is standard procedure. Lights were brought, and Blathers and Duff, followed by the local constable, Brittles, Giles, and everyone else, went to the small room at the end of the hall to examine the window. They circled the lawn to look in, inspected the shutter with a candle, traced footprints with a lantern, and poked the bushes with a pitchfork. Afterward, Giles and Brittles reenacted their roles in the previous night’s events six times, contradicting each other more frequently with every repetition. Once finished, Blathers and Duff cleared the room for a private consultation, which was far more solemn and secretive than any medical board meeting. Meanwhile, the doctor paced the next room in distress, while Mrs. Maylie and Rose watched with anxious expressions. Honestly, he said, stopping after several rapid turns, I hardly know what to do."
}

