            "text": "{\n  \"request_id\": \"0c4e0e91ce57cdf43ab63db8\",\n  \"rewritten_text\": \"Watching him in such states, I frequently pondered the ancient theory of the dual soul, entertaining the notion of a twofold Dupin—the imaginative and the analytical. Do not mistake my account for a mystery or a work of fiction. What I observed in the Frenchman was simply the product of an intense, perhaps even unhealthy, intellect. A specific instance of his commentary will best illustrate this. One evening, we were walking along a grimy street near the Palais Royal. Both lost in thought, we had remained silent for at least a quarter of an hour. Suddenly, Dupin remarked, \\\"He is quite small, it is true, and would be better suited for the Théâtre des Variétés.\\\" \\\"Undoubtedly,\\\" I answered instinctively, failing at first to realize—so deep was my contemplation—how perfectly he had aligned with my own thoughts. A moment later, I regained my composure, feeling utterly stunned. \\\"Dupin,\\\" I said seriously, \\\"this is beyond me. I am truly amazed and can hardly believe my ears. How could you possibly know I was thinking of—?\\\" I stopped, wanting to confirm if he truly knew my subject. \\\"Of Chantilly,\\\" he finished. \\\"Why do you hesitate? You were telling yourself that his small stature made him unsuitable for tragedy.\\\" This was exactly what I had been considering. Chantilly was a former cobbler from the Rue St. Denis who, obsessed with the stage, had tried to play Xerxes in Crébillon’s tragedy, only to be widely mocked for his efforts. \\\"For heaven's sake, tell me,\\\" I cried, \\\"the method—if there is one—by which you read my mind.\\\" I was more shaken than I cared to admit. \\\"It was the fruit seller,\\\" my friend replied, \\\"who led you to conclude that the cobbler lacked the height for Xerxes and his like.\\\" \\\"The fruit seller! You surprise me; I do not know any fruit seller.\\\" \\\"The man who bumped into you as we turned onto this street—perhaps fifteen minutes ago.\\\" I recalled then that a fruit seller, balancing a large basket of apples, had nearly knocked me over as we moved from the Rue C— into this thoroughfare, though I could not fathom how that connected to Chantilly.\"\n}",
