  "rewritten_text": "Her circumstances were truly fraught with unusual hardship. While she burned with a desperate need to uncover the secrets of Oliver’s past, she felt bound to honor the trust placed in her by the wretched woman she had just met. The woman’s demeanor had moved Rose Maylie deeply; alongside her devotion to her young ward, she felt a sincere, fervent desire to guide the outcast toward redemption. They intended to stay in London for only three days before traveling to the coast. It was already midnight on the first day. How could she act within forty-eight hours, or delay their departure without raising questions? Mr. Losberne was accompanying them, but Rose knew his impulsive nature too well. She feared his immediate, fiery reaction toward the person responsible for Oliver’s abduction, and she dared not confide in him without an experienced ally to plead the girl’s case. These same concerns made her wary of telling Mrs. Maylie, who would surely consult the doctor at once. Seeking legal counsel was equally unthinkable for similar reasons. She briefly considered turning to Harry, but the memory of their final parting stopped her. It felt improper to summon him—and as tears filled her eyes, she wondered if he had already moved on and found happiness in her absence. Troubled by these conflicting thoughts, Rose endured a restless, anxious night. After further deliberation the following day, she reached the difficult decision to reach out to Harry. If his return is painful for him, she reasoned, it will be equally difficult for me. Yet he might not come; he could write, or visit without seeking her out, just as he had done before. She set down her pen and turned away, as if the very letter meant to carry her message should not witness her sorrow."
}

