parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b8e159f91118a2175b5977a9",
  "rewritten_text": "It was well past midday when he roused. His valet had tiptoed into the chamber repeatedly to check for movement, puzzled by his master’s prolonged slumber. At last, the bell rang, and Victor entered quietly, bearing tea and a stack of mail on a delicate Sevres tray. He pulled back the olive-satin curtains, lined with shimmering blue, which covered the three large windows. Monsieur has slept quite late, he remarked with a smile. What time is it, Victor? Dorian Gray inquired sleepily. A quarter past one, Monsieur. How late! He sat up, sipped his tea, and examined his correspondence. One note from Lord Henry had been hand-delivered that morning; he paused, then set it aside. He opened the rest indifferently, finding the usual array of invitations, private view tickets, and charity programs typical for a fashionable man during the season. There was a substantial bill for a chased silver toilet set he dared not show his conservative guardians, who failed to grasp that in our era, luxuries are our only true necessities. Other letters included polite offers from Jermyn Street lenders to provide funds at a moment’s notice. Ten minutes later, he rose, donned an ornate cashmere dressing gown, and entered the onyx bathroom. The cold water revived him, and he seemed to shed his recent experiences. A vague memory of some dark tragedy flickered, but it felt as unreal as a dream. Once dressed, he moved to the library for a light French breakfast served at a small table by the open window. It was a beautiful day, the air heavy with spice. A bee buzzed around the blue-dragon bowl of yellow roses before him, and he felt entirely content. Suddenly, his gaze landed on the screen hiding the portrait, and he recoiled. Is it too cold for you, Monsieur? his valet asked, placing an omelette on the table. Shall I close the window? Dorian shook his head. I am not cold, he whispered."
}

