  "rewritten_text": "Tom managed to scratch the cupboard and was planning to work on the bureau when he was called to get ready for Sunday school. Mary gave him a tin basin and soap, but Tom merely poured the water out and pretended to wash. Mary took the towel away, scolding him for his laziness. Flustered, Tom tried again, though he emerged with soap dripping from his face and a clear line where the clean skin ended and the dirt began. Mary took over, scrubbing him until he was clean, brushing his hair, and styling his curls. Tom secretly flattened his hair afterward, as he hated the curls. She then produced his Sunday suit, the only other clothes he owned. After she buttoned his jacket, adjusted his collar, and placed his straw hat on his head, Tom looked much better but felt miserable. The restriction of clean, formal clothing irritated him. He hoped she would overlook his shoes, but she polished them with tallow anyway. Frustrated, Tom complained that he was always forced to do things he disliked."
}

