            "text": "{\n  \"request_id\": \"bde794a0ddc249e104d9069b\",\n  \"rewritten_text\": \"In a certain town, which I will not name, there stands a building common to most places: a workhouse. Inside this workhouse, on a date that does not matter to the reader, the boy whose name titles this story was born. For a long time after the parish surgeon brought him into this world of trouble, it was uncertain if the child would live long enough to be named. Had he died, this biography would have been incredibly short, though perhaps quite accurate. While being born in a workhouse is hardly a stroke of luck, it was actually the best thing that could have happened to Oliver Twist. He struggled to take his first breath, lying on a thin mattress, caught between life and death. Had he been surrounded by doting relatives and expert doctors, he surely would have been fussed to death. Instead, he was left with only a tipsy pauper and a busy parish surgeon. Oliver and nature fought it out alone. Eventually, he breathed, sneezed, and announced his arrival to the workhouse with a cry as loud as any newborn could manage after only three minutes of life.\"\n}",
