  "request_id": "b51eb0a0302d7cc79e8d665e",
  "rewritten_text": "“Certainly not,” Duff answered. “And, interpreting the term yokel for the ladies, I assume you mean this attempt was not the work of a countryman?” Mr. Losberne asked with a smile. “That is it, sir,” Blathers replied. “This concerns the robbery, does it?” “Entirely,” the doctor said. “Now, what is this regarding the boy the servants are discussing?” Blathers inquired. “Nothing at all,” the doctor replied. “A frightened servant imagined he was involved in the break-in, but it is pure nonsense.” “Easily dismissed, then,” Duff remarked. “He is right,” Blathers added, nodding while toying with his handcuffs. “Who is the boy? What is his story? Where is he from? He did not fall from the sky, did he?” “Of course not,” the doctor said, glancing nervously at the ladies. “I know his history, but we can discuss that later. You would prefer to see where the thieves attempted entry first, I suppose?” “Certainly,” Blathers agreed. “We should inspect the premises before questioning the servants. That is standard procedure.” Lights were brought, and Blathers and Duff, accompanied by the constable, Brittles, Giles, and the others, entered the small room to examine the window. They then circled the lawn to view it from outside, inspected the shutter with a candle, traced footprints with a lantern, and poked the bushes with a pitchfork. Once finished, they returned inside, where Giles and Brittles reenacted their roles from the previous night’s events, performing the scene six times and contradicting each other more frequently with each repetition. When finished, Blathers and Duff cleared the room for a private consultation, which was conducted with a gravity far exceeding that of a medical board. Meanwhile, the doctor paced the adjacent room in distress, while Mrs. Maylie and Rose watched with anxious expressions. “Upon my word,” he said, stopping after many rapid turns, “I hardly know how to proceed.”"
}

