parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "527bc5f74ce98799b180c5f7",
  "rewritten_text": "I want to see you well-settled, and it is best to limit your casual acquaintances. If you are still here when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your friendship with his sisters does not force you to know his wife, who will likely be an uneducated farmer’s daughter. Certainly. I do not think Mr. Martin would marry anyone without an education, but I will not argue with you. I value the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and would hate to lose them, as they are as well-educated as I am. But if he marries someone ignorant and vulgar, I will avoid her if I can. Emma watched her, seeing no signs of love. The young man had admired her first, but she trusted Harriet would not resist her own plans. They met Mr. Martin the next day on the Donwell road. He looked at Emma respectfully, then at her companion with genuine delight. Emma walked ahead to observe him. He looked neat and sensible, but lacked refinement. Compared to true gentlemen, he would surely lose Harriet’s interest. Harriet noticed manners, but Mr. Martin seemed to lack them entirely. They spoke for only a few minutes so as not to delay Miss Woodhouse. Harriet returned smiling and flustered, which Emma hoped to calm. Just think of meeting him! How strange! He said it was pure chance he did not go by way of Randalls, as he assumed we walked there most days."
}

