parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1665db74621f9b109823c085",
  "rewritten_text": "Will you? His eyes brightened as he replied, I will help you steal him! I was stunned, feeling as if I had been shot. It was the most shocking thing I had ever heard, and I must admit my opinion of Tom Sawyer dropped significantly. I simply could not believe it. Tom Sawyer, a person who would steal a slave? Oh, stop, I said. You are joking. I am not joking at all. Well then, I replied, joking or not, if you hear anything about a runaway slave, remember that you know nothing about him, and neither do I. We loaded the trunk into my wagon and went our separate ways. I was so excited and lost in thought that I drove much faster than I should have, arriving home far too quickly. The old man was at the door and exclaimed, This is incredible! Who would have thought that mare could do it? I wish we had timed her. She is not even sweating. I would not take a hundred dollars for her now, even though I would have sold her for fifteen earlier. He was the most innocent, kind-hearted man I have ever met. This was not surprising, as he was not just a farmer but also a preacher. He had built a small log church behind the plantation at his own expense to serve as a school and house of worship, never charging for his services. There were many such farmer-preachers in the South. About thirty minutes later, Tom’s wagon arrived at the front gate. Aunt Sally saw it from the window and said, Someone is here! I wonder who it is? It looks like a stranger. Jimmy, run and tell Lize to set another plate for dinner. Everyone rushed to the front door, as a visitor was a rare and exciting event. Tom climbed over the gate and headed toward the house while his wagon drove off toward the village. We all gathered at the door. Tom was wearing his best clothes and had an audience, which was exactly what he loved."
}

