parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c592614671d2e23824151d5c",
  "rewritten_text": "Without pausing to reflect, and barely aware of her own emotions, Elizabeth finished the first letter and immediately grabbed the second. She opened it with great impatience and began to read; it had been composed a day after the first. My dearest sister, you have surely received my rushed note by now. I hope this one is clearer, though my mind is so confused that I cannot guarantee coherence. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know how to begin, but I must share some urgent, distressing news. While a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be imprudent, we now desperately hope it has occurred, as there is grave reason to fear they never headed for Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having departed Brighton shortly after the express. Although Lydia’s brief note to Mrs. Forster suggested they were bound for Gretna Green, Denny let slip his belief that Wickham never intended to go there or marry her at all. The Colonel, immediately alarmed, set out to track them. He followed their path as far as Clapham, where they abandoned their carriage for a hackney-coach. From there, they were only seen continuing toward London. I am at a loss. After searching that area, the Colonel traveled to Hertfordshire, checking every turnpike and inn in Barnet and Hatfield, yet found no trace of them. He came to Longbourn with great kindness to share his fears. I feel deeply for him and Mrs. Forster, though they are blameless. Our distress is profound. While my parents fear the worst, I cannot think so poorly of him. Perhaps they chose a private London wedding over their original plan. Even if he could harbor such designs against a girl of Lydia’s standing—which seems unlikely—could she truly be so lost? It is impossible. Still, I am saddened that the Colonel does not share my optimism; he shook his head when I voiced my hopes, admitting he feared Wickham was not a man to be trusted."
}

