parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "88046dd6382d1df43e3ba415",
  "rewritten_text": "Amidst these shouts, many women, driven to a mindless rage, spun around, striking and clawing at their own companions until they collapsed in a fevered faint, rescued from being crushed only by their own men. Yet, not an instant was wasted. Foulon was at the Hotel de Ville and might escape. Not if Saint Antoine remembered his own torments and injustices. Armed citizens poured from the district so rapidly, pulling even the most reluctant along, that within fifteen minutes, only a few elderly women and crying children remained in Saint Antoine. They were already swarming the Hall of Examination where the wicked old man was held, spilling into the nearby streets. The Defarges, The Vengeance, and Jacques Three stood in the front of the throng, near the prisoner. Look, Madame shouted, gesturing with her blade. See the old scoundrel tied up. Tying grass to his back was a clever touch. Let him eat it now! She tucked her knife away and applauded as if watching a performance. Those behind her relayed her delight to the back, and soon the surrounding streets echoed with applause. Throughout the long, wordy proceedings, Madame Defarge’s impatience was mirrored by the crowd, aided by men perched on the building’s exterior who acted as messengers between her and the masses. Finally, the sun cast a beam of light upon the prisoner’s head. This perceived mercy was intolerable; the crowd’s restraint vanished, and Saint Antoine seized him. The news spread instantly. Defarge vaulted over a railing, grabbing the wretch, while Madame Defarge twisted her hand into his ropes. Before the others could reach him or the men at the windows could descend, the cry rose across the city: Bring him out!"
}

