parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8a6002df831adb8bff403eca",
  "rewritten_text": "Sandy and I resumed our journey early the following morning. It felt wonderful to breathe in the fresh, dew-kissed, forest-scented air again after enduring two days of the physical and moral decay within that wretched castle. Of course, the environment suited Sandy well enough, as she was accustomed to such aristocratic settings. The poor woman had been quiet for some time, and I anticipated the inevitable chatter. She had supported me admirably in the castle with absurd tales that proved more effective than any logic, so I felt she had earned the right to speak. I did not begrudge her when she began. \"Now we turn to Sir Marhaus, who rode south with the thirty-year-old damsel.\" \"Are you attempting to pick up the trail of the cowboys again, Sandy?\" \"Indeed, my lord.\" \"Proceed, then. I will try not to interrupt. Start fresh, tell the whole story, and I shall light my pipe and listen closely.\" \"Now we turn to Sir Marhaus, who rode south with the thirty-year-old damsel. They entered a dense forest, were overtaken by night, and eventually reached a courtyard where the Duke of South Marches lived, seeking shelter. In the morning, the Duke challenged Sir Marhaus to battle. Sir Marhaus armed himself, attended mass, ate, and mounted his horse in the courtyard. The Duke and his six sons were already waiting, each holding a spear. They charged; the Duke and two sons shattered their spears against Sir Marhaus, who did not strike back. The remaining four sons attacked in pairs, breaking their spears as well, while Sir Marhaus still refrained from retaliating. Finally, Sir Marhaus charged the Duke and struck him with such force that both man and horse were thrown to the ground.\""
}

