  "rewritten_text": "When we returned to the raft and tallied our earnings, the king had collected eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He had also swiped a three-gallon jug of whiskey from beneath a wagon while walking through the woods. The king remarked that, all things considered, this surpassed any day he had spent in missionary work. He insisted that heathens simply could not compete with pirates when it came to exploiting a camp meeting. The duke had felt proud of his own efforts until the king revealed his haul. He had printed two horse-sale flyers for local farmers, earning four dollars. He also secured ten dollars in newspaper advertisements, offering a discount to four dollars if paid upfront, which the clients accepted. Although the paper cost two dollars annually, he sold three subscriptions for fifty cents each by demanding cash instead of the usual payment in firewood and onions, claiming he had just purchased the business. He also composed a sentimental three-verse poem titled, \"Yes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart,\" and set it for publication for free. In total, he brought in nine dollars and fifty cents, which he considered a fair day's work. He then showed us a flyer he had printed for our own use, free of charge. It featured an illustration of a runaway slave carrying a bundle on a stick, with a two-hundred-dollar reward listed below. The text described Jim perfectly, claiming he had escaped from a plantation near New Orleans and was heading north. The duke explained that we could now travel during the day. If approached, we could tie Jim up, hide him in the shelter, and present the flyer to claim we had captured him upriver and were transporting him to collect the reward."
}

