  "request_id": "08d1a65adb755f03721f852c",
  "rewritten_text": "He was short but stood remarkably straight, and his voice was the most pleasant I had ever heard. He began his lecture by summarizing the history of chemistry and the advancements made by various scholars, speaking with passion about the most notable discoverers. He then gave a brief overview of the current state of the field and explained its basic terminology. After performing a few preliminary experiments, he concluded with a tribute to modern chemistry that I will never forget: The ancient teachers of this science promised the impossible and achieved nothing. Modern masters promise very little; they know metals cannot be transmuted and the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these scientists, whose hands seem meant only for dirt and whose eyes for microscopes or crucibles, have truly performed miracles. They delve into nature’s secrets, revealing how she works in her hidden places. They reach into the heavens; they have discovered how blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new and nearly limitless powers; they can command lightning, mimic earthquakes, and even replicate the invisible world with its own shadows. These were the professor’s words—or rather, the words of fate—spoken to destroy me. As he continued, I felt as if my soul were wrestling with a tangible enemy. One by one, the various components of my being were triggered; chord after chord was struck, and soon my mind was consumed by a single thought, a single purpose. So much has been done, my soul cried out—I will achieve far more. Following the path already marked, I will pioneer a new way, explore unknown powers, and reveal the deepest mysteries of creation to the world. I did not close my eyes that night. My inner self was in a state of rebellion and turmoil; I felt that order would eventually emerge, but I lacked the power to create it. Gradually, after dawn, sleep arrived. I awoke, and my thoughts from the night before felt like a dream. Only a resolution remained to return to my former studies and dedicate myself to a science for which I believed I had a natural talent. That same day, I visited M. Waldman. His private manner was even more gentle and engaging than his public one, for the dignity he held during his lecture was replaced in his home by the greatest warmth and kindness."
}

