parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "334a2ea2233cc697f8d8d9c1",
  "rewritten_text": "“Never!” the girl replied. “Tell me why?” “For one reason,” she said firmly, “the lady knows and will support me; I have her promise. Also, I have lived a bad life, just like the others I have known. I will not betray them, even though they are criminals, because they never betrayed me.” “Then,” the gentleman said quickly, “give me Monks, and let me handle him.” “What if he turns against the others?” she asked. “If the truth is forced from him, the matter will end there. Oliver’s history contains painful details that should not be made public. If we get the truth, the others will go free.” “And if not?” she asked. “Then,” the gentleman continued, “Fagin will not be prosecuted without your consent. I believe I could convince you to agree if it came to that.” “Do I have the lady’s promise?” she asked. “You do,” Rose replied. “My sincere pledge.” “Monks will never know how you found out?” the girl asked after a pause. “Never,” the gentleman said. “He will never even guess.” “I have been a liar since I was a child,” she said, “but I will trust your word.” After they assured her she was safe, she spoke in a low voice, describing the tavern she had left that night. As she paused, the gentleman took quick notes. Once she explained the location, how to watch it without being seen, and when Monks usually visited, she paused to recall his appearance. “He is tall and well-built, but not heavy,” she said. “He walks cautiously, constantly looking over his shoulder.”"
}

