parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f81a9d4ab22b325c2cdf830f",
  "rewritten_text": "Saturday at noon, I visited the well to observe. Merlin was still burning incense, waving his arms, and muttering nonsense, though he looked discouraged, having failed to produce even a drop of water. I asked him how things were progressing. He replied that he was attempting the most powerful Eastern enchantment, and if that failed, nothing would work, so I should remain silent. He produced a massive cloud of smoke that drifted toward the hermits' dens, followed by frantic gestures and chanting. After twenty minutes, he collapsed, exhausted. The abbot, a crowd of monks, nuns, pilgrims, and orphans arrived, drawn by the spectacle. When the abbot asked for results, Merlin claimed he had used the strongest magic possible. He declared that a spirit whose name cannot be spoken had cursed the well, and since no mortal could uncover the secret of that spell, the water was gone forever. He asked to be excused. The abbot, clearly distressed, turned to me and asked if it were true. I told him part of it was. When he pressed me, I confirmed that the spirit had indeed cursed the well. The abbot cried out in despair, fearing they were ruined. I suggested it was only a possibility. When he asked if that meant Merlin was wrong about the spell being unbreakable, I explained that while Merlin was mistaken, there remained a small, slim chance of success under the right conditions."
}

