  "request_id": "8f1f34af5372574dfb52ccfe",
  "rewritten_text": "Enough of this. I shall commence my narrative; it is my final duty, and I trust I possess the vigor to complete it. I recount no transgressions; my errors are easily forgiven, as they stemmed from poor judgment rather than malice. I suspect few would claim they could have escaped the calamities that have claimed me through better wisdom or conduct. My destiny was dictated by a grim, inescapable necessity. It demanded strength beyond my own—perhaps beyond any human capacity—to shatter the iron bonds that dragged me from a life of pure joy and virtue into this misery, which now nears its conclusion in death. But I digress; my story remains unshared. I will pause briefly, dry my weary eyes, and attempt to trade this dull, heavy sorrow for the sharper feelings of days gone by. I was born in England. My father was a nobleman who lost his own father young, raised by a doting mother who granted him every indulgence. He attended Eton and university, granted unrestricted access to wealth from childhood, gaining the independence such advantages typically foster in a public school boy. These conditions allowed his passions to take deep root, blossoming into either virtues or vices. Because he was always permitted to act on his own impulses, his character developed early and distinctly, revealing to the keen observer the seeds of both his noble traits and his eventual downfall. His reckless spending, which saw him waste fortunes on fleeting whims he grandly labeled as passions, often manifested as boundless generosity. Yet, while he busied himself with the needs of others, he never denied his own desires. He gave away money he did not miss, time he did not value, and affection he was pleased to exercise. I cannot say if he would have acted selfishly had his interests clashed with another's, for that test never arose. He was raised in prosperity, surrounded by every benefit, with everyone eager to please him."
}

