parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ed78d050853b1f743a7749ef",
  "rewritten_text": "Are you starving, Sir Simon? I have a sandwich in my case if you would like it. No, thank you, I do not eat, but you are much kinder than your rude, dishonest family. Stop, Virginia cried, stamping her foot. You are the rude one. You stole my paints to touch up that silly bloodstain in the library. You took my reds, my emerald green, and my yellow, leaving me only with indigo and white. I could only paint depressing moonlight scenes. I never told on you, even though emerald green blood is ridiculous. The ghost replied meekly, what else could I do? Real blood is hard to find, and since your brother started this with his detergent, I felt justified in taking your paints. As for the color, that is a matter of taste. The Cantervilles have blue blood, though I know you Americans do not care for such things. You know nothing about it, Virginia countered. You should move to America to improve your mind. My father would gladly pay for your trip, and there would be no trouble at customs. You would be a huge success in New York. Many people there would pay a fortune to have a family ghost. I do not think I would like America, the ghost said. I suppose because we have no ruins or history, Virginia remarked sarcastically. No ruins? The ghost retorted. You have your navy and your manners. Good evening, Virginia said. I am going to ask my father to give the twins an extra week of holiday. Please do not go, he pleaded. I am lonely and unhappy. I want to sleep, but I cannot. That is absurd, she replied. You simply need to go to bed and blow out the candle."
}

