parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7baec7d05f1a69ae356ed5fb",
  "rewritten_text": "With their agreement settled, the contract was sealed with another cup of peppermint tea, which helped calm the lady’s nerves. As she drank, she informed Mr. Bumble that the old woman had passed away. Very good, he said, sipping his drink. I will stop by Sowerberry’s on my way home and tell him to collect the body tomorrow. Was that what upset you, my dear? It was nothing specific, she replied evasively. It must have been something, Mr. Bumble insisted. Won’t you tell your own B.? Not now, she said. One of these days, after we are married. After we are married! Mr. Bumble exclaimed. It wasn’t any insolence from those male paupers, was it? No, no, love, she quickly interrupted. If I thought any of them had dared to look at your lovely face with vulgar eyes, he continued. They wouldn’t have dared, she replied. They had better not, Mr. Bumble said, clenching his fist. Let any man, parishioner or otherwise, try it, and I promise he won’t do it a second time. Though his words were harsh, his aggressive gestures convinced the lady of his devotion, and she admiringly called him a dove. The dove then turned up his collar, donned his hat, and after an affectionate embrace, stepped back into the cold night. He paused briefly in the men’s ward to berate the paupers, confirming his ability to manage the workhouse with necessary severity. Satisfied, Mr. Bumble left with a light heart and visions of his future promotion, which occupied his thoughts until he reached the undertaker’s shop. Since Mr. and Mrs. Sowerberry were out and Noah Claypole was too lazy to close the shop, the doors remained open despite the late hour. Mr. Bumble tapped his cane on the counter, but receiving no response, he noticed a light in the back parlor. He peeked inside and was quite surprised by what he saw."
}

