parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2a9d53d3156ad3fa89e6143a",
  "rewritten_text": "Although Perdita was never named, I felt certain her influence drove his indecision. Who better deserved the devotion of this self-important leader than my noble sister, a woman with the regal bearing of a queen? She loved him in return, even as his ambition warred with his feelings and her own disappointment dampened her ardor. We arrived at the House that evening. Raymond appeared lighthearted and indifferent, despite knowing his future would be determined by the coming debate. A deafening roar, like a massive swarm of bees, greeted us in the coffee-room. Groups of politicians gathered, their faces tense and their voices hushed or boisterous. The aristocratic faction, England’s wealthiest and most powerful men, seemed surprisingly calm, as the matter would be settled without their direct involvement. Near the hearth stood Ryland and his allies. Ryland, a man of humble origins who had amassed a fortune from his father’s manufacturing business, had long championed the popular reforms that followed the king’s abdication and the merger of the legislative houses. Initially, he had welcomed Lord Raymond’s schemes, as they siphoned support from his rivals, but the movement had grown excessive. The lesser nobility saw the potential return of monarchy as a path to reclaiming their vanished status. The dormant spirit of royalty was awakening; many citizens, like willing captives, seemed eager to submit to a crown. While some steadfast individuals remained, the term republic had lost its appeal to the masses, and many longed for the glitter of monarchy. Ryland, finally stirred to act, declared that he had tolerated this faction long enough and vowed to dismantle their influence entirely. As Raymond stepped into the room, his supporters cheered, crowding around him to tally their votes and discuss the new members who had yet to commit."
}

