parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cd7acb50e2ea7a315aef554e",
  "rewritten_text": "Elizabeth felt despondent and melancholy, finding no joy in her usual tasks. She believed any happiness was a betrayal of the deceased, feeling that perpetual grief was the only proper homage to such ruined innocence. She was no longer the joyful girl who once strolled with me by the lake, speaking with delight of our future. The first of those life-altering sorrows meant to detach us from the world had arrived, extinguishing her brightest smiles. When I consider the tragic end of Justine Moritz, she remarked, the world looks entirely different to me. Previously, reports of vice and injustice seemed like distant legends or abstract concepts, but now that misery has struck close to home, humanity appears like monsters craving blood. I am likely being unfair, as everyone thought her guilty; had she truly committed such a crime, she would have been the most wicked of beings. To kill the child of her benefactor for mere jewels, a boy she raised and loved as her own, would be monstrous. While I could never support an execution, I would have deemed such a person unfit for society. Yet she was innocent. I feel it, and your agreement confirms it. Victor, when lies appear so much like truth, who can be certain of happiness? I feel as though I am walking along a cliff edge, with crowds pushing me toward the void. William and Justine were murdered, yet the killer remains free and perhaps even honored. Even if I were to face the scaffold for those crimes, I would never trade places with such a wretch. I heard these words in absolute agony, knowing that while I did not commit the act, I was effectively the murderer. Elizabeth noticed my distress and, gently taking my hand, urged me to find calm, noting that while she was deeply affected, she was not as miserable as I appeared."
}

