parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1f11c0a9e5800020f5589f93",
  "rewritten_text": "It took some time before I could focus on managing the balloon. When I finally checked it, I was relieved to find it undamaged. My tools were secure, and I had not lost any ballast or supplies, as I had packed them carefully enough to prevent such a loss. Checking my watch, I saw it was six o’clock. I was still climbing rapidly, and my barometer showed an altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Below me, a small, dark, oblong object floated in the ocean, looking much like a domino. Using my telescope, I identified it as a British ninety-four-gun ship, struggling against the waves and heading west-southwest. Beyond that ship, there was nothing but the sea, the sky, and the sun, which had risen long ago. It is time I explain to your Excellencies the purpose of my dangerous journey. You should know that financial ruin in Rotterdam had driven me to consider suicide. I did not hate life itself, but I could no longer endure the misery of my situation. While I wanted to live, I was exhausted by my circumstances, and a book I found at a stall sparked an idea. I decided to leave the world behind while still remaining alive. To put it simply, I resolved to travel to the moon. So that you do not think me entirely mad, I will explain why I believed this difficult and dangerous feat was possible for a bold spirit. First, I considered the moon’s distance from Earth. The average distance between the centers of the two bodies is about 237,000 miles. However, because the moon’s orbit is elliptical and the Earth sits at one focus, I realized that if I could meet the moon at its closest point, or perigee, that distance would be significantly reduced."
}

