parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c5756f421767bc90aea69708",
  "rewritten_text": "The painter appeared at the studio door, gesturing for them to enter. They smiled at one another. He called out for them to hurry, noting the perfect light and inviting them to bring their drinks. They walked down the path together as butterflies flew by and a thrush sang in the pear tree. Lord Henry asked if Dorian was glad they had met. Dorian replied that he was, though he wondered if he always would be. Lord Henry shuddered at the word always, calling it a meaningless term used by women to ruin romance. He claimed the only difference between a whim and a lifelong passion is that the whim lasts a bit longer. Dorian touched Lord Henry’s arm and suggested their friendship be a whim, then returned to his pose. Lord Henry watched from a wicker chair as the only sound was the brush against the canvas. Dust motes danced in the light, and the scent of roses filled the room. After fifteen minutes, Hallward stopped, studied his subject and the painting, and frowned. He declared it finished and signed his name in red in the corner. Lord Henry examined the portrait, praising it as a masterpiece and a perfect likeness. He invited Dorian to look at himself. Dorian seemed to wake from a dream and stepped down. The painter thanked him for sitting so well. Lord Henry claimed credit for the success, but Dorian did not respond, walking slowly toward the painting to see it for the first time."
}

