  "rewritten_text": "He said nothing about the matter until their guest left for the night. Once they were alone, he turned to his daughter and said, \"Jane, congratulations. You will be a very happy woman.\" Jane immediately went to him, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness. \"You are a good girl,\" he replied, \"and it gives me great pleasure to think you will be so well settled. I have no doubt you will do very well together. Your temperaments are quite similar. You are both so accommodating that you will never decide on anything, so easygoing that every servant will cheat you, and so generous that you will always spend more than you earn.\" \"I hope not,\" Jane said. \"Financial recklessness would be inexcusable for me.\" \"Spend more than they earn? My dear Mr. Bennet,\" his wife exclaimed, \"what are you talking about? He has four or five thousand a year, if not more.\" Turning to her daughter, she added, \"Oh, my dear Jane, I am so happy! I am sure I won't sleep a wink tonight. I knew this would happen. I always said it would. I knew you couldn't be that beautiful for nothing! I remember thinking the moment I saw him arrive in Hertfordshire last year that you two would end up together. He is the handsomest young man I have ever seen!\" Wickham and Lydia were forgotten. Jane was undeniably her favorite child, and for the moment, she cared for no one else. Her younger sisters soon began asking her for favors she might be able to grant in the future. Mary requested access to the library at Netherfield, and Kitty pleaded for a few balls there every winter. From then on, Bingley was a daily visitor at Longbourn, arriving before breakfast and staying until after supper, unless some annoying neighbor forced him to accept a dinner invitation. Elizabeth had little time to talk to her sister, as Jane had no attention for anyone else while he was present. However, she proved useful to both during their brief separations. When Jane was away, Bingley would talk to Elizabeth about her, and when he left, Jane would seek the same comfort. \"He has made me so happy,\" Jane said one evening, \"by telling me he had no idea I was in town last spring!\""
}

