parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b3bec2ae108e911da33416fd",
  "rewritten_text": "We declared ourselves free men so loudly and earnestly that we drew the attention of a grandstanding orator and his patriotic followers, who surrounded us with a menacing air. The orator declared, \"If you are truly free, you have nothing to fear; Britain's God-given liberties protect you. Show us your proof.\" When I asked what proof he meant, he insisted we prove our status as free men. I suddenly remembered my situation and stayed silent, but the king exploded, \"You are insane. It would be more reasonable for this thief to prove we are not free.\" He understood the law only in theory, not in practice; laws become vivid only when they apply to you personally. The crowd looked disappointed and began to lose interest. The orator spoke again, this time with cold, professional detachment: \"If you do not know the laws of this land, it is time you learned. You are strangers here. You might be free, but you might also be slaves. The law is clear: it does not require the claimant to prove you are slaves; it requires you to prove you are not.\" I pleaded, \"Sir, just give us time to send word to Astolat or the Valley of Holiness.\" He dismissed me, claiming such requests were unreasonable and would inconvenience our master. \"Master, you idiot!\" the king roared. \"I have no master, I am the m--\" I cut him off just in time. We were in enough trouble; we did not need them thinking we were insane. There is no point in detailing the rest. The earl had us auctioned off. This same wretched law existed in the American South thirteen centuries later, where countless free men were sold into lifelong slavery without me giving it much thought. Yet, once the law and the auction block became my personal reality, what had seemed merely improper became truly hellish. That is simply how we are built. We were sold like livestock. In a bustling market, we might have fetched a decent price, but this town was so stagnant that we sold for an amount that still shames me to recall."
}

