parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0cae50d772a7ff7f5f50d307",
  "rewritten_text": "“Joseph! Joseph!” He shook his head and tapped his temple, signaling his disappointment in Joseph. “My dear young friend,” Mr. Pumblechook said, “you must be starving. Sit down. I have brought chicken, tongue, and other delicacies from the Boar that I hope you will enjoy. But am I truly looking at the boy I once played with during his happy childhood? May I—may I?” This meant he wanted to shake my hand. I agreed, and he shook it fervently before sitting down again. “Here is wine,” he continued. “Let us drink to Fortune, and may she always choose her favorites as wisely as she has today! And yet, I cannot look at you—or drink to you—without again asking, may I—may I?” I gave him permission, and he shook my hand again, drained his glass, and turned it upside down. I followed suit, and the wine went straight to my head. Mr. Pumblechook served me the best portions of chicken and tongue, neglecting his own plate. “Ah, poultry! You never imagined, as a fledgling, that you would provide refreshment under this humble roof for someone who—call it a weakness if you like—but may I? May I?” He began doing it without waiting for my consent. I do not know how he avoided cutting himself with my knife. “And your sister,” he added after eating, “who had the honor of raising you by hand! It is sad to think she is no longer capable of appreciating that honor. May—” I saw him preparing to reach for me again and stopped him. “Let us drink to her health,” I said. “Ah!” Mr. Pumblechook cried, leaning back in admiration, “that is how you recognize them, sir! That is how you know the noble-minded, sir!”"
}

