parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d99fad4db62be6dd602de716",
  "rewritten_text": "The following day was set for the confrontation to ensure our honor remained intact. Drummle arrived with a polite note from Estella confirming she had danced with him several times. I had no choice but to apologize for my previous outburst and retract my earlier claims. Drummle and I sat there glaring at each other for an hour while the rest of the group argued, eventually declaring that our relationship had improved significantly. I recount this casually, but it was deeply painful. It hurt to see Estella show any interest in such a clumsy, sullen, and inferior man. I believe my love for her was so selfless that I could not bear to see her lower herself to such a person. While I would have been miserable regardless of whom she chose, a more worthy man would have caused me less distress. I soon discovered that Drummle was pursuing her and she was allowing it. He followed her constantly, and we crossed paths daily. He persisted in his dull way, while Estella kept him on a string, alternating between encouragement and disdain, sometimes flattering him and other times acting as if she barely knew him. The Spider, as Mr. Jaggers called him, had the patience of a predator. His arrogant confidence in his wealth and family status served him well, often acting as a substitute for true ambition. By doggedly watching Estella, he outlasted more impressive suitors, ready to strike at the perfect moment. At an assembly ball in Richmond where Estella outshone everyone, Drummle hovered around her with her apparent permission. I decided I had to speak to her about him. I found my chance when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home, sitting alone among the flowers."
}

