parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dcb9dc30d0e14432cae4e9ad",
  "rewritten_text": "She would reside among those who cherished her and possessed greater wisdom than she; sufficiently secluded for security and busy enough to remain content. She would avoid temptation, neither seeking it nor being sought by it. She would lead a respectable, happy life, and Emma conceded that she was the most fortunate soul alive to have inspired such steadfast devotion in such a man—or, if not the absolute luckiest, second only to herself. Harriet, increasingly occupied by her commitments to the Martins, visited Hartfield less frequently, which was for the best. Their intimacy was destined to fade, transitioning into a more moderate goodwill, a shift that fortunately seemed to be occurring naturally and by degrees. By late September, Emma accompanied Harriet to the altar, witnessing her union with Robert Martin with a contentment that no recollection of Mr. Elton could diminish. Indeed, she barely noticed Mr. Elton, viewing him only as the minister whose benediction might soon be bestowed upon her own marriage. Robert Martin and Harriet Smith, though the last to become engaged, were the first to wed. Jane Fairfax had departed Highbury, returning to the comforts of the Campbells, while the Churchills remained in town, awaiting November. Emma and Mr. Knightley had tentatively settled on the intervening month for their own nuptials, hoping to marry while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield so they might enjoy a two-week seaside excursion. Their friends fully supported the plan, but Mr. Woodhouse presented a challenge; he had always spoken of their union as a remote possibility. When first approached, his distress was so profound that they nearly despaired. A subsequent mention caused less agony, as he began to accept the inevitability of the event—a hopeful sign of resignation. Yet he remained unhappy, and his visible suffering sapped his daughter’s resolve. Unable to bear his pain or the thought that he felt abandoned, Emma hesitated, despite the reassurances of both Mr. Knightleys that his misery would prove fleeting once the wedding had passed."
}

