parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c80abae0092cdfcf790a4562",
  "rewritten_text": "My father’s surname was Pirrip and my first name was Philip, but as a toddler I could only manage the name Pip. So, I called myself Pip, and that is how everyone knew me. I know my father’s name from his tombstone and my sister, Mrs. Joe Gargery, who married the local blacksmith. Since I never met my parents or saw any portraits of them—as they lived long before photography—my early impressions of them came entirely from their graves. The blocky lettering on my father’s stone made me imagine him as a stout, dark man with curly hair. From the inscription regarding my mother, Georgiana, I childishly concluded she was sickly and freckled. Beside their grave stood five small stones for my brothers who died in infancy. I grew up believing they had been born lying on their backs with their hands in their pockets, never having taken them out during their short lives. We lived in the marshlands near the river, about twenty miles from the sea. My first clear realization of the world occurred on a raw, late afternoon. That was when I understood that the nettle-choked field was a graveyard, that my parents and brothers were buried there, and that the dark, flat landscape beyond was the marshes. I saw the river as a leaden line and the distant, howling wind as the sea. I realized the small, shivering boy crying in fear was me. Suddenly, a terrifying voice shouted, \"Quiet!\" A man jumped up from behind the church porch. \"Stay still, you little devil, or I’ll cut your throat!\" He was a frightening figure dressed in coarse grey, with a heavy iron shackle locked around his leg."
}

