parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e0ad5441a7854a78006ead09",
  "rewritten_text": "In the pale moonlight, he saw an old man with a terrifying appearance. His eyes glowed like burning coals, his long grey hair hung in tangled locks, and his old-fashioned clothes were dirty and torn. Heavy, rusted chains hung from his wrists and ankles. Mr. Otis said, My dear sir, I must insist that you oil those chains. I have brought you a bottle of Tammany Rising Sun Lubricator for that purpose. It is said to work perfectly after one use, and there are several recommendations on the label from prominent local ministers. I will leave it here by your candles and can provide more if you need it. The American Minister placed the bottle on a marble table, closed his door, and went to bed. For a moment, the Canterville ghost stood still in shock. Then, he smashed the bottle against the floor and fled down the hallway, groaning and glowing with a ghostly green light. Just as he reached the top of the oak staircase, a door flew open, two children in white robes appeared, and a large pillow flew past his head. Realizing he had no time to waste, he escaped by slipping through the wall, leaving the house quiet. Reaching a secret room in the left wing, he leaned against a moonbeam to catch his breath and reflect on his situation. In three hundred years of a successful career, he had never been so insulted. He remembered the Duchess he had frightened into a fit, the four housemaids who had hysterical breakdowns when he grinned at them, the local priest whose candle he had blown out, and Madame de Tremouillac, who had been bedridden for six weeks after seeing a skeleton reading her diary by the fire."
}

