  "rewritten_text": "Each witness compares the sound not to a language they know, but to one they do not. The Frenchman claims it was Spanish, yet admits he would have known the words had he spoken the tongue. The Dutchman insists it was French, though he required an interpreter to testify. The Englishman identifies it as German, despite his ignorance of that language. The Spaniard is certain it was English, relying solely on intonation since he lacks knowledge of the speech. The Italian guesses Russian, having never spoken to a Russian native. A second Frenchman contradicts the first, asserting it was Italian, yet he too relies on intonation, being unfamiliar with that language. How peculiar that voice must have been to baffle citizens from five European nations! You might suggest an Asian or African origin, though such people are rare in Paris. Regardless, consider three facts: one witness calls it harsh rather than shrill, two describe it as quick and uneven, and no one could distinguish any recognizable words. Dupin continued, stating that while he was unsure of my impression, the deductions drawn from these conflicting accounts regarding the voices were enough to form a suspicion that would guide his investigation. He emphasized that these were the only logical conclusions, though he would not yet reveal the nature of his suspicion. He only wanted me to remember that it gave a clear direction to his search of the room. Let us imagine ourselves in that chamber. What should we look for first? The escape route used by the killers. It is safe to say that neither of us believes in the supernatural."
}

