parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "60898ae0ee7efe3a191587b8",
  "rewritten_text": "Three or four months passed, and winter had fully arrived. I had attended school regularly, learning to read, write, and spell a little, though I could never master the multiplication tables past six times seven. I never cared much for mathematics anyway. Initially, I despised school, but eventually, I grew accustomed to it. When I felt overwhelmed, I would skip class, and the subsequent punishment actually improved my mood. Over time, school became easier, and I grew used to the widow’s habits, which no longer bothered me as much. While living indoors and sleeping in a bed felt restrictive, I occasionally slipped out to sleep in the woods before the weather turned cold, which provided some relief. I still preferred my old life, but I was beginning to appreciate the new one. The widow told me I was progressing steadily and that she was proud of me. One morning at breakfast, I accidentally knocked over the salt cellar. I reached to throw some over my left shoulder to ward off bad luck, but Miss Watson stopped me, scolding me for the mess. Although the widow defended me, I knew the bad luck was inevitable. I left the house feeling anxious, wondering what misfortune awaited me. There was no way to prevent this specific curse, so I walked along, feeling discouraged and vigilant. I went to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the high board fence. A fresh inch of snow covered the ground, and I noticed footprints. Someone had come from the quarry, lingered by the stile, and then walked around the fence. It was strange that they had not entered after waiting there so long. I could not understand it. It was quite mysterious, so I knelt down to examine the tracks more closely."
}

