parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5159225bbfdf978ae0d6457a",
  "rewritten_text": "It was Lady Windermere’s final party before Easter, and Bentinck House was packed. Six Cabinet Ministers arrived wearing their official honors, fashionable women displayed their finest gowns, and Princess Sophia of Carlsrühe stood in the picture gallery. She was a heavy-set woman with small black eyes and stunning emeralds, speaking loud, broken French and laughing at everything. The crowd was a strange mix. Wealthy noblewomen spoke with radical politicians, famous preachers mingled with skeptics, bishops followed a lead singer, and artists filled the rooms. It was a successful night, and the Princess stayed until nearly midnight. Once she left, Lady Windermere went to the gallery, where a political economist was lecturing a frustrated Hungarian musician on music theory, and she began speaking with the Duchess of Paisley. Lady Windermere looked beautiful, with her ivory skin, blue eyes, and thick golden hair. Her hair was a rich, deep gold that framed her face like a saint’s, yet held the charm of a sinner. She was a complex person. Early on, she learned that nothing appears as innocent as a minor scandal, and through various harmless adventures, she had earned a reputation that allowed her to do as she pleased. She had been married three times, but because she kept the same lover, society stopped gossiping about her. At forty, she remained childless and possessed a relentless desire for pleasure that kept her looking young. Suddenly, she scanned the room and asked in her clear voice, Where is my palm reader? Your what, Gladys? the Duchess asked, startled. My palm reader, Duchess; I cannot live without him right now. Dear Gladys, you are always so unique, the Duchess replied, trying to figure out what a palm reader was while hoping it was not a foot doctor. He examines my hand twice a week, Lady Windermere continued, and he is fascinating."
}

