  "rewritten_text": "She was found dead in her bed the next morning, appearing to have passed away peacefully. Sir Mathew Reid was summoned immediately, but there was nothing to be done, and her funeral was scheduled for the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. She had written her will just days earlier, leaving Lord Arthur her Curzon Street house and all its contents, except for her miniature collection, which went to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which was left to Sybil Merton. The estate was modest, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, urged Lord Arthur to return quickly to settle numerous unpaid bills, as Lady Clementina had never managed her finances well. Lord Arthur was moved by her kindness and felt Mr. Podgers bore a heavy responsibility for the situation. Still, his love for Sybil outweighed everything else, and the knowledge that he had fulfilled his duty brought him peace. He felt truly happy upon arriving at Charing Cross. The Mertons welcomed him warmly. Sybil made him promise that nothing would ever separate them again, and they set their wedding date for June 7th. Life felt bright and beautiful once more, and his former joy returned. One day, while he and Sybil were clearing out the Curzon Street house with the solicitor—burning old letters and sorting through drawers—Sybil suddenly cried out in delight. Lord Arthur looked up from his work and smiled, asking what she had found. She held up a charming silver candy box, asking if she could have it since amethysts would not suit her for years. It was the very box that had contained the poison. Lord Arthur started, his face flushing. He had nearly forgotten his actions, and it felt strange that Sybil, the reason for his terrible ordeal, was the one to remind him. He told her she could have it, noting he had given it to Lady Clementina himself. When she asked if she could also have the candy inside, noting she had no idea Lady Clementina enjoyed sweets, Lord Arthur turned deathly pale as a horrifying thought struck him."
}

