  "rewritten_text": "She was correct regarding the rats. Occasionally, one would poke its snout from a corner crevice. She explained that she kept objects nearby to throw at them while alone, or they would pester her incessantly. She displayed a knotted bar of lead, claiming she was usually an accurate thrower, though a recent arm injury made her uncertain of her aim. She waited for an opportunity and fired at a rat, missing significantly and crying out in pain. She then instructed me to target the next one. I was anxious to depart before the man returned, but I concealed my intent. I took the lead, and when the next rat appeared, I threw it; had the creature remained stationary, it would have been quite injured. She praised my effort, predicting I would catch the next one. She retrieved the lead and brought a skein of yarn, asking for my assistance. I held my hands up for the yarn, and she resumed discussing her domestic affairs. Suddenly, she interrupted herself to warn me to watch for rats and keep the lead in my lap. She dropped the weight, and I clamped my legs around it while she continued speaking. Moments later, she removed the yarn, stared directly at me, and asked for my true name. I stammered, confused. She pressed for my identity, asking if I were Bill, Tom, or Bob. I trembled, unsure how to respond. I begged her not to mock a poor girl, offering to leave if I were a burden. She insisted I remain, promising she would not harm or betray me. She urged me to trust her with my secret, suggesting I was merely a runaway apprentice. She assured me there was no shame in fleeing poor treatment and that she and her husband would offer their support."
}

