  "rewritten_text": "He made it clear that Frank admired her immensely, finding her both beautiful and charming. Given his many positive qualities, she decided not to judge him too severely, agreeing with Mrs. Weston that all young people have their quirks. However, one person in Surrey was not so forgiving. While most in Donwell and Highbury were lenient toward such a handsome, polite young man, Mr. Knightley remained unmoved by his charm. When told the news at Hartfield, he stayed silent at first, but Emma soon heard him mutter to himself while reading the paper, \"Hum! Just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.\" She considered taking offense, but realized he was merely venting his own frustration rather than trying to provoke her, so she let it go. Although the Westons brought mixed news, their visit was timely. Emma needed advice on a specific matter, and their input proved exactly what she required. The Coles, a friendly and unpretentious family, had recently grown quite wealthy through trade. Having expanded their home and staff, they now lived in a style second only to the Hartfield estate. Their new dining room signaled their intent to host formal dinners, and they had already entertained some of the local bachelors. Emma doubted they would dare invite the area's elite families, such as those from Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls. She was determined to refuse any such invitation, though she regretted that her father’s predictable habits would make her rejection seem less significant than she intended."
}

