  "rewritten_text": "The instruments indicated an altitude of 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, allowing me to view about one three-hundred-twentieth of the Earth's total surface. By nine o'clock, I had lost sight of land to the east, noting the balloon was drifting rapidly north-northwest. The ocean's curvature was clearly visible, though often obscured by drifting clouds. I noticed that even the lightest vapors never rose beyond ten miles above sea level. At nine-thirty, I tested the air by dropping a handful of feathers through the valve. Instead of floating, they fell straight down like bullets, disappearing in seconds. I was initially baffled, unable to believe my ascent had accelerated so drastically. I soon realized the atmosphere was too thin to support the feathers, and their rapid descent combined with my own upward velocity created the illusion. By ten o'clock, I had little to do. The flight proceeded smoothly, and I felt the balloon rising faster every moment, though I lacked the means to measure it. I felt no discomfort and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam, alternating between checking my equipment and refreshing the cabin's air. I performed this air renewal every forty minutes, primarily for my health. Meanwhile, I let my mind wander to the moon. Unshackled, my imagination roamed through a dreamlike landscape. I envisioned ancient forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls crashing into bottomless abysses. Then, I drifted into silent, sunlit solitudes where no wind blew, and vast fields of poppies and lilies stretched out, forever still and motionless."
