parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "14cbfcdf84fcb54ea2ce3256",
  "rewritten_text": "No raising by hand for him, certainly not! Joe offered me more gravy, but I was too nervous to accept. He was a great burden to you, ma'am, Mrs. Hubble said, sympathizing with my sister. Burden? my sister repeated, and launched into a terrifying list of every illness I had caused, every sleepless night I had inflicted, every fall I had taken, and every injury I had sustained, concluding with how often she had wished me dead while I stubbornly refused to oblige. I suspect the Romans were constantly irritated by their own noses, perhaps explaining their restless nature. Regardless, Mr. Wopsle’s Roman nose so annoyed me during my sister’s tirade that I wanted to pull it until he screamed. Yet, my suffering was nothing compared to the dread I felt when the silence following her speech left everyone staring at me with clear indignation. Still, Mr. Pumblechook said, steering the conversation back, boiled pork is quite rich, isn't it? Have some brandy, uncle, my sister offered. Oh no, it was happening! He would realize it was watered down, and I would be ruined. I gripped the table leg beneath the cloth and braced for the end. My sister fetched the stone bottle and poured his drink. The man toyed with his glass, holding it to the light and setting it down, prolonging my agony while the others cleared the table. I couldn't look away. Clinging to the table, I watched him playfully swirl the liquid, smile, tilt his head back, and swallow. Suddenly, the room erupted in shock as he leaped up, spun around in a violent, wheezing dance, and bolted out the door. Through the window, I saw him gasping and spitting, making horrific faces as if he had lost his mind. I held on tight as Joe and my sister rushed to help him."
}

