  "request_id": "5cf65dbd26eb11ca8d494939",
  "rewritten_text": "The day unfolded much like the previous one. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley spent part of the morning with the recovering patient, and that evening, Elizabeth joined them in the drawing-room. There was no card table set up. Mr. Darcy sat writing while Miss Bingley hovered nearby, frequently interrupting him with messages for his sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley played piquet, watched by Mrs. Hurst. Elizabeth occupied herself with needlework, finding amusement in the interaction between Darcy and Miss Bingley. The lady’s constant praise of his penmanship and the indifference with which he met her remarks created a fascinating exchange that perfectly matched Elizabeth’s assessment of both individuals. How pleased Miss Darcy will be to get such a letter, she remarked. He did not reply. You write quite quickly, she noted. You are wrong, he said, I write rather slowly. Think of the many letters you must write annually, including business correspondence; how dreadful I should find that. It is fortunate, then, that the task falls to me rather than you, he replied. Please tell your sister I am eager to see her, she said. I have already conveyed that, as you requested, he answered. I fear you dislike your pen; let me mend it, as I am quite skilled at it. Thank you, but I always mend my own, he said. How do you manage to write so evenly? she asked. He remained silent. Tell your sister I am thrilled to hear of her harp progress and that I adore her table design, which is far better than Miss Grantley’s. May I postpone your enthusiasm until my next letter? I lack the space to do it justice now, he replied. It does not matter, as I shall see her in January, she said, but do you always write such delightful, lengthy letters to her, Mr. Darcy? They are usually long, he said, though whether they are delightful is not for me to judge. It is my belief that anyone who writes long letters with ease must write well, she stated. That is no compliment to Darcy, Caroline, her brother interjected, because he does not write with ease; he labors over his four-syllable words, do you not, Darcy? My writing style is quite unlike yours, he replied. Oh, Miss Bingley exclaimed, Charles writes in the most haphazard fashion imaginable."
}

