parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "055af7a3d63d9c963462e58b",
  "rewritten_text": "Poor Jack was later killed in a duel by Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara died of a broken heart at Tunbridge Wells before the year ended, so in every way, it was a great success. It was, however, an extremely difficult costume to prepare, and it took him three full hours to get ready. Finally, he was satisfied with his appearance. The large leather riding boots were slightly too big, and he could only find one of his two pistols, but he was pleased. At a quarter past one, he slipped out of the wall and crept down the hall. Reaching the twins' room, known as the Blue Bed Chamber for its curtains, he found the door slightly open. Hoping for a dramatic entrance, he threw it wide, only for a heavy jug of water to fall on him, soaking him and narrowly missing his shoulder. He heard muffled laughter from the bed. The shock was so intense that he fled to his room, catching a severe cold the next day. He was only comforted by the fact that he had left his head behind; otherwise, the consequences could have been dire. He gave up hope of scaring the rude American family, settling for creeping through the halls in soft slippers, wearing a thick red scarf to avoid drafts, and carrying a small antique gun in case the twins attacked. The final blow came on September 19th. He went to the entrance hall, feeling safe, and amused himself by mocking the large photographs of the American Minister and his wife that had replaced the Canterville portraits. He was dressed simply in a shroud stained with graveyard dirt, his jaw tied up with yellow linen, and he carried a lantern and a shovel."
}

