parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "00632a69cd19bf114a14b2a1",
  "rewritten_text": "My father had been the center of my existence, and his memory remained the life of my life. While I could feel gratitude toward others, I could no longer love or hope as I once had; everything was suffering, and even my pleasures were merely endured rather than enjoyed. I felt like a lonely valley surrounded by steep, dark cliffs, where no warmth could reach and there was no path to brighter lands. Consequently, while friendship offered temporary comfort, it could not restore me. It arrived like a fleeting visit, and when it left, I barely noticed. The spark of life was extinguished within me, so do not be surprised that I neither welcomed the gift of a friend with joy nor mourned its departure with bitterness. My friend was named Woodville. I will briefly share his story so you may understand how cold my heart must have been to remain unmoved by his eloquence and sympathy, and how, as we were both deeply unhappy, we might have been a mutual comfort had I not been turned to stone by the misery I endured. Woodville’s misfortunes were not as profound as mine; his was a natural grief that served to purify rather than destroy, and he would eventually emerge brighter and happier once the shadow passed. Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was one of the rare few favored by fortune from birth, gifted with boundless intellect and charm, and protected from even the slightest imperfection or disappointment. His mind seemed crafted of pure excellence, and his understanding was beyond error. His genius was transcendent, rising like a bright star that drew everyone’s admiration. He was a poet, though that title is often degraded and fails to capture his true nature. He resembled the poets of antiquity, crowned by the muses at birth. Walking among men, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly aura that set him apart. His striking beauty, the intense fire in his eyes, and his captivating words left listeners in silent, ecstatic wonder, making everyone else seem merely like attendants to his superior excellence."
}

