parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6e4cf99afc0ef0418e6286c0",
  "rewritten_text": "One day, while I was studying with Mr. Pocket, I received a letter that made me incredibly nervous. Although I had never seen the handwriting before, I immediately knew who it was from. It had no formal greeting, simply stating: I am coming to London the day after tomorrow on the midday coach. I believe it was arranged that you would meet me. Miss Havisham expects it, and I am writing to follow her instructions. She sends her regards. Yours, Estella. If I had more time, I would have bought new clothes, but I had to make do with what I owned. I lost my appetite and could not find any peace until the day arrived. Even then, I was restless and began waiting at the coach office in Wood Street long before the coach could have possibly arrived from my hometown. Despite knowing it was irrational, I felt I could not leave the office for more than five minutes. I had been waiting for about half an hour when Wemmick bumped into me. Hello, Mr. Pip, he said. How are you? I would not have expected to see you here. I explained that I was meeting someone arriving by coach and asked about his home and his father. Both are doing well, thank you, Wemmick replied, especially the old man. He is in great spirits and will be eighty-two soon. I am thinking of firing my cannon eighty-two times, provided the neighbors do not complain and the cannon holds up. But this is not business talk. Where do you think I am going? To the office? I asked. Almost, Wemmick said. I am going to Newgate Prison. We are handling a bank robbery case, and after visiting the scene, I need to speak with our client. Did your client commit the robbery? I asked. Goodness no, Wemmick answered dryly. But he is accused of it. You or I could just as easily be accused of the same thing."
}

