parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f63543c663e3aaa3689abc46",
  "rewritten_text": "By eight o’clock, I had reached seventeen miles above the earth. It was clear my ascent was accelerating, even without dropping more ballast. My head and ears throbbed with sharp, recurring pain, and my nose bled occasionally, though I felt better than expected. Breathing became increasingly difficult, with each breath causing my chest to spasm. I prepared my condensing equipment for use. The view below was stunning. To the west, north, and south, a vast, calm ocean stretched out, turning a deep blue and appearing slightly curved. Far to the east, I could see Great Britain, the coasts of France and Spain, and part of Africa. Human cities and buildings had vanished entirely. Gibraltar looked like a tiny speck, and the Mediterranean, filled with islands like stars in the sky, stretched until it seemed to pour over the horizon’s edge, making me strain to hear the sound of a great waterfall. Above, the sky was pitch black, and the stars shone brightly. The pigeons were suffering, so I decided to release them. I placed a gray-mottled bird on the edge of the basket. It looked around anxiously, fluttering and cooing, but refused to leave. When I tossed it a few yards away, it did not fly down as I expected. Instead, it struggled frantically to return, crying out shrilly. It managed to land back on the rim, but immediately collapsed and died inside the basket."
}

