parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eeb67ce3ba204a18573484e4",
  "rewritten_text": "I spent two years this way. Day after day passed without external change, yet these years slowly altered my mind as I drifted toward death. I studied more, empathizing with the ideas found in books and history, losing my sense of self among the people of the past. As my immediate suffering faded, I became more human. Solitude lost its appeal, and I began to crave companionship—not from a crowd, but from one friend who would love me. You might think this prepared me to rejoin society, but I disagree. The sympathy I wanted had to be so pure and free from worldly influence that I would inevitably be disappointed by the crude realities that taint even the best human feelings. I was less prepared for social interaction than ever. When I first left society, people tormented me like a sickness—something external I wanted to discard. Now, however, I craved a deep connection, which would only have invited more suffering, as I was as sensitive as a fragile plant. I did not want help with ambition or wisdom, but simple, mutual affection—smiles and gentle words. I wanted one heart to listen to my complaints, hoping something good might grow from such sorrow. Yet, how could I find this? True friendship is a rare spirit, usually found between those raised together or bonded by shared hardship. It comes to the chosen unexpectedly, like dew on barren ground, but it flees when pursued; it mocks those who beg for it. I knew this and did not go looking for it. Yet, there on my lonely heath, in my humble home, it found me like a winter sunbeam warming the snow. Sadly, the sun shone on ruined fruit; I could not revive under its light, for I was too broken to feel its kindness."
}

