{
  "request_id": "ac08700d9864c212e8952489",
  "rewritten_text": "Do not misunderstand; I was never truly insane. I remained aware of my condition even when wild thoughts pushed me toward madness, keeping those struggles hidden in silence. To others, I appeared only as a broken-spirited girl who spoke softly, shed hidden tears, avoided company, and never smiled. That was all they saw. Eventually, I escaped my guardian’s home and vanished. Based on the letters I left behind, people assumed I had taken my own life, so they stopped searching for me. I left London on a small ship bound for northern England. Once I was truly alone, peace finally returned. As the ship sailed across the calm sea, I sat on deck feeling like a changed person. I was no longer the miserable Mathilda, but a young hermit dedicated to solitude, determined to keep my heart free from despair. My simple, nun-like clothing, the secret of my survival, and the promise of a lonely future brought comfort to my wounded heart. The breeze refreshed me as I watched sunlight dance on the waves and birds skim the water. I slept without dreams and woke to enjoy my quiet freedom. After four days, we reached our destination. I left the coast immediately, heading inland to find the isolated home I had imagined. I wanted a house on a vast, empty plain where I could see the horizon and wander without meeting anyone. I did not hate people, but I knew my peace of mind required solitude. I settled in a desolate, stony heath covered in short grass, marked only by a few rushes near a small pool."
}

