parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e84907deaf5a989c00be15ed",
  "rewritten_text": "She and the children could certainly remain.” “Oh, papa, that is something you have never managed, and I doubt you ever will. Isabella cannot endure being separated from her husband.” This was undeniable. Though he disliked the thought, Mr. Woodhouse could only sigh in resignation; noticing his distress, Emma quickly shifted the conversation to a more cheerful topic. “Harriet must spend as much time with us as possible while my sister is visiting. I am certain she will enjoy the children. We are quite proud of them, aren't we, papa? I wonder which she will find more handsome, Henry or John?” “Yes, I wonder as well. Poor little things, they will be so happy to visit. They truly love being at Hartfield, Harriet.” “I am sure they do, sir. I cannot imagine who would not.” “Henry is a fine boy, but John resembles his mother. Henry is the eldest and was named for me, rather than his father. John, the second, bears his father’s name. Some were surprised the eldest was not named for him, but Isabella insisted on Henry, which I found very sweet. He is quite bright. They are all remarkably clever and have such charming habits. They often stand by my chair asking for string, and Henry once requested a knife, though I told him knives are only for grandfathers. I often feel their father is too harsh with them.” “He seems harsh to you,” Emma replied, “because you are so gentle; but compared to other fathers, he is not. He wants his sons to be active and robust, so he may speak sharply when they misbehave, but Mr. John Knightley is a devoted father. The children adore him.” “And then their uncle arrives and tosses them toward the ceiling in a terrifying manner!” “But they love it, papa; it is their greatest delight. They enjoy it so much that if their uncle did not insist they take turns, they would never stop.” “Well, I simply cannot comprehend it.” “That is true for all of us, papa. One half of the world rarely understands the joys of the other.”"
}

