parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "baa01f268264e6af9ddda35a",
  "rewritten_text": "Do you visit often? I have not returned since. Neither have I. As the moon ascended, I recalled the serene expression on the ceiling that had vanished, and the firm grip on my hand when I uttered his final earthly words. Estella eventually interrupted our quiet. I frequently intended to return, but various matters hindered me. Poor, sad place. Moonlight caught the silver mist and the tears falling from her eyes. Unaware I noticed, she composed herself and asked if I wondered why the property remained in such disrepair. Yes, Estella. The land is mine. It is the sole thing I have not surrendered. Everything else faded away, but I held onto this. It was the one thing I stubbornly defended during those miserable years. Will it be developed? Finally, yes. I came to bid it farewell before it changes. And you, she asked with gentle concern, do you still reside abroad? Still. And you are doing well, I trust? I labor diligently for a modest living, so yes, I am doing well. I have thought of you often, Estella remarked. Have you? Lately, quite often. There was a long, difficult period when I pushed away the memory of what I discarded before realizing its value. But since my obligations allow it, I have welcomed that memory back. You have always remained in my heart, I replied. We fell silent until she spoke again. I never imagined I would bid you farewell while saying goodbye to this place, Estella said. I am very glad to do so. Glad to separate again? Parting is painful to me; the memory of our last goodbye has always been sorrowful. But you told me, God bless you, God forgive you, she replied earnestly. If you could say that then, you will not hesitate to say it now, when suffering has taught me to understand your heart. I have been bent and broken, but I hope, into a better form."
}

