parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0d99b1133e5be286ad73b776",
  "rewritten_text": "Back home, we collected taxes through tariffs, and citizens liked to believe foreign importers paid them. In reality, the American people paid, and the cost was distributed so perfectly that a millionaire and a laborer's infant paid the exact same amount: six dollars. I thought that was quite fair. In Arthur's time, Scotland and Ireland were under his rule, with a total population of under one million. A worker earned three cents a day, covering his own expenses. The government's annual budget was ninety thousand dollars, or about two hundred fifty dollars a day. By using nickels instead of gold for the king's healing ceremony, I satisfied everyone and saved four-fifths of the daily budget, which would equal eight hundred thousand dollars in my time. I used a lesson from my childhood: I used to donate buttons to missionaries instead of pennies. The buttons satisfied the distant recipients just as well as coins, and keeping the coins benefited me. Everyone was happy, and no one was harmed. Marinel processed the patients. He checked their eligibility, then sent the qualified ones to the king. A priest recited scripture while the king touched the patients' ulcers. Finally, the king hung a nickel around each patient's neck, and they were dismissed. You might wonder if that could actually cure anyone, but it did. Any ritual works if the patient has enough faith. Near Astolat, there was a chapel built where a goose girl claimed to have seen the Virgin Mary. A painting of the event hung there; it was so poorly done that you might think it would make a sick person worse. Instead, thousands of sick and lame people prayed before it every year and left fully recovered, and even healthy people could look at it without harm."
}

