parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "baf1baadb635430c0043ed4c",
  "rewritten_text": "Do not assume I failed to see the inherent trouble in it. You can be certain they were perfectly comfortable. I might not like them more for that. So many women and children have no right to be comfortable on a ship. My dear Frederick, you are speaking nonsense. What would become of us sailors' wives, who often need to reach our husbands at different ports, if everyone shared your views? My views did not stop me from taking Mrs. Harville and her family to Plymouth. I hate hearing you talk like some posh gentleman, treating women as if they were delicate dolls rather than rational beings. None of us expect smooth sailing our entire lives. My dear, when he has a wife, he will change his tune. If we are lucky enough to see another war, we will see him act just as we have, grateful to anyone who brings his wife to him. Indeed we shall. I am finished, Captain Wentworth said. Once married people start telling me I will think differently when I am married, I can only say I will not, and they insist I will, and that is the end of it. He stood and walked away. You must have been a great traveler, ma'am, Mrs. Musgrove said to Mrs. Croft. Quite a bit in my fifteen years of marriage, though many have done more. I have crossed the Atlantic four times and been to the East Indies and back, along with time in Cork, Lisbon, and Gibraltar. I never went past the Straits or to the West Indies. We do not consider Bermuda or the Bahamas to be the West Indies, you know. Mrs. Musgrove had no response, as she had never called them anything else in her life. I assure you, ma'am, Mrs. Croft continued, that nothing beats the accommodations on a warship, specifically the larger ones. On a frigate, you are more confined, but any reasonable woman can be perfectly happy there. I can safely say the happiest years of my life were spent on a ship. As long as we were together, there was nothing to fear."
}

