parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "54d606b0462a1e4dc7f91f44",
  "rewritten_text": "Behold, Merlin remarked, there is the blade I mentioned. They observed a maiden walking across the water. Who is she? Arthur inquired. That is the Lady of the Lake, Merlin replied; within that water lies a rock containing a dwelling as beautiful as any on earth, and this woman will soon approach you; speak kindly so she might grant you the weapon. The damsel reached Arthur and greeted him, and he returned the gesture. Lady, Arthur asked, what blade is that held by the arm above the surface? I desire it, for I lack a sword. King Arthur, she answered, that weapon is mine, and if you promise me a future favor, it shall be yours. I swear it, Arthur pledged. Then board that barge, row to the blade, and take it along with the scabbard, and I will request my gift when the time is right. Arthur and Merlin dismounted, tethered their horses to trees, and entered the vessel. Arthur grasped the hilt, and as he took the weapon, the arm vanished beneath the waves. They returned to shore and continued their journey. Soon, Arthur noticed a grand tent. What does that pavilion signify? It belongs to Sir Pellinore, the knight you previously fought, Merlin explained, but he is absent; he has been battling your knight, Egglame, who fled toward Carlion to avoid death. We shall encounter him on the road shortly. That is fortunate, Arthur said, for now that I have a sword, I will challenge him and seek vengeance. Do not, Merlin cautioned, for the knight is exhausted from his pursuit, and you would gain no honor in such a fight; furthermore, he is nearly unbeatable, so I advise you to let him pass, as he and his sons will serve you well in the future. You will soon be pleased to offer him your sister in marriage. I will follow your counsel when we meet, Arthur agreed."
}

