  "rewritten_text": "Should I ask them? Or perhaps Miss Bates? She is closer, and I suspect she understands everyone's preferences as well as anyone. We need more input. Shall I invite her to join us? If you like, Mrs. Weston said hesitantly, if you think she will be helpful. You will get nothing useful from Miss Bates, Emma replied. She will be full of joy and gratitude, but she will not listen to your questions or tell you anything. I see no point in consulting her. But she is so entertaining! I love hearing her talk, and I do not have to invite the whole family. Mr. Weston joined them then and approved of the plan. Yes, Frank, go get Miss Bates and settle this. She will enjoy the idea, and she is the perfect person to help us overcome obstacles. She is a living lesson in how to be happy. But bring them both. Both, sir? Can the older lady? The older lady? No, the younger one, of course. I will think you a fool, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece. Oh, I apologize, sir. I did not realize. I will certainly try to persuade them both. He ran off. Long before he returned with the brisk aunt and her elegant niece, Mrs. Weston had reconsidered the hallway and decided the issues were trivial. The remaining plans for furniture, lighting, music, and refreshments seemed to resolve themselves or could be handled later with Mrs. Stokes. Everyone invited would surely attend, and Frank had already written to Enscombe to extend his stay, which was certain to be approved. It was going to be a wonderful dance. When Miss Bates arrived, she enthusiastically agreed. She was not needed for advice, but her constant, warm approval was very welcome. For another half-hour, they walked between the rooms, suggesting ideas and enjoying the anticipation of the event."
}

