            "text": "{\n  \"request_id\": \"f2e70010faf1d68ab99fdab1\",\n  \"rewritten_text\": \"He was brought to you. You see, I am aware of the details. That is true, Defarge replied. A subtle nudge from his wife, who continued knitting and humming, signaled him to keep his answers brief. The spy continued, It was to you that his daughter came, and under your care that she took him to England, accompanied by that man from Tellson’s Bank, Lorry. That is true, Defarge repeated. Very interesting, the spy remarked. I knew Doctor Manette and his daughter in England. Do you hear much about them now? No, Defarge said. Actually, Madame Defarge interjected, looking up from her work, we never hear from them. We heard they arrived safely, and perhaps received a letter or two, but since then, we have gone our separate ways. Quite right, madame, the spy replied. She is getting married. Getting married? she echoed. She was attractive enough to have married long ago. You English seem quite cold. You know I am English, he said. I can tell by your accent, she replied, and I assume the man matches the tongue. He laughed off the remark. Miss Manette is marrying a Frenchman, not an Englishman. It is ironic that she is marrying the nephew of the late Marquis—the man for whose death Gaspard was executed. He lives in England as Mr. Charles Darnay, though his mother’s family name is d’Aulnais. Madame Defarge continued knitting, but the news clearly unsettled her husband. As he tried to light his pipe, his hands shook. The spy noticed his reaction immediately. Having made his point, the spy paid for his drink and left, politely mentioning that he looked forward to seeing them again.\"\n}",
