parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3848a08eaa2cf7e46f3d48dd",
  "rewritten_text": "No imagination could capture the majesty of such a spectacle occurring in the dead of night; it would have resembled hell itself. Even in daylight, my hair bristled as I peered into the gaping depths, picturing my mind wandering through those strange, vaulted chambers and crimson chasms of unfathomable fire. I had narrowly avoided disaster. Had the balloon lingered in the cloud a moment longer—or had I not been prompted by the dampness to drop my ballast—certain destruction would have followed. Such dangers are often overlooked, yet they remain the most significant risks of ballooning. By now, however, I had reached an altitude where such concerns were no longer pressing. By seven o'clock, I was ascending swiftly, and the barometer marked a height of nine and a half miles. Breathing became arduous, and my head throbbed with intense pain. Feeling moisture on my cheeks, I realized it was blood flowing steadily from my ears. My eyes caused me further distress; they felt as though they had bulged from their sockets, and everything I saw appeared warped. These unexpected symptoms alarmed me. In a moment of reckless haste, I discarded fifteen pounds of ballast. This sudden surge upward thrust me too quickly into thin air, nearly ending my life and my journey. A spasm gripped me for over five minutes, and even afterward, I could only gasp for air, bleeding profusely from my nose, ears, and eyes. The pigeons were clearly suffering, struggling to break free, while the cat staggered about, mewing piteously with her tongue lolling out as if poisoned. I realized too late the folly of my actions and was overcome with agitation, expecting death to arrive at any moment."
}

