parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dac61ad2a7fe36d65704360e",
  "rewritten_text": "The powerful, unearthly voice—impossible to link to any instrument—ceased its resonance, and the girl, turning to help her elderly companion, finally noticed us. He was her father, and she had guided his sightless steps since childhood. They were Saxons who had emigrated years earlier, forming bonds with local villagers. When the plague began, a young German student joined them. Their story was clear: a nobleman who loved the musician’s daughter and followed them to escape his family’s disapproval. But the great equalizer arrived, and the youth was an early victim. She lived on for her father’s sake, keeping him unaware that the world had emptied, leaving them as the only listeners to her music. The day we arrived, she fell ill. Terrified of leaving her blind father alone, she hid her condition, driven by desperation to perform one last time. At vespers, she led him to the chapel and played a hymn to creation, her own impending funeral song, with perfect precision. We appeared to her like divine messengers, and her fragile composure shattered. She rushed to us, clutched Adrian’s knees, and begged him to save her father before collapsing into tears. Poor girl. She and her father now lie beside her lover beneath the walnut tree she identified before she died. Her father, finally realizing her state, held her hand until it grew cold in death. He remained motionless until he followed her into rest twelve hours later. They sleep beneath the tree, framed by the Jura mountains and the distant Alps. The church spire still rises above the trees, and though her hands are still, I imagine the music they loved continues to drift there, comforting their gentle spirits."
}

