parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dfa7b4062669c2d33d3315e3",
  "rewritten_text": "I felt no concern for her. She was a capable woman who handled worldly affairs without my help. Honestly, she likely viewed me as an aimless youth, useful only for daydreaming, and was pleased to see me go. It was a pitch-black night when I departed, accompanied by the three creditors who had plagued me. We transported the balloon and its gear via a circuitous route to the storage site. Finding everything undisturbed, I set to work. It was April first. The night remained dark and starless, with intermittent drizzle causing us misery. My main worry was the balloon, which grew heavy with moisture despite its varnish, and the risk to the gunpowder. I kept my three duns busy packing ice around the central cask and stirring the acid. They constantly pestered me about my intentions, complaining about the grueling labor and the pointlessness of getting soaked for such strange rituals. I grew anxious, working feverishly, as I suspected these fools believed I was consorting with the devil and that my actions were sinister. I feared they might abandon me, but I managed to calm them by promising full payment once my task was complete. They interpreted this in their own way, assuming I would soon possess great wealth; as long as they were paid, they likely cared little for my fate. After four and a half hours, the balloon was inflated. I secured the car and loaded my equipment, including the condenser, plenty of water, and compact, nutrient-dense provisions like pemmican."
}

