parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "28d2214055819e7a3911fda2",
  "rewritten_text": "P.S. Do tell me about the bows. Jennings insists they are in fashion. Lord Arthur looked so solemn and miserable over the note that the Duchess burst into laughter. My dear Arthur, she exclaimed, I will never show you a young lady’s letter again! But what should I say about the clock? I think it is a brilliant invention and would like one myself. I don’t think much of them, Lord Arthur replied with a sad smile, and after kissing his mother, he left the room. Upstairs, he collapsed onto a sofa, his eyes welling with tears. He had tried his best to commit this murder, yet failed twice through no fault of his own. He had attempted to fulfill his duty, but it seemed as if Destiny had betrayed him. He felt crushed by the pointlessness of good intentions and the futility of trying to be virtuous. Perhaps it would be better to end the engagement. Sybil would suffer, certainly, but such pain could not truly damage a nature as noble as hers. As for himself, what did it matter? There is always a war in which a man can die or a cause to which he can sacrifice his life; since existence held no joy for him, death held no terror. He would let Destiny decide his fate and would no longer intervene. At seven-thirty, he dressed and went to his club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and he was forced to dine with them. Their trivial chatter and idle jokes failed to interest him, and he left as soon as coffee was served, inventing an excuse to escape. As he exited, the porter handed him a letter from Herr Winckelkopf, inviting him to inspect an explosive umbrella that detonated upon opening. It was the latest invention from Geneva. He tore the letter into pieces, having decided to abandon his experiments. He wandered to the Thames Embankment and sat by the river for hours. The moon peered through tawny clouds like a lion’s eye, while countless stars glittered across the sky like gold dust on a purple dome. Occasionally, a barge drifted into the murky current, and railway signals shifted from green to red as trains shrieked across the bridge."
}

