parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5a72111d9af05a9d9d91edb4",
  "rewritten_text": "Poor Jack was later killed in a duel by Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara died of a broken heart at Tunbridge Wells before the year ended; in every sense, it was a complete success. It was, however, a difficult transformation to achieve, and it took him three full hours to prepare. Finally, he was satisfied with his appearance. The leather riding boots were slightly oversized, and he could only locate one of his two horse pistols, but he was pleased enough. At 1:15 a.m., he slipped from the wainscoting and crept down the hall. Reaching the twins' room, known as the Blue Bed Chamber for its curtains, he found the door slightly open. Hoping for a dramatic entrance, he threw it wide, only for a heavy water jug to fall on him, soaking him completely and narrowly missing his shoulder. Simultaneously, he heard muffled laughter from the four-poster bed. The shock sent him fleeing back to his room, and he spent the next day bedridden with a severe cold. He was only consoled by the fact that he had left his head behind; had he been wearing it, the outcome could have been disastrous. He abandoned all hope of terrifying the rude American family, settling for shuffling through the halls in soft slippers, wearing a thick red scarf to avoid drafts and carrying a small arquebus in case the twins attacked. The final indignity occurred on September 19th. He had gone to the main entrance hall, assuming he would be left alone, and was mocking the large Saroni photographs of the American Minister and his wife that had replaced the Canterville portraits. He was dressed simply in a burial shroud stained with grave dirt, his jaw bound with yellow linen, and he carried a small lantern and a sexton’s spade."
}

