parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "35dbbb20c734980726fc0b14",
  "rewritten_text": "Reviewing his letter while drafting a reply softened Harriet so much that she needed firm reminders to stay resolute. She felt terrible about causing him pain and worried deeply about his family’s opinion, fearing they might think her ungrateful. Emma suspected that if the young man had appeared right then, Harriet would have accepted him. However, the letter was written, sealed, and sent. The matter was settled, and Harriet was secure. She remained subdued all evening, but Emma tolerated her gentle regrets, occasionally easing them by affirming their friendship or steering the conversation toward Mr. Elton. I will never be invited to Abbey-Mill again, Harriet said sadly. Even if you were, I could not bear to part with you, my dear Harriet. You are far too essential at Hartfield to be spared. And I am sure I would never want to go there; I am only happy at Hartfield. Later, she added, I think Mrs. Goddard would be shocked if she knew what happened. Miss Nash certainly would, as she considers her own sister well-married, even though he is just a linen-draper. One would hate to see more pride or refinement in a schoolteacher, Harriet. I suspect Miss Nash would envy your opportunity to marry. Even this conquest would seem valuable to her. As for anything better for you, she is surely unaware. The attentions of a certain person are likely not yet the subject of Highbury gossip. I believe you and I are the only ones who have noticed his behavior. Harriet blushed and smiled, wondering why people liked her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton was encouraging, yet she soon felt sympathetic toward the rejected Mr. Martin again. Now he has my letter, she whispered. I wonder what they are doing, if his sisters know, and if he is miserable. I hope he will not take it too hard. Let us focus on our absent friends who are more happily occupied, Emma urged. Perhaps Mr. Elton is showing your portrait to his family right now, explaining how much more beautiful the original is, and after much pleading, finally revealing your name—your own dear name."
}

