parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "875e479e2226b6c8c62d3262",
  "rewritten_text": "I am uneducated, and it worries me, just a little. Tell me what is wrong. I have a cousin, my only relative and an orphan like me, whom I love dearly. She is five years younger and lives on a farm in the south. Poverty separated us, and she knows nothing of my fate because I cannot write, and even if I could, how would I explain it? It is better this way. Yes, it is better. As we traveled, and as I look at your kind, strong face that gives me such support, I have been thinking: if the Republic truly helps the poor so they suffer less, she might live a long time, perhaps even to old age. What then, my gentle sister? Do you think, she asks, her eyes filling with tears, that it will feel like a long wait for me in the afterlife where I hope we will both find mercy? It cannot be, my child; there is no time or suffering there. You comfort me so much. I am so ignorant. Should I kiss you now? Is it time? Yes. She kisses his lips, he kisses hers, and they solemnly bless each other. His hand does not tremble as he lets her go; her face remains patient and constant. She goes before him and is gone; the knitting women count twenty-two. I am the Resurrection and the Life, says the Lord; he who believes in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live. The murmuring voices, the upturned faces, and the pressing crowd surging forward like a wave all vanish. Twenty-three. That night, people in the city said his face was the most peaceful they had ever seen. Many added that he looked sublime and prophetic. Another notable victim of the same axe had recently asked at the scaffold for permission to write down her inspiring thoughts. If he had spoken his own, they would have been these: I see Barsad, Cly, Defarge, The Vengeance, the jury, and the judge, along with the new oppressors who rose from the ruins of the old, all perishing by this same instrument before its work is finally done."
}

