parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "bb0fe240ca9a9006a6970e5e",
  "rewritten_text": "He crept from the wall with a malicious smirk on his withered lips. The moon vanished behind a cloud as he slipped past the large bay window, which displayed his family crest and that of his murdered wife in blue and gold. He glided forward like a dark shadow, as if the night itself recoiled from his presence. He paused at a sound, but it was merely a dog barking at the Red Farm. Muttering archaic curses and waving his rusted dagger, he reached the hallway leading to Washington’s room. He stopped, the wind whipping his grey hair and twisting his burial shroud into grotesque shapes. As the clock struck the quarter-hour, he knew the moment had arrived. He chuckled and turned the corner, only to recoil with a cry of terror, shielding his pale face with bony hands. Before him stood a horrific specter, still as a statue and monstrous as a fever dream. Its head was bald and polished, its face round, fat, and white, twisted into a permanent, hideous grin. Scarlet light poured from its eyes, its mouth was a pit of fire, and a shroud like his own draped its massive frame. A placard on its chest bore strange, ancient writing—a record of sins and crimes. In its right hand, it held a gleaming steel sword. Having never encountered a ghost, he was terrified. After one glance, he fled down the corridor, tripping over his winding-sheet and dropping his dagger into the Minister’s boots, where the butler later found it. Safely in his room, he collapsed onto his bed and hid under the covers. Eventually, his courageous spirit returned, and he resolved to speak to the other ghost at daybreak. As dawn touched the hills, he returned to the spot, deciding that two ghosts were better than one and that his new ally might help him deal with the twins."
}

