parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "afa2e95bdc88e429351948a9",
  "rewritten_text": "On the morning Admiral and Mrs. Croft were scheduled to view Kellynch Hall, Anne decided to walk to Lady Russell’s house to stay out of the way. Later, she felt regret for missing the chance to meet them. The meeting went perfectly, and the deal was settled immediately. Both women were eager to agree, so they viewed each other favorably. The Admiral’s friendly, open nature impressed Sir Walter, especially since Mr. Shepherd had flattered him by claiming the Admiral already considered him a model of good manners. The house, grounds, and furniture were all accepted, and the legal paperwork began without any disagreements. Sir Walter even declared the Admiral the best-looking sailor he had ever met, joking that he would be proud to be seen with him if only his hair were styled better. Meanwhile, the Admiral told his wife that Sir Walter was harmless, even if not particularly brilliant. These mutual, backhanded compliments were essentially equal. The Crofts were set to move in at Michaelmas, and since Sir Walter planned to relocate to Bath the month before, they had to act quickly. Lady Russell knew Anne would have no say in choosing their new home and wanted her to stay behind until after Christmas. However, Lady Russell had her own travel plans and could not host her. Although Anne dreaded the heat of Bath and felt sad about leaving the countryside in autumn, she ultimately decided it was best to go."
}

