parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b9e0590971504b53181cfa5e",
  "rewritten_text": "P.S.—Do tell me about the bows. Jennings insists they are the fashion. Lord Arthur looked so serious and unhappy over the letter that the Duchess laughed uncontrollably. My dear Arthur, she cried, I shall never show you a young lady’s letter again! But what shall I say about the clock? I think it is a great invention, and I would like one myself. I do not think much of them, Lord Arthur said with a sad smile, and after kissing his mother, he left the room. Upstairs, he threw himself on a sofa, his eyes filling with tears. He had tried his best to commit the murder, but he had failed twice, through no fault of his own. He had tried to do his duty, but it seemed as if fate itself had betrayed him. He felt overwhelmed by the uselessness of good intentions and the futility of trying to be noble. Perhaps it would be better to end the engagement. Sybil would suffer, but suffering could not ruin a nature as fine as hers. As for himself, it did not matter. There is always a war where a man can die or a cause to which he can give his life, and since life held no pleasure for him, death held no terror. He would let fate decide his future; he would no longer interfere. At seven-thirty, he dressed and went to his club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and he had to dine with them. Their trivial talk and idle jokes did not interest him, and as soon as coffee arrived, he left, making up an excuse to get away. As he exited, the porter handed him a letter from Herr Winckelkopf, asking him to visit the next evening to see an explosive umbrella that went off when opened. It was a new invention from Geneva. He tore the letter into pieces. He had decided to stop his experiments. He wandered to the Thames Embankment and sat by the river for hours. The moon peeked through tawny clouds like a lion’s eye, and countless stars sparkled in the sky like gold dust on a purple dome. Occasionally, a barge drifted into the murky water, and railway signals shifted from green to red as trains shrieked across the bridge."
}

