parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fbb89021d81df0b7e5f7f77a",
  "rewritten_text": "Well, Mr. Bumble, it is true that since the new feeding system began, the coffins are narrower and shallower than before. But we need to make a profit. Good wood is expensive, and the iron handles are shipped from Birmingham. Mr. Bumble agreed that every business has its challenges and that a fair profit is reasonable. The undertaker chuckled, noting that he makes up for small losses over time. He then complained that his biggest problem is that the stouter people die the fastest. Those who were once well-off and paid taxes are the first to fail when they enter the workhouse. Losing a few inches of wood beyond his estimates hurts his profits, especially with a family to support. Sensing that Sowerberry’s complaints reflected poorly on the parish, Mr. Bumble changed the subject to Oliver Twist. He asked if the undertaker knew anyone looking for a parish apprentice, describing the boy as a burden on the parish. He pointed his cane at the sign advertising five pounds for an apprentice. Sowerberry grabbed Bumble’s coat lapel, saying that was exactly what he wanted to discuss, while complimenting the beadle’s elegant button. Bumble looked down proudly at his brass buttons, which featured the parish seal of the Good Samaritan. He mentioned that the board gave them to him on New Year’s Day, and he first wore them to an inquest for a poor tradesman who had died in a doorway."
}

