parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d9ef57e6767a71ec380ee03f",
  "rewritten_text": "I truly wish we owned a donkey. It would be ideal for Jane, Miss Bates, and myself to arrive on donkeys, with my dear husband walking alongside. I must discuss buying one with him. In the country, I consider it essential; regardless of a woman's interests, she cannot remain confined indoors forever, and long walks are plagued by summer dust and winter mud. You will encounter neither between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane remains free of dust and is currently quite dry. Nevertheless, arrive by donkey if you prefer; you are welcome to borrow Mrs. Cole’s. I want everything to suit your preferences. I am certain of that, and I truly appreciate your kindness. Despite your somewhat dry and blunt demeanor, I recognize your deeply affectionate heart. As I tell Mr. E., you are quite the eccentric. Believe me, Knightley, I am fully aware of your consideration in arranging this plan. It is exactly what I desired. Mr. Knightley had another motive for avoiding a shaded table. He hoped to convince Mr. Woodhouse to attend, knowing that dining outdoors would surely make the elderly man ill. He had to ensure Mr. Woodhouse was not lured into discomfort under the guise of a pleasant morning excursion. The invitation was genuine, and Mr. Woodhouse agreed. He had not visited Donwell in two years. He reasoned that on a fine morning, he, Emma, and Harriet could visit; he would sit with Mrs. Weston while the young women explored the gardens, assuming the ground was dry. He was eager to see the estate again and pleased to meet the Eltons and other neighbors. He found no fault in the plan, praising Mr. Knightley’s invitation as thoughtful and far superior to dining out, which he disliked. Mr. Knightley’s proposal met with universal approval. Everyone accepted the invitation as a personal compliment. Emma and Harriet expressed great anticipation, and Mr. Weston volunteered to bring Frank along, a gesture of gratitude that Mr. Knightley could have done without. Consequently, Mr. Knightley was forced to welcome the idea, and Mr. Weston promised to write immediately to persuade Frank to attend."
}

