parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0fb1cb1ee8bd489ddf306f31",
  "rewritten_text": "Elizabeth and I grew up together, less than a year apart in age. We never fought; harmony defined our bond, and our contrasting personalities only brought us closer. Elizabeth was calm and focused, while I was passionate, driven, and obsessed with learning. She loved the poetic beauty of our Swiss home, finding joy in the mountains, the changing seasons, and the quiet of winter. While she admired the world’s surface, I wanted to understand its inner workings. My earliest memories are of a deep curiosity and the thrill of discovering nature's hidden laws. When my younger brother was born seven years later, my parents settled permanently in Switzerland. We split our time between a house in Geneva and a country home on the shore of the lake, living a quiet, private life. I preferred a small circle of friends over crowds, so I ignored most of my classmates, except for Henry Clerval. Henry was the son of a local merchant, a boy of great imagination who loved adventure and danger. He was obsessed with stories of knights and chivalry, writing his own heroic songs and tales of magic. He often organized plays and masquerades based on legends like King Arthur or the crusaders. I had a perfectly happy childhood. My parents were incredibly kind and indulgent, acting not as strict rulers, but as the loving providers of all our joy."
}

