parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e49b3f365c60e5bb7384b779",
  "rewritten_text": "You might not know me unless you have read The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, but that does not matter. Mark Twain wrote that book, and he mostly told the truth, though he exaggerated a few things. Everyone lies occasionally, except perhaps Aunt Polly, the widow, or Mary. Those people are all featured in that book, which is largely accurate despite the embellishments. The story ends with Tom and me discovering the robbers' hidden treasure in the cave, which made us wealthy. We each received six thousand dollars in gold, a staggering amount. Judge Thatcher invested it for us, earning us a dollar a day each, which was more than we knew how to spend. The Widow Douglas adopted me and tried to civilize me, but living in her house was difficult because she was so incredibly rigid and proper. Eventually, I could not stand it anymore, so I ran away, put on my old rags, and returned to my sugar barrel, feeling free and content. However, Tom Sawyer found me and promised that if I returned to the widow and acted respectable, I could join his new band of robbers. So, I went back. The widow cried over me, calling me a poor lost lamb and other names, though she meant no harm. She forced me back into those new clothes, which made me sweat and feel restricted. The old routine resumed: she rang a bell for supper, and I had to be punctual. At the table, I had to wait for her to say grace over the food, even though there was nothing wrong with it, other than everything being cooked separately. I prefer when food is mixed together so the flavors blend. After dinner, she read to me about Moses and the bulrushes. I was interested at first, but when she mentioned that Moses had been dead for a long time, I lost interest, as I have no use for dead people."
}

