parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b11ee32ab2009f45d1051c32",
  "rewritten_text": "No imagination could capture the majesty of such a sight occurring in the dead of night; it would have resembled hell itself. Even in daylight, my hair stood on end as I peered into the yawning abyss, picturing myself wandering through those strange, vaulted halls and ghastly, glowing chasms of fire. I had narrowly escaped disaster. Had the balloon remained in the cloud a moment longer—or had I not decided to drop ballast to avoid the damp—I would have been doomed. Such dangers are often overlooked, yet they are the most severe risks of ballooning. By now, however, I had reached an altitude where I no longer feared them. By seven o'clock, my barometer showed I had climbed nine and a half miles. Breathing became difficult, and my head throbbed with intense pain. Feeling moisture on my cheeks, I realized it was blood streaming from my ears. My eyes also caused me distress; they felt as though they were bulging from their sockets, and everything I saw appeared distorted. Alarmed by these unexpected symptoms, I foolishly threw out fifteen pounds of ballast. This sudden, rapid ascent into the thin atmosphere nearly killed me. I was struck by a spasm lasting over five minutes, and even afterward, I could only gasp for air, bleeding profusely from my nose, ears, and eyes. The pigeons struggled in distress, while the cat staggered about, mewing piteously with its tongue lolling out as if poisoned. Realizing my reckless error too late, I was overcome with panic, fully expecting to die within minutes."
}

