parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "727a5e7deff297f1ea806cc6",
  "rewritten_text": "However, in that harsh critique of Chantilly published in yesterday’s Musée, the writer made shameful remarks regarding the cobbler’s name change upon becoming an actor, citing a Latin verse we have discussed before: Perdidit antiquum litera prima sonum. I had previously explained that this line concerns Orion, once spelled Urion, and I knew you would recall it. It was obvious you would connect Orion with Chantilly, and your smile confirmed you had done so. You were contemplating the cobbler’s tragic end. You had been walking with a stoop, but then you straightened up, and I knew you were considering Chantilly’s small stature. I interrupted your thoughts then to observe that, being such a tiny man, he would be better suited for the Théâtre des Variétés. Shortly after, while reading the evening Gazette des Tribunaux, we noticed these paragraphs: Extraordinary Murders. At three this morning, residents of the Quartier St. Roch were awakened by terrifying screams from the fourth floor of a Rue Morgue residence occupied by Madame L’Espanaye and her daughter, Camille. After failing to gain entry, neighbors forced the gate with a crowbar and entered with two gendarmes. The cries had stopped, but as they ascended the stairs, they heard two rough voices arguing above. By the second landing, silence had returned. The group searched the rooms, eventually forcing the locked door of a large back chamber on the fourth floor. The scene inside left everyone horrified and astonished. The room was in total chaos, with furniture smashed and scattered. The bed had been stripped and its mattress tossed into the center of the room. A blood-stained razor lay upon a chair."
}

