parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9d63e1e5843208bc33816856",
  "rewritten_text": "With no work to occupy him, he rhythmically clasped his hands together, alternating knuckles and palms, while rubbing his bearded chin in a constant, repetitive motion. Pulling him out of the mental drifting that followed his speech was like reviving someone from a faint or trying to catch the final words of a dying man. \"Did you ask for my name?\" \"I certainly did.\" \"One Hundred and Five, North Tower.\" \"Is that all?\" \"One Hundred and Five, North Tower.\" With a weary sound, he returned to his work until the silence was broken again. \"You are not a shoemaker by trade?\" Mr. Lorry asked, watching him closely. The man looked to Defarge for help, but receiving none, he turned back to Mr. Lorry. \"I am not a shoemaker? No, I was not. I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to--\" He drifted off again, his hands continuing their mechanical movements. Eventually, his eyes returned to Mr. Lorry, and he started as if waking from a dream. \"I asked permission to teach myself, and after a long time, I finally got it. I have made shoes ever since.\" As he reached for his shoe, Mr. Lorry asked, \"Monsieur Manette, do you remember me at all?\" The shoe fell from his hand as he stared at Mr. Lorry. Placing a hand on Defarge’s arm, Mr. Lorry continued, \"Monsieur Manette, do you remember this man? Look at him. Look at me. Does any memory of an old banker, your past business, a servant, or a different time come to mind?\" As the long-time prisoner stared between them, faint signs of his former intelligence flickered across his forehead, struggling through the mental fog. They appeared, faded, and vanished, but for a brief moment, they had been there."
}

