parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "65915808bb131af7c11e7f2b",
  "rewritten_text": "He stood and left the room with my nurse, and my father entered immediately. Nothing could have pleased me more than his arrival. I reached out my hand and cried, \"Are you safe—and Elizabeth—and Ernest?\" My father calmed me with assurances of their well-being, trying to lift my spirits by focusing on them, but he soon realized a prison cannot be a place of cheer. \"What a place you are in, my son!\" he said, looking sadly at the barred windows and the room's wretched state. \"You traveled to find happiness, but a curse seems to follow you. And poor Clerval—\" The mention of my murdered friend was too much for my fragile state; I wept. \"Alas, yes, Father,\" I replied. \"Some horrible destiny hangs over me, and I must live to fulfill it, or I would have died on Henry's coffin.\" We were not allowed to talk long, as my health required constant rest. Mr. Kirwin arrived and insisted I not exhaust myself. Yet, my father's presence felt like a guardian angel, and I slowly recovered. As my sickness faded, I was consumed by a dark, unshakable melancholy. Clerval’s ghastly, murdered image was always before me. My agitation often made my friends fear a relapse. Why did they preserve such a miserable life? It must be to fulfill my destiny, which is now nearing its end. Soon, death will extinguish this anguish and grant me rest. Back then, death seemed distant, though I often sat for hours, motionless, wishing for some catastrophe to bury both me and my destroyer. The trial approached. I had been imprisoned for three months, and though still weak, I had to travel nearly a hundred miles to the county town for court. Mr. Kirwin took charge of gathering witnesses and preparing my defense."
}

