parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d3912ddd7b8eda1f2f8e621c",
  "rewritten_text": "After unfastening a few more loops from the rim and feeding in more cloth, I reattached the loops to their buttons. This allowed me to slide the upper part of the bag between the netting and the hoop. The hoop could now drop into the car, while the weight of the car and its contents rested entirely on the buttons. While this seemed risky, the buttons were strong and spaced closely enough that each carried only a fraction of the load. Even if the weight had tripled, I would have felt secure. I then raised the hoop inside the rubberized covering, propping it up with three light poles to keep the bag expanded and the netting in place. Finally, I sealed the opening by gathering the fabric and twisting it tightly shut with a built-in tourniquet. Three thick glass windows were set into the sides of the enclosure, allowing me to see in every direction. A fourth window in the bottom, aligned with a hole in the floor, provided a view straight down. I could not install a window at the top due to the way the fabric bunched together, but this did not matter, as the balloon itself would have blocked the view anyway. About a foot below one side window, I placed an eight-inch circular opening fitted with a threaded brass rim. I screwed the condenser tube into this rim, keeping the machine itself inside the rubber chamber."
}

