parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c0c39e17f6aba3c5a0288853",
  "rewritten_text": "You should adopt some of my philosophy. Only think of the past when remembering it brings you joy. I cannot credit you with such a philosophy. Your memories must be so free of regret that your contentment comes not from wisdom, but from ignorance. It is not so for me. Painful memories intrude, and they should not be pushed away. I have been selfish my entire life, in practice if not in principle. As a child, I knew what was right, but I was never taught to control my temper. I was given good values but left to pursue them with arrogance. As an only son, I was spoiled by parents who, though good people, encouraged me to be overbearing and to value no one outside my own circle. I looked down on the rest of the world, believing them inferior to me. I was that way from age eight until twenty-eight, and I would remain so if not for you, my dearest, loveliest Elizabeth. I owe you everything. You taught me a difficult but vital lesson. You humbled me. I approached you certain of acceptance, but you showed me how inadequate I was to please a woman like you. Had you convinced yourself I would accept you? I had. What must you think of my vanity? I believed you were hoping for my proposal. My behavior must have been at fault, though I never meant to deceive you. My high spirits often led me astray. You must have hated me after that evening. Hate you? I was angry at first, but my anger soon shifted toward myself. I am almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming? No, I felt only surprise. Your surprise could not exceed mine at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no special treatment, and I expected nothing more than what I was owed. My goal then, Darcy replied, was to show you through every civility that I was not petty enough to resent the past. I hoped to earn your forgiveness and improve your opinion of me by showing that I had taken your criticisms to heart."
}

