parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8e671634867c489962c8efc3",
  "rewritten_text": "The physician was an elderly, gentle-looking man once I roused him. I explained that my brother and I had been hunting on Spanish Island yesterday, camping on a raft fragment, when he accidentally discharged his firearm in his sleep, wounding his leg. I asked him to treat the injury discreetly so we could surprise our family upon our return that evening. When he asked who our people were, I named the Phelpses. He inquired again about the accident, and I repeated that a dream had caused the shooting, which he called a peculiar dream. He prepared his lantern and saddlebags, but hesitated at my canoe, deeming it unsafe for two. I assured him it had carried three of us—my brother, myself, and the guns—quite easily. He remained skeptical and searched for a larger vessel, but finding none, he took my canoe and told me to wait or return home. I declined, gave him directions to the raft, and he departed. I soon realized that if the treatment took several days, we would be exposed. I decided that if he needed to return, I would follow him, restrain him if necessary, and continue downriver until Tom was recovered, at which point we would compensate him and set him free. I fell asleep in a lumber pile and awoke to find the sun high. I rushed to the doctor's house, only to learn he had left during the night and had not yet returned."
}

