parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "246f00b638753a8fc3ee5e2f",
  "rewritten_text": "Everyone compares it not to the voice of a person from any nation whose language they know, but the opposite. The Frenchman assumes it was a Spaniard, claiming he might have understood words had he known Spanish. The Dutchman insists it was a Frenchman, though he required an interpreter because he does not speak French. The Englishman thinks it was a German, yet he does not understand German. The Spaniard is certain it was an Englishman, judging solely by the intonation, as he has no knowledge of English. The Italian believes it was a Russian, despite never having spoken to one. A second Frenchman disagrees with the first, certain the voice was Italian, but since he does not know that language, he relies entirely on the intonation. How remarkably strange that voice must have been to elicit such testimony, where residents of five European nations could recognize nothing familiar in its tones. You might suggest it was the voice of an Asian or an African. While those groups are not common in Paris, I will simply point out three things: one witness calls the voice harsh rather than shrill, two others describe it as quick and uneven, and no witness could distinguish any words or sounds resembling them. I do not know what impression I have made on you, Dupin continued, but I believe the logical deductions from this testimony regarding the gruff and shrill voices are enough to create a suspicion that should guide our investigation. I mean that these are the only proper deductions, and the suspicion arises inevitably from them. I will not state the suspicion yet, but keep in mind that it was strong enough to shape my inquiries in the room. Let us imagine ourselves in that chamber. What should we look for first? The means of escape used by the killers. It is safe to say that neither of us believes in supernatural events."
}

