            "text": "{\n  \"request_id\": \"0857a64e0710ac67c62fd73d\",\n  \"rewritten_text\": \"What is the result? What impression have I made on your mind? I felt a chill as Dupin asked me this. A madman did this, I said, some raving lunatic escaped from a nearby asylum. In some ways, he replied, your idea is not off base. But the voices of madmen, even in their worst fits, never match that strange voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to a nation, and their language, however confused, always has the structure of speech. Besides, the hair of a madman is not like this. I pulled this tuft from the tight grip of Madame L’Espanaye. Tell me what you make of it. Dupin, I said, completely shaken, this hair is very strange; it is not human. I did not say it was, he replied, but before we decide, look at this sketch I have drawn. It is a copy of what was described in the testimony as dark bruises and deep nail marks on the throat of Mademoiselle L’Espanaye, and as a series of purple spots from fingers. You will see, my friend continued, spreading the paper on the table, that this drawing shows a firm, fixed hold. There is no slipping. Each finger kept its grip until the victim died. Try to place your fingers into these marks. I tried and failed. We are not giving this a fair test, he said. The paper is flat, but a human throat is round. Here is a piece of wood about the size of a throat. Wrap the drawing around it and try again. I did, but the difficulty was even clearer. This is not the mark of a human hand, I said. Read this passage from Cuvier, Dupin replied. It was a detailed anatomical description of the large orangutan from the East Indian Islands. The giant size, incredible strength, speed, wild ferocity, and tendency to imitate are well known. I understood the horror of the murder at once. The description of the fingers, I said after reading, matches this drawing perfectly.\"\n}",
