parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "78b9062389c2f8f9e9572d8c",
  "rewritten_text": "“Now,” Mr. Lorry said, placing his hand softly on the Doctor’s arm after a brief pause, “what do you attribute this episode to?” “I suspect,” Doctor Manette replied, “that there was a powerful and unusual resurgence of the memories that originally triggered his illness. I believe some deeply painful associations were suddenly brought to mind. It is likely he had long feared these associations might resurface under specific conditions. He attempted to brace himself, but perhaps that very effort left him less capable of enduring the shock.” “Would he recall what happened during the relapse?” Mr. Lorry inquired, hesitating slightly. The Doctor glanced sadly around the room, shook his head, and replied quietly, “Not in the least.” “And regarding the future?” Mr. Lorry suggested. “Regarding the future,” the Doctor said, regaining his composure, “I am very hopeful. Since Heaven mercifully restored him so quickly, I have great hope. Having succumbed to a complex pressure he long dreaded and fought, and having recovered once the storm passed, I trust the worst is behind him.” “Well, that is a great comfort. I am grateful!” Mr. Lorry exclaimed. “I am grateful!” the Doctor echoed, bowing his head reverently. “There are two other matters,” Mr. Lorry continued, “on which I seek guidance. May I proceed?” “You could do your friend no greater service.” The Doctor offered his hand. “First, he is studious and remarkably energetic; he pursues professional knowledge and experiments with great fervor. Is he doing too much?” “I believe not. It may be the nature of his mind to require constant occupation. This could be partly innate and partly a result of his suffering. If his mind were not engaged in constructive pursuits, it would be more prone to drift toward unhealthy thoughts. He may have recognized this himself.” “You are certain he is not under excessive strain?” “I am quite certain.” “My dear Manette, if he were currently overworked—” “My dear Lorry, I doubt that is possible. He has endured such intense pressure in one area that he requires a counterweight.” “Forgive my persistence as a businessman. Assuming he were overworked, would it manifest as a recurrence of this disorder?”"
}

