parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5dd8b7835dba2bdaec6b7f94",
  "rewritten_text": "In our village, Saturday nights brought a pleasant feeling of tidying up and pausing before starting fresh, which encouraged Joe to stay out thirty minutes past his usual time. When his rum and water were finished, Joe stood up to leave and took my hand. Stop a moment, Mr. Gargery, the stranger said. I believe I have a shiny new shilling in my pocket, and if I do, the boy shall have it. He found it among his change, wrapped it in crumpled paper, and handed it to me, insisting it was mine to keep. I thanked him while staring rudely, clutching Joe’s hand. He said goodnight to Joe and Mr. Wopsle, who left with us, but gave me only a strange look with his one good eye, closing the other as if to signal something. On the walk home, Mr. Wopsle left us at the tavern door, and Joe walked in silence, mouth agape to air out the taste of rum. I was too stunned by this encounter with a man from my past to speak. My sister was in a decent mood when we reached the kitchen, so Joe told her about the shilling. She immediately claimed it must be counterfeit, but when I unwrapped it, the coin was genuine. Then she noticed two one-pound notes inside the paper. They were dirty and worn from heavy use. Joe grabbed his hat and rushed back to the tavern to return the money. While he was gone, I sat on my stool, certain the man would be long gone. Joe soon returned, confirming the stranger had left, though he had alerted the tavern staff about the lost notes."
}

