parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e67d07c3e56aca8271f19b35",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Wickham felt entirely content with their discussion, ensuring he never again troubled himself or irritated his dear sister-in-law Elizabeth by revisiting the topic; she was relieved to see her words had successfully silenced him. When the time arrived for him and Lydia to depart, Mrs. Bennet had to accept the separation, which was expected to last at least a year since her husband refused to support her plan for the whole family to relocate to Newcastle. Oh, my dear Lydia, she lamented, when will we see each other again? Goodness, I have no idea. Perhaps not for two or three years, Lydia replied. Write to me frequently, my dear. I will write when I can, but you know married women have little time for correspondence. My sisters can write to me; they will have nothing else to occupy them. Mr. Wickham offered much warmer farewells than his wife, smiling handsomely while uttering many charming pleasantries. He is as fine a fellow as I have ever encountered, Mr. Bennet remarked once they had left, simpering and flirting with us all. I am immensely proud of him; I challenge even Sir William Lucas to present a more impressive son-in-law. Mrs. Bennet remained quite melancholy for several days following her daughter's departure. I often think there is nothing worse than parting with friends, she remarked, as one feels so abandoned without them. This is the result of marrying off a daughter, Elizabeth noted, which should make you more content that your other four remain single. That is not the case at all, Mrs. Bennet insisted. Lydia is not leaving because she is married, but because her husband's regiment is stationed so far away. Had it been closer, she would not have departed so soon. However, her despondency was soon lifted, and her spirits revived by news that began to circulate. The Netherfield housekeeper had been instructed to prepare for her master, who was arriving within a few days to shoot for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet became quite restless, alternating between smiling at Jane and shaking her head. Well, so Mr. Bingley is returning, she said to Mrs. Philips, who had delivered the news. That is all for the best. Not that I care, of course; he means nothing to us, and I certainly have no desire to see him again. Still, he is welcome to visit Netherfield if he chooses."
}

