parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1f7147f006b47784edd88e53",
  "rewritten_text": "Once my tears stopped, my mind cleared and I began to think calmly about my situation. Only a few hours had passed, yet my life had completely changed. It felt as though years had gone by; my father was dead to me, and I mourned him as if he were already in his grave. But he was not dead to the world, only to me. I had to run from him, avoiding him more carefully than my worst enemy. The thought left me breathless with pain, making it impossible to focus. I decided I would live in total isolation, perhaps moving to the Continent to join a convent—not for religious reasons, but simply to hide from the world and weep in peace. Yet, what about my father? Would he ever overcome the intense anger that controlled him? Perhaps, years from now, when age had cooled his temper, he could be a father to me again. This thought brought a sad smile to my face and gave me hope. I could wait. Let him wander for another sixteen years, shouting his complaints to the forests and waterfalls of distant lands. Let him endure hardships and the harsh elements of the world. I surrender you to this miserable life, Father. Go, live among savages, sleep under the open sky, and let your body grow weary and your heart grow cold until your youth is entirely gone."
}

