parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c25974294ac03becfd60180b",
  "rewritten_text": "I told him I needed the watermelon, but he argued that I did not need it to escape prison, which was the point of contention. He claimed it would have been acceptable if I intended to hide a knife inside to smuggle to Jim so he could kill the guard. I dropped the subject, though I found it frustrating to debate such fine, gold-leaf distinctions every time I saw a chance to grab a melon. As I was saying, we waited that morning until the yard was clear. Tom carried the sack into the shed while I kept watch. When he returned, we sat on the woodpile to talk. He said everything was ready except for tools, which would be easy to acquire. I asked what tools he meant, and he explained we needed them to dig. I asked if the old, broken picks in the shed were not sufficient. He looked at me with such pity it was almost heartbreaking. He asked if I had ever heard of a prisoner having modern tools to dig his way out. He argued that using such equipment would ruin the heroics of the situation; they might as well just hand him the key. He insisted that we needed a couple of case-knives instead. When I pointed out the absurdity of digging out a cabin foundation with knives, he insisted it was the proper, standard way. He claimed to have read all the books on the subject, noting that prisoners always use case-knives to tunnel through solid rock, a process that takes weeks or even years. He asked me to guess how long a prisoner in the Castle d'If took to dig his way out. I admitted I did not know, so he told me to guess. I guessed a month and a half."
}

