  "request_id": "6c2e534ce0eb05bd9ca6b738",
  "rewritten_text": "Must I be carried to the skies on flowery beds of ease, while others fight to win the prize and sail through bloody seas? He was considered an excellent reader. At church gatherings, he was always asked to read poetry. When he finished, the ladies would clasp their hands, roll their eyes, and shake their heads, as if to say the words were too beautiful for this world. After the hymn, Reverend Sprague acted like a bulletin board, reading announcements for meetings and societies until it felt like the list would last forever. This strange custom persists in America, even in cities, despite the availability of newspapers. Often, the less a tradition makes sense, the harder it is to stop. Then the minister prayed. It was a long, thorough prayer, covering the church, the village, the county, the state, the nation, the government, sailors at sea, the oppressed under foreign dictators, those who ignore the truth, and people in distant lands. He finished by asking that his upcoming sermon be well-received. The congregation sat down. The boy in this story did not enjoy the prayer; he merely endured it. He was restless, mentally tracking the familiar path of the minister’s words. He resented any new additions as unfair. During the prayer, a fly landed on the pew in front of him, grooming itself so calmly that it seemed to know it was perfectly safe, which only added to the boy's irritation."
}

