parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "175fabb1f7f8058f5cd9f8b4",
  "rewritten_text": "He explained that while traveling from his home through Datchet, he noticed smoke rising from the inn's chimney. Expecting to find fellow travelers, he knocked and entered. Only Lucy and her mother were present; the older woman was bedridden with rheumatism, and as the remaining local residents departed one by one, they were left behind. Lucy begged the man to stay until her mother recovered in a week or two, warning that they would otherwise perish in their helpless state. The man refused, insisting he had to rejoin his wife and children among the emigrants. As a final plea, Lucy entrusted him with a letter for Idris, which he successfully delivered. Idris read the following with great feeling: Honoured Lady, I trust you will recall and pity me, and I dare hope for your assistance, as I have no other recourse. Forgive my writing, for I am quite distraught. A month ago, my mother lost the use of her limbs. She is improving, and I am certain she could travel within another month using the arrangements you kindly offered. However, everyone has now departed. Three days ago, I visited Samuel Woods, hoping his family might wait for us, but found their home abandoned. I have seen no one since until this man arrived. What will become of us? My mother is too ill to know our true situation. Will you send someone to help? We will surely perish here. If I moved my mother now, she would die on the journey, and even if she recovered, I have no way to navigate the miles to the sea, only to find you in France with the vast, terrifying ocean between us."
}

