  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Elliot looked up with great respect. How delightful! Mrs. Clay cried, though she dared not look at Anne. It is just like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, may I not call them father and son? Oh, I do not forbid anyone from speaking their mind, but I hardly think his attentions are any different from those of other men. My dear Miss Elliot! Mrs. Clay exclaimed, raising her hands and eyes before falling into a convenient silence. Well, my dear Penelope, you need not be so concerned. I invited him, after all. When I learned he was going to visit friends at Thornberry Park tomorrow, I felt sorry for him. Anne admired how well her friend acted, showing such pleasure at the arrival of a man who clearly hindered her own goals. Mrs. Clay must have hated the sight of Mr. Elliot, yet she maintained a pleasant, calm expression, appearing satisfied despite having only half the time to devote to Sir Walter. For Anne, seeing Mr. Elliot enter the room was distressing, and his approach was painful. She had previously suspected he was not always sincere, but now she saw dishonesty in everything he did. His respectful behavior toward her father, which contradicted his earlier words, was repulsive. Knowing his cruel treatment of Mrs. Smith, she could barely stand his smiles or his artificial kindness. She intended to remain cool toward him without causing a scene, hoping to quietly distance herself from the unnecessary intimacy that had developed. She was more guarded and distant than she had been the night before. He tried to spark her curiosity again about where he had heard her praised, hoping for more questions, but the spell was broken. He realized that the excitement of a public room was needed to stir his cousin’s vanity, and he could not achieve that now while surrounded by the others."
