parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "83c5eea80399081b36561361",
  "rewritten_text": "At that moment, our eyes landed on a scrap of parchment, which I initially mistook for paper. It lay half-buried in the sand with one corner exposed. Nearby, I noticed the rotted remains of what appeared to be a ship’s longboat, so weathered that its structure was barely recognizable. Jupiter retrieved the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Shortly after, we encountered Lieutenant G. I showed him the insect, and he eagerly asked to take it to the fort. When I agreed, he quickly shoved the beetle into his waistcoat pocket, leaving behind the parchment I had been holding. Knowing his obsession with natural history, he likely feared I would change my mind. Meanwhile, I must have absentmindedly tucked the parchment into my own pocket. You recall that when I sat down to sketch the beetle, I could not find any paper in its usual place or in the drawer. While searching my pockets for a scrap of paper, I discovered the parchment. I recount these details because the sequence of events struck me as significant. You may think me imaginative, but I had already begun connecting the pieces. A boat lay on the coast, and nearby was a piece of parchment—not paper—bearing a skull. You might ask how they relate, but the skull is a classic pirate emblem, often displayed on their flags. I noted that the material was durable, nearly indestructible parchment, which is rarely used for trivial notes. This suggested the skull held real importance. I also observed that despite a missing corner, the parchment was originally oblong."
}

