parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "90b58f65a43f33ba850cb43b",
  "rewritten_text": "I am dining out, so I will not need you. Thank you, sir, the man replied, exiting the room. Now, Alan, we must hurry. This chest is quite heavy; I will carry it, and you can bring the rest. He spoke with urgent authority, and Campbell felt compelled to obey. They departed together. Upon reaching the landing, Dorian unlocked the door, then paused, his expression darkening with unease. He shuddered, whispering that he could not enter. It makes no difference to me, Campbell replied coldly. Dorian opened the door slightly, catching sight of his portrait leering in the sun. The torn curtain lay on the floor. He recalled failing to conceal the canvas the previous night and nearly rushed forward, but recoiled in horror. A loathsome, wet red dew glistened on one of the hands, as if the painting were sweating blood. It seemed more repulsive than the silent, still form on the table, whose distorted shadow remained fixed on the carpet. Taking a breath, he stepped inside with averted eyes, determined not to look at the corpse. He grabbed the gold-and-purple fabric and threw it over the painting. He stood there, afraid to turn, staring at the pattern of the cloth. He heard Campbell bring in the chest and tools. He wondered if Campbell and Basil Hallward had ever known one another. Leave me, a stern voice commanded. He hurried out, noting the dead man was back in the chair and Campbell was staring at a yellow face. As he descended the stairs, he heard the lock turn. Long after seven, Campbell entered the library, pale but composed. I have finished what you requested, he muttered."
}

