parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5a85eba9890c07724542d07e",
  "rewritten_text": "I walked beside a large, friendly-looking man, hoping to chat and learn more, but he immediately started telling the same old story Sir Dinadan had told me when I first angered Sir Sagramor. I excused myself and fell to the back of the group, feeling deeply depressed. I felt ready to leave this difficult life of struggle and defeat, yet I feared the unknown of eternity, especially knowing how many people there already were who knew that same story. Early that afternoon, we caught up to another group of pilgrims. Unlike the first, there was no laughter or joy here. People of all ages were present, from the elderly to infants, but everyone looked miserable. Their faces showed the deep hopelessness of those who have suffered for a long time. They were slaves. Chains connected their hands and feet to belts around their waists, and everyone but the children was linked together by a long chain running from collar to collar. They had walked three hundred miles in eighteen days on very little food, sleeping huddled together like animals. They wore only rags and were barely clothed. Their heavy irons had caused infected, worm-filled sores on their ankles, and their bare feet were cut and bleeding. Originally a group of one hundred, half had been sold along the way. The man in charge rode a horse and carried a long, knotted whip. He used it to strike anyone who stumbled from exhaustion, forcing them to keep moving without saying a word."
}

