parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8fa9e799c2fb50c40afcbf5f",
  "rewritten_text": "It was well past noon when he woke. His valet had checked on him several times, wondering why his master slept so late. Finally, the bell rang, and Victor entered quietly with tea and a stack of letters on a china tray, pulling back the satin curtains from the tall windows. Monsieur has slept well, he said with a smile. What time is it, Victor? Dorian Gray asked sleepily. It is a quarter past one, Monsieur. How late! He sat up, sipped his tea, and looked through his mail. One letter was from Lord Henry, delivered by hand that morning. He hesitated, then set it aside. He opened the others listlessly, finding the usual invitations to dinners, art shows, and charity concerts that fashionable young men receive daily. There was a large bill for a silver toilet set he had not yet dared show his old-fashioned guardians, who failed to understand that in this age, luxuries are necessities. There were also polite offers from money-lenders in Jermyn Street. After ten minutes, he rose, put on an embroidered silk dressing gown, and went to the onyx bathroom. The cool water refreshed him, and he seemed to have forgotten his recent troubles. A vague memory of some strange tragedy flickered, but it felt like a dream. Once dressed, he went to the library for a light French breakfast at a small table by the open window. It was a beautiful day, the air filled with the scent of spices. A bee buzzed around a bowl of yellow roses. He felt perfectly happy. Suddenly, his gaze landed on the screen hiding the portrait, and he jumped. Is it too cold, Monsieur? his valet asked, placing an omelette on the table. Shall I shut the window? Dorian shook his head. I am not cold, he murmured."
}

