parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c2c7288cce3f30cf9811c3e4",
  "rewritten_text": "I encountered various funeral processions; they were sparsely followed by mourners and viewed by onlookers as signs of terrible misfortune. Some watched these corteges with frantic intensity, others retreated in fear, and some sobbed openly. Adrian’s primary goal, beyond providing immediate aid to the ill, was to conceal the plague’s symptoms and advancement from London’s citizens. He understood that terror and gloomy expectations greatly aided the sickness, as despair made the human body uniquely vulnerable to contagion. Consequently, no unsightly scenes were visible; shops generally remained open, and a level of pedestrian traffic was maintained. Yet, despite the avoidance of a typical plague-stricken city’s appearance, London seemed significantly altered to me, having not seen it since the outbreak began. Carriages were absent, tall grass grew in the thoroughfares, houses looked abandoned, most shutters were drawn, and the people I passed wore a ghastly, fearful expression, quite unlike the usual industrious manner of Londoners. My lone carriage drew attention as it clattered toward the Protectoral Palace, and the elite streets approaching it appeared even more desolate. I discovered Adrian’s antechamber packed, as it was his time for receiving visitors. Reluctant to interrupt his work, I waited, observing the flow of petitioners. They were mostly from the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had vanished with the halt of commerce and the nation’s typical obsession with profit. The newcomers displayed an air of anxiety or terror, which stood in sharp contrast to the resigned, even content, expressions of those who had already been seen. I recognized my friend’s influence in their brisk movements and brightened faces. When the clock struck two, no more were admitted; those turned away departed sullenly or in sorrow, while I proceeded into the audience chamber. I was impressed by the improvement in Adrian’s health. He no longer stooped like a fragile spring flower, which, having grown too rapidly, is burdened by its own blooms. His eyes were clear, his expression calm, and a sense of focused vigor radiated from him, a stark change from his previous lethargy. He sat at a desk with several secretaries organizing petitions or recording notes from the day’s sessions. A few petitioners remained. I marveled at his fairness and patience. He advised those with the means to live outside London to depart at once, providing them with the necessary resources to do so."
}

