parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "66f3a2f598a0442b507c6b27",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Bennet remained silent during dinner, but once the servants left, he engaged his guest by noting how fortunate he was in his patroness. He remarked that Lady Catherine de Bourgh’s attentiveness to his comfort was truly remarkable. Mr. Collins responded with eloquent praise, his manner becoming exceptionally solemn as he insisted he had never encountered such affability from a person of rank. He boasted that she had approved his sermons, invited him to dine at Rosings twice, and even requested his presence for cards. Though some considered her proud, he had only experienced kindness. She treated him like any other gentleman, allowed him to socialize, and permitted him to visit his relatives. She had even advised him to marry discreetly and visited his parsonage, where she offered suggestions for his upstairs closets. Mrs. Bennet agreed that she sounded like a very agreeable woman and asked if she lived nearby. Mr. Collins explained that his home was separated from Rosings Park only by a lane. When Mrs. Bennet asked about her family, he mentioned her only daughter, the heiress to a vast estate. Mrs. Bennet wondered if the girl was handsome, to which Mr. Collins replied that she was charming. He added that Lady Catherine considered her superior to all other women, as her features clearly displayed her distinguished birth."
}

