parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "da38170f2d07bfa134775ab7",
  "rewritten_text": "He was dressed as Jonas the Graveless, the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn, a role that had famously sparked the Cantervilles' feud with Lord Rufford. It was past two in the morning, and the house seemed silent. As he headed toward the library to check for the bloodstain, two figures jumped from the shadows, waving their arms and screaming 'BOO!' in his ear. Terrified, he bolted for the stairs, only to find Washington Otis waiting with a garden syringe. Cornered, he dove into the unlit iron stove and escaped through the flues, reaching his room covered in soot and utterly defeated. He never attempted another midnight haunting. The twins tried to trap him, scattering nutshells in the halls, but he stayed hidden, his pride clearly wounded. Mr. Otis returned to his history of the Democratic Party, Mrs. Otis hosted a successful clam-bake, the boys played American games, and Virginia rode her pony with the visiting Duke of Cheshire. Everyone assumed the ghost had left, and Mr. Otis even wrote to Lord Canterville, who expressed his relief. The family was mistaken, however. The ghost remained, though weakened, and was eager to target the young Duke. His grand-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once bet he could play dice with the ghost, only to be found the next morning paralyzed and unable to speak anything but 'Double Sixes' for the rest of his life."
}

