parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8ca3b2352da94aa47b1ae606",
  "rewritten_text": "I must warn you that I intend to tell Sybil everything. She is joining me for lunch tomorrow to discuss hats, and if Mr. Podgers discovers you have a bad temper, gout, or a secret wife in Bayswater, I will certainly inform her. Lord Arthur smiled and shook his head. I am not afraid, he replied. Sybil knows me as well as I know her. I am sorry to hear that. A successful marriage is built on mutual misunderstanding. I am not being cynical, merely experienced, which is much the same. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile wants his palm read. Do not mention his engagement to one of London’s most beautiful women, as that was in the Morning Post a month ago. Dear Lady Windermere, the Marchioness of Jedburgh cried, let Mr. Podgers stay. He just told me I should be an actress, and I am fascinated. If he said that, I am taking him away. Come here, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur’s hand. Lady Jedburgh pouted as she rose. If I cannot be an actress, I must at least be in the audience. We all will be, Lady Windermere said. Now, Mr. Podgers, tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is a favorite of mine. When Mr. Podgers looked at Lord Arthur’s hand, he turned pale and fell silent. He shuddered, and his bushy eyebrows twitched in a strange, nervous way. Beads of sweat appeared on his yellow forehead, and his fat fingers turned cold and clammy. Lord Arthur noticed this agitation and felt fear for the first time. He wanted to run from the room but forced himself to stay. He preferred to know the worst rather than endure this terrible uncertainty. I am waiting, Mr. Podgers, he said. We are all waiting, Lady Windermere added impatiently, but the palmist remained silent. I suppose Arthur is going on the stage, Lady Jedburgh remarked, and Mr. Podgers is afraid to tell him after your scolding."
}

