parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4177fbf0f5e09105fccfcd7c",
  "rewritten_text": "The row of gas-lamps stretched far into the distance, and a lone hansom cab waited by a small walled house, its driver dozing within. He hurried toward Portland Place, glancing back occasionally as if dreading pursuit. At the corner of Rich Street, two men examined a notice on a hoarding. Driven by sudden curiosity, he crossed the road. The word 'Murder' in bold black type caught his gaze. He recoiled, his face flushing deeply. The bill offered a reward for a man of medium height, aged thirty to forty, dressed in a billy-cock hat, black coat, and check trousers, bearing a scar on his right cheek. He studied the text repeatedly, pondering the fugitive's fate and the origin of his mark. He wondered if his own name might one day appear on London's walls, with a price placed upon his head. The notion filled him with sickening dread. He turned abruptly and vanished into the darkness. He wandered aimlessly, retaining only vague recollections of traversing a maze of squalid dwellings and gloomy streets. By the time he reached Piccadilly Circus, the sun was rising. Walking toward Belgrave Square, he encountered heavy wagons bound for Covent Garden. White-smocked carters with sun-weathered faces and curly hair strode along, snapping whips and calling out. A young boy sat atop a massive grey horse, clutching its mane and laughing, a cluster of primroses tucked into his worn hat. The heaps of vegetables appeared like jade against the morning sky, contrasting with the pink hues of dawn. Lord Arthur felt an inexplicable emotion. The delicate beauty of the morning struck him as profoundly sad, reminding him of days that begin in splendor only to end in turmoil. He contemplated these rustics and their casual demeanor, wondering what version of London they perceived—a city stripped of nocturnal vice and daytime smog, a ghostly, desolate place. He questioned whether they grasped the city's extremes of glory and disgrace, its intense passions, its desperate poverty, and all it creates or destroys from dawn until dusk."
}

