  "rewritten_text": "Long ago, I became close with a man named William Legrand. Descended from an old Huguenot line, he was once affluent, but a string of calamities left him destitute. To escape the shame of his ruin, he departed New Orleans, his ancestral home, and settled on Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina. This island is quite unique, composed mostly of sea sand, stretching three miles long and never exceeding a quarter-mile in width. A barely visible creek, winding through a marshy thicket of reeds and mud favored by marsh hens, separates it from the mainland. Vegetation is sparse or stunted, with no large trees. Near the western tip, where Fort Moultrie and some modest summer cottages for Charleston residents stand, one finds the bristly palmetto. Otherwise, the island is blanketed in thick sweet myrtle, a shrub prized by English gardeners that grows up to twenty feet high, creating a fragrant, dense, and nearly impassable thicket. Deep within this brush, near the island's eastern end, Legrand constructed a small hut where he lived when we first met by chance. Our acquaintance quickly grew into friendship, as the recluse possessed qualities that commanded respect. He was highly educated and brilliant, though plagued by misanthropy and prone to erratic shifts between zeal and gloom. He owned many books but seldom read them, preferring to hunt, fish, or wander the beach and myrtle groves searching for shells and insects—his collection of which would have impressed a naturalist like Swammerdamm. He was usually joined on these outings by an elderly freedman named Jupiter. Despite the family's financial collapse, Jupiter refused to leave his young master, ignoring all threats or entreaties to depart. It is likely that Legrand’s relatives, fearing for his mental stability, encouraged this stubborn loyalty to ensure the wanderer remained under watch."
}

