parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b46b35df784c6c75216b96d7",
  "rewritten_text": "If he asked why I wanted it or what right I had to it, I would tell him—even if he cared little for such dreams—that I had loved Estella for a long time. Though I had lost her and faced a lonely life, everything regarding her remained more precious to me than anything else. Seeing Mr. Jaggers stand silent and unmoved, I turned to Wemmick. I told him I knew he had a kind heart, having seen his pleasant home and the gentle way he cared for his father. I begged him to speak for me and convince Mr. Jaggers to be more open. The two men exchanged a strange look. I feared Wemmick might be fired, but Mr. Jaggers softened into a smile, and Wemmick grew bolder. Mr. Jaggers teased him about his home and his father, to which Wemmick replied that his private life did not affect his work. Mr. Jaggers laughed, calling Wemmick a cunning impostor, and Wemmick playfully accused him of being one too. They looked at each other with lingering suspicion. Mr. Jaggers sighed, admitting that I knew more about dreams than he did. He then offered to present a hypothetical case, insisting he admitted nothing. He waited for my confirmation before continuing. He asked me to imagine a woman who hid her child and was forced to reveal this to her lawyer, who needed the truth to build her defense."
}

