  "request_id": "83810702c311e70cd2070a79",
  "rewritten_text": "Keeping in mind the strange voice, the remarkable agility, and the lack of motive in this horrific crime, let us examine the murder itself. A woman was strangled by hand and shoved headfirst into a chimney. Such methods are not used by typical killers, nor do they dispose of victims in this manner. The act of forcing the body into the flue was bizarre and defies human logic, even for the most depraved. Consider the immense strength required to wedge the corpse so tightly that it took several men to pull it out. Consider also the evidence of extraordinary power. Thick clumps of grey hair were ripped out by the roots, leaving scalp tissue attached—a sign of immense force. The victim’s throat was not just cut; her head was severed with a simple razor. Note the savage cruelty involved. The bruises on Madame L’Espanaye were caused by the stone pavement, as she fell from the window. The police missed this simple fact because their focus on the nails blinded them to the possibility that the window had been opened. When you combine the chaotic state of the room with the astounding agility, superhuman strength, brutal ferocity, lack of motive, grotesque horror, and the unintelligible, foreign voice, the evidence points to a singular conclusion."
}

