parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9dbf027faa4212c18b8afea1",
  "rewritten_text": "He was a man of slight stature yet stood remarkably straight, possessing the most melodic voice I had ever encountered. He opened his lecture by summarizing the history of chemistry and the advancements of various scholars, speaking the names of these notable pioneers with great passion. He then provided a brief overview of the field's current state and clarified several fundamental concepts. Following a few introductory demonstrations, he ended with a tribute to modern chemistry, the substance of which remains etched in my memory: The early practitioners of this discipline promised the impossible and achieved nothing. Modern experts promise little; they recognize that metal transmutation is impossible and the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these thinkers, whose hands appear suited only for filth and whose eyes are fixed upon microscopes or crucibles, have truly performed wonders. They delve into nature's secrets, revealing her hidden operations. They reach toward the heavens, having mapped the circulation of blood and the composition of the air. They have gained vast, nearly boundless capabilities; they can summon lightning, replicate earthquakes, and even imitate the unseen world with its own reflections. These were the professor's words—or rather, the words of fate—spoken to seal my doom. As he spoke, I felt as though my spirit were wrestling with a tangible foe; the various components of my character were being stirred one by one. My mind became consumed by a single, driving ambition. So much has been accomplished, the soul of Frankenstein cried out, yet I shall achieve far more. Following the established path, I will forge a new trail, investigate hidden forces, and reveal the profound mysteries of existence to the world. I did not sleep that night. My inner self was in a state of chaotic upheaval, and though I sensed that order might eventually emerge, I lacked the strength to bring it forth. Eventually, as dawn broke, slumber arrived. Upon waking, my thoughts from the previous night seemed like a dream. Only a firm resolve remained to return to my former studies and dedicate myself to a science for which I felt a natural aptitude. That same day, I visited M. Waldman. His private demeanor was even gentler and more engaging than his public persona, as the formal dignity he displayed during his lecture was replaced at home by immense warmth and kindness."
}

