parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6c5e0bc5109a360950ae433f",
  "rewritten_text": "Listen! Monks replied. When his father fell ill in Rome, my mother, from whom he had long been separated, traveled from Paris with me to manage his affairs, though there was little affection between them. He was unconscious when we arrived and died the next day. Among his papers were two documents addressed to you, Mr. Brownlow, with instructions to deliver them only after his death. One was a letter to Agnes, and the other was a will. What of the letter? Mr. Brownlow asked. It was a long, desperate confession, Monks said. He had deceived Agnes, claiming a secret mystery prevented their marriage. She trusted him until it was too late. She was pregnant at the time. He wrote that if he died, she should not curse his memory or fear for their child, as the guilt was entirely his. He mentioned the locket and ring he had given her, begging her to keep them close, and his writing became frantic and incoherent, as if he had lost his mind. I believe he had. The will, Mr. Brownlow said as Oliver wept, was written in the same spirit. He spoke of the misery his wife had caused, and of your own malice and hatred, you being his only son. He left you and your mother each an annuity of eight hundred pounds. The rest of his estate he divided equally between Agnes Fleming and their child, provided the child lived to come of age."
}

