  "rewritten_text": "We declared ourselves free men so loudly that a patriotic orator and his crowd gathered around us, looking quite aggressive. The orator announced that if we were truly free, Britain's liberties would protect us, and demanded we provide proof. When I hesitated, the king shouted that the man accusing us should be the one to prove we were not free. The king understood the law only in theory, not in practice. The crowd grew bored, and the orator explained that since we were strangers, the law required us to prove our own freedom. I asked for time to send for help, but the orator refused, claiming it would be too inconvenient for our master. When the king began to shout that he had no master, I silenced him before he could reveal his identity and appear insane. There is no need to detail the rest; the earl auctioned us off. This same cruel law existed in the American South centuries later, but it only felt truly hellish once I was the one on the auction block. We were sold like livestock. Because the town was so quiet, we fetched a price that still embarrasses me today."
}

