parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "da4ad88b41ab24d461fe0a72",
  "rewritten_text": "Emma found no cause to revise her negative assessment of Mrs. Elton. Her initial judgment held firm; every subsequent encounter confirmed that the woman was arrogant, presumptuous, overly familiar, unrefined, and ignorant. Though possessing minor beauty and skills, Mrs. Elton lacked the sense to realize she was not the sophisticated social arbiter she imagined herself to be, nor was her previous status as Miss Hawkins as grand as she claimed. Mr. Elton appeared entirely aligned with his wife, seemingly proud to have introduced such a woman to Highbury, whom he clearly viewed as superior even to Miss Woodhouse. Most of the neighborhood, influenced by Miss Bates’s enthusiasm or a general tendency toward easy acceptance, praised the bride, while Emma maintained a polite, if hollow, facade by describing her as pleasant and well-dressed. Over time, Mrs. Elton’s attitude toward Emma soured, likely due to the lack of warmth her overtures received. This growing distance was welcome to Emma, though it only deepened her underlying resentment. Furthermore, the Eltons treated Harriet with blatant disdain and neglect. Emma hoped this would help Harriet move on, yet the malice behind their behavior lowered her opinion of them both. It was evident that Harriet’s past feelings had been shared as fodder for their private amusement, with Emma’s own role distorted to suit their narrative. Consequently, the couple made Emma a frequent target of their shared hostility, expressing their enmity through the mistreatment of her friend. Meanwhile, Mrs. Elton developed an immediate and intense fixation on Jane Fairfax. She was not content with mere admiration; she insisted on offering unsolicited patronage and support, a crusade she began detailing to Emma long before their own relationship had fully deteriorated."
}

