parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6c6c5e01eaafa086d124fb81",
  "rewritten_text": "Reading his letter again made Harriet so sympathetic that Emma had to use firm language to keep her focused. Harriet worried so much about hurting him, and about what his family would think of her, that Emma feared she might have accepted him if he had appeared right then. However, the letter was written, sealed, and sent. The matter was settled, and Harriet was safe. She felt down that evening, but Emma understood her gentle regrets, comforting her by talking of their friendship or mentioning Mr. Elton. I shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again, Harriet said sadly. Even if you were, I could not bear to part with you, Emma replied. You are far too important to Hartfield to be spared. I would never want to go there anyway, because I am only happy at Hartfield, Harriet said. Later, she wondered if Mrs. Goddard or Miss Nash would be surprised, noting that Miss Nash thought her own sister was well-married to a mere shopkeeper. Emma dismissed this, suggesting that Miss Nash would likely envy Harriet’s prospects. She added that Mr. Elton’s interest was still a secret known only to them. Harriet blushed, pleased by the thought of Mr. Elton, yet she still felt pity for Mr. Martin. Now that he has my letter, she whispered, I wonder if he is unhappy, and if his sisters know. I hope he is not too upset. Let us think of happier things, Emma interrupted. Perhaps Mr. Elton is showing your portrait to his family right now, telling them how much more beautiful you are, and finally sharing your name with them."
}

