parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "210d8cc57acc3458bc1a1fce",
  "rewritten_text": "Mrs. Otis, who is quite an expert on art after spending several winters in Boston as a girl, tells me these jewels are very valuable. Under these circumstances, Lord Canterville, I am sure you understand that I cannot let my family keep them. Such flashy trinkets, while perhaps fitting for the British aristocracy, are entirely out of place for those raised on the strict, timeless principles of republican simplicity. Virginia would very much like to keep the box as a keepsake of your unfortunate ancestor. Since it is quite old and damaged, I hope you will agree. I am surprised my daughter feels any sympathy for medieval customs, though I suppose it is because she was born in a London suburb shortly after her mother returned from a trip to Athens. Lord Canterville listened seriously, tugging at his grey moustache to hide a smile. When Mr. Otis finished, he shook his hand warmly and said, My dear sir, your daughter did my ancestor, Sir Simon, a great service, and my family owes her a debt of gratitude for her courage. The jewels are hers. If I were heartless enough to take them, I suspect the old ghost would rise from his grave within two weeks to make my life miserable. These items are not legal heirlooms, as they were never mentioned in a will and their existence was unknown. I have no more claim to them than your butler does. When Virginia grows up, I am sure she will enjoy wearing them. Besides, you bought the furniture and the ghost together; legally, anything belonging to Sir Simon became yours when you purchased the property, as he was officially dead regardless of his nightly activities in the hallway."
}

