parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6f8facae67b023a89bcc3e67",
  "rewritten_text": "She was right about the rats. Every so often, one would poke its nose out from a corner hole. She explained that she kept objects nearby to throw at them when alone, or they would never leave her in peace. She showed me a knotted bar of lead, claiming she was usually a good shot, but a recent arm injury made her aim uncertain. She waited for an opening and threw it at a rat, missing widely and crying out in pain. She then asked me to try for the next one. I wanted to leave before her husband returned, but I kept that to myself. I took the lead, and when the next rat appeared, I threw it; had the rat stayed put, it would have been badly hurt. She praised my aim and fetched the lead, along with a hank of yarn for me to hold. As I held the yarn, she spoke of her marriage, though she paused to warn me to keep an eye on the rats and keep the lead in my lap. She dropped the metal into my lap, and I clamped my legs together over it. Moments later, she removed the yarn, looked me in the eye, and asked for my real name. I trembled, unsure how to respond. I asked her not to tease a poor girl, offering to leave if I were a burden. She insisted I stay, promising she would not hurt or betray me. She urged me to trust her with my secret, assuming I was merely a runaway apprentice who had been treated poorly. She assured me there was no harm in it and that she would help me."
}

