parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2d85a9cca098390d9970cba1",
  "rewritten_text": "She studied me intently for a moment before asking softly, \"What is your price for them?\" I replied, \"Only that you distinguish them from the rest. Though they share the same blood, they possess a different nature.\" Miss Havisham persisted, \"What do you want for them?\" Feeling my face flush, I answered, \"I am not clever enough to conceal that I do want something. If you would provide the funds to assist my friend Herbert in a way he must never discover, I could explain how.\" She rested her hands on her cane to watch me more closely and asked, \"Why must it remain secret?\" I told her, \"Because I began this aid two years ago without his awareness, and I cannot be exposed. My inability to complete it involves a secret that is not mine to share.\" She turned her gaze toward the fire, watching the embers fade in the candlelight. When the coals shifted, she looked back at me with renewed focus. As Estella continued knitting, Miss Havisham asked, \"What else?\" I turned to Estella, my voice wavering, and said, \"You know I have loved you long and deeply.\" She looked up, her expression indifferent as her needles kept moving. Miss Havisham watched us both. I continued, \"I would have spoken sooner, but I mistakenly believed Miss Havisham intended us for each other. I held back while I thought you had no choice in the matter, but I must speak now.\" Estella shook her head without pausing her work. I replied to her gesture, \"I understand. I have no expectation that you will ever be mine, Estella.\""
}

