  "rewritten_text": "He kept one secret from his close friends, a secret he had held since childhood. Although he loved his peers, he could not trust them with his feelings. He was deeply in love and feared they would mock him, treating his profound passion as something trivial. A man of modest means lived near his family home and had three beautiful daughters. The eldest was the most striking, possessing both a sharp mind and a gentle spirit. She and my father had been childhood playmates, and his mother had always favored her. During his school breaks, they were inseparable. Influenced by novels and his own sensitive nature, he began speaking of love to her when he was only eleven. Though she was two years older, her upbringing left her more naive. She accepted his declarations innocently, not fully grasping their meaning. By the time she understood the nature of their bond, she was already devoted to him, fearing only that he might eventually lose interest. Their love grew more intense and tender over time, becoming an inseparable part of his life. They kept their relationship private, and despite his fear that his friends might judge him for loving someone of lower social standing, he remained determined to marry her as soon as he could overcome the necessary obstacles. Diana was truly worthy of his devotion."
}

