parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cfb5d021306a4d51a9ebd6e1",
  "rewritten_text": "The doctor was an elderly, kind-looking man once I woke him. I explained that my brother and I had been hunting on Spanish Island yesterday, camping on a raft we found. Around midnight, he must have kicked his gun in his sleep, causing it to discharge and wound his leg. I asked the doctor to treat him secretly so we could return home that evening and surprise our family. When he asked who my family was, I told him the Phelpses. He paused, then asked again how the shooting happened. I repeated that a dream caused the gun to fire, which he called a strange dream. He grabbed his lantern and saddlebags, but hesitated when he saw my canoe, doubting it was safe for two people. I insisted it had carried the three of us—my brother, myself, and the guns—without issue. He remained skeptical and tried to find a larger boat, but since all others were locked, he took mine. He suggested I wait or head home to prepare for the surprise, but I declined and gave him directions to the raft instead. Once he left, I worried about what we would do if the injury took days to heal. I decided that if he needed to return, I would follow him, even if I had to swim, and we would hold him captive until Tom was treated, then pay him and let him go. I fell asleep in a lumber pile and woke to find the sun high in the sky. I rushed to the doctor’s house, only to learn he had left during the night and had not yet returned."
}

