  "rewritten_text": "I swear by the sun, the blue sky, and the fire of love in my heart, that if you grant my request, you will never see me again as long as they live. Go home and begin your work; I will watch your progress with intense anxiety. Do not fear; I will appear when you are ready. With that, he left, perhaps afraid I might change my mind. I watched him descend the mountain faster than an eagle, quickly vanishing into the icy landscape. His story had taken all day, and the sun was setting as he left. I knew I should hurry down to the valley before darkness fell, but my heart was heavy and my pace slow. Navigating the mountain paths was difficult, as I was preoccupied with the day's events. It was late when I reached the halfway point and sat by the fountain. Stars flickered between passing clouds, and the dark pines and fallen trees created a solemn, unsettling scene. I wept bitterly, wringing my hands in agony, and cried out to the stars, clouds, and winds, begging them to either end my consciousness or leave me in darkness. These were desperate thoughts, but I cannot explain how the constant starlight burdened me, or how I feared every gust of wind as if it were a destructive force. Morning had broken by the time I reached Chamounix. I did not rest, but returned immediately to Geneva. I could not articulate my feelings; they weighed on me like a mountain, crushing my spirit. I returned home and faced my family. My haggard, wild appearance alarmed them, but I could barely speak or answer their questions. I felt as though I were cursed, unworthy of their sympathy, and forever barred from their companionship."
}

