parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "482e3f1af6642cb82fec6a24",
  "rewritten_text": "The line of gas lamps curved into the distance, and a lone hansom cab waited outside a small walled house with its driver asleep inside. He walked quickly toward Portland Place, glancing back occasionally as if afraid of being followed. At the corner of Rich Street, two men were reading a notice on a wooden fence. Driven by curiosity, he crossed the street. As he approached, the word 'Murder' in bold black letters caught his eye. He jumped, his face flushing. It was a reward notice for a man of medium height, aged thirty to forty, wearing a bowler hat, black coat, and plaid trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it repeatedly, wondering if the man would be caught and how he had earned his scar. He feared that one day his own name might be posted on London walls, with a price on his head. The thought sickened him, and he hurried away into the night. He wandered aimlessly through a maze of grim streets, eventually reaching Piccadilly Circus at dawn. Walking toward Belgrave Square, he passed wagons heading to Covent Garden. The sunburnt carters in white smocks strode along, cracking whips and calling to one another. A chubby boy sat on a large grey horse, laughing while holding the mane, a bunch of primroses tucked into his hat. The piles of vegetables looked like jade against the pink morning sky. Lord Arthur felt strangely moved. The delicate beauty of the dawn seemed deeply sad, reminding him that days often begin in beauty and end in storms. He wondered what these simple, good-humored workers thought of the city. To them, London appeared as a pale, ghostly place, free from the night's sins and the day's smoke. He pondered whether they understood the city's hidden splendor and shame, its intense joys, its desperate hunger, and all it creates or destroys from morning until night."
}

