  "request_id": "36cf5a2940a0f9fdde77a772",
  "rewritten_text": "Lydia’s congratulatory letter to Elizabeth revealed that she, if not her husband, hoped for financial support. She wrote: My dear Lizzy, I wish you joy. If you love Mr. Darcy half as much as I love my dear Wickham, you must be very happy. It is a comfort that you are so rich; please think of us when you have time. I am sure Wickham would love a government position, and we will struggle to live without help. Anything paying three or four hundred a year would be fine, but do not ask Mr. Darcy if you would rather not. Elizabeth certainly preferred not to ask, and she firmly discouraged such requests in her reply. However, she often sent them money saved from her own personal expenses. She knew their income could never support two such extravagant and careless people. Whenever they moved, they asked Jane or Elizabeth for help paying their debts. Their lifestyle remained unstable even after they settled down; they constantly moved to find cheaper housing while overspending. Wickham’s affection for Lydia soon faded, though hers lasted longer. Despite her flaws, she kept the social standing her marriage provided. While Darcy would never welcome Wickham to Pemberley, he helped him advance his career for Elizabeth’s sake. Lydia occasionally visited when her husband was away in London or Bath. They also stayed with the Bingleys so often that even the good-natured Bingley eventually considered asking them to leave. Miss Bingley was deeply upset by Darcy’s marriage, but she chose to hide her resentment to keep visiting Pemberley. She became even closer to Georgiana, remained attentive to Darcy, and was unfailingly polite to Elizabeth."
}

