parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1cac56c7c3a98d56748f9f95",
  "rewritten_text": "Once my tears subsided, reason and memory returned, allowing me to reflect calmly on my situation. Though only hours had passed, my world had undergone a profound transformation; it felt as if years had elapsed since morning. My father was dead to me, and I felt as though I were mourning his actual passing. Yet, he was not dead to the world, only to me. I had to flee from him more urgently than from any enemy, never to see or speak to him again. The agony of this realization left me breathless, unable to think clearly. I resolved to live in total seclusion, perhaps moving to the Continent to join a convent—not for religious reasons, but to escape the world entirely and find a place to weep in silence. But what of my father? Would he ever overcome the fierce passion that now consumed him? Perhaps, years from now, when age had cooled his intensity, he might be a father to me again. This thought brought a melancholy smile to my lips and allowed me to hope. Time would pass, and though it might be difficult, I had not lost him forever. Let him wander in desolation for another sixteen years; let him cry out to the forests and waterfalls of distant lands; let him endure hardship and the elements. I consign you to this life, my miserable father. Go, live among savages, sleep beneath the open sky, let your body wither and your heart grow cold, until all your youth has vanished."
}

