  "request_id": "f77a027a497bc26485b6a974",
  "rewritten_text": "Years ago, I became close friends with William Legrand. He came from an old, wealthy Huguenot family, but a string of bad luck left him penniless. To escape the embarrassment of his situation, he left his home in New Orleans and moved to Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina. This island is quite unique. It is a narrow strip of sea sand about three miles long and less than a quarter-mile wide. A small, muddy creek separates it from the mainland, where marsh birds often gather. The plant life is sparse and stunted. Aside from some palmettos near the western end—where Fort Moultrie and a few summer cottages stand—the island is covered in thick, fragrant myrtle bushes that grow up to twenty feet tall, creating a dense, tangled forest. Legrand built a small hut deep within this thicket, where I first met him by chance. Our acquaintance soon grew into a deep friendship, as I found him to be a highly intelligent man, though he was also a recluse prone to sudden shifts between intense excitement and deep sadness. He owned many books but rarely read them, preferring to spend his time hunting, fishing, or walking the beach to collect shells and insects. His collection of bugs would have impressed any expert. He was usually joined by an elderly man named Jupiter, a former slave who had been freed before the family lost their fortune. Jupiter refused to leave Legrand’s side, regardless of any threats or promises. It is likely that Legrand’s family, worried about his mental state, encouraged Jupiter to stay and keep a watchful eye on him."
}

