  "request_id": "de6fe80d056d24f2651d1b78",
  "rewritten_text": "You have encouraged a spirit in him that is unsuitable for his station, ma'am. The board members are practical men, and they would tell you that paupers have no need for soul or spirit. It is enough that we provide them with bodies. If you had fed the boy only gruel, this would not have happened. Oh dear, Mrs. Sowerberry sighed, looking toward the ceiling. This is what comes of being generous. Her generosity had actually consisted of giving Oliver the scraps no one else would eat, so she showed great humility in accepting Mr. Bumble’s false accusation. The only solution now, Mr. Bumble continued, is to leave him in the cellar until he is starved into submission, then keep him on gruel for the rest of his apprenticeship. He comes from a bad family. His mother arrived here after enduring hardships that would have killed any decent woman weeks earlier. Hearing his mother mentioned, Oliver began kicking the door so violently that no other sound could be heard. Mr. Sowerberry returned just then, and after hearing an exaggerated account of Oliver’s behavior, he unlocked the cellar and dragged the boy out by his collar. Oliver’s clothes were torn, his face was bruised, and his hair was disheveled, but he still glared defiantly at Noah. You are a fine young man, aren't you? Sowerberry said, shaking him and boxing his ears. He insulted my mother, Oliver replied. What if he did, you ungrateful wretch? Mrs. Sowerberry cried. She deserved what he said and worse. She did not, Oliver insisted. She did, she countered. It is a lie, Oliver shouted. Mrs. Sowerberry burst into tears. This left Mr. Sowerberry no choice. If he had hesitated to punish Oliver severely, he would have been branded a brute and a terrible husband by all standards of marital disputes."
}

