parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dea9b0a0738c4dc3d312a7bd",
  "rewritten_text": "Emma did not regret her decision to attend the Coles' party. The visit left her with pleasant memories, and she felt the boost to her popularity more than compensated for any loss of social exclusivity. She had certainly delighted the Coles, who were good people, and she was sure her presence would be remembered for some time. Yet, perfect satisfaction is rare, and two things troubled her. She wondered if she had acted improperly by sharing her suspicions about Jane Fairfax’s feelings with Frank Churchill. It was a lapse, but the idea had been so compelling that she could not help but speak, and Frank’s agreement with her assessment was such a flattering validation of her insight that she struggled to regret it entirely. Her second regret was clearer: she genuinely felt inferior to Jane in musical ability. She lamented her own lack of childhood discipline and immediately sat down to practice for ninety minutes. Harriet then arrived, and if her praise could have sufficed, Emma would have been comforted. Oh, if I could play like you and Miss Fairfax, Harriet sighed. Do not compare us, Emma replied. My playing is like a lamp compared to her sunshine. Oh, I think you are just as good, Harriet insisted. Everyone last night said how well you played. Those who understood music knew the difference, Emma said. My playing is merely good enough to earn praise, but Jane’s is far superior. Harriet persisted, arguing that Mr. Cole and Frank Churchill had both praised Emma’s taste, which Frank valued more than technical skill. But Jane has both, Emma countered. Harriet remained unconvinced, noting that Jane’s skill was expected since she would eventually have to teach for a living."
}

