  "rewritten_text": "That powerful, unnatural voice—devoid of any mechanical origin—ceased its resonant vibration, and the young woman, turning to help her elderly companion, finally noticed our presence. It was her father, whom she had guided since her youth. They were Saxons who had settled nearby only a few years prior. When the plague emerged, a young German student had joined them. Their story was clear: a nobleman who loved the musician’s daughter and followed them to escape his family’s disapproval, only to be claimed by the pestilence. She survived solely for her father, shielding him from the truth of their isolation so he believed his music still reached an audience beyond the silent landscape. On the day we arrived, she fell ill. Terrified of leaving her blind father alone, she summoned her remaining strength to lead him to the chapel at vespers, performing a hymn to the earth that would soon become her grave. Seeing us felt like a divine intervention; her fragile resolve shattered, and she collapsed at Adrian’s feet, begging him to protect her father. Sadly, she and her father now rest together under the walnut tree beside her lover. Upon realizing her condition, the father held her hand until she passed, remaining motionless until he too succumbed twelve hours later. They lie beneath that tree, framed by the Jura mountains and the distant Alps, while the church spire rises above the trees, and I like to imagine their favorite music still lingers to comfort their spirits."
}

