parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6a0b3a67386d89cd6c22246c",
  "rewritten_text": "I wrapped him in paper and stuffed a piece in his mouth—that was the method. And you believe your master was truly bitten by the beetle, and that the bite caused his illness? I do not think anything about it—I know it. Why would he dream of gold if not for the gold-bug bite? I have heard of those gold-bugs before. But how do you know he dreams of gold? How do I know? Because he speaks of it while sleeping—that is how I know. Well, Jup, perhaps you are correct; but to what lucky event do I owe the pleasure of your visit today? What is the matter, master? Did you bring a message from Mr. Legrand? No, master, I brought this letter. Jupiter handed me a note that read: My dear friend, why have I not seen you lately? I hope you have not been foolish enough to take offense at my brief rudeness; though that is unlikely. Since we last met, I have been quite anxious. I have something to tell you, though I hardly know how to say it, or if I should at all. I have been unwell for days, and poor old Jup irritates me beyond measure with his well-intentioned care. Would you believe he prepared a large stick to punish me for slipping away to spend the day alone in the hills? I truly believe my sickly appearance saved me from a beating. I have added nothing to my collection since we met. If you can manage it, please come over with Jupiter. Do come. I must see you tonight regarding a matter of the highest importance. Ever yours, William Legrand. The tone of this note caused me significant distress. Its style was entirely unlike Legrand. What could he be imagining? What new obsession gripped his excitable mind? What business could be so vital? Jupiter’s report boded ill. I feared that persistent misfortune had finally unhinged my friend’s sanity. Without hesitation, I prepared to accompany the man. Upon reaching the dock, I observed a scythe and three new spades resting in the boat. What is the meaning of this, Jup?"
}

