parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4039f591d588dfc11d6dc94d",
  "rewritten_text": "She was discovered deceased in her bed the following morning, seemingly having passed without suffering. Sir Mathew Reid was summoned immediately, yet nothing could be done, and her interment was scheduled for the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. Shortly before her passing, she had drafted a will bequeathing Lord Arthur her Curzon Street residence, along with her furnishings, belongings, and artwork, excluding her miniatures, which were left to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, intended for Sybil Merton. The estate held little worth, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, urged Lord Arthur to return promptly to settle numerous outstanding debts, as Lady Clementina had never maintained orderly records. Lord Arthur was deeply moved by her generosity and felt Mr. Podgers bore significant responsibility for the situation. Nevertheless, his devotion to Sybil eclipsed all other feelings, and the knowledge that he had fulfilled his obligation brought him solace. Upon reaching Charing Cross, he felt entirely content. The Mertons welcomed him warmly. Sybil secured his promise that nothing would ever again separate them, and they set their wedding date for June 7th. Life appeared radiant once more, and his former joy returned. One day, while inspecting the Curzon Street house with Lady Clementina’s solicitor and Sybil, they burned bundles of old letters and cleared out drawers of clutter. Suddenly, the young woman exclaimed in delight. \"What have you discovered, Sybil?\" Lord Arthur asked, smiling as he looked up from his task. \"This charming little silver bonbonnière, Arthur. It is so quaint and Dutch. Please let me have it! I know amethysts won't suit me until I am over eighty.\" It was the container that had held the aconitine. Lord Arthur recoiled, a slight flush coloring his face. He had nearly forgotten his actions, and it struck him as a strange coincidence that Sybil, for whom he had endured such profound distress, should be the one to bring it to mind. \"Certainly, you may have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\" \"Oh, thank you, Arthur; and may I keep the bonbon inside? I had no idea Lady Clementina enjoyed sweets. I assumed she was far too intellectual.\" Lord Arthur turned deathly pale as a terrifying thought struck him."
}

