parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ac8d4577fc3b70aec227bf43",
  "rewritten_text": "When we returned to the raft and the king counted his earnings, he had eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He also brought back a three-gallon jug of whiskey he found under a wagon while walking through the woods. The king claimed it was his most successful day of missionary work, insisting that heathens were no match for pirates when it came to swindling a camp meeting. The duke had felt proud of his own efforts until the king arrived. He had printed two horse-sale flyers for farmers, earning four dollars. He also collected ten dollars in advertising fees by offering a discount for advance payment. He sold three newspaper subscriptions for half a dollar each, insisting on cash instead of the usual payment of wood or onions. He even wrote and typeset a sad three-verse poem titled, \"Yes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart,\" which he included for free. In total, he made nine dollars and a half and felt satisfied with his day. Then, he showed us a flyer he had printed for our own use. It featured a picture of a runaway slave with a bundle on a stick and offered a two-hundred-dollar reward. The text described Jim perfectly, claiming he had escaped from a plantation near New Orleans and was heading north. The duke explained that this would allow us to travel during the day. If we saw anyone approaching, we could tie Jim up, hide him in the shelter, and show the flyer to claim we had captured him and were taking him back to collect the reward."
}

