parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eb9d69828151171d6d622f00",
  "rewritten_text": "With no labor to occupy him, he rested his right knuckles in his left palm, then swapped them, then stroked his beard, repeating these motions rhythmically without pause. Bringing him back from the aimless wandering that followed his speech felt like rousing someone from a faint or trying to catch the final words of a dying soul. Did you inquire about my name? I certainly did. One Hundred and Five, North Tower. Is that everything? One Hundred and Five, North Tower. With a sound that was neither sigh nor groan, he returned to his work until the quiet was interrupted once more. You are not a shoemaker by profession? Mr. Lorry asked, watching him closely. His hollow eyes shifted to Defarge for assistance, but finding none, they returned to the questioner after glancing at the floor. I am not a shoemaker by trade? No, I was not. I learned it here. I taught myself. I requested permission to... He drifted off, continuing his repetitive hand motions for several minutes. Eventually, his gaze returned to the face before him, and he started, speaking like someone just roused from sleep about a topic from the previous night. I asked for permission to teach myself, which I finally obtained after much struggle, and I have produced shoes ever since. As he reached for the shoe that had been removed, Mr. Lorry asked, while maintaining steady eye contact: Monsieur Manette, do you recall anything of me? The shoe fell, and he stared intently at the man. Monsieur Manette, Mr. Lorry said, touching Defarge’s arm, do you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Does no memory of an old banker, a past business, a former servant, or a bygone time stir within you, Monsieur Manette? As the long-term prisoner stared alternately at Mr. Lorry and Defarge, faint traces of a once-sharp intellect emerged briefly on his forehead through the dark haze that clouded him. They were obscured again, fading away, but for a moment, they had been visible."
}

