  "request_id": "b45aad3a650bcdccd09ae9bd",
  "rewritten_text": "Sir Arnold and Sir Gauter, knights of the castle, clashed with Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay, shattering their spears upon impact. Then, Sir Pertolope emerged from the castle to face Sir Lionel, whom the Green Knight promptly unhorsed. Heralds carefully recorded the performance of every knight. Sir Bleobaris broke his spear against Sir Gareth but was himself knocked to the ground. Seeing this, Sir Galihodin challenged Gareth, only to be struck down as well. Sir Galihud then sought to avenge his brother, but Gareth defeated him just as he had the others. Gareth continued his streak, unhorsing Sir Dinadan, La Cote Male Taile, Sir Sagramore, and Sir Dodinas with a single spear each. King Agwisance of Ireland watched in wonder, noting that the knight seemed to change his colors from green to blue with every pass, making him impossible to identify. Gareth then unhorsed King Agwisance, King Carados of Scotland, King Uriens of Gore, and Sir Bagdemagus. When Bagdemagus’s son, Meliganus, broke a spear against him, Prince Galahault shouted for the multicolored knight to prepare for a duel. They collided, and while the prince’s spear shattered, Gareth struck him so hard that he nearly fell from his horse. King Arthur praised the mysterious knight, asking Sir Launcelot to challenge him. Launcelot refused, arguing that the knight had already endured enough for one day. He insisted that a true knight should not hinder another’s glory, especially after such a display of skill. Launcelot suggested the man might be fighting for a lady’s favor and deserved to keep his hard-earned honor, declaring that he would not interfere."
}

