  "request_id": "5b1a301a8999c1722826e883",
  "rewritten_text": "It was Lady Windermere’s final reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was packed. Six Cabinet Ministers arrived from the Speaker’s Levée in their formal regalia, while the most fashionable women wore their finest attire. At the end of the gallery, Princess Sophia of Carlsrühe, a stout woman with small black eyes and stunning emeralds, spoke loud, broken French and laughed boisterously. It was a remarkable mix of guests. Wealthy peeresses spoke to radical politicians, popular preachers mingled with famous skeptics, bishops trailed a prima donna, and artists stood on the staircase. It was one of Lady Windermere’s most successful evenings, and the Princess remained until nearly eleven-thirty. Once she left, Lady Windermere returned to the gallery, where a political economist lectured a frustrated Hungarian musician on the science of music, and she began speaking with the Duchess of Paisley. Lady Windermere looked stunning, with her ivory throat, blue eyes, and heavy coils of golden hair. Her hair was a rich, deep gold—not the pale straw shade often called gold today, but a color like sunbeams or amber. It gave her the appearance of a saint, yet with the allure of a sinner. She was a fascinating study. Early on, she realized that nothing mimics innocence quite like an indiscretion, and through various reckless, mostly harmless escapades, she had earned the freedom of a unique reputation. She had married three times, but because she kept the same lover, society had stopped gossiping about her. Now forty and childless, she possessed the insatiable hunger for pleasure that keeps one young. Suddenly, she scanned the room and asked in a clear voice, “Where is my palm reader?” “Your what, Gladys?” the Duchess asked, startled. “My palm reader, Duchess; I cannot live without him right now.” “Dear Gladys, you are always so original,” the Duchess murmured, hoping a palm reader was not the same thing as a foot doctor. “He examines my hand twice a week,” Lady Windermere continued, “and he is absolutely fascinating.”"
}

