parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f1f221a9db9abda0f2684867",
  "rewritten_text": "One more word, Rose! Dearest Rose, just one! Harry cried, blocking her path. If I had been less fortunate—if a quiet, simple life had been my fate, or if I had been poor, sick, or helpless—would you have turned away? Or is it my potential wealth and status that causes your hesitation? Do not force me to answer, Rose replied. That situation does not exist and never will. It is unfair to push me. If your answer is what I hope, it will bring light to my lonely life, Harry insisted. It is not a small thing to offer such comfort to someone who loves you above all else. In the name of my deep devotion and all I have suffered for you, please answer me. If your life had been different, Rose said, if you had been only slightly above my station, and I could have been a comfort to you in a humble, peaceful home rather than a burden in your ambitious social circles, I would have been spared this pain. I have every reason to be happy now, but I admit I would have been happier then. Memories of old hopes from her girlhood flooded her mind as she spoke, bringing tears that offered some relief. I cannot help this weakness, but it makes my resolve stronger, Rose said, reaching out her hand. I must leave you now. I ask for one promise, Harry said. Within a year, I may speak to you on this subject one last time. Not to pressure me to change my mind, Rose replied with a sad smile, because that would be useless. No, Harry said. Just to hear you repeat your decision one final time. I will offer you whatever status or fortune I have, and if you still refuse, I will not try to change your mind again. Then let it be so, Rose agreed. It is only one more moment of pain, and by then, I may be better able to handle it. She held out her hand once more."
}

