parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cc84bdec23079446feebabb1",
  "rewritten_text": "Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall in Somersetshire read only one book for pleasure: the Baronetage. It occupied his idle hours and comforted him in times of trouble. He admired the oldest entries and looked down upon the newer ones with pity. Most of all, he loved reading his own family history. The book always opened to the same page: ELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL. Walter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married Elizabeth Stevenson of Gloucester in 1784. She died in 1800, leaving behind their children: Elizabeth, born 1785; Anne, born 1787; a still-born son in 1789; and Mary, born 1791. Sir Walter had personally updated this entry to include Mary’s marriage to Charles Musgrove of Uppercross in 1810 and the exact date of his wife’s death. The book then detailed the family’s history, noting their origins in Cheshire, their service as high sheriffs, their time in Parliament, and their baronetcy granted by Charles II. It concluded with the family crest and motto, followed by Sir Walter’s handwritten note naming his heir, William Walter Elliot. Vanity defined Sir Walter’s character. He had been a handsome youth and remained a striking man at fifty-four. He obsessed over his appearance and his social standing more than anyone else. He believed beauty was second only to his title, and he worshipped himself for possessing both. His looks and rank were his greatest assets, as they had helped him secure a wife far better than he deserved."
}

