parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a3b067cce901f991a9db0df0",
  "rewritten_text": "Oh, I misunderstood. I thought you said anybody, and I assumed that meant everyone. It does, provided they are of noble birth, preferably royal. That seems reasonable, the abbot said, eager to keep the peace. It is unlikely such a gift would reveal the affairs of commoners. Our King Arthur— Would you like to know of him? the enchanter interrupted. Most gladly, the abbot replied. The crowd was immediately captivated again, those hopeless fools. They watched the magic intently, casting smug looks at me when the announcement came: The king is tired from the hunt and has been sleeping soundly in his palace for two hours. God bless him, the abbot said, crossing himself. May he find rest for body and soul. If he were actually sleeping, that might be true, I said, but the king is not sleeping; he is riding. Tension flared as the group struggled to choose who to believe, as I still held some credibility. The magician sneered, saying he had seen many prophets, but never one who could see the truth without performing a ritual. You have lived in the woods too long, I replied. I use rituals myself, as this brotherhood knows, but only when necessary. I knew how to hold my own in a war of words, and that barb made him squirm. When the abbot asked about the queen and the court, the magician claimed they were all overcome by fatigue and sleeping like the king. That is another lie, I said. Half of them are busy with their amusements, and the queen and the others are riding. Perhaps you can show off a bit more and tell us where they are headed? They are sleeping now, he insisted, but tomorrow they will ride toward the sea. And where will they be by sunset the day after? Far north of Camelot, with half their journey complete. That is another lie, off by a hundred and fifty miles."
}

