parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6b20d74e7c3d69639bb4f899",
  "rewritten_text": "So, let us hear the whole story. Well, it happened like this. Miss Watson was always nagging me and treating me harshly, yet she insisted she would never sell me down to New Orleans. Still, I noticed a slave trader hanging around the property quite a bit lately, which made me nervous. One night, I crept up to the door and found it slightly ajar. I overheard Miss Watson telling the widow that she intended to sell me to New Orleans. She claimed she did not want to, but the offer of eight hundred dollars was too much money to turn down. The widow tried to talk her out of it, but I did not wait to hear the rest. I left in a hurry. I ran down the hill, intending to steal a skiff along the shore somewhere above town, but people were still moving about, so I hid in the old, dilapidated cooperage by the riverbank to wait for the area to clear. I stayed there all night. People were around constantly. By six in the morning, boats began passing, and by eight or nine, everyone in every skiff was talking about how your father had come to town claiming you were murdered. These later boats were filled with ladies and gentlemen heading over to see the scene. Sometimes they would pull up to the shore to rest before crossing, so through their conversations, I learned all about the killing. I was deeply sorry you were dead, Huck, but I am not anymore. I lay there under the wood shavings all day. I was hungry, but I was not afraid, because I knew Miss Watson and the widow would head to the camp meeting right after breakfast and be gone all day. They knew I usually went out with the cattle at dawn, so they would not expect to see me around the house and would not miss me until after dark. The other servants would not notice my absence either, as they would sneak off to take a holiday as soon as the owners were out of sight. When darkness fell, I headed up the river road for two miles or more to where there were no houses. I had decided on my plan. You see, if I kept trying to escape on foot, the dogs would track me. If I stole a skiff to cross the river, they would notice it missing and know exactly where I landed on the other side, allowing them to pick up my trail."
}

