parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2f141a720ae265821023436a",
  "rewritten_text": "Good heavens, the Duchess mused, he is a foot doctor after all. How appalling. I hope he is at least a foreigner, as that would be slightly more acceptable. I must introduce him to you. Introduce him, the Duchess exclaimed, you cannot mean he is present? She began searching for her tortoise-shell fan and worn lace shawl, prepared to depart instantly. Naturally he is here; I would never host a gathering without him. He claims I possess a purely psychic hand and that had my thumb been shorter, I would have become a confirmed pessimist and entered a convent. Oh, I understand, the Duchess replied, feeling relieved, he predicts fortunes, I presume? And misfortunes as well, Lady Windermere responded, quite a few. Next year, for example, I face great danger by land and sea, so I plan to reside in a balloon and hoist my dinner up in a basket nightly. It is all inscribed on my little finger or palm, I cannot recall which. But surely that is tempting Providence, Gladys. My dear Duchess, surely Providence can resist temptation by now. I believe everyone should have their palms read monthly to know what to avoid. One does it anyway, but the warning is pleasant. If someone does not fetch Mr. Podgers immediately, I shall have to go myself. Allow me, Lady Windermere, offered a tall, handsome young man who had been listening with an amused smile. Thanks, Lord Arthur, but I fear you would not recognize him. If he is as remarkable as you claim, I could hardly miss him. Describe him, and I will bring him to you at once. He does not resemble a palmist at all. He is not mysterious, esoteric, or romantic. He is a short, stout man with a comical bald head and large gold-rimmed spectacles, appearing like a cross between a family physician and a country lawyer. I apologize, but it is not my fault. People are so vexing. My pianists resemble poets, and my poets resemble pianists. I recall inviting a terrible conspirator to dinner last season, a man who had bombed many people and wore chain mail with a dagger in his sleeve; yet when he arrived, he looked like a pleasant clergyman and spent the evening telling jokes."
}

