parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c815836182ff2618150dd7fa",
  "rewritten_text": "Raymond paced the room in a fury. I waited to hear his accusations. Adrian, leaning against the wall, cried out, \"This is utterly absurd; you are behaving like schoolboys.\" Raymond retorted, \"You fail to grasp the situation. This is a calculated tyranny I refuse to endure. Must I be the empire's only slave simply because I am Protector? My privacy is violated, my conduct judged, and my friends slighted. I am finished with it all.\" He unpinned the star of his office and tossed it onto the table, declaring, \"I resign my position and abdicate my authority—let someone else take it.\" Adrian countered, \"Let whoever considers themselves your superior take it, though no man in England would dare. Recognize your own worth, Raymond, and your anger will fade. Recently, our prayers for the nation's success were inseparable from our prayers for your welfare. You dedicated your time to our prosperity, beautifying our cities and enriching our land. You protected the weak and humbled the unjust. It is no wonder we are devastated by this change. This mood will pass; resume your duties, and your supporters will rejoice while your enemies fall silent. Control yourself, and the world remains yours.\" Raymond replied gloomily, \"That advice would be sound for someone else. You are the wise and just peer who could truly lead. I realize now, too late for England, that I am unfit for this role. I cannot govern my own impulses. Do not think this resignation is merely a temper tantrum. I swear by the living God that I will never reclaim that trinket or bear the burden of misery it represents again.\""
}

