parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "76548b11323fdaf73c96b385",
  "rewritten_text": "Oh, that is nothing! It is a pleasure. I do not work for money; I live entirely for my art. Lord Arthur placed four pounds, two shillings, and sixpence on the table, thanked the little German for his help, and after declining an invitation to meet some anarchists for tea on Saturday, left the house for the park. For the next two days, he was incredibly excited. On Friday at noon, he drove to the Buckingham to wait for news. All afternoon, the hall porter posted telegrams about horse races, divorce cases, and the weather, while the ticker tape reported on a long session in the House of Commons and a minor stock market panic. At four o’clock, the evening papers arrived. Lord Arthur took them into the library, much to the annoyance of Colonel Goodchild, who wanted to read his own speech about South African missions and the appointment of black bishops. None of the papers mentioned Chichester, so Lord Arthur assumed his attempt had failed. He was devastated and shaken. When he visited Herr Winckelkopf the next day, the man apologized profusely and offered a free replacement clock or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost. Lord Arthur had lost faith in explosives, and Winckelkopf admitted that even dynamite was often poor quality these days. The German insisted that while the mechanism had likely failed, the clock might still explode. He cited a barometer he once sent to a governor in Odessa that took three months to detonate instead of the planned ten days. Although it only killed a housemaid because the governor had left town weeks earlier, it proved that dynamite, when controlled by machinery, was a powerful if unreliable tool."
}

