parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d7c1be6ee14d9391bd386e68",
  "rewritten_text": "I passed several funerals, each with few mourners, viewed by onlookers as terrible omens. Some watched with intense curiosity, others fled in fear, and some wept openly. Adrian’s main goal, beyond helping the sick, was to hide the plague’s progress from Londoners. He knew fear and despair made people more vulnerable to infection. He kept shops open and maintained some activity to avoid a sense of panic. Still, to me, the city felt transformed. Carriages were gone, grass grew in the streets, houses looked abandoned, and people wore a haunted, frightened expression. My carriage drew attention as I traveled toward the Protectoral Palace, where the streets felt even more desolate. I found Adrian’s waiting room full. I watched the petitioners—mostly working-class people whose livelihoods had vanished with the collapse of trade. New arrivals looked anxious or terrified, while those leaving appeared calm and satisfied, a testament to Adrian’s influence. When the clock struck two, he stopped taking visitors. I then entered his office. I was struck by Adrian’s improved health. He no longer looked frail or exhausted; his eyes were bright, his expression composed, and he radiated a focused energy. He sat with secretaries organizing petitions. I admired his patience and fairness. He advised those who could afford to leave London to do so immediately, providing them with the necessary resources."
}

