parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2a25c2b3cd0986f66f3fd54e",
  "rewritten_text": "I told myself I wanted no part of such business. When I went downstairs the next morning, the parlor was closed and the night watchers had left. Only the family, the widow Bartley, and our group remained. I studied their faces for any sign of what might have occurred, but I could tell nothing. By midday, the undertaker arrived with his assistant. They placed the coffin on chairs in the center of the room and arranged our chairs in rows, borrowing more from neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were filled. I noticed the coffin lid remained as it was, but I dared not look inside while others were present. People began to arrive, and the con artists and the girls took the front row. For half an hour, the mourners filed past in a single line to gaze at the dead man’s face. Some shed tears in the heavy silence, while the girls and the men sobbed with heads bowed. The only sounds were the shuffling of feet and the blowing of noses, which always seems more frequent at funerals than anywhere else except church. Once the room was packed, the undertaker moved about in black gloves with a smooth, soothing manner, making final adjustments without a sound. He guided people into place with silent nods and hand gestures before taking his position against the wall. He was the most stealthy, gliding man I have ever seen, completely devoid of any expression. They had borrowed a wheezing melodeon, and when a young woman began to play, it sounded strained and discordant. Everyone joined in the singing, though in my opinion, only Peter sounded decent. Then Reverend Hobson began his slow, solemn address. Suddenly, the most outrageous noise erupted from the cellar. It was only one dog, but he created such a powerful racket that the parson had to stand over the coffin and wait, as it was impossible to hear oneself think."
}

