parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "05dd6c4d71dbbe1453382199",
  "rewritten_text": "Once finished, additional loops were detached from the rim, more fabric was fed through, and the freed loops were secured to their corresponding buttons. This allowed the entire upper section of the bag to be positioned between the netting and the hoop. Clearly, the hoop would then descend into the car, leaving the total weight of the vessel and its cargo supported solely by the buttons. While this might appear insufficient, it was quite secure, as the buttons were robust and spaced closely enough that each bore only a fraction of the load. Even with triple the weight, I would have felt no concern. I then lifted the hoop back inside the rubberized covering, bracing it near its original height with three slender poles. This maintained the bag's upper tension and kept the lower netting correctly aligned. Finally, I sealed the enclosure's mouth by gathering the fabric folds and twisting them firmly from within using a fixed tourniquet. Three circular panes of thick, clear glass were set into the sides of the covering, providing a full horizontal view. A fourth window was placed in the bottom fabric, aligning with a small floor opening to allow a downward view. I could not install a similar overhead window due to the fabric folds at the closure, though this was inconsequential, as the balloon itself would have obstructed any upward sight. About a foot beneath one side window, an eight-inch circular opening with a threaded brass rim was installed. The condenser's large tube was screwed into this rim, with the device itself housed inside the gum-elastic chamber."
}

