parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ba209d70eaca74a12ce42d51",
  "rewritten_text": "Three or four months passed, and winter had arrived. I had attended school consistently, learning to read, write, and spell a bit, though my multiplication skills stalled at six times seven equals thirty-five, a limit I felt I would never surpass. I never cared much for mathematics. Initially, I despised school, but eventually, I grew accustomed to it. When I felt overwhelmed, I would skip class, and the subsequent punishment actually improved my mood. Over time, school became easier, and I grew used to the widow’s habits, which no longer irritated me as much. While living indoors and sleeping in a bed felt restrictive, I occasionally slipped out to sleep in the woods before the weather turned cold. I still preferred my old life, but I was beginning to appreciate the new one. The widow noted my slow but steady progress, expressing satisfaction and pride in my development. One morning at breakfast, I accidentally knocked over the salt cellar. I reached to toss some over my left shoulder to ward off bad luck, but Miss Watson intervened, scolding me for the mess. Although the widow defended me, I knew the bad luck was inevitable. I left the house feeling anxious, wondering what misfortune awaited me. Since there was no way to prevent this specific omen, I walked along feeling dejected and vigilant. I headed to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the high board fence. A fresh inch of snow covered the ground, revealing footprints. Someone had come from the quarry, lingered by the stile, and then circled the garden fence. It was strange they had not entered. I could not understand it, and it seemed quite peculiar. Before following the tracks, I knelt to examine them more closely."
}

