parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d4704eb75ef7137627c8fe6a",
  "rewritten_text": "Despite her efforts, the boy grew paler and more miserable. She tried hot baths, showers, and plunges, but he remained as gloomy as ever. She then added a bland oatmeal diet and medicinal plasters, treating him like a vessel to be filled with various quack remedies. Tom had grown indifferent to her constant interference, which deeply worried his aunt. She felt she had to break through his apathy, so when she heard of a new medicine called Pain-killer, she bought some immediately. After tasting the fiery liquid, she abandoned her other treatments and pinned all her hopes on it. She gave Tom a teaspoonful and watched anxiously. Her worries vanished when he reacted with intense, wild energy, as if she had set him on fire. Tom decided he needed a change. He realized that while his suffering might seem romantic, the constant medical attention was becoming too much. He hatched a plan and began pretending to love the medicine. He asked for it so frequently that he became a nuisance, eventually leading his aunt to tell him to help himself. While she would have trusted Sid, she secretly monitored the bottle because it was Tom. She noticed the liquid level dropping, never suspecting that he was actually using it to fill a crack in the floorboards. One day, as Tom was pouring the medicine into the floor, the aunt’s yellow cat approached, begging for a taste. Tom warned the cat, Peter, that he should be sure he wanted it, but the animal insisted. Tom pried the cat’s mouth open and poured the medicine in. Peter leaped into the air, let out a loud yowl, and raced around the room, crashing into furniture and knocking over flowerpots in a chaotic frenzy."
}

