parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "65f7ec5fda0d287addfa2223",
  "rewritten_text": "After lightening the chest by removing two-thirds of its load, we managed, with significant effort, to lift it from the pit. We hid the removed items in the brush, instructing Jupiter to guard them and remain silent until we returned. We hauled the chest home, reaching our hut at one in the morning, exhausted by the labor. We rested until two, ate, and then headed back to the hills with three sturdy sacks. Arriving at the pit shortly before four, we split the remaining loot and returned to the hut, depositing our golden cargo just as dawn broke over the eastern horizon. Though completely drained, our intense excitement prevented us from sleeping. After a restless nap of a few hours, we woke simultaneously to inspect our fortune. The chest had been filled to capacity, and we spent the entire day and most of the following night sorting through the chaotic heap. We discovered our wealth was even greater than anticipated. We held over four hundred and fifty thousand dollars in gold coins, calculated by contemporary tables. There was no silver, only antique gold from France, Spain, Germany, and England, along with unfamiliar tokens. Some heavy coins were too worn to identify, and there was no American currency. Estimating the jewels proved harder. We found one hundred and ten large, fine diamonds, eighteen brilliant rubies, three hundred and ten beautiful emeralds, twenty-one sapphires, and an opal. These gems had been pried from their settings, which we found crushed and mangled among the gold, likely to obscure their origins."
}

