parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b87b06c00df32799d16d6f80",
  "rewritten_text": "Well, everyone lives that way, Huck. Tom, it makes no difference. I am not everyone, and I cannot stand it. It is terrible to be so restricted. Food comes too easily, and I have no appetite for it. I have to ask permission to go fishing or swimming; I have to ask to do everything. I had to speak so politely that it was no comfort at all. I had to go to the attic and vent my frustration every day just to feel like myself, or I would have died. The widow would not let me smoke, yell, yawn, stretch, or scratch in front of people. She prayed all the time! I had to leave, Tom; I just had to. Besides, school is starting, and I would have had to go. I would not stand for that. Look, being rich is not what people claim. It is just constant worry and effort, making you wish you were dead. These clothes and this barrel suit me, and I am never giving them up. I would never have had this trouble if not for that money. Take my share along with yours, give me a dime now and then, and please convince the widow to let me go. Oh, Huck, you know I cannot do that. It is not fair, and if you try it a little longer, you will learn to like it. Like it? Yes, the way I would like a hot stove if I sat on it long enough. No, Tom, I will not be rich, and I will not live in those suffocating houses. I like the woods, the river, and my barrel, and I am sticking to them. Just when we had guns, a cave, and everything ready for robbing, this nonsense has to ruin it all! Tom saw his chance. Look, Huck, being rich will not stop me from becoming a robber. Really? Are you serious, Tom? As serious as I am sitting here. But Huck, we cannot let you into the gang if you are not respectable. Huck’s joy vanished. You cannot let me in, Tom?"
}

