  "rewritten_text": "Emma found no reason to change her negative opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her initial assessment proved accurate; every subsequent meeting confirmed that the woman was self-important, presumptuous, overly familiar, ignorant, and rude. While Mrs. Elton possessed some beauty and minor talents, she lacked the judgment to realize she was not the sophisticated expert she imagined herself to be. She arrived in the country convinced she was there to enlighten the locals, believing her own social standing far surpassed that of any woman in Highbury. Mr. Elton appeared not only happy with his wife but genuinely proud of her. He acted as though he had brought a woman to Highbury who eclipsed even Emma Woodhouse. Most of their new acquaintances, influenced by Miss Bates’s enthusiasm or simply inclined to be agreeable, accepted Mrs. Elton at her own valuation. Consequently, her reputation for excellence spread, while Emma maintained a polite facade, describing her only as pleasant and well-dressed. Over time, Mrs. Elton’s attitude toward Emma soured. Offended by Emma’s lack of interest in a close friendship, she grew cold and distant. While this distance was a relief, the underlying malice only deepened Emma’s dislike. Furthermore, the Eltons treated Harriet with open sneering and neglect. Emma hoped this would help Harriet move on, but the behavior reflected poorly on the couple. It was clear they had discussed Harriet’s past feelings for Mr. Elton, likely painting Emma in the worst possible light to soothe his ego. As a result, they shared a mutual dislike for Emma, often bonding over their criticism of her. Unable to show their hostility toward Emma directly, they directed their contempt at Harriet. Meanwhile, Mrs. Elton took an immediate, intense interest in Jane Fairfax. She was not content with mere admiration; she insisted on inserting herself into Jane’s life as a patroness. Long before Emma lost favor with her, she had already been subjected to Mrs. Elton’s unsolicited plans to befriend and assist Jane."
}

