  "rewritten_text": "If I cannot survive on that, I shall not request assistance from anyone. Is that acceptable? A massive wave of cheering erupted, through which the monarch’s voice emerged, declaring: Release his shackles and grant him liberty! Let all, whether noble or humble, wealthy or destitute, pay him honor, for he is now the king’s primary advisor, vested with authority and seated upon the throne’s highest tier! Dispel this encroaching darkness and restore the light and joy, so that the entire world may offer you its blessings. I replied: It is of no consequence if a commoner is humiliated before the world, but it would be a disgrace to the king if those who witnessed his minister in such a state did not also see him redeemed from that shame. If I might request that my garments be returned. They are unsuitable, the king interrupted. Bring attire of a different quality; dress him as a prince! My plan succeeded. I needed to maintain the status quo until the eclipse reached totality, lest they attempt to force me to end the darkness, which I was unable to do. Requesting the clothes provided a brief delay, but insufficient time. I had to invent another pretext. I claimed it would be expected for the king to reconsider his impulsive actions; therefore, I would allow the darkness to persist briefly, and if the king remained resolute after a suitable interval, the darkness would be lifted. Neither the king nor the crowd was pleased with this, but I remained firm. The gloom deepened until it was pitch black, while I wrestled with those cumbersome medieval garments. The crowd moaned in terror as cold, unnatural breezes swept through and stars appeared in the sky. When the eclipse finally became total, I felt relieved, though everyone else was miserable, as expected. I announced: The king, by his silence, upholds the agreement. I raised my hands, paused, and declared with profound gravity: Let the spell dissolve and vanish without harm! There was no immediate reaction in that profound darkness and deathly silence. However, as the sun’s silver edge emerged moments later, the crowd erupted in a thunderous roar and surged forward like a flood to overwhelm me with gratitude; Clarence was certainly among the first to reach me."
