  "rewritten_text": "I view it as a primary refinement of civilized cultures. Certainly, sir; and it possesses the merit of being popular among the world's less refined societies as well: every savage dances. Sir William merely smiled. Your friend performs beautifully, he remarked after a silence, watching Bingley enter the group; and I have no doubt you are quite skilled in the art yourself, Mr. Darcy. You witnessed my dancing at Meryton, I believe, sir. Indeed I did, and I found it quite enjoyable. Do you dance often at St. James’s? Never, sir. Do you not consider it a fitting tribute to the location? It is a tribute I avoid paying to any location whenever possible. You own a residence in the city, I presume? Mr. Darcy inclined his head. I once considered settling in town myself, as I enjoy refined company; however, I was uncertain if the London climate would suit Lady Lucas. He waited for a reply, but his companion remained silent. As Elizabeth approached, Sir William decided on a gallant gesture and called out: My dear Miss Eliza, why are you not dancing? Mr. Darcy, you must permit me to introduce this young lady as a most desirable partner. Surely you cannot decline when such beauty stands before you. He reached for her hand to offer it to Mr. Darcy, who, though startled, seemed not entirely opposed. Elizabeth pulled away, telling Sir William with some agitation: Truly, sir, I have no desire to dance. Please do not imagine I approached with the intent of seeking a partner. Mr. Darcy formally requested the honor of her hand, but to no avail. Elizabeth remained resolute, and Sir William’s efforts to persuade her failed. You dance so well, Miss Eliza, that it is unkind to deprive me of the pleasure of watching you; and while this gentleman generally dislikes the pastime, I am certain he would not object to obliging us for a short while. Mr. Darcy is the soul of politeness, Elizabeth replied with a smile. He is indeed; yet given the incentive, my dear Miss Eliza, his compliance is hardly surprising; for who could refuse such a partner? Elizabeth gave a playful look and walked away. Her refusal had not diminished her in the gentleman’s estimation, and he was contemplating her with some satisfaction when Miss Bingley approached him."
}

