parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d90605daadc236409ba1e7bd",
  "rewritten_text": "The cycle of misery should have been broken, yet cruel fate would not allow such a violation of her eternal laws. Why should I complain? Misery was my natural state, and only the wretched could ever reach me; if Woodville had been happy, we would never have met. Having spent years fed by tears and raised in grief, how can I hesitate to tell this story of sorrow and death? Woodville had to travel to the country, where he was held back for days, away from his lovely bride. He received a letter from her mentioning she was slightly ill, but urging him to return, claiming his presence would be her best medicine. Delayed for three more days, he finally rushed to her. He felt a strange sense of dread; having heard nothing further, he feared she had worsened. This fear made him restless, yet a dark voice whispered that he would never see her as she once was. When he reached her home, everything was silent. He walked through the rooms and found a servant weeping bitterly. Faint with fear, he barely managed to ask if she was dead, only to hear the reply, \"Not yet.\" These words were less terrifying than he had expected; knowing she was still alive gave him a glimmer of hope. Recalling her letter, he clung to the desperate belief that his love could restore her spirit and that she could not die while he was near. He hurried to her room, where she lay feverish and unconscious. He wrapped her in his arms, kissing her burning lips and calling her name with quiet anguish. \"Return, Elinor; I am here with you. You promised me that I would bring you health. Let your spirit revive; you cannot die while I am near. What is death? To see you no more? To lose the part of myself without whom I have no past or future?\""
}

