parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "12bb9d305b1616e878543a9b",
  "rewritten_text": "I proceeded from store to store, acquiring the items needed to alter his look. Once finished, I headed toward Little Britain. Mr. Jaggers was seated at his desk but rose at once to stand by the hearth upon my arrival. Now, Pip, be cautious, he warned. I will, sir, I replied, having prepared my words. Do not implicate yourself, he continued, or anyone else. Understand me—anyone. Tell me nothing; I have no desire to know, nor am I curious. It was clear he was aware the man had arrived. I only wish, Mr. Jaggers, to confirm what I have been told. I do not expect it to be false, but I must verify it. Mr. Jaggers nodded. Did you say told or informed? he inquired, tilting his head to listen to the floor. Told implies speech, and you cannot speak with a man in New South Wales. I will say informed, Mr. Jaggers. Good. I have been informed by Abel Magwitch that he is my long-unknown benefactor. That is the man, Mr. Jaggers confirmed, in New South Wales. And only he? I asked. Only he, he replied. I am not so unreasonable as to hold you accountable for my errors, sir, but I always assumed it was Miss Havisham. As you say, Pip, he answered, eyeing me coolly while biting his finger, I am not responsible for that. Yet it appeared so likely, I pleaded, feeling disheartened. Not a shred of evidence, Pip, he said, shaking his head and adjusting his coat. Never judge by appearances; rely only on evidence. It is the best rule. I have nothing more to say, I sighed after a pause. I have verified the facts, and that is all. Since Magwitch has revealed himself, he continued, you understand how strictly I have always kept to the facts in our dealings. There has never been any deviation. You are aware of that? Quite, sir. I warned Magwitch when he first wrote from New South Wales that he must never expect me to stray from the truth."
}

