  "rewritten_text": "It is only fair to note that the young woman did not explicitly decline, but rather voiced a fervent wish to be damned if she would; a refined way of dodging the plea, revealing a natural courtesy that recoiled from the cruelty of a blunt rejection. The Jew’s face darkened. He shifted his gaze from this lady, dressed in a vibrant red gown, green boots, and yellow hair-papers, to the other woman. Nancy, my dear, Fagin asked coaxingly, what is your answer? It will not work, so do not bother, Fagin, Nancy replied. What do you mean by that? Mr. Sikes asked, glancing up with a scowl. Exactly what I said, Bill, Nancy answered calmly. You are the perfect person for this, Sikes argued, as no one around here knows you. Since I intend to keep it that way, my answer is more no than yes, Bill, Nancy replied with equal composure. She will go, Fagin, Sikes insisted. No, she will not, Fagin, Nancy countered. Yes, she will, Fagin, Sikes repeated. Sikes was correct. Through a mix of threats, promises, and bribes, the woman was finally persuaded to accept the task. She lacked the hesitations of her friend, for having moved recently from the distant but respectable Ratcliffe to the area near Field Lane, she feared no recognition from her many acquaintances. Thus, with a clean white apron over her dress and her hair-papers hidden beneath a straw bonnet—both items supplied from the Jew’s vast collection—Miss Nancy prepared to depart. Wait a moment, my dear, the Jew said, producing a small covered basket. Carry this in one hand. It appears more respectable. Give her a door-key for the other hand, Sikes added; it looks authentic. Yes, that is perfect, the Jew agreed, hanging a large key on the woman’s right forefinger. Excellent, he said, rubbing his hands. Oh, my brother! My poor, sweet, innocent little brother! Nancy cried, breaking into tears while clutching the basket and key in a display of feigned distress. What has become of him! Where have they taken him! Please, have pity and tell me what has happened to the dear boy, gentlemen; I beg you, gentlemen!"
}

