  "rewritten_text": "Mrs. Musgrove took the first chance to speak privately with Anne. She remarked that she wished Mrs. Charles possessed Anne’s way with the children, noting how they behaved differently around her. She lamented that they were generally spoiled and wished Anne could teach her sister how to manage them. While she considered them healthy and fine, she admitted their behavior was often difficult, which discouraged her from inviting them over as frequently as she might otherwise. She suspected Mrs. Charles was unhappy about this, but explained that it was exhausting to constantly correct children or bribe them with sweets to keep them quiet. Mary, meanwhile, complained to Anne that Mrs. Musgrove’s servants were constantly wandering the village instead of working, which she felt was a bad influence on her own reliable nursemaid, Jemima. Conversely, Mrs. Musgrove told Anne she avoided interfering in her daughter-in-law’s affairs, but warned her that Mary’s nursemaid was unreliable and overly concerned with her appearance, hoping Anne might keep a watchful eye on the situation. Finally, Mary expressed her frustration that Mrs. Musgrove often failed to grant her the social precedence she felt she deserved when they dined together at the Great House."
}

