parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "55b87a4e04128fb6fa4eebf8",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Elliot looked up with such profound respect. Quite delightful! Mrs. Clay cried, though she dared not look at Anne. It is just like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, may I not call them father and son? Oh, I do not restrict anyone’s speech. If you insist on such ideas! But honestly, I hardly notice his attentions being any different from other men’s. My dear Miss Elliot! Mrs. Clay exclaimed, raising her hands and eyes before falling into a convenient, astonished silence. Well, Penelope, you need not be so alarmed. I did invite him, after all. I sent him away with a smile. When I realized he was heading to Thornberry Park for the entire day tomorrow, I felt sorry for him. Anne admired her friend’s performance, managing to show such pleasure at the arrival of the very man who hindered her own goals. Mrs. Clay must have loathed the sight of Mr. Elliot, yet she maintained a pleasant, obliging expression, appearing satisfied despite having only half the time to devote to Sir Walter. For Anne, seeing Mr. Elliot enter was distressing, and his approach was painful. She had previously suspected his sincerity, but now she saw through everything. His deferential behavior toward her father, compared to his past remarks, was repulsive. Knowing his cruel treatment of Mrs. Smith, she could barely endure his smiles or his artificial sentiments. She intended to remain cool without provoking a confrontation. Her goal was to avoid any scene or inquiry, while quietly distancing herself from the unnecessary intimacy that had developed. She was more guarded and reserved than the night before. He tried to pique her curiosity again about where he had heard her praised, hoping for more engagement, but the spell was broken. He realized that the energy of a public room was required to stir his cousin’s vanity, and he could not achieve it now amidst the competing demands of the others."
}

