{
  "request_id": "88564dd7280af79d980eeda4",
  "rewritten_text": "Seeing the creature, the orangutan leaped through the door, down the stairs, and out an open window into the street. The Frenchman followed in despair, while the ape, still holding the razor, paused occasionally to gesture at him before darting away again. This chase continued through the quiet streets near three in the morning. Passing an alley behind the Rue Morgue, the ape noticed a light from Madame L’Espanaye’s fourth-story window. It rushed to the building, climbed the lightning rod with incredible speed, grabbed the open shutter, and swung onto the bed’s headboard in less than a minute, kicking the shutter as it entered. The sailor felt both relieved and worried. He hoped to trap the beast, as it could only escape by climbing back down the rod, but he feared what it might do inside. He followed, climbing the rod easily, but when he reached the window, he could only lean over to peer inside. What he saw nearly made him fall in terror. This was when the horrific screams began, waking the neighbors. Madame L’Espanaye and her daughter were in their nightclothes, sorting papers from an open iron chest in the middle of the room. They had their backs to the window and likely did not notice the beast at first, assuming the shutter had been blown by the wind. As the sailor watched, the giant animal grabbed Madame L’Espanaye by her loose hair and waved the razor near her face, mimicking a barber."
}

