  "request_id": "04561975e629f5f38badbc99",
  "rewritten_text": "On the hearth lay several thick locks of grey human hair, stained with blood and seemingly torn out by the roots. The floor held four gold coins, a topaz earring, three large silver spoons, three smaller ones made of base metal, and two bags containing nearly four thousand francs in gold. A desk in the corner had been ransacked, though many items remained inside. A small iron safe found under the bed was open with the key still in the lock, containing only unimportant papers. No trace of Madame L’Espanaye was found, but after noticing soot in the fireplace, searchers looked up the chimney and pulled out the daughter’s body, head-first. She had been forced deep into the narrow space. The body was still warm and covered in scrapes from the struggle. Her face was scratched, and her throat bore deep bruises and fingernail marks, suggesting she had been strangled. After searching the rest of the house, the group entered a small backyard where the old lady’s body lay. Her throat was so severely cut that her head detached when they tried to lift her. Both the body and head were horribly mutilated, barely recognizable as human. There is currently no clue to this mystery. The next day’s paper added that many people had been questioned regarding the tragedy, but nothing had shed light on the case. Pauline Dubourg, a laundress, testified that she had known the women for three years and found them affectionate toward each other and reliable with payments. She did not know how they earned their living, though she believed the mother told fortunes and was rumored to have saved money."
}

