parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ec8c5a4f4cce321be62fd182",
  "rewritten_text": "In reality, as you climb to higher altitudes, the amount of air you pass through does not decrease in direct proportion to the height gained; instead, the density drops at a constantly shrinking rate. It is clear that no matter how high we travel, we can never reach a point where the atmosphere ends entirely. I argued that it must continue to exist, even if it becomes infinitely thin. I knew that many people have argued for a definite limit to the atmosphere, beyond which there is no air at all. However, those who support this view have overlooked a detail that deserves serious study. When comparing the intervals between Encke’s comet returning to its closest point to the sun, and accounting for all gravitational pull from the planets, the orbital periods are clearly shrinking. The comet’s path is slowly but steadily shortening. This is exactly what would happen if the comet were meeting resistance from a very thin, invisible medium filling space. Such a medium would slow the comet down, weakening its outward force and allowing the sun’s gravity to pull it closer with every orbit. There is no other way to explain this change. Furthermore, the comet’s size shrinks rapidly as it nears the sun and expands as it moves away. Was I not right to assume, like M. Valz, that this change is caused by the compression of that same thin medium, which grows denser near the sun? The zodiacal light, a glowing phenomenon visible in the tropics, also caught my attention. It stretches upward from the horizon and follows the sun’s equator. It seemed to me to be a thin atmosphere extending from the sun, reaching at least past the orbit of Venus, and likely much further. I could not believe this medium was limited only to the comet’s path or the area immediately surrounding the sun."
}

