  "rewritten_text": "You ought to adopt some of my philosophy. Only dwell on the past when its memory brings you joy. I cannot attribute such a philosophy to you. Your reflections must be so entirely free of blame that your satisfaction stems not from philosophy, but from a much better source: ignorance. It is not so for me. Distressing memories arise that cannot and should not be dismissed. I have been selfish my entire life in practice, if not in principle. As a child, I was taught what was right, but never how to govern my temper. I was instilled with good principles but left to pursue them with pride and vanity. Being an only son for many years, I was indulged by parents who, though kind themselves, allowed and even encouraged me to be arrogant and selfish, to value only my own circle, and to look down upon the rest of the world. I remained that way from eight until twenty-eight, and I would likely still be that man if not for you, my dearest, loveliest Elizabeth. I owe you everything. You taught me a difficult but invaluable lesson. You humbled me. I approached you certain of my welcome, yet you revealed how inadequate my efforts were to please a woman truly worthy of admiration. Had you convinced yourself that I would accept? I truly had. What must you think of my vanity? I believed you were hoping for my proposal. My behavior must have been at fault, though never by design. I never intended to mislead you, but my high spirits may have led me astray. How you must have loathed me after that night! Loathe you? I was perhaps angry at first, but my resentment soon shifted toward a more appropriate target. I am hesitant to ask what you thought of me when we encountered one another at Pemberley. Did you resent my arrival? No, I felt only astonishment. Your surprise could not have exceeded my own at being acknowledged by you. My conscience whispered that I deserved no special courtesy, and I admit I expected no more than what was strictly necessary. My goal then, Darcy replied, was to demonstrate through every civility that I was not petty enough to harbor past grievances, and I hoped to earn your pardon and improve your opinion of me by showing that I had taken your rebukes to heart."
}

