  "request_id": "f00110a59d4ab547ddf7eb49",
  "rewritten_text": "That served as sufficient encouragement. \"Well, my dear, you should be aware that Mrs. Long reports Netherfield has been rented by a wealthy young gentleman from northern England. He arrived Monday in a carriage drawn by four horses to inspect the property, was quite pleased, and promptly reached an agreement with Mr. Morris. He intends to move in before Michaelmas, with his staff arriving late next week.\" \"What is he called?\" \"Bingley.\" \"Is he a bachelor?\" \"Certainly, my dear, he is single! A wealthy bachelor with an income of four or five thousand annually. What a wonderful prospect for our daughters!\" \"In what way? How does that concern them?\" \"My dear Mr. Bennet,\" his wife answered, \"how can you be so exasperating? You know perfectly well I am considering him as a husband for one of them.\" \"Is that his intention in moving here?\" \"Intention? Absurd, how can you say such things! Still, he might very well fall for one of them, so you must call upon him upon his arrival.\" \"I see no reason for that. You and the girls can go, or perhaps send them alone, which might be better; since you remain as attractive as any of them, Mr. Bingley might prefer you to the rest.\" \"You flatter me, my dear. I have certainly enjoyed my share of beauty, but I do not claim to be remarkable now. Once a woman has five adult daughters, she should stop dwelling on her own looks.\" \"In such instances, a woman rarely has much beauty left to dwell upon.\" \"Regardless, you must visit Mr. Bingley once he settles in the area.\" \"I cannot promise that, I assure you.\" \"But think of your daughters. Consider what a match it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas intend to go for that reason alone, as they typically do not visit newcomers. You must go, for we cannot visit him otherwise.\" \"You are being overly cautious. I am certain Mr. Bingley will be pleased to see you; I will provide a note confirming my full approval for him to marry whichever girl he prefers, though I must put in a good word for my dear Lizzy.\" \"I forbid you from doing any such thing. Lizzy is no better than the others; she is certainly not as pretty as Jane, nor as cheerful as Lydia.\""
}

