  "request_id": "124042166bf84c1ca44e43b1",
  "rewritten_text": "He had never oppressed or imprisoned anyone. Far from demanding his dues, he had voluntarily given them up, venturing into a harsh world to earn his own living. Monsieur Gabelle had managed the struggling estate under instructions to help the people, providing whatever fuel and produce the creditors allowed. Gabelle had surely documented these efforts for his own protection, and this would now be clear. This supported Charles Darnay’s growing resolve to travel to Paris. Like the sailor in the legend, he felt drawn toward the magnetic rock, pulled by an irresistible force. His mind drifted steadily toward this dangerous attraction. He had long felt uneasy that his homeland was suffering under cruel leadership, and he worried that he, who knew he was better than those in power, was not there to advocate for mercy. This guilt, combined with the stinging insults of Monseigneur and the coarse mockery of Stryver, weighed on him. Then came Gabelle’s letter, a desperate plea from an innocent man facing death. His decision was final: he had to go to Paris. He felt the pull of the magnetic rock and believed he must sail toward it, blind to the true danger. He convinced himself that his past actions, though incomplete, would be recognized and appreciated in France. He was captivated by the idealistic vision of doing good, imagining he might even have the influence to guide the chaotic Revolution toward a better path."
}

