parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0787af823a4fb6c169a03a0c",
  "rewritten_text": "He always pretended Peter wrote those letters, but that was a lie; he had gotten every single detail from the simpleton we had taken by canoe to the steamboat. Mary Jane brought out the letter her father had left, and the king read it aloud, weeping over the contents. It bequeathed the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls, while leaving the profitable tannery, other properties worth seven thousand dollars, and another three thousand in gold to Harvey and William, noting exactly where the six thousand dollars in cash was hidden in the cellar. The two frauds insisted on retrieving the money to ensure everything was handled transparently, and they told me to follow with a candle. Once we shut the cellar door, they dumped the bag onto the floor, revealing a beautiful pile of gold coins. The king’s eyes lit up as he slapped the duke on the shoulder, exclaiming that this was incredible and surpassed all their previous schemes. The duke agreed. They sifted the gold through their fingers, letting the coins jingle against the floor. The king remarked that posing as brothers to a wealthy deceased man and representing foreign heirs was their most successful strategy, proving that trusting in Providence was the best approach. Most people would have been satisfied with the haul, but they insisted on counting it, only to find it was four hundred and fifteen dollars short. The king cursed, wondering where the missing money had gone. After searching in vain, the duke suggested that the sick man had likely made a mistake, and they should just let it go. The king agreed they could afford the loss, but he was obsessed with the count. He insisted they needed to be completely transparent, planning to take the money upstairs and count it in front of everyone to avoid suspicion, though he worried that presenting a sum different from what the deceased had specified would look wrong."
}

