parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "8c503f62cbebcb761743a75a",
  "rewritten_text": "Do you visit here often? I have never returned since. Neither have I. As the moon rose, I remembered the peaceful look on his face before he died and the pressure of his hand when I spoke his final words. Estella broke the silence. I have often intended to return, but circumstances prevented me. Poor old place. Moonlight touched the mist and the tears falling from her eyes. Trying to compose herself, she asked if I wondered why the property was left in such ruins. Yes, Estella. The land is mine. It is the only thing I have kept. Everything else is gone, but I held onto this through all those miserable years. Is it going to be developed? Finally, yes. I came to say goodbye before it changes. And you, she asked with genuine interest, do you still live abroad? Still. And you are doing well, I hope? I work hard for a living, so yes, I am doing well. I have often thought of you, Estella said. Have you? Lately, very often. For a long time, I avoided remembering what I had thrown away when I was too young to understand its value. But now that I am free to remember, I have let those thoughts back into my heart. You have always held a place in mine, I replied. We were silent until she spoke again. I never thought I would say goodbye to you while saying goodbye to this place, Estella said. I am glad to do so. Glad to part again? Parting is painful for me. The memory of our last goodbye has always been sad. But you told me then, God bless you, God forgive you, she said earnestly. If you could say that then, you will say it now, now that suffering has taught me to understand your heart. I have been bent and broken, but I hope I have been reshaped into something better."
}

