parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "92cfed5bd711f7cc31b6f063",
  "rewritten_text": "These thoughts calmed me, and I fell into a deep sleep that afternoon, though my feverish blood prevented any peaceful dreams. The horror of the previous day played constantly in my mind: the women fleeing and Felix pulling me away from his father. I woke exhausted, and once night fell, I crept from my hiding spot to find food. After eating, I headed toward the cottage. Everything was quiet. I returned to my hovel and waited for the family to wake, but the morning passed and they never appeared. I trembled, fearing some disaster. The cottage remained dark and silent, and the suspense was agonizing. Soon, two men walked by and stopped to talk, gesturing wildly. I could not understand them, as they spoke the local language rather than the one my protectors used. Shortly after, Felix arrived with another man. I was surprised, as I thought he had been inside all morning, and I listened closely to understand what was happening. His companion asked if he realized he would lose three months of rent and his garden harvest, suggesting he take time to reconsider. Felix replied that it was useless, as they could never live there again. He explained that his father’s life was in danger because of what had happened, and his wife and sister were too traumatized to stay. He begged the man to stop arguing and just take back the property so they could leave. Felix was shaking as he spoke. They went inside for a few minutes and then left. I never saw the De Lacey family again. I spent the rest of the day in my hovel, paralyzed by despair. My protectors were gone, severing my only connection to the world. For the first time, I felt consumed by hatred and a desire for revenge. I made no effort to control these feelings, letting them carry me toward thoughts of destruction and death."
}

