parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d71a27860fe9aea7f2c3b3de",
  "rewritten_text": "So, let me tell you how it happened. Miss Watson was always hard on me, but she promised she would never sell me down to New Orleans. Lately, I noticed a slave trader hanging around, which made me nervous. One night, I crept to the door and heard Miss Watson telling the widow that she was going to sell me for eight hundred dollars. She said it was too much money to turn down. I did not wait to hear the rest; I left immediately. I ran down the hill, hoping to steal a skiff along the shore, but there were still people around, so I hid in an old, broken-down barrel shop by the river. I stayed there all night. By morning, boats were passing by, and everyone was talking about how your father had come to town claiming you were killed. I felt terrible that you were dead, Huck, but I am glad to see you now. I stayed hidden under the wood shavings all day. I was hungry, but I was not afraid because I knew Miss Watson and the widow would be at a camp meeting all day. They assumed I was out with the cattle, so they would not miss me until dark. The other servants would be gone too, taking the day off. When it got dark, I walked two miles up the river road to where there were no houses. I had decided on a plan. If I kept running on foot, the dogs would track me. If I stole a boat to cross the river, they would notice it was missing and know exactly where to look for me on the other side."
}

