  "rewritten_text": "During his brief visit, Emma had seen him rarely, yet enough to feel that their initial meeting was past and to conclude that his personality had not improved; he now carried an air of irritation and arrogance. She truly wondered how she had ever found him appealing. Seeing him was so linked to unpleasant memories that, unless viewed as a moral penance or a lesson in humility, she would have been relieved to never see him again. She wished him well, but his presence caused her distress, and she would be happiest knowing he was twenty miles away. His continued stay in Highbury would be easier to bear once he married. His wedding would resolve many awkward situations and allow their former intimacy to fade without comment, effectively resetting their social relationship. Emma gave little thought to the bride herself. She was likely suitable for Mr. Elton and Highbury, though she would probably look plain next to Harriet. Emma was unconcerned about the woman's social standing, convinced that despite Mr. Elton’s earlier disdain for Harriet, he had not married into a superior family. While her character remained a mystery, her background was clear: she brought no notable name or connections. Miss Hawkins was the daughter of a Bristol merchant, though his modest income suggested a similarly modest trade. She had spent winters in Bath, but her home was in Bristol, where she lived with an uncle who worked in the legal field—likely a low-level clerk. The family's only claim to prestige rested on an older sister who had married a man near Bristol wealthy enough to own two carriages. That was the extent of Miss Hawkins’s social glory."
}

