parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0ecdf47da9fe35239b9bc3bb",
  "rewritten_text": "Wickham was so content with their talk that he never again bothered himself or annoyed Elizabeth by bringing it up, and she was glad her words had been enough to silence him. The day of his and Lydia’s departure arrived, and Mrs. Bennet had to accept a separation that, since her husband refused to move the whole family to Newcastle, would likely last at least a year. Oh, my dear Lydia, she cried, when will we see each other again? Goodness, I don’t know. Maybe not for two or three years. Write to me often, my dear. As often as I can, but you know married women don’t have much time to write. My sisters can write to me; they will have nothing else to do. Wickham’s goodbyes were far warmer than his wife’s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said many charming things. He is as fine a man as I have ever seen, Mr. Bennet remarked once they had left. He simpers and flirts with us all. I am incredibly proud of him. I challenge even Sir William Lucas to produce a more valuable son-in-law. The loss of her daughter left Mrs. Bennet feeling quite down for several days. I often think there is nothing worse than parting with friends, she said. One feels so lonely without them. This is the result of marrying off a daughter, Elizabeth noted. It should make you more content that your other four are still single. Not at all. Lydia is not leaving because she is married, but only because her husband’s regiment is stationed so far away. If they were closer, she would not have left so soon. However, her low spirits were soon lifted by a new rumor. The housekeeper at Netherfield had been ordered to prepare for her master’s arrival; he was coming in a few days to shoot for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was in a state of excitement, alternating between smiling at Jane and shaking her head. Well, so Mr. Bingley is returning, she told her sister, Mrs. Philips, who had brought the news. That is fine. Not that I care, of course. He means nothing to us, and I am sure I never want to see him again. Still, he is welcome to visit Netherfield if he wishes."
}

