parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "40d9308e80192bb0f3c585f8",
  "rewritten_text": "While reaching through the valve with a cup of water for the cat, my shirt sleeve caught on the basket's suspension loop, instantly detaching it. It vanished from sight as if it had dissolved into thin air; less than a tenth of a second passed between its release and its total disappearance. I wished them well, though I held no hope that the cat or her kittens could survive the fall. By six o'clock, a thick shadow began covering the earth to the east, and by five minutes to seven, the entire view was swallowed by darkness. Still, the setting sun continued to light the balloon for some time, which gave me great satisfaction. I realized that, at this altitude, I would see the sunrise hours before the people of Rotterdam, and each day I would enjoy longer periods of sunlight. I decided to keep a journal, tracking time in continuous twenty-four-hour cycles regardless of the darkness. At ten o'clock, feeling tired, I prepared to sleep, but suddenly faced a problem I had overlooked: how would the cabin air be refreshed while I slept? Breathing the same air for more than an hour or so would be impossible and potentially fatal. This dilemma deeply unsettled me; it is strange that after surviving so many dangers, I considered abandoning my mission entirely over this issue. However, my hesitation lasted only a moment."
}

