parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2518d8eee4a1fdda8d66b173",
  "rewritten_text": "The surrounding oaks sheltered a colony of nightingales—I am there now, with Idris, in the prime of youth, by my side. I am twenty-two, and she has seen barely seventeen summers. The river, swollen by autumn rains, flooded the lowlands, and Adrian was busy in his boat, dangerously reaching for the highest branch of a submerged oak. I wondered if he was tired of living to toy with such peril. He secured his prize and navigated the flood. We watched anxiously as the current swept him away, forcing him to land far downstream and take a long path back to us. Idris cheered when he finally jumped ashore, waving the branch in triumph. We decided to wait for him. Alone together as the sun set, we listened to the nightingales while the evening star glowed in the fading western light. Idris gazed at it, remarking on how its flickering light seemed to mirror the wavering, uncertain nature of our own lives, full of fear and love. I begged her not to look at the star or seek meaning in its light, but to look at me instead. I told her I had long been desperate to confess my feelings and surrender my entire being to her. I asked the star to witness my vow: my love for her was as essential as light to that star, and it would endure until my end. The joy of that moment remains private. I still feel her against me and my breath still hitches at the memory of that first kiss. We walked in silence to meet Adrian. Later, after he took his sister home, I asked him to return. Walking through the moonlit woods, I confessed my love and hope to him. He looked troubled for a moment and said he should have seen this coming, noting the conflict that would surely follow."
}

