parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "04b06c068156769c1e415986",
  "rewritten_text": "The day passed much like the one before. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley spent the morning with the patient, who was slowly recovering, and Elizabeth joined them in the drawing-room that evening. There was no card game. Mr. Darcy was writing, while Miss Bingley sat nearby, watching him and constantly interrupting to send messages to his sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley played cards, watched by Mrs. Hurst. Elizabeth worked on her needlepoint, amused by the interaction between Darcy and Miss Bingley. The lady kept praising his handwriting and the length of his letter, while Darcy remained indifferent, which matched Elizabeth’s low opinion of both. Miss Bingley remarked on how happy Miss Darcy would be to receive the letter, but Darcy did not reply. She commented on how fast he wrote, but he corrected her, saying he wrote slowly. She complained about how tedious business letters would be, and he replied that it was fortunate they were his responsibility, not hers. When she asked him to tell his sister she wanted to see her, he noted he had already included that. She offered to fix his pen, but he declined, saying he always mended his own. When she asked how he wrote so neatly, he remained silent. She then asked him to tell his sister she loved her progress on the harp and her table design, but Darcy said he lacked the space to include such praise in this letter. She asked if he always wrote such long, charming letters, and he replied that while they were long, he could not judge if they were charming. Miss Bingley insisted that anyone who could write a long letter easily must write well. Her brother teased Darcy, saying he did not write with ease because he spent too much time choosing complex words. Darcy replied that his writing style was quite different from Bingley’s, to which Miss Bingley added that her brother wrote in a very careless manner."
}

