  "rewritten_text": "Emma Woodhouse was beautiful, clever, and wealthy, living in a comfortable home with a cheerful personality. She seemed to possess the best of life's blessings and had spent nearly twenty-one years with very little to trouble her. As the younger of two daughters to a doting father, she had managed his household since her sister married. Her mother had passed away when Emma was very young, leaving her with only faint memories. Her place was filled by an excellent governess who loved Emma like a mother. Miss Taylor had been with the family for sixteen years, acting more as a friend than a teacher. She was especially close to Emma, and their relationship was like that of sisters. Because Miss Taylor was so gentle, she never truly exerted authority, and they lived as equals. Emma valued Miss Taylor's advice but ultimately followed her own path. The only real downsides to Emma's life were that she was used to getting her own way and tended to be a bit too confident. These flaws were not yet a problem for her. However, a gentle sorrow arrived when Miss Taylor married. On the wedding day, Emma felt a deep sense of loss. After the guests left, she and her father were left to dine alone, with no one else to brighten the long evening. Once her father fell asleep, Emma was left to reflect on her loneliness. Although the marriage was a happy one for her friend—as Mr. Weston was a kind, wealthy, and pleasant man—it was a difficult day for Emma. She knew she would miss Miss Taylor every hour. She thought back on sixteen years of kindness, remembering how Miss Taylor had taught her, played with her, and cared for her through every childhood illness."
}

