parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "1ba379f1cf2a561a03251a12",
  "rewritten_text": "Emma found no reason to change her negative opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her initial assessment proved accurate; every meeting confirmed that the woman was self-important, presumptuous, overly familiar, ignorant, and rude. While Mrs. Elton possessed some beauty and minor talents, she lacked the judgment to realize she was not the sophisticated expert she imagined herself to be. She arrived in the country believing she was there to improve the neighborhood, convinced that her own social standing was superior to everyone else's. Mr. Elton appeared just as misguided, seeming not only happy with his wife but genuinely proud of her. He acted as though he had brought a woman to Highbury who surpassed even Emma in quality. Most of their new acquaintances, influenced by Miss Bates’s enthusiasm or simply willing to accept the bride at face value, were easily satisfied. Consequently, praise for Mrs. Elton spread freely, while Emma kept her own counsel, politely describing her as pleasant and well-dressed. Over time, Mrs. Elton’s behavior worsened. Offended by Emma’s lack of interest in a close friendship, she became cold and distant. Although this distance was a relief, the hostility behind it only deepened Emma’s dislike. Furthermore, both Mr. and Mrs. Elton treated Harriet with sneering neglect. Emma hoped this would help Harriet move on, but the couple’s behavior revealed their poor character. It was clear they had shared details of Harriet’s past with each other, likely painting Emma in the worst possible light to soothe Mr. Elton’s ego. As a result, the couple united in their dislike of Emma, frequently mocking her when they had nothing else to discuss. Since they dared not show open disrespect to Emma herself, they vented their spite by treating Harriet with contempt. Meanwhile, Mrs. Elton took an immediate liking to Jane Fairfax. She was not content with merely admiring her; she insisted on acting as a patron, offering unwanted help and friendship. By their third meeting, Emma had already heard all about Mrs. Elton’s self-appointed mission to champion Jane."
}

