parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c91d716e9a4c8af51cc76989",
  "rewritten_text": "Not everyone would have been so persistent as she was, refusing to accept Jane’s initial refusal. She flatly declared she would not write a rejection yesterday as Jane requested, insisting on waiting. Sure enough, by last night, it was decided that Jane would go. It was a complete surprise to me; I had no idea. Jane pulled Mrs. Elton aside and announced that, after considering the benefits of Mrs. Smallridge’s position, she had decided to accept. I knew nothing of it until it was finalized. You spent the evening with Mrs. Elton? Yes, all of us. Mrs. Elton insisted we come. It was arranged on the hill while we were walking with Mr. Knightley. She told us we all had to join her. Mr. Knightley was there too? No, he declined from the start. Even though Mrs. Elton claimed she wouldn't let him off the hook, he didn't show. My mother, Jane, and I were there, and we had a pleasant evening. Kind friends are always agreeable, Miss Woodhouse, though everyone seemed exhausted after the morning outing. Even pleasure is tiring, and I cannot say anyone seemed to enjoy it much. Still, I will always consider it a pleasant party and feel grateful to the friends who included me. I suppose Miss Fairfax had been making up her mind throughout the day? I expect so. Whenever the time comes, it will be difficult for her and her friends, but I hope her position has every possible advantage regarding the family's character and manners. Thank you, Miss Woodhouse. Indeed, everything is in place to make her happy. Aside from the Sucklings and Bragges, there is no nursery establishment as liberal or elegant in all of Mrs. Elton’s circle. Mrs. Smallridge is a delightful woman, and their lifestyle is nearly equal to Maple Grove. As for the children, they are the most elegant, sweet children anywhere, aside from the little Sucklings and Bragges. Jane will be treated with such regard and kindness; it will be a life of pure pleasure. And her salary! I really cannot venture to mention her salary to you, Miss Woodhouse."
}

