parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e9fa23bf948e162c1766b469",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Otis felt troubled by Lord Canterville’s refusal and urged him to rethink, but the kindly peer remained resolute. He eventually persuaded the Minister to let his daughter keep the ghost’s gift. When the young Duchess of Cheshire was presented at the Queen’s drawing-room in the spring of 1890 following her wedding, her jewels were widely admired. Virginia had earned the coronet that rewards virtuous American girls and wed her youthful suitor once he reached his majority. Their mutual affection was so delightful that everyone approved of the union, save for the Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had unsuccessfully sought the Duke for one of her seven daughters, and, oddly, Mr. Otis himself. While fond of the young Duke, Mr. Otis held theoretical objections to titles, fearing that the decadent influence of a pleasure-seeking aristocracy might erode republican values. These concerns were ultimately dismissed, and as he escorted his daughter down the aisle at St. George’s, Hanover Square, he was surely the proudest man in England. Following their honeymoon, the Duke and Duchess visited Canterville Chase, walking the next afternoon to the secluded churchyard near the pines. After much debate regarding Sir Simon’s tombstone, they had settled on his initials and the library window verse. Virginia scattered roses over the grave before they wandered into the abbey’s ruined chancel. The Duchess rested on a collapsed pillar while her husband reclined at her feet, smoking and gazing at her. He soon discarded his cigarette, grasped her hand, and remarked that a wife should keep no secrets from her husband. Virginia replied that she had none, but he smiled and insisted she had never revealed what occurred while she was trapped with the ghost. She answered solemnly that she had never told anyone."
}

