parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d604a3eff2c83237efa63ea0",
  "rewritten_text": "Colonel Grangerford was a true gentleman, as was his entire family. He came from a distinguished background, a trait the Widow Douglas valued as highly in men as in horses, and even my father agreed, despite his own lack of refinement. The Colonel was tall and slender, with a pale, sallow complexion. He shaved his thin face daily, revealing sharp features: thin lips, narrow nostrils, a prominent nose, and heavy brows over deep-set, dark eyes that seemed to peer from caverns. His hair was straight, black, and shoulder-length. He wore pristine white linen daily, adding a blue tail-coat with brass buttons on Sundays, and carried a silver-headed mahogany cane. He was serious and soft-spoken, yet radiated a kindness that inspired immediate trust. While his rare smile was pleasant, his sudden, rigid anger was terrifying. He never needed to demand respect; his presence alone ensured good behavior. He brought a sense of warmth to any room, and his brief moments of displeasure were enough to keep everyone in line for days. When he and his wife entered the room each morning, the family stood until they were seated. Tom and Bob would prepare bitters for him, waiting to toast him with a formal, \"Our duty to you, sir, and madam.\" The Colonel and his wife would acknowledge them with a slight bow, and after they drank, the sons would prepare a small mixture of water, sugar, and spirits for Buck and me to join in the toast."
}

