parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "960311e777e434f56176072a",
  "rewritten_text": "Do you play? I think, sir, the spy said, turning to Mr. Lorry with a servile air, that I might appeal to a man of your age and kindness to ask this younger gentleman if he can justify playing the Ace he mentioned. I admit I am a spy, a disreputable job that someone must perform, but this man is not; why should he lower himself to act like one? I will play my Ace, Mr. Barsad, Carton replied, checking his watch, without hesitation in a few minutes. I had hoped, gentlemen, the spy said, trying to draw Mr. Lorry into the argument, that your respect for my sister— I could not better show my respect for your sister than by finally removing her brother from her life, Sydney Carton interrupted. You think not, sir? I have fully decided. The spy’s smooth tone, which clashed with his rough clothes, faltered against Carton’s inscrutable nature. As the spy hesitated, Carton continued, as if studying his cards: And now that I think of it, I have another strong card. That fellow-informant who claimed to be working in the country prisons; who was he? He was French. You do not know him, the spy said quickly. French, eh? Carton mused, echoing him. Well, perhaps. He is, I assure you, though it is not important, the spy insisted. Though it is not important, Carton repeated mechanically. Yet I recognize the face. I think not. I am certain not. It cannot be, the spy said. It cannot be, Carton muttered, swirling his glass. He spoke good French, yet like a foreigner, I thought? Provincial, the spy suggested. No, foreign! Carton cried, striking the table as realization dawned. Cly! Disguised, but the same man. We had him before us at the Old Bailey. Now, you are being hasty, sir, Barsad said, with a smile that twisted his nose; you are really giving me an advantage over you."
}

