parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c8712be03ca6da888d9949c6",
  "rewritten_text": "A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired companion were riding through Brockley meadows when she snagged her riding habit on a hedge. Upon returning, she decided to use the back stairs to avoid being spotted. Passing the open door of the Tapestry Chamber, she thought she saw her mother’s maid and peered inside to request a repair. To her shock, she found the Canterville Ghost sitting by the window, gazing at the decaying autumn foliage. He appeared so dejected and disheveled that Virginia, initially inclined to flee, felt a surge of sympathy and resolved to console him. Her approach was so quiet and his sorrow so profound that he did not notice her until she spoke. I am sorry for you, she said, but my brothers return to Eton tomorrow, and if you act properly, no one will bother you. It is ridiculous to ask me to behave, he replied, turning in surprise toward the girl. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and wander at night; it is my sole purpose. That is no reason to exist, and you know you have been wicked, she countered. Mrs. Umney told us you murdered your wife. I admit it, the Ghost said peevishly, but that was a private family affair. Killing someone is wrong, Virginia insisted with a touch of Puritan solemnity. I despise such moralizing, he retorted. My wife was plain, failed to starch my ruffs, and was a poor cook. She even ruined a magnificent buck I had shot in Hogley Woods. Regardless, it is over now, though I still think it was unkind of her brothers to starve me to death in retaliation."
}

