{
  "request_id": "331fb48f35469640e3038a08",
  "rewritten_text": "After a brief silence, Mr. Lorry placed a hand on the Doctor’s arm. \"To what do you attribute this episode?\" he asked. The Doctor replied, \"I believe it was triggered by a powerful resurgence of the memories that first caused his illness. Distressing associations were vividly brought to mind. He likely feared these triggers for a long time, and his attempt to brace himself may have only weakened his resolve.\" When Mr. Lorry asked if the patient would remember the relapse, the Doctor looked around the room and quietly shook his head. \"Not at all.\" Regarding the future, the Doctor regained his composure. \"I am hopeful. Since he recovered so quickly, I believe the worst has passed.\" Mr. Lorry expressed his relief, and the Doctor bowed his head in gratitude. \"There are two other points I must discuss,\" Mr. Lorry continued. \"You may proceed,\" the Doctor said, offering his hand. \"He is studious and energetic, applying himself intensely to his work. Is he doing too much?\" The Doctor considered this. \"I think not. His mind requires constant occupation, perhaps due to his past trauma. Without healthy distractions, his thoughts might drift toward the unhealthy. He may have realized this himself.\" Mr. Lorry pressed further, \"Are you certain he is not under too much strain?\" The Doctor insisted he was sure, adding that his intense work served as a necessary counterweight to his past suffering. Mr. Lorry persisted, \"If he were overworked, would it manifest as a return of this disorder?\""
}

