  "request_id": "44b6d5e7578804c75160e103",
  "rewritten_text": "I examined the skull closely. The outer edges, nearest the vellum’s border, were sharper than the rest, suggesting the heat had been applied unevenly. I lit a fire and exposed the entire parchment to the warmth. Initially, only the skull’s faint lines darkened, but as I persisted, a figure appeared in the corner diagonally opposite the skull. I first thought it was a goat, but closer inspection convinced me it was a kid. I laughed, telling Legrand that while a fortune was no laughing matter, he shouldn't try to link pirates to goats, as they were agricultural creatures. He corrected me, noting it was not a goat. I replied that a kid was essentially the same. He explained that he viewed the animal as a punning signature for Captain Kidd, given its placement. The skull, positioned diagonally, seemed like a seal. Still, I was frustrated by the lack of accompanying text. I assumed he had hoped for a letter between the seal and signature. He agreed, admitting he felt a strong premonition of great fortune. He confessed that Jupiter’s foolish remark about the golden bug had sparked his imagination. He marveled at the series of coincidences, noting how the rare cool weather allowed for a fire, and how, without that fire or the dog’s timely arrival, he would never have discovered the skull or the treasure."
}

