parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eaafa81c5fe84d03552c54c4",
  "rewritten_text": "Citizens, butchers, cattle drivers, street vendors, children, thieves, and vagrants of every sort were packed into a chaotic mass. The whistling of drovers, barking dogs, bellowing oxen, bleating sheep, and squealing pigs mixed with the shouts, curses, and arguments of the crowd. Bells rang, taverns roared with noise, and people shoved and yelled in a hideous, discordant din. The unwashed, disheveled figures darting through the throng created a scene so overwhelming it dazed the senses. Mr. Sikes dragged Oliver through the thickest part of the crowd, ignoring the sights and sounds that stunned the boy. He nodded to a few acquaintances and declined several offers for a drink, pushing forward until they reached Holborn. Look here, kid, Sikes said, checking the church clock. It is nearly seven. Keep moving and do not lag behind, you lazy boy. Sikes jerked Oliver’s wrist, forcing the boy to trot to keep pace with his long strides. They continued until they passed Hyde Park Corner toward Kensington, where Sikes slowed down to wait for an approaching cart. Seeing Hounslow written on the side, he politely asked the driver for a ride to Isleworth. Jump in, the driver said. Is that your boy? Yes, he is my boy, Sikes replied, staring at Oliver while his hand drifted toward his pocketed pistol. Your father walks a bit fast for you, does he not? the driver asked, noticing Oliver was breathless. Not at all, Sikes interrupted. He is used to it. Come on, take my hand, Ned. Get in. Sikes helped Oliver into the cart, where the driver told him to rest on a pile of sacks. As they passed each milestone, Oliver wondered where he was being taken. They traveled through Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford, continuing as steadily as if they had only just begun."
}

