parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f4c95e961e612aa23a7221bc",
  "rewritten_text": "Must I be carried to the heavens on beds of ease, while others battle for the prize and sail through bloody seas? He was considered an excellent reader. At church gatherings, he was always asked to recite poetry; when he finished, the women would raise their hands, let them drop helplessly, roll their eyes, and shake their heads, signaling that the beauty was beyond mortal expression. After the hymn, Reverend Sprague acted as a bulletin board, reading announcements for meetings and societies until it felt like the list would last forever—a strange custom that persists in America, even in cities, despite the prevalence of newspapers. Often, the less a tradition is justified, the harder it is to abandon. Then, the minister prayed. It was a generous, detailed prayer: he pleaded for the church and its children, the village, the county, the state, the government, the President, Congress, sailors at sea, the oppressed under foreign tyrannies, those who ignore the truth, and the distant heathens, concluding with a hope that his sermon would fall on fertile ground. Amen. The congregation sat down with a rustle of fabric. The boy at the center of this story did not enjoy the prayer; he merely endured it. He was restless, keeping track of the familiar points out of habit. When the minister added new details, the boy felt resentful, viewing such additions as unfair. During the prayer, a fly landed on the pew in front of him, tormenting him by calmly grooming itself. It rubbed its hands, polished its head until the neck seemed ready to snap, and smoothed its wings like coat-tails, performing its toilet with the tranquility of one who knew it was perfectly safe."
}

