  "request_id": "0f696628d1bad106f9d6433f",
  "rewritten_text": "I will now share my own story. My early life holds little of note, so I will be brief. However, I must reflect on my childhood to show how, when my only hope vanished and the one affection I was allowed to hold was destroyed, my life effectively ended. My aunt was nothing like my father. While she was not a bad person, she possessed the coldest heart imaginable, entirely incapable of love. She took me in out of a sense of duty, but having lived alone for years, she refused to let a child disturb her peace. She was unmarried and had spent five years living in solitude on her inherited estate by Loch Lomond in Scotland. My father had wanted her to live with me at our family mansion near Richmond, but she refused. Instead, once she settled my father’s affairs, she took me with her to Scotland. Until I was eight, I was raised by my mother’s former servant. I lived in a distant part of the house and only saw my aunt twice a day: once at noon in my nursery, and once after her dinner. She never showed me affection and seemed constantly worried that I might annoy her. My nurse always coached me carefully before these visits, and because I was so intimidated by my aunt’s cold demeanor, I remained perfectly still and quiet during our brief meetings. Under my nurse’s care, I spent my time roaming the park and fields. Born of deep love, I was a sensitive child, and I felt a passionate attachment to everything around me, even the inanimate objects."
}

