parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d64684de27233b67fd2bd594",
  "rewritten_text": "We shared a mutual affection for Adrian, though she, still a child, could not fully grasp his virtues or align with his views as I did. I spent all my time with him. His gentle, sensitive nature lent a delicate, ethereal quality to our talks. He was as joyful as a singing lark, as intellectually soaring as an eagle, and as pure as a dove. He could lighten Perdita’s gravity and soothe my own restless, agonizing intensity. I viewed my past struggles and desires as a fading nightmare, feeling as transformed as if I had entered a new body with a fresh perspective on the world. Yet, I remained fundamentally the same in my strength and longing for connection. My masculine vigor persisted, softened and refined by his influence. Adrian did more than teach me history and philosophy; while helping me tame my wild spirit, he revealed his own heart, allowing me to grasp its remarkable depth. The former queen of England had tried since his infancy to instill ambition in her son. Recognizing his genius, she nurtured his talents to serve her own ends. She fostered his thirst for knowledge and his bold courage, hoping his love of liberty would eventually turn into a hunger for power. She sought to cultivate his resentment toward those who caused his father’s downfall, but failed. Instead, the stories of a nation claiming its right to self-governance earned his admiration, and he became a republican by conviction. Undeterred, his mother combined his pride of birth with patience and ambition. She studied his character intently, using praise and criticism to influence him. Though the results often disappointed her, she relied on his immense talent, confident she would eventually prevail."
}

