parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "35f5e65122ecf06828e42945",
  "rewritten_text": "She would be surrounded by those who loved her and possessed better judgment than she did; sufficiently secluded for safety and busy enough to remain cheerful. She would never be tempted, nor would temptation find her. She would be respectable and happy, and Emma conceded that she was the luckiest person alive to have inspired such steady, enduring devotion in such a man—or, if not the luckiest, second only to herself. Harriet, increasingly occupied by her commitments to the Martins, spent less time at Hartfield, which was for the best. Their intimacy was bound to fade, shifting into a more tempered goodwill, and this natural transition was already occurring. By late September, Emma accompanied Harriet to church, watching her marry Robert Martin with a satisfaction that no memory of Mr. Elton could diminish. Indeed, she barely noticed Mr. Elton, viewing him only as the clergyman whose blessing might soon be bestowed upon her. Robert Martin and Harriet Smith were the first of the three engaged couples to wed. Jane Fairfax had left Highbury to return to the comfort of the Campbells, and the Churchills were in town, awaiting November. Emma and Mr. Knightley tentatively chose the intervening month for their own wedding, hoping to marry while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield so they could enjoy a two-week seaside trip. Everyone approved, but how could they convince Mr. Woodhouse, who still spoke of the marriage as a distant prospect? When first approached, he was so distressed that they nearly lost hope. A second mention caused less pain; he began to accept the inevitable, a promising sign of resignation. Yet he remained unhappy, and his visible misery sapped his daughter’s resolve. She could not bear to see him suffer or feel neglected. Although she largely believed the Knightleys' assurances that his distress would vanish once the wedding was over, she hesitated, unable to move forward."
}

