parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "fcd1ae23e776abf0a2bff0ec",
  "rewritten_text": "The beetle was remarkably heavy, and I could hardly blame Jupiter for his assessment of it. However, I could not fathom why Legrand agreed with him. When I finished examining the insect, Legrand spoke grandly, saying he had summoned me for my counsel and assistance in furthering the designs of Fate and the bug. I interrupted, insisting he was unwell and needed rest, suggesting I stay with him until he recovered from his fever. He told me to check his pulse, but I found no sign of fever. I urged him to go to bed, but he insisted he was as well as he could be given his current excitement, which I could relieve by helping him. He explained that he and Jupiter were heading into the mainland hills and needed a trusted companion. I asked if the beetle was connected to this expedition. When he confirmed it was, I refused to participate in such an absurd plan. He expressed regret, saying they would have to go alone. Fearing he had lost his mind, I asked how long they would be gone. He said they would leave immediately and return by sunrise. I made him promise that once this obsession was settled, he would follow my medical advice. He agreed, and we set off. With a heavy heart, I joined Legrand, Jupiter, and the dog. Jupiter carried the scythe and spades, seemingly more to keep the tools away from his master than out of any desire to work."
}

