parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "23e239fb69f05b8edb4a4e7c",
  "rewritten_text": "My father’s last name was Pirrip and my first name was Philip, but as a baby I could only manage to say Pip. So, I called myself Pip, and that is what everyone called me. I know my father’s name from his tombstone and my sister, Mrs. Joe Gargery, who married the local blacksmith. Since I never met my parents or saw pictures of them—as they lived before photography—I imagined what they looked like based on their graves. The square letters on my father’s stone made me think he was a stout, dark man with curly hair. From the inscription about my mother, Georgiana, I childishly decided she was sickly and freckled. Beside their grave were five small stones for my brothers who died in infancy. I grew up believing they were born lying on their backs with their hands in their pockets, never taking them out. We lived in the marsh country near the river, about twenty miles from the sea. My first clear memory of the world comes from a cold, raw afternoon. That day, I realized this overgrown, bleak place was the churchyard where my parents and brothers were buried. I saw the dark, flat marshes filled with dikes and cattle, the grey river, and the distant, wild sea where the wind originated. I realized the small, shivering boy crying in fear was me. Suddenly, a terrifying voice shouted, \"Be quiet!\" A man jumped up from behind the gravestones. \"Stay still, you little devil, or I’ll cut your throat!\" He was a frightening man dressed in coarse grey clothes, with a heavy iron shackle on his leg."
}

