{
  "request_id": "22684167cc731dc09831d83d",
  "rewritten_text": "As they exited the dining hall, the clock tower struck midnight. Following the final chime, a crash and a sharp scream echoed, accompanied by a thunderous tremor that rattled the house. Ethereal music drifted by, a staircase panel snapped open, and Virginia emerged onto the landing, pale and clutching a small casket. The family hurried to her; Mrs. Otis embraced her, the Duke kissed her repeatedly, and the twins danced wildly. Good heavens, where have you been? Mr. Otis demanded, annoyed, assuming she had played a prank. Cecil and I searched everywhere, and your mother was terrified. Do not pull such stunts again. Except on the Ghost! the twins shouted. My darling, thank God you are safe; never leave me again, Mrs. Otis whispered, stroking her daughter's hair. Papa, Virginia said calmly, I was with the Ghost. He has passed away, and you must see him. He was wicked, but he felt remorse and gave me these jewels before he died. The family stared in stunned silence as she led them through the wall into a secret passage, Washington lighting the way with a candle. They reached a heavy oak door reinforced with rusted nails. It swung open to reveal a small, vaulted chamber with a single barred window. A skeletal figure was chained to an iron ring in the wall, lying prone on the floor, its bony fingers reaching toward a trencher and ewer just beyond its grasp. The jug held only mold, and the platter was covered in dust. Virginia knelt to pray silently while the others watched, awestruck by the grim mystery finally revealed."
}

