  "rewritten_text": "“Halt the starboard! Ting-a-ling-ling! Halt the larboard! Advance the starboard! Stop her! Let the outer wheel rotate slowly! Ting-a-ling-ling! Chow-ow-ow! Throw out that headline! Quickly now! Come—release the spring-line—what are you doing? Loop the bight around that stump! Prepare the stage—let it drop! Engines finished, sir! Ting-a-ling-ling! SH’T! S’H’T! SH’T!” (testing the gauge-cocks). Tom kept whitewashing, ignoring the steamboat. Ben watched for a moment and remarked: “Hi-Yi! You are in a predicament, aren't you!” Tom offered no reply. He examined his latest stroke with an artist’s gaze, brushed again, and inspected the result. Ben moved closer. Tom craved the apple but remained focused. Ben asked: “Hello, friend, forced to work, are you?” Tom spun around. “Oh, it’s you, Ben! I didn't see you.” “Listen, I am going swimming. Don't you wish you could? But you would rather work, wouldn't you? Of course you would!” Tom studied him briefly. “What do you define as work?” “Well, isn't that work?” Tom continued painting, answering casually: “Maybe it is, maybe it isn't. All I know is that it suits Tom Sawyer.” “Come now, you don't expect me to believe you enjoy it?” The brush kept moving. “Enjoy it? I don't see why I shouldn't. Does a boy get the opportunity to whitewash a fence every day?” This changed Ben’s perspective. He ceased eating his apple. Tom brushed delicately, stepped back to observe, added finishing touches, and critiqued his progress while Ben watched, increasingly fascinated. Finally, Ben asked: “Tom, let me whitewash a little.” Tom hesitated, nearly agreeing, but changed his mind. “No, I don't think that would work, Ben. Aunt Polly is extremely particular about this fence, since it faces the street. If it were the back fence, I wouldn't mind, and neither would she. She is very strict; it must be done perfectly. I doubt one boy in two thousand could handle it properly.” “Really? Is that so? Oh, please, let me try. Just a little—I would let you if you were me, Tom.” “Ben, I would like to, truly, but Aunt Polly—well, Jim wanted to do it, but she refused; Sid wanted to as well, and she wouldn't allow him either.”"
}

