parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5fc2c04cc6d9e6462c4e66d2",
  "rewritten_text": "She was right about the rats. Every so often, one would poke its nose out of a corner hole. She explained that she kept objects nearby to throw at them when she was alone, or they would never leave her in peace. She showed me a twisted bar of lead, claiming she was usually a good shot, but she had hurt her arm recently and was unsure of her aim. She waited for an opportunity and threw it at a rat, but she missed badly and winced in pain. She then asked me to try for the next one. I wanted to leave before her husband returned, but I did not show it. I took the lead, and when the next rat appeared, I threw it; if the rat had stayed put, it would have been badly hurt. She praised my aim and fetched the lead, then brought a bundle of yarn for me to help her with. As I held the yarn, she talked about her marriage, but suddenly interrupted herself. Keep your eye on the rats, she said. You should keep the lead in your lap. She dropped the lead into my lap, and I clamped my legs together to hold it. After a moment, she took the yarn away, looked me directly in the face, and asked pleasantly, Come now, what is your real name? Is it Bill, Tom, Bob, or what? I shook like a leaf, unsure of what to do. Please do not make fun of a poor girl like me, I said. If I am in the way, I will leave. No, you will not, she replied. Sit down. I will not hurt you or tell on you. Just tell me your secret and trust me. I will keep it, and I will help you. My husband will too. You are just a runaway apprentice, that is all. There is no harm in it. You were treated badly and decided to leave. Bless you, child, I would never tell on you."
}

