parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "71afe2fd1eec9746b684042e",
  "rewritten_text": "At one point, the lieutenant inquired why he had traveled so far across the ice in such an unusual craft. His face darkened with profound sorrow as he answered, \"I am searching for someone who escaped me.\" When asked if the man he chased used the same method of travel, he confirmed it. I mentioned that we had spotted a man on a dog-drawn sledge crossing the ice just before rescuing him. This caught his interest, and he began asking many questions about the path taken by the creature he called a dæmon. Later, when we were alone, he remarked that he had surely piqued my curiosity, yet I was too polite to pry. I agreed that it would be rude and cruel to bother him with questions, though he noted that I had saved his life. He then asked if the ice breaking might have destroyed the other sledge. I told him I could not be sure, as the ice did not fracture until late, and the traveler might have reached safety by then. Since then, a renewed vitality has stirred within the stranger. He is desperate to watch the deck for the sledge, but I have insisted he stay in the cabin, as he is too frail for the cold. I have assured him that we will alert him if anything appears. This concludes my record of these events. The stranger is recovering but remains quiet and anxious when others visit. Still, his gentle demeanor has won over the crew. I have grown to care for him like a brother, moved by his deep, persistent sadness. He must have been a remarkable man in his prime, for even in his current state, he is captivating. I once wrote to you, Margaret, that I would find no friend at sea, yet I have discovered a man whom I would have cherished as a brother had he not been so shattered by misfortune."
}

