parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d1355e195c1f5cf68e6bb49c",
  "rewritten_text": "Charles Evrémonde, known as Darnay, was finally brought to trial. The judges wore feathered hats, yet the red cap and tricolour cockade were the common attire. Observing the jury and the rowdy crowd, he felt as though the roles were inverted, with criminals judging the righteous. The city’s most vicious elements dominated the proceedings, shouting their opinions and dictating the outcome unchecked. Many men carried weapons, while women brandished knives or daggers, and some knitted while they watched. One woman, knitting intently, sat near a man Darnay recognized as Defarge. She whispered to him occasionally, appearing to be his wife, yet neither ever glanced at the prisoner, focusing instead on the jury with grim resolve. Doctor Manette sat beneath the President, dressed plainly, appearing alongside Mr. Lorry as the only observers not wearing the rough clothing of the revolution. The prosecutor charged Evrémonde as an emigrant, subject to the death penalty under a decree that ignored the timing of his return. The crowd clamored for his execution, branding him an enemy of the Republic. When the President silenced the room to question him, Darnay admitted to living in England for many years. Challenged on his status as an emigrant, Darnay argued that he did not fall under the law’s intent. He explained that he had abandoned his title and status long before the term was defined by the Tribunal, choosing instead to support himself through his own labor in England rather than exploiting the burdened people of France."
}

