parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "58c84be2341c11db9455dac7",
  "rewritten_text": "I avoid rushing him, which might lead to a clumsy failure. Instead, I approach from the side, moving slowly so he remains unaware of my intentions. Suddenly, he finds himself on his back, completely bewildered by the turn of events. That was my strategy with brother Dowley. I began speaking in a relaxed, casual manner, as if merely killing time, ensuring no one could possibly predict where my argument would lead. Boys, there are many fascinating aspects to law, custom, and tradition, as well as the evolution of human thought. Written laws fade, but unwritten ones endure. Consider the unwritten law of wages: it dictates a gradual, centuries-long increase. We observe current wages, calculate an average, and compare it to historical data from one or two centuries past. This provides a clear pattern of growth, allowing us to estimate wages from even further back without any written records. We then apply this same logic to the future. I can predict wage levels for centuries to come. What, goodman, what! Yes. In seven hundred years, wages in this region will increase sixfold, with farmhands earning three cents a day and mechanics six. I wish I could die now and be reborn then, exclaimed Smug the wheelwright, his eyes gleaming with greed. And that is not all; they will also receive board, though it will be meager. Two hundred and fifty years later, pay attention, a mechanic's wages will be twenty cents a day, and this is a certainty, not a guess. A collective gasp of amazement filled the room, and Dickon the mason murmured, his eyes and hands lifted in wonder."
}

