  "rewritten_text": "For a moment, I was too stunned to think clearly. I knew my own sketch differed in detail from this one, though the general outline was similar. I took a candle to the other side of the room to examine the parchment more closely. Turning it over, I found my own drawing on the back, exactly as I had left it. At first, I was merely surprised by the remarkable coincidence that a skull—matching my scarab in size and shape—had been on the other side of the paper all along. Such coincidences often leave the mind paralyzed as it struggles to find a logical connection. Once I recovered, however, a far more startling realization dawned on me. I clearly remembered that the parchment was blank when I drew the scarab. I was certain of this because I had checked both sides to find a clean spot. If the skull had been there, I would have seen it. This mystery felt impossible to explain, yet a faint intuition began to stir in my mind—a glimmer of the truth that last night’s events would eventually confirm. I stood up, secured the parchment, and stopped thinking about it until I could be alone. After you left and Jupiter fell asleep, I began a methodical investigation. I first considered how I acquired the parchment. We found the scarab on the mainland coast, a mile east of the island, just above the high-tide mark. When I picked it up, it bit me, causing me to drop it. Jupiter, with his usual caution, searched for a leaf to grab the insect after it flew toward him."
}

