parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0793d952ef365d4b809413bf",
  "rewritten_text": "We endured a few miserable hours until the trial began at eleven. My father and the family had to attend as witnesses, so I went with them. Throughout this pathetic mockery of justice, I suffered absolute agony. It was to be decided if my reckless curiosity would lead to the deaths of two people: one an innocent, joyful baby, the other a victim of a horrific, infamous murder. Justine was a virtuous girl with a bright future, yet now she faced a shameful death because of me. I would have gladly confessed to the crime myself, but my absence made such a claim seem like the ravings of a madman, and it would not have saved her. Justine appeared calm. Dressed in mourning, her naturally engaging face looked exquisitely beautiful in its solemnity. She seemed confident in her innocence and did not tremble, despite being stared at and cursed by thousands who viewed her as a monster. Her tranquility was clearly forced; having been accused of guilt earlier due to her confusion, she now summoned a facade of courage. Entering the courtroom, she scanned the room and spotted us. A tear clouded her eye, but she quickly regained her composure, her sorrowful, affectionate gaze confirming her innocence. The trial commenced, and after the prosecutor presented the charges, witnesses were called. Several bizarre facts emerged that might have convinced anyone lacking my knowledge of her innocence. She had been out all night when the murder occurred, and a market woman had seen her near the crime scene toward morning. When asked what she was doing there, she had acted strangely and given a confused, incoherent reply. She returned home at eight o’clock, and when asked where she had been, she claimed she was searching for the child and anxiously inquired if he had been found."
}

