parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "2e9fc201ee0fb49728cbf42b",
  "rewritten_text": "Waking up eventually, I would check the cause of a disturbance, perhaps spotting a steamboat chugging upstream, so distant toward the far bank that I could only distinguish if it had a stern or side wheel. Then, for an hour, there would be nothing to see or hear—just pure solitude. Later, a raft would drift past in the distance, likely with a fellow chopping wood, as they often did; I would watch the axe flash downward, yet hear nothing until the blade rose again, when the dull thud finally reached us across the water. We spent our days idling, soaking in the quiet. Once, during a dense fog, passing rafts banged tin pans to warn off steamboats. A vessel drifted so near we could clearly hear the crew swearing and laughing, yet they remained invisible, which felt eerie, like ghosts haunting the air. Jim insisted they were spirits, but I countered that ghosts would not curse the fog. When night fell, we pushed off, letting the current carry us while we smoked and dangled our legs in the water. We remained naked whenever the mosquitoes allowed, as the clothes from Buck’s family felt too restrictive, and I never cared much for dressing up anyway. Occasionally, the river belonged entirely to us. We would watch the distant banks and islands, spotting a faint candle glow from a cabin window or the occasional light on a passing raft, sometimes accompanied by the sound of a fiddle or a song. Life on a raft is wonderful. We would lie on our backs beneath the star-speckled sky, debating whether the stars were created or simply happened. Jim believed they were made, but I argued they just happened, as creating so many would have taken far too long."
}

