  "rewritten_text": "His temperament and intellect, though different from hers, would have fulfilled all her desires. It was a match that would have benefited both: her ease and vivacity might have softened his disposition and refined his manners, while she would have gained significantly from his judgment and worldly wisdom. Yet, no such ideal marriage would now demonstrate true marital bliss to the world. A different, inevitable union was soon to occur within their family instead. She could not imagine how Wickham and Lydia would maintain any independence, but she easily foresaw that a couple united only by passion rather than virtue could find little lasting happiness. Mr. Gardiner wrote to Mr. Bennet again. He briefly acknowledged Mr. Bennet’s thanks, expressed his commitment to the family’s welfare, and requested that the matter never be raised again. His main point was that Wickham had decided to leave the militia. He noted that he had strongly encouraged this once the marriage was settled, as leaving the regiment was advisable for both Wickham and his niece. Wickham intended to join the regular army, and former friends were willing to help him secure an ensigncy in a regiment stationed in the north. Being so far away was an advantage. Wickham made fair promises, and he hoped that among new acquaintances, where they would need to maintain their reputations, both would act more prudently. He had contacted Colonel Forster to settle Wickham’s debts in Brighton, for which he had personally vouched. He asked Mr. Bennet to provide similar assurances to Wickham’s creditors in Meryton, based on a list he provided. He hoped Wickham had been honest about his debts. Haggerston had their instructions, and everything would be finalized within a week. They would then join the regiment, unless they were first invited to Longbourn, as Mrs. Gardiner indicated that his niece was very eager to see the family before leaving the south."
}

