  "rewritten_text": "She would be surrounded by people who loved her and possessed better judgment than she did; living in a safe, quiet environment while staying busy enough to remain cheerful. She would be protected from temptation and live a respectable, happy life. Emma admitted that Harriet was incredibly lucky to have earned such steady devotion from a man like Robert Martin, though she felt she was perhaps the only person luckier than her. As Harriet spent more time with the Martins, she visited Hartfield less often, which was for the best. Their intense friendship was naturally fading into a more relaxed, friendly relationship. By late September, Emma watched Harriet marry Robert Martin with complete satisfaction, undisturbed by any thoughts of Mr. Elton. In fact, she barely noticed Mr. Elton, focusing instead on the idea that he might soon be officiating her own wedding. Harriet and Robert were the first of the three engaged couples to marry. Jane Fairfax had returned to her home with the Campbells, and the Churchills were waiting for November. Emma and Mr. Knightley hoped to marry in October while John and Isabella were still visiting Hartfield, allowing the couple to take a two-week seaside trip. Everyone approved of the plan, but they worried about how to convince Mr. Woodhouse, who still spoke of the wedding as a far-off event. When they first brought it up, he was so distressed that they nearly lost hope. A second attempt caused less pain, as he began to accept that the marriage was inevitable. Still, he remained unhappy, and seeing his suffering made Emma lose her nerve. Even though the Knightleys assured her that her father would recover quickly once the wedding was over, she hesitated to move forward."
}

