parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7ec0b5b8a320f1a80fdf3f1b",
  "rewritten_text": "Directly before him, illuminated by pale moonlight, stood a man of fearsome appearance. His eyes glowed like embers, long grey hair hung in tangled locks, and his archaic clothing was tattered and stained. Heavy, rusted shackles bound his wrists and ankles. Mr. Otis remarked that he must insist the chains be oiled, offering a bottle of Tammany Rising Sun Lubricator for the task. He noted its effectiveness, citing testimonials from prominent clergymen, and placed the bottle on a marble table before retiring to his room. The Canterville ghost stood frozen in shock, then hurled the bottle to the floor and fled, groaning and glowing with a sickly green light. As he reached the oak staircase, two figures in white emerged and hurled a pillow at him. Realizing he was under attack, he vanished through the wall, leaving the house silent. Reaching a hidden room, he leaned against a moonbeam to catch his breath, reflecting on his indignity. In three centuries of haunting, he had never been so insulted. He recalled the Duchess he had terrified, the hysterical housemaids, the rector he had traumatized, and Madame de Tremouillac, who had been bedridden for weeks after finding a skeleton reading her diary, eventually turning to the Church to escape her fright."
}

