parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "67b7c94b2590c8599c547f73",
  "rewritten_text": "I examined the skull drawing closely. The outer edges were clearer than the rest, suggesting the heat had not been applied evenly. I lit a fire and heated the entire parchment. At first, only the skull lines darkened, but as I continued, a figure appeared in the opposite corner. I initially thought it was a goat, but closer inspection revealed it was a kid. I laughed, telling Legrand that while a fortune was no laughing matter, he would not find a connection between pirates and a goat, as goats belonged to farmers. He corrected me, noting it was a kid, not a goat. I replied that they were essentially the same, but Legrand insisted they were different. He explained that he saw the animal as a punning signature for Captain Kidd, while the skull acted like a seal. He was frustrated by the lack of any accompanying text. I asked if he had hoped to find a letter between the seal and the signature. He admitted he had, driven by a strong feeling of impending good fortune. He confessed that Jupiter’s foolish comment about the gold bug had sparked his imagination. He marveled at the series of coincidences, noting that if it had not been cool enough for a fire that day, or if the dog had not appeared at that exact moment, he would never have discovered the skull or the treasure."
}

