parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7cfbbb3120d570fc9e5ade29",
  "rewritten_text": "Every normal boy eventually feels a strong urge to go dig for buried treasure. This desire suddenly struck Tom one day. He went to find Joe Harper but failed, and Ben Rogers was away fishing. Finally, he found Huck Finn, who was happy to join in. Huck was always ready for any adventure that was fun and cost nothing, as he had plenty of free time. Where should we dig, Huck asked. Oh, almost anywhere, Tom replied. Is it hidden everywhere, Huck wondered. No, Tom explained, it is hidden in specific spots like islands, under old dead trees where shadows fall at midnight, or under the floors of haunted houses. When Huck asked who hides it, Tom scoffed that it was obviously robbers, not Sunday-school leaders. Huck admitted he would rather spend the money than hide it, but Tom insisted robbers always leave it behind. They often forget where they put it or die, leaving the treasure to sit until someone finds a map covered in confusing symbols. When Huck asked if Tom had a map, Tom said no. He planned to search islands, haunted houses, and dead trees instead. When Huck pointed out that searching every tree would take all summer, Tom countered by asking how he would feel about finding a pot of gold or a chest of diamonds. Huck’s eyes lit up, and he agreed that finding even a hundred dollars would be wonderful."
}

