parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "75d204919480260f0fd81778",
  "rewritten_text": "They know how sharp he is; he will get a life sentence. They will make sure the Artful Dodger gets nothing less than life. What do you mean by lagging and a lifer? Mr. Bolter demanded. Why talk to me like that? Why not speak so I can understand you? Fagin was about to explain that these terms meant transportation for life, but the conversation was interrupted by Master Bates, who entered with his hands in his pockets and a look of mock misery on his face. It is all over, Fagin, Charley said, once he and his new companion were introduced. What do you mean? They found the man who owns the snuffbox; others are coming to identify him, and the Artful is booked for a trip overseas, Master Bates replied. I need a full mourning suit and a hatband to visit him before he leaves. To think of Jack Dawkins—the great Dodger—going away for a cheap, worthless snuffbox! I never thought he would go down for anything less than a gold watch and chain. Why didn't he rob some wealthy man of all his valuables and go out like a gentleman, rather than a common thief with no honor or glory? With this lament for his friend, Master Bates sat down, looking dejected. Why do you say he has no honor or glory? Fagin snapped, glaring at his pupil. Was he not always the best among you? Is there one of you who could match him? Not one, Master Bates replied, his voice thick with regret. Then why are you complaining? Fagin demanded. Why are you crying? Because it will not be on the record, will it? Charley retorted, his grief turning to defiance. It cannot be in the indictment; nobody will ever know how talented he was. How will he be remembered in the criminal records? Maybe he will not be mentioned at all. It is such a blow! Ha! ha! Fagin laughed, gesturing to Mr. Bolter while shaking with a fit of mirth. See what pride they take in their profession, my dear."
}

