parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "eb4338b6d5d7362823454db9",
  "rewritten_text": "Not everyone would have been so persistent as she was, refusing to accept Jane’s initial refusal. She insisted that Jane should not send a rejection letter yesterday, suggesting they wait instead. Sure enough, by last night, it was decided that Jane would take the position. It was a complete surprise to me; I had no idea. Jane pulled Mrs. Elton aside and explained that, after considering the benefits of Mrs. Smallridge’s offer, she had decided to accept it. I knew nothing about it until it was finalized. You spent the evening with Mrs. Elton? Yes, all of us. Mrs. Elton insisted we come. It was arranged while we were walking on the hill with Mr. Knightley. She told us we all had to join them for the evening. Mr. Knightley was there too? No, he declined from the start. Even though Mrs. Elton said she would not let him off the hook, he did not come. My mother, Jane, and I went, and we had a pleasant evening. Kind friends are always agreeable, though everyone seemed tired after the morning outing. Even pleasure can be exhausting, and I cannot say the others seemed to enjoy it much. Still, I will always consider it a lovely party and am grateful to the friends who invited me. I suppose Miss Fairfax had been making up her mind throughout the day? I imagine so. Whenever she leaves, it will be difficult for her and her friends, but I hope her new position is as comfortable as possible, especially regarding the family's character. Thank you, Miss Woodhouse. Indeed, everything points to her being happy there. Aside from the Sucklings and Bragges, there is no nursery establishment as generous or elegant in all of Mrs. Elton’s circle. Mrs. Smallridge is a delightful woman, and their lifestyle is nearly as grand as Maple Grove. As for the children, they are the most elegant, sweet children anywhere, aside from the Sucklings and Bragges. Jane will be treated with such kindness; it will be a life of pure pleasure. And her salary! I really cannot bring myself to mention the amount to you, Miss Woodhouse."
}

