  "rewritten_text": "Daylight arrived as I set the manuscript aside. The rain had nearly stopped, leaving the world gray and somber as the spent storm sighed itself into silence. I went to the stranger's room and listened at his door, which stood slightly open. Hearing his voice, I knocked, but received no response. I peeked inside to find him lying on his back, speaking in fragmented, spirited bursts while his arms thrashed with the restlessness of delirium. I entered quietly and leaned over him. As his muttering continued, I spoke a single word to catch his attention. His glassy eyes and pale face instantly brightened with joy and relief. Oh, Sandy, you have finally come. I have missed you so much. Sit by me and never leave again. Give me your hand; let me hold it. Now everything is peaceful and I am happy once more. You seem like a faint mist, but you are here, and that is enough. Don't pull away; I won't need it for long. Was that the child? Hello, Central? She doesn't answer. Bring her when she wakes so I can touch her hair and say goodbye. Sandy, you are still there. I drifted off and feared you had left. Have I been sick long? It feels like months. I have had such strange, terrifying dreams that felt entirely real. I dreamt the king was dead, that you were trapped in Gaul, and that a revolution had broken out. In my fever, I imagined Clarence and I fought and destroyed the entire chivalry of England. Even stranger, I felt like a man from a distant, future age. I seemed to have traveled back and forth through time, stranded as a lonely stranger in that foreign England, with thirteen centuries separating me from you, my home, and everything that makes life worth living."
}

