parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "de017f46094db9cb8d6a349b",
  "rewritten_text": "The following day was set for the confrontation to avoid any cooling of our honor, and Drummle arrived bearing a polite note from Estella confirming she had danced with him repeatedly. I had no choice but to express regret for my previous outburst and dismiss the notion that I was anywhere to be found. Drummle and I sat glaring at each other for an hour while the rest of the group argued, eventually concluding that our mutual goodwill had progressed remarkably. I recount this casually, yet it weighed heavily upon me. It caused me immense distress to imagine Estella favoring such a wretched, awkward, and sullen fool, who was so clearly beneath her. I still believe it was a spark of genuine, selfless love that made the idea of her stooping to such a man unbearable. While I would have been miserable regardless of her choice, a more suitable partner would have caused me a different sort of pain. I quickly discovered that Drummle was shadowing her and that she permitted it. Soon, he was constantly in pursuit, and we crossed paths daily. He persisted in his dull, stubborn way, while Estella kept him tethered, alternating between encouragement and disdain, sometimes flattering him and other times acting as if he were a stranger. The Spider, as Jaggers dubbed him, possessed the patient, predatory nature of his kind. Furthermore, he held a foolish confidence in his wealth and lineage that served him well, often mimicking the effect of true focus. Thus, by doggedly tracking Estella, he outlasted more capable rivals, ready to strike at the opportune moment. At an Assembly Ball in Richmond, where Estella eclipsed every other woman, Drummle hovered near her with her apparent consent. I decided to confront her about him, finding my chance when she sat alone among the flowers, waiting for Mrs. Blandley to escort her home."
}

