parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "aa3d724c4d58cc93ac35e28b",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Darcy spoke warmly of his sister’s musical talent. Lady Catherine replied that she was glad to hear it, but insisted that his sister would never excel without constant practice. Darcy assured her that his sister already practiced diligently. Lady Catherine insisted that one could never practice enough, adding that she often advised young ladies on the matter. She mentioned that she had told Miss Bennet she needed to practice more, and noted that Mrs. Collins was welcome to use the piano at Rosings whenever she liked. Mr. Darcy, embarrassed by his aunt’s rudeness, remained silent. After coffee, Colonel Fitzwilliam asked Elizabeth to play, and she sat at the piano. Lady Catherine listened briefly before turning to talk to Darcy, who eventually walked over to stand near Elizabeth, watching her closely. Elizabeth noticed him and, during a pause, smiled playfully. She told him that he was trying to intimidate her, but that she refused to be frightened, as her spirit only grew stronger when challenged. Darcy replied that he knew she did not truly believe he meant to scare her, noting that she enjoyed pretending to hold opinions she did not actually possess. Elizabeth laughed and told Colonel Fitzwilliam that his cousin was painting a poor picture of her, making it difficult for her to maintain the reputation she had hoped to establish."
}

