parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "75200041fcccb62077d2d4cd",
  "rewritten_text": "I am not eating at home, so I will not need you. Thank you, sir, the man replied, leaving the room. Now, Alan, we have no time to waste. This chest is heavy; I will carry it. You bring the rest. He spoke quickly and with authority. Campbell felt overwhelmed by him, and they left together. Upon reaching the landing, Dorian unlocked the door, then hesitated, a look of distress crossing his face. He shuddered. I do not think I can go in, Alan, he whispered. It makes no difference to me. I do not need you, Campbell said coldly. Dorian opened the door slightly. He saw his portrait leering in the sunlight, the torn curtain lying on the floor before it. He realized he had forgotten to cover the canvas the night before and started to rush forward, but recoiled in horror. What was that disgusting red dew glistening on one of the hands, as if the painting were sweating blood? It was more terrifying than the silent figure he knew lay on the table, whose distorted shadow on the carpet confirmed it had not moved. Taking a deep breath, he opened the door wider and stepped inside with his eyes half-closed and his head turned away, determined not to look at the corpse. He picked up the gold-and-purple cloth and threw it over the picture. He stood there, afraid to turn around, staring at the fabric pattern. He heard Campbell bring in the chest and the tools needed for his grim task. He wondered if Campbell and Basil Hallward had ever met. Leave me now, a stern voice commanded. He turned and hurried out, catching a glimpse of the dead man pushed back into the chair and Campbell staring at a glistening yellow face. As he descended the stairs, he heard the key turn in the lock. It was well past seven when Campbell returned to the library. He was pale but composed. I have done what you asked, he muttered."
}

