parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9b62e5d5798237fd6bf44979",
  "rewritten_text": "With no work to occupy him, he rhythmically cupped his knuckles into his palms, alternating hands and stroking his beard in a continuous, mechanical cycle. Pulling him from the mental drift that followed his speech felt like reviving someone from a faint or trying to extract a final confession from a dying man. \"Did you ask for my name?\" \"I certainly did.\" \"One Hundred and Five, North Tower.\" \"Is that all?\" \"One Hundred and Five, North Tower.\" With a sound that was neither a sigh nor a groan, he returned to his labor until the silence was broken again. \"You are not a shoemaker by trade?\" Mr. Lorry asked, watching him closely. The man’s hollow eyes darted to Defarge for help, but finding none, they returned to Lorry. \"I am not a shoemaker by trade? No. I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to--\" He drifted off again, his hands continuing their repetitive motion. Eventually, his gaze returned to Lorry, and he started, speaking as if waking from a dream. \"I asked permission to teach myself, and after a long time, I finally got it. I have made shoes ever since.\" As he reached for his work, Lorry persisted. \"Monsieur Manette, do you remember me?\" The shoe fell, and he stared intently at Lorry. \"Monsieur Manette,\" Lorry said, touching Defarge’s arm, \"do you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Does no memory of an old banker, a past business, or a former servant stir in your mind?\" As the long-time prisoner stared between them, faint traces of a once-sharp intellect flickered across his forehead, struggling through the fog of his mind. They clouded over, faded, and vanished, but for a moment, they had been there."
}

