parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "40553e18fe4093e958026145",
  "rewritten_text": "Observing a star indirectly—by shifting one's gaze so the outer retina catches the light—allows for a clearer view and a better sense of its brilliance, which fades when stared at directly. While more light hits the eye when looking straight on, the peripheral vision offers a more nuanced perception. Excessive intensity can confuse and weaken the mind; even Venus can disappear from sight if scrutinized with too much focus or persistence. Regarding these killings, let us investigate them ourselves before forming a judgment. It will be an interesting diversion, and Le Bon once helped me, so I owe him a debt. We shall visit the scene. I am acquainted with G—, the Prefect of Police, and can easily secure access. Permission was granted, and we traveled to the Rue Morgue, a dreary street between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. It was late when we arrived, as the location was far from our home. The house was easy to spot, as a crowd still lingered across the way, staring at the shuttered windows with aimless curiosity. It was a typical Parisian building featuring a gateway and a concierge's booth. Before entering, we walked the street, turned into an alley, and circled behind the structure, while Dupin inspected the area and the house with a level of detail I found inexplicable. Returning to the front, we rang the bell, presented our papers, and were let in by the officers. We ascended to the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye’s body had been discovered and where the victims remained. The disarray had been left undisturbed. I observed nothing beyond what the Gazette had reported. Dupin examined everything, including the corpses, before we toured the remaining rooms and the yard, escorted by a gendarme."
}

