  "rewritten_text": "It hardly matters. She replied, I might as well ask why you chose to tell me you loved me against your will and better judgment, while clearly intending to insult me. Was that not reason enough for my rudeness? But I have other grievances. Even if I had been indifferent to you, do you think I would marry the man who ruined my beloved sister’s happiness? Darcy changed color, but he remained silent as she continued. I have every reason to think poorly of you. You cannot deny that you were the main cause of their separation, exposing them both to public judgment and deep misery. She paused, noticing with anger that he seemed entirely unrepentant, even wearing a look of skeptical amusement. Can you deny it? she asked. He replied calmly, I have no desire to deny that I did everything possible to separate my friend from your sister, and I am glad I succeeded. I acted out of kindness to him. Elizabeth ignored his comment, though it only deepened her resentment. My dislike is not based solely on that, she continued. Long before that, I had already formed my opinion of you through Mr. Wickham. What can you say to defend yourself regarding him? Darcy’s face flushed as he replied, You take a keen interest in that gentleman’s affairs. Who could hear of his misfortunes and not care? she asked. His misfortunes! Darcy repeated with contempt. Yes, his misfortunes have been great indeed. And you are the one who caused them, Elizabeth exclaimed. You have reduced him to poverty."
}

