  "request_id": "4716b7be4a0def14595ef37c",
  "rewritten_text": "When the wedding occurred three weeks later, St. Peter’s overflowed with a fashionable crowd. The Dean of Chichester conducted the ceremony impressively, and observers noted the couple’s striking appearance. Beyond their looks, they were genuinely happy. Lord Arthur never regretted his sacrifices for Sybil, and she offered him her deepest devotion and affection. Reality did not diminish their romance; they remained forever youthful. Years later, after two children arrived, Lady Windermere visited Alton Priory, the Duke’s gift to his son. While sitting under a lime-tree watching the children play, she asked Sybil if she was happy. Sybil confirmed her joy and asked the same of her guest. Lady Windermere claimed she lacked time for happiness, noting she grew bored with people once she knew them. When asked about her social favorites, she dismissed them as dull once their novelty faded, specifically recalling the fraudulent Mr. Podgers, whose romantic advances had soured her on palmistry in favor of telepathy. Sybil warned her not to mock palmistry, as Arthur took it quite seriously. When Lady Windermere questioned this, Sybil directed her to Arthur, who approached with yellow roses and his children. Upon being asked if he truly believed in palmistry, Arthur smiled and replied that he owed his entire life’s happiness to it."
}

