parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "683fc4be41d84775079658c7",
  "rewritten_text": "Their arrival ignited a spark throughout Saint Antoine, quickly igniting faces at every doorway and window. Still, no one pursued them, and silence reigned as they stepped into the wine-shop, despite every gaze fixing upon them. Good day, gentlemen, Monsieur Defarge announced. This seemed to break the collective reserve, prompting a chorus of replies. It is poor weather, gentlemen, Defarge added, shaking his head. At this, the men exchanged glances before lowering their eyes in silence, save for one who rose and departed. My wife, Defarge called out to Madame Defarge, I have journeyed some distance with this road-mender, Jacques. I encountered him by chance a day and a half from Paris. He is a fine fellow, this Jacques. Serve him some wine! A second man stood and left. Madame Defarge placed wine before the road-mender, who tipped his blue cap and drank. He pulled coarse bread from his blouse, eating between sips near the counter. A third man rose and exited. Defarge took a modest drink himself, then waited for the traveler to finish. He ignored the others, and they ignored him, even Madame Defarge, who was busy with her knitting. Have you finished your meal, friend? he eventually asked. Yes, thank you. Then come! You shall see the room I mentioned. It will suit you perfectly. They moved from the shop to the street, through a courtyard, up a steep flight of stairs, and into a garret—the same room where an elderly man once sat hunched over, crafting shoes. The old man was gone, but the three men who had left the shop were waiting. The only connection to the former occupant was the memory of them peering through the wall's cracks. Defarge shut the door and whispered, Jacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness I, Jacques Four, have brought by appointment."
}

