parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9cc52c6724a1053221547050",
  "rewritten_text": "You should adopt my philosophy. Only think of the past when it brings you joy. I cannot credit you with such a philosophy. Your memories must be so free of regret that your contentment comes from ignorance, not wisdom. It is not so for me. Painful memories intrude, and they should. I have been selfish my entire life. As a child, I knew what was right, but I was never taught to control my temper. I was raised with good principles but allowed to follow them with pride and arrogance. As an only son, I was spoiled by my parents. They encouraged me to be selfish, to care only for my own family, and to look down on everyone else. I remained that way until I was twenty-eight. I would still be that man if not for you, dearest Elizabeth. You taught me a difficult but necessary lesson. You humbled me. I approached you expecting to be welcomed, but you showed me that I was not worthy of a woman like you. Had you convinced yourself that I would accept you? I had. What must you think of my vanity? I truly believed you were waiting for my proposal. My behavior must have been at fault, though I never meant to mislead you. My high spirits often led me astray. You must have hated me after that evening. Hate you? I was angry at first, but my anger soon turned toward myself. I am afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming? No, I felt only surprise. Your surprise could not be greater than mine at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no special treatment, and I expected nothing more than what I was owed. My goal then, Darcy replied, was to show you through my kindness that I was not petty enough to hold a grudge. I hoped to earn your forgiveness and improve your opinion of me by showing that I had taken your criticisms to heart."
}

