parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "edef25075f68ec81cb78502d",
  "rewritten_text": "She felt a sudden urge to go to the door to check if it was raining. Why doubt her own intentions? Captain Wentworth had to be gone by now. She stood up, determined to go; she was tired of her mind constantly second-guessing itself. She would simply see if it was raining. However, she was immediately forced back by the arrival of Captain Wentworth himself, accompanied by a group of friends he must have met nearby. He seemed more surprised and flustered by the sight of her than she had ever seen; his face turned quite red. For the first time since they had met again, she felt she was the more composed of the two. She had been prepared by the last few moments, so the initial shock had passed. Still, she felt a mix of agitation, pain, and pleasure—a confusing blend of delight and misery. He spoke to her briefly, then turned away, clearly embarrassed. It was neither cold nor friendly; it was simply awkward. After a short time, he approached her again to exchange polite, meaningless questions. Anne noticed he was far less at ease than usual. They had grown accustomed to speaking with a calm indifference, but he could not manage that now. Whether time or Louisa had changed him, he was clearly unsettled. He looked healthy and spoke of Uppercross and the Musgroves, even mentioning Louisa with a brief, familiar spark in his eye, yet he remained visibly uncomfortable. It saddened, though did not surprise, Anne to see Elizabeth refuse to acknowledge him. She knew they both recognized each other and that he expected a greeting, making it painful to watch her sister turn away with cold indifference. Finally, the servant arrived to announce that Lady Dalrymple’s carriage had pulled up, much to Elizabeth’s relief."
}

