parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "591b5272e4be1df719599385",
  "rewritten_text": "Winters near Sullivan’s Island are rarely harsh, and fires are seldom needed during the autumn. However, in mid-October of that year, a day arrived with an unusual chill. Shortly before dusk, I pushed through the evergreens to reach my friend’s cabin. I had not visited in weeks, as I lived in Charleston nine miles away, and travel was far more difficult then than it is now. Finding no response to my knock, I retrieved the hidden key, unlocked the door, and entered. A pleasant fire burned in the hearth, a welcome surprise. I removed my coat, sat by the logs, and waited for my host. He returned after dark, greeting me warmly. Jupiter, beaming, hurried to prepare marsh-hens for our meal. Legrand was in one of his characteristic states of excitement. He had discovered a new bivalve and, with Jupiter’s help, captured a beetle he believed to be a unique species, which he wanted me to examine the next day. Why not tonight? I asked, warming my hands and wishing the insect elsewhere. Legrand explained that he had foolishly lent the specimen to Lieutenant G from the fort, so I would have to wait until morning. He urged me to stay, promising to send Jupiter for it at dawn, calling it the most beautiful creature imaginable. When I asked if he meant sunrise, he clarified he was speaking of the bug, describing its brilliant gold color, its size, and its black markings. Jupiter interrupted, insisting the creature was solid gold, noting its remarkable weight."
}

