parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d003a55143d5c8b28ea94468",
  "rewritten_text": "The bewilderment I felt at this sudden shift in circumstances was perhaps the most inexplicable part of the experience. The reversal was not only logical and certain, but had been long foreseen as an inevitable consequence of reaching the point in my journey where the moon’s gravitational pull would overtake the earth’s. While I awoke from a deep sleep to a truly jarring sight, the event itself had been anticipated, if not at that precise instant. The transition must have occurred smoothly, and it is doubtful that even had I been awake, I would have noticed any physical sensation of the inversion or any disturbance to my equipment. Once I regained my composure and shook off the terror that had gripped me, I focused entirely on the moon’s physical landscape. It spread out below me like a map; though it remained quite distant, the surface features were remarkably sharp. The total lack of oceans, lakes, or any water was the most striking geological detail. Curiously, I observed vast, level alluvial plains, though most of the visible hemisphere was dominated by countless conical volcanic peaks that looked more artificial than natural. The tallest did not exceed three and three-quarter miles in height, yet a chart of the Campi Phlegraei would provide a better impression of the terrain than any description I could offer. Most were clearly erupting, demonstrating their immense power through the frequent, terrifying volleys of meteoric stones that whistled past my balloon with increasing intensity."
}

