parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7c0a7f14edcba3064cfa52b3",
  "rewritten_text": "I drew near a stout man with a jovial expression, hoping to strike up a conversation and learn more, but I had barely introduced myself before he clumsily attempted to recount that same tiresome story Sir Dinadan had told me when I first clashed with Sir Sagramor. I retreated to the back of the group, feeling despondent and weary of this life of struggle and sorrow, yet fearing the finality of death, knowing how many souls in eternity had already heard that anecdote. By early afternoon, we encountered another group of pilgrims, though this one lacked all joy or levity. It included people of every age, from gray-haired elders to nursing infants, yet every face was marked by the profound hopelessness born of long-standing misery. They were slaves. Shackles bound their hands and feet to waist belts, and the adults were connected in a line by chains running from collar to collar. They had marched three hundred miles in eighteen days on meager scraps of food, sleeping huddled like animals while still in irons. Their clothing was nothing but rags, and their skin was raw and infested where the metal had rubbed. Their feet were mangled, forcing every one of them to limp. Originally numbering a hundred, half had been sold along the way. Their overseer rode nearby, wielding a heavy, multi-tailed whip; he never spoke, using the lash to strike the shoulders of any who faltered from exhaustion, forcing them back into line."
}

