parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c2d9241a0de6025853c7683e",
  "rewritten_text": "The daughter lay motionless, having fainted. The old woman’s screams and struggles, during which her hair was torn out, turned the orangutan’s likely peaceful intentions into rage. With one powerful swipe, it nearly severed her head. The sight of blood drove the beast into a frenzy. Gnashing its teeth and with eyes flashing, it attacked the girl, digging its claws into her throat until she died. Suddenly, the creature spotted its master’s face peering from the head of the bed, rigid with horror. The beast’s fury, tempered by the memory of the whip, instantly turned to fear. Knowing it deserved punishment, it tried to hide its bloody work, pacing the room in a nervous panic, breaking furniture and dragging the bed apart. Finally, it shoved the daughter’s body up the chimney and threw the old woman’s corpse out the window. As the ape approached the window with the body, the sailor recoiled in terror, slid down the lightning rod, and fled home, abandoning the animal in his fear. The sounds heard on the stairs were the Frenchman’s cries of horror mixed with the beast’s wild jabbering. There is little left to add. The orangutan must have escaped down the rod just before the door was broken, closing the window behind it. The owner later captured it and sold it for a large sum to the Jardin des Plantes. Le Bon was released immediately after we explained the situation to the Prefect of Police. Though the Prefect was friendly toward Dupin, he could not hide his annoyance at how things turned out, making a few sarcastic remarks about people minding their own business. Let him talk, Dupin said, choosing not to respond. It will ease his conscience. I am satisfied with having outsmarted him. His failure to solve this mystery is not as surprising as he thinks; the Prefect is simply too clever for his own good, rather than truly profound."
}

