parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "7b4ba8f0006c5cf79502f949",
  "rewritten_text": "How could I, a confused village boy, avoid the same contradictions that even the wisest men face daily? I told Biddy that while her warnings might be true, I still admired Estella deeply. I buried my face in my hands and pulled my hair, feeling like a fool for my misplaced feelings. I knew I deserved to be punished for my stupidity. Biddy, being wise, stopped arguing. She gently removed my hands from my hair and patted my shoulder. I cried into my sleeve, feeling sorry for myself, though I did not know who to blame. Biddy told me she was glad I trusted her and promised to keep my secrets. She hinted that if she were still my teacher, she would give me a difficult lesson, but she knew I had moved past her guidance. With a sigh, she stood up and asked if we should walk further or head home. I hugged her and promised to always tell her everything. She replied that I would only do so until I became a gentleman. I insisted I would never be one, so my promise would last forever. Biddy looked toward the ships and whispered softly before asking again if we should walk or go home. We chose to walk further as the summer afternoon faded into a beautiful evening."
}

