parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f295a98b8b27ff4f1ba5f73d",
  "rewritten_text": "Why, you don't mean to say— my sister began. Yes I do, Mum, said Pumblechook, but wait a bit. Go on, Joseph. Good in you! Go on! What would present company say, Joe continued, to twenty pounds? Handsome would be the word, my sister replied. Well, then, said Joe, it is more than twenty pounds. That hypocrite Pumblechook nodded again and said with a patronizing laugh, It is more than that, Mum. Good again! Follow her up, Joseph! Then to make an end of it, Joe said, happily handing the bag to my sister, it is twenty-five pounds. It is twenty-five pounds, Mum, echoed that swindler Pumblechook, rising to shake her hand, and it is no more than you deserve, as I said when asked, and I wish you joy of the money! If the villain had stopped there, his behavior would have been bad enough, but he made it worse by taking me into custody with a sense of authority that surpassed his previous offenses. Now you see, Joseph and wife, Pumblechook said, grabbing my arm, I am one of those people who always finish what they start. This boy must be apprenticed immediately. That is my way. Goodness knows, Uncle Pumblechook, my sister said, clutching the money, we are deeply grateful to you. Never mind me, Mum, the corn-dealer replied. A pleasure is a pleasure. But this boy, you know, we must have him apprenticed. I said I would see to it. The Justices were sitting in the nearby Town Hall, so we went over to have me officially apprenticed to Joe. I say we went over, but Pumblechook shoved me along as if I had just committed a crime. The crowd clearly thought I had been caught red-handed, as people asked what I had done and remarked that I looked like a bad kid. One kind person even gave me a religious pamphlet about a young criminal in prison. The Hall was a strange place with high pews like a church, onlookers leaning over the sides, and powerful Justices who were busy reading, sleeping, or taking snuff. On the walls hung dark portraits that looked to my untrained eyes like lumps of candy and adhesive."
}

