parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ab8012752252f4ccf3d84e5e",
  "rewritten_text": "Get up! What are you doing? I opened my eyes, disoriented. It was past sunrise, and I had been fast asleep. Pap stood over me, looking ill and irritable. He asked what I was doing with the gun. Assuming he had no memory of his actions, I told him someone had tried to enter, so I was waiting for them. He asked why I had not woken him, and I replied that I had tried but could not move him. He told me to stop talking and go check the fishing lines for breakfast, promising to follow shortly. He unlocked the door, and I hurried up the riverbank. Seeing debris and bark floating by, I realized the river was rising. I thought of the good fortune the June rise usually brought, as it carried cordwood and logs downstream that I could catch and sell to the mills. I walked along the bank, watching for Pap and any useful drift. Suddenly, a beautiful canoe, about fourteen feet long, appeared, bobbing on the water. I dove in like a frog, fully clothed, and swam to it. I feared someone might be hiding inside to play a trick, but it was truly a drift-canoe. I paddled it to the bank, thinking Pap would be pleased with its ten-dollar value. However, as I hid it in a vine-covered creek, a new plan formed: I would conceal it well and use it to escape downriver fifty miles instead of traveling on foot. I worked quickly, constantly expecting Pap to appear. After hiding the boat, I peered through the willows and spotted him down the path, aiming his gun at a bird."
}

