parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "460501683e6d33589052c056",
  "rewritten_text": "I recall the conditions during our nineteenth-century civil war. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day in gold, while in the South, he received fifty in nearly worthless Confederate currency. Since overalls cost a day's wages in the North but two days' wages in the South, Northern wages held significantly more purchasing power. I made various acquaintances in the village and was pleased to see our new currency circulating among the common people, including milrays, mills, cents, nickels, and some silver. Gold was only found at the goldsmith's shop. I stopped there while Marco was arguing over the price of salt and requested change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They scrutinized the coin, testing it with acid and ringing it on the counter, before interrogating me about my identity, origin, and destination. When their questions stalled, I volunteered irrelevant details about my dog, my late wife's religious background, and a man I once knew with extra thumbs. This eventually satisfied the curious clerk, though he seemed annoyed. He dared not challenge a man of my wealth, choosing instead to berate his subordinates. They changed my twenty, though it clearly strained their resources, much like asking a small-town shopkeeper to break a two-thousand-dollar bill. The goldsmith likely wondered how a traveler could carry such a sum, as he followed me to the door, watching me with reverent admiration."
}

