parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "dfe6a12c9766ab2fd45b071c",
  "rewritten_text": "While these events unfolded at the workhouse, Fagin sat in his old hideout—the same place from which the girl had taken Oliver—brooding over a dim, smoky fire. He held a pair of bellows on his lap, having tried to stir the flames, but he had drifted into deep thought, resting his chin on his folded arms while staring blankly at the rusty grate. At a table behind him, the Artful Dodger, Charley Bates, and Mr. Chitling were focused on a game of whist, with the Dodger playing dummy against the other two. The Dodger’s face, always sharp, grew even more intense as he studied the game and peeked at Chitling’s hand, adjusting his own strategy based on what he saw. Because it was cold, the Dodger kept his hat on, as he often did indoors. He also kept a clay pipe in his teeth, removing it only to sip gin-and-water from a quart pot on the table. Charley Bates was also focused, but being more excitable than his friend, he drank more frequently and made constant, distracting jokes that were hardly appropriate for a serious game. The Dodger, relying on their friendship, occasionally scolded him for his behavior, but Bates just laughed it off, telling his friend to get lost or offering some other witty retort that clearly impressed Mr. Chitling. It was notable that Chitling and his partner always lost, yet this didn't bother Bates, who laughed loudly after every hand, claiming he had never had so much fun. That is two doubles and the rubber, Chitling said with a long face, pulling half-a-crown from his pocket. I have never seen anyone like you, Jack; you win everything."
}

