parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b6748fecdffe42c2a7779d6b",
  "rewritten_text": "I was not worried about her. She was a capable woman who handled life well without my help. Honestly, she likely saw me as an idle boy, good for nothing but daydreaming, and was happy to see me go. It was a dark night when I said goodbye. I took the three creditors who had been bothering me along as helpers, and we carried the balloon and its gear to the station where the rest of my supplies were stored. Everything was untouched, so I got to work immediately. It was April first. The night was dark, starless, and a drizzling rain made us miserable. My main concern was the balloon; despite its varnish, it was getting heavy from the moisture, and the gunpowder was at risk. I kept my three creditors busy packing ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. They constantly complained, asking what I was doing and grumbling about the hard labor. They could not understand why they should get soaked just to help with such strange tasks. I grew anxious and worked faster, fearing they might quit, as the fools clearly thought I was involved in some dark, supernatural business. I managed to calm them by promising to pay all my debts in full once I finished. They interpreted this to mean I would soon have plenty of cash, and as long as they were paid, they did not care what happened to me. After about four and a half hours, the balloon was inflated. I attached the basket and loaded my equipment, including the condensing device, plenty of water, and compact, nutritious food like pemmican."
}

