  "request_id": "800bd7af53e8e8e13115a3d5",
  "rewritten_text": "Elizabeth was despondent, finding no joy in her daily life. To her, happiness felt like a betrayal of the dead, and she believed only endless mourning could honor such a tragic loss of innocence. She was no longer the cheerful girl who once walked by the lake with me, dreaming of our future. The first of life's great sorrows had arrived, extinguishing her light. Reflecting on Justine Moritz’s miserable death, she told me, \"The world looks different now. I used to view accounts of injustice as distant, fictional tales, but now that misery has struck close to home, people seem like monsters hungry for blood. I know I am being unfair; everyone believed her guilty, and if she had truly murdered the child she raised for mere jewels, she would have been a monster. But she was innocent. I feel it, and your agreement confirms it. Victor, when lies can mimic the truth so perfectly, how can anyone be sure of happiness? I feel as though I am walking on a cliff’s edge, surrounded by people trying to push me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, yet the killer remains free, perhaps even respected. I would rather face the scaffold myself than trade places with such a wretch.\" I listened in agony, knowing that while I did not commit the act, I was the true cause. Elizabeth saw my pain and took my hand, saying, \"My dearest friend, you must calm yourself. These events have devastated me, but I am not as miserable as you.\""
}

