parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "4ac1920dc083c0f33d4713e4",
  "rewritten_text": "His deeply tanned face was largely obscured by a thick beard and mustache. He carried a heavy oak club but seemed to possess no other weapons. He offered an ungainly bow and greeted us in French, his accent clearly Parisian despite a slight Neufchatel inflection. Sit down, my friend, Dupin invited. I assume you are here regarding the orangutan. I must say, I almost envy you owning such a magnificent and valuable creature. What is your estimate of his age? The sailor exhaled deeply, as if shedding a heavy weight, and answered confidently: I cannot say for certain, but he is likely no more than four or five. Is he here? Oh no, we lacked the facilities to house him, Dupin replied. He is at a nearby stable on Rue Dubourg. You may collect him tomorrow. Naturally, you are ready to prove ownership? Certainly, sir. I shall regret losing him, Dupin remarked. I do not expect you to go to such trouble for nothing, the man insisted. I am happy to offer a reasonable reward for his recovery. That is quite fair, Dupin agreed. Let me consider. My reward shall be this: you must provide all the information you possess concerning the murders in the Rue Morgue. Dupin spoke these final words softly. With equal composure, he walked to the door, locked it, and pocketed the key. He then produced a pistol from his coat and placed it calmly upon the table. The sailor turned crimson, appearing as if he were choking. He leaped up and seized his club, but immediately collapsed back into his chair, shaking uncontrollably and looking deathly pale. He remained silent. I felt profound pity for him. My friend, Dupin said gently, you are distressing yourself without cause. We intend you no harm. I give you my word as a gentleman and a Frenchman that we mean you no injury. I am fully aware that you are innocent of the crimes in the Rue Morgue. Nevertheless, you cannot deny that you are involved in them to some degree."
}

