parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0193b8fc24f269aed9a8df32",
  "rewritten_text": "I must point out, Sir Walter, Mr. Shepherd remarked one morning at Kellynch Hall while setting aside his newspaper, that the current situation works in our favor. This peace will bring many wealthy naval officers back to shore, all of whom will need homes. There could not be a better time to secure reliable tenants. Many fortunes were made during the war, and if a rich admiral were to come our way, Sir Walter— He would be a very lucky man, Shepherd, Sir Walter replied. Kellynch Hall would be a prize to him, the greatest he has ever taken, would it not? Mr. Shepherd laughed as expected at the wit, then added, I would suggest that naval officers are excellent to deal with in business. They have liberal ideas and make desirable tenants. If rumors of your intentions were to spread—which is possible, as it is difficult to keep the affairs of a man of your standing private—I would not be surprised if word got out. Should that happen, I believe applications from wealthy naval commanders would be worth considering. I can be here in two hours to handle any inquiries, saving you the trouble. Sir Walter merely nodded. Soon after, he stood and paced the room, remarking sarcastically, I imagine few naval officers would not be surprised to find themselves in a house of this caliber. They would look around and count their blessings, Mrs. Clay added, having accompanied her father to Kellynch for her health. I agree with my father that a sailor would be a very desirable tenant."
}

