parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f5d9ceb96fef680f7c647f00",
  "rewritten_text": "I had tucked my handkerchief inside my helmet, along with a few other items, forgetting that I could not remove the helmet on my own. The fact that the cloth was so close yet impossible to reach made my situation unbearable. It is human nature to crave what we cannot have, and this thought consumed me. For miles, I could think of nothing but that handkerchief while salty sweat stung my eyes. It sounds trivial, but it was genuine misery. I decided that next time I would carry a small bag, regardless of what the knights of the Round Table thought. I prioritized comfort over their rigid standards of style. As we traveled, clouds of dust made me sneeze and cry, leading me to use language I probably should have avoided. We encountered no one in that desolate land, not even an ogre. If we had, I would have gladly traded his armor for his handkerchief. Meanwhile, the sun beat down, turning my iron suit into an oven. Every minor detail became a source of irritation. When I trotted, my armor rattled like a crate of dishes, and my shield banged against my body. If I slowed to a walk, my joints screeched like a rusty wheelbarrow, and the lack of airflow made me feel as though I were being cooked alive. The slower I moved, the heavier the iron felt, pressing down on me with increasing weight."
}

