parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0e236eb394305323ace291f8",
  "rewritten_text": "Despite her efforts, the boy grew increasingly pale and despondent. She tried various baths and treatments, but he remained as gloomy as ever. She then supplemented these with a strict oatmeal diet and medicinal plasters, force-feeding him various quack remedies. Tom had become indifferent to her interference, which deeply worried his aunt. She felt this apathy had to be shattered. Hearing of a new remedy called Pain-killer, she bought some and, after tasting its fiery intensity, abandoned all other treatments in its favor. She gave Tom a spoonful and watched anxiously. The effect was immediate; the boy reacted with such explosive energy that her worries vanished. Tom realized he needed a new strategy. He decided to pretend he enjoyed the medicine, asking for it so frequently that his aunt eventually told him to help himself. She noticed the bottle emptying, never suspecting he was pouring the liquid into a floorboard crack. One day, as Tom was dosing the floor, her yellow cat, Peter, begged for a taste. Tom warned him, but the cat insisted. Tom pried the cat's mouth open and poured the medicine in. Peter leaped high into the air, let out a screech, and began racing around the room, crashing into furniture and causing total chaos."
}

