parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "088d1cf74005835ba3262934",
  "rewritten_text": "Had she realized her sister intended to deprive her of such prospects and realities, her reaction would have been understood only by her mother, who likely shared those feelings. Lydia’s departure for Brighton was the only comfort for her mother, who was sadly convinced her husband would never visit the town himself. Yet, they remained unaware of these developments, and their excitement persisted until the day Lydia departed. Elizabeth prepared to see Mr. Wickham for the final time. Having spent much time with him since her return, her previous agitation had faded, along with her former partiality. She now recognized the same gentleness that once charmed her as mere affectation, becoming tiresome and wearying. Furthermore, his attempt to renew the attentions he had shown earlier in their acquaintance only annoyed her. She lost all sympathy for him, finding his idle gallantry insulting. While she firmly discouraged his advances, she felt offended that he assumed her vanity would be easily flattered by his renewed interest, regardless of his past neglect. On the regiment's final day in Meryton, Wickham dined at Longbourn. Elizabeth, feeling little desire to part on friendly terms, mentioned Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy’s three-week stay at Rosings when he asked about her time at Hunsford, then inquired if he knew the Colonel. Wickham appeared surprised, annoyed, and alarmed, but quickly recovered with a smile, noting he had known him well. After calling him a gentleman, he asked for her opinion. She praised him warmly. Wickham then asked with feigned indifference how long he had stayed at Rosings. Upon learning it was nearly three weeks and that she had seen him daily, he remarked on the difference between the cousins' manners. When Elizabeth noted that Mr. Darcy improved upon acquaintance, Wickham’s reaction was telling. He began to ask a question but stopped himself, instead asking with forced cheerfulness if it was in his manner that Darcy improved."
}

