parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c33197bbc37233b109ca0834",
  "rewritten_text": "His primary concern was selecting a target, as he understood that murder, much like ancient pagan rites, demanded both a priest and a sacrifice. Lacking the imagination to have enemies, and recognizing that his solemn task left no room for petty grudges, he drafted a list of acquaintances and kin. After deliberation, he chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a beloved second cousin residing in Curzon Street. He held her in high regard, and since his inheritance of Lord Rugby’s estate rendered him independently wealthy, her passing offered no financial gain. Convinced she was the ideal candidate and unwilling to keep Sybil waiting, he resolved to act immediately. First, he addressed the palmist by writing a cheque for 105 pounds to Mr. Septimus Podgers, dispatching his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. He then summoned his hansom and prepared to depart. Before exiting, he glanced at Sybil Merton’s portrait, vowing to guard the secret of his sacrifice and shield her from the truth. En route to the Buckingham, he purchased a basket of narcissi for Sybil. Upon reaching the club, he retreated to the library and requested a lemon-and-soda along with a volume on toxicology. He concluded that poison was the most suitable method; he found physical violence repulsive and wished to avoid the notoriety that would surely follow a more conspicuous crime, as he had no desire to become a spectacle in society columns or at Lady Windermere’s gatherings."
}

