parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "70276986566be6b76c9aa7b1",
  "rewritten_text": "They brought in a distinguished-looking older man and a younger companion with his arm in a sling. The crowd roared with laughter, but I saw nothing funny, and I doubted the duke and king would either. I expected them to turn pale, yet they remained unfazed. The duke acted blissfully unaware, while the king stared down at the newcomers with a sorrowful expression, as if the existence of such frauds pained his very soul. He performed brilliantly. Many prominent citizens gathered around the king to show their support. The older man appeared utterly bewildered. Soon he began to speak, and I immediately noticed his authentic English accent, which differed from the king’s decent imitation. He addressed the crowd, saying: This is an unexpected surprise, and I admit I am ill-prepared to respond, as my brother and I have suffered misfortunes; he has a broken arm, and our luggage was mistakenly left at a town upstream last night. I am Harvey Wilks, brother of Peter, and this is William, who is deaf and mute, and now struggles to communicate with only one hand. We are who we claim to be, and I shall provide proof once our baggage arrives. Until then, we will retire to the hotel. As he and his silent brother departed, the king scoffed, mocking the broken arm as a convenient excuse for a fraud unable to use sign language, and dismissing the lost luggage story as a clever fabrication. He laughed, joined by most of the crowd. Only a few remained serious, including the doctor, a sharp-looking lawyer named Levi Bell who had just arrived from Louisville, and a large, rugged man who listened intently to both the newcomer and the king."
}

