parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d1d8d3537396066634670965",
  "rewritten_text": "Their arrival ignited a fervor in Saint Antoine, spreading quickly as they passed, with faces appearing at every door and window. Yet, no one followed, and silence fell as they entered the wine-shop, though every gaze fixed upon them. Good day, gentlemen, Monsieur Defarge said. This prompted a chorus of replies. It is bad weather, gentlemen, Defarge added, shaking his head. Everyone looked at their neighbor, then cast their eyes down in silence, save for one man who stood and left. My wife, Defarge announced to Madame Defarge, I have traveled with this road-mender, Jacques. I met him by chance outside Paris. He is a good man. Give him a drink! A second man stood and left. Madame Defarge served the road-mender, who tipped his cap and drank. He ate coarse bread from his blouse as he sat by the counter. A third man stood and left. Defarge drank some wine, then waited for the stranger to finish. He ignored the others, and they ignored him, even Madame Defarge, who was busy knitting. Have you finished, friend? he eventually asked. Yes, thank you. Come then! You shall see the room I mentioned. It will suit you perfectly. They left the shop, crossed a courtyard, climbed a steep staircase, and entered a garret—the same room where an old man once sat making shoes. The old man was gone, but the three men who had left the shop were waiting. They were linked to the old man by the memory of having once watched him through the wall. Defarge closed the door and whispered, Jacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness I, Jacques Four, have brought to meet you."
}

