parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6e24d5a14c2e253722571bb9",
  "rewritten_text": "What is the trouble? I said, Pap, and mam, and sis, and— Then I broke down. He said, Oh, don't take on so; we all have our troubles, and this will turn out fine. What is wrong with them? They are—are you the watchman of the boat? Yes, he said, sounding quite pleased. I am the captain, owner, mate, pilot, watchman, and head deck-hand; sometimes I am the freight and passengers too. I am not as rich as old Jim Hornback, and I cannot be as generous as he is with his money, but I have told him many times that I would not trade places with him. A sailor's life is for me, and I would not live two miles out of town where nothing ever happens, not for all his money. I interrupted and said, They are in a terrible mess, and— Who is? Why, pap and mam and sis and Miss Hooker; if you would take your ferry-boat and go up there— Up where? Where are they? On the wreck. What wreck? Why, there is only one. You do not mean the Walter Scott? Yes. Good heavens! What are they doing there? Well, they did not go there on purpose. I bet they did not! There is no chance for them if they do not get off quickly! How on earth did they get into such a scrape? It was easy. Miss Hooker was visiting up at Booth's Landing. In the early evening, she started across with her servant in the horse-ferry to stay the night at a friend's house. They lost their steering-oar, drifted down about two miles, and got stuck on the wreck. The ferryman, the servant, and the horses were all lost, but Miss Hooker managed to grab onto the wreck. About an hour after dark, we came along in our trading boat. It was so dark we did not see the wreck until we were right on top of it, and we got stuck too. Everyone was saved except Bill Whipple—and he was such a good person! I almost wish it had been me. My goodness! That is the most incredible thing I have ever heard."
}

