parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6298c0ac7465b28a3824aa95",
  "rewritten_text": "Watching him in these moods, I often thought about the old idea of a dual soul, imagining Dupin as two people: the creative and the analytical. Do not think I am describing a mystery or writing fiction. What I saw in him was simply the result of an intense or perhaps troubled mind. An example of his comments will best explain this. One night, we were walking down a long, dirty street near the Palais Royal. We had both been silent for at least fifteen minutes, lost in thought. Suddenly, Dupin spoke: \"He is a very small man, it is true, and would be better suited for the Variétés Theater.\" I replied without thinking, not immediately realizing how perfectly he had matched my own thoughts. A moment later, I was stunned. \"Dupin,\" I said seriously, \"this is beyond me. I am amazed and can hardly believe it. How could you know I was thinking of—?\" I paused to be sure he knew who I meant. \"—of Chantilly,\" he said. \"Why do you pause? You were thinking that his small size made him unfit for tragedy.\" That was exactly what I had been thinking. Chantilly was a former shoemaker who had become obsessed with acting, tried to play Xerxes in a tragedy, and was widely mocked for it. \"For heaven's sake, tell me the method you used to read my mind,\" I exclaimed, more startled than I cared to admit. \"It was the fruit seller,\" my friend replied, \"who led you to conclude that the shoemaker was too short for Xerxes.\" \"The fruit seller? You surprise me. I do not know any fruit seller.\" \"The man who bumped into you when we entered the street fifteen minutes ago.\" I remembered then that a fruit seller carrying a large basket of apples had nearly knocked me over as we turned onto the street, but I could not understand what that had to do with Chantilly."
}

