  "request_id": "e7114c3a64e3fd3e79b23e07",
  "rewritten_text": "So you won't, won't you? Oh, you precious fool! It is naughty, isn't it, Oliver? Charley Bates asked. He will end up being hanged, won't he? I do not know what that means, Oliver replied. Something like this, old fellow, Charley said. As he spoke, he grabbed the end of his neckerchief, held it up, dropped his head to his shoulder, and made a strange sound through his teeth, clearly acting out a hanging. That is what it means, Charley said. Look how he stares, Jack! I have never seen such funny company as this boy; he will be the death of me. Charley laughed heartily and went back to his pipe with tears in his eyes. You have been raised poorly, the Artful Dodger said, looking at his boots with satisfaction after Oliver finished polishing them. Fagin will make something of you, or you will be the first one he ever had who was not profitable. You had better start right away, because you will join the trade sooner than you think, and you are just wasting time, Oliver. Charley Bates added his own moral advice, and then he and the Dodger described the many pleasures of their life, hinting that Oliver should win Fagin’s favor immediately. And always remember this, Nolly, the Dodger said as they heard the old man unlocking the door above, if you do not take handkerchiefs and watches— What is the point of talking like that? Charley interrupted. He does not know what you mean. If you do not take pocket handkerchiefs and watches, the Dodger said, simplifying his language for Oliver, someone else will. The people who lose them will be worse off, you will be worse off, and nobody will be any better except the person who takes them, and you have just as much right to them as they do. To be sure, to be sure! said the old man, who had entered without Oliver noticing. It is simple, my dear; take the Dodger’s word for it. He understands the lessons of his trade. The old man rubbed his hands together gleefully, agreeing with the Dodger’s logic and chuckling at his student’s skill. The conversation ended there, as the old man returned with Miss Betsy and a man Oliver had never seen before, whom the Dodger called Tom Chitling."
}

