parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "deda9706bf783ed162f6bedf",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Wickham was so satisfied with their talk that he never brought up the subject again, much to Elizabeth’s relief. Soon, the day arrived for him and Lydia to leave. Mrs. Bennet had to accept the separation, as her husband refused to let the whole family move to Newcastle, meaning they would likely be apart for a year. Oh, my dear Lydia, when will we see each other again? Mrs. Bennet cried. Oh, I don’t know. Maybe not for two or three years, Lydia replied. Write to me often, her mother urged. I will when I can, but married women are busy. My sisters can write to me; they have nothing else to do. Mr. Wickham’s farewells were far warmer than his wife’s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said many charming things. He is a fine fellow, Mr. Bennet remarked once they had left. He charms us all. I am very proud of him. I doubt Sir William Lucas has a son-in-law as impressive. Mrs. Bennet was miserable for several days after Lydia left. I think there is nothing worse than parting with friends, she lamented. It makes one feel so lonely. This is the result of having a married daughter, Elizabeth noted. Surely this makes you glad your other four are still single? Not at all, her mother insisted. Lydia is only leaving because her husband’s regiment is stationed so far away. If they were closer, she would not have left so soon. Mrs. Bennet’s sadness soon vanished, replaced by excitement when news began to spread. The housekeeper at Netherfield had been told to prepare for her master’s arrival; he was coming to hunt for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was restless, glancing at Jane with knowing smiles. Well, Mr. Bingley is returning, she told her sister, Mrs. Philips. That is fine, though I do not care about it. He means nothing to us, and I certainly do not need to see him again. Still, he is welcome to visit Netherfield if he wishes."
}

