parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e5a83861ff3f13fa82140815",
  "rewritten_text": "It was early February, and Anne had been in Bath for a month. She was anxious for news from Uppercross and Lyme, having heard nothing from Mary for three weeks. She only knew that Henrietta had returned home and that Louisa was recovering in Lyme. One evening, she received a thick letter from Mary, sent with the compliments of Admiral and Mrs. Croft. The Crofts were in Bath, which delighted Anne, as she was very fond of them. Sir Walter immediately asked about the letter, noting that he intended to visit his tenant, Admiral Croft. Anne ignored him, focused entirely on her letter, which had been started days earlier. Mary wrote that she had not written sooner because she assumed Anne was too busy enjoying Bath to care about dull Uppercross. She complained about a boring Christmas, noting that the Musgroves had hosted no parties. She also grumbled that the holidays had lasted too long and that the Harville children were still staying at the house, which she found strange. Mary disliked the children and felt Mrs. Musgrove favored them over her own grandchildren. She also complained about the terrible weather and mentioned that Charles Hayter had been visiting far too often. She added that she wished Henrietta had stayed in Lyme longer to avoid him."
}

