parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e50ed746376b4de659049b7f",
  "rewritten_text": "Men marry out of exhaustion and women out of curiosity; both end up disappointed. I doubt I will marry, Harry. I am too much in love, which is one of your own sayings that I am now putting into practice. Who is she? Lord Henry asked. Dorian Gray blushed and said, An actress. Lord Henry shrugged. That is a common start. You would not say that if you saw her, Harry. Who is she? Her name is Sibyl Vane. I have never heard of her. No one has yet, but they will. She is a genius. My dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women are merely decorative. They have nothing to say, but they say it charmingly. They represent the triumph of matter over mind, just as men represent the triumph of mind over morals. Harry, how can you say that? It is true. I am studying women right now, so I should know. There are only two kinds: the plain and the colorful. The plain ones are useful for appearing respectable at dinner. The others are charming but make the mistake of using makeup to look young, whereas our grandmothers used it to appear witty. Now, women are satisfied just looking younger than their daughters. There are only five women in London worth talking to, and two of them are social outcasts. But tell me about your genius. How long have you known her? Your views terrify me, Harry. Never mind that. How long? About three weeks. Where did you meet her? I will tell you, but do not be unkind. It never would have happened if I had not met you. You gave me a wild desire to understand life. For days, I felt a pulse in my veins. Walking through the park or down Piccadilly, I looked at everyone and wondered about their lives with a frantic curiosity. Some fascinated me, while others terrified me. The air felt poisoned, and I developed a passion for new sensations."
}

