parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3c3b1bc635d49cc5e93d6ebb",
  "rewritten_text": "I turn now to my own history. My early life holds little of note, yet I must reflect on my childhood to show how the loss of one hope rendered my future hollow, and how the destruction of my only cherished affection extinguished my spirit. My aunt was nothing like my father. While not malicious, she possessed the coldest heart imaginable, entirely incapable of love. She took me in out of a sense of duty, yet having lived undisturbed for years, she refused to let a child disrupt her peace. Unmarried, she had spent five years alone on her inherited estate by Loch Lomond in Scotland. Though my father requested she live with me at our family home near Richmond, she refused. Once she settled my father’s affairs, she left England, taking me to her Scottish estate. Until I was eight, my mother’s former servant cared for me. I lived in a secluded wing, seeing my aunt only twice daily: once at noon in my nursery, and once after her dinner in the parlor. She never showed affection, seemingly fearing I might disturb her. My nurse coached me carefully, and my aunt’s cold demeanor instilled such awe that I remained perfectly still during our brief, strained visits. Under my nurse’s guidance, I roamed the park and fields freely. Born of deep love, I was a sensitive child, passionately attached to everything around me, even inanimate objects."
}

