parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6d4842257a4d83907864a25c",
  "rewritten_text": "Had she realized her sister intended to ruin such prospects, her reaction would have been intense, understood only by her mother. Lydia’s trip to Brighton was the only comfort for her mother, who knew her husband would never visit. They remained unaware of the truth, and their excitement lasted until the day Lydia departed. Elizabeth was seeing Mr. Wickham for the final time. Having spent much time with him recently, her former feelings had faded. She now found his once-charming gentleness affected and tiresome. His attempt to renew his past attentions only annoyed her. She felt insulted that he assumed his renewed interest would flatter her vanity regardless of his previous neglect. On the regiment’s final day in Meryton, Wickham dined at Longbourn. Elizabeth, eager to distance herself, mentioned that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had spent three weeks at Rosings, asking if he knew the Colonel. Wickham looked startled and uneasy, but quickly regained his composure, calling the Colonel a gentleman and asking for her opinion. Elizabeth praised him warmly. Wickham then asked, with feigned indifference, how long he had stayed. When she confirmed it was nearly three weeks and that they met daily, he remarked on the difference between the cousins. Elizabeth noted that Mr. Darcy improved upon acquaintance. Wickham looked visibly disturbed, then asked with a forced, lighthearted tone if it was his manner that improved."
}

