parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0e5b092fc9f64c159697b33c",
  "rewritten_text": "Mrs. Otis, whose expertise in Art is significant given her winters in Boston during her youth, informs me that these jewels possess considerable value and would command a high price if sold. Consequently, Lord Canterville, I am certain you understand why I cannot permit my family to keep them. Such frivolous ornaments, while perhaps fitting for the British aristocracy, are entirely inappropriate for those raised on the rigorous, enduring tenets of republican simplicity. I should add that Virginia wishes to keep the box as a keepsake of your ill-fated ancestor. Since it is quite aged and somewhat damaged, you might see fit to grant her request. Personally, I am surprised my daughter shows sympathy for medievalism, though I attribute it to her birth in a London suburb shortly after Mrs. Otis returned from Athens. Lord Canterville listened to the Minister with solemnity, tugging his grey moustache to conceal a smile. Once Mr. Otis finished, he shook his hand warmly and replied, My dear sir, your delightful daughter performed a vital service for my unfortunate ancestor, Sir Simon, and my family owes her a great debt for her remarkable bravery. The jewels are hers; indeed, if I were cruel enough to reclaim them, the old spirit would likely rise from his grave within a fortnight to torment me. They are not heirlooms, as they were never mentioned in any legal document and remained unknown until now. I have no more right to them than your butler, and I imagine Miss Virginia will enjoy wearing them when she is older. Furthermore, you purchased the furniture and the ghost at a valuation, meaning anything belonging to the spirit became yours; legally, Sir Simon was deceased, and you acquired his assets through that transaction."
}

