  "rewritten_text": "He does not comprehend English, yet evaluates based on tone. Alberto Montani, a confectioner, states he was among those who first climbed the stairs. He heard the voices in question, noting the gruff one belonged to a Frenchman who seemed to be arguing. He could not decipher the shrill voice, which sounded rapid and irregular; he suspects it was Russian, though he has never spoken with a Russian native. This aligns with the general testimony. Several witnesses confirmed that the fourth-floor chimneys were too narrow for a person to enter. The term sweeps refers to the cylindrical brushes used for cleaning flues, which were passed through every chimney in the building. No rear exit existed for anyone to escape while the group ascended. Mademoiselle L’Espanaye’s body was wedged so tightly in the chimney that it required the combined effort of four or five men to extract it. Paul Dumas, a physician, testified that he examined the bodies at dawn. They lay on the bed frame where the young woman was discovered. Her corpse was bruised and scraped, likely from being forced up the chimney. Her throat was chafed, showing deep scratches beneath the chin and livid finger marks. Her face was discolored, her eyes bulged, and her tongue was bitten. A bruise on her stomach suggested pressure from a knee. Dr. Dumas concluded she had been strangled by unknown parties. The mother’s corpse was severely mutilated; the bones of her right arm and leg were shattered, her left tibia and ribs were splintered, and her entire body was bruised. The cause of these injuries is uncertain, though a heavy club, iron bar, or chair wielded by a powerful man could have caused them; no woman could have inflicted such damage. The victim’s head was severed and crushed, and her throat had clearly been cut with a sharp blade, likely a razor."
