  "rewritten_text": "During his brief visit, Emma had seen him rarely, yet enough to sense that their initial encounter had passed, leaving her with the impression that his manner had grown increasingly pretentious and irritable. She truly marveled that she had ever found him agreeable; his presence now evoked such unpleasantness that, unless viewed as a necessary moral penance, she would have preferred never to encounter him again. She wished him well, but his proximity caused her distress, and she would have been far happier knowing he was settled twenty miles away. His marriage would surely mitigate the discomfort of his remaining in Highbury, as a Mrs. Elton would provide a convenient pretext for altering their social interactions and allowing their former intimacy to fade unnoticed. Emma gave little thought to the woman herself. She was likely suitable for Mr. Elton and sufficient for Highbury, though she would probably appear plain compared to Harriet. Regarding social standing, Emma felt entirely secure, convinced that despite his arrogance and rejection of Harriet, he had made no significant match. While her character remained unknown, her background was discoverable, and aside from her ten thousand pounds, she seemed no better than Harriet. She possessed no notable lineage or connections. Miss Hawkins was the daughter of a Bristol merchant, though given his modest earnings, his trade was likely equally unremarkable. She spent winters in Bath, but her home was in Bristol, where she had lived with an uncle in the legal profession—presumably a dull clerk. The family's only claim to prestige rested on an elder sister who had married a man near Bristol wealthy enough to maintain two carriages, which served as the pinnacle of Miss Hawkins's social history."
}

