parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "f4a6ecefac16805e9b0a936e",
  "rewritten_text": "May I ask you two questions? Monks looked surprised. You may ask, but whether I answer is another matter. That makes three, Mr. Bumble joked. Is that what you expected from me? the matron asked. It is, Monks replied. What is your other question? What will you do with it? Can it be used against me? Never, Monks said. It will not be used against me either. Stay back, or your life is in danger. He pushed the table aside, pulled an iron ring, and opened a trap-door at Mr. Bumble’s feet, causing him to jump back. Look down, Monks said, lowering his lantern. Do not fear me. I could have dropped you in earlier if I wanted. The matron approached the edge, and Mr. Bumble followed out of curiosity. Below, the muddy water rushed rapidly, its sound drowning out everything else as it swirled against the rotting wooden supports of an old water-mill. If you threw a body down there, where would it be by morning? Monks asked, swinging the lantern. Twelve miles downstream and torn to pieces, Bumble replied, pulling away. Monks took the small packet from his coat, tied it to a heavy lead weight, and dropped it into the water. It sank instantly without a sound. The three looked at each other, breathing more easily. There, Monks said, closing the trap-door. If the sea ever gives up its dead, it will keep its gold and silver, along with that trash. Our meeting is over. By all means, Mr. Bumble agreed quickly. You will keep quiet about this, won't you? Monks asked with a threatening look."
}

