parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "c260c5ff4fdf2160ac668708",
  "rewritten_text": "When Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, purchased Canterville Chase, everyone warned him he was making a mistake, as the property was undoubtedly haunted. Lord Canterville, a man of strict integrity, felt obligated to mention this when they discussed the sale. We have not lived there ourselves, Lord Canterville explained, since my grandaunt, the Duchess of Bolton, suffered a permanent shock when two skeleton hands appeared on her shoulders while she dressed for dinner. I must inform you that the ghost has been seen by my relatives and the local rector. After the Duchess's incident, our younger servants refused to stay, and my wife often lost sleep due to strange noises in the halls and library. Mr. Otis replied that he would take the furniture and the ghost as they were. I come from a modern country where money buys everything, he said. If ghosts existed in Europe, we would have brought one home for a museum or a traveling show by now. Lord Canterville smiled and noted that the ghost had been seen since 1584 and always appeared before a family death. The family doctor does the same, Mr. Otis countered. Ghosts do not exist, and I doubt the laws of nature change for the British aristocracy. You are certainly very direct in America, Lord Canterville said, not fully grasping the remark. If you do not mind a ghost, that is fine, but remember I warned you. Weeks later, the sale was finalized, and the Otis family moved to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, formerly a famous New York beauty, was now a handsome, middle-aged woman with striking features."
}

