  "request_id": "f8f005d6b99634c9f79170b2",
  "rewritten_text": "Are you referring to Mr. Knightley? Certainly. I never imagined anyone else, and I assumed you understood. Our previous conversation seemed perfectly clear. Not entirely, Emma replied with strained composure, as your comments appeared to concern someone else. I could almost swear you mentioned Mr. Frank Churchill, particularly regarding his assistance when the gypsies accosted you. Oh, Miss Woodhouse, how your memory fails you! My dear Harriet, I recall our discussion vividly. I remarked that your affection was understandable given his help, and you agreed, speaking fervently of your gratitude and your feelings when he arrived to save you. I remember it distinctly. Oh dear, Harriet exclaimed, I recall your meaning now, but I was focused on something entirely different. It was not the gypsies or Mr. Frank Churchill. I was thinking of a far more significant moment: when Mr. Knightley asked me to dance after Mr. Elton refused, leaving me without a partner. That act of kindness and noble generosity made me realize his superiority over everyone else. Good heavens, Emma cried, this has been a truly disastrous misunderstanding! What can be done? You would not have encouraged me had you known? At least I am no worse off than I would have been otherwise, and now it is possible. She hesitated. Emma remained silent. I do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse, Harriet continued, that you perceive a vast difference between the two men regarding me. You must consider one vastly superior to the other. Yet, I hope, even if it seems strange, that you might recall your own words about how even greater disparities have resulted in marriage. If I were so incredibly fortunate that Mr. Knightley did not mind the difference in our stations, I hope you will not oppose it or create obstacles."
}

