  "request_id": "7223f82ec9ad1dfc5c790a02",
  "rewritten_text": "I do not have the nerves for this kind of thing. But could you really be comfortable spending the entire evening away from the poor boy? Yes, his father can do it, so why shouldn't I? Jemima is very careful and could send us updates every hour. I really think Charles should have told his father we would all come. I am no more worried about little Charles now than he is. I was terrified yesterday, but today is different. Well, if you do not think it is too late to let them know, why not go with your husband? Leave little Charles in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot object while I am here with him. Are you serious? Mary asked, her eyes lighting up. That is a wonderful idea. I might as well go, since I am of no use at home and it only stresses me out. You, lacking a mother's instincts, are the most suitable person to stay. You can make little Charles do anything; he always listens to you. It will be much better than leaving him with only Jemima. I will certainly go; I should, just as much as Charles, since they really want me to meet Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being alone. What an excellent thought, Anne. I will tell Charles and get ready immediately. You can send for us if anything happens, but I am sure there will be no cause for alarm. I would not go if I were not completely at ease about my dear child. A moment later, she was knocking on her husband's door. As Anne followed her upstairs, she overheard the entire conversation, which began with Mary saying triumphantly, I am going with you, Charles, because I am no more useful at home than you are. If I stayed locked up with the child forever, I could never convince him to do anything he disliked. Anne is staying; she offered to stay and look after him. It was her own idea, so I will go with you, which is much better, as I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday. That is very kind of Anne, her husband replied, and I would be glad to have you come, but it seems unfair that she should be left alone to nurse our sick child."
}

