parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9f6dc1881b034d8070cd1109",
  "rewritten_text": "These thoughts calmed me, and I fell into a deep sleep that afternoon, though my feverish blood denied me peaceful dreams. The horrors of the previous day played on a loop in my mind: the women fleeing and the enraged Felix dragging me from his father’s feet. I woke exhausted, and seeing it was night, I crept from my hiding place to find food. Once fed, I headed toward the familiar path to the cottage. Everything was quiet. I retreated to my hovel to wait for the family to rise. The hour passed and the sun climbed high, but they did not appear. I trembled, fearing some catastrophe. The cottage remained dark and silent; the agony of that suspense was indescribable. Soon, two countrymen stopped by the cottage and spoke with agitated gestures, but I could not understand their local dialect. Shortly after, Felix arrived with another man. I was surprised, as I knew he had not left earlier, and I listened intently to understand the situation. His companion asked, 'Do you realize you will owe three months' rent and lose your garden's harvest? I don't want to be unfair, so please take a few days to reconsider.' Felix replied, 'It is useless. We can never live here again. My father’s life is in grave danger because of what happened. My wife and sister will never recover from the shock. Please, do not argue. Take the property; I must leave.' Felix shook as he spoke. They entered the cottage for a few minutes, then left. I never saw the De Laceys again. I spent the rest of the day in my hovel, paralyzed by despair. My protectors were gone, severing the only link I had to the world. For the first time, hatred and a desire for revenge filled me. I made no effort to resist these feelings, letting them carry me toward thoughts of malice and destruction."
}

