parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "58c9f95664a231f763525443",
  "rewritten_text": "Upon my return, I found this letter from my father: My dear Victor, you have likely been waiting for me to set a date for your homecoming. I considered writing only a brief note, but that would be a cruel kindness. How could I let you arrive expecting a joyful welcome only to find us in tears? I cannot spare you this pain, though I know you are already scanning these lines for the terrible truth. William is dead. That sweet, gentle, happy child has been murdered. I will not try to comfort you, but will simply explain what happened. Last Thursday, May 7th, I went for a walk in Plainpalais with my niece and your brothers. The evening was warm, and we stayed out longer than planned. As it grew dark, we realized William and Ernest were missing. We waited for them, but soon Ernest returned alone, explaining that William had run off to hide and never came back. We searched until nightfall, then assumed he had gone home. When he was not there, we searched with torches, terrified for his safety. At five the next morning, I found my boy, who had been so full of life, lying motionless on the grass. The marks of a killer’s fingers were on his neck. We brought him home, and my grief revealed the truth to Elizabeth. She insisted on seeing the body, and when she looked at his neck, she clasped her hands and cried out, O God!"
}

