parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d1fd5189f216d911eac2233f",
  "rewritten_text": "They were her life, more precious than any sacred place or earthly treasure. The boys were thrilled by the news of our departure, and when Clara asked if we were moving to Athens, my affirmative reply made her face glow with joy. She longed to see her parents' tomb and the land defined by her father’s legacy, memories that had long ago replaced her childhood playfulness with a restless, solemn ambition. We had many humble friends to consider: the spirited horse Lord Raymond had gifted his daughter, Alfred’s dog, and an aging pet eagle. Yet, listing these favorites only deepened our sorrow for what we had already lost and what we were forced to abandon. Idris wept, while Alfred and Evelyn begged to bring along rose bushes and marble vases, lamenting that we could not pack the castle, the forest, and the wildlife. I told them they were being foolish, as we were leaving behind mere trifles to protect far greater treasures. I urged them to focus on our hope, which would surely stem the tide of our regret. The children were soon distracted, but Idris had vanished. I found her in the park, clinging to an old oak tree, sobbing uncontrollably. My heart broke to see her in such despair. As I held her and kissed her eyelids, she regained her composure. She thanked me for my kindness, admitting that while her grief was agonizing, she remained happy because I and our children were still by her side, unlike so many others who had lost everything."
}

