  "rewritten_text": "I haven't mentioned the most important point. What is that, Harry? the boy asked listlessly. The obvious consolation. Stealing someone else's partner when you lose your own. In high society, that always redeems a woman. But honestly, Dorian, Sibyl Vane must have been so different from the women we usually meet. There is something quite beautiful about her death. I am glad to live in an era where such wonders occur. They make the concepts we toy with—romance, passion, and love—feel real. I was terribly cruel to her, you forget that. I suspect women appreciate cruelty, genuine cruelty, above all else. They possess remarkably primitive instincts. We may have emancipated them, but they remain slaves seeking masters. They love being dominated. I am sure you were magnificent. I have never seen you truly furious, but I can imagine how striking you looked. Besides, you said something to me the day before yesterday that I thought was merely fanciful, but now realize is the absolute truth. What was that? You said Sibyl Vane represented every romantic heroine to you—that she was Desdemona one night and Ophelia the next; that if she died as Juliet, she was reborn as Imogen. She will never come to life again, the boy muttered, burying his face in his hands. No, she is finished. She has played her final role. But you must view that lonely death in her cheap dressing-room as a dark, dramatic fragment from a classic tragedy. She never truly lived, so she never truly died. To you, she was always a dream, a phantom drifting through Shakespeare’s plays, making them more beautiful. The moment she touched reality, she ruined it, and it ruined her, so she vanished. Mourn Ophelia if you wish. Grieve for Cordelia. Cry out against heaven for Desdemona. But do not waste tears on Sibyl Vane. She was less real than they were. Silence filled the room as evening deepened. Shadows crept in from the garden like silent, silver-footed ghosts. The colors faded from the room. After a while, Dorian Gray looked up."
}

