parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "cc32bb6676234b926173be96",
  "rewritten_text": "As the weeks passed, my fixation on you deepened. Initially, I depicted you as Paris in elegant armor or as Adonis carrying a boar-spear. You appeared crowned with lotus flowers on Adrian’s barge upon the Nile, or peering into a Greek woodland pool at your own reflection. Those works were everything art should be: ideal, remote, and unconscious. Eventually, I decided to paint you as you truly are, in modern attire. I cannot say if it was the realism or the raw intensity of your presence, but as I painted, every stroke seemed to expose my hidden devotion. I feared my idolatry was visible, so I resolved never to exhibit the portrait. You were annoyed, though you failed to grasp the significance. Harry mocked my concerns, yet I remained convinced I was right. Once the painting left my studio, however, I felt foolish for believing it held anything beyond your beauty and my technical skill. I suspect now that the passion of creation is never truly captured in the final work; art is more abstract than we assume. It often hides the artist rather than revealing him. Consequently, when the Paris offer arrived, I intended to feature your portrait, never imagining you would object. I realize now you were correct. The painting must remain hidden. Please do not be angry with me, Dorian, for my confession."
}

