parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "5a8135acbb6986ee1550dd8e",
  "rewritten_text": "If you want one for home use, I can provide an excellent item and guarantee your satisfaction. May I ask who it is for? If it is for the police or anyone at Scotland Yard, I cannot help. English detectives are our best friends, and their stupidity allows us to do as we please. I would hate to lose them. Lord Arthur assured him it had nothing to do with the police, explaining the clock was for the Dean of Chichester. Herr Winckelkopf remarked that he was surprised by such religious devotion in a young man. Lord Arthur blushed and admitted he knew nothing of theology. After confirming it was a private matter, Winckelkopf left and returned with a small piece of dynamite and a French clock topped with a gold figure of Liberty. Lord Arthur was pleased and asked how it worked. Winckelkopf explained that he would set the timer for the exact moment of the explosion. Lord Arthur requested it be set for Friday at noon, when the Dean would be home. Winckelkopf noted this in his ledger. When Lord Arthur asked for the bill, Winckelkopf replied that the dynamite cost seven shillings and sixpence, the clock three pounds ten, and shipping five shillings. He added that he was happy to help a friend of Count Rouvaloff. Lord Arthur asked about the cost of his labor."
}

