parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "263e1e2a68aa32d96e6d4c58",
  "rewritten_text": "In a certain town—which I will leave unnamed for good reason—there stands a building common to most communities: the workhouse. Within these walls, on a date that holds no significance for the reader at this moment, was born the individual whose name titles this story. For a long time after the parish surgeon brought him into this world of sorrow, it was uncertain if the child would survive long enough to be named. Had he perished, these memoirs would have been brief indeed, though perhaps remarkably concise and accurate. While being born in a workhouse is hardly an enviable start, it was, in this specific case, the best possible outcome for Oliver Twist. He struggled to take his first breath—a tiresome but necessary habit for survival—and lay gasping on a mattress, balanced precariously between life and death. Had he been surrounded by doting relatives and expert doctors, he would surely have been killed by their interference. Instead, with only a tipsy pauper and a contract-bound surgeon present, Oliver and nature fought the battle alone. After a few struggles, he breathed, sneezed, and announced his arrival to the workhouse by crying as loudly as any infant could who had possessed a voice for less than four minutes."
}

