parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "6ea0937c2e1ac8f1942c2fe3",
  "rewritten_text": "He never tried again. The boys lay there counting the slow seconds until it felt as though time itself had stopped, and they were relieved when the sun finally began to set. Suddenly, a snore stopped. Injun Joe sat up, looked around, and gave a grim smile at his partner, whose head was resting on his knees. He nudged him with his foot and said, \"You're a great watchman, aren't you? Still, nothing happened.\" \"My, was I asleep?\" \"A little bit. It's almost time to go. What should we do with the rest of our loot?\" \"I don't know. Leave it here like we always do, I suppose. No point in carrying six hundred and fifty dollars in silver until we head south.\" \"Fine, but we might need to come back once more.\" \"True, but we should come at night like before; it's safer.\" \"Maybe, but it could be a while before I get my chance at that job. Things happen, and this isn't the best spot. Let's bury it deep.\" The partner agreed, walked over, and lifted a hearthstone to reveal a jingling bag. He took out some cash for both of them and handed the bag to Joe, who was already digging in the corner with his knife. The boys watching forgot their fear and misery instantly. They watched with wide eyes, amazed by their luck. Six hundred dollars was a fortune! This was treasure hunting at its best, with no guesswork involved. They nudged each other, silently celebrating their decision to stay. Joe’s knife hit something hard. \"Hello!\" he said. \"What is it?\" his partner asked. \"A rotten board, or maybe a box. Help me out, I’ve broken a hole in it.\" He reached inside and pulled out a handful of gold coins. The boys above were just as thrilled as the men. Joe’s partner said, \"Let's finish this quickly. I saw an old rusty pick in the weeds by the fireplace a moment ago.\""
}

