parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "90aeeabe6512dc1b7816554e",
  "rewritten_text": "Meanwhile, gravity would constantly weaken as the square of the distance increased. Consequently, I would accelerate rapidly toward the regions where the moon’s pull replaces the earth’s. Given this, I saw no reason to pack more than forty days of supplies. Another concern troubled me, however. Balloonists reaching great heights often suffer from painful breathing, bodily discomfort, and nosebleeds, which worsen with altitude. I wondered if these symptoms might prove fatal. I eventually decided they would not. These issues stem from the loss of atmospheric pressure causing blood vessels to swell, rather than a fundamental breakdown of the body’s systems. Since breathing is merely a muscular action, I believed life could persist even in a vacuum. I assumed my body would eventually adjust to the lack of pressure, and I trusted my own physical toughness to endure the initial pain. Your Excellencies, I have now explained the reasoning behind my lunar voyage. I will now describe the outcome of this bold and unprecedented attempt. After reaching an altitude of three and three-quarters miles, I tossed out some feathers and found I was still rising quickly, so I did not need to drop any ballast. I was pleased, as I wanted to keep as much weight as possible for reasons I will explain later."
}

