parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a21ae5c83a49d0f8377df14b",
  "rewritten_text": "His sunburnt face was mostly obscured by a thick beard and mustache. He carried a heavy oak club but seemed to have no other weapons. He bowed awkwardly and greeted us in French, which, despite a regional accent, clearly marked him as a Parisian. Sit down, my friend, Dupin said. I assume you are here about the orangutan. I almost envy you for owning such a fine and valuable animal. How old do you think he is? The sailor sighed with relief and replied confidently, I cannot say for sure, but he is likely no more than four or five years old. Do you have him here? Oh no, we had no place to keep him. He is at a stable on Rue Dubourg nearby. You can collect him in the morning. I assume you are prepared to prove ownership? Certainly, sir. I will be sorry to lose him, Dupin remarked. I do not expect you to go to this trouble for nothing, the man said. I am happy to pay a reasonable reward for his recovery. Well, my friend replied, that is fair. Let me think. I will tell you what I want. My reward shall be all the information you can provide regarding the murders on Rue Morgue. Dupin spoke these last words quietly. He then walked to the door, locked it, and put the key in his pocket. He calmly pulled a pistol from his coat and placed it on the table. The sailor turned red and struggled to breathe. He jumped up and grabbed his club, but immediately collapsed back into his chair, shaking and pale as death. He remained silent. I felt truly sorry for him. My friend, Dupin said kindly, you are worrying for no reason. We mean you no harm. I give you my word as a gentleman and a Frenchman that we intend no injury. I know you are innocent of the crimes on Rue Morgue, but you cannot deny that you are involved in some way."
}

