parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "bb944934123c78971c916d28",
  "rewritten_text": "Directly before him, illuminated by the pale moonlight, stood an old man of terrifying appearance. His eyes glowed like burning coals, his long grey hair hung in matted tangles, and his antique, tattered clothing was complemented by heavy, rusted shackles on his wrists and ankles. Mr. Otis remarked that he must insist the man oil his chains, offering a bottle of Tammany Rising Sun Lubricator. He noted its effectiveness, citing testimonials from prominent local clergy, and left the bottle on a marble table by the candles before retiring to bed. The Canterville ghost stood motionless in shock, then smashed the bottle against the floor and fled, groaning and glowing with a sickly green light. As he reached the oak staircase, a door swung open, and two figures in white robes threw a pillow at his head. Realizing he had no time to waste, he vanished through the wall, leaving the house silent. Reaching a secret room, he leaned against a moonbeam to catch his breath, struggling to process the situation. In three hundred years of a successful career, he had never been so insulted. He recalled the Dowager Duchess he had terrified, the housemaids he had sent into hysterics, the rector whose candle he had extinguished, and Madame de Tremouillac, who had fallen ill for weeks after finding a skeleton reading her diary, eventually turning to the Church and abandoning her friendship with Voltaire."
}

