parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3a21a8e4fc3bac321da0075b",
  "rewritten_text": "The daughter lay motionless, having fainted. The older woman’s screams and struggles, during which her hair was torn out, turned the orangutan’s likely peaceful intentions into rage. With one powerful swipe, it nearly decapitated her. The sight of blood drove the beast into a frenzy. Gnashing its teeth and with eyes flashing, it attacked the girl, burying its claws in her throat until she died. Its wild gaze then fell upon the head of the bed, where its master’s face, frozen in horror, was visible. The beast, still fearing the whip, was suddenly terrified. Aware of its guilt, it tried to hide the carnage, pacing the room in agitation, smashing furniture, and dragging the bed from its frame. Finally, it shoved the daughter’s body up the chimney and hurled the older woman’s corpse through the window. As the ape approached the window with the body, the sailor retreated in terror, sliding down the lightning rod and fleeing home, abandoning the beast. The sounds heard on the stairs were the Frenchman’s horrified cries mixed with the animal’s savage jabbering. There is little to add. The orangutan must have escaped down the rod just before the door was broken, closing the window behind it. The owner later captured it and sold it for a large sum to the Jardin des Plantes. Le Bon was released immediately after we explained the circumstances to the Prefect of Police. Though the Prefect was friendly toward my friend, he could not hide his annoyance at how things turned out, offering a few sarcastic remarks about people minding their own business. Let him talk, Dupin said, choosing not to respond. Let him speak; it will ease his conscience. I am satisfied with having outplayed him in his own domain. His failure to solve this mystery is not as surprising as he thinks; in truth, our friend the Prefect is far too cunning to be truly profound."
}

