parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "a209e60527cd2767be3bb435",
  "rewritten_text": "What is the trouble? I said, Dad, and Mom, and my sister, and— Then I broke down. He said, Oh, don't carry on so; we all have our troubles, and this one will turn out fine. What is the matter with them? They are—are you the watchman of the boat? Yes, he said, sounding quite pleased with himself. I am the captain, owner, mate, pilot, watchman, and head deckhand; sometimes I am the freight and passengers too. I am not as rich as old Jim Hornback, and I cannot be as generous to everyone as he is, throwing money around, but I have told him many times I would not trade places with him. A sailor's life is the life for me, and I would not live two miles out of town where nothing ever happens, not for all his money and more. I interrupted and said, They are in a terrible amount of trouble, and— Who is? Why, Dad, Mom, my sister, and Miss Hooker; if you would take your ferryboat and go up there— Up where? Where are they? On the wreck. What wreck? Why, there is only one. You do not mean the Walter Scott? Yes. Good heavens! What are they doing there? Well, they did not go there on purpose. I bet they did not! Goodness, there is no chance for them if they do not get off quickly! How on earth did they get into such a mess? It was easy enough. Miss Hooker was visiting up at Booth's Landing. She was visiting there, and just at dusk she started across with her maid in the horse-ferry to stay the night at a friend's house, but they lost their steering oar, drifted downstream, and crashed onto the wreck. The ferryman, the maid, and the horses were all lost, but Miss Hooker managed to grab hold and get onto the wreck. About an hour after dark, we came along in our trading scow, and it was so dark we did not notice the wreck until we were right on top of it. We crashed too, but all of us were saved except Bill Whipple—and he was such a good person! I almost wish it had been me. My word! That is the most incredible thing I have ever encountered."
}

