parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "155f7b8270c92f470d762d84",
  "rewritten_text": "This must have been the period of Mr. Elliot’s life that has always piqued my curiosity, Anne remarked. It was around the time he first met my father and sister. I never met him myself, only heard of him, but his behavior toward them and the circumstances of his marriage never quite aligned with the man I see today. It suggested a completely different character. I know everything, Mrs. Smith exclaimed. He was introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I knew him, but he spoke of them constantly. I know he was invited and encouraged, yet he chose not to go. I can clarify things you would not expect, and I knew all about his marriage at the time. I was the friend he confided in regarding his hopes and plans. Although I did not know his wife beforehand—her lower social standing made that impossible—I knew her for the rest of her life and can answer any questions you have. No, Anne said, I have no specific questions about her. I always understood they were unhappy. I only want to know why he avoided my father’s acquaintance back then, when my father was clearly inclined to treat him with kindness. Why did he pull away? At that time, Mrs. Smith replied, Mr. Elliot had one goal: to make his fortune, and faster than a legal career would allow. He was determined to marry for money and avoid an imprudent match. He believed—rightly or wrongly—that your father and sister were trying to arrange a marriage between the heir and your sister, which did not fit his plans for wealth and independence. That was his reason for withdrawing, I assure you. He told me the entire story; he kept no secrets from me. It is ironic that after leaving you in Bath, my first major acquaintance upon marrying was your cousin, and through him, I constantly heard about your father and sister."
}

