parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "556270c35b0c19d58932ae8a",
  "rewritten_text": "We spent a few miserable hours waiting for the trial to begin at eleven. My father and the rest of the family had to attend as witnesses, so I went with them. Throughout this pathetic excuse for a trial, I endured absolute torture. It was being decided whether my reckless curiosity would lead to the deaths of two people: one an innocent, joyful baby, and the other a victim of a horrific, infamous murder. Justine was a talented, promising young woman, yet now she faced a shameful death because of me. I would have gladly confessed to the crime myself, but I was away when it happened, and such a claim would have been dismissed as the ramblings of a madman without saving her. Justine looked calm. Dressed in mourning, her naturally engaging face was made even more beautiful by her solemnity. She seemed confident in her innocence and did not tremble, despite being stared at and cursed by thousands. The crowd’s sympathy had been replaced by horror at the crime she was accused of. Though her composure was clearly forced, she summoned the courage to appear brave. Upon entering the court, she spotted us and briefly teared up, but she quickly regained her composure, her sad, affectionate look proving her innocence. The trial started, and after the prosecutor presented the charges, witnesses were called. Several strange facts were used against her that might have convinced anyone who did not know the truth as I did. She had been out all night when the murder occurred and was seen by a market woman near the spot where the child’s body was later found. When asked what she was doing there, she gave a strange, confused answer. She returned home at eight o’clock, and when asked where she had been, she claimed she had been searching for the child and anxiously asked if there was any news."
}

