{
  "request_id": "9b5911c3eef7e75b5b670210",
  "rewritten_text": "She and the children could easily stay. Ah, papa, that is something you have never managed, and I doubt you ever will. Isabella cannot bear to be away from her husband. This was undeniable. Though he disliked the thought, Mr. Woodhouse could only sigh in resignation. Seeing his spirits dampen at the thought of his daughter’s devotion to her husband, Emma quickly shifted the conversation to cheer him. Harriet must spend as much time with us as possible while my brother and sister are visiting. I am sure she will adore the children. We are quite proud of them, aren't we, papa? I wonder which she will find more handsome, Henry or John? Yes, I wonder. Poor little things, they will be so happy to visit. They love being at Hartfield, Harriet. I am sure they do, sir. I cannot imagine who would not. Henry is a fine boy, but John looks just like his mother. Henry is the eldest, named after me rather than his father. John, the second, is named after his father. Some were surprised the eldest was not, but Isabella insisted on Henry, which I thought was very sweet. He is a clever boy, indeed. They are all remarkably bright and have such charming ways. They stand by my chair and ask, Grandpapa, can you give me some string? Once, Henry asked for a knife, but I told him knives are only for grandpapas. I think their father is often too rough with them. He seems rough to you, Emma replied, because you are so gentle. If you compared him to other fathers, you would not think so. He wants his boys to be active and hardy, and while he may be sharp when they misbehave, he is an affectionate father. Mr. John Knightley is truly devoted. The children love him. And then their uncle comes in and tosses them toward the ceiling in a terrifying way! But they love it, papa; it is their favorite thing. They enjoy it so much that if their uncle did not make them take turns, they would never stop. Well, I cannot understand it. That is true for all of us, papa. Half the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other."
}

