parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "18e5d60b32ea7918f74ffe27",
  "rewritten_text": "The night was dark and stormy. No stars shone above, but heavy thunder rumbled constantly, and lightning flashed across the clouds, seemingly mocking Franklin’s scientific control. The winds howled, adding to the wild atmosphere. In that dreary moment, I longed for human connection, and then my dearest friend, my guide and comfort, appeared beside me. She moved like a vision from a dream, a queen of natural beauty. Her step was silent, and had she not touched me, I might have missed her entirely. A strange sadness marked her face as she gestured toward the storm and asked me to observe the two figures outside. This nightmare filled ten pages and concluded with a sermon so exclusionary toward non-Presbyterians that it won first prize. The village mayor praised the work as the most eloquent he had ever heard, claiming even Daniel Webster would be proud. It is worth noting that the usual number of essays relied heavily on flowery language and clichés about life. The schoolmaster, slightly intoxicated, pushed his chair aside to draw a map of America on the blackboard for the geography class. His unsteady hand made a mess of it, causing the audience to giggle. He tried to fix his mistakes, but only made the map more distorted, which increased the laughter. Determined to succeed, he focused intently on his work, unaware that the amusement of the crowd was only growing."
}

