parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "3a3626cb4b1682583265df74",
  "rewritten_text": "Once my tears subsided, logic and recollection resumed, allowing me to contemplate my situation with composure. Though only hours had elapsed, my world had undergone a profound transformation; the weight of years seemed to have descended upon me since dawn. My father was effectively dead to me, and I felt as though I were mourning his actual passing. Yet he lived, though I, in my wretchedness, was forbidden from ever seeing or speaking to him again. I had to flee from him more urgently than from a foe, avoiding him in both city and wilderness. This realization left me gasping in agony, unable to organize my thoughts. I resolved to seek a life of bleak isolation, perhaps retreating to the Continent to live as a recluse, not out of religious devotion, but to remain forever severed from the world where I might weep undisturbed. Yet, what of my beloved, tormented father? Might he eventually conquer the fierce passion currently ruling him? Could he, years from now, once age had cooled his burning emotions, be a father to me again? This thought eased my brow, and a melancholy smile touched my lips. I dared to nurture hope; though years must pass, they would be buoyed by the prospect of reunion. Let him endure another sixteen years of lonely wandering, voicing his laments to distant forests and waterfalls, facing perils and hardships, his skin scorched by southern suns and his blood chilled by nocturnal rains. To this existence, miserable father, I consign you. Go, spend your days among savages and your nights beneath the open sky, until your body is weary, your heart frozen, and every trace of youth extinguished."
}

