parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b7bf79c9783fa0bfef9d09ac",
  "rewritten_text": "He stayed inside the next day, mostly hiding in his room, paralyzed by a frantic fear of death yet feeling indifferent toward life. The feeling of being hunted and trapped consumed him. A gust of wind moving the tapestry made him tremble, and dead leaves hitting the window felt like his own failed promises and regrets. Closing his eyes, he saw the sailor’s face through the glass again, and terror gripped his heart. Perhaps he had only imagined this vengeance. Real life was chaotic, but his imagination was terrifyingly logical, turning remorse into a shadow that followed his sins. In reality, the wicked were not always punished, nor the good rewarded; success simply went to the strong. Surely, if a stranger had been lurking, the servants or gardeners would have noticed. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not come for him; he had likely perished at sea. He was safe, especially since the man could not possibly know his identity. His youthful appearance had protected him. Yet, if it were only an illusion, it was terrifying that his conscience could create such vivid, haunting figures. What kind of life would he lead if shadows of his crimes mocked him from every corner or whispered in his ear? As this thought took hold, he turned pale, and the room seemed to grow cold. He had killed his friend in a moment of madness, and the memory was unbearable. Every gruesome detail returned, and the image of his sin rose before him, vivid and horrifying."
}

