parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "b7113a72d77c4141fc5f2d63",
  "rewritten_text": "The following day was set for the confrontation to ensure our honor remained intact. Drummle arrived with a polite note from Estella confirming she had danced with him several times. I had no choice but to apologize for my previous outburst and retract my earlier claims. Drummle and I sat there glaring at each other for an hour while the rest of the group argued, eventually declaring that our reconciliation was progressing remarkably well. I recount this casually, but it was deeply painful. It devastated me that Estella would show any interest in such a clumsy, dim-witted fool. I believe my reaction stemmed from a genuine, selfless love; I could not bear to see her lower herself to such a man. I would have been miserable regardless of her choice, but a more worthy suitor would have caused me a different kind of grief. I soon discovered that Drummle was pursuing her and she was permitting it. He followed her constantly, and we crossed paths daily. He persisted in his dull way, while Estella kept him on a string, alternating between encouragement and disdain, sometimes flattering him and other times acting as if he were a stranger. The Spider, as Jaggers called him, possessed the patience of his namesake. His arrogant reliance on his wealth and family status often served him as well as true focus. By doggedly watching Estella, he outlasted more impressive rivals, ready to strike at the perfect moment. At an assembly ball in Richmond, where Estella outshone everyone, Drummle hovered over her with her apparent consent. I decided to confront her about him at the first opportunity, which came while she sat alone among the flowers, waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home."
}

