parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "d6b556222e75a8705c2c3844",
  "rewritten_text": "He jumped up and grabbed her arm. \"Show him to me. Which one is he? Point him out. I must see him!\" he shouted, but the Duke of Berwick’s carriage blocked their view, and by the time it passed, the other carriage had vanished. \"He is gone,\" Sibyl whispered sadly. \"I wish you had seen him.\" \"I wish I had, because I swear to God, if he ever hurts you, I will kill him.\" She looked at him in horror. He repeated the threat, his words sharp as a blade. Bystanders stared, and a nearby woman giggled. \"Come away, Jim,\" she whispered. He followed her through the crowd, feeling satisfied with his outburst. At the Achilles Statue, she turned to him with pity in her eyes, though she soon laughed. \"You are foolish, Jim; just a bad-tempered boy. How can you say such horrible things? You are just jealous. I wish you would fall in love; it makes people good, and what you said was wicked.\" \"I am sixteen,\" he replied, \"and I know what I am doing. Mother cannot help you. I wish I were not going to Australia. I would quit if my contract were not already signed.\" \"Don't be so serious. You sound like a character from one of Mother’s silly plays. I won't argue with you. Seeing him is perfect happiness. You would never harm someone I love, would you?\" \"Not as long as you love him, I suppose,\" he muttered. \"I shall love him forever!\" she cried. \"And he?\" \"Forever, too!\" \"He had better.\" She pulled away, then laughed and touched his arm, dismissing him as just a boy. At Marble Arch, they caught a bus to their shabby home on Euston Road. It was after five, and Sibyl needed to rest before her performance. Jim insisted she lie down, preferring to say goodbye without their mother present, as he hated the scenes she was prone to making."
}

