parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "91374c6638e0cb8e47faead2",
  "rewritten_text": "He was short but stood remarkably straight, and his voice was the most pleasant I had ever heard. He began his lecture by summarizing the history of chemistry and the advancements made by various scholars, speaking with passion about the most famous discoverers. He then gave a brief overview of the current state of the field and explained its basic terms. After performing a few introductory experiments, he concluded with high praise for modern chemistry, the words of which I will never forget: The ancient teachers of this science promised the impossible and achieved nothing. Modern masters promise very little; they know that metals cannot be changed and that the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these scientists, whose hands seem meant only for dirt and whose eyes for microscopes or crucibles, have truly performed miracles. They explore nature’s secrets and reveal how she works in hiding. They reach into the heavens; they have discovered how blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new and nearly unlimited powers; they can command lightning, mimic earthquakes, and even imitate the invisible world. These were the professor’s words—or rather, the words of fate—spoken to destroy me. As he continued, I felt as if my soul were fighting a physical enemy. One by one, the various parts of my being were stirred, and soon my mind was filled with a single purpose. So much has been done, my soul exclaimed, but I will achieve far more. Following the path already marked, I will pioneer a new way, explore unknown powers, and reveal the deepest mysteries of creation to the world. I did not sleep that night. My mind was in a state of chaos, and though I felt that order would eventually emerge, I could not create it myself. By dawn, sleep finally came. When I awoke, my thoughts from the night before felt like a dream, yet I remained determined to return to my old studies and devote myself to a science for which I believed I had a natural talent. That same day, I visited M. Waldman. His private manner was even more gentle and engaging than his public one, as the dignity he displayed during his lecture was replaced at home by great warmth and kindness."
}

