parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "992703217387175d74248696",
  "rewritten_text": "But do not think I failed to see it as a problem. You can be sure they were perfectly comfortable. That does not make me like it any better. So many women and children have no right to be comfortable on a ship. My dear Frederick, you are being foolish. What would happen to us sailors' wives, who often need to travel to join our husbands, if everyone felt as you do? My feelings did not stop me from taking Mrs. Harville and her family to Plymouth. I dislike hearing you talk like a snob, as if women were delicate creatures rather than rational people. None of us expect a life without challenges. Ah, my dear, when he has a wife, he will change his tune. If we are lucky enough to see another war, he will act just as we have. He will be very grateful to anyone who brings his wife to him. Yes, he will. I am finished with this, Captain Wentworth said. When married people tell me I will think differently once I am married, I can only say I will not, and they insist I will, and that is the end of it. He stood up and walked away. You must have traveled a great deal, ma'am, Mrs. Musgrove said to Mrs. Croft. Quite a bit in my fifteen years of marriage, though many have done more. I have crossed the Atlantic four times and been to the East Indies once, along with time in Cork, Lisbon, and Gibraltar. I never went past the Mediterranean or to the West Indies. We do not consider Bermuda or the Bahamas to be the West Indies, you know. Mrs. Musgrove had nothing to say, as she had never even thought about the matter. I assure you, ma'am, Mrs. Croft continued, that the accommodations on a large warship are excellent. On a frigate, you are more crowded, but any reasonable woman can be happy there. I can honestly say the happiest years of my life were spent on a ship. As long as we were together, there was nothing to fear."
}

