parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "52268bfd7cf64d1923b998fc",
  "rewritten_text": "Basil, this is incredible! I must meet Dorian Gray. Hallward stood up and paced the garden before returning. Harry, he said, Dorian Gray is simply an artistic inspiration to me. You might see nothing in him, but I see everything. He influences my work even when he is not physically present. He suggests a new style, appearing in the curves of my lines and the subtlety of my colors. That is all. Then why won't you exhibit his portrait? Lord Henry asked. Because I have unintentionally poured my own deep artistic devotion into it, and I have never spoken to him about it. He knows nothing, and he never will. The public might guess, however, and I refuse to expose my soul to their shallow curiosity. I will not let them examine my heart under a microscope. There is too much of myself in that painting. Poets are less careful, Lord Henry replied. They know that passion sells. Nowadays, a broken heart can lead to many book sales. I hate them for that, Hallward cried. An artist should create beauty without putting their own life into it. We live in an era where people treat art like an autobiography. We have lost the true sense of abstract beauty. One day I will show the world what art should be, and for that reason, the world will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray. I think you are wrong, but I will not argue. Only those who are intellectually lost argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray fond of you? The painter paused. He likes me, he said. I flatter him constantly, and I find a strange pleasure in saying things I later regret. Usually, he is charming, and we talk for hours in the studio. Occasionally, however, he is thoughtless and seems to enjoy hurting me. At those times, I feel I have given my entire soul to someone who treats it like a simple flower for his lapel—a mere decoration for his vanity."
}

