parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "9fbc4f2b8c6e694fd62639c7",
  "rewritten_text": "Mr. Merton felt quite troubled by the wedding’s second delay, and Lady Julia, having already purchased her gown, urged Sybil to end the engagement. Despite her deep devotion to her mother, Sybil had committed her future to Lord Arthur and remained steadfast. Lord Arthur struggled for days with his frustration, his nerves frayed by the setback. Eventually, his practical nature prevailed, and he concluded that since poison had failed, an explosive device was the logical next step. He reviewed his acquaintances and decided to target his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean was a scholar with a passion for antique clocks, a hobby Lord Arthur believed provided the perfect opening for his plan. Finding an explosive proved difficult; the London Directory offered no leads, and he doubted Scotland Yard would be helpful, as they rarely anticipated such events. He then recalled Count Rouvaloff, a Russian radical he had met at Lady Windermere’s. Though ostensibly in England to research Peter the Great, Rouvaloff was widely considered a Nihilist agent, and the Russian Embassy viewed him with suspicion. Believing him to be the right contact, Lord Arthur visited his Bloomsbury lodgings to seek help. When Lord Arthur explained his request, Rouvaloff asked if he was becoming involved in politics. Lord Arthur, disliking pretense, clarified that he had no interest in social issues and required the device solely for a private family affair."
}

