parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "ace183032d4bcf6844dc7674",
  "rewritten_text": "Rising at dawn and once more eluding my creditors, I hurried to the bookseller to spend my remaining cash on texts regarding Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. Back home, I studied these volumes diligently, quickly gaining the expertise required for my scheme. During this time, I worked to appease the three creditors who plagued me. I eventually succeeded by selling off half my furniture to cover part of the debt and promising the remainder upon the completion of a project for which I requested their aid. Being simple men, they were easily persuaded. Once settled, I secretly arranged with my wife to sell my remaining belongings and borrowed significant sums under various pretexts, ignoring how I might repay them. With these funds, I acquired fine cambric muslin, twine, caoutchouc varnish, a custom wicker basket, and other materials for a massive balloon. I instructed my wife on its assembly while I fashioned the twine into a net, fitted it with a hoop and cords, and purchased a quadrant, compass, spyglass, modified barometer, and two obscure astronomical tools. Under cover of night, I transported to a secluded spot east of Rotterdam five fifty-gallon iron-bound casks, a larger one, six ten-foot tinned tubes, a specific semi-metal, and twelve demijohns of common acid. The gas produced from these is unique to my work, or at least never before used this way. I would reveal the secret, but it rightfully belongs to a citizen of Nantz, France, who shared it with me under conditions."
}

