parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "0277963b8c03d8044f8cb0bc",
  "rewritten_text": "A chaotic mix of farmers, butchers, traders, thieves, and idlers crowded the market. The air was filled with the whistling of drovers, barking dogs, the noise of livestock, and the shouting and swearing of the mob. Bells rang from taverns, adding to the overwhelming roar of voices and the constant pushing and shoving. The scene was filthy and deafening, leaving the senses completely overwhelmed. Mr. Sikes pulled Oliver through the thickest part of the crowd, ignoring the sights that shocked the boy. He nodded to a few acquaintances and refused several offers for a drink, pushing forward until they reached Holborn. Looking at the clock on St. Andrew’s Church, Sikes told Oliver it was nearly seven and urged him to walk faster. He jerked the boy’s wrist, forcing Oliver to trot to keep up with his long strides. They continued at this pace past Hyde Park Corner toward Kensington. When Sikes saw an empty cart labeled Hounslow, he politely asked the driver for a ride to Isleworth. The driver agreed and asked if Oliver was his son. Sikes confirmed it, his hand resting on his pocket where he kept a pistol. The driver noticed Oliver was breathless and remarked that his father walked quite fast. Sikes quickly interrupted, claiming the boy was used to it, and helped him into the cart. He told Oliver to rest on a pile of sacks. As they passed various milestones, Oliver wondered where they were going. They traveled through Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford, continuing their journey without stopping."
}

