parts.text:
{
  "request_id": "e93ed036df094b2adbc8e6e8",
  "rewritten_text": "Will you? His eyes brightened, and he said, I will help you steal him! I was stunned, feeling as if I had been shot. It was the most shocking thing I had ever heard, and I must admit my opinion of Tom Sawyer dropped significantly. I simply could not believe it. Tom Sawyer, a person who would help steal a slave? Oh, stop, I said. You are joking. I am not joking at all. Well then, I replied, joking or not, if you hear anything about a runaway, remember that you know nothing about him, and neither do I. We loaded the trunk into my wagon, and he drove off in one direction while I went the other. I was so preoccupied and excited that I forgot to drive slowly, arriving home much faster than expected. The old man was at the door and remarked, This is incredible! Who would have thought that mare had it in her? I wish we had timed her. She is not even sweating. It is amazing. I would not take a hundred dollars for her now, even though I would have sold her for fifteen earlier. He was the most innocent, kind-hearted man I have ever known. This was not surprising, as he was not just a farmer but also a preacher who had built a small log church behind his plantation at his own expense, where he held services and taught school for free. There were many such farmer-preachers in the South. About thirty minutes later, Tom’s wagon pulled up to the front gate. Aunt Sally saw it from the window and said, Someone has arrived! I wonder who it is? It looks like a stranger. Jimmy, run and tell Lize to set another plate for dinner. Everyone rushed to the front door, as a visitor was a rare and exciting event. Tom climbed over the gate and headed toward the house while his wagon sped off toward the village. We all gathered at the entrance. Tom was wearing his best clothes and had an audience, which was always exactly what he wanted."
}

